\par
                                   I.\par
\par
                           PLAYING PILGRIMS.\par
\par
\par
"Christmas won't be Christmas without any presents," grumbled Jo, lying\par
on the rug.\par
\par
"It's so dreadful to be poor!" sighed Meg, looking down at her old\par
dress.\par
\par
"I don't think it's fair for some girls to have plenty of pretty things,\par
and other girls nothing at all," added little Amy, with an injured\par
sniff.\par
\par
"We've got father and mother and each other," said Beth contentedly,\par
from her corner.\par
\par
The four young faces on which the firelight shone brightened at the\par
cheerful words, but darkened again as Jo said sadly,--\par
\par
"We haven't got father, and shall not have him for a long time." She\par
didn't say "perhaps never," but each silently added it, thinking of\par
father far away, where the fighting was.\par
\par
Nobody spoke for a minute; then Meg said in an altered tone,--\par
\par
"You know the reason mother proposed not having any presents this\par
Christmas was because it is going to be a hard winter for every one; and\par
she thinks we ought not to spend money for pleasure, when our men are\par
suffering so in the army. We can't do much, but we can make our little\par
sacrifices, and ought to do it gladly. But I am afraid I don't;" and Meg\par
shook her head, as she thought regretfully of all the pretty things she\par
wanted.\par
\par
"But I don't think the little we should spend would do any good. We've\par
each got a dollar, and the army wouldn't be much helped by our giving\par
that. I agree not to expect anything from mother or you, but I do want\par
to buy Undine and Sintram for myself; I've wanted it _so_ long," said\par
Jo, who was a bookworm.\par
\par
"I planned to spend mine in new music," said Beth, with a little sigh,\par
which no one heard but the hearth-brush and kettle-holder.\par
\par
"I shall get a nice box of Faber's drawing-pencils; I really need them,"\par
said Amy decidedly.\par
\par
"Mother didn't say anything about our money, and she won't wish us to\par
give up everything. Let's each buy what we want, and have a little fun;\par
I'm sure we work hard enough to earn it," cried Jo, examining the heels\par
of her shoes in a gentlemanly manner.\par
\par
"I know _I_ do,--teaching those tiresome children nearly all day, when\par
I'm longing to enjoy myself at home," began Meg, in the complaining tone\par
again.\par
\par
"You don't have half such a hard time as I do," said Jo. "How would you\par
like to be shut up for hours with a nervous, fussy old lady, who keeps\par
you trotting, is never satisfied, and worries you till you're ready to\par
fly out of the window or cry?"\par
\par
"It's naughty to fret; but I do think washing dishes and keeping things\par
tidy is the worst work in the world. It makes me cross; and my hands get\par
so stiff, I can't practise well at all;" and Beth looked at her rough\par
hands with a sigh that any one could hear that time.\par
\par
"I don't believe any of you suffer as I do," cried Amy; "for you don't\par
have to go to school with impertinent girls, who plague you if you don't\par
know your lessons, and laugh at your dresses, and label your father if\par
he isn't rich, and insult you when your nose isn't nice."\par
\par
"If you mean _libel_, I'd say so, and not talk about _labels_, as if\par
papa was a pickle-bottle," advised Jo, laughing.\par
\par
"I know what I mean, and you needn't be _statirical_ about it. It's\par
proper to use good words, and improve your _vocabilary_," returned Amy,\par
with dignity.\par
\par
"Don't peck at one another, children. Don't you wish we had the money\par
papa lost when we were little, Jo? Dear me! how happy and good we'd be,\par
if we had no worries!" said Meg, who could remember better times.\par
\par
"You said the other day, you thought we were a deal happier than the\par
King children, for they were fighting and fretting all the time, in\par
spite of their money."\par
\par
"So I did, Beth. Well, I think we are; for, though we do have to work,\par
we make fun for ourselves, and are a pretty jolly set, as Jo would say."\par
\par
"Jo does use such slang words!" observed Amy, with a reproving look at\par
the long figure stretched on the rug. Jo immediately sat up, put her\par
hands in her pockets, and began to whistle.\par
\par
"Don't, Jo; it's so boyish!"\par
\par
"That's why I do it."\par
\par
"I detest rude, unlady-like girls!"\par
\par
"I hate affected, niminy-piminy chits!"\par
\par
"'Birds in their little nests agree,'" sang Beth, the peace-maker, with\par
such a funny face that both sharp voices softened to a laugh, and the\par
"pecking" ended for that time.\par
\par
"Really, girls, you are both to be blamed," said Meg, beginning to\par
lecture in her elder-sisterly fashion. "You are old enough to leave off\par
boyish tricks, and to behave better, Josephine. It didn't matter so much\par
when you were a little girl; but now you are so tall, and turn up your\par
hair, you should remember that you are a young lady."\par
\par
"I'm not! and if turning up my hair makes me one, I'll wear it in two\par
tails till I'm twenty," cried Jo, pulling off her net, and shaking down\par
a chestnut mane. "I hate to think I've got to grow up, and be Miss\par
March, and wear long gowns, and look as prim as a China-aster! It's bad\par
enough to be a girl, anyway, when I like boys' games and work and\par
manners! I can't get over my disappointment in not being a boy; and it's\par
worse than ever now, for I'm dying to go and fight with papa, and I can\par
only stay at home and knit, like a poky old woman!" And Jo shook the\par
blue army-sock till the needles rattled like castanets, and her ball\par
bounded across the room.\par
\par
"Poor Jo! It's too bad, but it can't be helped; so you must try to be\par
contented with making your name boyish, and playing brother to us\par
girls," said Beth, stroking the rough head at her knee with a hand that\par
all the dish-washing and dusting in the world could not make ungentle in\par
its touch.\par
\par
"As for you, Amy," continued Meg, "you are altogether too particular and\par
prim. Your airs are funny now; but you'll grow up an affected little\par
goose, if you don't take care. I like your nice manners and refined ways\par
of speaking, when you don't try to be elegant; but your absurd words are\par
as bad as Jo's slang."\par
\par
"If Jo is a tom-boy and Amy a goose, what am I, please?" asked Beth,\par
ready to share the lecture.\par
\par
"You're a dear, and nothing else," answered Meg warmly; and no one\par
contradicted her, for the "Mouse" was the pet of the family.\par
\par
As young readers like to know "how people look," we will take this\par
moment to give them a little sketch of the four sisters, who sat\par
knitting away in the twilight, while the December snow fell quietly\par
without, and the fire crackled cheerfully within. It was a comfortable\par
old room, though the carpet was faded and the furniture very plain; for\par
a good picture or two hung on the walls, books filled the recesses,\par
chrysanthemums and Christmas roses bloomed in the windows, and a\par
pleasant atmosphere of home-peace pervaded it.\par
\par
Margaret, the eldest of the four, was sixteen, and very pretty, being\par
plump and fair, with large eyes, plenty of soft, brown hair, a sweet\par
mouth, and white hands, of which she was rather vain. Fifteen-year-old\par
Jo was very tall, thin, and brown, and reminded one of a colt; for she\par
never seemed to know what to do with her long limbs, which were very\par
much in her way. She had a decided mouth, a comical nose, and sharp,\par
gray eyes, which appeared to see everything, and were by turns fierce,\par
funny, or thoughtful. Her long, thick hair was her one beauty; but it\par
was usually bundled into a net, to be out of her way. Round shoulders\par
had Jo, big hands and feet, a fly-away look to her clothes, and the\par
uncomfortable appearance of a girl who was rapidly shooting up into a\par
woman, and didn't like it. Elizabeth--or Beth, as every one called\par
her--was a rosy, smooth-haired, bright-eyed girl of thirteen, with a shy\par
manner, a timid voice, and a peaceful expression, which was seldom\par
disturbed. Her father called her "Little Tranquillity," and the name\par
suited her excellently; for she seemed to live in a happy world of her\par
own, only venturing out to meet the few whom she trusted and loved. Amy,\par
though the youngest, was a most important person,--in her own opinion at\par
least. A regular snow-maiden, with blue eyes, and yellow hair, curling\par
on her shoulders, pale and slender, and always carrying herself like a\par
young lady mindful of her manners. What the characters of the four\par
sisters were we will leave to be found out.\par
\par
The clock struck six; and, having swept up the hearth, Beth put a pair\par
of slippers down to warm. Somehow the sight of the old shoes had a good\par
effect upon the girls; for mother was coming, and every one brightened\par
to welcome her. Meg stopped lecturing, and lighted the lamp, Amy got out\par
of the easy-chair without being asked, and Jo forgot how tired she was\par
as she sat up to hold the slippers nearer to the blaze.\par
\par
        [Illustration: Beth put a pair of slippers down to warm]\par
\par
"They are quite worn out; Marmee must have a new pair."\par
\par
"I thought I'd get her some with my dollar," said Beth.\par
\par
"No, I shall!" cried Amy.\par
\par
"I'm the oldest," began Meg, but Jo cut in with a decided--\par
\par
"I'm the man of the family now papa is away, and _I_ shall provide the\par
slippers, for he told me to take special care of mother while he was\par
gone."\par
\par
"I'll tell you what we'll do," said Beth; "let's each get her something\par
for Christmas, and not get anything for ourselves."\par
\par
"That's like you, dear! What will we get?" exclaimed Jo.\par
\par
Every one thought soberly for a minute; then Meg announced, as if the\par
idea was suggested by the sight of her own pretty hands, "I shall give\par
her a nice pair of gloves."\par
\par
"Army shoes, best to be had," cried Jo.\par
\par
"Some handkerchiefs, all hemmed," said Beth.\par
\par
"I'll get a little bottle of cologne; she likes it, and it won't cost\par
much, so I'll have some left to buy my pencils," added Amy.\par
\par
"How will we give the things?" asked Meg.\par
\par
"Put them on the table, and bring her in and see her open the bundles.\par
Don't you remember how we used to do on our birthdays?" answered Jo.\par
\par
        [Illustration: I used to be so frightened when it was my\par
                     turn to sit in the big chair]\par
\par
"I used to be _so_ frightened when it was my turn to sit in the big\par
chair with the crown on, and see you all come marching round to give the\par
presents, with a kiss. I liked the things and the kisses, but it was\par
dreadful to have you sit looking at me while I opened the bundles," said\par
Beth, who was toasting her face and the bread for tea, at the same time.\par
\par
"Let Marmee think we are getting things for ourselves, and then surprise\par
her. We must go shopping to-morrow afternoon, Meg; there is so much to\par
do about the play for Christmas night," said Jo, marching up and down,\par
with her hands behind her back and her nose in the air.\par
\par
"I don't mean to act any more after this time; I'm getting too old for\par
such things," observed Meg, who was as much a child as ever about\par
"dressing-up" frolics.\par
\par
"You won't stop, I know, as long as you can trail round in a white gown\par
with your hair down, and wear gold-paper jewelry. You are the best\par
actress we've got, and there'll be an end of everything if you quit the\par
boards," said Jo. "We ought to rehearse to-night. Come here, Amy, and do\par
the fainting scene, for you are as stiff as a poker in that."\par
\par
"I can't help it; I never saw any one faint, and I don't choose to make\par
myself all black and blue, tumbling flat as you do. If I can go down\par
easily, I'll drop; if I can't, I shall fall into a chair and be\par
graceful; I don't care if Hugo does come at me with a pistol," returned\par
Amy, who was not gifted with dramatic power, but was chosen because she\par
was small enough to be borne out shrieking by the villain of the piece.\par
\par
          [Illustration: Do it this way, clasp your hands so]\par
\par
"Do it this way; clasp your hands so, and stagger across the room,\par
crying frantically, 'Roderigo! save me! save me!'" and away went Jo,\par
with a melodramatic scream which was truly thrilling.\par
\par
Amy followed, but she poked her hands out stiffly before her, and jerked\par
herself along as if she went by machinery; and her "Ow!" was more\par
suggestive of pins being run into her than of fear and anguish. Jo gave\par
a despairing groan, and Meg laughed outright, while Beth let her bread\par
burn as she watched the fun, with interest.\par
\par
"It's no use! Do the best you can when the time comes, and if the\par
audience laugh, don't blame me. Come on, Meg."\par
\par
Then things went smoothly, for Don Pedro defied the world in a speech of\par
two pages without a single break; Hagar, the witch, chanted an awful\par
incantation over her kettleful of simmering toads, with weird effect;\par
Roderigo rent his chains asunder manfully, and Hugo died in agonies of\par
remorse and arsenic, with a wild "Ha! ha!"\par
\par
"It's the best we've had yet," said Meg, as the dead villain sat up and\par
rubbed his elbows.\par
\par
"I don't see how you can write and act such splendid things, Jo. You're\par
a regular Shakespeare!" exclaimed Beth, who firmly believed that her\par
sisters were gifted with wonderful genius in all things.\par
\par
"Not quite," replied Jo modestly. "I do think 'The Witch's Curse, an\par
Operatic Tragedy,' is rather a nice thing; but I'd like to try Macbeth,\par
if we only had a trap-door for Banquo. I always wanted to do the killing\par
part. 'Is that a dagger that I see before me?'" muttered Jo, rolling her\par
eyes and clutching at the air, as she had seen a famous tragedian do.\par
\par
"No, it's the toasting fork, with mother's shoe on it instead of the\par
bread. Beth's stage-struck!" cried Meg, and the rehearsal ended in a\par
general burst of laughter.\par
\par
"Glad to find you so merry, my girls," said a cheery voice at the door,\par
and actors and audience turned to welcome a tall, motherly lady, with a\par
"can-I-help-you" look about her which was truly delightful. She was not\par
elegantly dressed, but a noble-looking woman, and the girls thought the\par
gray cloak and unfashionable bonnet covered the most splendid mother in\par
the world.\par
\par
"Well, dearies, how have you got on to-day? There was so much to do,\par
getting the boxes ready to go to-morrow, that I didn't come home to\par
dinner. Has any one called, Beth? How is your cold, Meg? Jo, you look\par
tired to death. Come and kiss me, baby."\par
\par
While making these maternal inquiries Mrs. March got her wet things off,\par
her warm slippers on, and sitting down in the easy-chair, drew Amy to\par
her lap, preparing to enjoy the happiest hour of her busy day. The girls\par
flew about, trying to make things comfortable, each in her own way. Meg\par
arranged the tea-table; Jo brought wood and set chairs, dropping,\par
overturning, and clattering everything she touched; Beth trotted to and\par
fro between parlor and kitchen, quiet and busy; while Amy gave\par
directions to every one, as she sat with her hands folded.\par
\par
As they gathered about the table, Mrs. March said, with a particularly\par
happy face, "I've got a treat for you after supper."\par
\par
A quick, bright smile went round like a streak of sunshine. Beth clapped\par
her hands, regardless of the biscuit she held, and Jo tossed up her\par
napkin, crying, "A letter! a letter! Three cheers for father!"\par
\par
"Yes, a nice long letter. He is well, and thinks he shall get through\par
the cold season better than we feared. He sends all sorts of loving\par
wishes for Christmas, and an especial message to you girls," said Mrs.\par
March, patting her pocket as if she had got a treasure there.\par
\par
"Hurry and get done! Don't stop to quirk your little finger, and simper\par
over your plate, Amy," cried Jo, choking in her tea, and dropping her\par
bread, butter side down, on the carpet, in her haste to get at the\par
treat.\par
\par
Beth ate no more, but crept away, to sit in her shadowy corner and brood\par
over the delight to come, till the others were ready.\par
\par
"I think it was so splendid in father to go as a chaplain when he was\par
too old to be drafted, and not strong enough for a soldier," said Meg\par
warmly.\par
\par
"Don't I wish I could go as a drummer, a _vivan_--what's its name? or a\par
nurse, so I could be near him and help him," exclaimed Jo, with a groan.\par
\par
"It must be very disagreeable to sleep in a tent, and eat all sorts of\par
bad-tasting things, and drink out of a tin mug," sighed Amy.\par
\par
"When will he come home, Marmee?" asked Beth, with a little quiver in\par
her voice.\par
\par
"Not for many months, dear, unless he is sick. He will stay and do his\par
work faithfully as long as he can, and we won't ask for him back a\par
minute sooner than he can be spared. Now come and hear the letter."\par
\par
They all drew to the fire, mother in the big chair with Beth at her\par
feet, Meg and Amy perched on either arm of the chair, and Jo leaning on\par
the back, where no one would see any sign of emotion if the letter\par
should happen to be touching.\par
\par
Very few letters were written in those hard times that were not\par
touching, especially those which fathers sent home. In this one little\par
was said of the hardships endured, the dangers faced, or the\par
homesickness conquered; it was a cheerful, hopeful letter, full of\par
lively descriptions of camp life, marches, and military news; and only\par
at the end did the writer's heart overflow with fatherly love and\par
longing for the little girls at home.\par
\par
           [Illustration: It was a cheerful, hopeful letter]\par
\par
"Give them all my dear love and a kiss. Tell them I think of them by\par
day, pray for them by night, and find my best comfort in their affection\par
at all times. A year seems very long to wait before I see them, but\par
remind them that while we wait we may all work, so that these hard days\par
need not be wasted. I know they will remember all I said to them, that\par
they will be loving children to you, will do their duty faithfully,\par
fight their bosom enemies bravely, and conquer themselves so\par
beautifully, that when I come back to them I may be fonder and prouder\par
than ever of my little women."\par
\par
Everybody sniffed when they came to that part; Jo wasn't ashamed of the\par
great tear that dropped off the end of her nose, and Amy never minded\par
the rumpling of her curls as she hid her face on her mother's shoulder\par
and sobbed out, "I _am_ a selfish girl! but I'll truly try to be better,\par
so he mayn't be disappointed in me by and by."\par
\par
"We all will!" cried Meg. "I think too much of my looks, and hate to\par
work, but won't any more, if I can help it."\par
\par
"I'll try and be what he loves to call me, 'a little woman,' and not be\par
rough and wild; but do my duty here instead of wanting to be somewhere\par
else," said Jo, thinking that keeping her temper at home was a much\par
harder task than facing a rebel or two down South.\par
\par
Beth said nothing, but wiped away her tears with the blue army-sock, and\par
began to knit with all her might, losing no time in doing the duty that\par
lay nearest her, while she resolved in her quiet little soul to be all\par
that father hoped to find her when the year brought round the happy\par
coming home.\par
\par
        [Illustration: How you used to play Pilgrim's Progress]\par
\par
Mrs. March broke the silence that followed Jo's words, by saying in her\par
cheery voice, "Do you remember how you used to play Pilgrim's Progress\par
when you were little things? Nothing delighted you more than to have me\par
tie my piece-bags on your backs for burdens, give you hats and sticks\par
and rolls of paper, and let you travel through the house from the\par
cellar, which was the City of Destruction, up, up, to the house-top,\par
where you had all the lovely things you could collect to make a\par
Celestial City."\par
\par
"What fun it was, especially going by the lions, fighting Apollyon, and\par
passing through the Valley where the hobgoblins were!" said Jo.\par
\par
"I liked the place where the bundles fell off and tumbled down stairs,"\par
said Meg.\par
\par
"My favorite part was when we came out on the flat roof where our\par
flowers and arbors and pretty things were, and all stood and sung for\par
joy up there in the sunshine," said Beth, smiling, as if that pleasant\par
moment had come back to her.\par
\par
"I don't remember much about it, except that I was afraid of the cellar\par
and the dark entry, and always liked the cake and milk we had up at the\par
top. If I wasn't too old for such things, I'd rather like to play it\par
over again," said Amy, who began to talk of renouncing childish things\par
at the mature age of twelve.\par
\par
"We never are too old for this, my dear, because it is a play we are\par
playing all the time in one way or another. Our burdens are here, our\par
road is before us, and the longing for goodness and happiness is the\par
guide that leads us through many troubles and mistakes to the peace\par
which is a true Celestial City. Now, my little pilgrims, suppose you\par
begin again, not in play, but in earnest, and see how far on you can get\par
before father comes home."\par
\par
"Really, mother? Where are our bundles?" asked Amy, who was a very\par
literal young lady.\par
\par
"Each of you told what your burden was just now, except Beth; I rather\par
think she hasn't got any," said her mother.\par
\par
"Yes, I have; mine is dishes and dusters, and envying girls with nice\par
pianos, and being afraid of people."\par
\par
Beth's bundle was such a funny one that everybody wanted to laugh; but\par
nobody did, for it would have hurt her feelings very much.\par
\par
"Let us do it," said Meg thoughtfully. "It is only another name for\par
trying to be good, and the story may help us; for though we do want to\par
be good, it's hard work, and we forget, and don't do our best."\par
\par
"We were in the Slough of Despond to-night, and mother came and pulled\par
us out as Help did in the book. We ought to have our roll of directions,\par
like Christian. What shall we do about that?" asked Jo, delighted with\par
the fancy which lent a little romance to the very dull task of doing her\par
duty.\par
\par
"Look under your pillows, Christmas morning, and you will find your\par
guide-book," replied Mrs. March.\par
\par
They talked over the new plan while old Hannah cleared the table; then\par
out came the four little work-baskets, and the needles flew as the girls\par
made sheets for Aunt March. It was uninteresting sewing, but to-night no\par
one grumbled. They adopted Jo's plan of dividing the long seams into\par
four parts, and calling the quarters Europe, Asia, Africa, and America,\par
and in that way got on capitally, especially when they talked about the\par
different countries as they stitched their way through them.\par
\par
   [Illustration: No one but Beth could get much music out of the old\par
                                 piano]\par
\par
At nine they stopped work, and sung, as usual, before they went to bed.\par
No one but Beth could get much music out of the old piano; but she had a\par
way of softly touching the yellow keys, and making a pleasant\par
accompaniment to the simple songs they sung. Meg had a voice like a\par
flute, and she and her mother led the little choir. Amy chirped like a\par
cricket, and Jo wandered through the airs at her own sweet will, always\par
coming out at the wrong place with a croak or a quaver that spoilt the\par
most pensive tune. They had always done this from the time they could\par
lisp\par
\par
        "Crinkle, crinkle, 'ittle 'tar,"\par
\par
and it had become a household custom, for the mother was a born singer.\par
The first sound in the morning was her voice, as she went about the\par
house singing like a lark; and the last sound at night was the same\par
cheery sound, for the girls never grew too old for that familiar\par
lullaby.\par
\par
      [Illustration: At nine they stopped work and sung as usual]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                   [Illustration: A Merry Christmas]\par
\par
                                   II.\par
\par
                           A MERRY CHRISTMAS.\par
\par
\par
Jo was the first to wake in the gray dawn of Christmas morning. No\par
stockings hung at the fireplace, and for a moment she felt as much\par
disappointed as she did long ago, when her little sock fell down because\par
it was so crammed with goodies. Then she remembered her mother's\par
promise, and, slipping her hand under her pillow, drew out a little\par
crimson-covered book. She knew it very well, for it was that beautiful\par
old story of the best life ever lived, and Jo felt that it was a true\par
guide-book for any pilgrim going the long journey. She woke Meg with a\par
"Merry Christmas," and bade her see what was under her pillow. A\par
green-covered book appeared, with the same picture inside, and a few\par
words written by their mother, which made their one present very\par
precious in their eyes. Presently Beth and Amy woke, to rummage and find\par
their little books also,--one dove-colored, the other blue; and all sat\par
looking at and talking about them, while the east grew rosy with the\par
coming day.\par
\par
In spite of her small vanities, Margaret had a sweet and pious nature,\par
which unconsciously influenced her sisters, especially Jo, who loved her\par
very tenderly, and obeyed her because her advice was so gently given.\par
\par
"Girls," said Meg seriously, looking from the tumbled head beside her to\par
the two little night-capped ones in the room beyond, "mother wants us to\par
read and love and mind these books, and we must begin at once. We used\par
to be faithful about it; but since father went away, and all this war\par
trouble unsettled us, we have neglected many things. You can do as you\par
please; but _I_ shall keep my book on the table here, and read a little\par
every morning as soon as I wake, for I know it will do me good, and help\par
me through the day."\par
\par
Then she opened her new book and began to read. Jo put her arm round\par
her, and, leaning cheek to cheek, read also, with the quiet expression\par
so seldom seen on her restless face.\par
\par
"How good Meg is! Come, Amy, let's do as they do. I'll help you with the\par
hard words, and they'll explain things if we don't understand,"\par
whispered Beth, very much impressed by the pretty books and her sisters'\par
example.\par
\par
"I'm glad mine is blue," said Amy; and then the rooms were very still\par
while the pages were softly turned, and the winter sunshine crept in to\par
touch the bright heads and serious faces with a Christmas greeting.\par
\par
"Where is mother?" asked Meg, as she and Jo ran down to thank her for\par
their gifts, half an hour later.\par
\par
"Goodness only knows. Some poor creeter come a-beggin', and your ma went\par
straight off to see what was needed. There never _was_ such a woman for\par
givin' away vittles and drink, clothes and firin'," replied Hannah, who\par
had lived with the family since Meg was born, and was considered by them\par
all more as a friend than a servant.\par
\par
"She will be back soon, I think; so fry your cakes, and have everything\par
ready," said Meg, looking over the presents which were collected in a\par
basket and kept under the sofa, ready to be produced at the proper time.\par
"Why, where is Amy's bottle of cologne?" she added, as the little flask\par
did not appear.\par
\par
"She took it out a minute ago, and went off with it to put a ribbon on\par
it, or some such notion," replied Jo, dancing about the room to take the\par
first stiffness off the new army-slippers.\par
\par
"How nice my handkerchiefs look, don't they? Hannah washed and ironed\par
them for me, and I marked them all myself," said Beth, looking proudly\par
at the somewhat uneven letters which had cost her such labor.\par
\par
"Bless the child! she's gone and put 'Mother' on them instead of 'M.\par
March.' How funny!" cried Jo, taking up one.\par
\par
"Isn't it right? I thought it was better to do it so, because Meg's\par
initials are 'M. M.,' and I don't want any one to use these but Marmee,"\par
said Beth, looking troubled.\par
\par
"It's all right, dear, and a very pretty idea,--quite sensible, too, for\par
no one can ever mistake now. It will please her very much, I know," said\par
Meg, with a frown for Jo and a smile for Beth.\par
\par
"There's mother. Hide the basket, quick!" cried Jo, as a door slammed,\par
and steps sounded in the hall.\par
\par
Amy came in hastily, and looked rather abashed when she saw her sisters\par
all waiting for her.\par
\par
"Where have you been, and what are you hiding behind you?" asked Meg,\par
surprised to see, by her hood and cloak, that lazy Amy had been out so\par
early.\par
\par
"Don't laugh at me, Jo! I didn't mean any one should know till the time\par
came. I only meant to change the little bottle for a big one, and I gave\par
_all_ my money to get it, and I'm truly trying not to be selfish any\par
more."\par
\par
As she spoke, Amy showed the handsome flask which replaced the cheap\par
one; and looked so earnest and humble in her little effort to forget\par
herself that Meg hugged her on the spot, and Jo pronounced her "a\par
trump," while Beth ran to the window, and picked her finest rose to\par
ornament the stately bottle.\par
\par
"You see I felt ashamed of my present, after reading and talking about\par
being good this morning, so I ran round the corner and changed it the\par
minute I was up: and I'm _so_ glad, for mine is the handsomest now."\par
\par
Another bang of the street-door sent the basket under the sofa, and the\par
girls to the table, eager for breakfast.\par
\par
"Merry Christmas, Marmee! Many of them! Thank you for our books; we read\par
some, and mean to every day," they cried, in chorus.\par
\par
"Merry Christmas, little daughters! I'm glad you began at once, and hope\par
you will keep on. But I want to say one word before we sit down. Not far\par
away from here lies a poor woman with a little new-born baby. Six\par
children are huddled into one bed to keep from freezing, for they have\par
no fire. There is nothing to eat over there; and the oldest boy came to\par
tell me they were suffering hunger and cold. My girls, will you give\par
them your breakfast as a Christmas present?"\par
\par
They were all unusually hungry, having waited nearly an hour, and for a\par
minute no one spoke; only a minute, for Jo exclaimed impetuously,--\par
\par
"I'm so glad you came before we began!"\par
\par
"May I go and help carry the things to the poor little children?" asked\par
Beth, eagerly.\par
\par
"_I_ shall take the cream and the muffins," added Amy, heroically giving\par
up the articles she most liked.\par
\par
Meg was already covering the buckwheats, and piling the bread into one\par
big plate.\par
\par
"I thought you'd do it," said Mrs. March, smiling as if satisfied. "You\par
shall all go and help me, and when we come back we will have bread and\par
milk for breakfast, and make it up at dinner-time."\par
\par
They were soon ready, and the procession set out. Fortunately it was\par
early, and they went through back streets, so few people saw them, and\par
no one laughed at the queer party.\par
\par
                 [Illustration: The procession set out]\par
\par
A poor, bare, miserable room it was, with broken windows, no fire,\par
ragged bed-clothes, a sick mother, wailing baby, and a group of pale,\par
hungry children cuddled under one old quilt, trying to keep warm.\par
\par
How the big eyes stared and the blue lips smiled as the girls went in!\par
\par
"Ach, mein Gott! it is good angels come to us!" said the poor woman,\par
crying for joy.\par
\par
"Funny angels in hoods and mittens," said Jo, and set them laughing.\par
\par
In a few minutes it really did seem as if kind spirits had been at work\par
there. Hannah, who had carried wood, made a fire, and stopped up the\par
broken panes with old hats and her own cloak. Mrs. March gave the mother\par
tea and gruel, and comforted her with promises of help, while she\par
dressed the little baby as tenderly as if it had been her own. The\par
girls, meantime, spread the table, set the children round the fire, and\par
fed them like so many hungry birds,--laughing, talking, and trying to\par
understand the funny broken English.\par
\par
"Das ist gut!" "Die Engel-kinder!" cried the poor things, as they ate,\par
and warmed their purple hands at the comfortable blaze.\par
\par
The girls had never been called angel children before, and thought it\par
very agreeable, especially Jo, who had been considered a "Sancho" ever\par
since she was born. That was a very happy breakfast, though they didn't\par
get any of it; and when they went away, leaving comfort behind, I think\par
there were not in all the city four merrier people than the hungry\par
little girls who gave away their breakfasts and contented themselves\par
with bread and milk on Christmas morning.\par
\par
"That's loving our neighbor better than ourselves, and I like it," said\par
Meg, as they set out their presents, while their mother was upstairs\par
collecting clothes for the poor Hummels.\par
\par
Not a very splendid show, but there was a great deal of love done up in\par
the few little bundles; and the tall vase of red roses, white\par
chrysanthemums, and trailing vines, which stood in the middle, gave\par
quite an elegant air to the table.\par
\par
"She's coming! Strike up, Beth! Open the door, Amy! Three cheers for\par
Marmee!" cried Jo, prancing about, while Meg went to conduct mother to\par
the seat of honor.\par
\par
Beth played her gayest march, Amy threw open the door, and Meg enacted\par
escort with great dignity. Mrs. March was both surprised and touched;\par
and smiled with her eyes full as she examined her presents, and read the\par
little notes which accompanied them. The slippers went on at once, a new\par
handkerchief was slipped into her pocket, well scented with Amy's\par
cologne, the rose was fastened in her bosom, and the nice gloves were\par
pronounced a "perfect fit."\par
\par
There was a good deal of laughing and kissing and explaining, in the\par
simple, loving fashion which makes these home-festivals so pleasant at\par
the time, so sweet to remember long afterward, and then all fell to\par
work.\par
\par
The morning charities and ceremonies took so much time that the rest of\par
the day was devoted to preparations for the evening festivities. Being\par
still too young to go often to the theatre, and not rich enough to\par
afford any great outlay for private performances, the girls put their\par
wits to work, and--necessity being the mother of invention,--made\par
whatever they needed. Very clever were some of their\par
productions,--pasteboard guitars, antique lamps made of old-fashioned\par
butter-boats covered with silver paper, gorgeous robes of old cotton,\par
glittering with tin spangles from a pickle factory, and armor covered\par
with the same useful diamond-shaped bits, left in sheets when the lids\par
of tin preserve-pots were cut out. The furniture was used to being\par
turned topsy-turvy, and the big chamber was the scene of many innocent\par
revels.\par
\par
No gentlemen were admitted; so Jo played male parts to her heart's\par
content, and took immense satisfaction in a pair of russet-leather boots\par
given her by a friend, who knew a lady who knew an actor. These boots,\par
an old foil, and a slashed doublet once used by an artist for some\par
picture, were Jo's chief treasures, and appeared on all occasions. The\par
smallness of the company made it necessary for the two principal actors\par
to take several parts apiece; and they certainly deserved some credit\par
for the hard work they did in learning three or four different parts,\par
whisking in and out of various costumes, and managing the stage besides.\par
It was excellent drill for their memories, a harmless amusement, and\par
employed many hours which otherwise would have been idle, lonely, or\par
spent in less profitable society.\par
\par
On Christmas night, a dozen girls piled on to the bed which was the\par
dress-circle, and sat before the blue and yellow chintz curtains in a\par
most flattering state of expectancy. There was a good deal of rustling\par
and whispering behind the curtain, a trifle of lamp-smoke, and an\par
occasional giggle from Amy, who was apt to get hysterical in the\par
excitement of the moment. Presently a bell sounded, the curtains flew\par
apart, and the Operatic Tragedy began.\par
\par
"A gloomy wood," according to the one play-bill, was represented by a\par
few shrubs in pots, green baize on the floor, and a cave in the\par
distance. This cave was made with a clothes-horse for a roof, bureaus\par
for walls; and in it was a small furnace in full blast, with a black pot\par
on it, and an old witch bending over it. The stage was dark, and the\par
glow of the furnace had a fine effect, especially as real steam issued\par
from the kettle when the witch took off the cover. A moment was allowed\par
for the first thrill to subside; then Hugo, the villain, stalked in with\par
a clanking sword at his side, a slouched hat, black beard, mysterious\par
cloak, and the boots. After pacing to and fro in much agitation, he\par
struck his forehead, and burst out in a wild strain, singing of his\par
hatred to Roderigo, his love for Zara, and his pleasing resolution to\par
kill the one and win the other. The gruff tones of Hugo's voice, with an\par
occasional shout when his feelings overcame him, were very impressive,\par
and the audience applauded the moment he paused for breath. Bowing with\par
the air of one accustomed to public praise, he stole to the cavern, and\par
ordered Hagar to come forth with a commanding "What ho, minion! I need\par
thee!"\par
\par
[Illustration: Out came Meg with gray horse-hair hanging about her face]\par
\par
Out came Meg, with gray horse-hair hanging about her face, a red and\par
black robe, a staff, and cabalistic signs upon her cloak. Hugo demanded\par
a potion to make Zara adore him, and one to destroy Roderigo. Hagar, in\par
a fine dramatic melody, promised both, and proceeded to call up the\par
spirit who would bring the love philter:--\par
\par
        "Hither, hither, from thy home,\par
         Airy sprite, I bid thee come!\par
         Born of roses, fed on dew,\par
         Charms and potions canst thou brew?\par
         Bring me here, with elfin speed,\par
         The fragrant philter which I need;\par
         Make it sweet and swift and strong,\par
         Spirit, answer now my song!"\par
\par
            [Illustration: A little figure in cloudy white]\par
\par
A soft strain of music sounded, and then at the back of the cave\par
appeared a little figure in cloudy white, with glittering wings, golden\par
hair, and a garland of roses on its head. Waving a wand, it sang,--\par
\par
         "Hither I come,\par
          From my airy home,\par
        Afar in the silver moon.\par
          Take the magic spell,\par
          And use it well,\par
        Or its power will vanish soon!"\par
\par
And, dropping a small, gilded bottle at the witch's feet, the spirit\par
vanished. Another chant from Hagar produced another apparition,--not a\par
lovely one; for, with a bang, an ugly black imp appeared, and, having\par
croaked a reply, tossed a dark bottle at Hugo, and disappeared with a\par
mocking laugh. Having warbled his thanks and put the potions in his\par
boots, Hugo departed; and Hagar informed the audience that, as he had\par
killed a few of her friends in times past, she has cursed him, and\par
intends to thwart his plans, and be revenged on him. Then the curtain\par
fell, and the audience reposed and ate candy while discussing the merits\par
of the play.\par
\par
A good deal of hammering went on before the curtain rose again; but when\par
it became evident what a masterpiece of stage-carpentering had been got\par
up, no one murmured at the delay. It was truly superb! A tower rose to\par
the ceiling; half-way up appeared a window, with a lamp burning at it,\par
and behind the white curtain appeared Zara in a lovely blue and silver\par
dress, waiting for Roderigo. He came in gorgeous array, with plumed cap,\par
red cloak, chestnut love-locks, a guitar, and the boots, of course.\par
Kneeling at the foot of the tower, he sang a serenade in melting tones.\par
Zara replied, and, after a musical dialogue, consented to fly. Then came\par
the grand effect of the play. Roderigo produced a rope-ladder, with five\par
steps to it, threw up one end, and invited Zara to descend. Timidly she\par
crept from her lattice, put her hand on Roderigo's shoulder, and was\par
about to leap gracefully down, when, "Alas! alas for Zara!" she forgot\par
her train,--it caught in the window; the tower tottered, leaned forward,\par
fell with a crash, and buried the unhappy lovers in the ruins!\par
\par
A universal shriek arose as the russet boots waved wildly from the\par
wreck, and a golden head emerged, exclaiming, "I told you so! I told you\par
so!" With wonderful presence of mind, Don Pedro, the cruel sire, rushed\par
in, dragged out his daughter, with a hasty aside,--\par
\par
"Don't laugh! Act as if it was all right!"--and, ordering Roderigo up,\par
banished him from the kingdom with wrath and scorn. Though decidedly\par
shaken by the fall of the tower upon him, Roderigo defied the old\par
gentleman, and refused to stir. This dauntless example fired Zara: she\par
also defied her sire, and he ordered them both to the deepest dungeons\par
of the castle. A stout little retainer came in with chains, and led them\par
away, looking very much frightened, and evidently forgetting the speech\par
he ought to have made.\par
\par
Act third was the castle hall; and here Hagar appeared, having come to\par
free the lovers and finish Hugo. She hears him coming, and hides; sees\par
him put the potions into two cups of wine, and bid the timid little\par
servant "Bear them to the captives in their cells, and tell them I shall\par
come anon." The servant takes Hugo aside to tell him something, and\par
Hagar changes the cups for two others which are harmless. Ferdinando,\par
the "minion," carries them away, and Hagar puts back the cup which holds\par
the poison meant for Roderigo. Hugo, getting thirsty after a long\par
warble, drinks it, loses his wits, and, after a good deal of clutching\par
and stamping, falls flat and dies; while Hagar informs him what she has\par
done in a song of exquisite power and melody.\par
\par
This was a truly thrilling scene, though some persons might have thought\par
that the sudden tumbling down of a quantity of long hair rather marred\par
the effect of the villain's death. He was called before the curtain, and\par
with great propriety appeared, leading Hagar, whose singing was\par
considered more wonderful than all the rest of the performance put\par
together.\par
\par
Act fourth displayed the despairing Roderigo on the point of stabbing\par
himself, because he has been told that Zara has deserted him. Just as\par
the dagger is at his heart, a lovely song is sung under his window,\par
informing him that Zara is true, but in danger, and he can save her, if\par
he will. A key is thrown in, which unlocks the door, and in a spasm of\par
rapture he tears off his chains, and rushes away to find and rescue his\par
lady-love.\par
\par
Act fifth opened with a stormy scene between Zara and Don Pedro. He\par
wishes her to go into a convent, but she won't hear of it; and, after a\par
touching appeal, is about to faint, when Roderigo dashes in and demands\par
her hand. Don Pedro refuses, because he is not rich. They shout and\par
gesticulate tremendously, but cannot agree, and Roderigo is about to\par
bear away the exhausted Zara, when the timid servant enters with a\par
letter and a bag from Hagar, who has mysteriously disappeared. The\par
latter informs the party that she bequeaths untold wealth to the young\par
pair, and an awful doom to Don Pedro, if he doesn't make them happy. The\par
bag is opened, and several quarts of tin money shower down upon the\par
stage, till it is quite glorified with the glitter. This entirely\par
softens the "stern sire": he consents without a murmur, all join in a\par
joyful chorus, and the curtain falls upon the lovers kneeling to receive\par
Don Pedro's blessing in attitudes of the most romantic grace.\par
\par
  [Illustration: The lovers kneeling to receive Don Pedro's blessing]\par
\par
Tumultuous applause followed, but received an unexpected check; for the\par
cot-bed, on which the "dress-circle" was built, suddenly shut up, and\par
extinguished the enthusiastic audience. Roderigo and Don Pedro flew to\par
the rescue, and all were taken out unhurt, though many were speechless\par
with laughter. The excitement had hardly subsided, when Hannah appeared,\par
with "Mrs. March's compliments, and would the ladies walk down to\par
supper."\par
\par
This was a surprise, even to the actors; and, when they saw the table,\par
they looked at one another in rapturous amazement. It was like Marmee to\par
get up a little treat for them; but anything so fine as this was\par
unheard-of since the departed days of plenty. There was\par
ice-cream,--actually two dishes of it, pink and white,--and cake and\par
fruit and distracting French bonbons, and, in the middle of the table,\par
four great bouquets of hot-house flowers!\par
\par
It quite took their breath away; and they stared first at the table and\par
then at their mother, who looked as if she enjoyed it immensely.\par
\par
"Is it fairies?" asked Amy,\par
\par
"It's Santa Claus," said Beth.\par
\par
"Mother did it"; and Meg smiled her sweetest, in spite of her gray beard\par
and white eyebrows.\par
\par
"Aunt March had a good fit, and sent the supper," cried Jo, with a\par
sudden inspiration.\par
\par
"All wrong. Old Mr. Laurence sent it," replied Mrs. March.\par
\par
"The Laurence boy's grandfather! What in the world put such a thing into\par
his head? We don't know him!" exclaimed Meg.\par
\par
"Hannah told one of his servants about your breakfast party. He is an\par
odd old gentleman, but that pleased him. He knew my father, years ago;\par
and he sent me a polite note this afternoon, saying he hoped I would\par
allow him to express his friendly feeling toward my children by sending\par
them a few trifles in honor of the day. I could not refuse; and so you\par
have a little feast at night to make up for the bread-and-milk\par
breakfast."\par
\par
"That boy put it into his head, I know he did! He's a capital fellow,\par
and I wish we could get acquainted. He looks as if he'd like to know us;\par
but he's bashful, and Meg is so prim she won't let me speak to him when\par
we pass," said Jo, as the plates went round, and the ice began to melt\par
out of sight, with "Ohs!" and "Ahs!" of satisfaction.\par
\par
"You mean the people who live in the big house next door, don't you?"\par
asked one of the girls. "My mother knows old Mr. Laurence; but says he's\par
very proud, and doesn't like to mix with his neighbors. He keeps his\par
grandson shut up, when he isn't riding or walking with his tutor, and\par
makes him study very hard. We invited him to our party, but he didn't\par
come. Mother says he's very nice, though he never speaks to us girls."\par
\par
"Our cat ran away once, and he brought her back, and we talked over the\par
fence, and were getting on capitally,--all about cricket, and so\par
on,--when he saw Meg coming, and walked off. I mean to know him some\par
day; for he needs fun, I'm sure he does," said Jo decidedly.\par
\par
                [Illustration: We talked over the fence]\par
\par
"I like his manners, and he looks like a little gentleman; so I've no\par
objection to your knowing him, if a proper opportunity comes. He brought\par
the flowers himself; and I should have asked him in, if I had been sure\par
what was going on upstairs. He looked so wistful as he went away,\par
hearing the frolic, and evidently having none of his own."\par
\par
"It's a mercy you didn't, mother!" laughed Jo, looking at her boots.\par
"But we'll have another play, some time, that he _can_ see. Perhaps\par
he'll help act; wouldn't that be jolly?"\par
\par
"I never had such a fine bouquet before! How pretty it is!" And Meg\par
examined her flowers with great interest.\par
\par
"They _are_ lovely! But Beth's roses are sweeter to me," said Mrs.\par
March, smelling the half-dead posy in her belt.\par
\par
Beth nestled up to her, and whispered softly, "I wish I could send my\par
bunch to father. I'm afraid he isn't having such a merry Christmas as we\par
are."\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
  [Illustration: Eating apples and crying over the "Heir of Redclyffe"]\par
\par
                                  III.\par
\par
                           THE LAURENCE BOY.\par
\par
\par
"Jo! Jo! where are you?" cried Meg, at the foot of the garret stairs.\par
\par
"Here!" answered a husky voice from above; and, running up, Meg found\par
her sister eating apples and crying over the "Heir of Redclyffe,"\par
wrapped up in a comforter on an old three-legged sofa by the sunny\par
window. This was Jo's favorite refuge; and here she loved to retire with\par
half a dozen russets and a nice book, to enjoy the quiet and the society\par
of a pet rat who lived near by, and didn't mind her a particle. As Meg\par
appeared, Scrabble whisked into his hole. Jo shook the tears off her\par
cheeks, and waited to hear the news.\par
\par
"Such fun! only see! a regular note of invitation from Mrs. Gardiner for\par
to-morrow night!" cried Meg, waving the precious paper, and then\par
proceeding to read it, with girlish delight.\par
\par
"'Mrs. Gardiner would be happy to see Miss March and Miss Josephine at a\par
little dance on New-Year's Eve.' Marmee is willing we should go; now\par
what _shall_ we wear?"\par
\par
"What's the use of asking that, when you know we shall wear our poplins,\par
because we haven't got anything else?" answered Jo, with her mouth full.\par
\par
"If I only had a silk!" sighed Meg. "Mother says I may when I'm\par
eighteen, perhaps; but two years is an everlasting time to wait."\par
\par
"I'm sure our pops look like silk, and they are nice enough for us.\par
Yours is as good as new, but I forgot the burn and the tear in mine.\par
Whatever shall I do? the burn shows badly, and I can't take any out."\par
\par
"You must sit still all you can, and keep your back out of sight; the\par
front is all right. I shall have a new ribbon for my hair, and Marmee\par
will lend me her little pearl pin, and my new slippers are lovely, and\par
my gloves will do, though they aren't as nice as I'd like."\par
\par
"Mine are spoilt with lemonade, and I can't get any new ones, so I shall\par
have to go without," said Jo, who never troubled herself much about\par
dress.\par
\par
"You _must_ have gloves, or I won't go," cried Meg decidedly. "Gloves\par
are more important than anything else; you can't dance without them, and\par
if you don't I should be _so_ mortified."\par
\par
"Then I'll stay still. I don't care much for company dancing; it's no\par
fun to go sailing round; I like to fly about and cut capers."\par
\par
"You can't ask mother for new ones, they are so expensive, and you are\par
so careless. She said, when you spoilt the others, that she shouldn't\par
get you any more this winter. Can't you make them do?" asked Meg\par
anxiously.\par
\par
"I can hold them crumpled up in my hand, so no one will know how stained\par
they are; that's all I can do. No! I'll tell you how we can manage--each\par
wear one good one and carry a bad one; don't you see?"\par
\par
"Your hands are bigger than mine, and you will stretch my glove\par
dreadfully," began Meg, whose gloves were a tender point with her.\par
\par
"Then I'll go without. I don't care what people say!" cried Jo, taking\par
up her book.\par
\par
"You may have it, you may! only don't stain it, and do behave nicely.\par
Don't put your hands behind you, or stare, or say 'Christopher\par
Columbus!' will you?"\par
\par
"Don't worry about me; I'll be as prim as I can, and not get into any\par
scrapes, if I can help it. Now go and answer your note, and let me\par
finish this splendid story."\par
\par
So Meg went away to "accept with thanks," look over her dress, and sing\par
blithely as she did up her one real lace frill; while Jo finished her\par
story, her four apples, and had a game of romps with Scrabble.\par
\par
On New-Year's Eve the parlor was deserted, for the two younger girls\par
played dressing-maids, and the two elder were absorbed in the\par
all-important business of "getting ready for the party." Simple as the\par
toilets were, there was a great deal of running up and down, laughing\par
and talking, and at one time a strong smell of burnt hair pervaded the\par
house. Meg wanted a few curls about her face, and Jo undertook to pinch\par
the papered locks with a pair of hot tongs.\par
\par
        [Illustration: Jo undertook to pinch the papered locks]\par
\par
"Ought they to smoke like that?" asked Beth, from her perch on the bed.\par
\par
"It's the dampness drying," replied Jo.\par
\par
"What a queer smell! it's like burnt feathers," observed Amy, smoothing\par
her own pretty curls with a superior air.\par
\par
"There, now I'll take off the papers and you'll see a cloud of little\par
ringlets," said Jo, putting down the tongs.\par
\par
She did take off the papers, but no cloud of ringlets appeared, for the\par
hair came with the papers, and the horrified hair-dresser laid a row of\par
little scorched bundles on the bureau before her victim.\par
\par
"Oh, oh, oh! what _have_ you done? I'm spoilt! I can't go! My hair, oh,\par
my hair!" wailed Meg, looking with despair at the uneven frizzle on her\par
forehead.\par
\par
"Just my luck! you shouldn't have asked me to do it; I always spoil\par
everything. I'm so sorry, but the tongs were too hot, and so I've made a\par
mess," groaned poor Jo, regarding the black pancakes with tears of\par
regret.\par
\par
"It isn't spoilt; just frizzle it, and tie your ribbon so the ends come\par
on your forehead a bit, and it will look like the last fashion. I've\par
seen many girls do it so," said Amy consolingly.\par
\par
"Serves me right for trying to be fine. I wish I'd let my hair alone,"\par
cried Meg petulantly.\par
\par
"So do I, it was so smooth and pretty. But it will soon grow out again,"\par
said Beth, coming to kiss and comfort the shorn sheep.\par
\par
After various lesser mishaps, Meg was finished at last, and by the\par
united exertions of the family Jo's hair was got up and her dress on.\par
They looked very well in their simple suits,--Meg in silvery drab, with\par
a blue velvet snood, lace frills, and the pearl pin; Jo in maroon, with\par
a stiff, gentlemanly linen collar, and a white chrysanthemum or two for\par
her only ornament. Each put on one nice light glove, and carried one\par
soiled one, and all pronounced the effect "quite easy and fine." Meg's\par
high-heeled slippers were very tight, and hurt her, though she would not\par
own it, and Jo's nineteen hair-pins all seemed stuck straight into her\par
head, which was not exactly comfortable; but, dear me, let us be elegant\par
or die!\par
\par
"Have a good time, dearies!" said Mrs. March, as the sisters went\par
daintily down the walk. "Don't eat much supper, and come away at eleven,\par
when I send Hannah for you." As the gate clashed behind them, a voice\par
cried from a window,--\par
\par
"Girls, girls! _have_ you both got nice pocket-handkerchiefs?"\par
\par
"Yes, yes, spandy nice, and Meg has cologne on hers," cried Jo, adding,\par
with a laugh, as they went on, "I do believe Marmee would ask that if we\par
were all running away from an earthquake."\par
\par
"It is one of her aristocratic tastes, and quite proper, for a real lady\par
is always known by neat boots, gloves, and handkerchief," replied Meg,\par
who had a good many little "aristocratic tastes" of her own.\par
\par
"Now don't forget to keep the bad breadth out of sight, Jo. Is my sash\par
right? and does my hair look _very_ bad?" said Meg, as she turned from\par
the glass in Mrs. Gardiner's dressing-room, after a prolonged prink.\par
\par
"I know I shall forget. If you see me doing anything wrong, just remind\par
me by a wink, will you?" returned Jo, giving her collar a twitch and her\par
head a hasty brush.\par
\par
"No, winking isn't lady-like; I'll lift my eyebrows if anything is\par
wrong, and nod if you are all right. Now hold your shoulders straight,\par
and take short steps, and don't shake hands if you are introduced to any\par
one: it isn't the thing."\par
\par
"How _do_ you learn all the proper ways? I never can. Isn't that music\par
gay?"\par
\par
             [Illustration: Mrs. Gardiner greeted them]\par
\par
Down they went, feeling a trifle timid, for they seldom went to parties,\par
and, informal as this little gathering was, it was an event to them.\par
Mrs. Gardiner, a stately old lady, greeted them kindly, and handed them\par
over to the eldest of her six daughters. Meg knew Sallie, and was at her\par
ease very soon; but Jo, who didn't care much for girls or girlish\par
gossip, stood about, with her back carefully against the wall, and felt\par
as much out of place as a colt in a flower-garden. Half a dozen jovial\par
lads were talking about skates in another part of the room, and she\par
longed to go and join them, for skating was one of the joys of her life.\par
She telegraphed her wish to Meg, but the eyebrows went up so alarmingly\par
that she dared not stir. No one came to talk to her, and one by one the\par
group near her dwindled away, till she was left alone. She could not\par
roam about and amuse herself, for the burnt breadth would show, so she\par
stared at people rather forlornly till the dancing began. Meg was asked\par
at once, and the tight slippers tripped about so briskly that none would\par
have guessed the pain their wearer suffered smilingly. Jo saw a big\par
red-headed youth approaching her corner, and fearing he meant to engage\par
her, she slipped into a curtained recess, intending to peep and enjoy\par
herself in peace. Unfortunately, another bashful person had chosen the\par
same refuge; for, as the curtain fell behind her, she found herself face\par
to face with the "Laurence boy."\par
\par
           [Illustration: Face to face with the Laurence boy]\par
\par
"Dear me, I didn't know any one was here!" stammered Jo, preparing to\par
back out as speedily as she had bounced in.\par
\par
But the boy laughed, and said pleasantly, though he looked a little\par
startled,--\par
\par
"Don't mind me; stay, if you like."\par
\par
"Sha'n't I disturb you?"\par
\par
"Not a bit; I only came here because I don't know many people, and felt\par
rather strange at first, you know."\par
\par
"So did I. Don't go away, please, unless you'd rather."\par
\par
The boy sat down again and looked at his pumps, till Jo said, trying to\par
be polite and easy,--\par
\par
"I think I've had the pleasure of seeing you before; you live near us,\par
don't you?"\par
\par
"Next door"; and he looked up and laughed outright, for Jo's prim manner\par
was rather funny when he remembered how they had chatted about cricket\par
when he brought the cat home.\par
\par
That put Jo at her ease; and she laughed too, as she said, in her\par
heartiest way,--\par
\par
"We did have such a good time over your nice Christmas present."\par
\par
"Grandpa sent it."\par
\par
"But you put it into his head, didn't you, now?"\par
\par
"How is your cat, Miss March?" asked the boy, trying to look sober,\par
while his black eyes shone with fun.\par
\par
"Nicely, thank you, Mr. Laurence; but I am not Miss March, I'm only Jo,"\par
returned the young lady.\par
\par
"I'm not Mr. Laurence, I'm only Laurie."\par
\par
"Laurie Laurence,--what an odd name!"\par
\par
"My first name is Theodore, but I don't like it, for the fellows called\par
me Dora, so I made them say Laurie instead."\par
\par
"I hate my name, too--so sentimental! I wish every one would say Jo,\par
instead of Josephine. How did you make the boys stop calling you Dora?"\par
\par
"I thrashed 'em."\par
\par
"I can't thrash Aunt March, so I suppose I shall have to bear it"; and\par
Jo resigned herself with a sigh.\par
\par
"Don't you like to dance, Miss Jo?" asked Laurie, looking as if he\par
thought the name suited her.\par
\par
"I like it well enough if there is plenty of room, and every one is\par
lively. In a place like this I'm sure to upset something, tread on\par
people's toes, or do something dreadful, so I keep out of mischief, and\par
let Meg sail about. Don't you dance?"\par
\par
"Sometimes; you see I've been abroad a good many years, and haven't been\par
into company enough yet to know how you do things here."\par
\par
"Abroad!" cried Jo. "Oh, tell me about it! I love dearly to hear people\par
describe their travels."\par
\par
Laurie didn't seem to know where to begin; but Jo's eager questions soon\par
set him going, and he told her how he had been at school in Vevay, where\par
the boys never wore hats, and had a fleet of boats on the lake, and for\par
holiday fun went walking trips about Switzerland with their teachers.\par
\par
"Don't I wish I'd been there!" cried Jo. "Did you go to Paris?"\par
\par
"We spent last winter there."\par
\par
"Can you talk French?"\par
\par
"We were not allowed to speak any thing else at Vevay."\par
\par
"Do say some! I can read it, but can't pronounce."\par
\par
"Quel nom a cette jeune demoiselle en les pantoufles jolis?" said Laurie\par
good-naturedly.\par
\par
"How nicely you do it! Let me see,--you said, 'Who is the young lady in\par
the pretty slippers,' didn't you?"\par
\par
"Oui, mademoiselle."\par
\par
"It's my sister Margaret, and you knew it was! Do you think she is\par
pretty?"\par
\par
"Yes; she makes me think of the German girls, she looks so fresh and\par
quiet, and dances like a lady."\par
\par
Jo quite glowed with pleasure at this boyish praise of her sister, and\par
stored it up to repeat to Meg. Both peeped and criticised and chatted,\par
till they felt like old acquaintances. Laurie's bashfulness soon wore\par
off; for Jo's gentlemanly demeanor amused and set him at his ease, and\par
Jo was her merry self again, because her dress was forgotten, and nobody\par
lifted their eyebrows at her. She liked the "Laurence boy" better than\par
ever, and took several good looks at him, so that she might describe him\par
to the girls; for they had no brothers, very few male cousins, and boys\par
were almost unknown creatures to them.\par
\par
"Curly black hair; brown skin; big, black eyes; handsome nose; fine\par
teeth; small hands and feet; taller than I am; very polite, for a boy,\par
and altogether jolly. Wonder how old he is?"\par
\par
It was on the tip of Jo's tongue to ask; but she checked herself in\par
time, and, with unusual tact, tried to find out in a roundabout way.\par
\par
"I suppose you are going to college soon? I see you pegging away at your\par
books,--no, I mean studying hard"; and Jo blushed at the dreadful\par
"pegging" which had escaped her.\par
\par
Laurie smiled, but didn't seem shocked, and answered, with a shrug,--\par
\par
"Not for a year or two; I won't go before seventeen, anyway."\par
\par
"Aren't you but fifteen?" asked Jo, looking at the tall lad, whom she\par
had imagined seventeen already.\par
\par
"Sixteen, next month."\par
\par
"How I wish I was going to college! You don't look as if you liked it."\par
\par
"I hate it! Nothing but grinding or skylarking. And I don't like the way\par
fellows do either, in this country."\par
\par
"What do you like?"\par
\par
"To live in Italy, and to enjoy myself in my own way."\par
\par
Jo wanted very much to ask what his own way was; but his black brows\par
looked rather threatening as he knit them; so she changed the subject by\par
saying, as her foot kept time, "That's a splendid polka! Why don't you\par
go and try it?"\par
\par
"If you will come too," he answered, with a gallant little bow.\par
\par
"I can't; for I told Meg I wouldn't, because--" There Jo stopped, and\par
looked undecided whether to tell or to laugh.\par
\par
"Because what?" asked Laurie curiously.\par
\par
"You won't tell?"\par
\par
"Never!"\par
\par
"Well, I have a bad trick of standing before the fire, and so I burn my\par
frocks, and I scorched this one; and, though it's nicely mended, it\par
shows, and Meg told me to keep still, so no one would see it. You may\par
laugh, if you want to; it is funny, I know."\par
\par
But Laurie didn't laugh; he only looked down a minute, and the\par
expression of his face puzzled Jo, when he said very gently,--\par
\par
"Never mind that; I'll tell you how we can manage: there's a long hall\par
out there, and we can dance grandly, and no one will see us. Please\par
come?"\par
\par
Jo thanked him, and gladly went, wishing she had two neat gloves, when\par
she saw the nice, pearl-colored ones her partner wore. The hall was\par
empty, and they had a grand polka; for Laurie danced well, and taught\par
her the German step, which delighted Jo, being full of swing and spring.\par
When the music stopped, they sat down on the stairs to get their breath;\par
and Laurie was in the midst of an account of a students' festival at\par
Heidelberg, when Meg appeared in search of her sister. She beckoned, and\par
Jo reluctantly followed her into a side-room, where she found her on a\par
sofa, holding her foot, and looking pale.\par
\par
              [Illustration: They sat down on the stairs]\par
\par
"I've sprained my ankle. That stupid high heel turned, and gave me a sad\par
wrench. It aches so, I can hardly stand, and I don't know how I'm ever\par
going to get home," she said, rocking to and fro in pain.\par
\par
"I knew you'd hurt your feet with those silly shoes. I'm sorry. But I\par
don't see what you can do, except get a carriage, or stay here all\par
night," answered Jo, softly rubbing the poor ankle as she spoke.\par
\par
"I can't have a carriage, without its costing ever so much. I dare say I\par
can't get one at all; for most people come in their own, and it's a long\par
way to the stable, and no one to send."\par
\par
"I'll go."\par
\par
"No, indeed! It's past nine, and dark as Egypt. I can't stop here, for\par
the house is full. Sallie has some girls staying with her. I'll rest\par
till Hannah comes, and then do the best I can."\par
\par
"I'll ask Laurie; he will go," said Jo, looking relieved as the idea\par
occurred to her.\par
\par
"Mercy, no! Don't ask or tell any one. Get me my rubbers, and put these\par
slippers with our things. I can't dance any more; but as soon as supper\par
is over, watch for Hannah, and tell me the minute she comes."\par
\par
"They are going out to supper now. I'll stay with you; I'd rather."\par
\par
"No, dear, run along, and bring me some coffee. I'm so tired, I can't\par
stir!"\par
\par
So Meg reclined, with rubbers well hidden, and Jo went blundering away\par
to the dining-room, which she found after going into a china-closet, and\par
opening the door of a room where old Mr. Gardiner was taking a little\par
private refreshment. Making a dart at the table, she secured the coffee,\par
which she immediately spilt, thereby making the front of her dress as\par
bad as the back.\par
\par
"Oh, dear, what a blunderbuss I am!" exclaimed Jo, finishing Meg's glove\par
by scrubbing her gown with it.\par
\par
"Can I help you?" said a friendly voice; and there was Laurie, with a\par
full cup in one hand and a plate of ice in the other.\par
\par
"I was trying to get something for Meg, who is very tired, and some one\par
shook me; and here I am, in a nice state," answered Jo, glancing\par
dismally from the stained skirt to the coffee-colored glove.\par
\par
"Too bad! I was looking for some one to give this to. May I take it to\par
your sister?"\par
\par
"Oh, thank you! I'll show you where she is. I don't offer to take it\par
myself, for I should only get into another scrape if I did."\par
\par
Jo led the way; and, as if used to waiting on ladies, Laurie drew up a\par
little table, brought a second instalment of coffee and ice for Jo, and\par
was so obliging that even particular Meg pronounced him a "nice boy."\par
They had a merry time over the bonbons and mottoes, and were in the\par
midst of a quiet game of "Buzz," with two or three other young people\par
who had strayed in, when Hannah appeared. Meg forgot her foot, and rose\par
so quickly that she was forced to catch hold of Jo, with an exclamation\par
of pain.\par
\par
"Hush! Don't say anything," she whispered, adding aloud, "It's nothing.\par
I turned my foot a little, that's all"; and limped up-stairs to put her\par
things on.\par
\par
Hannah scolded, Meg cried, and Jo was at her wits' end, till she\par
decided to take things into her own hands. Slipping out, she ran down,\par
and, finding a servant, asked if he could get her a carriage. It\par
happened to be a hired waiter, who knew nothing about the neighborhood;\par
and Jo was looking round for help, when Laurie, who had heard what she\par
said, came up, and offered his grandfather's carriage, which had just\par
come for him, he said.\par
\par
"It's so early! You can't mean to go yet?" began Jo, looking relieved,\par
but hesitating to accept the offer.\par
\par
"I always go early,--I do, truly! Please let me take you home? It's all\par
on my way, you know, and it rains, they say."\par
\par
That settled it; and, telling him of Meg's mishap, Jo gratefully\par
accepted, and rushed up to bring down the rest of the party. Hannah\par
hated rain as much as a cat does; so she made no trouble, and they\par
rolled away in the luxurious close carriage, feeling very festive and\par
elegant. Laurie went on the box; so Meg could keep her foot up, and the\par
girls talked over their party in freedom.\par
\par
"I had a capital time. Did you?" asked Jo, rumpling up her hair, and\par
making herself comfortable.\par
\par
"Yes, till I hurt myself. Sallie's friend, Annie Moffat, took a fancy to\par
me, and asked me to come and spend a week with her, when Sallie does.\par
She is going in the spring, when the opera comes; and it will be\par
perfectly splendid, if mother only lets me go," answered Meg, cheering\par
up at the thought.\par
\par
"I saw you dancing with the red-headed man I ran away from. Was he\par
nice?"\par
\par
"Oh, very! His hair is auburn, not red; and he was very polite, and I\par
had a delicious redowa with him."\par
\par
"He looked like a grasshopper in a fit, when he did the new step. Laurie\par
and I couldn't help laughing. Did you hear us?"\par
\par
"No; but it was very rude. What _were_ you about all that time, hidden\par
away there?"\par
\par
Jo told her adventures, and, by the time she had finished, they were at\par
home. With many thanks, they said "Good night," and crept in, hoping to\par
disturb no one; but the instant their door creaked, two little\par
night-caps bobbed up, and two sleepy but eager voices cried out,--\par
\par
"Tell about the party! tell about the party!"\par
\par
With what Meg called "a great want of manners," Jo had saved some\par
bonbons for the little girls; and they soon subsided, after hearing the\par
most thrilling events of the evening.\par
\par
"I declare, it really seems like being a fine young lady, to come home\par
from the party in a carriage, and sit in my dressing-gown, with a maid\par
to wait on me," said Meg, as Jo bound up her foot with arnica, and\par
brushed her hair.\par
\par
"I don't believe fine young ladies enjoy themselves a bit more than we\par
do, in spite of our burnt hair, old gowns, one glove apiece, and tight\par
slippers that sprain our ankles when we are silly enough to wear them."\par
And I think Jo was quite right.\par
\par
                  [Illustration: Tell about the party]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
     [Illustration: The kitten stuck like a burr just out of reach]\par
\par
                                  IV.\par
\par
                                BURDENS.\par
\par
\par
"Oh dear, how hard it does seem to take up our packs and go on," sighed\par
Meg, the morning after the party; for, now the holidays were over, the\par
week of merry-making did not fit her for going on easily with the task\par
she never liked.\par
\par
"I wish it was Christmas or New-Year all the time; wouldn't it be fun?"\par
answered Jo, yawning dismally.\par
\par
"We shouldn't enjoy ourselves half so much as we do now. But it does\par
seem so nice to have little suppers and bouquets, and go to parties, and\par
drive home, and read and rest, and not work. It's like other people,\par
you know, and I always envy girls who do such things; I'm so fond of\par
luxury," said Meg, trying to decide which of two shabby gowns was the\par
least shabby.\par
\par
"Well, we can't have it, so don't let us grumble, but shoulder our\par
bundles and trudge along as cheerfully as Marmee does. I'm sure Aunt\par
March is a regular Old Man of the Sea to me, but I suppose when I've\par
learned to carry her without complaining, she will tumble off, or get so\par
light that I sha'n't mind her."\par
\par
This idea tickled Jo's fancy, and put her in good spirits; but Meg\par
didn't brighten, for her burden, consisting of four spoilt children,\par
seemed heavier than ever. She hadn't heart enough even to make herself\par
pretty, as usual, by putting on a blue neck-ribbon, and dressing her\par
hair in the most becoming way.\par
\par
"Where's the use of looking nice, when no one sees me but those cross\par
midgets, and no one cares whether I'm pretty or not?" she muttered,\par
shutting her drawer with a jerk. "I shall have to toil and moil all my\par
days, with only little bits of fun now and then, and get old and ugly\par
and sour, because I'm poor, and can't enjoy my life as other girls do.\par
It's a shame!"\par
\par
So Meg went down, wearing an injured look, and wasn't at all agreeable\par
at breakfast-time. Every one seemed rather out of sorts, and inclined to\par
croak. Beth had a headache, and lay on the sofa, trying to comfort\par
herself with the cat and three kittens; Amy was fretting because her\par
lessons were not learned, and she couldn't find her rubbers; Jo _would_\par
whistle and make a great racket getting ready; Mrs. March was very busy\par
trying to finish a letter, which must go at once; and Hannah had the\par
grumps, for being up late didn't suit her.\par
\par
"There never _was_ such a cross family!" cried Jo, losing her temper\par
when she had upset an inkstand, broken both boot-lacings, and sat down\par
upon her hat.\par
\par
"You're the crossest person in it!" returned Amy, washing out the sum,\par
that was all wrong, with the tears that had fallen on her slate.\par
\par
"Beth, if you don't keep these horrid cats down cellar I'll have them\par
drowned," exclaimed Meg angrily, as she tried to get rid of the kitten,\par
which had scrambled up her back, and stuck like a burr just out of\par
reach.\par
\par
Jo laughed, Meg scolded, Beth implored, and Amy wailed, because she\par
couldn't remember how much nine times twelve was.\par
\par
"Girls, girls, do be quiet one minute! I _must_ get this off by the\par
early mail, and you drive me distracted with your worry," cried Mrs.\par
March, crossing out the third spoilt sentence in her letter.\par
\par
There was a momentary lull, broken by Hannah, who stalked in, laid two\par
hot turn-overs on the table, and stalked out again. These turn-overs\par
were an institution; and the girls called them "muffs," for they had no\par
others, and found the hot pies very comforting to their hands on cold\par
mornings. Hannah never forgot to make them, no matter how busy or grumpy\par
she might be, for the walk was long and bleak; the poor things got no\par
other lunch, and were seldom home before two.\par
\par
"Cuddle your cats, and get over your headache, Bethy. Good-by, Marmee;\par
we are a set of rascals this morning, but we'll come home regular\par
angels. Now then, Meg!" and Jo tramped away, feeling that the pilgrims\par
were not setting out as they ought to do.\par
\par
They always looked back before turning the corner, for their mother was\par
always at the window, to nod and smile, and wave her hand to them.\par
Somehow it seemed as if they couldn't have got through the day without\par
that; for, whatever their mood might be, the last glimpse of that\par
motherly face was sure to affect them like sunshine.\par
\par
"If Marmee shook her fist instead of kissing her hand to us, it would\par
serve us right, for more ungrateful wretches than we are were never\par
seen," cried Jo, taking a remorseful satisfaction in the snowy walk and\par
bitter wind.\par
\par
"Don't use such dreadful expressions," said Meg, from the depths of the\par
vail in which she had shrouded herself like a nun sick of the world.\par
\par
"I like good strong words, that mean something," replied Jo, catching\par
her hat as it took a leap off her head, preparatory to flying away\par
altogether.\par
\par
"Call yourself any names you like; but _I_ am neither a rascal nor a\par
wretch, and I don't choose to be called so."\par
\par
"You're a blighted being, and decidedly cross to-day because you can't\par
sit in the lap of luxury all the time. Poor dear, just wait till I make\par
my fortune, and you shall revel in carriages and ice-cream and\par
high-heeled slippers and posies and red-headed boys to dance with."\par
\par
"How ridiculous you are, Jo!" but Meg laughed at the nonsense, and felt\par
better in spite of herself.\par
\par
"Lucky for you I am; for if I put on crushed airs, and tried to be\par
dismal, as you do, we should be in a nice state. Thank goodness, I can\par
always find something funny to keep me up. Don't croak any more, but\par
come home jolly, there's a dear."\par
\par
Jo gave her sister an encouraging pat on the shoulder as they parted for\par
the day, each going a different way, each hugging her little warm\par
turn-over, and each trying to be cheerful in spite of wintry weather,\par
hard work, and the unsatisfied desires of pleasure-loving youth.\par
\par
When Mr. March lost his property in trying to help an unfortunate\par
friend, the two oldest girls begged to be allowed to do something toward\par
their own support, at least. Believing that they could not begin too\par
early to cultivate energy, industry, and independence, their parents\par
consented, and both fell to work with the hearty good-will which in\par
spite of all obstacles, is sure to succeed at last. Margaret found a\par
place as nursery governess, and felt rich with her small salary. As she\par
said, she _was_ "fond of luxury," and her chief trouble was poverty. She\par
found it harder to bear than the others, because she could remember a\par
time when home was beautiful, life full of ease and pleasure, and want\par
of any kind unknown. She tried not to be envious or discontented, but it\par
was very natural that the young girl should long for pretty things, gay\par
friends, accomplishments, and a happy life. At the Kings' she daily saw\par
all she wanted, for the children's older sisters were just out, and Meg\par
caught frequent glimpses of dainty ball-dresses and bouquets, heard\par
lively gossip about theatres, concerts, sleighing parties, and\par
merry-makings of all kinds, and saw money lavished on trifles which\par
would have been so precious to her. Poor Meg seldom complained, but a\par
sense of injustice made her feel bitter toward every one sometimes, for\par
she had not yet learned to know how rich she was in the blessings which\par
alone can make life happy.\par
\par
Jo happened to suit Aunt March, who was lame, and needed an active\par
person to wait upon her. The childless old lady had offered to adopt one\par
of the girls when the troubles came, and was much offended because her\par
offer was declined. Other friends told the Marches that they had lost\par
all chance of being remembered in the rich old lady's will; but the\par
unworldly Marches only said,--\par
\par
"We can't give up our girls for a dozen fortunes. Rich or poor, we will\par
keep together and be happy in one another."\par
\par
The old lady wouldn't speak to them for a time, but happening to meet Jo\par
at a friend's, something in her comical face and blunt manners struck\par
the old lady's fancy, and she proposed to take her for a companion. This\par
did not suit Jo at all; but she accepted the place since nothing better\par
appeared, and, to every one's surprise, got on remarkably well with her\par
irascible relative. There was an occasional tempest, and once Jo had\par
marched home, declaring she couldn't bear it any longer; but Aunt March\par
always cleared up quickly, and sent for her back again with such urgency\par
that she could not refuse, for in her heart she rather liked the peppery\par
old lady.\par
\par
I suspect that the real attraction was a large library of fine books,\par
which was left to dust and spiders since Uncle March died. Jo remembered\par
the kind old gentleman, who used to let her build railroads and bridges\par
with his big dictionaries, tell her stories about the queer pictures in\par
his Latin books, and buy her cards of gingerbread whenever he met her in\par
the street. The dim, dusty room, with the busts staring down from the\par
tall book-cases, the cosy chairs, the globes, and, best of all, the\par
wilderness of books, in which she could wander where she liked, made the\par
library a region of bliss to her. The moment Aunt March took her nap, or\par
was busy with company, Jo hurried to this quiet place, and, curling\par
herself up in the easy-chair, devoured poetry, romance, history,\par
travels, and pictures, like a regular book-worm. But, like all\par
happiness, it did not last long; for as sure as she had just reached the\par
heart of the story, the sweetest verse of the song, or the most perilous\par
adventure of her traveller, a shrill voice called, "Josy-phine!\par
Josy-phine!" and she had to leave her paradise to wind yarn, wash the\par
poodle, or read Belsham's Essays by the hour together.\par
\par
          [Illustration: Curling herself up in the big chair]\par
\par
Jo's ambition was to do something very splendid; what it was she had no\par
idea, as yet, but left it for time to tell her; and, meanwhile, found\par
her greatest affliction in the fact that she couldn't read, run, and\par
ride as much as she liked. A quick temper, sharp tongue, and restless\par
spirit were always getting her into scrapes, and her life was a series\par
of ups and downs, which were both comic and pathetic. But the training\par
she received at Aunt March's was just what she needed; and the thought\par
that she was doing something to support herself made her happy, in spite\par
of the perpetual "Josy-phine!"\par
\par
Beth was too bashful to go to school; it had been tried, but she\par
suffered so much that it was given up, and she did her lessons at home,\par
with her father. Even when he went away, and her mother was called to\par
devote her skill and energy to Soldiers' Aid Societies, Beth went\par
faithfully on by herself, and did the best she could. She was a\par
housewifely little creature, and helped Hannah keep home neat and\par
comfortable for the workers, never thinking of any reward but to be\par
loved. Long, quiet days she spent, not lonely nor idle, for her little\par
world was peopled with imaginary friends, and she was by nature a busy\par
bee. There were six dolls to be taken up and dressed every morning, for\par
Beth was a child still, and loved her pets as well as ever. Not one\par
whole or handsome one among them; all were outcasts till Beth took them\par
in; for, when her sisters outgrew these idols, they passed to her,\par
because Amy would have nothing old or ugly. Beth cherished them all the\par
more tenderly for that very reason, and set up a hospital for infirm\par
dolls. No pins were ever stuck into their cotton vitals; no harsh words\par
or blows were ever given them; no neglect ever saddened the heart of the\par
most repulsive: but all were fed and clothed, nursed and caressed, with\par
an affection which never failed. One forlorn fragment of _dollanity_ had\par
belonged to Jo; and, having led a tempestuous life, was left a wreck in\par
the rag-bag, from which dreary poorhouse it was rescued by Beth, and\par
taken to her refuge. Having no top to its head, she tied on a neat\par
little cap, and, as both arms and legs were gone, she hid these\par
deficiencies by folding it in a blanket, and devoting her best bed to\par
this chronic invalid. If any one had known the care lavished on that\par
dolly, I think it would have touched their hearts, even while they\par
laughed. She brought it bits of bouquets; she read to it, took it out to\par
breathe the air, hidden under her coat; she sung it lullabys, and never\par
went to bed without kissing its dirty face, and whispering tenderly, "I\par
hope you'll have a good night, my poor dear."\par
\par
Beth had her troubles as well as the others; and not being an angel, but\par
a very human little girl, she often "wept a little weep," as Jo said,\par
because she couldn't take music lessons and have a fine piano. She loved\par
music so dearly, tried so hard to learn, and practised away so patiently\par
at the jingling old instrument, that it did seem as if some one (not to\par
hint Aunt March) ought to help her. Nobody did, however, and nobody saw\par
Beth wipe the tears off the yellow keys, that wouldn't keep in tune,\par
when she was all alone. She sang like a little lark about her work,\par
never was too tired to play for Marmee and the girls, and day after day\par
said hopefully to herself, "I know I'll get my music some time, if I'm\par
good."\par
\par
There are many Beths in the world, shy and quiet, sitting in corners\par
till needed, and living for others so cheerfully that no one sees the\par
sacrifices till the little cricket on the hearth stops chirping, and the\par
sweet, sunshiny presence vanishes, leaving silence and shadow behind.\par
\par
If anybody had asked Amy what the greatest trial of her life was, she\par
would have answered at once, "My nose." When she was a baby, Jo had\par
accidentally dropped her into the coal-hod, and Amy insisted that the\par
fall had ruined her nose forever. It was not big, nor red, like poor\par
"Petrea's"; it was only rather flat, and all the pinching in the world\par
could not give it an aristocratic point. No one minded it but herself,\par
and it was doing its best to grow, but Amy felt deeply the want of a\par
Grecian nose, and drew whole sheets of handsome ones to console herself.\par
\par
"Little Raphael," as her sisters called her, had a decided talent for\par
drawing, and was never so happy as when copying flowers, designing\par
fairies, or illustrating stories with queer specimens of art. Her\par
teachers complained that, instead of doing her sums, she covered her\par
slate with animals; the blank pages of her atlas were used to copy maps\par
on; and caricatures of the most ludicrous description came fluttering\par
out of all her books at unlucky moments. She got through her lessons as\par
well as she could, and managed to escape reprimands by being a model of\par
deportment. She was a great favorite with her mates, being\par
good-tempered, and possessing the happy art of pleasing without effort.\par
Her little airs and graces were much admired, so were her\par
accomplishments; for beside her drawing, she could play twelve tunes,\par
crochet, and read French without mispronouncing more than two thirds of\par
the words. She had a plaintive way of saying, "When papa was rich we did\par
so-and-so," which was very touching; and her long words were considered\par
"perfectly elegant" by the girls.\par
\par
Amy was in a fair way to be spoilt; for every one petted her, and her\par
small vanities and selfishnesses were growing nicely. One thing,\par
however, rather quenched the vanities; she had to wear her cousin's\par
clothes. Now Florence's mamma hadn't a particle of taste, and Amy\par
suffered deeply at having to wear a red instead of a blue bonnet,\par
unbecoming gowns, and fussy aprons that did not fit. Everything was\par
good, well made, and little worn; but Amy's artistic eyes were much\par
afflicted, especially this winter, when her school dress was a dull\par
purple, with yellow dots, and no trimming.\par
\par
"My only comfort," she said to Meg, with tears in her eyes, "is, that\par
mother don't take tucks in my dresses whenever I'm naughty, as Maria\par
Parks' mother does. My dear, it's really dreadful; for sometimes she is\par
so bad, her frock is up to her knees, and she can't come to school. When\par
I think of this _deggerredation_, I feel that I can bear even my flat\par
nose and purple gown, with yellow sky-rockets on it."\par
\par
Meg was Amy's confidant and monitor, and, by some strange attraction of\par
opposites, Jo was gentle Beth's. To Jo alone did the shy child tell her\par
thoughts; and over her big, harum-scarum sister, Beth unconsciously\par
exercised more influence than any one in the family. The two older girls\par
were a great deal to one another, but each took one of the younger into\par
her keeping, and watched over her in her own way; "playing mother" they\par
called it, and put their sisters in the places of discarded dolls, with\par
the maternal instinct of little women.\par
\par
"Has anybody got anything to tell? It's been such a dismal day I'm\par
really dying for some amusement," said Meg, as they sat sewing together\par
that evening.\par
\par
"I had a queer time with aunt to-day, and, as I got the best of it, I'll\par
tell you about it," began Jo, who dearly loved to tell stories. "I was\par
reading that everlasting Belsham, and droning away as I always do, for\par
aunt soon drops off, and then I take out some nice book, and read like\par
fury till she wakes up. I actually made myself sleepy; and, before she\par
began to nod, I gave such a gape that she asked me what I meant by\par
opening my mouth wide enough to take the whole book in at once.\par
\par
            [Illustration: Reading that everlasting Belsham]\par
\par
"'I wish I could, and be done with it,' said I, trying not to be saucy.\par
\par
"Then she gave me a long lecture on my sins, and told me to sit and\par
think them over while she just 'lost' herself for a moment. She never\par
finds herself very soon; so the minute her cap began to bob, like a\par
top-heavy dahlia, I whipped the 'Vicar of Wakefield' out of my pocket,\par
and read away, with one eye on him, and one on aunt. I'd just got to\par
where they all tumbled into the water, when I forgot, and laughed out\par
loud. Aunt woke up; and, being more good-natured after her nap, told me\par
to read a bit, and show what frivolous work I preferred to the worthy\par
and instructive Belsham. I did my very best, and she liked it, though\par
she only said,--\par
\par
"'I don't understand what it's all about. Go back and begin it, child.'\par
\par
"Back I went, and made the Primroses as interesting as ever I could.\par
Once I was wicked enough to stop in a thrilling place, and say meekly,\par
'I'm afraid it tires you, ma'am; sha'n't I stop now?'\par
\par
"She caught up her knitting, which had dropped out of her hands, gave me\par
a sharp look through her specs, and said, in her short way,--\par
\par
"'Finish the chapter, and don't be impertinent, miss.'"\par
\par
"Did she own she liked it?" asked Meg.\par
\par
"Oh, bless you, no! but she let old Belsham rest; and, when I ran back\par
after my gloves this afternoon, there she was, so hard at the Vicar that\par
she didn't hear me laugh as I danced a jig in the hall, because of the\par
good time coming. What a pleasant life she might have, if she only\par
chose. I don't envy her much, in spite of her money, for after all rich\par
people have about as many worries as poor ones, I think," added Jo.\par
\par
"That reminds me," said Meg, "that I've got something to tell. It isn't\par
funny, like Jo's story, but I thought about it a good deal as I came\par
home. At the Kings to-day I found everybody in a flurry, and one of the\par
children said that her oldest brother had done something dreadful, and\par
papa had sent him away. I heard Mrs. King crying and Mr. King talking\par
very loud, and Grace and Ellen turned away their faces when they passed\par
me, so I shouldn't see how red their eyes were. I didn't ask any\par
questions, of course; but I felt so sorry for them, and was rather glad\par
I hadn't any wild brothers to do wicked things and disgrace the family."\par
\par
"I think being disgraced in school is a great deal try_inger_ than\par
anything bad boys can do," said Amy, shaking her head, as if her\par
experience of life had been a deep one. "Susie Perkins came to school\par
to-day with a lovely red carnelian ring; I wanted it dreadfully, and\par
wished I was her with all my might. Well, she drew a picture of Mr.\par
Davis, with a monstrous nose and a hump, and the words, 'Young ladies,\par
my eye is upon you!' coming out of his mouth in a balloon thing. We were\par
laughing over it, when all of a sudden his eye _was_ on us, and he\par
ordered Susie to bring up her slate. She was _parry_lized with fright,\par
but she went, and oh, what _do_ you think he did? He took her by the\par
ear, the ear! just fancy how horrid!--and led her to the recitation\par
platform, and made her stand there half an hour, holding that slate so\par
every one could see."\par
\par
          [Illustration: He took her by the ear! by the ear!]\par
\par
"Didn't the girls laugh at the picture?" asked Jo, who relished the\par
scrape.\par
\par
"Laugh? Not one! They sat as still as mice; and Susie cried quarts, I\par
know she did. I didn't envy her then; for I felt that millions of\par
carnelian rings wouldn't have made me happy, after that. I never, never\par
should have got over such a agonizing mortification." And Amy went on\par
with her work, in the proud consciousness of virtue, and the successful\par
utterance of two long words in a breath.\par
\par
"I saw something that I liked this morning, and I meant to tell it at\par
dinner, but I forgot," said Beth, putting Jo's topsy-turvy basket in\par
order as she talked. "When I went to get some oysters for Hannah, Mr.\par
Laurence was in the fish-shop; but he didn't see me, for I kept behind a\par
barrel, and he was busy with Mr. Cutter, the fish-man. A poor woman came\par
in, with a pail and a mop, and asked Mr. Cutter if he would let her do\par
some scrubbing for a bit of fish, because she hadn't any dinner for her\par
children, and had been disappointed of a day's work. Mr. Cutter was in a\par
hurry, and said 'No,' rather crossly; so she was going away, looking\par
hungry and sorry, when Mr. Laurence hooked up a big fish with the\par
crooked end of his cane, and held it out to her. She was so glad and\par
surprised, she took it right in her arms, and thanked him over and over.\par
He told her to 'go along and cook it,' and she hurried off, so happy!\par
Wasn't it good of him? Oh, she did look so funny, hugging the big,\par
slippery fish, and hoping Mr. Laurence's bed in heaven would be 'aisy.'"\par
\par
           [Illustration: Mr. Laurence hooked up a big fish]\par
\par
When they had laughed at Beth's story, they asked their mother for one;\par
and, after a moment's thought, she said soberly,--\par
\par
"As I sat cutting out blue flannel jackets to-day, at the rooms, I felt\par
very anxious about father, and thought how lonely and helpless we should\par
be, if anything happened to him. It was not a wise thing to do; but I\par
kept on worrying, till an old man came in, with an order for some\par
clothes. He sat down near me, and I began to talk to him; for he looked\par
poor and tired and anxious.\par
\par
"'Have you sons in the army?' I asked; for the note he brought was not\par
to me.\par
\par
"'Yes, ma'am. I had four, but two were killed, one is a prisoner, and\par
I'm going to the other, who is very sick in a Washington hospital,' he\par
answered quietly.\par
\par
"'You have done a great deal for your country, sir,' I said, feeling\par
respect now, instead of pity.\par
\par
"'Not a mite more than I ought, ma'am. I'd go myself, if I was any use;\par
as I ain't, I give my boys, and give 'em free.'\par
\par
"He spoke so cheerfully, looked so sincere, and seemed so glad to give\par
his all, that I was ashamed of myself. I'd given one man, and thought it\par
too much, while he gave four, without grudging them. I had all my girls\par
to comfort me at home; and his last son was waiting, miles away, to say\par
'good by' to him, perhaps! I felt so rich, so happy, thinking of my\par
blessings, that I made him a nice bundle, gave him some money, and\par
thanked him heartily for the lesson he had taught me."\par
\par
"Tell another story, mother,--one with a moral to it, like this. I like\par
to think about them afterwards, if they are real, and not too preachy,"\par
said Jo, after a minute's silence.\par
\par
Mrs. March smiled, and began at once; for she had told stories to this\par
little audience for many years, and knew how to please them.\par
\par
"Once upon a time, there were four girls, who had enough to eat and\par
drink and wear, a good many comforts and pleasures, kind friends and\par
parents, who loved them dearly, and yet they were not contented." (Here\par
the listeners stole sly looks at one another, and began to sew\par
diligently.) "These girls were anxious to be good, and made many\par
excellent resolutions; but they did not keep them very well, and were\par
constantly saying, 'If we only had this,' or 'If we could only do that,'\par
quite forgetting how much they already had, and how many pleasant things\par
they actually could do. So they asked an old woman what spell they could\par
use to make them happy, and she said, 'When you feel discontented, think\par
over your blessings, and be grateful.'" (Here Jo looked up quickly, as\par
if about to speak, but changed her mind, seeing that the story was not\par
done yet.)\par
\par
"Being sensible girls, they decided to try her advice, and soon were\par
surprised to see how well off they were. One discovered that money\par
couldn't keep shame and sorrow out of rich people's houses; another\par
that, though she was poor, she was a great deal happier, with her youth,\par
health, and good spirits, than a certain fretful, feeble old lady, who\par
couldn't enjoy her comforts; a third that, disagreeable as it was to\par
help get dinner, it was harder still to have to go begging for it; and\par
the fourth, that even carnelian rings were not so valuable as good\par
behavior. So they agreed to stop complaining, to enjoy the blessings\par
already possessed, and try to deserve them, lest they should be taken\par
away entirely, instead of increased; and I believe they were never\par
disappointed, or sorry that they took the old woman's advice."\par
\par
"Now, Marmee, that is very cunning of you to turn our own stories\par
against us, and give us a sermon instead of a romance!" cried Meg.\par
\par
"I like that kind of sermon. It's the sort father used to tell us," said\par
Beth thoughtfully, putting the needles straight on Jo's cushion.\par
\par
"I don't complain near as much as the others do, and I shall be more\par
careful than ever now; for I've had warning from Susie's downfall," said\par
Amy morally.\par
\par
"We needed that lesson, and we won't forget it. If we do, you just say\par
to us, as old Chloe did in 'Uncle Tom,' 'Tink ob yer marcies, chillen!\par
tink ob yer marcies!'" added Jo, who could not, for the life of her,\par
help getting a morsel of fun out of the little sermon, though she took\par
it to heart as much as any of them.\par
\par
                     [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                   V.\par
\par
                           BEING NEIGHBORLY.\par
\par
\par
                    [Illustration: Being neighborly]\par
\par
"What in the world are you going to do now, Jo?" asked Meg, one snowy\par
afternoon, as her sister came tramping through the hall, in rubber\par
boots, old sack and hood, with a broom in one hand and a shovel in the\par
other.\par
\par
"Going out for exercise," answered Jo, with a mischievous twinkle in her\par
eyes.\par
\par
"I should think two long walks this morning would have been enough! It's\par
cold and dull out; and I advise you to stay, warm and dry, by the fire,\par
as I do," said Meg, with a shiver.\par
\par
"Never take advice! Can't keep still all day, and, not being a pussycat,\par
I don't like to doze by the fire. I like adventures, and I'm going to\par
find some."\par
\par
Meg went back to toast her feet and read "Ivanhoe"; and Jo began to dig\par
paths with great energy. The snow was light, and with her broom she soon\par
swept a path all round the garden, for Beth to walk in when the sun came\par
out; and the invalid dolls needed air. Now, the garden separated the\par
Marches' house from that of Mr. Laurence. Both stood in a suburb of the\par
city, which was still country-like, with groves and lawns, large\par
gardens, and quiet streets. A low hedge parted the two estates. On one\par
side was an old, brown house, looking rather bare and shabby, robbed of\par
the vines that in summer covered its walls, and the flowers which then\par
surrounded it. On the other side was a stately stone mansion, plainly\par
betokening every sort of comfort and luxury, from the big coach-house\par
and well-kept grounds to the conservatory and the glimpses of lovely\par
things one caught between the rich curtains. Yet it seemed a lonely,\par
lifeless sort of house; for no children frolicked on the lawn, no\par
motherly face ever smiled at the windows, and few people went in and\par
out, except the old gentleman and his grandson.\par
\par
To Jo's lively fancy, this fine house seemed a kind of enchanted palace,\par
full of splendors and delights, which no one enjoyed. She had long\par
wanted to behold these hidden glories, and to know the "Laurence boy,"\par
who looked as if he would like to be known, if he only knew how to\par
begin. Since the party, she had been more eager than ever, and had\par
planned many ways of making friends with him; but he had not been seen\par
lately, and Jo began to think he had gone away, when she one day spied a\par
brown face at an upper window, looking wistfully down into their garden,\par
where Beth and Amy were snow-balling one another.\par
\par
"That boy is suffering for society and fun," she said to herself. "His\par
grandpa does not know what's good for him, and keeps him shut up all\par
alone. He needs a party of jolly boys to play with, or somebody young\par
and lively. I've a great mind to go over and tell the old gentleman so!"\par
\par
The idea amused Jo, who liked to do daring things, and was always\par
scandalizing Meg by her queer performances. The plan of "going over" was\par
not forgotten; and when the snowy afternoon came, Jo resolved to try\par
what could be done. She saw Mr. Laurence drive off, and then sallied out\par
to dig her way down to the hedge, where she paused, and took a survey.\par
All quiet,--curtains down at the lower windows; servants out of sight,\par
and nothing human visible but a curly black head leaning on a thin hand\par
at the upper window.\par
\par
"There he is," thought Jo, "poor boy! all alone and sick this dismal\par
day. It's a shame! I'll toss up a snow-ball, and make him look out, and\par
then say a kind word to him."\par
\par
Up went a handful of soft snow, and the head turned at once, showing a\par
face which lost its listless look in a minute, as the big eyes\par
brightened and the mouth began to smile. Jo nodded and laughed, and\par
flourished her broom as she called out,--\par
\par
"How do you do? Are you sick?"\par
\par
                [Illustration: Laurie opened the window]\par
\par
Laurie opened the window, and croaked out as hoarsely as a raven,--\par
\par
"Better, thank you. I've had a bad cold, and been shut up a week."\par
\par
"I'm sorry. What do you amuse yourself with?"\par
\par
"Nothing; it's as dull as tombs up here."\par
\par
"Don't you read?"\par
\par
"Not much; they won't let me."\par
\par
"Can't somebody read to you?"\par
\par
"Grandpa does, sometimes; but my books don't interest him, and I hate to\par
ask Brooke all the time."\par
\par
"Have some one come and see you, then."\par
\par
"There isn't any one I'd like to see. Boys make such a row, and my head\par
is weak."\par
\par
"Isn't there some nice girl who'd read and amuse you? Girls are quiet,\par
and like to play nurse."\par
\par
"Don't know any."\par
\par
"You know us," began Jo, then laughed, and stopped.\par
\par
"So I do! Will you come, please?" cried Laurie.\par
\par
"I'm not quiet and nice; but I'll come, if mother will let me. I'll go\par
ask her. Shut that window, like a good boy, and wait till I come."\par
\par
With that, Jo shouldered her broom and marched into the house, wondering\par
what they would all say to her. Laurie was in a flutter of excitement at\par
the idea of having company, and flew about to get ready; for, as Mrs.\par
March said, he was "a little gentleman," and did honor to the coming\par
guest by brushing his curly pate, putting on a fresh collar, and trying\par
to tidy up the room, which, in spite of half a dozen servants, was\par
anything but neat. Presently there came a loud ring, then a decided\par
voice, asking for "Mr. Laurie," and a surprised-looking servant came\par
running up to announce a young lady.\par
\par
"All right, show her up, it's Miss Jo," said Laurie, going to the door\par
of his little parlor to meet Jo, who appeared, looking rosy and kind and\par
quite at her ease, with a covered dish in one hand and Beth's three\par
kittens in the other.\par
\par
"Here I am, bag and baggage," she said briskly. "Mother sent her love,\par
and was glad if I could do anything for you. Meg wanted me to bring some\par
of her blanc-mange; she makes it very nicely, and Beth thought her cats\par
would be comforting. I knew you'd laugh at them, but I couldn't refuse,\par
she was so anxious to do something."\par
\par
It so happened that Beth's funny loan was just the thing; for, in\par
laughing over the kits, Laurie forgot his bashfulness, and grew sociable\par
at once.\par
\par
"That looks too pretty to eat," he said, smiling with pleasure, as Jo\par
uncovered the dish, and showed the blanc-mange, surrounded by a garland\par
of green leaves, and the scarlet flowers of Amy's pet geranium.\par
\par
"It isn't anything, only they all felt kindly, and wanted to show it.\par
Tell the girl to put it away for your tea: it's so simple, you can eat\par
it; and, being soft, it will slip down without hurting your sore throat.\par
What a cosy room this is!"\par
\par
"It might be if it was kept nice; but the maids are lazy, and I don't\par
know how to make them mind. It worries me, though."\par
\par
"I'll right it up in two minutes; for it only needs to have the hearth\par
brushed, so,--and the things made straight on the mantel-piece so,--and\par
the books put here, and the bottles there, and your sofa turned from the\par
light, and the pillows plumped up a bit. Now, then, you're fixed."\par
\par
And so he was; for, as she laughed and talked, Jo had whisked things\par
into place, and given quite a different air to the room. Laurie watched\par
her in respectful silence; and when she beckoned him to his sofa, he sat\par
down with a sigh of satisfaction, saying gratefully,--\par
\par
"How kind you are! Yes, that's what it wanted. Now please take the big\par
chair, and let me do something to amuse my company."\par
\par
"No; I came to amuse you. Shall I read aloud?" and Jo looked\par
affectionately toward some inviting books near by.\par
\par
"Thank you; I've read all those, and if you don't mind, I'd rather\par
talk," answered Laurie.\par
\par
"Not a bit; I'll talk all day if you'll only set me going. Beth says I\par
never know when to stop."\par
\par
"Is Beth the rosy one, who stays at home a good deal, and sometimes goes\par
out with a little basket?" asked Laurie, with interest.\par
\par
"Yes, that's Beth; she's my girl, and a regular good one she is, too."\par
\par
"The pretty one is Meg, and the curly-haired one is Amy, I believe?"\par
\par
"How did you find that out?"\par
\par
Laurie colored up, but answered frankly, "Why, you see, I often hear you\par
calling to one another, and when I'm alone up here, I can't help looking\par
over at your house, you always seem to be having such good times. I beg\par
your pardon for being so rude, but sometimes you forget to put down the\par
curtain at the window where the flowers are; and when the lamps are\par
lighted, it's like looking at a picture to see the fire, and you all\par
round the table with your mother; her face is right opposite, and it\par
looks so sweet behind the flowers, I can't help watching it. I haven't\par
got any mother, you know;" and Laurie poked the fire to hide a little\par
twitching of the lips that he could not control.\par
\par
The solitary, hungry look in his eyes went straight to Jo's warm heart.\par
She had been so simply taught that there was no nonsense in her head,\par
and at fifteen she was as innocent and frank as any child. Laurie was\par
sick and lonely; and, feeling how rich she was in home-love and\par
happiness, she gladly tried to share it with him. Her face was very\par
friendly and her sharp voice unusually gentle as she said,--\par
\par
"We'll never draw that curtain any more, and I give you leave to look as\par
much as you like. I just wish, though, instead of peeping, you'd come\par
over and see us. Mother is so splendid, she'd do you heaps of good, and\par
Beth would sing to you if _I_ begged her to, and Amy would dance; Meg\par
and I would make you laugh over our funny stage properties, and we'd\par
have jolly times. Wouldn't your grandpa let you?"\par
\par
"I think he would, if your mother asked him. He's very kind, though he\par
does not look so; and he lets me do what I like, pretty much, only he's\par
afraid I might be a bother to strangers," began Laurie, brightening more\par
and more.\par
\par
"We are not strangers, we are neighbors, and you needn't think you'd be\par
a bother. We _want_ to know you, and I've been trying to do it this ever\par
so long. We haven't been here a great while, you know, but we have got\par
acquainted with all our neighbors but you."\par
\par
"You see grandpa lives among his books, and doesn't mind much what\par
happens outside. Mr. Brooke, my tutor, doesn't stay here, you know, and\par
I have no one to go about with me, so I just stop at home and get on as\par
I can."\par
\par
"That's bad. You ought to make an effort, and go visiting everywhere you\par
are asked; then you'll have plenty of friends, and pleasant places to go\par
to. Never mind being bashful; it won't last long if you keep going."\par
\par
Laurie turned red again, but wasn't offended at being accused of\par
bashfulness; for there was so much good-will in Jo, it was impossible\par
not to take her blunt speeches as kindly as they were meant.\par
\par
"Do you like your school?" asked the boy, changing the subject, after a\par
little pause, during which he stared at the fire, and Jo looked about\par
her, well pleased.\par
\par
"Don't go to school; I'm a business man--girl, I mean. I go to wait on\par
my great-aunt, and a dear, cross old soul she is, too," answered Jo.\par
\par
Laurie opened his mouth to ask another question; but remembering just in\par
time that it wasn't manners to make too many inquiries into people's\par
affairs, he shut it again, and looked uncomfortable. Jo liked his good\par
breeding, and didn't mind having a laugh at Aunt March, so she gave him\par
a lively description of the fidgety old lady, her fat poodle, the parrot\par
that talked Spanish, and the library where she revelled. Laurie enjoyed\par
that immensely; and when she told about the prim old gentleman who came\par
once to woo Aunt March, and, in the middle of a fine speech, how Poll\par
had tweaked his wig off to his great dismay, the boy lay back and\par
laughed till the tears ran down his cheeks, and a maid popped her head\par
in to see what was the matter.\par
\par
                [Illustration: Poll tweaked off his wig]\par
\par
"Oh! that does me no end of good. Tell on, please," he said, taking his\par
face out of the sofa-cushion, red and shining with merriment.\par
\par
Much elated with her success, Jo did "tell on," all about their plays\par
and plans, their hopes and fears for father, and the most interesting\par
events of the little world in which the sisters lived. Then they got to\par
talking about books; and to Jo's delight, she found that Laurie loved\par
them as well as she did, and had read even more than herself.\par
\par
"If you like them so much, come down and see ours. Grandpa is out, so\par
you needn't be afraid," said Laurie, getting up.\par
\par
"I'm not afraid of anything," returned Jo, with a toss of the head.\par
\par
"I don't believe you are!" exclaimed the boy, looking at her with much\par
admiration, though he privately thought she would have good reason to be\par
a trifle afraid of the old gentleman, if she met him in some of his\par
moods.\par
\par
The atmosphere of the whole house being summer-like, Laurie led the way\par
from room to room, letting Jo stop to examine whatever struck her fancy;\par
and so at last they came to the library, where she clapped her hands,\par
and pranced, as she always did when especially delighted. It was lined\par
with books, and there were pictures and statues, and distracting little\par
cabinets full of coins and curiosities, and sleepy-hollow chairs, and\par
queer tables, and bronzes; and, best of all, a great open fireplace,\par
with quaint tiles all round it.\par
\par
"What richness!" sighed Jo, sinking into the depth of a velvet chair,\par
and gazing about her with an air of intense satisfaction. "Theodore\par
Laurence, you ought to be the happiest boy in the world," she added\par
impressively.\par
\par
"A fellow can't live on books," said Laurie, shaking his head, as he\par
perched on a table opposite.\par
\par
Before he could say more, a bell rung, and Jo flew up, exclaiming with\par
alarm, "Mercy me! it's your grandpa!"\par
\par
"Well, what if it is? You are not afraid of anything, you know,"\par
returned the boy, looking wicked.\par
\par
"I think I am a little bit afraid of him, but I don't know why I should\par
be. Marmee said I might come, and I don't think you're any the worse for\par
it," said Jo, composing herself, though she kept her eyes on the door.\par
\par
"I'm a great deal better for it, and ever so much obliged. I'm only\par
afraid you are very tired talking to me; it was _so_ pleasant, I\par
couldn't bear to stop," said Laurie gratefully.\par
\par
"The doctor to see you, sir," and the maid beckoned as she spoke.\par
\par
"Would you mind if I left you for a minute? I suppose I must see him,"\par
said Laurie.\par
\par
"Don't mind me. I'm as happy as a cricket here," answered Jo.\par
\par
Laurie went away, and his guest amused herself in her own way. She was\par
standing before a fine portrait of the old gentleman, when the door\par
opened again, and, without turning, she said decidedly, "I'm sure now\par
that I shouldn't be afraid of him, for he's got kind eyes, though his\par
mouth is grim, and he looks as if he had a tremendous will of his own.\par
He isn't as handsome as _my_ grandfather, but I like him."\par
\par
"Thank you, ma'am," said a gruff voice behind her; and there, to her\par
great dismay, stood old Mr. Laurence.\par
\par
Poor Jo blushed till she couldn't blush any redder, and her heart began\par
to beat uncomfortably fast as she thought what she had said. For a\par
minute a wild desire to run away possessed her; but that was cowardly,\par
and the girls would laugh at her: so she resolved to stay, and get out\par
of the scrape as she could. A second look showed her that the living\par
eyes, under the bushy gray eyebrows, were kinder even than the painted\par
ones; and there was a sly twinkle in them, which lessened her fear a\par
good deal. The gruff voice was gruffer than ever, as the old gentleman\par
said abruptly, after that dreadful pause, "So you're not afraid of me,\par
hey?"\par
\par
"Not much, sir."\par
\par
"And you don't think me as handsome as your grandfather?"\par
\par
"Not quite, sir."\par
\par
"And I've got a tremendous will, have I?"\par
\par
"I only said I thought so."\par
\par
"But you like me, in spite of it?"\par
\par
"Yes, I do, sir."\par
\par
That answer pleased the old gentleman; he gave a short laugh, shook\par
hands with her, and, putting his finger under her chin, turned up her\par
face, examined it gravely, and let it go, saying, with a nod, "You've\par
got your grandfather's spirit, if you haven't his face. He _was_ a fine\par
man, my dear; but, what is better, he was a brave and an honest one, and\par
I was proud to be his friend."\par
\par
           [Illustration: Putting his finger under her chin]\par
\par
"Thank you, sir;" and Jo was quite comfortable after that, for it suited\par
her exactly.\par
\par
"What have you been doing to this boy of mine, hey?" was the next\par
question, sharply put.\par
\par
"Only trying to be neighborly, sir;" and Jo told how her visit came\par
about.\par
\par
"You think he needs cheering up a bit, do you?"\par
\par
"Yes, sir; he seems a little lonely, and young folks would do him good\par
perhaps. We are only girls, but we should be glad to help if we could,\par
for we don't forget the splendid Christmas present you sent us," said Jo\par
eagerly.\par
\par
"Tut, tut, tut! that was the boy's affair. How is the poor woman?"\par
\par
"Doing nicely, sir;" and off went Jo, talking very fast, as she told all\par
about the Hummels, in whom her mother had interested richer friends than\par
they were.\par
\par
"Just her father's way of doing good. I shall come and see your mother\par
some fine day. Tell her so. There's the tea-bell; we have it early, on\par
the boy's account. Come down, and go on being neighborly."\par
\par
"If you'd like to have me, sir."\par
\par
"Shouldn't ask you, if I didn't;" and Mr. Laurence offered her his arm\par
with old-fashioned courtesy.\par
\par
"What _would_ Meg say to this?" thought Jo, as she was marched away,\par
while her eyes danced with fun as she imagined herself telling the story\par
at home.\par
\par
"Hey! Why, what the dickens has come to the fellow?" said the old\par
gentleman, as Laurie came running down stairs, and brought up with a\par
start of surprise at the astonishing sight of Jo arm-in-arm with his\par
redoubtable grandfather.\par
\par
"I didn't know you'd come, sir," he began, as Jo gave him a triumphant\par
little glance.\par
\par
"That's evident, by the way you racket down stairs. Come to your tea,\par
sir, and behave like a gentleman;" and having pulled the boy's hair by\par
way of a caress, Mr. Laurence walked on, while Laurie went through a\par
series of comic evolutions behind their backs, which nearly produced an\par
explosion of laughter from Jo.\par
\par
The old gentleman did not say much as he drank his four cups of tea, but\par
he watched the young people, who soon chatted away like old friends, and\par
the change in his grandson did not escape him. There was color, light,\par
and life in the boy's face now, vivacity in his manner, and genuine\par
merriment in his laugh.\par
\par
"She's right; the lad _is_ lonely. I'll see what these little girls can\par
do for him," thought Mr. Laurence, as he looked and listened. He liked\par
Jo, for her odd, blunt ways suited him; and she seemed to understand the\par
boy almost as well as if she had been one herself.\par
\par
If the Laurences had been what Jo called "prim and poky," she would not\par
have got on at all, for such people always made her shy and awkward; but\par
finding them free and easy, she was so herself, and made a good\par
impression. When they rose she proposed to go, but Laurie said he had\par
something more to show her, and took her away to the conservatory, which\par
had been lighted for her benefit. It seemed quite fairylike to Jo, as\par
she went up and down the walks, enjoying the blooming walls on either\par
side, the soft light, the damp sweet air, and the wonderful vines and\par
trees that hung above her,--while her new friend cut the finest flowers\par
till his hands were full; then he tied them up, saying, with the happy\par
look Jo liked to see, "Please give these to your mother, and tell her I\par
like the medicine she sent me very much."\par
\par
            [Illustration: Please give these to your mother]\par
\par
They found Mr. Laurence standing before the fire in the great\par
drawing-room, but Jo's attention was entirely absorbed by a grand piano,\par
which stood open.\par
\par
"Do you play?" she asked, turning to Laurie with a respectful\par
expression.\par
\par
"Sometimes," he answered modestly.\par
\par
"Please do now. I want to hear it, so I can tell Beth."\par
\par
"Won't you first?"\par
\par
"Don't know how; too stupid to learn, but I love music dearly."\par
\par
So Laurie played, and Jo listened, with her nose luxuriously buried in\par
heliotrope and tea-roses. Her respect and regard for the "Laurence boy"\par
increased very much, for he played remarkably well, and didn't put on\par
any airs. She wished Beth could hear him, but she did not say so; only\par
praised him till he was quite abashed, and his grandfather came to the\par
rescue. "That will do, that will do, young lady. Too many sugar-plums\par
are not good for him. His music isn't bad, but I hope he will do as well\par
in more important things. Going? Well, I'm much obliged to you, and I\par
hope you'll come again. My respects to your mother. Good-night, Doctor\par
Jo."\par
\par
He shook hands kindly, but looked as if something did not please him.\par
When they got into the hall, Jo asked Laurie if she had said anything\par
amiss. He shook his head.\par
\par
"No, it was me; he doesn't like to hear me play."\par
\par
"Why not?"\par
\par
"I'll tell you some day. John is going home with you, as I can't."\par
\par
"No need of that; I am not a young lady, and it's only a step. Take care\par
of yourself, won't you?"\par
\par
"Yes; but you will come again, I hope?"\par
\par
"If you promise to come and see us after you are well."\par
\par
"I will."\par
\par
"Good-night, Laurie!"\par
\par
"Good-night, Jo, good-night!"\par
\par
When all the afternoon's adventures had been told, the family felt\par
inclined to go visiting in a body, for each found something very\par
attractive in the big house on the other side of the hedge. Mrs. March\par
wanted to talk of her father with the old man who had not forgotten him;\par
Meg longed to walk in the conservatory; Beth sighed for the grand piano;\par
and Amy was eager to see the fine pictures and statues.\par
\par
"Mother, why didn't Mr. Laurence like to have Laurie play?" asked Jo,\par
who was of an inquiring disposition.\par
\par
"I am not sure, but I think it was because his son, Laurie's father,\par
married an Italian lady, a musician, which displeased the old man, who\par
is very proud. The lady was good and lovely and accomplished, but he did\par
not like her, and never saw his son after he married. They both died\par
when Laurie was a little child, and then his grandfather took him home.\par
I fancy the boy, who was born in Italy, is not very strong, and the old\par
man is afraid of losing him, which makes him so careful. Laurie comes\par
naturally by his love of music, for he is like his mother, and I dare\par
say his grandfather fears that he may want to be a musician; at any\par
rate, his skill reminds him of the woman he did not like, and so he\par
'glowered,' as Jo said."\par
\par
"Dear me, how romantic!" exclaimed Meg.\par
\par
"How silly!" said Jo. "Let him be a musician, if he wants to, and not\par
plague his life out sending him to college, when he hates to go."\par
\par
"That's why he has such handsome black eyes and pretty manners, I\par
suppose. Italians are always nice," said Meg, who was a little\par
sentimental.\par
\par
"What do you know about his eyes and his manners? You never spoke to\par
him, hardly," cried Jo, who was _not_ sentimental.\par
\par
"I saw him at the party, and what you tell shows that he knows how to\par
behave. That was a nice little speech about the medicine mother sent\par
him."\par
\par
"He meant the blanc-mange, I suppose."\par
\par
"How stupid you are, child! He meant you, of course."\par
\par
"Did he?" and Jo opened her eyes as if it had never occurred to her\par
before.\par
\par
"I never saw such a girl! You don't know a compliment when you get it,"\par
said Meg, with the air of a young lady who knew all about the matter.\par
\par
"I think they are great nonsense, and I'll thank you not to be silly,\par
and spoil my fun. Laurie's a nice boy, and I like him, and I won't have\par
any sentimental stuff about compliments and such rubbish. We'll all be\par
good to him, because he hasn't got any mother, and he _may_ come over\par
and see us, mayn't he, Marmee?"\par
\par
"Yes, Jo, your little friend is very welcome, and I hope Meg will\par
remember that children should be children as long as they can."\par
\par
"I don't call myself a child, and I'm not in my teens yet," observed\par
Amy. "What do you say, Beth?"\par
\par
"I was thinking about our 'Pilgrim's Progress,'" answered Beth, who had\par
not heard a word. "How we got out of the Slough and through the Wicket\par
Gate by resolving to be good, and up the steep hill by trying; and that\par
maybe the house over there, full of splendid things, is going to be our\par
Palace Beautiful."\par
\par
"We have got to get by the lions, first," said Jo, as if she rather\par
liked the prospect.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                  VI.\par
\par
                    BETH FINDS THE PALACE BEAUTIFUL.\par
\par
\par
The big house did prove a Palace Beautiful, though it took some time for\par
all to get in, and Beth found it very hard to pass the lions. Old Mr.\par
Laurence was the biggest one; but after he had called, said something\par
funny or kind to each one of the girls, and talked over old times with\par
their mother, nobody felt much afraid of him, except timid Beth. The\par
other lion was the fact that they were poor and Laurie rich; for this\par
made them shy of accepting favors which they could not return. But,\par
after a while, they found that he considered them the benefactors, and\par
could not do enough to show how grateful he was for Mrs. March's\par
motherly welcome, their cheerful society, and the comfort he took in\par
that humble home of theirs. So they soon forgot their pride, and\par
interchanged kindnesses without stopping to think which was the greater.\par
\par
All sorts of pleasant things happened about that time; for the new\par
friendship flourished like grass in spring. Every one liked Laurie, and\par
he privately informed his tutor that "the Marches were regularly\par
splendid girls." With the delightful enthusiasm of youth, they took the\par
solitary boy into their midst, and made much of him, and he found\par
something very charming in the innocent companionship of these\par
simple-hearted girls. Never having known mother or sisters, he was quick\par
to feel the influences they brought about him; and their busy, lively\par
ways made him ashamed of the indolent life he led. He was tired of\par
books, and found people so interesting now that Mr. Brooke was obliged\par
to make very unsatisfactory reports; for Laurie was always playing\par
truant, and running over to the Marches.\par
\par
"Never mind; let him take a holiday, and make it up afterwards," said\par
the old gentleman. "The good lady next door says he is studying too\par
hard, and needs young society, amusement, and exercise. I suspect she is\par
right, and that I've been coddling the fellow as if I'd been his\par
grandmother. Let him do what he likes, as long as he is happy. He can't\par
get into mischief in that little nunnery over there; and Mrs. March is\par
doing more for him than we can."\par
\par
What good times they had, to be sure! Such plays and tableaux, such\par
sleigh-rides and skating frolics, such pleasant evenings in the old\par
parlor, and now and then such gay little parties at the great house. Meg\par
could walk in the conservatory whenever she liked, and revel in\par
bouquets; Jo browsed over the new library voraciously, and convulsed the\par
old gentleman with her criticisms; Amy copied pictures, and enjoyed\par
beauty to her heart's content; and Laurie played "lord of the manor" in\par
the most delightful style.\par
\par
But Beth, though yearning for the grand piano, could not pluck up\par
courage to go to the "Mansion of Bliss," as Meg called it. She went once\par
with Jo; but the old gentleman, not being aware of her infirmity, stared\par
at her so hard from under his heavy eyebrows, and said "Hey!" so loud,\par
that he frightened her so much her "feet chattered on the floor," she\par
told her mother; and she ran away, declaring she would never go there\par
any more, not even for the dear piano. No persuasions or enticements\par
could overcome her fear, till, the fact coming to Mr. Laurence's ear in\par
some mysterious way, he set about mending matters. During one of the\par
brief calls he made, he artfully led the conversation to music, and\par
talked away about great singers whom he had seen, fine organs he had\par
heard, and told such charming anecdotes that Beth found it impossible to\par
stay in her distant corner, but crept nearer and nearer, as if\par
fascinated. At the back of his chair she stopped, and stood listening,\par
with her great eyes wide open, and her cheeks red with the excitement of\par
this unusual performance. Taking no more notice of her than if she had\par
been a fly, Mr. Laurence talked on about Laurie's lessons and teachers;\par
and presently, as if the idea had just occurred to him, he said to Mrs.\par
March,--\par
\par
"The boy neglects his music now, and I'm glad of it, for he was getting\par
too fond of it. But the piano suffers for want of use. Wouldn't some of\par
your girls like to run over, and practise on it now and then, just to\par
keep it in tune, you know, ma'am?"\par
\par
Beth took a step forward, and pressed her hands tightly together to keep\par
from clapping them, for this was an irresistible temptation; and the\par
thought of practising on that splendid instrument quite took her breath\par
away. Before Mrs. March could reply, Mr. Laurence went on with an odd\par
little nod and smile,--\par
\par
"They needn't see or speak to any one, but run in at any time; for I'm\par
shut up in my study at the other end of the house, Laurie is out a great\par
deal, and the servants are never near the drawing-room after nine\par
o'clock."\par
\par
Here he rose, as if going, and Beth made up her mind to speak, for that\par
last arrangement left nothing to be desired. "Please tell the young\par
ladies what I say; and if they don't care to come, why, never mind."\par
Here a little hand slipped into his, and Beth looked up at him with a\par
face full of gratitude, as she said, in her earnest yet timid way,--\par
\par
"O sir, they do care, very, very much!"\par
\par
             [Illustration: O sir, they do care very much]\par
\par
"Are you the musical girl?" he asked, without any startling "Hey!" as he\par
looked down at her very kindly.\par
\par
"I'm Beth. I love it dearly, and I'll come, if you are quite sure nobody\par
will hear me--and be disturbed," she added, fearing to be rude, and\par
trembling at her own boldness as she spoke.\par
\par
"Not a soul, my dear. The house is empty half the day; so come, and drum\par
away as much as you like, and I shall be obliged to you."\par
\par
"How kind you are, sir!"\par
\par
Beth blushed like a rose under the friendly look he wore; but she was\par
not frightened now, and gave the big hand a grateful squeeze, because\par
she had no words to thank him for the precious gift he had given her.\par
The old gentleman softly stroked the hair off her forehead, and,\par
stooping down, he kissed her, saying, in a tone few people ever heard,--\par
\par
"I had a little girl once, with eyes like these. God bless you, my dear!\par
Good day, madam;" and away he went, in a great hurry.\par
\par
Beth had a rapture with her mother, and then rushed up to impart the\par
glorious news to her family of invalids, as the girls were not at home.\par
How blithely she sung that evening, and how they all laughed at her,\par
because she woke Amy in the night by playing the piano on her face in\par
her sleep. Next day, having seen both the old and young gentleman out of\par
the house, Beth, after two or three retreats, fairly got in at the\par
side-door, and made her way, as noiselessly as any mouse, to the\par
drawing-room, where her idol stood. Quite by accident, of course, some\par
pretty, easy music lay on the piano; and, with trembling fingers, and\par
frequent stops to listen and look about, Beth at last touched the great\par
instrument, and straightway forgot her fear, herself, and everything\par
else but the unspeakable delight which the music gave her, for it was\par
like the voice of a beloved friend.\par
\par
She stayed till Hannah came to take her home to dinner; but she had no\par
appetite, and could only sit and smile upon every one in a general state\par
of beatitude.\par
\par
After that, the little brown hood slipped through the hedge nearly every\par
day, and the great drawing-room was haunted by a tuneful spirit that\par
came and went unseen. She never knew that Mr. Laurence often opened his\par
study-door to hear the old-fashioned airs he liked; she never saw Laurie\par
mount guard in the hall to warn the servants away; she never suspected\par
that the exercise-books and new songs which she found in the rack were\par
put there for her especial benefit; and when he talked to her about\par
music at home, she only thought how kind he was to tell things that\par
helped her so much. So she enjoyed herself heartily, and found, what\par
isn't always the case, that her granted wish was all she had hoped.\par
Perhaps it was because she was so grateful for this blessing that a\par
greater was given her; at any rate, she deserved both.\par
\par
        [Illustration: Mr. Laurence often opened his study door]\par
\par
"Mother, I'm going to work Mr. Laurence a pair of slippers. He is so\par
kind to me, I must thank him, and I don't know any other way. Can I do\par
it?" asked Beth, a few weeks after that eventful call of his.\par
\par
"Yes, dear. It will please him very much, and be a nice way of thanking\par
him. The girls will help you about them, and I will pay for the making\par
up," replied Mrs. March, who took peculiar pleasure in granting Beth's\par
requests, because she so seldom asked anything for herself.\par
\par
After many serious discussions with Meg and Jo, the pattern was chosen,\par
the materials bought, and the slippers begun. A cluster of grave yet\par
cheerful pansies, on a deeper purple ground, was pronounced very\par
appropriate and pretty; and Beth worked away early and late, with\par
occasional lifts over hard parts. She was a nimble little needle-woman,\par
and they were finished before any one got tired of them. Then she wrote\par
a very short, simple note, and, with Laurie's help, got them smuggled on\par
to the study-table one morning before the old gentleman was up.\par
\par
When this excitement was over, Beth waited to see what would happen. All\par
that day passed, and a part of the next, before any acknowledgment\par
arrived, and she was beginning to fear she had offended her crotchety\par
friend. On the afternoon of the second day, she went out to do an\par
errand, and give poor Joanna, the invalid doll, her daily exercise. As\par
she came up the street, on her return, she saw three, yes, four, heads\par
popping in and out of the parlor windows, and the moment they saw her,\par
several hands were waved, and several joyful voices screamed,--\par
\par
"Here's a letter from the old gentleman! Come quick, and read it!"\par
\par
"O Beth, he's sent you--" began Amy, gesticulating with unseemly energy;\par
but she got no further, for Jo quenched her by slamming down the window.\par
\par
Beth hurried on in a flutter of suspense. At the door, her sisters\par
seized and bore her to the parlor in a triumphal procession, all\par
pointing, and all saying at once, "Look there! look there!" Beth did\par
look, and turned pale with delight and surprise; for there stood a\par
little cabinet-piano, with a letter lying on the glossy lid, directed,\par
like a sign-board, to "Miss Elizabeth March."\par
\par
"For me?" gasped Beth, holding on to Jo, and feeling as if she should\par
tumble down, it was such an overwhelming thing altogether.\par
\par
"Yes; all for you, my precious! Isn't it splendid of him? Don't you\par
think he's the dearest old man in the world? Here's the key in the\par
letter. We didn't open it, but we are dying to know what he says," cried\par
Jo, hugging her sister, and offering the note.\par
\par
"You read it! I can't, I feel so queer! Oh, it is too lovely!" and Beth\par
hid her face in Jo's apron, quite upset by her present.\par
\par
Jo opened the paper, and began to laugh, for the first words she saw\par
were,--\par
\par
    "MISS MARCH:\par
\par
        "_Dear Madam_,--"\par
\par
"How nice it sounds! I wish some one would write to me so!" said Amy,\par
who thought the old-fashioned address very elegant.\par
\par
    "'I have had many pairs of slippers in my life, but I never had\par
    any that suited me so well as yours,'" continued Jo.\par
    "'Heart's-ease is my favorite flower, and these will always\par
    remind me of the gentle giver. I like to pay my debts; so I\par
    know you will allow "the old gentleman" to send you something\par
    which once belonged to the little granddaughter he lost. With\par
    hearty thanks and best wishes, I remain,\par
\par
    "'Your grateful friend and humble servant,\par
\par
                                        "'JAMES LAURENCE.'"\par
\par
"There, Beth, that's an honor to be proud of, I'm sure! Laurie told me\par
how fond Mr. Laurence used to be of the child who died, and how he kept\par
all her little things carefully. Just think, he's given you her piano.\par
That comes of having big blue eyes and loving music," said Jo, trying to\par
soothe Beth, who trembled, and looked more excited than she had ever\par
been before.\par
\par
"See the cunning brackets to hold candles, and the nice green silk,\par
puckered up, with a gold rose in the middle, and the pretty rack and\par
stool, all complete," added Meg, opening the instrument and displaying\par
its beauties.\par
\par
"'Your humble servant, James Laurence'; only think of his writing that\par
to you. I'll tell the girls. They'll think it's splendid," said Amy,\par
much impressed by the note.\par
\par
"Try it, honey. Let's hear the sound of the baby-pianny," said Hannah,\par
who always took a share in the family joys and sorrows.\par
\par
So Beth tried it; and every one pronounced it the most remarkable piano\par
ever heard. It had evidently been newly tuned and put in apple-pie\par
order; but, perfect as it was, I think the real charm of it lay in the\par
happiest of all happy faces which leaned over it, as Beth lovingly\par
touched the beautiful black and white keys and pressed the bright\par
pedals.\par
\par
"You'll have to go and thank him," said Jo, by way of a joke; for the\par
idea of the child's really going never entered her head.\par
\par
"Yes, I mean to. I guess I'll go now, before I get frightened thinking\par
about it." And, to the utter amazement of the assembled family, Beth\par
walked deliberately down the garden, through the hedge, and in at the\par
Laurences' door.\par
\par
"Well, I wish I may die if it ain't the queerest thing I ever see! The\par
pianny has turned her head! She'd never have gone in her right mind,"\par
cried Hannah, staring after her, while the girls were rendered quite\par
speechless by the miracle.\par
\par
They would have been still more amazed if they had seen what Beth did\par
afterward. If you will believe me, she went and knocked at the\par
study-door before she gave herself time to think; and when a gruff voice\par
called out, "Come in!" she did go in, right up to Mr. Laurence, who\par
looked quite taken aback, and held out her hand, saying, with only a\par
small quaver in her voice, "I came to thank you, sir, for--" But she\par
didn't finish; for he looked so friendly that she forgot her speech,\par
and, only remembering that he had lost the little girl he loved, she put\par
both arms round his neck, and kissed him.\par
\par
    [Illustration: She put both arms around his neck and kissed him]\par
\par
If the roof of the house had suddenly flown off, the old gentleman\par
wouldn't have been more astonished; but he liked it,--oh, dear, yes, he\par
liked it amazingly!--and was so touched and pleased by that confiding\par
little kiss that all his crustiness vanished; and he just set her on his\par
knee, and laid his wrinkled cheek against her rosy one, feeling as if he\par
had got his own little granddaughter back again. Beth ceased to fear him\par
from that moment, and sat there talking to him as cosily as if she had\par
known him all her life; for love casts out fear, and gratitude can\par
conquer pride. When she went home, he walked with her to her own gate,\par
shook hands cordially, and touched his hat as he marched back again,\par
looking very stately and erect, like a handsome, soldierly old\par
gentleman, as he was.\par
\par
When the girls saw that performance, Jo began to dance a jig, by way of\par
expressing her satisfaction; Amy nearly fell out of the window in her\par
surprise; and Meg exclaimed, with uplifted hands, "Well, I do believe\par
the world is coming to an end!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                  VII.\par
\par
                      AMY'S VALLEY OF HUMILIATION.\par
\par
\par
                      [Illustration: The Cyclops]\par
\par
"That boy is a perfect Cyclops, isn't he?" said Amy, one day, as Laurie\par
clattered by on horseback, with a flourish of his whip as he passed.\par
\par
"How dare you say so, when he's got both his eyes? and very handsome\par
ones they are, too," cried Jo, who resented any slighting remarks about\par
her friend.\par
\par
"I didn't say anything about his eyes, and I don't see why you need fire\par
up when I admire his riding."\par
\par
"Oh, my goodness! that little goose means a centaur, and she called him\par
a Cyclops," exclaimed Jo, with a burst of laughter.\par
\par
"You needn't be so rude; it's only a 'lapse of lingy,' as Mr. Davis\par
says," retorted Amy, finishing Jo with her Latin. "I just wish I had a\par
little of the money Laurie spends on that horse," she added, as if to\par
herself, yet hoping her sisters would hear.\par
\par
"Why?" asked Meg kindly, for Jo had gone off in another laugh at Amy's\par
second blunder.\par
\par
"I need it so much; I'm dreadfully in debt, and it won't be my turn to\par
have the rag-money for a month."\par
\par
"In debt, Amy? What do you mean?" and Meg looked sober.\par
\par
"Why, I owe at least a dozen pickled limes, and I can't pay them, you\par
know, till I have money, for Marmee forbade my having anything charged\par
at the shop."\par
\par
"Tell me all about it. Are limes the fashion now? It used to be pricking\par
bits of rubber to make balls;" and Meg tried to keep her countenance,\par
Amy looked so grave and important.\par
\par
"Why, you see, the girls are always buying them, and unless you want to\par
be thought mean, you must do it, too. It's nothing but limes now, for\par
every one is sucking them in their desks in school-time, and trading\par
them off for pencils, bead-rings, paper dolls, or something else, at\par
recess. If one girl likes another, she gives her a lime; if she's mad\par
with her, she eats one before her face, and don't offer even a suck.\par
They treat by turns; and I've had ever so many, but haven't returned\par
them; and I ought, for they are debts of honor, you know."\par
\par
"How much will pay them off, and restore your credit?" asked Meg, taking\par
out her purse.\par
\par
"A quarter would more than do it, and leave a few cents over for a treat\par
for you. Don't you like limes?"\par
\par
"Not much; you may have my share. Here's the money. Make it last as long\par
as you can, for it isn't very plenty, you know."\par
\par
"Oh, thank you! It must be so nice to have pocket-money! I'll have a\par
grand feast, for I haven't tasted a lime this week. I felt delicate\par
about taking any, as I couldn't return them, and I'm actually suffering\par
for one."\par
\par
Next day Amy was rather late at school; but could not resist the\par
temptation of displaying, with pardonable pride, a moist brown-paper\par
parcel, before she consigned it to the inmost recesses of her desk.\par
During the next few minutes the rumor that Amy March had got twenty-four\par
delicious limes (she ate one on the way), and was going to treat,\par
circulated through her "set," and the attentions of her friends became\par
quite overwhelming. Katy Brown invited her to her next party on the\par
spot; Mary Kingsley insisted on lending her her watch till recess; and\par
Jenny Snow, a satirical young lady, who had basely twitted Amy upon her\par
limeless state, promptly buried the hatchet, and offered to furnish\par
answers to certain appalling sums. But Amy had not forgotten Miss Snow's\par
cutting remarks about "some persons whose noses were not too flat to\par
smell other people's limes, and stuck-up people, who were not too proud\par
to ask for them;" and she instantly crushed "that Snow girl's" hopes by\par
the withering telegram, "You needn't be so polite all of a sudden, for\par
you won't get any."\par
\par
A distinguished personage happened to visit the school that morning, and\par
Amy's beautifully drawn maps received praise, which honor to her foe\par
rankled in the soul of Miss Snow, and caused Miss March to assume the\par
airs of a studious young peacock. But, alas, alas! pride goes before a\par
fall, and the revengeful Snow turned the tables with disastrous success.\par
No sooner had the guest paid the usual stale compliments, and bowed\par
himself out, than Jenny, under pretence of asking an important question,\par
informed Mr. Davis, the teacher, that Amy March had pickled limes in her\par
desk.\par
\par
Now Mr. Davis had declared limes a contraband article, and solemnly\par
vowed to publicly ferrule the first person who was found breaking the\par
law. This much-enduring man had succeeded in banishing chewing-gum after\par
a long and stormy war, had made a bonfire of the confiscated novels and\par
newspapers, had suppressed a private post-office, had forbidden\par
distortions of the face, nicknames, and caricatures, and done all that\par
one man could do to keep half a hundred rebellious girls in order. Boys\par
are trying enough to human patience, goodness knows! but girls are\par
infinitely more so, especially to nervous gentlemen, with tyrannical\par
tempers, and no more talent for teaching than Dr. Blimber. Mr. Davis\par
knew any quantity of Greek, Latin, Algebra, and ologies of all sorts, so\par
he was called a fine teacher; and manners, morals, feelings, and\par
examples were not considered of any particular importance. It was a most\par
unfortunate moment for denouncing Amy, and Jenny knew it. Mr. Davis had\par
evidently taken his coffee too strong that morning; there was an east\par
wind, which always affected his neuralgia; and his pupils had not done\par
him the credit which he felt he deserved: therefore, to use the\par
expressive, if not elegant, language of a school-girl, "he was as\par
nervous as a witch and as cross as a bear." The word "limes" was like\par
fire to powder; his yellow face flushed, and he rapped on his desk with\par
an energy which made Jenny skip to her seat with unusual rapidity.\par
\par
"Young ladies, attention, if you please!"\par
\par
At the stern order the buzz ceased, and fifty pairs of blue, black,\par
gray, and brown eyes were obediently fixed upon his awful countenance.\par
\par
"Miss March, come to the desk."\par
\par
Amy rose to comply with outward composure, but a secret fear oppressed\par
her, for the limes weighed upon her conscience.\par
\par
"Bring with you the limes you have in your desk," was the unexpected\par
command which arrested her before she got out of her seat.\par
\par
"Don't take all," whispered her neighbor, a young lady of great presence\par
of mind.\par
\par
Amy hastily shook out half a dozen, and laid the rest down before Mr.\par
Davis, feeling that any man possessing a human heart would relent when\par
that delicious perfume met his nose. Unfortunately, Mr. Davis\par
particularly detested the odor of the fashionable pickle, and disgust\par
added to his wrath.\par
\par
"Is that all?"\par
\par
"Not quite," stammered Amy.\par
\par
"Bring the rest immediately."\par
\par
With a despairing glance at her set, she obeyed.\par
\par
"You are sure there are no more?"\par
\par
"I never lie, sir."\par
\par
"So I see. Now take these disgusting things two by two, and throw them\par
out of the window."\par
\par
There was a simultaneous sigh, which created quite a little gust, as the\par
last hope fled, and the treat was ravished from their longing lips.\par
Scarlet with shame and anger, Amy went to and fro six dreadful times;\par
and as each doomed couple--looking oh! so plump and juicy--fell from her\par
reluctant hands, a shout from the street completed the anguish of the\par
girls, for it told them that their feast was being exulted over by the\par
little Irish children, who were their sworn foes. This--this was too\par
much; all flashed indignant or appealing glances at the inexorable\par
Davis, and one passionate lime-lover burst into tears.\par
\par
As Amy returned from her last trip, Mr. Davis gave a portentous "Hem!"\par
and said, in his most impressive manner,--\par
\par
"Young ladies, you remember what I said to you a week ago. I am sorry\par
this has happened, but I never allow my rules to be infringed, and I\par
_never_ break my word. Miss March, hold out your hand."\par
\par
Amy started, and put both hands behind her, turning on him an imploring\par
look which pleaded for her better than the words she could not utter.\par
She was rather a favorite with "old Davis," as, of course, he was\par
called, and it's my private belief that he _would_ have broken his word\par
if the indignation of one irrepressible young lady had not found vent in\par
a hiss. That hiss, faint as it was, irritated the irascible gentleman,\par
and sealed the culprit's fate.\par
\par
"Your hand, Miss March!" was the only answer her mute appeal received;\par
and, too proud to cry or beseech, Amy set her teeth, threw back her\par
head defiantly, and bore without flinching several tingling blows on her\par
little palm. They were neither many nor heavy, but that made no\par
difference to her. For the first time in her life she had been struck;\par
and the disgrace, in her eyes, was as deep as if he had knocked her\par
down.\par
\par
   [Illustration: Amy bore without flinching several tingling blows]\par
\par
"You will now stand on the platform till recess," said Mr. Davis,\par
resolved to do the thing thoroughly, since he had begun.\par
\par
That was dreadful. It would have been bad enough to go to her seat, and\par
see the pitying faces of her friends, or the satisfied ones of her few\par
enemies; but to face the whole school, with that shame fresh upon her,\par
seemed impossible, and for a second she felt as if she could only drop\par
down where she stood, and break her heart with crying. A bitter sense of\par
wrong, and the thought of Jenny Snow, helped her to bear it; and, taking\par
the ignominious place, she fixed her eyes on the stove-funnel above what\par
now seemed a sea of faces, and stood there, so motionless and white that\par
the girls found it very hard to study, with that pathetic figure before\par
them.\par
\par
During the fifteen minutes that followed, the proud and sensitive little\par
girl suffered a shame and pain which she never forgot. To others it\par
might seem a ludicrous or trivial affair, but to her it was a hard\par
experience; for during the twelve years of her life she had been\par
governed by love alone, and a blow of that sort had never touched her\par
before. The smart of her hand and the ache of her heart were forgotten\par
in the sting of the thought,--\par
\par
"I shall have to tell at home, and they will be so disappointed in me!"\par
\par
The fifteen minutes seemed an hour; but they came to an end at last, and\par
the word "Recess!" had never seemed so welcome to her before.\par
\par
"You can go, Miss March," said Mr. Davis, looking, as he felt,\par
uncomfortable.\par
\par
He did not soon forget the reproachful glance Amy gave him, as she went,\par
without a word to any one, straight into the ante-room, snatched her\par
things, and left the place "forever," as she passionately declared to\par
herself. She was in a sad state when she got home; and when the older\par
girls arrived, some time later, an indignation meeting was held at\par
once. Mrs. March did not say much, but looked disturbed, and comforted\par
her afflicted little daughter in her tenderest manner. Meg bathed the\par
insulted hand with glycerine and tears; Beth felt that even her beloved\par
kittens would fail as a balm for griefs like this; Jo wrathfully\par
proposed that Mr. Davis be arrested without delay; and Hannah shook her\par
fist at the "villain," and pounded potatoes for dinner as if she had him\par
under her pestle.\par
\par
No notice was taken of Amy's flight, except by her mates; but the\par
sharp-eyed demoiselles discovered that Mr. Davis was quite benignant in\par
the afternoon, also unusually nervous. Just before school closed, Jo\par
appeared, wearing a grim expression, as she stalked up to the desk, and\par
delivered a letter from her mother; then collected Amy's property, and\par
departed, carefully scraping the mud from her boots on the door-mat, as\par
if she shook the dust of the place off her feet.\par
\par
"Yes, you can have a vacation from school, but I want you to study a\par
little every day, with Beth," said Mrs. March, that evening. "I don't\par
approve of corporal punishment, especially for girls. I dislike Mr.\par
Davis's manner of teaching, and don't think the girls you associate with\par
are doing you any good, so I shall ask your father's advice before I\par
send you anywhere else."\par
\par
"That's good! I wish all the girls would leave, and spoil his old\par
school. It's perfectly maddening to think of those lovely limes," sighed\par
Amy, with the air of a martyr.\par
\par
"I am not sorry you lost them, for you broke the rules, and deserved\par
some punishment for disobedience," was the severe reply, which rather\par
disappointed the young lady, who expected nothing but sympathy.\par
\par
"Do you mean you are glad I was disgraced before the whole school?"\par
cried Amy.\par
\par
"I should not have chosen that way of mending a fault," replied her\par
mother; "but I'm not sure that it won't do you more good than a milder\par
method. You are getting to be rather conceited, my dear, and it is quite\par
time you set about correcting it. You have a good many little gifts and\par
virtues, but there is no need of parading them, for conceit spoils the\par
finest genius. There is not much danger that real talent or goodness\par
will be overlooked long; even if it is, the consciousness of possessing\par
and using it well should satisfy one, and the great charm of all power\par
is modesty."\par
\par
"So it is!" cried Laurie, who was playing chess in a corner with Jo. "I\par
knew a girl, once, who had a really remarkable talent for music, and she\par
didn't know it; never guessed what sweet little things she composed when\par
she was alone, and wouldn't have believed it if any one had told her."\par
\par
"I wish I'd known that nice girl; maybe she would have helped me, I'm so\par
stupid," said Beth, who stood beside him, listening eagerly.\par
\par
"You do know her, and she helps you better than any one else could,"\par
answered Laurie, looking at her with such mischievous meaning in his\par
merry black eyes, that Beth suddenly turned very red, and hid her face\par
in the sofa-cushion, quite overcome by such an unexpected discovery.\par
\par
                    [Illustration: You do know her]\par
\par
Jo let Laurie win the game, to pay for that praise of her Beth, who\par
could not be prevailed upon to play for them after her compliment. So\par
Laurie did his best, and sung delightfully, being in a particularly\par
lively humor, for to the Marches he seldom showed the moody side of his\par
character. When he was gone, Amy, who had been pensive all the evening,\par
said suddenly, as if busy over some new idea,--\par
\par
"Is Laurie an accomplished boy?"\par
\par
"Yes; he has had an excellent education, and has much talent; he will\par
make a fine man, if not spoilt by petting," replied her mother.\par
\par
"And he isn't conceited, is he?" asked Amy.\par
\par
"Not in the least; that is why he is so charming, and we all like him so\par
much."\par
\par
"I see; it's nice to have accomplishments, and be elegant; but not to\par
show off, or get perked up," said Amy thoughtfully.\par
\par
"These things are always seen and felt in a person's manner and\par
conversation, if modestly used; but it is not necessary to display\par
them," said Mrs. March.\par
\par
"Any more than it's proper to wear all your bonnets and gowns and\par
ribbons at once, that folks may know you've got them," added Jo; and the\par
lecture ended in a laugh.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
              [Illustration: Girls, where are you going?]\par
\par
                                 VIII.\par
\par
                           JO MEETS APOLLYON.\par
\par
\par
"Girls, where are you going?" asked Amy, coming into their room one\par
Saturday afternoon, and finding them getting ready to go out, with an\par
air of secrecy which excited her curiosity.\par
\par
"Never mind; little girls shouldn't ask questions," returned Jo sharply.\par
\par
Now if there _is_ anything mortifying to our feelings, when we are\par
young, it is to be told that; and to be bidden to "run away, dear," is\par
still more trying to us. Amy bridled up at this insult, and determined\par
to find out the secret, if she teased for an hour. Turning to Meg, who\par
never refused her anything very long, she said coaxingly, "Do tell me! I\par
should think you might let me go, too; for Beth is fussing over her\par
piano, and I haven't got anything to do, and am _so_ lonely."\par
\par
"I can't, dear, because you aren't invited," began Meg; but Jo broke in\par
impatiently, "Now, Meg, be quiet, or you will spoil it all. You can't\par
go, Amy; so don't be a baby, and whine about it."\par
\par
"You are going somewhere with Laurie, I know you are; you were\par
whispering and laughing together, on the sofa, last night, and you\par
stopped when I came in. Aren't you going with him?"\par
\par
"Yes, we are; now do be still, and stop bothering."\par
\par
Amy held her tongue, but used her eyes, and saw Meg slip a fan into her\par
pocket.\par
\par
"I know! I know! you're going to the theatre to see the 'Seven\par
Castles!'" she cried; adding resolutely, "and I _shall_ go, for mother\par
said I might see it; and I've got my rag-money, and it was mean not to\par
tell me in time."\par
\par
"Just listen to me a minute, and be a good child," said Meg soothingly.\par
"Mother doesn't wish you to go this week, because your eyes are not well\par
enough yet to bear the light of this fairy piece. Next week you can go\par
with Beth and Hannah, and have a nice time."\par
\par
"I don't like that half as well as going with you and Laurie. Please let\par
me; I've been sick with this cold so long, and shut up, I'm dying for\par
some fun. Do, Meg! I'll be ever so good," pleaded Amy, looking as\par
pathetic as she could.\par
\par
"Suppose we take her. I don't believe mother would mind, if we bundle\par
her up well," began Meg.\par
\par
"If _she_ goes _I_ sha'n't; and if I don't, Laurie won't like it; and it\par
will be very rude, after he invited only us, to go and drag in Amy. I\par
should think she'd hate to poke herself where she isn't wanted," said Jo\par
crossly, for she disliked the trouble of overseeing a fidgety child,\par
when she wanted to enjoy herself.\par
\par
Her tone and manner angered Amy, who began to put her boots on, saying,\par
in her most aggravating way, "I _shall_ go; Meg says I may; and if I pay\par
for myself, Laurie hasn't anything to do with it."\par
\par
"You can't sit with us, for our seats are reserved, and you mustn't sit\par
alone; so Laurie will give you his place, and that will spoil our\par
pleasure; or he'll get another seat for you, and that isn't proper, when\par
you weren't asked. You sha'n't stir a step; so you may just stay where\par
you are," scolded Jo, crosser than ever, having just pricked her finger\par
in her hurry.\par
\par
Sitting on the floor, with one boot on, Amy began to cry, and Meg to\par
reason with her, when Laurie called from below, and the two girls\par
hurried down, leaving their sister wailing; for now and then she forgot\par
her grown-up ways, and acted like a spoilt child. Just as the party was\par
setting out, Amy called over the banisters, in a threatening tone,\par
"You'll be sorry for this, Jo March; see if you ain't."\par
\par
"Fiddlesticks!" returned Jo, slamming the door.\par
\par
They had a charming time, for "The Seven Castles of the Diamond Lake"\par
were as brilliant and wonderful as heart could wish. But, in spite of\par
the comical red imps, sparkling elves, and gorgeous princes and\par
princesses, Jo's pleasure had a drop of bitterness in it; the fairy\par
queen's yellow curls reminded her of Amy; and between the acts she\par
amused herself with wondering what her sister would do to make her\par
"sorry for it." She and Amy had had many lively skirmishes in the course\par
of their lives, for both had quick tempers, and were apt to be violent\par
when fairly roused. Amy teased Jo, and Jo irritated Amy, and\par
semi-occasional explosions occurred, of which both were much ashamed\par
afterward. Although the oldest, Jo had the least self-control, and had\par
hard times trying to curb the fiery spirit which was continually getting\par
her into trouble; her anger never lasted long, and, having humbly\par
confessed her fault, she sincerely repented, and tried to do better. Her\par
sisters used to say that they rather liked to get Jo into a fury,\par
because she was such an angel afterward. Poor Jo tried desperately to be\par
good, but her bosom enemy was always ready to flame up and defeat her;\par
and it took years of patient effort to subdue it.\par
\par
When they got home, they found Amy reading in the parlor. She assumed an\par
injured air as they came in; never lifted her eyes from her book, or\par
asked a single question. Perhaps curiosity might have conquered\par
resentment, if Beth had not been there to inquire, and receive a glowing\par
description of the play. On going up to put away her best hat, Jo's\par
first look was toward the bureau; for, in their last quarrel, Amy had\par
soothed her feelings by turning Jo's top drawer upside down on the\par
floor. Everything was in its place, however; and after a hasty glance\par
into her various closets, bags, and boxes, Jo decided that Amy had\par
forgiven and forgotten her wrongs.\par
\par
There Jo was mistaken; for next day she made a discovery which produced\par
a tempest. Meg, Beth, and Amy were sitting together, late in the\par
afternoon, when Jo burst into the room, looking excited, and demanding\par
breathlessly, "Has any one taken my book?"\par
\par
Meg and Beth said "No," at once, and looked surprised; Amy poked the\par
fire, and said nothing. Jo saw her color rise, and was down upon her in\par
a minute.\par
\par
"Amy, you've got it?"\par
\par
"No, I haven't."\par
\par
"You know where it is, then?"\par
\par
"No, I don't."\par
\par
"That's a fib!" cried Jo, taking her by the shoulders, and looking\par
fierce enough to frighten a much braver child than Amy.\par
\par
"It isn't. I haven't got it, don't know where it is now, and don't\par
care."\par
\par
"You know something about it, and you'd better tell at once, or I'll\par
make you," and Jo gave her a slight shake.\par
\par
"Scold as much as you like, you'll never see your silly old book again,"\par
cried Amy, getting excited in her turn.\par
\par
"Why not?"\par
\par
"I burnt it up."\par
\par
                     [Illustration: I burnt it up]\par
\par
"What! my little book I was so fond of, and worked over, and meant to\par
finish before father got home? Have you really burnt it?" said Jo,\par
turning very pale, while her eyes kindled and her hands clutched Amy\par
nervously.\par
\par
"Yes, I did! I told you I'd make you pay for being so cross yesterday,\par
and I have, so--"\par
\par
Amy got no farther, for Jo's hot temper mastered her, and she shook Amy\par
till her teeth chattered in her head; crying, in a passion of grief and\par
anger,--\par
\par
"You wicked, wicked girl! I never can write it again, and I'll never\par
forgive you as long as I live."\par
\par
Meg flew to rescue Amy, and Beth to pacify Jo, but Jo was quite beside\par
herself; and, with a parting box on her sister's ear, she rushed out of\par
the room up to the old sofa in the garret, and finished her fight alone.\par
\par
The storm cleared up below, for Mrs. March came home, and, having heard\par
the story, soon brought Amy to a sense of the wrong she had done her\par
sister. Jo's book was the pride of her heart, and was regarded by her\par
family as a literary sprout of great promise. It was only half a dozen\par
little fairy tales, but Jo had worked over them patiently, putting her\par
whole heart into her work, hoping to make something good enough to\par
print. She had just copied them with great care, and had destroyed the\par
old manuscript, so that Amy's bonfire had consumed the loving work of\par
several years. It seemed a small loss to others, but to Jo it was a\par
dreadful calamity, and she felt that it never could be made up to her.\par
Beth mourned as for a departed kitten, and Meg refused to defend her\par
pet; Mrs. March looked grave and grieved, and Amy felt that no one would\par
love her till she had asked pardon for the act which she now regretted\par
more than any of them.\par
\par
When the tea-bell rung, Jo appeared, looking so grim and unapproachable\par
that it took all Amy's courage to say meekly,--\par
\par
"Please forgive me, Jo; I'm very, very sorry."\par
\par
"I never shall forgive you," was Jo's stern answer; and, from that\par
moment, she ignored Amy entirely.\par
\par
No one spoke of the great trouble,--not even Mrs. March,--for all had\par
learned by experience that when Jo was in that mood words were wasted;\par
and the wisest course was to wait till some little accident, or her own\par
generous nature, softened Jo's resentment, and healed the breach. It was\par
not a happy evening; for, though they sewed as usual, while their mother\par
read aloud from Bremer, Scott, or Edgeworth, something was wanting, and\par
the sweet home-peace was disturbed. They felt this most when\par
singing-time came; for Beth could only play, Jo stood dumb as a stone,\par
and Amy broke down, so Meg and mother sung alone. But, in spite of their\par
efforts to be as cheery as larks, the flute-like voices did not seem to\par
chord as well as usual, and all felt out of tune.\par
\par
As Jo received her good-night kiss, Mrs. March whispered gently,--\par
\par
"My dear, don't let the sun go down upon your anger; forgive each other,\par
help each other, and begin again to-morrow."\par
\par
Jo wanted to lay her head down on that motherly bosom, and cry her grief\par
and anger all away; but tears were an unmanly weakness, and she felt so\par
deeply injured that she really _couldn't_ quite forgive yet. So she\par
winked hard, shook her head, and said, gruffly because Amy was\par
listening,--\par
\par
"It was an abominable thing, and she don't deserve to be forgiven."\par
\par
With that she marched off to bed, and there was no merry or confidential\par
gossip that night.\par
\par
Amy was much offended that her overtures of peace had been repulsed, and\par
began to wish she had not humbled herself, to feel more injured than\par
ever, and to plume herself on her superior virtue in a way which was\par
particularly exasperating. Jo still looked like a thunder-cloud, and\par
nothing went well all day. It was bitter cold in the morning; she\par
dropped her precious turn-over in the gutter, Aunt March had an attack\par
of fidgets, Meg was pensive, Beth _would_ look grieved and wistful when\par
she got home, and Amy kept making remarks about people who were always\par
talking about being good, and yet wouldn't try, when other people set\par
them a virtuous example.\par
\par
"Everybody is so hateful, I'll ask Laurie to go skating. He is always\par
kind and jolly, and will put me to rights, I know," said Jo to herself,\par
and off she went.\par
\par
Amy heard the clash of skates, and looked out with an impatient\par
exclamation,--\par
\par
"There! she promised I should go next time, for this is the last ice we\par
shall have. But it's no use to ask such a cross-patch to take me."\par
\par
"Don't say that; you _were_ very naughty, and it _is_ hard to forgive\par
the loss of her precious little book; but I think she might do it now,\par
and I guess she will, if you try her at the right minute," said Meg. "Go\par
after them; don't say anything till Jo has got good-natured with Laurie,\par
then take a quiet minute, and just kiss her, or do some kind thing, and\par
I'm sure she'll be friends again, with all her heart."\par
\par
"I'll try," said Amy, for the advice suited her; and, after a flurry to\par
get ready, she ran after the friends, who were just disappearing over\par
the hill.\par
\par
It was not far to the river, but both were ready before Amy reached\par
them. Jo saw her coming, and turned her back; Laurie did not see, for he\par
was carefully skating along the shore, sounding the ice, for a warm\par
spell had preceded the cold snap.\par
\par
"I'll go on to the first bend, and see if it's all right, before we\par
begin to race," Amy heard him say, as he shot away, looking like a young\par
Russian, in his fur-trimmed coat and cap.\par
\par
Jo heard Amy panting after her run, stamping her feet and blowing her\par
fingers, as she tried to put her skates on; but Jo never turned, and\par
went slowly zigzagging down the river, taking a bitter, unhappy sort of\par
satisfaction in her sister's troubles. She had cherished her anger till\par
it grew strong, and took possession of her, as evil thoughts and\par
feelings always do, unless cast out at once. As Laurie turned the bend,\par
he shouted back,--\par
\par
"Keep near the shore; it isn't safe in the middle."\par
\par
Jo heard, but Amy was just struggling to her feet, and did not catch a\par
word. Jo glanced over her shoulder, and the little demon she was\par
harboring said in her ear,--\par
\par
"No matter whether she heard or not, let her take care of herself."\par
\par
Laurie had vanished round the bend; Jo was just at the turn, and Amy,\par
far behind, striking out toward the smoother ice in the middle of the\par
river. For a minute Jo stood still, with a strange feeling at her heart;\par
then she resolved to go on, but something held and turned her round,\par
just in time to see Amy throw up her hands and go down, with the sudden\par
crash of rotten ice, the splash of water, and a cry that made Jo's heart\par
stand still with fear. She tried to call Laurie, but her voice was gone;\par
she tried to rush forward, but her feet seemed to have no strength in\par
them; and, for a second, she could only stand motionless, staring, with\par
a terror-stricken face, at the little blue hood above the black water.\par
Something rushed swiftly by her, and Laurie's voice cried out,--\par
\par
"Bring a rail; quick, quick!"\par
\par
How she did it, she never knew; but for the next few minutes she worked\par
as if possessed, blindly obeying Laurie, who was quite self-possessed,\par
and, lying flat, held Amy up by his arm and hockey till Jo dragged a\par
rail from the fence, and together they got the child out, more\par
frightened than hurt.\par
\par
           [Illustration: Held Amy up by his arms and hockey]\par
\par
"Now then, we must walk her home as fast as we can; pile our things on\par
her, while I get off these confounded skates," cried Laurie, wrapping\par
his coat round Amy, and tugging away at the straps, which never seemed\par
so intricate before.\par
\par
Shivering, dripping, and crying, they got Amy home; and, after an\par
exciting time of it, she fell asleep, rolled in blankets, before a hot\par
fire. During the bustle Jo had scarcely spoken; but flown about, looking\par
pale and wild, with her things half off, her dress torn, and her hands\par
cut and bruised by ice and rails, and refractory buckles. When Amy was\par
comfortably asleep, the house quiet, and Mrs. March sitting by the bed,\par
she called Jo to her, and began to bind up the hurt hands.\par
\par
"Are you sure she is safe?" whispered Jo, looking remorsefully at the\par
golden head, which might have been swept away from her sight forever\par
under the treacherous ice.\par
\par
"Quite safe, dear; she is not hurt, and won't even take cold, I think,\par
you were so sensible in covering and getting her home quickly," replied\par
her mother cheerfully.\par
\par
"Laurie did it all; I only let her go. Mother, if she _should_ die, it\par
would be my fault"; and Jo dropped down beside the bed, in a passion of\par
penitent tears, telling all that had happened, bitterly condemning her\par
hardness of heart, and sobbing out her gratitude for being spared the\par
heavy punishment which might have come upon her.\par
\par
"It's my dreadful temper! I try to cure it; I think I have, and then it\par
breaks out worse than ever. O mother, what shall I do? what shall I do?"\par
cried poor Jo, in despair.\par
\par
"Watch and pray, dear; never get tired of trying; and never think it is\par
impossible to conquer your fault," said Mrs. March, drawing the blowzy\par
head to her shoulder, and kissing the wet cheek so tenderly that Jo\par
cried harder than ever.\par
\par
"You don't know, you can't guess how bad it is! It seems as if I could\par
do anything when I'm in a passion; I get so savage, I could hurt any\par
one, and enjoy it. I'm afraid I _shall_ do something dreadful some day,\par
and spoil my life, and make everybody hate me. O mother, help me, do\par
help me!"\par
\par
"I will, my child, I will. Don't cry so bitterly, but remember this day,\par
and resolve, with all your soul, that you will never know another like\par
it. Jo, dear, we all have our temptations, some far greater than yours,\par
and it often takes us all our lives to conquer them. You think your\par
temper is the worst in the world; but mine used to be just like it."\par
\par
"Yours, mother? Why, you are never angry!" and, for the moment, Jo\par
forgot remorse in surprise.\par
\par
"I've been trying to cure it for forty years, and have only succeeded in\par
controlling it. I am angry nearly every day of my life, Jo; but I have\par
learned not to show it; and I still hope to learn not to feel it, though\par
it may take me another forty years to do so."\par
\par
The patience and the humility of the face she loved so well was a better\par
lesson to Jo than the wisest lecture, the sharpest reproof. She felt\par
comforted at once by the sympathy and confidence given her; the\par
knowledge that her mother had a fault like hers, and tried to mend it,\par
made her own easier to bear and strengthened her resolution to cure it;\par
though forty years seemed rather a long time to watch and pray, to a\par
girl of fifteen.\par
\par
"Mother, are you angry when you fold your lips tight together, and go\par
out of the room sometimes, when Aunt March scolds, or people worry\par
you?" asked Jo, feeling nearer and dearer to her mother than ever\par
before.\par
\par
"Yes, I've learned to check the hasty words that rise to my lips; and\par
when I feel that they mean to break out against my will, I just go away\par
a minute, and give myself a little shake, for being so weak and wicked,"\par
answered Mrs. March, with a sigh and a smile, as she smoothed and\par
fastened up Jo's dishevelled hair.\par
\par
"How did you learn to keep still? That is what troubles me--for the\par
sharp words fly out before I know what I'm about; and the more I say the\par
worse I get, till it's a pleasure to hurt people's feelings, and say\par
dreadful things. Tell me how you do it, Marmee dear."\par
\par
"My good mother used to help me--"\par
\par
"As you do us--" interrupted Jo, with a grateful kiss.\par
\par
"But I lost her when I was a little older than you are, and for years\par
had to struggle on alone, for I was too proud to confess my weakness to\par
any one else. I had a hard time, Jo, and shed a good many bitter tears\par
over my failures; for, in spite of my efforts, I never seemed to get on.\par
Then your father came, and I was so happy that I found it easy to be\par
good. But by and by, when I had four little daughters round me, and we\par
were poor, then the old trouble began again; for I am not patient by\par
nature, and it tried me very much to see my children wanting anything."\par
\par
"Poor mother! what helped you then?"\par
\par
"Your father, Jo. He never loses patience,--never doubts or\par
complains,--but always hopes, and works and waits so cheerfully, that\par
one is ashamed to do otherwise before him. He helped and comforted me,\par
and showed me that I must try to practise all the virtues I would have\par
my little girls possess, for I was their example. It was easier to try\par
for your sakes than for my own; a startled or surprised look from one of\par
you, when I spoke sharply, rebuked me more than any words could have\par
done; and the love, respect, and confidence of my children was the\par
sweetest reward I could receive for my efforts to be the woman I would\par
have them copy."\par
\par
"O mother, if I'm ever half as good as you, I shall be satisfied," cried\par
Jo, much touched.\par
\par
"I hope you will be a great deal better, dear; but you must keep watch\par
over your 'bosom enemy,' as father calls it, or it may sadden, if not\par
spoil your life. You have had a warning; remember it, and try with heart\par
and soul to master this quick temper, before it brings you greater\par
sorrow and regret than you have known to-day."\par
\par
"I will try, mother; I truly will. But you must help me, remind me, and\par
keep me from flying out. I used to see father sometimes put his finger\par
on his lips, and look at you with a very kind, but sober face, and you\par
always folded your lips tight or went away: was he reminding you then?"\par
asked Jo softly.\par
\par
"Yes; I asked him to help me so, and he never forgot it, but saved me\par
from many a sharp word by that little gesture and kind look."\par
\par
Jo saw that her mother's eyes filled and her lips trembled, as she\par
spoke; and, fearing that she had said too much, she whispered anxiously,\par
"Was it wrong to watch you, and to speak of it? I didn't mean to be\par
rude, but it's so comfortable to say all I think to you, and feel so\par
safe and happy here."\par
\par
"My Jo, you may say anything to your mother, for it is my greatest\par
happiness and pride to feel that my girls confide in me, and know how\par
much I love them."\par
\par
"I thought I'd grieved you."\par
\par
"No, dear; but speaking of father reminded me how much I miss him, how\par
much I owe him, and how faithfully I should watch and work to keep his\par
little daughters safe and good for him."\par
\par
"Yet you told him to go, mother, and didn't cry when he went, and never\par
complain now, or seem as if you needed any help," said Jo, wondering.\par
\par
"I gave my best to the country I love, and kept my tears till he was\par
gone. Why should I complain, when we both have merely done our duty and\par
will surely be the happier for it in the end? If I don't seem to need\par
help, it is because I have a better friend, even than father, to comfort\par
and sustain me. My child, the troubles and temptations of your life are\par
beginning, and may be many; but you can overcome and outlive them all if\par
you learn to feel the strength and tenderness of your Heavenly Father as\par
you do that of your earthly one. The more you love and trust Him, the\par
nearer you will feel to Him, and the less you will depend on human power\par
and wisdom. His love and care never tire or change, can never be taken\par
from you, but may become the source of life-long peace, happiness, and\par
strength. Believe this heartily, and go to God with all your little\par
cares, and hopes, and sins, and sorrows, as freely and confidingly as\par
you come to your mother."\par
\par
Jo's only answer was to hold her mother close, and, in the silence which\par
followed, the sincerest prayer she had ever prayed left her heart\par
without words; for in that sad, yet happy hour, she had learned not only\par
the bitterness of remorse and despair, but the sweetness of self-denial\par
and self-control; and, led by her mother's hand, she had drawn nearer to\par
the Friend who welcomes every child with a love stronger than that of\par
any father, tenderer than that of any mother.\par
\par
Amy stirred, and sighed in her sleep; and, as if eager to begin at once\par
to mend her fault, Jo looked up with an expression on her face which it\par
had never worn before.\par
\par
"I let the sun go down on my anger; I wouldn't forgive her, and to-day,\par
if it hadn't been for Laurie, it might have been too late! How could I\par
be so wicked?" said Jo, half aloud, as she leaned over her sister,\par
softly stroking the wet hair scattered on the pillow.\par
\par
As if she heard, Amy opened her eyes, and held out her arms, with a\par
smile that went straight to Jo's heart. Neither said a word, but they\par
hugged one another close, in spite of the blankets, and everything was\par
forgiven and forgotten in one hearty kiss.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
              [Illustration: Packing the go abroady trunk]\par
\par
                                  IX.\par
\par
                        MEG GOES TO VANITY FAIR.\par
\par
\par
"I do think it was the most fortunate thing in the world that those\par
children should have the measles just now," said Meg, one April day, as\par
she stood packing the "go abroady" trunk in her room, surrounded by her\par
sisters.\par
\par
"And so nice of Annie Moffat not to forget her promise. A whole\par
fortnight of fun will be regularly splendid," replied Jo, looking like a\par
windmill, as she folded skirts with her long arms.\par
\par
"And such lovely weather; I'm so glad of that," added Beth, tidily\par
sorting neck and hair ribbons in her best box, lent for the great\par
occasion.\par
\par
"I wish I was going to have a fine time, and wear all these nice\par
things," said Amy, with her mouth full of pins, as she artistically\par
replenished her sister's cushion.\par
\par
"I wish you were all going; but, as you can't, I shall keep my\par
adventures to tell you when I come back. I'm sure it's the least I can\par
do, when you have been so kind, lending me things, and helping me get\par
ready," said Meg, glancing round the room at the very simple outfit,\par
which seemed nearly perfect in their eyes.\par
\par
"What did mother give you out of the treasure-box?" asked Amy, who had\par
not been present at the opening of a certain cedar chest, in which Mrs.\par
March kept a few relics of past splendor, as gifts for her girls when\par
the proper time came.\par
\par
"A pair of silk stockings, that pretty carved fan, and a lovely blue\par
sash. I wanted the violet silk; but there isn't time to make it over, so\par
I must be contented with my old tarlatan."\par
\par
"It will look nicely over my new muslin skirt, and the sash will set it\par
off beautifully. I wish I hadn't smashed my coral bracelet, for you\par
might have had it," said Jo, who loved to give and lend, but whose\par
possessions were usually too dilapidated to be of much use.\par
\par
"There is a lovely old-fashioned pearl set in the treasure-box; but\par
mother said real flowers were the prettiest ornament for a young girl,\par
and Laurie promised to send me all I want," replied Meg. "Now, let me\par
see; there's my new gray walking-suit--just curl up the feather in my\par
hat, Beth,--then my poplin, for Sunday, and the small party,--it looks\par
heavy for spring, doesn't it? The violet silk would be so nice; oh,\par
dear!"\par
\par
"Never mind; you've got the tarlatan for the big party, and you always\par
look like an angel in white," said Amy, brooding over the little store\par
of finery in which her soul delighted.\par
\par
"It isn't low-necked, and it doesn't sweep enough, but it will have to\par
do. My blue house-dress looks so well, turned and freshly trimmed, that\par
I feel as if I'd got a new one. My silk sacque isn't a bit the fashion,\par
and my bonnet doesn't look like Sallie's; I didn't like to say\par
anything, but I was sadly disappointed in my umbrella. I told mother\par
black, with a white handle, but she forgot, and bought a green one, with\par
a yellowish handle. It's strong and neat, so I ought not to complain,\par
but I know I shall feel ashamed of it beside Annie's silk one with a\par
gold top," sighed Meg, surveying the little umbrella with great\par
disfavor.\par
\par
"Change it," advised Jo.\par
\par
"I won't be so silly, or hurt Marmee's feelings, when she took so much\par
pains to get my things. It's a nonsensical notion of mine, and I'm not\par
going to give up to it. My silk stockings and two pairs of new gloves\par
are my comfort. You are a dear, to lend me yours, Jo. I feel so rich,\par
and sort of elegant, with two new pairs, and the old ones cleaned up for\par
common;" and Meg took a refreshing peep at her glove-box.\par
\par
"Annie Moffat has blue and pink bows on her night-caps; would you put\par
some on mine?" she asked, as Beth brought up a pile of snowy muslins,\par
fresh from Hannah's hands.\par
\par
"No, I wouldn't; for the smart caps won't match the plain gowns, without\par
any trimming on them. Poor folks shouldn't rig," said Jo decidedly.\par
\par
"I wonder if I shall _ever_ be happy enough to have real lace on my\par
clothes, and bows on my caps?" said Meg impatiently.\par
\par
"You said the other day that you'd be perfectly happy if you could only\par
go to Annie Moffat's," observed Beth, in her quiet way.\par
\par
"So I did! Well, I _am_ happy, and I _won't_ fret; but it does seem as\par
if the more one gets the more one wants, doesn't it? There, now, the\par
trays are ready, and everything in but my ball-dress, which I shall\par
leave for mother to pack," said Meg, cheering up, as she glanced from\par
the half-filled trunk to the many-times pressed and mended white\par
tarlatan, which she called her "ball-dress," with an important air.\par
\par
The next day was fine, and Meg departed, in style, for a fortnight of\par
novelty and pleasure. Mrs. March had consented to the visit rather\par
reluctantly, fearing that Margaret would come back more discontented\par
than she went. But she had begged so hard, and Sallie had promised to\par
take good care of her, and a little pleasure seemed so delightful after\par
a winter of irksome work, that the mother yielded, and the daughter went\par
to take her first taste of fashionable life.\par
\par
The Moffats _were_ very fashionable, and simple Meg was rather daunted,\par
at first, by the splendor of the house and the elegance of its\par
occupants. But they were kindly people, in spite of the frivolous life\par
they led, and soon put their guest at her ease. Perhaps Meg felt,\par
without understanding why, that they were not particularly cultivated or\par
intelligent people, and that all their gilding could not quite conceal\par
the ordinary material of which they were made. It certainly was\par
agreeable to fare sumptuously, drive in a fine carriage, wear her best\par
frock every day, and do nothing but enjoy herself. It suited her\par
exactly; and soon she began to imitate the manners and conversation of\par
those about her; to put on little airs and graces, use French phrases,\par
crimp her hair, take in her dresses, and talk about the fashions as well\par
as she could. The more she saw of Annie Moffat's pretty things, the more\par
she envied her, and sighed to be rich. Home now looked bare and dismal\par
as she thought of it, work grew harder than ever, and she felt that she\par
was a very destitute and much-injured girl, in spite of the new gloves\par
and silk stockings.\par
\par
She had not much time for repining, however, for the three young girls\par
were busily employed in "having a good time." They shopped, walked,\par
rode, and called all day; went to theatres and operas, or frolicked at\par
home in the evening; for Annie had many friends, and knew how to\par
entertain them. Her older sisters were very fine young ladies, and one\par
was engaged, which was extremely interesting and romantic, Meg thought.\par
Mr. Moffat was a fat, jolly old gentleman, who knew her father; and Mrs.\par
Moffat, a fat, jolly old lady, who took as great a fancy to Meg as her\par
daughter had done. Every one petted her; and "Daisy," as they called\par
her, was in a fair way to have her head turned.\par
\par
When the evening for the "small party" came, she found that the poplin\par
wouldn't do at all, for the other girls were putting on thin dresses,\par
and making themselves very fine indeed; so out came the tarlatan,\par
looking older, limper, and shabbier than ever beside Sallie's crisp new\par
one. Meg saw the girls glance at it and then at one another, and her\par
cheeks began to burn, for, with all her gentleness, she was very proud.\par
No one said a word about it, but Sallie offered to dress her hair, and\par
Annie to tie her sash, and Belle, the engaged sister, praised her white\par
arms; but in their kindness Meg saw only pity for her poverty, and her\par
heart felt very heavy as she stood by herself, while the others laughed,\par
chattered, and flew about like gauzy butterflies. The hard, bitter\par
feeling was getting pretty bad, when the maid brought in a box of\par
flowers. Before she could speak, Annie had the cover off, and all were\par
exclaiming at the lovely roses, heath, and fern within.\par
\par
"It's for Belle, of course; George always sends her some, but these are\par
altogether ravishing," cried Annie, with a great sniff.\par
\par
"They are for Miss March, the man said. And here's a note," put in the\par
maid, holding it to Meg.\par
\par
"What fun! Who are they from? Didn't know you had a lover," cried the\par
girls, fluttering about Meg in a high state of curiosity and surprise.\par
\par
"The note is from mother, and the flowers from Laurie," said Meg simply,\par
yet much gratified that he had not forgotten her.\par
\par
"Oh, indeed!" said Annie, with a funny look, as Meg slipped the note\par
into her pocket, as a sort of talisman against envy, vanity, and false\par
pride; for the few loving words had done her good, and the flowers\par
cheered her up by their beauty.\par
\par
Feeling almost happy again, she laid by a few ferns and roses for\par
herself, and quickly made up the rest in dainty bouquets for the\par
breasts, hair, or skirts of her friends, offering them so prettily that\par
Clara, the elder sister, told her she was "the sweetest little thing she\par
ever saw;" and they looked quite charmed with her small attention.\par
Somehow the kind act finished her despondency; and when all the rest\par
went to show themselves to Mrs. Moffat, she saw a happy, bright-eyed\par
face in the mirror, as she laid her ferns against her rippling hair, and\par
fastened the roses in the dress that didn't strike her as so _very_\par
shabby now.\par
\par
She enjoyed herself very much that evening, for she danced to her\par
heart's content; every one was very kind, and she had three compliments.\par
Annie made her sing, and some one said she had a remarkably fine voice;\par
Major Lincoln asked who "the fresh little girl, with the beautiful\par
eyes," was; and Mr. Moffat insisted on dancing with her, because she\par
"didn't dawdle, but had some spring in her," as he gracefully expressed\par
it. So, altogether, she had a very nice time, till she overheard a bit\par
of a conversation, which disturbed her extremely. She was sitting just\par
inside the conservatory, waiting for her partner to bring her an ice,\par
when she heard a voice ask, on the other side of the flowery wall,--\par
\par
"How old is he?"\par
\par
"Sixteen or seventeen, I should say," replied another voice.\par
\par
"It would be a grand thing for one of those girls, wouldn't it? Sallie\par
says they are very intimate now, and the old man quite dotes on them."\par
\par
"Mrs M. has made her plans, I dare say, and will play her cards well,\par
early as it is. The girl evidently doesn't think of it yet," said Mrs.\par
Moffat.\par
\par
"She told that fib about her mamma, as if she did know, and colored up\par
when the flowers came, quite prettily. Poor thing! she'd be so nice if\par
she was only got up in style. Do you think she'd be offended if we\par
offered to lend her a dress for Thursday?" asked another voice.\par
\par
"She's proud, but I don't believe she'd mind, for that dowdy tarlatan is\par
all she has got. She may tear it to-night, and that will be a good\par
excuse for offering a decent one."\par
\par
"We'll see. I shall ask young Laurence, as a compliment to her, and\par
we'll have fun about it afterward."\par
\par
                 [Illustration: Meg's partner appeared]\par
\par
Here Meg's partner appeared, to find her looking much flushed and rather\par
agitated. She _was_ proud, and her pride was useful just then, for it\par
helped her hide her mortification, anger, and disgust at what she had\par
just heard; for, innocent and unsuspicious as she was, she could not\par
help understanding the gossip of her friends. She tried to forget it,\par
but could not, and kept repeating to herself, "Mrs. M. has made her\par
plans," "that fib about her mamma," and "dowdy tarlatan," till she was\par
ready to cry, and rush home to tell her troubles and ask for advice. As\par
that was impossible, she did her best to seem gay; and, being rather\par
excited, she succeeded so well that no one dreamed what an effort she\par
was making. She was very glad when it was all over, and she was quiet in\par
her bed, where she could think and wonder and fume till her head ached\par
and her hot cheeks were cooled by a few natural tears. Those foolish,\par
yet well-meant words, had opened a new world to Meg, and much disturbed\par
the peace of the old one, in which, till now, she had lived as happily\par
as a child. Her innocent friendship with Laurie was spoilt by the silly\par
speeches she had overheard; her faith in her mother was a little shaken\par
by the worldly plans attributed to her by Mrs. Moffat, who judged others\par
by herself; and the sensible resolution to be contented with the simple\par
wardrobe which suited a poor man's daughter, was weakened by the\par
unnecessary pity of girls who thought a shabby dress one of the greatest\par
calamities under heaven.\par
\par
Poor Meg had a restless night, and got up heavy-eyed, unhappy, half\par
resentful toward her friends, and half ashamed of herself for not\par
speaking out frankly, and setting everything right. Everybody dawdled\par
that morning, and it was noon before the girls found energy enough even\par
to take up their worsted work. Something in the manner of her friends\par
struck Meg at once; they treated her with more respect, she thought;\par
took quite a tender interest in what she said, and looked at her with\par
eyes that plainly betrayed curiosity. All this surprised and flattered\par
her, though she did not understand it till Miss Belle looked up from her\par
writing, and said, with a sentimental air,--\par
\par
"Daisy, dear, I've sent an invitation to your friend, Mr. Laurence, for\par
Thursday. We should like to know him, and it's only a proper compliment\par
to you."\par
\par
Meg colored, but a mischievous fancy to tease the girls made her reply\par
demurely,--\par
\par
"You are very kind, but I'm afraid he won't come."\par
\par
"Why not, _chérie_?" asked Miss Belle.\par
\par
"He's too old."\par
\par
"My child, what do you mean? What is his age, I beg to know!" cried Miss\par
Clara.\par
\par
"Nearly seventy, I believe," answered Meg, counting stitches, to hide\par
the merriment in her eyes.\par
\par
"You sly creature! Of course we meant the young man," exclaimed Miss\par
Belle, laughing.\par
\par
"There isn't any; Laurie is only a little boy," and Meg laughed also at\par
the queer look which the sisters exchanged as she thus described her\par
supposed lover.\par
\par
"About your age," Nan said.\par
\par
"Nearer my sister Jo's; _I_ am seventeen in August," returned Meg,\par
tossing her head.\par
\par
"It's very nice of him to send you flowers, isn't it?" said Annie,\par
looking wise about nothing.\par
\par
"Yes, he often does, to all of us; for their house is full, and we are\par
so fond of them. My mother and old Mr. Laurence are friends, you know,\par
so it is quite natural that we children should play together;" and Meg\par
hoped they would say no more.\par
\par
"It's evident Daisy isn't out yet," said Miss Clara to Belle, with a\par
nod.\par
\par
"Quite a pastoral state of innocence all round," returned Miss Belle,\par
with a shrug.\par
\par
"I'm going out to get some little matters for my girls; can I do\par
anything for you, young ladies?" asked Mrs. Moffat, lumbering in, like\par
an elephant, in silk and lace.\par
\par
"No, thank you, ma'am," replied Sallie. "I've got my new pink silk for\par
Thursday, and don't want a thing."\par
\par
"Nor I,--" began Meg, but stopped, because it occurred to her that she\par
_did_ want several things, and could not have them.\par
\par
"What shall you wear?" asked Sallie.\par
\par
"My old white one again, if I can mend it fit to be seen; it got sadly\par
torn last night," said Meg, trying to speak quite easily, but feeling\par
very uncomfortable.\par
\par
"Why don't you send home for another?" said Sallie, who was not an\par
observing young lady.\par
\par
"I haven't got any other." It cost Meg an effort to say that, but Sallie\par
did not see it, and exclaimed, in amiable surprise,--\par
\par
"Only that? How funny--" She did not finish her speech, for Belle shook\par
her head at her, and broke in, saying kindly,--\par
\par
"Not at all; where is the use of having a lot of dresses when she isn't\par
out? There's no need of sending home, Daisy, even if you had a dozen,\par
for I've got a sweet blue silk laid away, which I've outgrown, and you\par
shall wear it, to please me, won't you, dear?"\par
\par
"You are very kind, but I don't mind my old dress, if you don't; it does\par
well enough for a little girl like me," said Meg.\par
\par
"Now do let me please myself by dressing you up in style. I admire to do\par
it, and you'd be a regular little beauty, with a touch here and there. I\par
sha'n't let any one see you till you are done, and then we'll burst upon\par
them like Cinderella and her godmother, going to the ball," said Belle,\par
in her persuasive tone.\par
\par
Meg couldn't refuse the offer so kindly made, for a desire to see if she\par
would be "a little beauty" after touching up, caused her to accept, and\par
forget all her former uncomfortable feelings towards the Moffats.\par
\par
On the Thursday evening, Belle shut herself up with her maid; and,\par
between them, they turned Meg into a fine lady. They crimped and curled\par
her hair, they polished her neck and arms with some fragrant powder,\par
touched her lips with coralline salve, to make them redder, and Hortense\par
would have added "a _soupçon_ of rouge," if Meg had not rebelled. They\par
laced her into a sky-blue dress, which was so tight she could hardly\par
breathe, and so low in the neck that modest Meg blushed at herself in\par
the mirror. A set of silver filagree was added, bracelets, necklace,\par
brooch, and even ear-rings, for Hortense tied them on, with a bit of\par
pink silk, which did not show. A cluster of tea-rosebuds at the bosom,\par
and a _ruche_, reconciled Meg to the display of her pretty white\par
shoulders, and a pair of high-heeled blue silk boots satisfied the last\par
wish of her heart. A laced handkerchief, a plumy fan, and a bouquet in a\par
silver holder finished her off; and Miss Belle surveyed her with the\par
satisfaction of a little girl with a newly dressed doll.\par
\par
"Mademoiselle is charmante, très jolie, is she not?" cried Hortense,\par
clasping her hands in an affected rapture.\par
\par
"Come and show yourself," said Miss Belle, leading the way to the room\par
where the others were waiting.\par
\par
As Meg went rustling after, with her long skirts trailing, her ear-rings\par
tinkling, her curls waving, and her heart beating, she felt as if her\par
"fun" had really begun at last, for the mirror had plainly told her that\par
she _was_ "a little beauty." Her friends repeated the pleasing phrase\par
enthusiastically; and, for several minutes, she stood, like the jackdaw\par
in the fable, enjoying her borrowed plumes, while the rest chattered\par
like a party of magpies.\par
\par
"While I dress, do you drill her, Nan, in the management of her skirt,\par
and those French heels, or she will trip herself up. Take your silver\par
butterfly, and catch up that long curl on the left side of her head,\par
Clara, and don't any of you disturb the charming work of my hands," said\par
Belle, as she hurried away, looking well pleased with her success.\par
\par
"I'm afraid to go down, I feel so queer and stiff and half-dressed,"\par
said Meg to Sallie, as the bell rang, and Mrs. Moffat sent to ask the\par
young ladies to appear at once.\par
\par
"You don't look a bit like yourself, but you are very nice. I'm nowhere\par
beside you, for Belle has heaps of taste, and you're quite French, I\par
assure you. Let your flowers hang; don't be so careful of them, and be\par
sure you don't trip," returned Sallie, trying not to care that Meg was\par
prettier than herself.\par
\par
                 [Illustration: Asked to be introduced]\par
\par
Keeping that warning carefully in mind, Margaret got safely down stairs,\par
and sailed into the drawing-rooms, where the Moffats and a few early\par
guests were assembled. She very soon discovered that there is a charm\par
about fine clothes which attracts a certain class of people, and secures\par
their respect. Several young ladies, who had taken no notice of her\par
before, were very affectionate all of a sudden; several young gentlemen,\par
who had only stared at her at the other party, now not only stared, but\par
asked to be introduced, and said all manner of foolish but agreeable\par
things to her; and several old ladies, who sat on sofas, and criticised\par
the rest of the party, inquired who she was, with an air of interest.\par
She heard Mrs. Moffat reply to one of them,--\par
\par
"Daisy March--father a colonel in the army--one of our first families,\par
but reverses of fortune, you know; intimate friends of the Laurences;\par
sweet creature, I assure you; my Ned is quite wild about her."\par
\par
"Dear me!" said the old lady, putting up her glass for another\par
observation of Meg, who tried to look as if she had not heard, and been\par
rather shocked at Mrs. Moffat's fibs.\par
\par
The "queer feeling" did not pass away, but she imagined herself acting\par
the new part of fine lady, and so got on pretty well, though the tight\par
dress gave her a side-ache, the train kept getting under her feet, and\par
she was in constant fear lest her ear-rings should fly off, and get lost\par
or broken. She was flirting her fan and laughing at the feeble jokes of\par
a young gentleman who tried to be witty, when she suddenly stopped\par
laughing and looked confused; for, just opposite, she saw Laurie. He was\par
staring at her with undisguised surprise, and disapproval also, she\par
thought; for, though he bowed and smiled, yet something in his honest\par
eyes made her blush, and wish she had her old dress on. To complete her\par
confusion, she saw Belle nudge Annie, and both glance from her to\par
Laurie, who, she was happy to see, looked unusually boyish and shy.\par
\par
"Silly creatures, to put such thoughts into my head! I won't care for\par
it, or let it change me a bit," thought Meg, and rustled across the room\par
to shake hands with her friend.\par
\par
"I'm glad you came, I was afraid you wouldn't," she said, with her most\par
grown-up air.\par
\par
"Jo wanted me to come, and tell her how you looked, so I did;" answered\par
Laurie, without turning his eyes upon her, though he half smiled at her\par
maternal tone.\par
\par
"What shall you tell her?" asked Meg, full of curiosity to know his\par
opinion of her, yet feeling ill at ease with him, for the first time.\par
\par
"I shall say I didn't know you; for you look so grown-up, and unlike\par
yourself, I'm quite afraid of you," he said, fumbling at his\par
glove-button.\par
\par
"How absurd of you! The girls dressed me up for fun, and I rather like\par
it. Wouldn't Jo stare if she saw me?" said Meg, bent on making him say\par
whether he thought her improved or not.\par
\par
"Yes, I think she would," returned Laurie gravely.\par
\par
"Don't you like me so?" asked Meg.\par
\par
"No, I don't," was the blunt reply.\par
\par
"Why not?" in an anxious tone.\par
\par
He glanced at her frizzled head, bare shoulders, and fantastically\par
trimmed dress, with an expression that abashed her more than his answer,\par
which had not a particle of his usual politeness about it.\par
\par
"I don't like fuss and feathers."\par
\par
That was altogether too much from a lad younger than herself; and Meg\par
walked away, saying petulantly,--\par
\par
"You are the rudest boy I ever saw."\par
\par
Feeling very much ruffled, she went and stood at a quiet window, to cool\par
her cheeks, for the tight dress gave her an uncomfortably brilliant\par
color. As she stood there, Major Lincoln passed by; and, a minute after,\par
she heard him saying to his mother,--\par
\par
"They are making a fool of that little girl; I wanted you to see her,\par
but they have spoilt her entirely; she's nothing but a doll, to-night."\par
\par
"Oh, dear!" sighed Meg; "I wish I'd been sensible, and worn my own\par
things; then I should not have disgusted other people, or felt so\par
uncomfortable and ashamed myself."\par
\par
She leaned her forehead on the cool pane, and stood half hidden by the\par
curtains, never minding that her favorite waltz had begun, till some one\par
touched her; and, turning, she saw Laurie, looking penitent, as he said,\par
with his very best bow, and his hand out,--\par
\par
"Please forgive my rudeness, and come and dance with me."\par
\par
"I'm afraid it will be too disagreeable to you," said Meg, trying to\par
look offended, and failing entirely.\par
\par
"Not a bit of it; I'm dying to do it. Come, I'll be good; I don't like\par
your gown, but I do think you are--just splendid;" and he waved his\par
hands, as if words failed to express his admiration.\par
\par
Meg smiled and relented, and whispered, as they stood waiting to catch\par
the time,--\par
\par
"Take care my skirt don't trip you up; it's the plague of my life, and I\par
was a goose to wear it."\par
\par
"Pin it round your neck, and then it will be useful," said Laurie,\par
looking down at the little blue boots, which he evidently approved of.\par
\par
Away they went, fleetly and gracefully; for, having practised at home,\par
they were well matched, and the blithe young couple were a pleasant\par
sight to see, as they twirled merrily round and round, feeling more\par
friendly than ever after their small tiff.\par
\par
"Laurie, I want you to do me a favor; will you?" said Meg, as he stood\par
fanning her, when her breath gave out, which it did very soon, though\par
she would not own why.\par
\par
"Won't I!" said Laurie, with alacrity.\par
\par
"Please don't tell them at home about my dress to-night. They won't\par
understand the joke, and it will worry mother."\par
\par
"Then why did you do it?" said Laurie's eyes, so plainly that Meg\par
hastily added,--\par
\par
"I shall tell them, myself, all about it, and ''fess' to mother how\par
silly I've been. But I'd rather do it myself; so you'll not tell, will\par
you?"\par
\par
"I give you my word I won't; only what shall I say when they ask me?"\par
\par
"Just say I looked pretty well, and was having a good time."\par
\par
"I'll say the first, with all my heart; but how about the other? You\par
don't look as if you were having a good time; are you?" and Laurie\par
looked at her with an expression which made her answer, in a whisper,--\par
\par
"No; not just now. Don't think I'm horrid; I only wanted a little fun,\par
but this sort doesn't pay, I find, and I'm getting tired of it."\par
\par
"Here comes Ned Moffat; what does he want?" said Laurie, knitting his\par
black brows, as if he did not regard his young host in the light of a\par
pleasant addition to the party.\par
\par
"He put his name down for three dances, and I suppose he's coming for\par
them. What a bore!" said Meg, assuming a languid air, which amused\par
Laurie immensely.\par
\par
He did not speak to her again till supper-time, when he saw her drinking\par
champagne with Ned and his friend Fisher, who were behaving "like a pair\par
of fools," as Laurie said to himself, for he felt a brotherly sort of\par
right to watch over the Marches, and fight their battles whenever a\par
defender was needed.\par
\par
                    [Illustration: I wouldn't, Meg]\par
\par
"You'll have a splitting headache to-morrow, if you drink much of that.\par
I wouldn't Meg; your mother doesn't like it, you know," he whispered,\par
leaning over her chair, as Ned turned to refill her glass, and Fisher\par
stooped to pick up her fan.\par
\par
"I'm not Meg, to-night; I'm 'a doll,' who does all sorts of crazy\par
things. To-morrow I shall put away my 'fuss and feathers,' and be\par
desperately good again," she answered, with an affected little laugh.\par
\par
"Wish to-morrow was here, then," muttered Laurie, walking off,\par
ill-pleased at the change he saw in her.\par
\par
Meg danced and flirted, chattered and giggled, as the other girls did;\par
after supper she undertook the German, and blundered through it, nearly\par
upsetting her partner with her long skirt, and romping in a way that\par
scandalized Laurie, who looked on and meditated a lecture. But he got no\par
chance to deliver it, for Meg kept away from him till he came to say\par
good-night.\par
\par
"Remember!" she said, trying to smile, for the splitting headache had\par
already begun.\par
\par
"Silence à la mort," replied Laurie, with a melodramatic flourish, as he\par
went away.\par
\par
This little bit of by-play excited Annie's curiosity; but Meg was too\par
tired for gossip, and went to bed, feeling as if she had been to a\par
masquerade, and hadn't enjoyed herself as much as she expected. She was\par
sick all the next day, and on Saturday went home, quite used up with her\par
fortnight's fun, and feeling that she had "sat in the lap of luxury"\par
long enough.\par
\par
"It does seem pleasant to be quiet, and not have company manners on all\par
the time. Home _is_ a nice place, though it isn't splendid," said Meg,\par
looking about her with a restful expression, as she sat with her mother\par
and Jo on the Sunday evening.\par
\par
"I'm glad to hear you say so, dear, for I was afraid home would seem\par
dull and poor to you, after your fine quarters," replied her mother, who\par
had given her many anxious looks that day; for motherly eyes are quick\par
to see any change in children's faces.\par
\par
Meg had told her adventures gayly, and said over and over what a\par
charming time she had had; but something still seemed to weigh upon her\par
spirits, and, when the younger girls were gone to bed, she sat\par
thoughtfully staring at the fire, saying little, and looking worried. As\par
the clock struck nine, and Jo proposed bed, Meg suddenly left her chair,\par
and, taking Beth's stool, leaned her elbows on her mother's knee, saying\par
bravely,--\par
\par
"Marmee, I want to ''fess.'"\par
\par
"I thought so; what is it, dear?"\par
\par
"Shall I go away?" asked Jo discreetly.\par
\par
"Of course not; don't I always tell you everything? I was ashamed to\par
speak of it before the children, but I want you to know all the dreadful\par
things I did at the Moffat's."\par
\par
"We are prepared," said Mrs. March, smiling, but looking a little\par
anxious.\par
\par
"I told you they dressed me up, but I didn't tell you that they powdered\par
and squeezed and frizzled, and made me look like a fashion-plate. Laurie\par
thought I wasn't proper; I know he did, though he didn't say so, and one\par
man called me 'a doll.' I knew it was silly, but they flattered me, and\par
said I was a beauty, and quantities of nonsense, so I let them make a\par
fool of me."\par
\par
"Is that all?" asked Jo, as Mrs. March looked silently at the downcast\par
face of her pretty daughter, and could not find it in her heart to blame\par
her little follies.\par
\par
"No; I drank champagne and romped and tried to flirt, and was altogether\par
abominable," said Meg self-reproachfully.\par
\par
"There is something more, I think;" and Mrs. March smoothed the soft\par
cheek, which suddenly grew rosy, as Meg answered slowly,--\par
\par
"Yes; it's very silly, but I want to tell it, because I hate to have\par
people say and think such things about us and Laurie."\par
\par
Then she told the various bits of gossip she had heard at the Moffats;\par
and, as she spoke, Jo saw her mother fold her lips tightly, as if ill\par
pleased that such ideas should be put into Meg's innocent mind.\par
\par
"Well, if that isn't the greatest rubbish I ever heard," cried Jo\par
indignantly. "Why didn't you pop out and tell them so, on the spot?"\par
\par
"I couldn't, it was so embarrassing for me. I couldn't help hearing, at\par
first, and then I was so angry and ashamed, I didn't remember that I\par
ought to go away."\par
\par
"Just wait till _I_ see Annie Moffat, and I'll show you how to settle\par
such ridiculous stuff. The idea of having 'plans,' and being kind to\par
Laurie, because he's rich, and may marry us by and by! Won't he shout,\par
when I tell him what those silly things say about us poor children?" and\par
Jo laughed, as if, on second thoughts, the thing struck her as a good\par
joke.\par
\par
"If you tell Laurie, I'll never forgive you! She mustn't, must she,\par
mother?" said Meg, looking distressed.\par
\par
"No; never repeat that foolish gossip, and forget it as soon as you\par
can," said Mrs. March gravely. "I was very unwise to let you go among\par
people of whom I know so little,--kind, I dare say, but worldly,\par
ill-bred, and full of these vulgar ideas about young people. I am more\par
sorry than I can express for the mischief this visit may have done you,\par
Meg."\par
\par
"Don't be sorry, I won't let it hurt me; I'll forget all the bad, and\par
remember only the good; for I did enjoy a great deal, and thank you very\par
much for letting me go. I'll not be sentimental or dissatisfied, mother;\par
I know I'm a silly little girl, and I'll stay with you till I'm fit to\par
take care of myself. But it _is_ nice to be praised and admired, and I\par
can't help saying I like it," said Meg, looking half ashamed of the\par
confession.\par
\par
"That is perfectly natural, and quite harmless, if the liking does not\par
become a passion, and lead one to do foolish or unmaidenly things. Learn\par
to know and value the praise which is worth having, and to excite the\par
admiration of excellent people by being modest as well as pretty, Meg."\par
\par
Margaret sat thinking a moment, while Jo stood with her hands behind\par
her, looking both interested and a little perplexed; for it was a new\par
thing to see Meg blushing and talking about admiration, lovers, and\par
things of that sort; and Jo felt as if, during that fortnight, her\par
sister had grown up amazingly, and was drifting away from her into a\par
world where she could not follow.\par
\par
"Mother, do you have 'plans,' as Mrs. Moffat said?" asked Meg bashfully.\par
\par
"Yes, my dear, I have a great many; all mothers do, but mine differ\par
somewhat from Mrs. Moffat's, I suspect. I will tell you some of them,\par
for the time has come when a word may set this romantic little head and\par
heart of yours right, on a very serious subject. You are young, Meg, but\par
not too young to understand me; and mothers' lips are the fittest to\par
speak of such things to girls like you. Jo, your turn will come in time,\par
perhaps, so listen to my 'plans,' and help me carry them out, if they\par
are good."\par
\par
Jo went and sat on one arm of the chair, looking as if she thought they\par
were about to join in some very solemn affair. Holding a hand of each,\par
and watching the two young faces wistfully, Mrs. March said, in her\par
serious yet cheery way,--\par
\par
      [Illustration: Holding a hand of each, Mrs. March said, &c.]\par
\par
"I want my daughters to be beautiful, accomplished, and good; to be\par
admired, loved, and respected; to have a happy youth, to be well and\par
wisely married, and to lead useful, pleasant lives, with as little care\par
and sorrow to try them as God sees fit to send. To be loved and chosen\par
by a good man is the best and sweetest thing which can happen to a\par
woman; and I sincerely hope my girls may know this beautiful experience.\par
It is natural to think of it, Meg; right to hope and wait for it, and\par
wise to prepare for it; so that, when the happy time comes, you may\par
feel ready for the duties and worthy of the joy. My dear girls, I _am_\par
ambitious for you, but not to have you make a dash in the world,--marry\par
rich men merely because they are rich, or have splendid houses, which\par
are not homes because love is wanting. Money is a needful and precious\par
thing,--and, when well used, a noble thing,--but I never want you to\par
think it is the first or only prize to strive for. I'd rather see you\par
poor men's wives, if you were happy, beloved, contented, than queens on\par
thrones, without self-respect and peace."\par
\par
"Poor girls don't stand any chance, Belle says, unless they put\par
themselves forward," sighed Meg.\par
\par
"Then we'll be old maids," said Jo stoutly.\par
\par
"Right, Jo; better be happy old maids than unhappy wives, or unmaidenly\par
girls, running about to find husbands," said Mrs. March decidedly.\par
"Don't be troubled, Meg; poverty seldom daunts a sincere lover. Some of\par
the best and most honored women I know were poor girls, but so\par
love-worthy that they were not allowed to be old maids. Leave these\par
things to time; make this home happy, so that you may be fit for homes\par
of your own, if they are offered you, and contented here if they are\par
not. One thing remember, my girls: mother is always ready to be your\par
confidant, father to be your friend; and both of us trust and hope that\par
our daughters, whether married or single, will be the pride and comfort\par
of our lives."\par
\par
"We will, Marmee, we will!" cried both, with all their hearts, as she\par
bade them good-night.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                   X.\par
\par
                          THE P. C. AND P. O.\par
\par
\par
As spring came on, a new set of amusements became the fashion, and the\par
lengthening days gave long afternoons for work and play of all sorts.\par
The garden had to be put in order, and each sister had a quarter of the\par
little plot to do what she liked with. Hannah used to say, "I'd know\par
which each of them gardings belonged to, ef I see 'em in Chiny;" and so\par
she might, for the girls' tastes differed as much as their characters.\par
Meg's had roses and heliotrope, myrtle, and a little orange-tree in it.\par
Jo's bed was never alike two seasons, for she was always trying\par
experiments; this year it was to be a plantation of sun-flowers, the\par
seeds of which cheerful and aspiring plant were to feed "Aunt\par
Cockle-top" and her family of chicks. Beth had old-fashioned, fragrant\par
flowers in her garden,--sweet peas and mignonette, larkspur, pinks,\par
pansies, and southernwood, with chickweed for the bird, and catnip for\par
the pussies. Amy had a bower in hers,--rather small and earwiggy, but\par
very pretty to look at,--with honeysuckles and morning-glories hanging\par
their colored horns and bells in graceful wreaths all over it; tall,\par
white lilies, delicate ferns, and as many brilliant, picturesque plants\par
as would consent to blossom there.\par
\par
Gardening, walks, rows on the river, and flower-hunts employed the fine\par
days; and for rainy ones, they had house diversions,--some old, some\par
new,--all more or less original. One of these was the "P. C."; for, as\par
secret societies were the fashion, it was thought proper to have one;\par
and, as all of the girls admired Dickens, they called themselves the\par
Pickwick Club. With a few interruptions, they had kept this up for a\par
year, and met every Saturday evening in the big garret, on which\par
occasions the ceremonies were as follows: Three chairs were arranged in\par
a row before a table, on which was a lamp, also four white badges, with\par
a big "P. C." in different colors on each, and the weekly newspaper,\par
called "The Pickwick Portfolio," to which all contributed something;\par
while Jo, who revelled in pens and ink, was the editor. At seven\par
o'clock, the four members ascended to the club-room, tied their badges\par
round their heads, and took their seats with great solemnity. Meg, as\par
the eldest, was Samuel Pickwick; Jo, being of a literary turn, Augustus\par
Snodgrass; Beth, because she was round and rosy, Tracy Tupman, and Amy,\par
who was always trying to do what she couldn't, was Nathaniel Winkle.\par
Pickwick, the president, read the paper, which was filled with original\par
tales, poetry, local news, funny advertisements, and hints, in which\par
they good-naturedly reminded each other of their faults and\par
short-comings.\par
\par
                      [Illustration: Mr. Pickwick]\par
\par
On one occasion, Mr. Pickwick put on a pair of spectacles without any\par
glasses, rapped upon the table, hemmed, and, having stared hard at Mr.\par
Snodgrass, who was tilting back in his chair, till he arranged himself\par
properly, began to read:--\par
\par
\par
                        The Pickwick Portfolio.\par
\par
                              MAY 20, 18--\par
\par
                             Poet's Corner.\par
\par
                            ANNIVERSARY ODE.\par
\par
        Again we meet to celebrate\par
          With badge and solemn rite,\par
        Our fifty-second anniversary,\par
          In Pickwick Hall, to-night.\par
\par
        We all are here in perfect health,\par
          None gone from our small band;\par
        Again we see each well-known face,\par
          And press each friendly hand.\par
\par
        Our Pickwick, always at his post,\par
          With reverence we greet,\par
        As, spectacles on nose, he reads\par
          Our well-filled weekly sheet.\par
\par
        Although he suffers from a cold,\par
          We joy to hear him speak,\par
        For words of wisdom from him fall,\par
          In spite of croak or squeak.\par
\par
        Old six-foot Snodgrass looms on high,\par
          With elephantine grace,\par
        And beams upon the company,\par
          With brown and jovial face.\par
\par
        Poetic fire lights up his eye,\par
          He struggles 'gainst his lot.\par
        Behold ambition on his brow,\par
          And on his nose a blot!\par
\par
        Next our peaceful Tupman comes,\par
          So rosy, plump, and sweet.\par
        Who chokes with laughter at the puns,\par
          And tumbles off his seat.\par
\par
        Prim little Winkle too is here,\par
          With every hair in place,\par
        A model of propriety,\par
          Though he hates to wash his face.\par
\par
        The year is gone, we still unite\par
          To joke and laugh and read,\par
        And tread the path of literature\par
          That doth to glory lead.\par
\par
        Long may our paper prosper well,\par
          Our club unbroken be,\par
        And coming years their blessings pour\par
          On the useful, gay "P. C."\par
\par
                               A. SNODGRASS.\par
\par
\par
                          THE MASKED MARRIAGE.\par
\par
                           A TALE OF VENICE.\par
\par
    Gondola after gondola swept up to the marble steps, and left its\par
    lovely load to swell the brilliant throng that filled the\par
    stately halls of Count de Adelon. Knights and ladies, elves and\par
    pages, monks and flower-girls, all mingled gayly in the dance.\par
    Sweet voices and rich melody filled the air; and so with mirth\par
    and music the masquerade went on.\par
\par
    "Has your Highness seen the Lady Viola to-night?" asked a\par
    gallant troubadour of the fairy queen who floated down the hall\par
    upon his arm.\par
\par
    "Yes; is she not lovely, though so sad! Her dress is well\par
    chosen, too, for in a week she weds Count Antonio, whom she\par
    passionately hates."\par
\par
    "By my faith, I envy him. Yonder he comes, arrayed like a\par
    bridegroom, except the black mask. When that is off we shall see\par
    how he regards the fair maid whose heart he cannot win, though\par
    her stern father bestows her hand," returned the troubadour.\par
\par
    "'Tis whispered that she loves the young English artist who\par
    haunts her steps, and is spurned by the old count," said the\par
    lady, as they joined the dance.\par
\par
    The revel was at its height when a priest appeared, and,\par
    withdrawing the young pair to an alcove hung with purple velvet,\par
    he motioned them to kneel. Instant silence fell upon the gay\par
    throng; and not a sound, but the dash of fountains or the rustle\par
    of orange-groves sleeping in the moonlight, broke the hush, as\par
    Count de Adelon spoke thus:--\par
\par
    "My lords and ladies, pardon the ruse by which I have gathered\par
    you here to witness the marriage of my daughter. Father, we wait\par
    your services."\par
\par
    All eyes turned toward the bridal party, and a low murmur of\par
    amazement went through the throng, for neither bride nor groom\par
    removed their masks. Curiosity and wonder possessed all hearts,\par
    but respect restrained all tongues till the holy rite was over.\par
    Then the eager spectators gathered round the count, demanding an\par
    explanation.\par
\par
    "Gladly would I give it if I could; but I only know that it was\par
    the whim of my timid Viola, and I yielded to it. Now, my\par
    children, let the play end. Unmask, and receive my blessing."\par
\par
    But neither bent the knee; for the young bridegroom replied, in\par
    a tone that startled all listeners, as the mask fell, disclosing\par
    the noble face of Ferdinand Devereux, the artist lover; and,\par
    leaning on the breast where now flashed the star of an English\par
    earl, was the lovely Viola, radiant with joy and beauty.\par
\par
    "My lord, you scornfully bade me claim your daughter when I\par
    could boast as high a name and vast a fortune as the Count\par
    Antonio. I can do more; for even your ambitious soul cannot\par
    refuse the Earl of Devereux and De Vere, when he gives his\par
    ancient name and boundless wealth in return for the beloved hand\par
    of this fair lady, now my wife."\par
\par
    The count stood like one changed to stone; and, turning to the\par
    bewildered crowd, Ferdinand added, with a gay smile of triumph,\par
    "To you, my gallant friends, I can only wish that your wooing\par
    may prosper as mine has done; and that you may all win as fair a\par
    bride as I have, by this masked marriage."\par
\par
