                             WEEKLY REPORT.\par
\par
        Meg--Good.\par
        Jo--Bad.\par
        Beth--Very good.\par
        Amy--Middling.\par
\par
\par
As the President finished reading the paper (which I beg leave to assure\par
my readers is a _bona fide_ copy of one written by _bona fide_ girls\par
once upon a time), a round of applause followed, and then Mr. Snodgrass\par
rose to make a proposition.\par
\par
"Mr. President and gentlemen," he began, assuming a parliamentary\par
attitude and tone, "I wish to propose the admission of a new\par
member,--one who highly deserves the honor, would be deeply grateful for\par
it, and would add immensely to the spirit of the club, the literary\par
value of the paper, and be no end jolly and nice. I propose Mr. Theodore\par
Laurence as an honorary member of the P. C. Come now, do have him."\par
\par
Jo's sudden change of tone made the girls laugh; but all looked rather\par
anxious, and no one said a word, as Snodgrass took his seat.\par
\par
"We'll put it to vote," said the President. "All in favor of this motion\par
please to manifest it by saying 'Ay.'"\par
\par
A loud response from Snodgrass, followed, to everybody's surprise, by a\par
timid one from Beth.\par
\par
"Contrary minded say 'No.'"\par
\par
Meg and Amy were contrary minded; and Mr. Winkle rose to say, with great\par
elegance, "We don't wish any boys; they only joke and bounce about. This\par
is a ladies' club, and we wish to be private and proper."\par
\par
"I'm afraid he'll laugh at our paper, and make fun of us afterward,"\par
observed Pickwick, pulling the little curl on her forehead, as she\par
always did when doubtful.\par
\par
Up rose Snodgrass, very much in earnest. "Sir, I give you my word as a\par
gentleman, Laurie won't do anything of the sort. He likes to write, and\par
he'll give a tone to our contributions, and keep us from being\par
sentimental, don't you see? We can do so little for him, and he does so\par
much for us, I think the least we can do is to offer him a place here,\par
and make him welcome if he comes."\par
\par
This artful allusion to benefits conferred brought Tupman to his feet,\par
looking as if he had quite made up his mind.\par
\par
"Yes, we ought to do it, even if we _are_ afraid. I say he _may_ come,\par
and his grandpa, too, if he likes."\par
\par
This spirited burst from Beth electrified the club, and Jo left her\par
seat to shake hands approvingly. "Now then, vote again. Everybody\par
remember it's our Laurie, and say 'Ay!'" cried Snodgrass excitedly.\par
\par
"Ay! ay! ay!" replied three voices at once.\par
\par
"Good! Bless you! Now, as there's nothing like 'taking time by the\par
_fetlock_,' as Winkle characteristically observes, allow me to present\par
the new member;" and, to the dismay of the rest of the club, Jo threw\par
open the door of the closet, and displayed Laurie sitting on a rag-bag,\par
flushed and twinkling with suppressed laughter.\par
\par
          [Illustration: Jo threw open the door of the closet]\par
\par
"You rogue! you traitor! Jo, how could you?" cried the three girls, as\par
Snodgrass led her friend triumphantly forth; and, producing both a chair\par
and a badge, installed him in a jiffy.\par
\par
"The coolness of you two rascals is amazing," began Mr. Pickwick, trying\par
to get up an awful frown, and only succeeding in producing an amiable\par
smile. But the new member was equal to the occasion; and, rising, with a\par
grateful salutation to the Chair, said, in the most engaging manner,\par
"Mr. President and ladies,--I beg pardon, gentlemen,--allow me to\par
introduce myself as Sam Weller, the very humble servant of the club."\par
\par
"Good! good!" cried Jo, pounding with the handle of the old warming-pan\par
on which she leaned.\par
\par
"My faithful friend and noble patron," continued Laurie, with a wave of\par
the hand, "who has so flatteringly presented me, is not to be blamed for\par
the base stratagem of to-night. I planned it, and she only gave in after\par
lots of teasing."\par
\par
"Come now, don't lay it all on yourself; you know I proposed the\par
cupboard," broke in Snodgrass, who was enjoying the joke amazingly.\par
\par
"Never you mind what she says. I'm the wretch that did it, sir," said\par
the new member, with a Welleresque nod to Mr. Pickwick. "But on my\par
honor, I never will do so again, and henceforth _dewote_ myself to the\par
interest of this immortal club."\par
\par
"Hear! hear!" cried Jo, clashing the lid of the warming-pan like a\par
cymbal.\par
\par
"Go on, go on!" added Winkle and Tupman, while the President bowed\par
benignly.\par
\par
"I merely wish to say, that as a slight token of my gratitude for the\par
honor done me, and as a means of promoting friendly relations between\par
adjoining nations, I have set up a post-office in the hedge in the lower\par
corner of the garden; a fine, spacious building, with padlocks on the\par
doors, and every convenience for the mails,--also the females, if I may\par
be allowed the expression. It's the old martin-house; but I've stopped\par
up the door, and made the roof open, so it will hold all sorts of\par
things, and save our valuable time. Letters, manuscripts, books, and\par
bundles can be passed in there; and, as each nation has a key, it will\par
be uncommonly nice, I fancy. Allow me to present the club key; and,\par
with many thanks for your favor, take my seat."\par
\par
Great applause as Mr. Weller deposited a little key on the table, and\par
subsided; the warming-pan clashed and waved wildly, and it was some time\par
before order could be restored. A long discussion followed, and every\par
one came out surprising, for every one did her best; so it was an\par
unusually lively meeting, and did not adjourn till a late hour, when it\par
broke up with three shrill cheers for the new member.\par
\par
No one ever regretted the admittance of Sam Weller, for a more devoted,\par
well-behaved, and jovial member no club could have. He certainly did add\par
"spirit" to the meetings, and "a tone" to the paper; for his orations\par
convulsed his hearers, and his contributions were excellent, being\par
patriotic, classical, comical, or dramatic, but never sentimental. Jo\par
regarded them as worthy of Bacon, Milton, or Shakespeare; and remodelled\par
her own works with good effect, she thought.\par
\par
The P. O. was a capital little institution, and flourished wonderfully,\par
for nearly as many queer things passed through it as through the real\par
office. Tragedies and cravats, poetry and pickles, garden-seeds and long\par
letters, music and gingerbread, rubbers, invitations, scoldings and\par
puppies. The old gentleman liked the fun, and amused himself by sending\par
odd bundles, mysterious messages, and funny telegrams; and his gardener,\par
who was smitten with Hannah's charms, actually sent a love-letter to\par
Jo's care. How they laughed when the secret came out, never dreaming how\par
many love-letters that little post-office would hold in the years to\par
come!\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
           [Illustration: Jo spent the morning on the river]\par
\par
                                  XI.\par
\par
                              EXPERIMENTS.\par
\par
\par
"The first of June! The Kings are off to the seashore to-morrow, and I'm\par
free. Three months' vacation,--how I shall enjoy it!" exclaimed Meg,\par
coming home one warm day to find Jo laid upon the sofa in an unusual\par
state of exhaustion, while Beth took off her dusty boots, and Amy made\par
lemonade for the refreshment of the whole party.\par
\par
"Aunt March went to-day, for which, oh, be joyful!" said Jo. "I was\par
mortally afraid she'd ask me to go with her; if she had, I should have\par
felt as if I ought to do it; but Plumfield is about as gay as a\par
churchyard, you know, and I'd rather be excused. We had a flurry getting\par
the old lady off, and I had a fright every time she spoke to me, for I\par
was in such a hurry to be through that I was uncommonly helpful and\par
sweet, and feared she'd find it impossible to part from me. I quaked\par
till she was fairly in the carriage, and had a final fright, for, as it\par
drove off, she popped out her head, saying, 'Josy-phine, won't you--?'\par
I didn't hear any more, for I basely turned and fled; I did actually\par
run, and whisked round the corner, where I felt safe."\par
\par
"Poor old Jo! she came in looking as if bears were after her," said\par
Beth, as she cuddled her sister's feet with a motherly air.\par
\par
"Aunt March is a regular samphire, is she not?" observed Amy, tasting\par
her mixture critically.\par
\par
"She means _vampire_, not sea-weed; but it doesn't matter; it's too warm\par
to be particular about one's parts of speech," murmured Jo.\par
\par
"What shall you do all your vacation?" asked Amy, changing the subject,\par
with tact.\par
\par
"I shall lie abed late, and do nothing," replied Meg, from the depths of\par
the rocking-chair. "I've been routed up early all winter, and had to\par
spend my days working for other people; so now I'm going to rest and\par
revel to my heart's content."\par
\par
"No," said Jo; "that dozy way wouldn't suit me. I've laid in a heap of\par
books, and I'm going to improve my shining hours reading on my perch in\par
the old apple-tree, when I'm not having l------"\par
\par
"Don't say 'larks!'" implored Amy, as a return snub for the "samphire"\par
correction.\par
\par
"I'll say 'nightingales,' then, with Laurie; that's proper and\par
appropriate, since he's a warbler."\par
\par
"Don't let us do any lessons, Beth, for a while, but play all the time,\par
and rest, as the girls mean to," proposed Amy.\par
\par
"Well, I will, if mother doesn't mind. I want to learn some new songs,\par
and my children need fitting up for the summer; they are dreadfully out\par
of order, and really suffering for clothes."\par
\par
"May we, mother?" asked Meg, turning to Mrs. March, who sat sewing, in\par
what they called "Marmee's corner."\par
\par
"You may try your experiment for a week, and see how you like it. I\par
think by Saturday night you will find that all play and no work is as\par
bad as all work and no play."\par
\par
"Oh, dear, no! it will be delicious, I'm sure," said Meg complacently.\par
\par
"I now propose a toast, as my 'friend and pardner, Sairy Gamp,' says.\par
Fun forever, and no grubbing!" cried Jo, rising, glass in hand, as the\par
lemonade went round.\par
\par
They all drank it merrily, and began the experiment by lounging for the\par
rest of the day. Next morning, Meg did not appear till ten o'clock; her\par
solitary breakfast did not taste nice, and the room seemed lonely and\par
untidy; for Jo had not filled the vases, Beth had not dusted, and Amy's\par
books lay scattered about. Nothing was neat and pleasant but "Marmee's\par
corner," which looked as usual; and there Meg sat, to "rest and read,"\par
which meant yawn, and imagine what pretty summer dresses she would get\par
with her salary. Jo spent the morning on the river, with Laurie, and the\par
afternoon reading and crying over "The Wide, Wide World," up in the\par
apple-tree. Beth began by rummaging everything out of the big closet,\par
where her family resided; but, getting tired before half done, she left\par
her establishment topsy-turvy, and went to her music, rejoicing that she\par
had no dishes to wash. Amy arranged her bower, put on her best white\par
frock, smoothed her curls, and sat down to draw, under the honeysuckles,\par
hoping some one would see and inquire who the young artist was. As no\par
one appeared but an inquisitive daddy-long-legs, who examined her work\par
with interest, she went to walk, got caught in a shower, and came home\par
dripping.\par
\par
                  [Illustration: Amy sat down to draw]\par
\par
At tea-time they compared notes, and all agreed that it had been a\par
delightful, though unusually long day. Meg, who went shopping in the\par
afternoon, and got a "sweet blue muslin," had discovered, after she had\par
cut the breadths off, that it wouldn't wash, which mishap made her\par
slightly cross. Jo had burnt the skin off her nose boating, and got a\par
raging headache by reading too long. Beth was worried by the confusion\par
of her closet, and the difficulty of learning three or four songs at\par
once; and Amy deeply regretted the damage done her frock, for Katy\par
Brown's party was to be the next day; and now, like Flora McFlimsey, she\par
had "nothing to wear." But these were mere trifles; and they assured\par
their mother that the experiment was working finely. She smiled, said\par
nothing, and, with Hannah's help, did their neglected work, keeping home\par
pleasant, and the domestic machinery running smoothly. It was\par
astonishing what a peculiar and uncomfortable state of things was\par
produced by the "resting and revelling" process. The days kept getting\par
longer and longer; the weather was unusually variable, and so were\par
tempers; an unsettled feeling possessed every one, and Satan found\par
plenty of mischief for the idle hands to do. As the height of luxury,\par
Meg put out some of her sewing, and then found time hang so heavily that\par
she fell to snipping and spoiling her clothes, in her attempts to\par
furbish them up à la Moffat. Jo read till her eyes gave out, and she was\par
sick of books; got so fidgety that even good-natured Laurie had a\par
quarrel with her, and so reduced in spirits that she desperately wished\par
she had gone with Aunt March. Beth got on pretty well, for she was\par
constantly forgetting that it was to be _all play, and no work_, and\par
fell back into her old ways now and then; but something in the air\par
affected her, and, more than once, her tranquillity was much disturbed;\par
so much so, that, on one occasion, she actually shook poor dear Joanna,\par
and told her she was "a fright." Amy fared worst of all, for her\par
resources were small; and when her sisters left her to amuse and care\par
for herself, she soon found that accomplished and important little self\par
a great burden. She didn't like dolls, fairy-tales were childish, and\par
one couldn't draw all the time; tea-parties didn't amount to much,\par
neither did picnics, unless very well conducted. "If one could have a\par
fine house, full of nice girls, or go travelling, the summer would be\par
delightful; but to stay at home with three selfish sisters and a\par
grown-up boy was enough to try the patience of a Boaz," complained Miss\par
Malaprop, after several days devoted to pleasure, fretting, and _ennui_.\par
\par
No one would own that they were tired of the experiment; but, by Friday\par
night, each acknowledged to herself that she was glad the week was\par
nearly done. Hoping to impress the lesson more deeply, Mrs. March, who\par
had a good deal of humor, resolved to finish off the trial in an\par
appropriate manner; so she gave Hannah a holiday, and let the girls\par
enjoy the full effect of the play system.\par
\par
When they got up on Saturday morning, there was no fire in the kitchen,\par
no breakfast in the dining-room, and no mother anywhere to be seen.\par
\par
"Mercy on us! what _has_ happened?" cried Jo, staring about her in\par
dismay.\par
\par
Meg ran upstairs, and soon came back again, looking relieved, but rather\par
bewildered, and a little ashamed.\par
\par
"Mother isn't sick, only very tired, and she says she is going to stay\par
quietly in her room all day, and let us do the best we can. It's a very\par
queer thing for her to do, she doesn't act a bit like herself; but she\par
says it has been a hard week for her, so we mustn't grumble, but take\par
care of ourselves."\par
\par
"That's easy enough, and I like the idea; I'm aching for something to\par
do--that is, some new amusement, you know," added Jo quickly.\par
\par
In fact it _was_ an immense relief to them all to have a little work,\par
and they took hold with a will, but soon realized the truth of Hannah's\par
saying, "Housekeeping ain't no joke." There was plenty of food in the\par
larder, and, while Beth and Amy set the table, Meg and Jo got breakfast,\par
wondering, as they did so, why servants ever talked about hard work.\par
\par
"I shall take some up to mother, though she said we were not to think of\par
her, for she'd take care of herself," said Meg, who presided, and felt\par
quite matronly behind the teapot.\par
\par
So a tray was fitted out before any one began, and taken up, with the\par
cook's compliments. The boiled tea was very bitter, the omelette\par
scorched, and the biscuits speckled with saleratus; but Mrs. March\par
received her repast with thanks, and laughed heartily over it after Jo\par
was gone.\par
\par
"Poor little souls, they will have a hard time, I'm afraid; but they\par
won't suffer, and it will do them good," she said, producing the more\par
palatable viands with which she had provided herself, and disposing of\par
the bad breakfast, so that their feelings might not be hurt,--a motherly\par
little deception, for which they were grateful.\par
\par
Many were the complaints below, and great the chagrin of the head cook\par
at her failures. "Never mind, I'll get the dinner, and be servant; you\par
be mistress, keep your hands nice, see company, and give orders," said\par
Jo, who knew still less than Meg about culinary affairs.\par
\par
This obliging offer was gladly accepted; and Margaret retired to the\par
parlor, which she hastily put in order by whisking the litter under the\par
sofa, and shutting the blinds, to save the trouble of dusting. Jo, with\par
perfect faith in her own powers, and a friendly desire to make up the\par
quarrel, immediately put a note in the office, inviting Laurie to\par
dinner.\par
\par
"You'd better see what you have got before you think of having company,"\par
said Meg, when informed of the hospitable but rash act.\par
\par
"Oh, there's corned beef and plenty of potatoes; and I shall get some\par
asparagus, and a lobster, 'for a relish,' as Hannah says. We'll have\par
lettuce, and make a salad. I don't know how, but the book tells. I'll\par
have blanc-mange and strawberries for dessert; and coffee, too, if you\par
want to be elegant."\par
\par
"Don't try too many messes, Jo, for you can't make anything but\par
gingerbread and molasses candy, fit to eat. I wash my hands of the\par
dinner-party; and, since you have asked Laurie on your own\par
responsibility, you may just take care of him."\par
\par
"I don't want you to do anything but be civil to him, and help to the\par
pudding. You'll give me your advice if I get in a muddle, won't you?"\par
asked Jo, rather hurt.\par
\par
"Yes; but I don't know much, except about bread, and a few trifles. You\par
had better ask mother's leave before you order anything," returned Meg\par
prudently.\par
\par
"Of course I shall; I'm not a fool," and Jo went off in a huff at the\par
doubts expressed of her powers.\par
\par
"Get what you like, and don't disturb me; I'm going out to dinner, and\par
can't worry about things at home," said Mrs. March, when Jo spoke to\par
her. "I never enjoyed housekeeping, and I'm going to take a vacation\par
to-day, and read, write, go visiting, and amuse myself."\par
\par
The unusual spectacle of her busy mother rocking comfortably, and\par
reading, early in the morning, made Jo feel as if some natural\par
phenomenon had occurred, for an eclipse, an earthquake, or a volcanic\par
eruption would hardly have seemed stranger.\par
\par
"Everything is out of sorts, somehow," she said to herself, going down\par
stairs. "There's Beth crying; that's a sure sign that something is wrong\par
with this family. If Amy is bothering, I'll shake her."\par
\par
Feeling very much out of sorts herself, Jo hurried into the parlor to\par
find Beth sobbing over Pip, the canary, who lay dead in the cage, with\par
his little claws pathetically extended, as if imploring the food for\par
want of which he had died.\par
\par
"It's all my fault--I forgot him--there isn't a seed or a drop left. O\par
Pip! O Pip! how could I be so cruel to you?" cried Beth, taking the poor\par
thing in her hands, and trying to restore him.\par
\par
                     [Illustration: O Pip! O Pip!]\par
\par
Jo peeped into his half-open eye, felt his little heart, and finding him\par
stiff and cold, shook her head, and offered her domino-box for a coffin.\par
\par
"Put him in the oven, and maybe he will get warm and revive," said Amy\par
hopefully.\par
\par
"He's been starved, and he sha'n't be baked, now he's dead. I'll make\par
him a shroud, and he shall be buried in the garden; and I'll never have\par
another bird, never, my Pip! for I am too bad to own one," murmured\par
Beth, sitting on the floor with her pet folded in her hands.\par
\par
"The funeral shall be this afternoon, and we will all go. Now, don't\par
cry, Bethy; it's a pity, but nothing goes right this week, and Pip has\par
had the worst of the experiment. Make the shroud, and lay him in my box;\par
and, after the dinner-party, we'll have a nice little funeral," said Jo,\par
beginning to feel as if she had undertaken a good deal.\par
\par
Leaving the others to console Beth, she departed to the kitchen, which\par
was in a most discouraging state of confusion. Putting on a big apron,\par
she fell to work, and got the dishes piled up ready for washing, when\par
she discovered that the fire was out.\par
\par
"Here's a sweet prospect!" muttered Jo, slamming the stove-door open,\par
and poking vigorously among the cinders.\par
\par
Having rekindled the fire, she thought she would go to market while the\par
water heated. The walk revived her spirits; and, flattering herself that\par
she had made good bargains, she trudged home again, after buying a very\par
young lobster, some very old asparagus, and two boxes of acid\par
strawberries. By the time she got cleared up, the dinner arrived, and\par
the stove was red-hot. Hannah had left a pan of bread to rise, Meg had\par
worked it up early, set it on the hearth for a second rising, and\par
forgotten it. Meg was entertaining Sallie Gardiner in the parlor, when\par
the door flew open, and a floury, crocky, flushed, and dishevelled\par
figure appeared, demanding tartly,--\par
\par
"I say, isn't bread 'riz' enough when it runs over the pans?"\par
\par
Sallie began to laugh; but Meg nodded, and lifted her eyebrows as high\par
as they would go, which caused the apparition to vanish, and put the\par
sour bread into the oven without further delay. Mrs. March went out,\par
after peeping here and there to see how matters went, also saying a word\par
of comfort to Beth, who sat making a winding-sheet, while the dear\par
departed lay in state in the domino-box. A strange sense of helplessness\par
fell upon the girls as the gray bonnet vanished round the corner; and\par
despair seized them, when, a few minutes later, Miss Crocker appeared,\par
and said she'd come to dinner. Now, this lady was a thin, yellow\par
spinster, with a sharp nose and inquisitive eyes, who saw everything,\par
and gossiped about all she saw. They disliked her, but had been taught\par
to be kind to her, simply because she was old and poor, and had few\par
friends. So Meg gave her the easy-chair, and tried to entertain her,\par
while she asked questions, criticised everything, and told stories of\par
the people whom she knew.\par
\par
Language cannot describe the anxieties, experiences, and exertions which\par
Jo underwent that morning; and the dinner she served up became a\par
standing joke. Fearing to ask any more advice, she did her best alone,\par
and discovered that something more than energy and good-will is\par
necessary to make a cook. She boiled the asparagus for an hour, and was\par
grieved to find the heads cooked off and the stalks harder than ever.\par
The bread burnt black; for the salad-dressing so aggravated her, that\par
she let everything else go till she had convinced herself that she could\par
not make it fit to eat. The lobster was a scarlet mystery to her, but\par
she hammered and poked, till it was unshelled, and its meagre\par
proportions concealed in a grove of lettuce-leaves. The potatoes had to\par
be hurried, not to keep the asparagus waiting, and were not done at\par
last. The blanc-mange was lumpy, and the strawberries not as ripe as\par
they looked, having been skilfully "deaconed."\par
\par
"Well, they can eat beef, and bread and butter, if they are hungry; only\par
it's mortifying to have to spend your whole morning for nothing,"\par
thought Jo, as she rang the bell half an hour later than usual, and\par
stood, hot, tired, and dispirited, surveying the feast spread for\par
Laurie, accustomed to all sorts of elegance, and Miss Crocker, whose\par
curious eyes would mark all failures, and whose tattling tongue would\par
report them far and wide.\par
\par
Poor Jo would gladly have gone under the table, as one thing after\par
another was tasted and left; while Amy giggled, Meg looked distressed,\par
Miss Crocker pursed up her lips, and Laurie talked and laughed with all\par
his might, to give a cheerful tone to the festive scene. Jo's one strong\par
point was the fruit, for she had sugared it well, and had a pitcher of\par
rich cream to eat with it. Her hot cheeks cooled a trifle, and she drew\par
a long breath, as the pretty glass plates went round, and every one\par
looked graciously at the little rosy islands floating in a sea of cream.\par
Miss Crocker tasted first, made a wry face, and drank some water\par
hastily. Jo, who had refused, thinking there might not be enough, for\par
they dwindled sadly after the picking over, glanced at Laurie, but he\par
was eating away manfully, though there was a slight pucker about his\par
mouth, and he kept his eye fixed on his plate. Amy, who was fond of\par
delicate fare, took a heaping spoonful, choked, hid her face in her\par
napkin, and left the table precipitately.\par
\par
              [Illustration: Miss Crocker made a wry face]\par
\par
"Oh, what is it?" exclaimed Jo trembling.\par
\par
"Salt instead of sugar, and the cream is sour," replied Meg, with a\par
tragic gesture.\par
\par
Jo uttered a groan, and fell back in her chair; remembering that she had\par
given a last hasty powdering to the berries out of one of the two boxes\par
on the kitchen table, and had neglected to put the milk in the\par
refrigerator. She turned scarlet, and was on the verge of crying, when\par
she met Laurie's eyes, which _would_ look merry in spite of his heroic\par
efforts; the comical side of the affair suddenly struck her, and she\par
laughed till the tears ran down her cheeks. So did every one else, even\par
"Croaker," as the girls called the old lady; and the unfortunate dinner\par
ended gayly, with bread and butter, olives and fun.\par
\par
"I haven't strength of mind enough to clear up now, so we will sober\par
ourselves with a funeral," said Jo, as they rose; and Miss Crocker made\par
ready to go, being eager to tell the new story at another friend's\par
dinner-table.\par
\par
They did sober themselves, for Beth's sake; Laurie dug a grave under the\par
ferns in the grove, little Pip was laid in, with many tears, by his\par
tender-hearted mistress, and covered with moss, while a wreath of\par
violets and chickweed was hung on the stone which bore his epitaph,\par
composed by Jo, while she struggled with the dinner:--\par
\par
        "Here lies Pip March,\par
           Who died the 7th of June;\par
         Loved and lamented sore,\par
           And not forgotten soon."\par
\par
At the conclusion of the ceremonies, Beth retired to her room, overcome\par
with emotion and lobster; but there was no place of repose, for the beds\par
were not made, and she found her grief much assuaged by beating up\par
pillows and putting things in order. Meg helped Jo clear away the\par
remains of the feast, which took half the afternoon, and left them so\par
tired that they agreed to be contented with tea and toast for supper.\par
Laurie took Amy to drive, which was a deed of charity, for the sour\par
cream seemed to have had a bad effect upon her temper. Mrs. March came\par
home to find the three older girls hard at work in the middle of the\par
afternoon; and a glance at the closet gave her an idea of the success of\par
one part of the experiment.\par
\par
Before the housewives could rest, several people called, and there was a\par
scramble to get ready to see them; then tea must be got, errands done;\par
and one or two necessary bits of sewing neglected till the last minute.\par
As twilight fell, dewy and still, one by one they gathered in the porch\par
where the June roses were budding beautifully, and each groaned or\par
sighed as she sat down, as if tired or troubled.\par
\par
"What a dreadful day this has been!" begun Jo, usually the first to\par
speak.\par
\par
"It has seemed shorter than usual, but _so_ uncomfortable," said Meg.\par
\par
"Not a bit like home," added Amy.\par
\par
"It can't seem so without Marmee and little Pip," sighed Beth, glancing,\par
with full eyes, at the empty cage above her head.\par
\par
"Here's mother, dear, and you shall have another bird to-morrow, if you\par
want it."\par
\par
As she spoke, Mrs. March came and took her place among them, looking as\par
if her holiday had not been much pleasanter than theirs.\par
\par
"Are you satisfied with your experiment, girls, or do you want another\par
week of it?" she asked, as Beth nestled up to her, and the rest turned\par
toward her with brightening faces, as flowers turn toward the sun.\par
\par
"I don't!" cried Jo decidedly.\par
\par
"Nor I," echoed the others.\par
\par
"You think, then, that it is better to have a few duties, and live a\par
little for others, do you?"\par
\par
"Lounging and larking doesn't pay," observed Jo, shaking her head. "I'm\par
tired of it, and mean to go to work at something right off."\par
\par
"Suppose you learn plain cooking; that's a useful accomplishment, which\par
no woman should be without," said Mrs. March, laughing inaudibly at the\par
recollection of Jo's dinner-party; for she had met Miss Crocker, and\par
heard her account of it.\par
\par
"Mother, did you go away and let everything be, just to see how we'd get\par
on?" cried Meg, who had had suspicions all day.\par
\par
"Yes; I wanted you to see how the comfort of all depends on each doing\par
her share faithfully. While Hannah and I did your work, you got on\par
pretty well, though I don't think you were very happy or amiable; so I\par
thought, as a little lesson, I would show you what happens when every\par
one thinks only of herself. Don't you feel that it is pleasanter to help\par
one another, to have daily duties which make leisure sweet when it\par
comes, and to bear and forbear, that home may be comfortable and lovely\par
to us all?"\par
\par
"We do, mother, we do!" cried the girls.\par
\par
"Then let me advise you to take up your little burdens again; for though\par
they seem heavy sometimes, they are good for us, and lighten as we learn\par
to carry them. Work is wholesome, and there is plenty for every one; it\par
keeps us from _ennui_ and mischief, is good for health and spirits, and\par
gives us a sense of power and independence better than money or\par
fashion."\par
\par
"We'll work like bees, and love it too; see if we don't!" said Jo. "I'll\par
learn plain cooking for my holiday task; and the next dinner-party I\par
have shall be a success."\par
\par
"I'll make the set of shirts for father, instead of letting you do it,\par
Marmee. I can and I will, though I'm not fond of sewing; that will be\par
better than fussing over my own things, which are plenty nice enough as\par
they are," said Meg.\par
\par
"I'll do my lessons every day, and not spend so much time with my music\par
and dolls. I am a stupid thing, and ought to be studying, not playing,"\par
was Beth's resolution; while Amy followed their example by heroically\par
declaring, "I shall learn to make button-holes, and attend to my parts\par
of speech."\par
\par
"Very good! then I am quite satisfied with the experiment, and fancy\par
that we shall not have to repeat it; only don't go to the other extreme,\par
and delve like slaves. Have regular hours for work and play; make each\par
day both useful and pleasant, and prove that you understand the worth of\par
time by employing it well. Then youth will be delightful, old age will\par
bring few regrets, and life become a beautiful success, in spite of\par
poverty."\par
\par
"We'll remember, mother!" and they did.\par
\par
                  [Illustration: We'll work like bees]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                  XII.\par
\par
                             CAMP LAURENCE.\par
\par
\par
                 [Illustration: Beth was post-mistress]\par
\par
Beth was post-mistress, for, being most at home, she could attend to it\par
regularly, and dearly liked the daily task of unlocking the little door\par
and distributing the mail. One July day she came in with her hands full,\par
and went about the house leaving letters and parcels, like the penny\par
post.\par
\par
"Here's your posy, mother! Laurie never forgets that," she said, putting\par
the fresh nosegay in the vase that stood in "Marmee's corner," and was\par
kept supplied by the affectionate boy.\par
\par
"Miss Meg March, one letter and a glove," continued Beth, delivering the\par
articles to her sister, who sat near her mother, stitching wristbands.\par
\par
"Why, I left a pair over there, and here is only one," said Meg, looking\par
at the gray cotton glove.\par
\par
"Didn't you drop the other in the garden?"\par
\par
"No, I'm sure I didn't; for there was only one in the office."\par
\par
"I hate to have odd gloves! Never mind, the other may be found. My\par
letter is only a translation of the German song I wanted; I think Mr.\par
Brooke did it, for this isn't Laurie's writing."\par
\par
Mrs. March glanced at Meg, who was looking very pretty in her gingham\par
morning-gown, with the little curls blowing about her forehead, and very\par
womanly, as she sat sewing at her little work-table, full of tidy white\par
rolls; so unconscious of the thought in her mother's mind as she sewed\par
and sung, while her fingers flew, and her thoughts were busied with\par
girlish fancies as innocent and fresh as the pansies in her belt, that\par
Mrs. March smiled, and was satisfied.\par
\par
"Two letters for Doctor Jo, a book, and a funny old hat, which covered\par
the whole post-office, stuck outside," said Beth, laughing, as she went\par
into the study, where Jo sat writing.\par
\par
"What a sly fellow Laurie is! I said I wished bigger hats were the\par
fashion, because I burn my face every hot day. He said, 'Why mind the\par
fashion? Wear a big hat, and be comfortable!' I said I would if I had\par
one, and he has sent me this, to try me. I'll wear it, for fun, and show\par
him I _don't_ care for the fashion;" and, hanging the antique broad-brim\par
on a bust of Plato, Jo read her letters.\par
\par
One from her mother made her cheeks glow and her eyes fill, for it said\par
to her,--\par
\par
    "MY DEAR:\par
\par
    "I write a little word to tell you with how much satisfaction I\par
    watch your efforts to control your temper. You say nothing about\par
    your trials, failures, or successes, and think, perhaps, that no\par
    one sees them but the Friend whose help you daily ask, if I may\par
    trust the well-worn cover of your guide-book. _I_, too, have\par
    seen them all, and heartily believe in the sincerity of your\par
    resolution, since it begins to bear fruit. Go on, dear,\par
    patiently and bravely, and always believe that no one\par
    sympathizes more tenderly with you than your loving\par
\par
                                                           "MOTHER."\par
\par
"That does me good! that's worth millions of money and pecks of praise.\par
O Marmee, I do try! I will keep on trying, and not get tired, since I\par
have you to help me."\par
\par
Laying her head on her arms, Jo wet her little romance with a few happy\par
tears, for she _had_ thought that no one saw and appreciated her efforts\par
to be good; and this assurance was doubly precious, doubly encouraging,\par
because unexpected, and from the person whose commendation she most\par
valued. Feeling stronger than ever to meet and subdue her Apollyon, she\par
pinned the note inside her frock, as a shield and a reminder, lest she\par
be taken unaware, and proceeded to open her other letter, quite ready\par
for either good or bad news. In a big, dashing hand, Laurie wrote,--\par
\par
                "DEAR JO,\par
                 What ho!\par
\par
    Some English girls and boys are coming to see me to-morrow and\par
    I want to have a jolly time. If it's fine, I'm going to pitch\par
    my tent in Longmeadow, and row up the whole crew to lunch and\par
    croquet,--have a fire, make messes, gypsy fashion, and all\par
    sorts of larks. They are nice people, and like such things.\par
    Brooke will go, to keep us boys steady, and Kate Vaughn will\par
    play propriety for the girls. I want you all to come; can't let\par
    Beth off, at any price, and nobody shall worry her. Don't\par
    bother about rations,--I'll see to that, and everything\par
    else,--only do come, there's a good fellow!\par
\par
                "In a tearing hurry,\par
                 Yours ever, LAURIE."\par
\par
"Here's richness!" cried Jo, flying in to tell the news to Meg.\par
\par
"Of course we can go, mother? it will be such a help to Laurie, for I\par
can row, and Meg see to the lunch, and the children be useful in some\par
way."\par
\par
"I hope the Vaughns are not fine, grown-up people. Do you know anything\par
about them, Jo?" asked Meg.\par
\par
"Only that there are four of them. Kate is older than you, Fred and\par
Frank (twins) about my age, and a little girl (Grace), who is nine or\par
ten. Laurie knew them abroad, and liked the boys; I fancied, from the\par
way he primmed up his mouth in speaking of her, that he didn't admire\par
Kate much."\par
\par
"I'm so glad my French print is clean; it's just the thing, and so\par
becoming!" observed Meg complacently. "Have you anything decent, Jo?"\par
\par
"Scarlet and gray boating suit, good enough for me. I shall row and\par
tramp about, so I don't want any starch to think of. You'll come,\par
Bethy?"\par
\par
"If you won't let any of the boys talk to me."\par
\par
"Not a boy!"\par
\par
"I like to please Laurie; and I'm not afraid of Mr. Brooke, he is so\par
kind; but I don't want to play, or sing, or say anything. I'll work\par
hard, and not trouble any one; and you'll take care of me, Jo, so I'll\par
go."\par
\par
"That's my good girl; you do try to fight off your shyness, and I love\par
you for it. Fighting faults isn't easy, as I know; and a cheery word\par
kind of gives a lift. Thank you, mother," and Jo gave the thin cheek a\par
grateful kiss, more precious to Mrs. March than if it had given back the\par
rosy roundness of her youth.\par
\par
"I had a box of chocolate drops, and the picture I wanted to copy," said\par
Amy, showing her mail.\par
\par
"And I got a note from Mr. Laurence, asking me to come over and play to\par
him to-night, before the lamps are lighted, and I shall go," added Beth,\par
whose friendship with the old gentleman prospered finely.\par
\par
"Now let's fly round, and do double duty to-day, so that we can play\par
to-morrow with free minds," said Jo, preparing to replace her pen with a\par
broom.\par
\par
When the sun peeped into the girls' room early next morning, to promise\par
them a fine day, he saw a comical sight. Each had made such preparation\par
for the fête as seemed necessary and proper. Meg had an extra row of\par
little curl-papers across her forehead, Jo had copiously anointed her\par
afflicted face with cold cream, Beth had taken Joanna to bed with her to\par
atone for the approaching separation, and Amy had capped the climax by\par
putting a clothes-pin on her nose, to uplift the offending feature. It\par
was one of the kind artists use to hold the paper on their\par
drawing-boards, therefore quite appropriate and effective for the\par
purpose to which it was now put. This funny spectacle appeared to amuse\par
the sun, for he burst out with such radiance that Jo woke up, and\par
roused all her sisters by a hearty laugh at Amy's ornament.\par
\par
  [Illustration: Amy capped the climax by putting a clothes-pin on her\par
                                 nose]\par
\par
Sunshine and laughter were good omens for a pleasure party, and soon a\par
lively bustle began in both houses. Beth, who was ready first, kept\par
reporting what went on next door, and enlivened her sisters' toilets by\par
frequent telegrams from the window.\par
\par
"There goes the man with the tent! I see Mrs. Barker doing up the lunch\par
in a hamper and a great basket. Now Mr. Laurence is looking up at the\par
sky, and the weathercock; I wish he would go, too. There's Laurie,\par
looking like a sailor,--nice boy! Oh, mercy me! here's a carriage full\par
of people--a tall lady, a little girl, and two dreadful boys. One is\par
lame; poor thing, he's got a crutch. Laurie didn't tell us that. Be\par
quick, girls! it's getting late. Why, there is Ned Moffat, I do declare.\par
Look, Meg, isn't that the man who bowed to you one day, when we were\par
shopping?"\par
\par
"So it is. How queer that he should come. I thought he was at the\par
Mountains. There is Sallie; I'm glad she got back in time. Am I all\par
right, Jo?" cried Meg, in a flutter.\par
\par
"A regular daisy. Hold up your dress and put your hat straight; it looks\par
sentimental tipped that way, and will fly off at the first puff. Now,\par
then, come on!"\par
\par
"O Jo, you are not going to wear that awful hat? It's too absurd! You\par
shall _not_ make a guy of yourself," remonstrated Meg, as Jo tied down,\par
with a red ribbon, the broad-brimmed, old-fashioned Leghorn Laurie had\par
sent for a joke.\par
\par
"I just will, though, for it's capital,--so shady, light, and big. It\par
will make fun; and I don't mind being a guy if I'm comfortable." With\par
that Jo marched straight away, and the rest followed,--a bright little\par
band of sisters, all looking their best, in summer suits, with happy\par
faces under the jaunty hat-brims.\par
\par
Laurie ran to meet, and present them to his friends, in the most cordial\par
manner. The lawn was the reception-room, and for several minutes a\par
lively scene was enacted there. Meg was grateful to see that Miss Kate,\par
though twenty, was dressed with a simplicity which American girls would\par
do well to imitate; and she was much flattered by Mr. Ned's assurances\par
that he came especially to see her. Jo understood why Laurie "primmed up\par
his mouth" when speaking of Kate, for that young lady had a\par
stand-off-don't-touch-me air, which contrasted strongly with the free\par
and easy demeanor of the other girls. Beth took an observation of the\par
new boys, and decided that the lame one was not "dreadful," but gentle\par
and feeble, and she would be kind to him on that account. Amy found\par
Grace a well-mannered, merry little person; and after staring dumbly at\par
one another for a few minutes, they suddenly became very good friends.\par
\par
Tents, lunch, and croquet utensils having been sent on beforehand, the\par
party was soon embarked, and the two boats pushed off together, leaving\par
Mr. Laurence waving his hat on the shore. Laurie and Jo rowed one boat;\par
Mr. Brooke and Ned the other; while Fred Vaughn, the riotous twin, did\par
his best to upset both by paddling about in a wherry like a disturbed\par
water-bug. Jo's funny hat deserved a vote of thanks, for it was of\par
general utility; it broke the ice in the beginning, by producing a\par
laugh; it created quite a refreshing breeze, flapping to and fro, as she\par
rowed, and would make an excellent umbrella for the whole party, if a\par
shower came up, she said. Kate looked rather amazed at Jo's proceedings,\par
especially as she exclaimed "Christopher Columbus!" when she lost her\par
oar; and Laurie said, "My dear fellow, did I hurt you?" when he tripped\par
over her feet in taking his place. But after putting up her glass to\par
examine the queer girl several times, Miss Kate decided that she was\par
"odd, but rather clever," and smiled upon her from afar.\par
\par
              [Illustration: Mr. Laurence waving his hat]\par
\par
Meg, in the other boat, was delightfully situated, face to face with the\par
rowers, who both admired the prospect, and feathered their oars with\par
uncommon "skill and dexterity." Mr. Brooke was a grave, silent young\par
man, with handsome brown eyes and a pleasant voice. Meg liked his quiet\par
manners, and considered him a walking encyclopædia of useful knowledge.\par
He never talked to her much; but he looked at her a good deal, and she\par
felt sure that he did not regard her with aversion. Ned, being in\par
college, of course put on all the airs which Freshmen think it their\par
bounden duty to assume; he was not very wise, but very good-natured, and\par
altogether an excellent person to carry on a picnic. Sallie Gardiner was\par
absorbed in keeping her white piqué dress clean, and chattering with the\par
ubiquitous Fred, who kept Beth in constant terror by his pranks.\par
\par
It was not far to Longmeadow; but the tent was pitched and the wickets\par
down by the time they arrived. A pleasant green field, with three\par
wide-spreading oaks in the middle, and a smooth strip of turf for\par
croquet.\par
\par
"Welcome to Camp Laurence!" said the young host, as they landed, with\par
exclamations of delight.\par
\par
"Brooke is commander-in-chief; I am commissary-general; the other\par
fellows are staff-officers; and you, ladies, are company. The tent is\par
for your especial benefit, and that oak is your drawing-room; this is\par
the mess-room, and the third is the camp-kitchen. Now, let's have a game\par
before it gets hot, and then we'll see about dinner."\par
\par
Frank, Beth, Amy, and Grace sat down to watch the game played by the\par
other eight. Mr. Brooke chose Meg, Kate, and Fred; Laurie took Sallie,\par
Jo, and Ned. The Englishers played well; but the Americans played\par
better, and contested every inch of the ground as strongly as if the\par
spirit of '76 inspired them. Jo and Fred had several skirmishes, and\par
once narrowly escaped high words. Jo was through the last wicket, and\par
had missed the stroke, which failure ruffled her a good deal. Fred was\par
close behind her, and his turn came before hers; he gave a stroke, his\par
ball hit the wicket, and stopped an inch on the wrong side. No one was\par
very near; and running up to examine, he gave it a sly nudge with his\par
toe, which put it just an inch on the right side.\par
\par
"I'm through! Now, Miss Jo, I'll settle you, and get in first," cried\par
the young gentleman, swinging his mallet for another blow.\par
\par
             [Illustration: Now, Miss Jo, I'll settle you]\par
\par
"You pushed it; I saw you; it's my turn now," said Jo sharply.\par
\par
"Upon my word, I didn't move it; it rolled a bit, perhaps, but that is\par
allowed; so stand off, please, and let me have a go at the stake."\par
\par
"We don't cheat in America, but you can, if you choose," said Jo\par
angrily.\par
\par
"Yankees are a deal the most tricky, everybody knows. There you go!"\par
returned Fred, croqueting her ball far away.\par
\par
Jo opened her lips to say something rude, but checked herself in time,\par
colored up to her forehead, and stood a minute, hammering down a wicket\par
with all her might, while Fred hit the stake, and declared himself out\par
with much exultation. She went off to get her ball, and was a long time\par
finding it, among the bushes; but she came back, looking cool and quiet,\par
and waited her turn patiently. It took several strokes to regain the\par
place she had lost; and, when she got there, the other side had nearly\par
won, for Kate's ball was the last but one, and lay near the stake.\par
\par
"By George, it's all up with us! Good-by, Kate. Miss Jo owes me one, so\par
you are finished," cried Fred excitedly, as they all drew near to see\par
the finish.\par
\par
"Yankees have a trick of being generous to their enemies," said Jo, with\par
a look that made the lad redden, "especially when they beat them," she\par
added, as, leaving Kate's ball untouched, she won the game by a clever\par
stroke.\par
\par
Laurie threw up his hat; then remembered that it wouldn't do to exult\par
over the defeat of his guests, and stopped in the middle of a cheer to\par
whisper to his friend,--\par
\par
"Good for you, Jo! He did cheat, I saw him; we can't tell him so, but he\par
won't do it again, take my word for it."\par
\par
Meg drew her aside, under pretence of pinning up a loose braid, and said\par
approvingly,--\par
\par
"It was dreadfully provoking; but you kept your temper, and I'm so glad,\par
Jo."\par
\par
"Don't praise me, Meg, for I could box his ears this minute. I should\par
certainly have boiled over if I hadn't stayed among the nettles till I\par
got my rage under enough to hold my tongue. It's simmering now, so I\par
hope he'll keep out of my way," returned Jo, biting her lips, as she\par
glowered at Fred from under her big hat.\par
\par
"Time for lunch," said Mr. Brooke, looking at his watch.\par
"Commissary-general, will you make the fire and get water, while Miss\par
March, Miss Sallie, and I spread the table? Who can make good coffee?"\par
\par
"Jo can," said Meg, glad to recommend her sister. So Jo, feeling that\par
her late lessons in cookery were to do her honor, went to preside over\par
the coffee-pot, while the children collected dry sticks, and the boys\par
made a fire, and got water from a spring near by. Miss Kate sketched,\par
and Frank talked to Beth, who was making little mats of braided rushes\par
to serve as plates.\par
\par
The commander-in-chief and his aids soon spread the table-cloth with an\par
inviting array of eatables and drinkables, prettily decorated with green\par
leaves. Jo announced that the coffee was ready, and every one settled\par
themselves to a hearty meal; for youth is seldom dyspeptic, and exercise\par
develops wholesome appetites. A very merry lunch it was; for everything\par
seemed fresh and funny, and frequent peals of laughter startled a\par
venerable horse who fed near by. There was a pleasing inequality in the\par
table, which produced many mishaps to cups and plates; acorns dropped\par
into the milk, little black ants partook of the refreshments without\par
being invited, and fuzzy caterpillars swung down from the tree, to see\par
what was going on. Three white-headed children peeped over the fence,\par
and an objectionable dog barked at them from the other side of the river\par
with all his might and main.\par
\par
        [Illustration: "A very merry lunch it was."--Page 156.]\par
\par
"There's salt here, if you prefer it," said Laurie, as he handed Jo a\par
saucer of berries.\par
\par
"Thank you, I prefer spiders," she replied, fishing up two unwary little\par
ones who had gone to a creamy death. "How dare you remind me of that\par
horrid dinner-party, when yours is so nice in every way?" added Jo,\par
as they both laughed, and ate out of one plate, the china having run\par
short.\par
\par
"I had an uncommonly good time that day, and haven't got over it yet.\par
This is no credit to me, you know; I don't do anything; it's you and Meg\par
and Brooke who make it go, and I'm no end obliged to you. What shall we\par
do when we can't eat any more?" asked Laurie, feeling that his trump\par
card had been played when lunch was over.\par
\par
"Have games, till it's cooler. I brought 'Authors,' and I dare say Miss\par
Kate knows something new and nice. Go and ask her; she's company, and\par
you ought to stay with her more."\par
\par
"Aren't you company too? I thought she'd suit Brooke; but he keeps\par
talking to Meg, and Kate just stares at them through that ridiculous\par
glass of hers. I'm going, so you needn't try to preach propriety, for\par
you can't do it, Jo."\par
\par
Miss Kate did know several new games; and as the girls would not, and\par
the boys could not, eat any more, they all adjourned to the drawing-room\par
to play "Rigmarole."\par
\par
"One person begins a story, any nonsense you like, and tells as long as\par
he pleases, only taking care to stop short at some exciting point, when\par
the next takes it up and does the same. It's very funny when well done,\par
and makes a perfect jumble of tragical comical stuff to laugh over.\par
Please start it, Mr. Brooke," said Kate, with a commanding air, which\par
surprised Meg, who treated the tutor with as much respect as any other\par
gentleman.\par
\par
Lying on the grass at the feet of the two young ladies, Mr. Brooke\par
obediently began the story, with the handsome brown eyes steadily fixed\par
upon the sunshiny river.\par
\par
"Once on a time, a knight went out into the world to seek his fortune,\par
for he had nothing but his sword and his shield. He travelled a long\par
while, nearly eight-and-twenty years, and had a hard time of it, till he\par
came to the palace of a good old king, who had offered a reward to any\par
one who would tame and train a fine but unbroken colt, of which he was\par
very fond. The knight agreed to try, and got on slowly but surely; for\par
the colt was a gallant fellow, and soon learned to love his new master,\par
though he was freakish and wild. Every day, when he gave his lessons to\par
this pet of the king's, the knight rode him through the city; and, as\par
he rode, he looked everywhere for a certain beautiful face, which he had\par
seen many times in his dreams, but never found. One day, as he went\par
prancing down a quiet street, he saw at the window of a ruinous castle\par
the lovely face. He was delighted, inquired who lived in this old\par
castle, and was told that several captive princesses were kept there by\par
a spell, and spun all day to lay up money to buy their liberty. The\par
knight wished intensely that he could free them; but he was poor, and\par
could only go by each day, watching for the sweet face, and longing to\par
see it out in the sunshine. At last, he resolved to get into the castle\par
and ask how he could help them. He went and knocked; the great door flew\par
open, and he beheld--"\par
\par
          [Illustration: He went prancing down a quiet street]\par
\par
"A ravishingly lovely lady, who exclaimed, with a cry of rapture, 'At\par
last! at last!'" continued Kate, who had read French novels, and admired\par
the style. "''Tis she!' cried Count Gustave, and fell at her feet in an\par
ecstasy of joy. 'Oh, rise!' she said, extending a hand of marble\par
fairness. 'Never! till you tell me how I may rescue you,' swore the\par
knight, still kneeling. 'Alas, my cruel fate condemns me to remain here\par
till my tyrant is destroyed.' 'Where is the villain?' 'In the mauve\par
salon. Go, brave heart, and save me from despair.' 'I obey, and return\par
victorious or dead!' With these thrilling words he rushed away, and\par
flinging open the door of the mauve salon, was about to enter, when he\par
received--"\par
\par
                  [Illustration: "Oh, rise," she said]\par
\par
"A stunning blow from the big Greek lexicon, which an old fellow in a\par
black gown fired at him," said Ned. "Instantly Sir What's-his-name\par
recovered himself, pitched the tyrant out of the window, and turned to\par
join the lady, victorious, but with a bump on his brow; found the door\par
locked, tore up the curtains, made a rope ladder, got half-way down when\par
the ladder broke, and he went head first into the moat, sixty feet\par
below. Could swim like a duck, paddled round the castle till he came to\par
a little door guarded by two stout fellows; knocked their heads together\par
till they cracked like a couple of nuts, then, by a trifling exertion of\par
his prodigious strength, he smashed in the door, went up a pair of stone\par
steps covered with dust a foot thick, toads as big as your fist, and\par
spiders that would frighten you into hysterics, Miss March. At the top\par
of these steps he came plump upon a sight that took his breath away and\par
chilled his blood--"\par
\par
       [Illustration: A stunning blow from the big Greek lexicon]\par
\par
"A tall figure, all in white with a veil over its face and a lamp in its\par
wasted hand," went on Meg. "It beckoned, gliding noiselessly before him\par
down a corridor as dark and cold as any tomb. Shadowy effigies in armor\par
stood on either side, a dead silence reigned, the lamp burned blue, and\par
the ghostly figure ever and anon turned its face toward him, showing the\par
glitter of awful eyes through its white veil. They reached a curtained\par
door, behind which sounded lovely music; he sprang forward to enter, but\par
the spectre plucked him back, and waved threateningly before him a--"\par
\par
"Snuff-box," said Jo, in a sepulchral tone, which convulsed the\par
audience. "'Thankee,' said the knight politely, as he took a pinch, and\par
sneezed seven times so violently that his head fell off. 'Ha! ha!'\par
laughed the ghost; and having peeped through the key-hole at the\par
princesses spinning away for dear life, the evil spirit picked up her\par
victim and put him in a large tin box, where there were eleven other\par
knights packed together without their heads, like sardines, who all rose\par
and began to--"\par
\par
                       [Illustration: He sneezed]\par
\par
"Dance a hornpipe," cut in Fred, as Jo paused for breath; "and, as they\par
danced, the rubbishy old castle turned to a man-of-war in full sail. 'Up\par
with the jib, reef the tops'l halliards, helm hard a lee, and man the\par
guns!' roared the captain, as a Portuguese pirate hove in sight, with a\par
flag black as ink flying from her foremast. 'Go in and win, my\par
hearties!' says the captain; and a tremendous fight begun. Of course the\par
British beat; they always do."\par
\par
"No, they don't!" cried Jo, aside.\par
\par
"Having taken the pirate captain prisoner, sailed slap over the\par
schooner, whose decks were piled with dead, and whose lee-scuppers ran\par
blood, for the order had been 'Cutlasses, and die hard!' 'Bosen's mate,\par
take a bight of the flying-jib sheet, and start this villain if he don't\par
confess his sins double quick,' said the British captain. The Portuguese\par
held his tongue like a brick, and walked the plank, while the jolly tars\par
cheered like mad. But the sly dog dived, came up under the man-of-war,\par
scuttled her, and down she went, with all sail set, 'To the bottom of\par
the sea, sea, sea,' where--"\par
\par
            [Illustration: The Portuguese walked the plank]\par
\par
"Oh, gracious! what _shall_ I say?" cried Sallie, as Fred ended his\par
rigmarole, in which he had jumbled together, pell-mell, nautical phrases\par
and facts, out of one of his favorite books. "Well they went to the\par
bottom, and a nice mermaid welcomed them, but was much grieved on\par
finding the box of headless knights, and kindly pickled them in brine,\par
hoping to discover the mystery about them; for, being a woman, she was\par
curious. By and by a diver came down, and the mermaid said, 'I'll give\par
you this box of pearls if you can take it up;' for she wanted to restore\par
the poor things to life, and couldn't raise the heavy load herself. So\par
the diver hoisted it up, and was much disappointed, on opening it, to\par
find no pearls. He left it in a great lonely field, where it was found\par
by a--"\par
\par
"Little goose-girl, who kept a hundred fat geese in the field," said\par
Amy, when Sallie's invention gave out. "The little girl was sorry for\par
them, and asked an old woman what she should do to help them. 'Your\par
geese will tell you, they know everything,' said the old woman. So she\par
asked what she should use for new heads, since the old ones were lost,\par
and all the geese opened their hundred mouths and screamed--"\par
\par
"'Cabbages!'" continued Laurie promptly. "'Just the thing,' said the\par
girl, and ran to get twelve fine ones from her garden. She put them on,\par
the knights revived at once, thanked her, and went on their way\par
rejoicing, never knowing the difference, for there were so many other\par
heads like them in the world that no one thought anything of it. The\par
knight in whom I'm interested went back to find the pretty face, and\par
learned that the princesses had spun themselves free, and all gone to be\par
married, but one. He was in a great state of mind at that; and mounting\par
the colt, who stood by him through thick and thin, rushed to the castle\par
to see which was left. Peeping over the hedge, he saw the queen of his\par
affections picking flowers in her garden. 'Will you give me a rose?'\par
said he. 'You must come and get it. I can't come to you; it isn't\par
proper,' said she, as sweet as honey. He tried to climb over the hedge,\par
but it seemed to grow higher and higher; then he tried to push through,\par
but it grew thicker and thicker, and he was in despair. So he patiently\par
broke twig after twig, till he had made a little hole, through which he\par
peeped, saying imploringly, 'Let me in! let me in!' But the pretty\par
princess did not seem to understand, for she picked her roses quietly,\par
and left him to fight his way in. Whether he did or not, Frank will tell\par
you."\par
\par
                [Illustration: Will you give me a rose?]\par
\par
"I can't; I'm not playing, I never do," said Frank, dismayed at the\par
sentimental predicament out of which he was to rescue the absurd couple.\par
Beth had disappeared behind Jo, and Grace was asleep.\par
\par
"So the poor knight is to be left sticking in the hedge, is he?" asked\par
Mr. Brooke, still watching the river, and playing with the wild rose in\par
his button-hole.\par
\par
"I guess the princess gave him a posy, and opened the gate, after a\par
while," said Laurie, smiling to himself, as he threw acorns at his\par
tutor.\par
\par
"What a piece of nonsense we have made! With practice we might do\par
something quite clever. Do you know 'Truth'?" asked Sallie, after they\par
had laughed over their story.\par
\par
"I hope so," said Meg soberly.\par
\par
"The game, I mean?"\par
\par
"What is it?" said Fred.\par
\par
"Why, you pile up your hands, choose a number, and draw out in turn, and\par
the person who draws at the number has to answer truly any questions put\par
by the rest. It's great fun."\par
\par
"Let's try it," said Jo, who liked new experiments.\par
\par
Miss Kate and Mr. Brooke, Meg, and Ned declined, but Fred, Sallie, Jo,\par
and Laurie piled and drew; and the lot fell to Laurie.\par
\par
"Who are your heroes?" asked Jo.\par
\par
"Grandfather and Napoleon."\par
\par
"Which lady here do you think prettiest?" said Sallie.\par
\par
"Margaret."\par
\par
"Which do you like best?" from Fred.\par
\par
"Jo, of course."\par
\par
"What silly questions you ask!" and Jo gave a disdainful shrug as the\par
rest laughed at Laurie's matter-of-fact tone.\par
\par
"Try again; Truth isn't a bad game," said Fred.\par
\par
"It's a very good one for you," retorted Jo, in a low voice.\par
\par
Her turn came next.\par
\par
"What is your greatest fault?" asked Fred, by way of testing in her the\par
virtue he lacked himself.\par
\par
"A quick temper."\par
\par
"What do you most wish for?" said Laurie.\par
\par
"A pair of boot-lacings," returned Jo, guessing and defeating his\par
purpose.\par
\par
"Not a true answer; you must say what you really do want most."\par
\par
"Genius; don't you wish you could give it to me, Laurie?" and she slyly\par
smiled in his disappointed face.\par
\par
"What virtues do you most admire in a man?" asked Sallie.\par
\par
"Courage and honesty."\par
\par
"Now my turn," said Fred, as his hand came last.\par
\par
"Let's give it to him," whispered Laurie to Jo, who nodded, and asked at\par
once,--\par
\par
"Didn't you cheat at croquet?"\par
\par
"Well, yes, a little bit."\par
\par
"Good! Didn't you take your story out of 'The Sea-Lion?'" said Laurie.\par
\par
"Rather."\par
\par
"Don't you think the English nation perfect in every respect?" asked\par
Sallie.\par
\par
"I should be ashamed of myself if I didn't."\par
\par
"He's a true John Bull. Now, Miss Sallie, you shall have a chance\par
without waiting to draw. I'll harrow up your feelings first, by asking\par
if you don't think you are something of a flirt," said Laurie, as Jo\par
nodded to Fred, as a sign that peace was declared.\par
\par
"You impertinent boy! of course I'm not," exclaimed Sallie, with an air\par
that proved the contrary.\par
\par
"What do you hate most?" asked Fred.\par
\par
"Spiders and rice-pudding."\par
\par
"What do you like best?" asked Jo.\par
\par
"Dancing and French gloves."\par
\par
"Well, _I_ think Truth is a very silly play; let's have a sensible game\par
of Authors, to refresh our minds," proposed Jo.\par
\par
Ned, Frank, and the little girls joined in this, and, while it went on,\par
the three elders sat apart, talking. Miss Kate took out her sketch\par
again, and Margaret watched her, while Mr. Brooke lay on the grass, with\par
a book, which he did not read.\par
\par
"How beautifully you do it! I wish I could draw," said Meg, with mingled\par
admiration and regret in her voice.\par
\par
"Why don't you learn? I should think you had taste and talent for it,"\par
replied Miss Kate graciously.\par
\par
"I haven't time."\par
\par
"Your mamma prefers other accomplishments, I fancy. So did mine; but I\par
proved to her that I had talent, by taking a few lessons privately, and\par
then she was quite willing I should go on. Can't you do the same with\par
your governess?"\par
\par
"I have none."\par
\par
"I forgot; young ladies in America go to school more than with us. Very\par
fine schools they are, too, papa says. You go to a private one, I\par
suppose?"\par
\par
"I don't go at all; I am a governess myself."\par
\par
"Oh, indeed!" said Miss Kate; but she might as well have said, "Dear me,\par
how dreadful!" for her tone implied it, and something in her face made\par
Meg color, and wish she had not been so frank.\par
\par
Mr. Brooke looked up, and said quickly, "Young ladies in America love\par
independence as much as their ancestors did, and are admired and\par
respected for supporting themselves."\par
\par
"Oh, yes; of course it's very nice and proper in them to do so. We have\par
many most respectable and worthy young women, who do the same and are\par
employed by the nobility, because, being the daughters of gentlemen,\par
they are both well-bred and accomplished, you know," said Miss Kate, in\par
a patronizing tone, that hurt Meg's pride, and made her work seem not\par
only more distasteful, but degrading.\par
\par
"Did the German song suit, Miss March?" inquired Mr. Brooke, breaking an\par
awkward pause.\par
\par
"Oh, yes! it was very sweet, and I'm much obliged to whoever translated\par
it for me;" and Meg's downcast face brightened as she spoke.\par
\par
"Don't you read German?" asked Miss Kate, with a look of surprise.\par
\par
"Not very well. My father, who taught me, is away, and I don't get on\par
very fast alone, for I've no one to correct my pronunciation."\par
\par
"Try a little now; here is Schiller's 'Mary Stuart,' and a tutor who\par
loves to teach," and Mr. Brooke laid his book on her lap, with an\par
inviting smile.\par
\par
"It's so hard I'm afraid to try," said Meg, grateful, but bashful in the\par
presence of the accomplished young lady beside her.\par
\par
"I'll read a bit to encourage you;" and Miss Kate read one of the most\par
beautiful passages, in a perfectly correct but perfectly expressionless\par
manner.\par
\par
Mr. Brooke made no comment, as she returned the book to Meg, who said\par
innocently,--\par
\par
"I thought it was poetry."\par
\par
"Some of it is. Try this passage."\par
\par
There was a queer smile about Mr. Brooke's mouth as he opened at poor\par
Mary's lament.\par
\par
Meg, obediently following the long grass-blade which her new tutor used\par
to point with, read slowly and timidly, unconsciously making poetry of\par
the hard words by the soft intonation of her musical voice. Down the\par
page went the green guide, and presently, forgetting her listener in the\par
beauty of the sad scene, Meg read as if alone, giving a little touch of\par
tragedy to the words of the unhappy queen. If she had seen the brown\par
eyes then, she would have stopped short; but she never looked up, and\par
the lesson was not spoiled for her.\par
\par
"Very well indeed!" said Mr. Brooke, as she paused, quite ignoring her\par
many mistakes, and looking as if he did, indeed, "love to teach."\par
\par
Miss Kate put up her glass, and, having taken a survey of the little\par
tableau before her, shut her sketch-book, saying, with condescension,--\par
\par
               [Illustration: Miss Kate put up her glass]\par
\par
"You've a nice accent, and, in time, will be a clever reader. I advise\par
you to learn, for German is a valuable accomplishment to teachers. I\par
must look after Grace, she is romping;" and Miss Kate strolled away,\par
adding to herself, with a shrug, "I didn't come to chaperone a\par
governess, though she _is_ young and pretty. What odd people these\par
Yankees are; I'm afraid Laurie will be quite spoilt among them."\par
\par
"I forgot that English people rather turn up their noses at governesses,\par
and don't treat them as we do," said Meg, looking after the retreating\par
figure with an annoyed expression.\par
\par
"Tutors, also, have rather a hard time of it there, as I know to my\par
sorrow. There's no place like America for us workers, Miss Margaret;"\par
and Mr. Brooke looked so contented and cheerful, that Meg was ashamed to\par
lament her hard lot.\par
\par
"I'm glad I live in it then. I don't like my work, but I get a good deal\par
of satisfaction out of it after all, so I won't complain; I only wish I\par
liked teaching as you do."\par
\par
"I think you would if you had Laurie for a pupil. I shall be very sorry\par
to lose him next year," said Mr. Brooke, busily punching holes in the\par
turf.\par
\par
"Going to college, I suppose?" Meg's lips asked that question, but her\par
eyes added, "And what becomes of you?"\par
\par
"Yes; it's high time he went, for he is ready; and as soon as he is off,\par
I shall turn soldier. I am needed."\par
\par
"I am glad of that!" exclaimed Meg. "I should think every young man\par
would want to go; though it is hard for the mothers and sisters who stay\par
at home," she added sorrowfully.\par
\par
"I have neither, and very few friends, to care whether I live or die,"\par
said Mr. Brooke, rather bitterly, as he absently put the dead rose in\par
the hole he had made and covered it up, like a little grave.\par
\par
"Laurie and his grandfather would care a great deal, and we should all\par
be very sorry to have any harm happen to you," said Meg heartily.\par
\par
"Thank you; that sounds pleasant," began Mr. Brooke, looking cheerful\par
again; but before he could finish his speech, Ned, mounted on the old\par
horse, came lumbering up to display his equestrian skill before the\par
young ladies, and there was no more quiet that day.\par
\par
"Don't you love to ride?" asked Grace of Amy, as they stood resting,\par
after a race round the field with the others, led by Ned.\par
\par
"I dote upon it; my sister Meg used to ride when papa was rich, but we\par
don't keep any horses now, except Ellen Tree," added Amy, laughing.\par
\par
"Tell me about Ellen Tree; is it a donkey?" asked Grace curiously.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Ellen Tree]\par
\par
"Why, you see, Jo is crazy about horses, and so am I, but we've only got\par
an old side-saddle, and no horse. Out in our garden is an apple-tree,\par
that has a nice low branch; so Jo put the saddle on it, fixed some reins\par
on the part that turns up, and we bounce away on Ellen Tree whenever we\par
like."\par
\par
"How funny!" laughed Grace. "I have a pony at home, and ride nearly\par
every day in the park, with Fred and Kate; it's very nice, for my\par
friends go too, and the Row is full of ladies and gentlemen."\par
\par
"Dear, how charming! I hope I shall go abroad some day; but I'd rather\par
go to Rome than the Row," said Amy, who had not the remotest idea what\par
the Row was, and wouldn't have asked for the world.\par
\par
Frank, sitting just behind the little girls, heard what they were\par
saying, and pushed his crutch away from him with an impatient gesture as\par
he watched the active lads going through all sorts of comical\par
gymnastics. Beth, who was collecting the scattered Author-cards, looked\par
up, and said, in her shy yet friendly way,--\par
\par
"I'm afraid you are tired; can I do anything for you?"\par
\par
"Talk to me, please; it's dull, sitting by myself," answered Frank, who\par
had evidently been used to being made much of at home.\par
\par
If he had asked her to deliver a Latin oration, it would not have seemed\par
a more impossible task to bashful Beth; but there was no place to run\par
to, no Jo to hide behind now, and the poor boy looked so wistfully at\par
her, that she bravely resolved to try.\par
\par
"What do you like to talk about?" she asked, fumbling over the cards,\par
and dropping half as she tried to tie them up.\par
\par
"Well, I like to hear about cricket and boating and hunting," said\par
Frank, who had not yet learned to suit his amusements to his strength.\par
\par
"My heart! what shall I do? I don't know anything about them," thought\par
Beth; and, forgetting the boy's misfortune in her flurry, she said,\par
hoping to make him talk, "I never saw any hunting, but I suppose you\par
know all about it."\par
\par
"I did once; but I can never hunt again, for I got hurt leaping a\par
confounded five-barred gate; so there are no more horses and hounds for\par
me," said Frank, with a sigh that made Beth hate herself for her\par
innocent blunder.\par
\par
"Your deer are much prettier than our ugly buffaloes," she said, turning\par
to the prairies for help, and feeling glad that she had read one of the\par
boys' books in which Jo delighted.\par
\par
Buffaloes proved soothing and satisfactory; and, in her eagerness to\par
amuse another, Beth forgot herself, and was quite unconscious of her\par
sisters' surprise and delight at the unusual spectacle of Beth talking\par
away to one of the dreadful boys, against whom she had begged\par
protection.\par
\par
"Bless her heart! She pities him, so she is good to him," said Jo,\par
beaming at her from the croquet-ground.\par
\par
"I always said she was a little saint," added Meg, as if there could be\par
no further doubt of it.\par
\par
"I haven't heard Frank laugh so much for ever so long," said Grace to\par
Amy, as they sat discussing dolls, and making tea-sets out of the\par
acorn-cups.\par
\par
"My sister Beth is a very _fastidious_ girl, when she likes to be," said\par
Amy, well pleased at Beth's success. She meant "fascinating," but as\par
Grace didn't know the exact meaning of either word, "fastidious" sounded\par
well, and made a good impression.\par
\par
An impromptu circus, fox and geese, and an amicable game of croquet,\par
finished the afternoon. At sunset the tent was struck, hampers packed,\par
wickets pulled up, boats loaded, and the whole party floated down the\par
river, singing at the tops of their voices. Ned, getting sentimental,\par
warbled a serenade with the pensive refrain,--\par
\par
        "Alone, alone, ah! woe, alone,"\par
\par
and at the lines--\par
\par
       "We each are young, we each have a heart,\par
        Oh, why should we stand thus coldly apart?"\par
\par
he looked at Meg with such a lackadaisical expression that she laughed\par
outright and spoilt his song.\par
\par
"How can you be so cruel to me?" he whispered, under cover of a lively\par
chorus. "You've kept close to that starched-up Englishwoman all day, and\par
now you snub me."\par
\par
"I didn't mean to; but you looked so funny I really couldn't help it,"\par
replied Meg, passing over the first part of his reproach; for it was\par
quite true that she _had_ shunned him, remembering the Moffat party and\par
the talk after it.\par
\par
Ned was offended, and turned to Sallie for consolation, saying to her\par
rather pettishly, "There isn't a bit of flirt in that girl, is there?"\par
\par
"Not a particle; but she's a dear," returned Sallie, defending her\par
friend even while confessing her short-comings.\par
\par
"She's not a stricken deer, any way," said Ned, trying to be witty, and\par
succeeding as well as very young gentlemen usually do.\par
\par
On the lawn, where it had gathered, the little party separated with\par
cordial good-nights and good-byes, for the Vaughns were going to Canada.\par
As the four sisters went home through the garden, Miss Kate looked after\par
them, saying, without the patronizing tone in her voice, "In spite of\par
their demonstrative manners, American girls are very nice when one knows\par
them."\par
\par
"I quite agree with you," said Mr. Brooke.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
           [Illustration: Swinging to and fro in his hammock]\par
\par
                                 XIII.\par
\par
                          CASTLES IN THE AIR.\par
\par
\par
Laurie lay luxuriously swinging to and fro in his hammock, one warm\par
September afternoon, wondering what his neighbors were about, but too\par
lazy to go and find out. He was in one of his moods; for the day had\par
been both unprofitable and unsatisfactory, and he was wishing he could\par
live it over again. The hot weather made him indolent, and he had\par
shirked his studies, tried Mr. Brooke's patience to the utmost,\par
displeased his grandfather by practising half the afternoon, frightened\par
the maid-servants half out of their wits, by mischievously hinting that\par
one of his dogs was going mad, and, after high words with the stable-man\par
about some fancied neglect of his horse, he had flung himself into his\par
hammock, to fume over the stupidity of the world in general, till the\par
peace of the lovely day quieted him in spite of himself. Staring up into\par
the green gloom of the horse-chestnut trees above him, he dreamed dreams\par
of all sorts, and was just imagining himself tossing on the ocean, in a\par
voyage round the world, when the sound of voices brought him ashore in a\par
flash. Peeping through the meshes of the hammock, he saw the Marches\par
coming out, as if bound on some expedition.\par
\par
"What in the world are those girls about now?" thought Laurie, opening\par
his sleepy eyes to take a good look, for there was something rather\par
peculiar in the appearance of his neighbors. Each wore a large, flapping\par
hat, a brown linen pouch slung over one shoulder, and carried a long\par
staff. Meg had a cushion, Jo a book, Beth a basket, and Amy a portfolio.\par
All walked quietly through the garden, out at the little back gate, and\par
began to climb the hill that lay between the house and river.\par
\par
"Well, that's cool!" said Laurie to himself, "to have a picnic and never\par
ask me. They can't be going in the boat, for they haven't got the key.\par
Perhaps they forgot it; I'll take it to them, and see what's going on."\par
\par
Though possessed of half a dozen hats, it took him some time to find\par
one; then there was a hunt for the key, which was at last discovered in\par
his pocket; so that the girls were quite out of sight when he leaped the\par
fence and ran after them. Taking the shortest way to the boat-house, he\par
waited for them to appear: but no one came, and he went up the hill to\par
take an observation. A grove of pines covered one part of it, and from\par
the heart of this green spot came a clearer sound than the soft sigh of\par
the pines or the drowsy chirp of the crickets.\par
\par
"Here's a landscape!" thought Laurie, peeping through the bushes, and\par
looking wide-awake and good-natured already.\par
\par
It _was_ rather a pretty little picture; for the sisters sat together in\par
the shady nook, with sun and shadow flickering over them, the aromatic\par
wind lifting their hair and cooling their hot cheeks, and all the little\par
wood-people going on with their affairs as if these were no strangers,\par
but old friends. Meg sat upon her cushion, sewing daintily with her\par
white hands, and looking as fresh and sweet as a rose, in her pink\par
dress, among the green. Beth was sorting the cones that lay thick under\par
the hemlock near by, for she made pretty things of them. Amy was\par
sketching a group of ferns, and Jo was knitting as she read aloud. A\par
shadow passed over the boy's face as he watched them, feeling that he\par
ought to go away, because uninvited; yet lingering, because home seemed\par
very lonely, and this quiet party in the woods most attractive to his\par
restless spirit. He stood so still that a squirrel, busy with its\par
harvesting, ran down a pine close beside him, saw him suddenly and\par
skipped back, scolding so shrilly that Beth looked up, espied the\par
wistful face behind the birches, and beckoned with a reassuring smile.\par
\par
          [Illustration: It was rather a pretty little picture]\par
\par
"May I come in, please? or shall I be a bother?" he asked, advancing\par
slowly.\par
\par
Meg lifted her eyebrows, but Jo scowled at her defiantly, and said, at\par
once, "Of course you may. We should have asked you before, only we\par
thought you wouldn't care for such a girl's game as this."\par
\par
"I always liked your games; but if Meg doesn't want me, I'll go away."\par
\par
"I've no objection, if you do something; it's against the rules to be\par
idle here," replied Meg, gravely but graciously.\par
\par
"Much obliged; I'll do anything if you'll let me stop a bit, for it's as\par
dull as the Desert of Sahara down there. Shall I sew, read, cone, draw,\par
or do all at once? Bring on your bears; I'm ready," and Laurie sat down,\par
with a submissive expression delightful to behold.\par
\par
"Finish this story while I set my heel," said Jo, handing him the book.\par
\par
"Yes'm," was the meek answer, as he began, doing his best to prove his\par
gratitude for the favor of an admission into the "Busy Bee Society."\par
\par
The story was not a long one, and, when it was finished, he ventured to\par
ask a few questions as a reward of merit.\par
\par
"Please, ma'am, could I inquire if this highly instructive and charming\par
institution is a new one?"\par
\par
"Would you tell him?" asked Meg of her sisters.\par
\par
"He'll laugh," said Amy warningly.\par
\par
"Who cares?" said Jo.\par
\par
"I guess he'll like it," added Beth.\par
\par
"Of course I shall! I give you my word I won't laugh. Tell away, Jo, and\par
don't be afraid."\par
\par
"The idea of being afraid of you! Well, you see we used to play\par
'Pilgrim's Progress,' and we have been going on with it in earnest, all\par
winter and summer."\par
\par
"Yes, I know," said Laurie, nodding wisely.\par
\par
"Who told you?" demanded Jo.\par
\par
"Spirits."\par
\par
"No, I did; I wanted to amuse him one night when you were all away, and\par
he was rather dismal. He did like it, so don't scold, Jo," said Beth\par
meekly.\par
\par
"You can't keep a secret. Never mind; it saves trouble now."\par
\par
"Go on, please," said Laurie, as Jo became absorbed in her work, looking\par
a trifle displeased.\par
\par
"Oh, didn't she tell you about this new plan of ours? Well, we have\par
tried not to waste our holiday, but each has had a task, and worked at\par
it with a will. The vacation is nearly over, the stints are all done,\par
and we are ever so glad that we didn't dawdle."\par
\par
"Yes, I should think so;" and Laurie thought regretfully of his own idle\par
days.\par
\par
"Mother likes to have us out of doors as much as possible; so we bring\par
our work here, and have nice times. For the fun of it we bring our\par
things in these bags, wear the old hats, use poles to climb the hill,\par
and play pilgrims, as we used to do years ago. We call this hill the\par
'Delectable Mountain,' for we can look far away and see the country\par
where we hope to live some time."\par
\par
Jo pointed, and Laurie sat up to examine; for through an opening in the\par
wood one could look across the wide, blue river, the meadows on the\par
other side, far over the outskirts of the great city, to the green hills\par
that rose to meet the sky. The sun was low, and the heavens plowed with\par
the splendor of an autumn sunset. Gold and purple clouds lay on the\par
hill-tops; and rising high into the ruddy light were silvery white\par
peaks, that shone like the airy spires of some Celestial City.\par
\par
"How beautiful that is!" said Laurie softly, for he was quick to see and\par
feel beauty of any kind.\par
\par
"It's often so; and we like to watch it, for it is never the same, but\par
always splendid," replied Amy, wishing she could paint it.\par
\par
"Jo talks about the country where we hope to live some time,--the real\par
country, she means, with pigs and chickens, and haymaking. It would be\par
nice, but I wish the beautiful country up there was real, and we could\par
ever go to it," said Beth musingly.\par
\par
"There is a lovelier country even than that, where we _shall_ go, by and\par
by, when we are good enough," answered Meg, with her sweet voice.\par
\par
"It seems so long to wait, so hard to do; I want to fly away at once, as\par
those swallows fly, and go in at that splendid gate."\par
\par
"You'll get there, Beth, sooner or later; no fear of that," said Jo;\par
"I'm the one that will have to fight and work, and climb and wait, and\par
maybe never get in after all."\par
\par
"You'll have me for company, if that's any comfort. I shall have to do a\par
deal of travelling before I come in sight of your Celestial City. If I\par
arrive late, you'll say a good word for me, won't you, Beth?"\par
\par
Something in the boy's face troubled his little friend; but she said\par
cheerfully, with her quiet eyes on the changing clouds, "If people\par
really want to go, and really try all their lives, I think they will get\par
in; for I don't believe there are any locks on that door, or any guards\par
at the gate. I always imagine it is as it is in the picture, where the\par
shining ones stretch out their hands to welcome poor Christian as he\par
comes up from the river."\par
\par
"Wouldn't it be fun if all the castles in the air which we make could\par
come true, and we could live in them?" said Jo, after a little pause.\par
\par
"I've made such quantities it would be hard to choose which I'd have,"\par
said Laurie, lying flat, and throwing cones at the squirrel who had\par
betrayed him.\par
\par
"You'd have to take your favorite one. What is it?" asked Meg.\par
\par
"If I tell mine, will you tell yours?"\par
\par
"Yes, if the girls will too."\par
\par
"We will. Now, Laurie."\par
\par
"After I'd seen as much of the world as I want to, I'd like to settle in\par
Germany, and have just as much music as I choose. I'm to be a famous\par
musician myself, and all creation is to rush to hear me; and I'm never\par
to be bothered about money or business, but just enjoy myself, and live\par
for what I like. That's my favorite castle. What's yours, Meg?"\par
\par
Margaret seemed to find it a little hard to tell hers, and waved a brake\par
before her face, as if to disperse imaginary gnats, while she said\par
slowly, "I should like a lovely house, full of all sorts of luxurious\par
things,--nice food, pretty clothes, handsome furniture, pleasant people,\par
and heaps of money. I am to be mistress of it, and manage it as I like,\par
with plenty of servants, so I never need work a bit. How I should enjoy\par
it! for I wouldn't be idle, but do good, and make every one love me\par
dearly."\par
\par
             [Illustration: Waved a brake before her face]\par
\par
"Wouldn't you have a master for your castle in the air?" asked Laurie\par
slyly.\par
\par
"I said 'pleasant people,' you know;" and Meg carefully tied up her shoe\par
as she spoke, so that no one saw her face.\par
\par
"Why don't you say you'd have a splendid, wise, good husband, and some\par
angelic little children? You know your castle wouldn't be perfect\par
without," said blunt Jo, who had no tender fancies yet, and rather\par
scorned romance, except in books.\par
\par
"You'd have nothing but horses, inkstands, and novels in yours,"\par
answered Meg petulantly.\par
\par
"Wouldn't I, though? I'd have a stable full of Arabian steeds, rooms\par
piled with books, and I'd write out of a magic inkstand, so that my\par
works should be as famous as Laurie's music. I want to do something\par
splendid before I go into my castle,--something heroic or wonderful,\par
that won't be forgotten after I'm dead. I don't know what, but I'm on\par
the watch for it, and mean to astonish you all, some day. I think I\par
shall write books, and get rich and famous: that would suit me, so that\par
is _my_ favorite dream."\par
\par
"Mine is to stay at home safe with father and mother, and help take care\par
of the family," said Beth contentedly.\par
\par
"Don't you wish for anything else?" asked Laurie.\par
\par
"Since I had my little piano, I am perfectly satisfied. I only wish we\par
may all keep well and be together; nothing else."\par
\par
"I have ever so many wishes; but the pet one is to be an artist, and go\par
to Rome, and do fine pictures, and be the best artist in the whole\par
world," was Amy's modest desire.\par
\par
"We're an ambitious set, aren't we? Every one of us, but Beth, wants to\par
be rich and famous, and gorgeous in every respect. I do wonder if any of\par
us will ever get our wishes," said Laurie, chewing grass, like a\par
meditative calf.\par
\par
"I've got the key to my castle in the air; but whether I can unlock the\par
door remains to be seen," observed Jo mysteriously.\par
\par
"I've got the key to mine, but I'm not allowed to try it. Hang college!"\par
muttered Laurie, with an impatient sigh.\par
\par
"Here's mine!" and Amy waved her pencil.\par
\par
"I haven't got any," said Meg forlornly.\par
\par
"Yes, you have," said Laurie at once.\par
\par
"Where?"\par
\par
"In your face."\par
\par
"Nonsense; that's of no use."\par
\par
"Wait and see if it doesn't bring you something worth having," replied\par
the boy, laughing at the thought of a charming little secret which he\par
fancied he knew.\par
\par
Meg colored behind the brake, but asked no questions, and looked across\par
the river with the same expectant expression which Mr. Brooke had worn\par
when he told the story of the knight.\par
\par
"If we are all alive ten years hence, let's meet, and see how many of us\par
have got our wishes, or how much nearer we are then than now," said Jo,\par
always ready with a plan.\par
\par
"Bless me! how old I shall be,--twenty-seven!" exclaimed Meg who felt\par
grown up already, having just reached seventeen.\par
\par
"You and I shall be twenty-six, Teddy, Beth twenty-four, and Amy\par
twenty-two. What a venerable party!" said Jo.\par
\par
"I hope I shall have done something to be proud of by that time; but I'm\par
such a lazy dog, I'm afraid I shall 'dawdle,' Jo."\par
\par
"You need a motive, mother says; and when you get it, she is sure you'll\par
work splendidly."\par
\par
"Is she? By Jupiter I will, if I only get the chance!" cried Laurie,\par
sitting up with sudden energy. "I ought to be satisfied to please\par
grandfather, and I do try, but it's working against the grain, you see,\par
and comes hard. He wants me to be an India merchant, as he was, and I'd\par
rather be shot. I hate tea and silk and spices, and every sort of\par
rubbish his old ships bring, and I don't care how soon they go to the\par
bottom when I own them. Going to college ought to satisfy him, for if I\par
give him four years he ought to let me off from the business; but he's\par
set, and I 've got to do just as he did, unless I break away and please\par
myself, as my father did. If there was any one left to stay with the old\par
gentleman, I'd do it to-morrow."\par
\par
Laurie spoke excitedly, and looked ready to carry his threat into\par
execution on the slightest provocation; for he was growing up very fast,\par
and, in spite of his indolent ways, had a young man's hatred of\par
subjection, a young man's restless longing to try the world for himself.\par
\par
"I advise you to sail away in one of your ships, and never come home\par
again till you have tried your own way," said Jo, whose imagination was\par
fired by the thought of such a daring exploit, and whose sympathy was\par
excited by what she called "Teddy's wrongs."\par
\par
"That's not right, Jo; you mustn't talk in that way, and Laurie mustn't\par
take your bad advice. You should do just what your grandfather wishes,\par
my dear boy," said Meg, in her most maternal tone. "Do your best at\par
college, and, when he sees that you try to please him, I'm sure he won't\par
be hard or unjust to you. As you say, there is no one else to stay with\par
and love him, and you'd never forgive yourself if you left him without\par
his permission. Don't be dismal or fret, but do your duty; and you'll\par
get your reward, as good Mr. Brooke has, by being respected and loved."\par
\par
"What do you know about him?" asked Laurie, grateful for the good\par
advice, but objecting to the lecture, and glad to turn the conversation\par
from himself, after his unusual outbreak.\par
\par
"Only what your grandpa told us about him,--how he took good care of his\par
own mother till she died, and wouldn't go abroad as tutor to some nice\par
person, because he wouldn't leave her; and how he provides now for an\par
old woman who nursed his mother; and never tells any one, but is just as\par
generous and patient and good as he can be."\par
\par
"So he is, dear old fellow!" said Laurie heartily, as Meg paused,\par
looking flushed and earnest with her story. "It's like grandpa to find\par
out all about him, without letting him know, and to tell all his\par
goodness to others, so that they might like him. Brooke couldn't\par
understand why your mother was so kind to him, asking him over with me,\par
and treating him in her beautiful friendly way. He thought she was just\par
perfect, and talked about it for days and days, and went on about you\par
all in flaming style. If ever I do get my wish, you see what I'll do for\par
Brooke."\par
\par
"Begin to do something now, by not plaguing his life out," said Meg\par
sharply.\par
\par
"How do you know I do, miss?"\par
\par
"I can always tell by his face, when he goes away. If you have been\par
good, he looks satisfied and walks briskly; if you have plagued him,\par
he's sober and walks slowly, as if he wanted to go back and do his work\par
better."\par
\par
"Well, I like that! So you keep an account of my good and bad marks in\par
Brooke's face, do you? I see him bow and smile as he passes your window,\par
but I didn't know you'd got up a telegraph."\par
\par
                [Illustration: I see him bow and smile]\par
\par
"We haven't; don't be angry, and oh, don't tell him I said anything! It\par
was only to show that I cared how you get on, and what is said here is\par
said in confidence, you know," cried Meg, much alarmed at the thought of\par
what might follow from her careless speech.\par
\par
"_I_ don't tell tales," replied Laurie, with his "high and mighty" air,\par
as Jo called a certain expression which he occasionally wore. "Only if\par
Brooke is going to be a thermometer, I must mind and have fair weather\par
for him to report."\par
\par
"Please don't be offended. I didn't mean to preach or tell tales or be\par
silly; I only thought Jo was encouraging you in a feeling which you'd be\par
sorry for, by and by. You are so kind to us, we feel as if you were our\par
brother, and say just what we think. Forgive me, I meant it kindly." And\par
Meg offered her hand with a gesture both affectionate and timid.\par
\par
Ashamed of his momentary pique, Laurie squeezed the kind little hand,\par
and said frankly, "I'm the one to be forgiven; I'm cross, and have been\par
out of sorts all day. I like to have you tell me my faults and be\par
sisterly, so don't mind if I am grumpy sometimes; I thank you all the\par
same."\par
\par
Bent on showing that he was not offended, he made himself as agreeable\par
as possible,--wound cotton for Meg, recited poetry to please Jo, shook\par
down cones for Beth, and helped Amy with her ferns, proving himself a\par
fit person to belong to the "Busy Bee Society." In the midst of an\par
animated discussion on the domestic habits of turtles (one of those\par
amiable creatures having strolled up from the river), the faint sound of\par
a bell warned them that Hannah had put the tea "to draw," and they would\par
just have time to get home to supper.\par
\par
"May I come again?" asked Laurie.\par
\par
"Yes, if you are good, and love your book, as the boys in the primer are\par
told to do," said Meg smiling.\par
\par
"I'll try."\par
\par
"Then you may come, and I'll teach you to knit as the Scotchmen do;\par
there's a demand for socks just now," added Jo, waving hers, like a big\par
blue worsted banner, as they parted at the gate.\par
\par
That night, when Beth played to Mr. Laurence in the twilight, Laurie,\par
standing in the shadow of the curtain, listened to the little David,\par
whose simple music always quieted his moody spirit, and watched the old\par
man, who sat with his gray head on his hand, thinking tender thoughts of\par
the dead child he had loved so much. Remembering the conversation of the\par
afternoon, the boy said to himself, with the resolve to make the\par
sacrifice cheerfully, "I'll let my castle go, and stay with the dear old\par
gentleman while he needs me, for I am all he has."\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                    [Illustration: Jo was very busy]\par
\par
                                  XIV.\par
\par
                                SECRETS.\par
\par
\par
Jo was very busy in the garret, for the October days began to grow\par
chilly, and the afternoons were short. For two or three hours the sun\par
lay warmly in the high window, showing Jo seated on the old sofa,\par
writing busily, with her papers spread out upon a trunk before her,\par
while Scrabble, the pet rat, promenaded the beams overhead, accompanied\par
by his oldest son, a fine young fellow, who was evidently very proud of\par
his whiskers. Quite absorbed in her work, Jo scribbled away till the\par
last page was filled, when she signed her name with a flourish, and\par
threw down her pen, exclaiming,--\par
\par
"There, I've done my best! If this won't suit I shall have to wait till\par
I can do better."\par
\par
Lying back on the sofa, she read the manuscript carefully through,\par
making dashes here and there, and putting in many exclamation points,\par
which looked like little balloons; then she tied it up with a smart red\par
ribbon, and sat a minute looking at it with a sober, wistful expression,\par
which plainly showed how earnest her work had been. Jo's desk up here\par
was an old tin kitchen, which hung against the wall. In it she kept her\par
papers and a few books, safely shut away from Scrabble, who, being\par
likewise of a literary turn, was fond of making a circulating library of\par
such books as were left in his way, by eating the leaves. From this tin\par
receptacle Jo produced another manuscript; and, putting both in her\par
pocket, crept quietly down stairs, leaving her friends to nibble her\par
pens and taste her ink.\par
\par
She put on her hat and jacket as noiselessly as possible, and, going to\par
the back entry window, got out upon the roof of a low porch, swung\par
herself down to the grassy bank, and took a roundabout way to the road.\par
Once there, she composed herself, hailed a passing omnibus, and rolled\par
away to town, looking very merry and mysterious.\par
\par
If any one had been watching her, he would have thought her movements\par
decidedly peculiar; for, on alighting, she went off at a great pace till\par
she reached a certain number in a certain busy street; having found the\par
place with some difficulty, she went into the door-way, looked up the\par
dirty stairs, and, after standing stock still a minute, suddenly dived\par
into the street, and walked away as rapidly as she came. This\par
manœuvre she repeated several times, to the great amusement of a\par
black-eyed young gentleman lounging in the window of a building\par
opposite. On returning for the third time, Jo gave herself a shake,\par
pulled her hat over her eyes, and walked up the stairs, looking as if\par
she were going to have all her teeth out.\par
\par
There was a dentist's sign, among others, which adorned the entrance,\par
and, after staring a moment at the pair of artificial jaws which slowly\par
opened and shut to draw attention to a fine set of teeth, the young\par
gentleman put on his coat, took his hat, and went down to post himself\par
in the opposite door-way, saying, with a smile and a shiver,--\par
\par
"It's like her to come alone, but if she has a bad time she'll need some\par
one to help her home."\par
\par
In ten minutes Jo came running down stairs with a very red face, and the\par
general appearance of a person who had just passed through a trying\par
ordeal of some sort. When she saw the young gentleman she looked\par
anything but pleased, and passed him with a nod; but he followed, asking\par
with an air of sympathy,--\par
\par
"Did you have a bad time?"\par
\par
"Not very."\par
\par
"You got through quickly."\par
\par
"Yes, thank goodness!"\par
\par
"Why did you go alone?"\par
\par
"Didn't want any one to know."\par
\par
"You're the oddest fellow I ever saw. How many did you have out?"\par
\par
Jo looked at her friend as if she did not understand him; then began to\par
laugh, as if mightily amused at something.\par
\par
"There are two which I want to have come out, but I must wait a week."\par
\par
"What are you laughing at? You are up to some mischief, Jo," said\par
Laurie, looking mystified.\par
\par
"So are you. What were you doing, sir, up in that billiard saloon?"\par
\par
"Begging your pardon, ma'am, it wasn't a billiard saloon, but a\par
gymnasium, and I was taking a lesson in fencing."\par
\par
"I'm glad of that."\par
\par
"Why?"\par
\par
"You can teach me, and then when we play Hamlet, you can be Laertes, and\par
we'll make a fine thing of the fencing scene."\par
\par
Laurie burst out with a hearty boy's laugh, which made several\par
passers-by smile in spite of themselves.\par
\par
"I'll teach you whether we play Hamlet or not; it's grand fun, and will\par
straighten you up capitally. But I don't believe that was your only\par
reason for saying 'I'm glad,' in that decided way; was it, now?"\par
\par
"No, I was glad that you were not in the saloon, because I hope you\par
never go to such places. Do you?"\par
\par
"Not often."\par
\par
"I wish you wouldn't."\par
\par
"It's no harm, Jo. I have billiards at home, but it's no fun unless you\par
have good players; so, as I'm fond of it, I come sometimes and have a\par
game with Ned Moffat or some of the other fellows."\par
\par
"Oh dear, I'm so sorry, for you'll get to liking it better and better,\par
and will waste time and money, and grow like those dreadful boys. I did\par
hope you'd stay respectable, and be a satisfaction to your friends,"\par
said Jo, shaking her head.\par
\par
"Can't a fellow take a little innocent amusement now and then without\par
losing his respectability?" asked Laurie, looking nettled.\par
\par
"That depends upon how and where he takes it. I don't like Ned and his\par
set, and wish you'd keep out of it. Mother won't let us have him at our\par
house, though he wants to come; and if you grow like him she won't be\par
willing to have us frolic together as we do now."\par
\par
"Won't she?" asked Laurie anxiously.\par
\par
"No, she can't bear fashionable young men, and she'd shut us all up in\par
bandboxes rather than have us associate with them."\par
\par
"Well, she needn't get out her bandboxes yet; I'm not a fashionable\par
party, and don't mean to be; but I do like harmless larks now and then,\par
don't you?"\par
\par
"Yes, nobody minds them, so lark away, but don't get wild, will you? or\par
there will be an end of all our good times."\par
\par
"I'll be a double-distilled saint."\par
\par
"I can't bear saints: just be a simple, honest, respectable boy, and\par
we'll never desert you. I don't know what I _should_ do if you acted\par
like Mr. King's son; he had plenty of money, but didn't know how to\par
spend it, and got tipsy and gambled, and ran away, and forged his\par
father's name, I believe, and was altogether horrid."\par
\par
"You think I'm likely to do the same? Much obliged."\par
\par
"No, I don't--oh, _dear_, no!--but I hear people talking about money\par
being such a temptation, and I sometimes wish you were poor; I shouldn't\par
worry then."\par
\par
"Do you worry about me, Jo?"\par
\par
"A little, when you look moody or discontented, as you sometimes do; for\par
you've got such a strong will, if you once get started wrong, I'm afraid\par
it would be hard to stop you."\par
\par
Laurie walked in silence a few minutes, and Jo watched him, wishing she\par
had held her tongue, for his eyes looked angry, though his lips still\par
smiled as if at her warnings.\par
\par
"Are you going to deliver lectures all the way home?" he asked\par
presently.\par
\par
"Of course not; why?"\par
\par
"Because if you are, I'll take a 'bus; if you are not, I'd like to walk\par
with you, and tell you something very interesting."\par
\par
"I won't preach any more, and I'd like to hear the news immensely."\par
\par
"Very well, then; come on. It's a secret, and if I tell you, you must\par
tell me yours."\par
\par
"I haven't got any," began Jo, but stopped suddenly, remembering that\par
she had.\par
\par
"You know you have,--you can't hide anything; so up and 'fess, or I\par
won't tell," cried Laurie.\par
\par
"Is your secret a nice one?"\par
\par
"Oh, isn't it! all about people you know, and such fun! You ought to\par
hear it, and I've been aching to tell it this long time. Come, you\par
begin."\par
\par
"You'll not say anything about it at home, will you?"\par
\par
"Not a word."\par
\par
"And you won't tease me in private?"\par
\par
"I never tease."\par
\par
"Yes, you do; you get everything you want out of people. I don't know\par
how you do it, but you are a born wheedler."\par
\par
"Thank you; fire away."\par
\par
"Well, I've left two stories with a newspaper man, and he's to give his\par
answer next week," whispered Jo, in her confidant's ear.\par
\par
"Hurrah for Miss March, the celebrated American authoress!" cried\par
Laurie, throwing up his hat and catching it again, to the great delight\par
of two ducks, four cats, five hens, and half a dozen Irish children; for\par
they were out of the city now.\par
\par
                 [Illustration: Hurrah for Miss March]\par
\par
"Hush! It won't come to anything, I dare say; but I couldn't rest till I\par
had tried, and I said nothing about it, because I didn't want any one\par
else to be disappointed."\par
\par
"It won't fail. Why, Jo, your stories are works of Shakespeare,\par
compared to half the rubbish that is published every day. Won't it be\par
fun to see them in print; and sha'n't we feel proud of our authoress?"\par
\par
Jo's eyes sparkled, for it is always pleasant to be believed in; and a\par
friend's praise is always sweeter than a dozen newspaper puffs.\par
\par
"Where's _your_ secret? Play fair, Teddy, or I'll never believe you\par
again," she said, trying to extinguish the brilliant hopes that blazed\par
up at a word of encouragement.\par
\par
"I may get into a scrape for telling; but I didn't promise not to, so I\par
will, for I never feel easy in my mind till I've told you any plummy bit\par
of news I get. I know where Meg's glove is."\par
\par
"Is that all?" said Jo, looking disappointed, as Laurie nodded and\par
twinkled, with a face full of mysterious intelligence.\par
\par
"It's quite enough for the present, as you'll agree when I tell you\par
where it is."\par
\par
"Tell, then."\par
\par
Laurie bent, and whispered three words in Jo's ear, which produced a\par
comical change. She stood and stared at him for a minute, looking both\par
surprised and displeased, then walked on, saying sharply, "How do you\par
know?"\par
\par
"Saw it."\par
\par
"Where?"\par
\par
"Pocket."\par
\par
"All this time?"\par
\par
"Yes; isn't that romantic?"\par
\par
"No, it's horrid."\par
\par
"Don't you like it?"\par
\par
"Of course I don't. It's ridiculous; it won't be allowed. My patience!\par
what would Meg say?"\par
\par
"You are not to tell any one; mind that."\par
\par
"I didn't promise."\par
\par
"That was understood, and I trusted you."\par
\par
"Well, I won't for the present, any way; but I'm disgusted, and wish you\par
hadn't told me."\par
\par
"I thought you'd be pleased."\par
\par
"At the idea of anybody coming to take Meg away? No, thank you."\par
\par
"You'll feel better about it when somebody comes to take you away."\par
\par
"I'd like to see any one try it," cried Jo fiercely.\par
\par
"So should I!" and Laurie chuckled at the idea.\par
\par
"I don't think secrets agree with me; I feel rumpled up in my mind since\par
you told me that," said Jo, rather ungratefully.\par
\par
"Race down this hill with me, and you'll be all right," suggested\par
Laurie.\par
\par
No one was in sight; the smooth road sloped invitingly before her; and\par
finding the temptation irresistible, Jo darted away, soon leaving hat\par
and comb behind her, and scattering hair-pins as she ran. Laurie reached\par
the goal first, and was quite satisfied with the success of his\par
treatment; for his Atalanta came panting up, with flying hair, bright\par
eyes, ruddy cheeks, and no signs of dissatisfaction in her face.\par
\par
                     [Illustration: Jo darted away]\par
\par
"I wish I was a horse; then I could run for miles in this splendid air,\par
and not lose my breath. It was capital; but see what a guy it's made me.\par
Go, pick up my things, like a cherub as you are," said Jo, dropping down\par
under a maple-tree, which was carpeting the bank with crimson leaves.\par
\par
Laurie leisurely departed to recover the lost property, and Jo bundled\par
up her braids, hoping no one would pass by till she was tidy again. But\par
some one did pass, and who should it be but Meg, looking particularly\par
ladylike in her state and festival suit, for she had been making calls.\par
\par
"What in the world are you doing here?" she asked, regarding her\par
dishevelled sister with well-bred surprise.\par
\par
"Getting leaves," meekly answered Jo, sorting the rosy handful she had\par
just swept up.\par
\par
"And hair-pins," added Laurie, throwing half a dozen into Jo's lap.\par
"They grow on this road, Meg; so do combs and brown straw hats."\par
\par
"You have been running, Jo; how could you? When _will_ you stop such\par
romping ways?" said Meg reprovingly, as she settled her cuffs, and\par
smoothed her hair, with which the wind had taken liberties.\par
\par
"Never till I'm stiff and old, and have to use a crutch. Don't try to\par
make me grow up before my time, Meg: it's hard enough to have you change\par
all of a sudden; let me be a little girl as long as I can."\par
\par
As she spoke, Jo bent over the leaves to hide the trembling of her lips;\par
for lately she had felt that Margaret was fast getting to be a woman,\par
and Laurie's secret made her dread the separation which must surely come\par
some time, and now seemed very near. He saw the trouble in her face, and\par
drew Meg's attention from it by asking quickly, "Where have you been\par
calling, all so fine?"\par
\par
"At the Gardiners', and Sallie has been telling me all about Belle\par
Moffat's wedding. It was very splendid, and they have gone to spend the\par
winter in Paris. Just think how delightful that must be!"\par
\par
"Do you envy her, Meg?" said Laurie.\par
\par
"I'm afraid I do."\par
\par
"I'm glad of it!" muttered Jo, tying on her hat with a jerk.\par
\par
"Why?" asked Meg, looking surprised.\par
\par
"Because if you care much about riches, you will never go and marry a\par
poor man," said Jo, frowning at Laurie, who was mutely warning her to\par
mind what she said.\par
\par
"I shall never '_go_ and marry' any one," observed Meg, walking on with\par
great dignity, while the others followed, laughing, whispering, skipping\par
stones, and "behaving like children," as Meg said to herself, though she\par
might have been tempted to join them if she had not had her best dress\par
on.\par
\par
For a week or two, Jo behaved so queerly that her sisters were quite\par
bewildered. She rushed to the door when the postman rang; was rude to\par
Mr. Brooke whenever they met; would sit looking at Meg with a woe-begone\par
face, occasionally jumping up to shake, and then to kiss her, in a very\par
mysterious manner; Laurie and she were always making signs to one\par
another, and talking about "Spread Eagles," till the girls declared they\par
had both lost their wits. On the second Saturday after Jo got out of the\par
window, Meg, as she sat sewing at her window, was scandalized by the\par
sight of Laurie chasing Jo all over the garden, and finally capturing\par
her in Amy's bower. What went on there, Meg could not see; but shrieks\par
of laughter were heard, followed by the murmur of voices and a great\par
flapping of newspapers.\par
\par
"What shall we do with that girl? She never _will_ behave like a young\par
lady," sighed Meg, as she watched the race with a disapproving face.\par
\par
"I hope she won't; she is so funny and dear as she is," said Beth, who\par
had never betrayed that she was a little hurt at Jo's having secrets\par
with any one but her.\par
\par
"It's very trying, but we never can make her _commy la fo_," added Amy,\par
who sat making some new frills for herself, with her curls tied up in a\par
very becoming way,--two agreeable things, which made her feel unusually\par
elegant and ladylike.\par
\par
In a few minutes Jo bounced in, laid herself on the sofa, and affected\par
to read.\par
\par
    [Illustration: Jo laid herself on the sofa and affected to read]\par
\par
"Have you anything interesting there?" asked Meg, with condescension.\par
\par
"Nothing but a story; won't amount to much, I guess," returned Jo,\par
carefully keeping the name of the paper out of sight.\par
\par
"You'd better read it aloud; that will amuse us and keep you out of\par
mischief," said Amy, in her most grown-up tone.\par
\par
"What's the name?" asked Beth, wondering why Jo kept her face behind the\par
sheet.\par
\par
"The Rival Painters."\par
\par
"That sounds well; read it," said Meg.\par
\par
With a loud "Hem!" and a long breath, Jo began to read very fast. The\par
girls listened with interest, for the tale was romantic, and somewhat\par
pathetic, as most of the characters died in the end.\par
\par
"I like that about the splendid picture," was Amy's approving remark, as\par
Jo paused.\par
\par
"I prefer the lovering part. Viola and Angelo are two of our favorite\par
names; isn't that queer?" said Meg, wiping her eyes, for the "lovering\par
part" was tragical.\par
\par
"Who wrote it?" asked Beth, who had caught a glimpse of Jo's face.\par
\par
The reader suddenly sat up, cast away the paper, displaying a flushed\par
countenance, and, with a funny mixture of solemnity and excitement,\par
replied in a loud voice, "Your sister."\par
\par
"You?" cried Meg, dropping her work.\par
\par
"It's very good," said Amy critically.\par
\par
"I knew it! I knew it! O my Jo, I _am_ so proud!" and Beth ran to hug\par
her sister, and exult over this splendid success.\par
\par
Dear me, how delighted they all were, to be sure! how Meg wouldn't\par
believe it till she saw the words, "Miss Josephine March," actually\par
printed in the paper; how graciously Amy criticised the artistic parts\par
of the story, and offered hints for a sequel, which unfortunately\par
couldn't be carried out, as the hero and heroine were dead; how Beth got\par
excited, and skipped and sung with joy; how Hannah came in to exclaim\par
"Sakes alive, well I never!" in great astonishment at "that Jo's\par
doin's;" how proud Mrs. March was when she knew it; how Jo laughed, with\par
tears in her eyes, as she declared she might as well be a peacock and\par
done with it; and how the "Spread Eagle" might be said to flap his wings\par
triumphantly over the House of March, as the paper passed from hand to\par
hand.\par
\par
"Tell us all about it." "When did it come?" "How much did you get for\par
it?" "What _will_ father say?" "Won't Laurie laugh?" cried the family,\par
all in one breath, as they clustered about Jo; for these foolish,\par
affectionate people made a jubilee of every little household joy.\par
\par
"Stop jabbering, girls, and I'll tell you everything," said Jo,\par
wondering if Miss Burney felt any grander over her "Evelina" than she\par
did over her "Rival Painters." Having told how she disposed of her\par
tales, Jo added, "And when I went to get my answer, the man said he\par
liked them both, but didn't pay beginners, only let them print in his\par
paper, and noticed the stories. It was good practice, he said; and when\par
the beginners improved, any one would pay. So I let him have the two\par
stories, and to-day this was sent to me, and Laurie caught me with it,\par
and insisted on seeing it, so I let him; and he said it was good, and I\par
shall write more, and he's going to get the next paid for, and I _am_ so\par
happy, for in time I may be able to support myself and help the girls."\par
\par
Jo's breath gave out here; and, wrapping her head in the paper, she\par
bedewed her little story with a few natural tears; for to be\par
independent, and earn the praise of those she loved were the dearest\par
wishes of her heart, and this seemed to be the first step toward that\par
happy end.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                  XV.\par
\par
                              A TELEGRAM.\par
\par
\par
  [Illustration: November is the most disagreeable month in the year]\par
\par
"November is the most disagreeable month in the whole year," said\par
Margaret, standing at the window one dull afternoon, looking out at the\par
frost-bitten garden.\par
\par
"That's the reason I was born in it," observed Jo pensively, quite\par
unconscious of the blot on her nose.\par
\par
"If something very pleasant should happen now, we should think it a\par
delightful month," said Beth, who took a hopeful view of everything,\par
even November.\par
\par
"I dare say; but nothing pleasant ever _does_ happen in this family,"\par
said Meg, who was out of sorts. "We go grubbing along day after day,\par
without a bit of change, and very little fun. We might as well be in a\par
treadmill."\par
\par
"My patience, how blue we are!" cried Jo. "I don't much wonder, poor\par
dear, for you see other girls having splendid times, while you grind,\par
grind, year in and year out. Oh, don't I wish I could manage things for\par
you as I do for my heroines! You're pretty enough and good enough\par
already, so I'd have some rich relation leave you a fortune\par
unexpectedly; then you'd dash out as an heiress, scorn every one who\par
has slighted you, go abroad, and come home my Lady Something, in a blaze\par
of splendor and elegance."\par
\par
"People don't have fortunes left them in that style now-a-days; men have\par
to work, and women to marry for money. It's a dreadfully unjust world,"\par
said Meg bitterly.\par
\par
"Jo and I are going to make fortunes for you all; just wait ten years,\par
and see if we don't," said Amy, who sat in a corner, making mud pies, as\par
Hannah called her little clay models of birds, fruit, and faces.\par
\par
"Can't wait, and I'm afraid I haven't much faith in ink and dirt, though\par
I'm grateful for your good intentions."\par
\par
Meg sighed, and turned to the frost-bitten garden again; Jo groaned, and\par
leaned both elbows on the table in a despondent attitude, but Amy\par
spatted away energetically; and Beth, who sat at the other window, said,\par
smiling, "Two pleasant things are going to happen right away: Marmee is\par
coming down the street, and Laurie is tramping through the garden as if\par
he had something nice to tell."\par
\par
In they both came, Mrs. March with her usual question, "Any letter from\par
father, girls?" and Laurie to say in his persuasive way, "Won't some of\par
you come for a drive? I've been working away at mathematics till my head\par
is in a muddle, and I'm going to freshen my wits by a brisk turn. It's a\par
dull day, but the air isn't bad, and I'm going to take Brooke home, so\par
it will be gay inside, if it isn't out. Come, Jo, you and Beth will go,\par
won't you?"\par
\par
"Of course we will."\par
\par
"Much obliged, but I'm busy;" and Meg whisked out her work-basket, for\par
she had agreed with her mother that it was best, for her at least, not\par
to drive often with the young gentleman.\par
\par
"We three will be ready in a minute," cried Amy, running away to wash\par
her hands.\par
\par
"Can I do anything for you, Madam Mother?" asked Laurie, leaning over\par
Mrs. March's chair, with the affectionate look and tone he always gave\par
her.\par
\par
"No, thank you, except call at the office, if you'll be so kind, dear.\par
It's our day for a letter, and the postman hasn't been. Father is as\par
regular as the sun, but there's some delay on the way, perhaps."\par
\par
A sharp ring interrupted her, and a minute after Hannah came in with a\par
letter.\par
\par
"It's one of them horrid telegraph things, mum," she said, handing it as\par
if she was afraid it would explode and do some damage.\par
\par
          [Illustration: One of them horrid telegraph things]\par
\par
At the word "telegraph," Mrs. March snatched it, read the two lines it\par
contained, and dropped back into her chair as white as if the little\par
paper had sent a bullet to her heart. Laurie dashed down stairs for\par
water, while Meg and Hannah supported her, and Jo read aloud, in a\par
frightened voice,--\par
\par
    "MRS. MARCH:\par
\par
    "Your husband is very ill. Come at once.\par
                           "S. HALE,\par
                "Blank Hospital, Washington"\par
\par
How still the room was as they listened breathlessly, how strangely the\par
day darkened outside, and how suddenly the whole world seemed to change,\par
as the girls gathered about their mother, feeling as if all the\par
happiness and support of their lives was about to be taken from them.\par
Mrs. March was herself again directly; read the message over, and\par
stretched out her arms to her daughters, saying, in a tone they never\par
forgot, "I shall go at once, but it may be too late. O children,\par
children, help me to bear it!"\par
\par
For several minutes there was nothing but the sound of sobbing in the\par
room, mingled with broken words of comfort, tender assurances of help,\par
and hopeful whispers that died away in tears. Poor Hannah was the first\par
to recover, and with unconscious wisdom she set all the rest a good\par
example; for, with her, work was the panacea for most afflictions.\par
\par
"The Lord keep the dear man! I won't waste no time a cryin', but git\par
your things ready right away, mum," she said, heartily, as she wiped her\par
face on her apron, gave her mistress a warm shake of the hand with her\par
own hard one, and went away, to work like three women in one.\par
\par
"She's right; there's no time for tears now. Be calm, girls, and let me\par
think."\par
\par
They tried to be calm, poor things, as their mother sat up, looking\par
pale, but steady, and put away her grief to think and plan for them.\par
\par
"Where's Laurie?" she asked presently, when she had collected her\par
thoughts, and decided on the first duties to be done.\par
\par
"Here, ma'am. Oh, let me do something!" cried the boy, hurrying from the\par
next room, whither he had withdrawn, feeling that their first sorrow was\par
too sacred for even his friendly eyes to see.\par
\par
"Send a telegram saying I will come at once. The next train goes early\par
in the morning. I'll take that."\par
\par
"What else? The horses are ready; I can go anywhere, do anything," he\par
said, looking ready to fly to the ends of the earth.\par
\par
"Leave a note at Aunt March's. Jo, give me that pen and paper."\par
\par
Tearing off the blank side of one of her newly copied pages, Jo drew the\par
table before her mother, well knowing that money for the long, sad\par
journey must be borrowed, and feeling as if she could do anything to add\par
a little to the sum for her father.\par
\par
"Now go, dear; but don't kill yourself driving at a desperate pace;\par
there is no need of that."\par
\par
Mrs. March's warning was evidently thrown away; for five minutes later\par
Laurie tore by the window on his own fleet horse, riding as if for his\par
life.\par
\par
"Jo, run to the rooms, and tell Mrs. King that I can't come. On the way\par
get these things. I'll put them down; they'll be needed, and I must go\par
prepared for nursing. Hospital stores are not always good. Beth, go and\par
ask Mr. Laurence for a couple of bottles of old wine: I'm not too proud\par
to beg for father; he shall have the best of everything. Amy, tell\par
Hannah to get down the black trunk; and, Meg, come and help me find my\par
things, for I'm half bewildered."\par
\par
Writing, thinking, and directing, all at once, might well bewilder the\par
poor lady, and Meg begged her to sit quietly in her room for a little\par
while, and let them work. Every one scattered like leaves before a gust\par
of wind; and the quiet, happy household was broken up as suddenly as if\par
the paper had been an evil spell.\par
\par
Mr. Laurence came hurrying back with Beth, bringing every comfort the\par
kind old gentleman could think of for the invalid, and friendliest\par
promises of protection for the girls during the mother's absence, which\par
comforted her very much. There was nothing he didn't offer, from his own\par
dressing-gown to himself as escort. But that last was impossible. Mrs.\par
March would not hear of the old gentleman's undertaking the long\par
journey; yet an expression of relief was visible when he spoke of it,\par
for anxiety ill fits one for travelling. He saw the look, knit his heavy\par
eyebrows, rubbed his hands, and marched abruptly away, saying he'd be\par
back directly. No one had time to think of him again till, as Meg ran\par
through the entry, with a pair of rubbers in one hand and a cup of tea\par
in the other, she came suddenly upon Mr. Brooke.\par
\par
           [Illustration: She came suddenly upon Mr. Brooke]\par
\par
"I'm very sorry to hear of this, Miss March," he said, in the kind,\par
quiet tone which sounded very pleasantly to her perturbed spirit. "I\par
came to offer myself as escort to your mother. Mr. Laurence has\par
commissions for me in Washington, and it will give me real satisfaction\par
to be of service to her there."\par
\par
Down dropped the rubbers, and the tea was very near following, as Meg\par
put out her hand, with a face so full of gratitude, that Mr. Brooke\par
would have felt repaid for a much greater sacrifice than the trifling\par
one of time and comfort which he was about to make.\par
\par
"How kind you all are! Mother will accept, I'm sure; and it will be such\par
a relief to know that she has some one to take care of her. Thank you\par
very, very much!"\par
\par
Meg spoke earnestly, and forgot herself entirely till something in the\par
brown eyes looking down at her made her remember the cooling tea, and\par
lead the way into the parlor, saying she would call her mother.\par
\par
Everything was arranged by the time Laurie returned with a note from\par
Aunt March, enclosing the desired sum, and a few lines repeating what\par
she had often said before,--that she had always told them it was absurd\par
for March to go into the army, always predicted that no good would come\par
of it, and she hoped they would take her advice next time. Mrs. March\par
put the note in the fire, the money in her purse, and went on with her\par
preparations, with her lips folded tightly, in a way which Jo would have\par
understood if she had been there.\par
\par
The short afternoon wore away; all the other errands were done, and Meg\par
and her mother busy at some necessary needle-work, while Beth and Amy\par
got tea, and Hannah finished her ironing with what she called a "slap\par
and a bang," but still Jo did not come. They began to get anxious; and\par
Laurie went off to find her, for no one ever knew what freak Jo might\par
take into her head. He missed her, however, and she came walking in with\par
a very queer expression of countenance, for there was a mixture of fun\par
and fear, satisfaction and regret, in it, which puzzled the family as\par
much as did the roll of bills she laid before her mother, saying, with a\par
little choke in her voice, "That's my contribution towards making\par
father comfortable and bringing him home!"\par
\par
"My dear, where did you get it? Twenty-five dollars! Jo, I hope you\par
haven't done anything rash?\par
\par
"No, it's mine honestly; I didn't beg, borrow, or steal it. I earned it;\par
and I don't think you'll blame me, for I only sold what was my own."\par
\par
As she spoke, Jo took off her bonnet, and a general outcry arose, for\par
all her abundant hair was cut short.\par
\par
"Your hair! Your beautiful hair!" "O Jo, how could you? Your one\par
beauty." "My dear girl, there was no need of this." "She doesn't look\par
like my Jo any more, but I love her dearly for it!"\par
\par
As every one exclaimed, and Beth hugged the cropped head tenderly, Jo\par
assumed an indifferent air, which did not deceive any one a particle,\par
and said, rumpling up the brown bush, and trying to look as if she liked\par
it, "It doesn't affect the fate of the nation, so don't wail, Beth. It\par
will be good for my vanity; I was getting too proud of my wig. It will\par
do my brains good to have that mop taken off; my head feels deliciously\par
light and cool, and the barber said I could soon have a curly crop,\par
which will be boyish, becoming, and easy to keep in order. I'm\par
satisfied; so please take the money, and let's have supper."\par
\par
"Tell me all about it, Jo. _I_ am not quite satisfied, but I can't blame\par
you, for I know how willingly you sacrificed your vanity, as you call\par
it, to your love. But, my dear, it was not necessary, and I'm afraid you\par
will regret it, one of these days," said Mrs. March.\par
\par
"No, I won't!" returned Jo stoutly, feeling much relieved that her prank\par
was not entirely condemned.\par
\par
"What made you do it?" asked Amy, who would as soon have thought of\par
cutting off her head as her pretty hair.\par
\par
"Well, I was wild to do something for father," replied Jo, as they\par
gathered about the table, for healthy young people can eat even in the\par
midst of trouble. "I hate to borrow as much as mother does, and I knew\par
Aunt March would croak; she always does, if you ask for a ninepence. Meg\par
gave all her quarterly salary toward the rent, and I only got some\par
clothes with mine, so I felt wicked, and was bound to have some money,\par
if I sold the nose off my face to get it."\par
\par
"You needn't feel wicked, my child: you had no winter things, and got\par
the simplest with your own hard earnings," said Mrs. March, with a look\par
that warmed Jo's heart.\par
\par
"I hadn't the least idea of selling my hair at first, but as I went\par
along I kept thinking what I could do, and feeling as if I'd like to\par
dive into some of the rich stores and help myself. In a barber's window\par
I saw tails of hair with the prices marked; and one black tail, not so\par
thick as mine, was forty dollars. It came over me all of a sudden that I\par
had one thing to make money out of, and without stopping to think, I\par
walked in, asked if they bought hair, and what they would give for\par
mine."\par
\par
"I don't see how you dared to do it," said Beth, in a tone of awe.\par
\par
"Oh, he was a little man who looked as if he merely lived to oil his\par
hair. He rather stared, at first, as if he wasn't used to having girls\par
bounce into his shop and ask him to buy their hair. He said he didn't\par
care about mine, it wasn't the fashionable color, and he never paid much\par
for it in the first place; the work put into it made it dear, and so on.\par
It was getting late, and I was afraid, if it wasn't done right away,\par
that I shouldn't have it done at all, and you know when I start to do a\par
thing, I hate to give it up; so I begged him to take it, and told him\par
why I was in such a hurry. It was silly, I dare say, but it changed his\par
mind, for I got rather excited, and told the story in my topsy-turvy\par
way, and his wife heard, and said so kindly,--\par
\par
"'Take it, Thomas, and oblige the young lady; I'd do as much for our\par
Jimmy any day if I had a spire of hair worth selling.'"\par
\par
"Who was Jimmy?" asked Amy, who liked to have things explained as they\par
went along.\par
\par
"Her son, she said, who was in the army. How friendly such things make\par
strangers feel, don't they? She talked away all the time the man\par
clipped, and diverted my mind nicely."\par
\par
                    [Illustration: The man clipped]\par
\par
"Didn't you feel dreadfully when the first cut came?" asked Meg, with a\par
shiver.\par
\par
"I took a last look at my hair while the man got his things, and that\par
was the end of it. I never snivel over trifles like that; I will\par
confess, though, I felt queer when I saw the dear old hair laid out on\par
the table, and felt only the short, rough ends on my head. It almost\par
seemed as if I'd an arm or a leg off. The woman saw me look at it, and\par
picked out a long lock for me to keep. I'll give it to you, Marmee, just\par
to remember past glories by; for a crop is so comfortable I don't think\par
I shall ever have a mane again."\par
\par
Mrs. March folded the wavy, chestnut lock, and laid it away with a short\par
gray one in her desk. She only said "Thank you, deary," but something in\par
her face made the girls change the subject, and talk as cheerfully as\par
they could about Mr. Brooke's kindness, the prospect of a fine day\par
to-morrow, and the happy times they would have when father came home to\par
be nursed.\par
\par
No one wanted to go to bed, when, at ten o'clock, Mrs. March put by the\par
last finished job, and said, "Come, girls." Beth went to the piano and\par
played the father's favorite hymn; all began bravely, but broke down\par
one by one, till Beth was left alone, singing with all her heart, for to\par
her music was always a sweet consoler.\par
\par
"Go to bed and don't talk, for we must be up early, and shall need all\par
the sleep we can get. Good-night, my darlings," said Mrs. March, as the\par
hymn ended, for no one cared to try another.\par
\par
They kissed her quietly, and went to bed as silently as if the dear\par
invalid lay in the next room. Beth and Amy soon fell asleep in spite of\par
the great trouble, but Meg lay awake, thinking the most serious thoughts\par
she had ever known in her short life. Jo lay motionless, and her sister\par
fancied that she was asleep, till a stifled sob made her exclaim, as she\par
touched a wet cheek,--\par
\par
"Jo, dear, what is it? Are you crying about father?"\par
\par
"No, not now."\par
\par
"What then?"\par
\par
"My--my hair!" burst out poor Jo, trying vainly to smother her emotion\par
in the pillow.\par
\par
It did not sound at all comical to Meg, who kissed and caressed the\par
afflicted heroine in the tenderest manner.\par
\par
"I'm not sorry," protested Jo, with a choke. "I'd do it again to-morrow,\par
if I could. It's only the vain, selfish part of me that goes and cries\par
in this silly way. Don't tell any one, it's all over now. I thought you\par
were asleep, so I just made a little private moan for my one beauty. How\par
came you to be awake?"\par
\par
"I can't sleep, I'm so anxious," said Meg.\par
\par
"Think about something pleasant, and you'll soon drop off."\par
\par
"I tried it, but felt wider awake than ever."\par
\par
"What did you think of?"\par
\par
"Handsome faces,--eyes particularly," answered Meg, smiling to herself,\par
in the dark.\par
\par
"What color do you like best?"\par
\par
"Brown--that is, sometimes; blue are lovely."\par
\par
Jo laughed, and Meg sharply ordered her not to talk, then amiably\par
promised to make her hair curl, and fell asleep to dream of living in\par
her castle in the air.\par
\par
The clocks were striking midnight, and the rooms were very still, as a\par
figure glided quietly from bed to bed, smoothing a coverlid here,\par
settling a pillow there, and pausing to look long and tenderly at each\par
unconscious face, to kiss each with lips that mutely blessed, and to\par
pray the fervent prayers which only mothers utter. As she lifted the\par
curtain to look out into the dreary night, the moon broke suddenly from\par
behind the clouds, and shone upon her like a bright, benignant face,\par
which seemed to whisper in the silence, "Be comforted, dear soul! There\par
is always light behind the clouds."\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                        [Illustration: Letters]\par
\par
                                  XVI.\par
\par
                                LETTERS.\par
\par
\par
In the cold gray dawn the sisters lit their lamp, and read their chapter\par
with an earnestness never felt before; for now the shadow of a real\par
trouble had come, the little books were full of help and comfort; and,\par
as they dressed, they agreed to say good-by cheerfully and hopefully,\par
and send their mother on her anxious journey unsaddened by tears or\par
complaints from them. Everything seemed very strange when they went\par
down,--so dim and still outside, so full of light and bustle within.\par
Breakfast at that early hour seemed odd, and even Hannah's familiar face\par
looked unnatural as she flew about her kitchen with her night-cap on.\par
The big trunk stood ready in the hall, mother's cloak and bonnet lay on\par
the sofa, and mother herself sat trying to eat, but looking so pale and\par
worn with sleeplessness and anxiety that the girls found it very hard to\par
keep their resolution. Meg's eyes kept filling in spite of herself; Jo\par
was obliged to hide her face in the kitchen roller more than once; and\par
the little girls' wore a grave, troubled expression, as if sorrow was a\par
new experience to them.\par
\par
Nobody talked much, but as the time drew very near, and they sat waiting\par
for the carriage, Mrs. March said to the girls, who were all busied\par
about her, one folding her shawl, another smoothing out the strings of\par
her bonnet, a third putting on her overshoes, and a fourth fastening up\par
her travelling bag,--\par
\par
"Children, I leave you to Hannah's care and Mr. Laurence's protection.\par
Hannah is faithfulness itself, and our good neighbor will guard you as\par
if you were his own. I have no fears for you, yet I am anxious that you\par
should take this trouble rightly. Don't grieve and fret when I am gone,\par
or think that you can comfort yourselves by being idle and trying to\par
forget. Go on with your work as usual, for work is a blessed solace.\par
Hope and keep busy; and whatever happens, remember that you never can be\par
fatherless."\par
\par
"Yes, mother."\par
\par
"Meg, dear, be prudent, watch over your sisters, consult Hannah, and, in\par
any perplexity, go to Mr. Laurence. Be patient, Jo, don't get despondent\par
or do rash things; write to me often, and be my brave girl, ready to\par
help and cheer us all. Beth, comfort yourself with your music, and be\par
faithful to the little home duties; and you, Amy, help all you can, be\par
obedient, and keep happy safe at home."\par
\par
"We will, mother! we will!"\par
\par
The rattle of an approaching carriage made them all start and listen.\par
That was the hard minute, but the girls stood it well: no one cried, no\par
one ran away or uttered a lamentation, though their hearts were very\par
heavy as they sent loving messages to father, remembering, as they\par
spoke, that it might be too late to deliver them. They kissed their\par
mother quietly, clung about her tenderly, and tried to wave their hands\par
cheerfully when she drove away.\par
\par
Laurie and his grandfather came over to see her off, and Mr. Brooke\par
looked so strong and sensible and kind that the girls christened him\par
"Mr. Greatheart" on the spot.\par
\par
"Good-by, my darlings! God bless and keep us all!" whispered Mrs. March,\par
as she kissed one dear little face after the other, and hurried into the\par
carriage.\par
\par
As she rolled away, the sun came out, and, looking back, she saw it\par
shining on the group at the gate, like a good omen. They saw it also,\par
and smiled and waved their hands; and the last thing she beheld, as she\par
turned the corner, was the four bright faces, and behind them, like a\par
body-guard, old Mr. Laurence, faithful Hannah, and devoted Laurie.\par
\par
                    [Illustration: She rolled away]\par
\par
"How kind every one is to us!" she said, turning to find fresh proof of\par
it in the respectful sympathy of the young man's face.\par
\par
"I don't see how they can help it," returned Mr. Brooke, laughing so\par
infectiously that Mrs. March could not help smiling; and so the long\par
journey began with the good omens of sunshine, smiles, and cheerful\par
words.\par
\par
"I feel as if there had been an earthquake," said Jo, as their neighbors\par
went home to breakfast, leaving them to rest and refresh themselves.\par
\par
"It seems as if half the house was gone," added Meg forlornly.\par
\par
Beth opened her lips to say something, but could only point to the pile\par
of nicely-mended hose which lay on mother's table, showing that even in\par
her last hurried moments she had thought and worked for them. It was a\par
little thing, but it went straight to their hearts; and, in spite of\par
their brave resolutions, they all broke down, and cried bitterly.\par
\par
Hannah wisely allowed them to relieve their feelings, and, when the\par
shower showed signs of clearing up, she came to the rescue, armed with a\par
coffee-pot.\par
\par
"Now, my dear young ladies, remember what your ma said, and don't fret.\par
Come and have a cup of coffee all round, and then let's fall to work,\par
and be a credit to the family."\par
\par
Coffee was a treat, and Hannah showed great tact in making it that\par
morning. No one could resist her persuasive nods, or the fragrant\par
invitation issuing from the nose of the coffee-pot. They drew up to the\par
table, exchanged their handkerchiefs for napkins, and in ten minutes\par
were all right again.\par
\par
"'Hope and keep busy;' that's the motto for us, so let's see who will\par
remember it best. I shall go to Aunt March, as usual. Oh, won't she\par
lecture though!" said Jo, as she sipped with returning spirit.\par
\par
"I shall go to my Kings, though I'd much rather stay at home and attend\par
to things here," said Meg, wishing she hadn't made her eyes so red.\par
\par
"No need of that; Beth and I can keep house perfectly well," put in Amy,\par
with an important air.\par
\par
"Hannah will tell us what to do; and we'll have everything nice when you\par
come home," added Beth, getting out her mop and dish-tub without delay.\par
\par
"I think anxiety is very interesting," observed Amy, eating sugar,\par
pensively.\par
\par
The girls couldn't help laughing, and felt better for it, though Meg\par
shook her head at the young lady who could find consolation in a\par
sugar-bowl.\par
\par
The sight of the turn-overs made Jo sober again; and when the two went\par
out to their daily tasks, they looked sorrowfully back at the window\par
where they were accustomed to see their mother's face. It was gone; but\par
Beth had remembered the little household ceremony, and there she was,\par
nodding away at them like a rosy-faced mandarin.\par
\par
"That's so like my Beth!" said Jo, waving her hat, with a grateful face.\par
"Good-by, Meggy; I hope the Kings won't train to-day. Don't fret about\par
father, dear," she added, as they parted.\par
\par
"And I hope Aunt March won't croak. Your hair _is_ becoming, and it\par
looks very boyish and nice," returned Meg, trying not to smile at the\par
curly head, which looked comically small on her tall sister's shoulders.\par
\par
"That's my only comfort;" and, touching her hat _à la_ Laurie, away went\par
Jo, feeling like a shorn sheep on a wintry day.\par
\par
News from their father comforted the girls very much; for, though\par
dangerously ill, the presence of the best and tenderest of nurses had\par
already done him good. Mr. Brooke sent a bulletin every day, and, as the\par
head of the family, Meg insisted on reading the despatches, which grew\par
more and more cheering as the week passed. At first, every one was eager\par
to write, and plump envelopes were carefully poked into the letter-box\par
by one or other of the sisters, who felt rather important with their\par
Washington correspondence. As one of these packets contained\par
characteristic notes from the party, we will rob an imaginary mail, and\par
read them:--\par
\par
    "MY DEAREST MOTHER,--\par
\par
    "It is impossible to tell you how happy your last letter made\par
    us, for the news was so good we couldn't help laughing and\par
    crying over it. How very kind Mr. Brooke is, and how fortunate\par
    that Mr. Laurence's business detains him near you so long,\par
    since he is so useful to you and father. The girls are all as\par
    good as gold. Jo helps me with the sewing, and insists on doing\par
    all sorts of hard jobs. I should be afraid she might overdo, if\par
    I didn't know that her 'moral fit' wouldn't last long. Beth is\par
    as regular about her tasks as a clock, and never forgets what\par
    you told her. She grieves about father, and looks sober except\par
    when she is at her little piano. Amy minds me nicely, and I\par
    take great care of her. She does her own hair, and I am\par
    teaching her to make button-holes and mend her stockings. She\par
    tries very hard, and I know you will be pleased with her\par
    improvement when you come. Mr. Laurence watches over us like a\par
    motherly old hen, as Jo says; and Laurie is very kind and\par
    neighborly. He and Jo keep us merry, for we get pretty blue\par
    sometimes, and feel like orphans, with you so far away. Hannah\par
    is a perfect saint; she does not scold at all, and always calls\par
    me Miss 'Margaret,' which is quite proper, you know, and treats\par
    me with respect. We are all well and busy; but we long, day\par
    and night, to have you back. Give my dearest love to father,\par
    and believe me, ever your own\par
                                                              "MEG."\par
\par
This note, prettily written on scented paper, was a great contrast to\par
the next, which was scribbled on a big sheet of thin foreign paper,\par
ornamented with blots and all manner of flourishes and curly-tailed\par
letters:--\par
\par
    "MY PRECIOUS MARMEE,--\par
\par
    "Three cheers for dear father! Brooke was a trump to telegraph\par
    right off, and let us know the minute he was better. I rushed\par
    up garret when the letter came, and tried to thank God for\par
    being so good to us; but I could only cry, and say, 'I'm glad!\par
    I'm glad!' Didn't that do as well as a regular prayer? for I\par
    felt a great many in my heart. We have such funny times; and\par
    now I can enjoy them, for every one is so desperately good,\par
    it's like living in a nest of turtle-doves. You'd laugh to see\par
    Meg head the table and try to be motherish. She gets prettier\par
    every day, and I'm in love with her sometimes. The children are\par
    regular archangels, and I--well, I'm Jo, and never shall be\par
    anything else. Oh, I must tell you that I came near having a\par
    quarrel with Laurie. I freed my mind about a silly little\par
    thing, and he was offended. I was right, but didn't speak as I\par
    ought, and he marched home, saying he wouldn't come again till\par
    I begged pardon. I declared I wouldn't, and got mad. It lasted\par
    all day; I felt bad, and wanted you very much. Laurie and I are\par
    both so proud, it's hard to beg pardon; but I thought he'd come\par
    to it, for I _was_ in the right. He didn't come; and just at\par
    night I remembered what you said when Amy fell into the river.\par
    I read my little book, felt better, resolved not to let the sun\par
    set on _my_ anger, and ran over to tell Laurie I was sorry. I\par
    met him at the gate, coming for the same thing. We both\par
    laughed, begged each other's pardon, and felt all good and\par
    comfortable again.\par
\par
    "I made a 'pome' yesterday, when I was helping Hannah wash; and,\par
    as father likes my silly little things, I put it in to amuse\par
    him. Give him the lovingest hug that ever was, and kiss yourself\par
    a dozen times for your\par
                                                   "TOPSY-TURVY JO."\par
\par
                         "A SONG FROM THE SUDS.\par
\par
        "Queen of my tub, I merrily sing,\par
           While the white foam rises high;\par
         And sturdily wash and rinse and wring,\par
           And fasten the clothes to dry;\par
         Then out in the free fresh air they swing,\par
           Under the sunny sky.\par
\par
        "I wish we could wash from our hearts and souls\par
           The stains of the week away,\par
         And let water and air by their magic make\par
           Ourselves as pure as they;\par
         Then on the earth there would be indeed\par
           A glorious washing-day!\par
\par
        "Along the path of a useful life,\par
           Will heart's-ease ever bloom;\par
         The busy mind has no time to think\par
           Of sorrow or care or gloom;\par
         And anxious thoughts may be swept away,\par
           As we bravely wield a broom.\par
\par
        "I am glad a task to me is given,\par
           To labor at day by day;\par
         For it brings me health and strength and hope,\par
           And I cheerfully learn to say,--\par
         'Head, you may think, Heart, you may feel,\par
           But, Hand, you shall work alway!'"\par
\par
    "DEAR MOTHER,--\par
\par
    "There is only room for me to send my love, and some pressed\par
    pansies from the root I have been keeping safe in the house for\par
    father to see. I read every morning, try to be good all day,\par
    and sing myself to sleep with father's tune. I can't sing 'Land\par
    of the Leal' now; it makes me cry. Every one is very kind, and\par
    we are as happy as we can be without you. Amy wants the rest of\par
    the page, so I must stop. I didn't forget to cover the holders,\par
    and I wind the clock and air the rooms every day.\par
\par
    "Kiss dear father on the cheek he calls mine. Oh, do come soon\par
    to your loving\par
                                                      "LITTLE BETH."\par
\par
                    [Illustration: I wind the clock]\par
\par
    "MA CHERE MAMMA,--\par
\par
    "We are all well I do my lessons always and never corroberate\par
    the girls--Meg says I mean contradick so I put in both words\par
    and you can take the properest. Meg is a great comfort to me\par
    and lets me have jelly every night at tea its so good for me Jo\par
    says because it keeps me sweet tempered. Laurie is not as\par
    respeckful as he ought to be now I am almost in my teens, he\par
    calls me Chick and hurts my feelings by talking French to me\par
    very fast when I say Merci or Bon jour as Hattie King does. The\par
    sleeves of my blue dress were all worn out, and Meg put in new\par
    ones, but the full front came wrong and they are more blue than\par
    the dress. I felt bad but did not fret I bear my troubles well\par
    but I do wish Hannah would put more starch in my aprons and\par
    have buckwheats every day. Can't she? Didn't I make that\par
    interrigation point nice? Meg says my punchtuation and spelling\par
    are disgraceful and I am mortyfied but dear me I have so many\par
    things to do, I can't stop. Adieu, I send heaps of love to\par
    Papa.\par
                      "Your affectionate daughter,\par
                                                 "AMY CURTIS MARCH."\par
\par
    "DEAR MIS MARCH,--\par
\par
    "I jes drop a line to say we git on fust rate. The girls is\par
    clever and fly round right smart. Miss Meg is going to make a\par
    proper good housekeeper; she hes the liking for it, and gits\par
    the hang of things surprisin quick. Jo doos beat all for goin\par
    ahead, but she don't stop to cal'k'late fust, and you never\par
    know where she's like to bring up. She done out a tub of\par
    clothes on Monday, but she starched 'em afore they was\par
    wrenched, and blued a pink calico dress till I thought I should\par
    a died a laughin. Beth is the best of little creeters, and a\par
    sight of help to me, bein so forehanded and dependable. She\par
    tries to learn everything, and really goes to market beyond her\par
    years; likewise keeps accounts, with my help, quite wonderful.\par
    We have got on very economical so fur; I don't let the girls\par
    hev coffee only once a week, accordin to your wish, and keep em\par
    on plain wholesome vittles. Amy does well about frettin, wearin\par
    her best clothes and eatin sweet stuff. Mr. Laurie is as full\par
    of didoes as usual, and turns the house upside down frequent;\par
    but he heartens up the girls, and so I let em hev full swing.\par
    The old gentleman sends heaps of things, and is rather wearin,\par
    but means wal, and it aint my place to say nothin. My bread is\par
    riz, so no more at this time. I send my duty to Mr. March, and\par
    hope he's seen the last of his Pewmonia.\par
                           "Yours Respectful,\par
                                                "HANNAH MULLET."\par
