\par
\par
\par
\par
                                 XVII.\par
\par
                            LITTLE FAITHFUL.\par
\par
\par
For a week the amount of virtue in the old house would have supplied the\par
neighborhood. It was really amazing, for every one seemed in a heavenly\par
frame of mind, and self-denial was all the fashion. Relieved of their\par
first anxiety about their father, the girls insensibly relaxed their\par
praiseworthy efforts a little, and began to fall back into the old ways.\par
They did not forget their motto, but hoping and keeping busy seemed to\par
grow easier; and after such tremendous exertions, they felt that\par
Endeavor deserved a holiday, and gave it a good many.\par
\par
Jo caught a bad cold through neglect to cover the shorn head enough, and\par
was ordered to stay at home till she was better, for Aunt March didn't\par
like to hear people read with colds in their heads. Jo liked this, and\par
after an energetic rummage from garret to cellar, subsided on the sofa\par
to nurse her cold with arsenicum and books. Amy found that housework and\par
art did not go well together, and returned to her mud pies. Meg went\par
daily to her pupils, and sewed, or thought she did, at home, but much\par
time was spent in writing long letters to her mother, or reading the\par
Washington despatches over and over. Beth kept on, with only slight\par
relapses into idleness or grieving. All the little duties were\par
faithfully done each day, and many of her sisters' also, for they were\par
forgetful, and the house seemed like a clock whose pendulum was gone\par
a-visiting. When her heart got heavy with longings for mother or fears\par
for father, she went away into a certain closet, hid her face in the\par
folds of a certain dear old gown, and made her little moan and prayed\par
her little prayer quietly by herself. Nobody knew what cheered her up\par
after a sober fit, but every one felt how sweet and helpful Beth was,\par
and fell into a way of going to her for comfort or advice in their small\par
affairs.\par
\par
All were unconscious that this experience was a test of character; and,\par
when the first excitement was over, felt that they had done well, and\par
deserved praise. So they did; but their mistake was in ceasing to do\par
well, and they learned this lesson through much anxiety and regret.\par
\par
"Meg, I wish you'd go and see the Hummels; you know mother told us not\par
to forget them," said Beth, ten days after Mrs. March's departure.\par
\par
"I'm too tired to go this afternoon," replied Meg, rocking comfortably\par
as she sewed.\par
\par
"Can't you, Jo?" asked Beth.\par
\par
"Too stormy for me with my cold."\par
\par
"I thought it was almost well."\par
\par
"It's well enough for me to go out with Laurie, but not well enough to\par
go to the Hummels'," said Jo, laughing, but looking a little ashamed of\par
her inconsistency.\par
\par
"Why don't you go yourself?" asked Meg.\par
\par
"I _have_ been every day, but the baby is sick, and I don't know what to\par
do for it. Mrs. Hummel goes away to work, and Lottchen takes care of it;\par
but it gets sicker and sicker, and I think you or Hannah ought to go."\par
\par
Beth spoke earnestly, and Meg promised she would go to-morrow.\par
\par
"Ask Hannah for some nice little mess, and take it round, Beth; the air\par
will do you good," said Jo, adding apologetically, "I'd go, but I want\par
to finish my writing."\par
\par
"My head aches and I'm tired, so I thought may be some of you would go,"\par
said Beth.\par
\par
"Amy will be in presently, and she will run down for us," suggested Meg.\par
\par
"Well, I'll rest a little and wait for her."\par
\par
So Beth lay down on the sofa, the others returned to their work, and the\par
Hummels were forgotten. An hour passed: Amy did not come; Meg went to\par
her room to try on a new dress; Jo was absorbed in her story, and Hannah\par
was sound asleep before the kitchen fire, when Beth quietly put on her\par
hood, filled her basket with odds and ends for the poor children, and\par
went out into the chilly air, with a heavy head, and a grieved look in\par
her patient eyes. It was late when she came back, and no one saw her\par
creep upstairs and shut herself into her mother's room. Half an hour\par
after Jo went to "mother's closet" for something, and there found Beth\par
sitting on the medicine chest, looking very grave, with red eyes, and a\par
camphor-bottle in her hand.\par
\par
"Christopher Columbus! What's the matter?" cried Jo, as Beth put out her\par
hand as if to warn her off, and asked quickly,--\par
\par
"You've had the scarlet fever, haven't you?"\par
\par
"Years ago, when Meg did. Why?"\par
\par
"Then I'll tell you. Oh, Jo, the baby's dead!"\par
\par
"What baby?"\par
\par
"Mrs. Hummel's; it died in my lap before she got home," cried Beth, with\par
a sob.\par
\par
"My poor dear, how dreadful for you! I ought to have gone," said Jo,\par
taking her sister in her arms as she sat down in her mother's big chair,\par
with a remorseful face.\par
\par
"It wasn't dreadful, Jo, only so sad! I saw in a minute that it was\par
sicker, but Lottchen said her mother had gone for a doctor, so I took\par
baby and let Lotty rest. It seemed asleep, but all of a sudden it gave a\par
little cry, and trembled, and then lay very still. I tried to warm its\par
feet, and Lotty gave it some milk, but it didn't stir, and I knew it was\par
dead."\par
\par
         [Illustration: It didn't stir, and I knew it was dead]\par
\par
"Don't cry, dear! What did you do?"\par
\par
"I just sat and held it softly till Mrs. Hummel came with the doctor. He\par
said it was dead, and looked at Heinrich and Minna, who have got sore\par
throats. 'Scarlet fever, ma'am. Ought to have called me before,' he said\par
crossly. Mrs. Hummel told him she was poor, and had tried to cure baby\par
herself, but now it was too late, and she could only ask him to help the\par
others, and trust to charity for his pay. He smiled then, and was\par
kinder; but it was very sad, and I cried with them till he turned round,\par
all of a sudden, and told me to go home and take belladonna right away,\par
or I'd have the fever."\par
\par
"No, you won't!" cried Jo, hugging her close, with a frightened look. "O\par
Beth, if you should be sick I never could forgive myself! What _shall_\par
we do?"\par
\par
"Don't be frightened, I guess I shan't have it badly. I looked in\par
mother's book, and saw that it begins with headache, sore throat, and\par
queer feelings like mine, so I did take some belladonna, and I feel\par
better," said Beth, laying her cold hands on her hot forehead, and\par
trying to look well.\par
\par
"If mother was only at home!" exclaimed Jo, seizing the book, and\par
feeling that Washington was an immense way off. She read a page, looked\par
at Beth, felt her head, peeped into her throat, and then said gravely;\par
"You've been over the baby every day for more than a week, and among the\par
others who are going to have it; so I'm afraid _you_ are going to have\par
it, Beth. I'll call Hannah, she knows all about sickness."\par
\par
"Don't let Amy come; she never had it, and I should hate to give it to\par
her. Can't you and Meg have it over again?" asked Beth, anxiously.\par
\par
"I guess not; don't care if I do; serve me right, selfish pig, to let\par
you go, and stay writing rubbish myself!" muttered Jo, as she went to\par
consult Hannah.\par
\par
The good soul was wide awake in a minute, and took the lead at once,\par
assuring Jo that there was no need to worry; every one had scarlet\par
fever, and, if rightly treated, nobody died,--all of which Jo believed,\par
and felt much relieved as they went up to call Meg.\par
\par
"Now I'll tell you what we'll do," said Hannah, when she had examined\par
and questioned Beth; "we will have Dr. Bangs, just to take a look at\par
you, dear, and see that we start right; then we'll send Amy off to Aunt\par
March's, for a spell, to keep her out of harm's way, and one of you\par
girls can stay at home and amuse Beth for a day or two."\par
\par
"I shall stay, of course; I'm oldest," began Meg, looking anxious and\par
self-reproachful.\par
\par
"_I_ shall, because it's my fault she is sick; I told mother I'd do the\par
errands, and I haven't," said Jo decidedly.\par
\par
"Which will you have, Beth? there ain't no need of but one," said\par
Hannah.\par
\par
"Jo, please;" and Beth leaned her head against her sister, with a\par
contented look, which effectually settled that point.\par
\par
"I'll go and tell Amy," said Meg, feeling a little hurt, yet rather\par
relieved, on the whole, for she did not like nursing, and Jo did.\par
\par
Amy rebelled outright, and passionately declared that she had rather\par
have the fever than go to Aunt March. Meg reasoned, pleaded, and\par
commanded: all in vain. Amy protested that she would _not_ go; and Meg\par
left her in despair, to ask Hannah what should be done. Before she came\par
back, Laurie walked into the parlor to find Amy sobbing, with her head\par
in the sofa-cushions. She told her story, expecting to be consoled; but\par
Laurie only put his hands in his pockets and walked about the room,\par
whistling softly, as he knit his brows in deep thought. Presently he sat\par
down beside her, and said, in his most wheedlesome tone, "Now be a\par
sensible little woman, and do as they say. No, don't cry, but hear what\par
a jolly plan I've got. You go to Aunt March's, and I'll come and take\par
you out every day, driving or walking, and we'll have capital times.\par
Won't that be better than moping here?"\par
\par
                 [Illustration: He sat down beside her]\par
\par
"I don't wish to be sent off as if I was in the way," began Amy, in an\par
injured voice.\par
\par
"Bless your heart, child, it's to keep you well. You don't want to be\par
sick, do you?"\par
\par
"No, I'm sure I don't; but I dare say I shall be, for I've been with\par
Beth all the time."\par
\par
"That's the very reason you ought to go away at once, so that you may\par
escape it. Change of air and care will keep you well, I dare say; or, if\par
it does not entirely, you will have the fever more lightly. I advise you\par
to be off as soon as you can, for scarlet fever is no joke, miss."\par
\par
"But it's dull at Aunt March's, and she is so cross," said Amy, looking\par
rather frightened.\par
\par
"It won't be dull with me popping in every day to tell you how Beth is,\par
and take you out gallivanting. The old lady likes me, and I'll be as\par
sweet as possible to her, so she won't peck at us, whatever we do."\par
\par
"Will you take me out in the trotting wagon with Puck?"\par
\par
"On my honor as a gentleman."\par
\par
"And come every single day?"\par
\par
"See if I don't."\par
\par
"And bring me back the minute Beth is well?"\par
\par
"The identical minute."\par
\par
"And go to the theatre, truly?"\par
\par
"A dozen theatres, if we may."\par
\par
"Well--I guess--I will," said Amy slowly.\par
\par
"Good girl! Call Meg, and tell her you'll give in," said Laurie, with an\par
approving pat, which annoyed Amy more than the "giving in."\par
\par
Meg and Jo came running down to behold the miracle which had been\par
wrought; and Amy, feeling very precious and self-sacrificing, promised\par
to go, if the doctor said Beth was going to be ill.\par
\par
"How is the little dear?" asked Laurie; for Beth was his especial pet,\par
and he felt more anxious about her than he liked to show.\par
\par
"She is lying down on mother's bed, and feels better. The baby's death\par
troubled her, but I dare say she has only got cold. Hannah _says_ she\par
thinks so; but she _looks_ worried, and that makes me fidgety," answered\par
Meg.\par
\par
"What a trying world it is!" said Jo, rumpling up her hair in a fretful\par
sort of way. "No sooner do we get out of one trouble than down comes\par
another. There doesn't seem to be anything to hold on to when mother's\par
gone; so I'm all at sea."\par
\par
"Well, don't make a porcupine of yourself, it isn't becoming. Settle\par
your wig, Jo, and tell me if I shall telegraph to your mother, or do\par
anything?" asked Laurie, who never had been reconciled to the loss of\par
his friend's one beauty.\par
\par
"That is what troubles me," said Meg. "I think we ought to tell her if\par
Beth is really ill, but Hannah says we mustn't, for mother can't leave\par
father, and it will only make them anxious. Beth won't be sick long, and\par
Hannah knows just what to do, and mother said we were to mind her, so I\par
suppose we must, but it doesn't seem quite right to me."\par
\par
"Hum, well, I can't say; suppose you ask grandfather after the doctor\par
has been."\par
\par
"We will. Jo, go and get Dr. Bangs at once," commanded Meg; "we can't\par
decide anything till he has been."\par
\par
"Stay where you are, Jo; I'm errand-boy to this establishment," said\par
Laurie, taking up his cap.\par
\par
"I'm afraid you are busy," began Meg.\par
\par
"No, I've done my lessons for the day."\par
\par
"Do you study in vacation time?" asked Jo.\par
\par
"I follow the good example my neighbors set me," was Laurie's answer, as\par
he swung himself out of the room.\par
\par
"I have great hopes of my boy," observed Jo, watching him fly over the\par
fence with an approving smile.\par
\par
"He does very well--for a boy," was Meg's somewhat ungracious answer,\par
for the subject did not interest her.\par
\par
Dr. Bangs came, said Beth had symptoms of the fever, but thought she\par
would have it lightly, though he looked sober over the Hummel story. Amy\par
was ordered off at once, and provided with something to ward off danger,\par
she departed in great state, with Jo and Laurie as escort.\par
\par
Aunt March received them with her usual hospitality.\par
\par
"What do you want now?" she asked, looking sharply over her spectacles,\par
while the parrot, sitting on the back of her chair, called out,--\par
\par
                 [Illustration: What do you want now?]\par
\par
"Go away. No boys allowed here."\par
\par
Laurie retired to the window, and Jo told her story.\par
\par
"No more than I expected, if you are allowed to go poking about among\par
poor folks. Amy can stay and make herself useful if she isn't sick,\par
which I've no doubt she will be,--looks like it now. Don't cry, child,\par
it worries me to hear people sniff."\par
\par
Amy _was_ on the point of crying, but Laurie slyly pulled the parrot's\par
tail, which caused Polly to utter an astonished croak, and call out,--\par
\par
"Bless my boots!" in such a funny way, that she laughed instead.\par
\par
"What do you hear from your mother?" asked the old lady gruffly.\par
\par
"Father is much better," replied Jo, trying to keep sober.\par
\par
"Oh, is he? Well, that won't last long, I fancy; March never had any\par
stamina," was the cheerful reply.\par
\par
"Ha, ha! never say die, take a pinch of snuff, good by, good by!"\par
squalled Polly, dancing on her perch, and clawing at the old lady's cap\par
as Laurie tweaked him in the rear.\par
\par
"Hold your tongue, you disrespectful old bird! and, Jo, you'd better go\par
at once; it isn't proper to be gadding about so late with a rattle-pated\par
boy like--"\par
\par
"Hold your tongue, you disrespectful old bird!" cried Polly, tumbling\par
off the chair with a bounce, and running to peck the "rattle-pated" boy,\par
who was shaking with laughter at the last speech.\par
\par
"I don't think I _can_ bear it, but I'll try," thought Amy, as she was\par
left alone with Aunt March.\par
\par
"Get along, you fright!" screamed Polly; and at that rude speech Amy\par
could not restrain a sniff.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                 XVIII.\par
\par
                               DARK DAYS.\par
\par
\par
                [Illustration: Beth did have the fever]\par
\par
Beth did have the fever, and was much sicker than any one but Hannah and\par
the doctor suspected. The girls knew nothing about illness, and Mr.\par
Laurence was not allowed to see her, so Hannah had everything all her\par
own way, and busy Dr. Bangs did his best, but left a good deal to the\par
excellent nurse. Meg stayed at home, lest she should infect the Kings,\par
and kept house, feeling very anxious and a little guilty when she wrote\par
letters in which no mention was made of Beth's illness. She could not\par
think it right to deceive her mother, but she had been bidden to mind\par
Hannah, and Hannah wouldn't hear of "Mrs. March bein' told, and worried\par
just for sech a trifle." Jo devoted herself to Beth day and night; not a\par
hard task, for Beth was very patient, and bore her pain uncomplainingly\par
as long as she could control herself. But there came a time when during\par
the fever fits she began to talk in a hoarse, broken voice, to play on\par
the coverlet, as if on her beloved little piano, and try to sing with a\par
throat so swollen that there was no music left; a time when she did not\par
know the familiar faces round her, but addressed them by wrong names,\par
and called imploringly for her mother. Then Jo grew frightened, Meg\par
begged to be allowed to write the truth, and even Hannah said she "would\par
think of it, though there was no danger _yet_." A letter from Washington\par
added to their trouble, for Mr. March had had a relapse, and could not\par
think of coming home for a long while.\par
\par
How dark the days seemed now, how sad and lonely the house, and how\par
heavy were the hearts of the sisters as they worked and waited, while\par
the shadow of death hovered over the once happy home! Then it was that\par
Margaret, sitting alone with tears dropping often on her work, felt how\par
rich she had been in things more precious than any luxuries money could\par
buy,--in love, protection, peace, and health, the real blessings of\par
life. Then it was that Jo, living in the darkened room, with that\par
suffering little sister always before her eyes, and that pathetic voice\par
sounding in her ears, learned to see the beauty and the sweetness of\par
Beth's nature, to feel how deep and tender a place she filled in all\par
hearts, and to acknowledge the worth of Beth's unselfish ambition, to\par
live for others, and make home happy by the exercise of those simple\par
virtues which all may possess, and which all should love and value more\par
than talent, wealth, or beauty. And Amy, in her exile, longed eagerly to\par
be at home, that she might work for Beth, feeling now that no service\par
would be hard or irksome, and remembering, with regretful grief, how\par
many neglected tasks those willing hands had done for her. Laurie\par
haunted the house like a restless ghost, and Mr. Laurence locked the\par
grand piano, because he could not bear to be reminded of the young\par
neighbor who used to make the twilight pleasant for him. Every one\par
missed Beth. The milkman, baker, grocer, and butcher inquired how she\par
did; poor Mrs. Hummel came to beg pardon for her thoughtlessness, and to\par
get a shroud for Minna; the neighbors sent all sorts of comforts and\par
good wishes, and even those who knew her best were surprised to find how\par
many friends shy little Beth had made.\par
\par
Meanwhile she lay on her bed with old Joanna at her side, for even in\par
her wanderings she did not forget her forlorn _protégé_. She longed for\par
her cats, but would not have them brought, lest they should get sick;\par
and, in her quiet hours, she was full of anxiety about Jo. She sent\par
loving messages to Amy, bade them tell her mother that she would write\par
soon; and often begged for pencil and paper to try to say a word, that\par
father might not think she had neglected him. But soon even these\par
intervals of consciousness ended, and she lay hour after hour, tossing\par
to and fro, with incoherent words on her lips, or sank into a heavy\par
sleep which brought her no refreshment. Dr. Bangs came twice a day,\par
Hannah sat up at night, Meg kept a telegram in her desk all ready to\par
send off at any minute, and Jo never stirred from Beth's side.\par
\par
The first of December was a wintry day indeed to them, for a bitter wind\par
blew, snow fell fast, and the year seemed getting ready for its death.\par
When Dr. Bangs came that morning, he looked long at Beth, held the hot\par
hand in both his own a minute, and laid it gently down, saying, in a low\par
tone, to Hannah,--\par
\par
"If Mrs. March _can_ leave her husband, she'd better be sent for."\par
\par
Hannah nodded without speaking, for her lips twitched nervously; Meg\par
dropped down into a chair as the strength seemed to go out of her limbs\par
at the sound of those words; and Jo, after standing with a pale face for\par
a minute, ran to the parlor, snatched up the telegram, and, throwing on\par
her things, rushed out into the storm. She was soon back, and, while\par
noiselessly taking off her cloak, Laurie came in with a letter, saying\par
that Mr. March was mending again. Jo read it thankfully, but the heavy\par
weight did not seem lifted off her heart, and her face was so full of\par
misery that Laurie asked quickly,--\par
\par
"What is it? is Beth worse?"\par
\par
"I've sent for mother," said Jo, tugging at her rubber boots with a\par
tragical expression.\par
\par
"Good for you, Jo! Did you do it on your own responsibility?" asked\par
Laurie, as he seated her in the hall chair, and took off the rebellious\par
boots, seeing how her hands shook.\par
\par
"No, the doctor told us to."\par
\par
"O Jo, it's not so bad as that?" cried Laurie, with a startled face.\par
\par
"Yes, it is; she doesn't know us, she doesn't even talk about the\par
flocks of green doves, as she calls the vine-leaves on the wall; she\par
doesn't look like my Beth, and there's nobody to help us bear it; mother\par
and father both gone, and God seems so far away I can't find Him."\par
\par
As the tears streamed fast down poor Jo's cheeks, she stretched out her\par
hand in a helpless sort of way, as if groping in the dark, and Laurie\par
took it in his, whispering, as well as he could, with a lump in his\par
throat,--\par
\par
"I'm here. Hold on to me, Jo, dear!"\par
\par
She could not speak, but she did "hold on," and the warm grasp of the\par
friendly human hand comforted her sore heart, and seemed to lead her\par
nearer to the Divine arm which alone could uphold her in her trouble.\par
Laurie longed to say something tender and comfortable, but no fitting\par
words came to him, so he stood silent, gently stroking her bent head as\par
her mother used to do. It was the best thing he could have done; far\par
more soothing than the most eloquent words, for Jo felt the unspoken\par
sympathy, and, in the silence, learned the sweet solace which affection\par
administers to sorrow. Soon she dried the tears which had relieved her,\par
and looked up with a grateful face.\par
\par
   [Illustration: Gently stroking her head as her mother used to do]\par
\par
"Thank you, Teddy, I'm better now; I don't feel so forlorn, and will try\par
to bear it if it comes."\par
\par
"Keep hoping for the best; that will help you, Jo. Soon your mother will\par
be here, and then everything will be right."\par
\par
"I'm so glad father is better; now she won't feel so bad about leaving\par
him. Oh, me! it does seem as if all the troubles came in a heap, and I\par
got the heaviest part on my shoulders," sighed Jo, spreading her wet\par
handkerchief over her knees to dry.\par
\par
"Doesn't Meg pull fair?" asked Laurie, looking indignant.\par
\par
"Oh, yes; she tries to, but she can't love Bethy as I do; and she won't\par
miss her as I shall. Beth is my conscience, and I _can't_ give her up. I\par
can't! I can't!"\par
\par
Down went Jo's face into the wet handkerchief, and she cried\par
despairingly; for she had kept up bravely till now, and never shed a\par
tear. Laurie drew his hand across his eyes, but could not speak till he\par
had subdued the choky feeling in his throat and steadied his lips. It\par
might be unmanly, but he couldn't help it, and I am glad of it.\par
Presently, as Jo's sobs quieted, he said hopefully, "I don't think she\par
will die; she's so good, and we all love her so much, I don't believe\par
God will take her away yet."\par
\par
"The good and dear people always do die," groaned Jo, but she stopped\par
crying, for her friend's words cheered her up, in spite of her own\par
doubts and fears.\par
\par
"Poor girl, you're worn out. It isn't like you to be forlorn. Stop a\par
bit; I'll hearten you up in a jiffy."\par
\par
Laurie went off two stairs at a time, and Jo laid her wearied head down\par
on Beth's little brown hood, which no one had thought of moving from the\par
table where she left it. It must have possessed some magic, for the\par
submissive spirit of its gentle owner seemed to enter into Jo; and, when\par
Laurie came running down with a glass of wine, she took it with a smile,\par
and said bravely, "I drink--Health to my Beth! You are a good doctor,\par
Teddy, and _such_ a comfortable friend; how can I ever pay you?" she\par
added, as the wine refreshed her body, as the kind words had done her\par
troubled mind.\par
\par
"I'll send in my bill, by and by; and to-night I'll give you something\par
that will warm the cockles of your heart better than quarts of wine,"\par
said Laurie, beaming at her with a face of suppressed satisfaction at\par
something.\par
\par
"What is it?" cried Jo, forgetting her woes for a minute, in her wonder.\par
\par
"I telegraphed to your mother yesterday, and Brooke answered she'd come\par
at once, and she'll be here to-night, and everything will be all right.\par
Aren't you glad I did it?"\par
\par
Laurie spoke very fast, and turned red and excited all in a minute, for\par
he had kept his plot a secret, for fear of disappointing the girls or\par
harming Beth. Jo grew quite white, flew out of her chair, and the moment\par
he stopped speaking she electrified him by throwing her arms round his\par
neck, and crying out, with a joyful cry, "O Laurie! O mother! I _am_ so\par
glad!" She did not weep again, but laughed hysterically, and trembled\par
and clung to her friend as if she was a little bewildered by the sudden\par
news. Laurie, though decidedly amazed, behaved with great presence of\par
mind; he patted her back soothingly, and, finding that she was\par
recovering, followed it up by a bashful kiss or two, which brought Jo\par
round at once. Holding on to the banisters, she put him gently away,\par
saying breathlessly, "Oh, don't! I didn't mean to; it was dreadful of\par
me; but you were such a dear to go and do it in spite of Hannah that I\par
couldn't help flying at you. Tell me all about it, and don't give me\par
wine again; it makes me act so."\par
\par
"I don't mind," laughed Laurie, as he settled his tie. "Why, you see I\par
got fidgety, and so did grandpa. We thought Hannah was overdoing the\par
authority business, and your mother ought to know. She'd never forgive\par
us if Beth--well, if anything happened, you know. So I got grandpa to\par
say it was high time we did something, and off I pelted to the office\par
yesterday, for the doctor looked sober, and Hannah most took my head off\par
when I proposed a telegram. I never _can_ bear to be 'lorded over;' so\par
that settled my mind, and I did it. Your mother will come, I know, and\par
the late train is in at two, A.M. I shall go for her; and you've only\par
got to bottle up your rapture, and keep Beth quiet, till that blessed\par
lady gets here."\par
\par
"Laurie, you're an angel! How shall I ever thank you?"\par
\par
"Fly at me again; I rather like it," said Laurie, looking\par
mischievous,--a thing he had not done for a fortnight.\par
\par
"No, thank you. I'll do it by proxy, when your grandpa comes. Don't\par
tease, but go home and rest, for you'll be up half the night. Bless you,\par
Teddy, bless you!"\par
\par
Jo had backed into a corner; and, as she finished her speech, she\par
vanished precipitately into the kitchen, where she sat down upon a\par
dresser, and told the assembled cats that she was "happy, oh, _so_\par
happy!" while Laurie departed, feeling that he had made rather a neat\par
thing of it.\par
\par
"That's the interferingest chap I ever see; but I forgive him, and do\par
hope Mrs. March is coming on right away," said Hannah, with an air of\par
relief, when Jo told the good news.\par
\par
Meg had a quiet rapture, and then brooded over the letter, while Jo set\par
the sick-room in order, and Hannah "knocked up a couple of pies in case\par
of company unexpected." A breath of fresh air seemed to blow through the\par
house, and something better than sunshine brightened the quiet rooms.\par
Everything appeared to feel the hopeful change; Beth's bird began to\par
chirp again, and a half-blown rose was discovered on Amy's bush in the\par
window; the fires seemed to burn with unusual cheeriness; and every time\par
the girls met, their pale faces broke into smiles as they hugged one\par
another, whispering encouragingly, "Mother's coming, dear! mother's\par
coming!" Every one rejoiced but Beth; she lay in that heavy stupor,\par
alike unconscious of hope and joy, doubt and danger. It was a piteous\par
sight,--the once rosy face so changed and vacant, the once busy hands so\par
weak and wasted, the once smiling lips quite dumb, and the once pretty,\par
well-kept hair scattered rough and tangled on the pillow. All day she\par
lay so, only rousing now and then to mutter, "Water!" with lips so\par
parched they could hardly shape the word; all day Jo and Meg hovered\par
over her, watching, waiting, hoping, and trusting in God and mother; and\par
all day the snow fell, the bitter wind raged, and the hours dragged\par
slowly by. But night came at last; and every time the clock struck, the\par
sisters, still sitting on either side the bed, looked at each other with\par
brightening eyes, for each hour brought help nearer. The doctor had been\par
in to say that some change, for better or worse, would probably take\par
place about midnight, at which time he would return.\par
\par
Hannah, quite worn out, lay down on the sofa at the bed's foot, and fell\par
fast asleep; Mr. Laurence marched to and fro in the parlor, feeling that\par
he would rather face a rebel battery than Mrs. March's anxious\par
countenance as she entered; Laurie lay on the rug, pretending to rest,\par
but staring into the fire with the thoughtful look which made his black\par
eyes beautifully soft and clear.\par
\par
The girls never forgot that night, for no sleep came to them as they\par
kept their watch, with that dreadful sense of powerlessness which comes\par
to us in hours like those.\par
\par
"If God spares Beth I never will complain again," whispered Meg\par
earnestly.\par
\par
"If God spares Beth I'll try to love and serve Him all my life,"\par
answered Jo, with equal fervor.\par
\par
"I wish I had no heart, it aches so," sighed Meg, after a pause.\par
\par
"If life is often as hard as this, I don't see how we ever shall get\par
through it," added her sister despondently.\par
\par
Here the clock struck twelve, and both forgot themselves in watching\par
Beth, for they fancied a change passed over her wan face. The house was\par
still as death, and nothing but the wailing of the wind broke the deep\par
hush. Weary Hannah slept on, and no one but the sisters saw the pale\par
shadow which seemed to fall upon the little bed. An hour went by, and\par
nothing happened except Laurie's quiet departure for the station.\par
Another hour,--still no one came; and anxious fears of delay in the\par
storm, or accidents by the way, or, worst of all, a great grief at\par
Washington, haunted the poor girls.\par
\par
It was past two, when Jo, who stood at the window thinking how dreary\par
the world looked in its winding-sheet of snow, heard a movement by the\par
bed, and, turning quickly, saw Meg kneeling before their mother's\par
easy-chair, with her face hidden. A dreadful fear passed coldly over Jo,\par
as she thought, "Beth is dead, and Meg is afraid to tell me."\par
\par
She was back at her post in an instant, and to her excited eyes a great\par
change seemed to have taken place. The fever flush and the look of pain\par
were gone, and the beloved little face looked so pale and peaceful in\par
its utter repose, that Jo felt no desire to weep or to lament. Leaning\par
low over this dearest of her sisters, she kissed the damp forehead with\par
her heart on her lips, and softly whispered, "Good-by, my Beth;\par
good-by!"\par
\par
As if waked by the stir, Hannah started out of her sleep, hurried to the\par
bed, looked at Beth, felt her hands, listened at her lips, and then,\par
throwing her apron over her head, sat down to rock to and fro,\par
exclaiming, under her breath, "The fever's turned; she's sleepin'\par
nat'ral; her skin's damp, and she breathes easy. Praise be given! Oh, my\par
goodness me!"\par
\par
Before the girls could believe the happy truth, the doctor came to\par
confirm it. He was a homely man, but they thought his face quite\par
heavenly when he smiled, and said, with a fatherly look at them, "Yes,\par
my dears, I think the little girl will pull through this time. Keep the\par
house quiet; let her sleep, and when she wakes, give her--"\par
\par
What they were to give, neither heard; for both crept into the dark\par
hall, and, sitting on the stairs, held each other close, rejoicing with\par
hearts too full for words. When they went back to be kissed and cuddled\par
by faithful Hannah, they found Beth lying, as she used to do, with her\par
cheek pillowed on her hand, the dreadful pallor gone, and breathing\par
quietly, as if just fallen asleep.\par
\par
"If mother would only come now!" said Jo, as the winter night began to\par
wane.\par
\par
"See," said Meg, coming up with a white, half-opened rose, "I thought\par
this would hardly be ready to lay in Beth's hand to-morrow if she--went\par
away from us. But it has blossomed in the night, and now I mean to put\par
it in my vase here, so that when the darling wakes, the first thing she\par
sees will be the little rose, and mother's face."\par
\par
Never had the sun risen so beautifully, and never had the world seemed\par
so lovely, as it did to the heavy eyes of Meg and Jo, as they looked out\par
in the early morning, when their long, sad vigil was done.\par
\par
"It looks like a fairy world," said Meg, smiling to herself, as she\par
stood behind the curtain, watching the dazzling sight.\par
\par
"Hark!" cried Jo, starting to her feet.\par
\par
Yes, there was a sound of bells at the door below, a cry from Hannah,\par
and then Laurie's voice saying, in a joyful whisper, "Girls, she's come!\par
she's come!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Amy's Will]\par
\par
                                  XIX.\par
\par
                              AMY'S WILL.\par
\par
\par
While these things were happening at home, Amy was having hard times at\par
Aunt March's. She felt her exile deeply, and, for the first time in her\par
life, realized how much she was beloved and petted at home. Aunt March\par
never petted any one; she did not approve of it; but she meant to be\par
kind, for the well-behaved little girl pleased her very much, and Aunt\par
March had a soft place in her old heart for her nephew's children,\par
though she didn't think proper to confess it. She really did her best to\par
make Amy happy, but, dear me, what mistakes she made! Some old people\par
keep young at heart in spite of wrinkles and gray hairs, can sympathize\par
with children's little cares and joys, make them feel at home, and can\par
hide wise lessons under pleasant plays, giving and receiving friendship\par
in the sweetest way. But Aunt March had not this gift, and she worried\par
Amy very much with her rules and orders, her prim ways, and long, prosy\par
talks. Finding the child more docile and amiable than her sister, the\par
old lady felt it her duty to try and counteract, as far as possible, the\par
bad effects of home freedom and indulgence. So she took Amy in hand, and\par
taught her as she herself had been taught sixty years ago,--a process\par
which carried dismay to Amy's soul, and made her feel like a fly in the\par
web of a very strict spider.\par
\par
     [Illustration: Polish up the spoons and the fat silver teapot]\par
\par
She had to wash the cups every morning, and polish up the old-fashioned\par
spoons, the fat silver teapot, and the glasses, till they shone. Then\par
she must dust the room, and what a trying job that was! Not a speck\par
escaped Aunt March's eye, and all the furniture had claw legs, and much\par
carving, which was never dusted to suit. Then Polly must be fed, the\par
lap-dog combed, and a dozen trips upstairs and down, to get things, or\par
deliver orders, for the old lady was very lame, and seldom left her big\par
chair. After these tiresome labors, she must do her lessons, which was a\par
daily trial of every virtue she possessed. Then she was allowed one hour\par
for exercise or play, and didn't she enjoy it? Laurie came every day,\par
and wheedled Aunt March, till Amy was allowed to go out with him, when\par
they walked and rode, and had capital times. After dinner, she had to\par
read aloud, and sit still while the old lady slept, which she usually\par
did for an hour, as she dropped off over the first page. Then patchwork\par
or towels appeared, and Amy sewed with outward meekness and inward\par
rebellion till dusk, when she was allowed to amuse herself as she liked\par
till tea-time. The evenings were the worst of all, for Aunt March fell\par
to telling long stories about her youth, which were so unutterably dull\par
that Amy was always ready to go to bed, intending to cry over her hard\par
fate, but usually going to sleep before she had squeezed out more than a\par
tear or two.\par
\par
If it had not been for Laurie, and old Esther, the maid, she felt that\par
she never could have got through that dreadful time. The parrot alone\par
was enough to drive her distracted, for he soon felt that she did not\par
admire him, and revenged himself by being as mischievous as possible. He\par
pulled her hair whenever she came near him, upset his bread and milk to\par
plague her when she had newly cleaned his cage, made Mop bark by pecking\par
at him while Madam dozed; called her names before company, and behaved\par
in all respects like a reprehensible old bird. Then she could not endure\par
the dog,--a fat, cross beast, who snarled and yelped at her when she\par
made his toilet, and who lay on his back, with all his legs in the air\par
and a most idiotic expression of countenance when he wanted something to\par
eat, which was about a dozen times a day. The cook was bad-tempered, the\par
old coachman deaf, and Esther the only one who ever took any notice of\par
the young lady.\par
\par
       [Illustration: On his back, with all his legs in the air]\par
\par
Esther was a Frenchwoman, who had lived with "Madame," as she called her\par
mistress, for many years, and who rather tyrannized over the old lady,\par
who could not get along without her. Her real name was Estelle, but\par
Aunt March ordered her to change it, and she obeyed, on condition that\par
she was never asked to change her religion. She took a fancy to\par
Mademoiselle, and amused her very much, with odd stories of her life in\par
France, when Amy sat with her while she got up Madame's laces. She also\par
allowed her to roam about the great house, and examine the curious and\par
pretty things stored away in the big wardrobes and the ancient chests;\par
for Aunt March hoarded like a magpie. Amy's chief delight was an Indian\par
cabinet, full of queer drawers, little pigeon-holes, and secret places,\par
in which were kept all sorts of ornaments, some precious, some merely\par
curious, all more or less antique. To examine and arrange these things\par
gave Amy great satisfaction, especially the jewel-cases, in which, on\par
velvet cushions, reposed the ornaments which had adorned a belle forty\par
years ago. There was the garnet set which Aunt March wore when she came\par
out, the pearls her father gave her on her wedding-day, her lover's\par
diamonds, the jet mourning rings and pins, the queer lockets, with\par
portraits of dead friends, and weeping willows made of hair inside; the\par
baby bracelets her one little daughter had worn; Uncle March's big\par
watch, with the red seal so many childish hands had played with, and in\par
a box, all by itself, lay Aunt March's wedding-ring, too small now for\par
her fat finger, but put carefully away, like the most precious jewel of\par
them all.\par
\par
                  [Illustration: I should choose this]\par
\par
"Which would Mademoiselle choose if she had her will?" asked Esther, who\par
always sat near to watch over and lock up the valuables.\par
\par
"I like the diamonds best, but there is no necklace among them, and I'm\par
fond of necklaces, they are so becoming. I should choose this if I\par
might," replied Amy, looking with great admiration at a string of gold\par
and ebony beads, from which hung a heavy cross of the same.\par
\par
"I, too, covet that, but not as a necklace; ah, no! to me it is a\par
rosary, and as such I should use it like a good Catholic," said Esther,\par
eying the handsome thing wistfully.\par
\par
"Is it meant to use as you use the string of good-smelling wooden beads\par
hanging over your glass?" asked Amy.\par
\par
"Truly, yes, to pray with. It would be pleasing to the saints if one\par
used so fine a rosary as this, instead of wearing it as a vain bijou."\par
\par
"You seem to take a great deal of comfort in your prayers, Esther, and\par
always come down looking quiet and satisfied. I wish I could."\par
\par
"If Mademoiselle was a Catholic, she would find true comfort; but, as\par
that is not to be, it would be well if you went apart each day, to\par
meditate and pray, as did the good mistress whom I served before Madame.\par
She had a little chapel, and in it found solacement for much trouble."\par
\par
"Would it be right for me to do so too?" asked Amy, who, in her\par
loneliness, felt the need of help of some sort, and found that she was\par
apt to forget her little book, now that Beth was not there to remind her\par
of it.\par
\par
"It would be excellent and charming; and I shall gladly arrange the\par
little dressing-room for you if you like it. Say nothing to Madame, but\par
when she sleeps go you and sit alone a while to think good thoughts, and\par
pray the dear God to preserve your sister."\par
\par
Esther was truly pious, and quite sincere in her advice; for she had an\par
affectionate heart, and felt much for the sisters in their anxiety. Amy\par
liked the idea, and gave her leave to arrange the light closet next her\par
room, hoping it would do her good.\par
\par
"I wish I knew where all these pretty things would go when Aunt March\par
dies," she said, as she slowly replaced the shining rosary, and shut the\par
jewel-cases one by one.\par
\par
"To you and your sisters. I know it; Madame confides in me; I witnessed\par
her will, and it is to be so," whispered Esther, smiling.\par
\par
"How nice! but I wish she'd let us have them now. Pro-cras-ti-nation is\par
not agreeable," observed Amy, taking a last look at the diamonds.\par
\par
"It is too soon yet for the young ladies to wear these things. The first\par
one who is affianced will have the pearls--Madame has said it; and I\par
have a fancy that the little turquoise ring will be given to you when\par
you go, for Madame approves your good behavior and charming manners."\par
\par
"Do you think so? Oh, I'll be a lamb, if I can only have that lovely\par
ring! It's ever so much prettier than Kitty Bryant's. I do like Aunt\par
March, after all;" and Amy tried on the blue ring with a delighted face,\par
and a firm resolve to earn it.\par
\par
From that day she was a model of obedience, and the old lady\par
complacently admired the success of her training. Esther fitted up the\par
closet with a little table, placed a footstool before it, and over it a\par
picture taken from one of the shut-up rooms. She thought it was of no\par
great value, but, being appropriate, she borrowed it, well knowing that\par
Madame would never know it, nor care if she did. It was, however, a very\par
valuable copy of one of the famous pictures of the world, and Amy's\par
beauty-loving eyes were never tired of looking up at the sweet face of\par
the divine mother, while tender thoughts of her own were busy at her\par
heart. On the table she laid her little Testament and hymn-book, kept a\par
vase always full of the best flowers Laurie brought her, and came every\par
day to "sit alone, thinking good thoughts, and praying the dear God to\par
preserve her sister." Esther had given her a rosary of black beads, with\par
a silver cross, but Amy hung it up and did not use it, feeling doubtful\par
as to its fitness for Protestant prayers.\par
\par
The little girl was very sincere in all this, for, being left alone\par
outside the safe home-nest, she felt the need of some kind hand to hold\par
by so sorely, that she instinctively turned to the strong and tender\par
Friend, whose fatherly love most closely surrounds his little children.\par
She missed her mother's help to understand and rule herself, but having\par
been taught where to look, she did her best to find the way, and walk in\par
it confidingly. But Amy was a young pilgrim, and just now her burden\par
seemed very heavy. She tried to forget herself, to keep cheerful, and be\par
satisfied with doing right, though no one saw or praised her for it. In\par
her first effort at being very, very good, she decided to make her will,\par
as Aunt March had done; so that if she _did_ fall ill and die, her\par
possessions might be justly and generously divided. It cost her a pang\par
even to think of giving up the little treasures which in her eyes were\par
as precious as the old lady's jewels.\par
\par
During one of her play-hours she wrote out the important document as\par
well as she could, with some help from Esther as to certain legal terms,\par
and, when the good-natured Frenchwoman had signed her name, Amy felt\par
relieved, and laid it by to show Laurie, whom she wanted as a second\par
witness. As it was a rainy day, she went upstairs to amuse herself in\par
one of the large chambers, and took Polly with her for company. In this\par
room there was a wardrobe full of old-fashioned costumes, with which\par
Esther allowed her to play, and it was her favorite amusement to array\par
herself in the faded brocades, and parade up and down before the long\par
mirror, making stately courtesies, and sweeping her train about, with a\par
rustle which delighted her ears. So busy was she on this day that she\par
did not hear Laurie's ring, nor see his face peeping in at her, as she\par
gravely promenaded to and fro, flirting her fan and tossing her head, on\par
which she wore a great pink turban, contrasting oddly with her blue\par
brocade dress and yellow quilted petticoat. She was obliged to walk\par
carefully, for she had on high-heeled shoes, and, as Laurie told Jo\par
afterward, it was a comical sight to see her mince along in her gay\par
suit, with Polly sidling and bridling just behind her, imitating her as\par
well as he could, and occasionally stopping to laugh or exclaim, "Ain't\par
we fine? Get along, you fright! Hold your tongue! Kiss me, dear! Ha!\par
ha!"\par
\par
             [Illustration: Gravely promenaded to and fro]\par
\par
Having with difficulty restrained an explosion of merriment, lest it\par
should offend her majesty, Laurie tapped, and was graciously received.\par
\par
"Sit down and rest while I put these things away; then I want to consult\par
you about a very serious matter," said Amy, when she had shown her\par
splendor, and driven Polly into a corner. "That bird is the trial of my\par
life," she continued, removing the pink mountain from her head, while\par
Laurie seated himself astride of a chair. "Yesterday, when aunt was\par
asleep, and I was trying to be as still as a mouse, Polly began to\par
squall and flap about in his cage; so I went to let him out, and found a\par
big spider there. I poked it out, and it ran under the bookcase; Polly\par
marched straight after it, stooped down and peeped under the bookcase,\par
saying, in his funny way, with a cock of his eye, 'Come out and take a\par
walk, my dear.' I _couldn't_ help laughing, which made Poll swear, and\par
aunt woke up and scolded us both."\par
\par
"Did the spider accept the old fellow's invitation?" asked Laurie,\par
yawning.\par
\par
"Yes; out it came, and away ran Polly, frightened to death, and\par
scrambled up on aunt's chair, calling out, 'Catch her! catch her! catch\par
her!' as I chased the spider.\par
\par
"That's a lie! Oh lor!" cried the parrot, pecking at Laurie's toes.\par
\par
"I'd wring your neck if you were mine, you old torment," cried Laurie,\par
shaking his fist at the bird, who put his head on one side, and gravely\par
croaked, "Allyluyer! bless your buttons, dear!"\par
\par
"Now I'm ready," said Amy, shutting the wardrobe, and taking a paper out\par
of her pocket. "I want you to read that, please, and tell me if it is\par
legal and right. I felt that I ought to do it, for life is uncertain and\par
I don't want any ill-feeling over my tomb."\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Amy's Will]\par
\par
Laurie bit his lips, and turning a little from the pensive speaker, read\par
the following document, with praiseworthy gravity, considering the\par
spelling:--\par
\par
    "MY LAST WILL AND TESTIMENT.\par
\par
    "I, Amy Curtis March, being in my sane mind, do give and\par
    bequeethe all my earthly property--viz. to wit:--namely\par
\par
    "To my father, my best pictures, sketches, maps, and works of\par
    art, including frames. Also my $100, to do what he likes with.\par
\par
    "To my mother, all my clothes, except the blue apron with\par
    pockets,--also my likeness, and my medal, with much love.\par
\par
    "To my dear sister Margaret, I give my turkquoise ring (if I get\par
    it), also my green box with the doves on it, also my piece of\par
    real lace for her neck, and my sketch of her as a memorial of\par
    her 'little girl.'\par
\par
    "To Jo I leave my breast-pin, the one mended with sealing wax,\par
    also my bronze inkstand--she lost the cover--and my most\par
    precious plaster rabbit, because I am sorry I burnt up her\par
    story.\par
\par
    "To Beth (if she lives after me) I give my dolls and the little\par
    bureau, my fan, my linen collars and my new slippers if she can\par
    wear them being thin when she gets well. And I herewith also\par
    leave her my regret that I ever made fun of old Joanna.\par
\par
    "To my friend and neighbor Theodore Laurence I bequeethe my\par
    paper marshay portfolio, my clay model of a horse though he did\par
    say it hadn't any neck. Also in return for his great kindness in\par
    the hour of affliction any one of my artistic works he likes,\par
    Noter Dame is the best.\par
\par
    "To our venerable benefactor Mr. Laurence I leave my purple box\par
    with a looking glass in the cover which will be nice for his\par
    pens and remind him of the departed girl who thanks him for his\par
    favors to her family, specially Beth.\par
\par
    "I wish my favorite playmate Kitty Bryant to have the blue silk\par
    apron and my gold-bead ring with a kiss.\par
\par
    "To Hannah I give the bandbox she wanted and all the patch work\par
    I leave hoping she 'will remember me, when it you see.'\par
\par
    "And now having disposed of my most valuable property I hope all\par
    will be satisfied and not blame the dead. I forgive every one,\par
    and trust we may all meet when the trump shall sound. Amen.\par
\par
    "To this will and testiment I set my hand and seal on this 20th\par
    day of Nov. Anni Domino 1861.\par
                                                  "AMY CURTIS MARCH.\par
\par
                  \{ESTELLE VALNOR,\par
    "_Witnesses_: \{\par
                  \{THEODORE LAURENCE."\par
\par
The last name was written in pencil, and Amy explained that he was to\par
rewrite it in ink, and seal it up for her properly.\par
\par
"What put it into your head? Did any one tell you about Beth's giving\par
away her things?" asked Laurie soberly, as Amy laid a bit of red tape,\par
with sealing-wax, a taper, and a standish before him.\par
\par
She explained; and then asked anxiously, "What about Beth?"\par
\par
"I'm sorry I spoke; but as I did, I'll tell you. She felt so ill one day\par
that she told Jo she wanted to give her piano to Meg, her cats to you,\par
and the poor old doll to Jo, who would love it for her sake. She was\par
sorry she had so little to give, and left locks of hair to the rest of\par
us, and her best love to grandpa. _She_ never thought of a will."\par
\par
Laurie was signing and sealing as he spoke, and did not look up till a\par
great tear dropped on the paper. Amy's face was full of trouble; but she\par
only said, "Don't people put sort of postscripts to their wills,\par
sometimes?"\par
\par
"Yes; 'codicils,' they call them."\par
\par
"Put one in mine then--that I wish _all_ my curls cut off, and given\par
round to my friends. I forgot it; but I want it done, though it will\par
spoil my looks."\par
\par
Laurie added it, smiling at Amy's last and greatest sacrifice. Then he\par
amused her for an hour, and was much interested in all her trials. But\par
when he came to go, Amy held him back to whisper, with trembling lips,\par
"Is there really any danger about Beth?"\par
\par
"I'm afraid there is; but we must hope for the best, so don't cry,\par
dear;" and Laurie put his arm about her with a brotherly gesture which\par
was very comforting.\par
\par
When he had gone, she went to her little chapel, and, sitting in the\par
twilight, prayed for Beth, with streaming tears and an aching heart,\par
feeling that a million turquoise rings would not console her for the\par
loss of her gentle little sister.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
         [Illustration: Mrs. March would not leave Beth's side]\par
\par
                                  XX.\par
\par
                             CONFIDENTIAL.\par
\par
\par
I don't think I have any words in which to tell the meeting of the\par
mother and daughters; such hours are beautiful to live, but very hard to\par
describe, so I will leave it to the imagination of my readers, merely\par
saying that the house was full of genuine happiness, and that Meg's\par
tender hope was realized; for when Beth woke from that long, healing\par
sleep, the first objects on which her eyes fell _were_ the little rose\par
and mother's face. Too weak to wonder at anything, she only smiled, and\par
nestled close into the loving arms about her, feeling that the hungry\par
longing was satisfied at last. Then she slept again, and the girls\par
waited upon their mother, for she would not unclasp the thin hand which\par
clung to hers even in sleep. Hannah had "dished up" an astonishing\par
breakfast for the traveller, finding it impossible to vent her\par
excitement in any other way; and Meg and Jo fed their mother like\par
dutiful young storks, while they listened to her whispered account of\par
father's state, Mr. Brooke's promise to stay and nurse him, the delays\par
which the storm occasioned on the homeward journey, and the unspeakable\par
comfort Laurie's hopeful face had given her when she arrived, worn out\par
with fatigue, anxiety, and cold.\par
\par
What a strange, yet pleasant day that was! so brilliant and gay without,\par
for all the world seemed abroad to welcome the first snow; so quiet and\par
reposeful within, for every one slept, spent with watching, and a\par
Sabbath stillness reigned through the house, while nodding Hannah\par
mounted guard at the door. With a blissful sense of burdens lifted off,\par
Meg and Jo closed their weary eyes, and lay at rest, like storm-beaten\par
boats, safe at anchor in a quiet harbor. Mrs. March would not leave\par
Beth's side, but rested in the big chair, waking often to look at,\par
touch, and brood over her child, like a miser over some recovered\par
treasure.\par
\par
Laurie, meanwhile, posted off to comfort Amy, and told his story so well\par
that Aunt March actually "sniffed" herself, and never once said, "I told\par
you so." Amy came out so strong on this occasion that I think the good\par
thoughts in the little chapel really began to bear fruit. She dried her\par
tears quickly, restrained her impatience to see her mother, and never\par
even thought of the turquoise ring, when the old lady heartily agreed in\par
Laurie's opinion, that she behaved "like a capital little woman." Even\par
Polly seemed impressed, for he called her "good girl," blessed her\par
buttons, and begged her to "come and take a walk, dear," in his most\par
affable tone. She would very gladly have gone out to enjoy the bright\par
wintry weather; but, discovering that Laurie was dropping with sleep in\par
spite of manful efforts to conceal the fact, she persuaded him to rest\par
on the sofa, while she wrote a note to her mother. She was a long time\par
about it; and, when she returned, he was stretched out, with both arms\par
under his head, sound asleep, while Aunt March had pulled down the\par
curtains, and sat doing nothing in an unusual fit of benignity.\par
\par
After a while, they began to think he was not going to wake till night,\par
and I'm not sure that he would, had he not been effectually roused by\par
Amy's cry of joy at sight of her mother. There probably were a good\par
many happy little girls in and about the city that day, but it is my\par
private opinion that Amy was the happiest of all, when she sat in her\par
mother's lap and told her trials, receiving consolation and compensation\par
in the shape of approving smiles and fond caresses. They were alone\par
together in the chapel, to which her mother did not object when its\par
purpose was explained to her.\par
\par
"On the contrary, I like it very much, dear," looking from the dusty\par
rosary to the well-worn little book, and the lovely picture with its\par
garland of evergreen. "It is an excellent plan to have some place where\par
we can go to be quiet, when things vex or grieve us. There are a good\par
many hard times in this life of ours, but we can always bear them if we\par
ask help in the right way. I think my little girl is learning this?"\par
\par
"Yes, mother; and when I go home I mean to have a corner in the big\par
closet to put my books, and the copy of that picture which I've tried to\par
make. The woman's face is not good,--it's too beautiful for me to\par
draw,--but the baby is done better, and I love it very much. I like to\par
think He was a little child once, for then I don't seem so far away, and\par
that helps me."\par
\par
As Amy pointed to the smiling Christ-child on his mother's knee, Mrs.\par
March saw something on the lifted hand that made her smile. She said\par
nothing, but Amy understood the look, and, after a minute's pause, she\par
added gravely,--\par
\par
"I wanted to speak to you about this, but I forgot it. Aunt gave me the\par
ring to-day; she called me to her and kissed me, and put it on my\par
finger, and said I was a credit to her, and she'd like to keep me\par
always. She gave that funny guard to keep the turquoise on, as it's too\par
big. I'd like to wear them, mother; can I?"\par
\par
"They are very pretty, but I think you're rather too young for such\par
ornaments, Amy," said Mrs. March, looking at the plump little hand, with\par
the band of sky-blue stones on the forefinger, and the quaint guard,\par
formed of two tiny, golden hands clasped together.\par
\par
"I'll try not to be vain," said Amy. "I don't think I like it only\par
because it's so pretty; but I want to wear it as the girl in the story\par
wore her bracelet, to remind me of something."\par
\par
"Do you mean Aunt March?" asked her mother, laughing.\par
\par
"No, to remind me not to be selfish." Amy looked so earnest and sincere\par
about it, that her mother stopped laughing, and listened respectfully to\par
the little plan.\par
\par
"I've thought a great deal lately about my 'bundle of naughties,' and\par
being selfish is the largest one in it; so I'm going to try hard to cure\par
it, if I can. Beth isn't selfish, and that's the reason every one loves\par
her and feels so bad at the thoughts of losing her. People wouldn't feel\par
half so bad about me if I was sick, and I don't deserve to have them;\par
but I'd like to be loved and missed by a great many friends, so I'm\par
going to try and be like Beth all I can. I'm apt to forget my\par
resolutions; but if I had something always about me to remind me, I\par
guess I should do better. May I try this way?"\par
\par
"Yes; but I have more faith in the corner of the big closet. Wear your\par
ring, dear, and do your best; I think you will prosper, for the sincere\par
wish to be good is half the battle. Now I must go back to Beth. Keep up\par
your heart, little daughter, and we will soon have you home again."\par
\par
That evening, while Meg was writing to her father, to report the\par
traveller's safe arrival, Jo slipped up stairs into Beth's room, and,\par
finding her mother in her usual place, stood a minute twisting her\par
fingers in her hair, with a worried gesture and an undecided look.\par
\par
"What is it, deary?" asked Mrs. March, holding out her hand, with a face\par
which invited confidence.\par
\par
"I want to tell you something, mother."\par
\par
"About Meg?"\par
\par
"How quickly you guessed! Yes, it's about her, and though it's a little\par
thing, it fidgets me."\par
\par
"Beth is asleep; speak low, and tell me all about it. That Moffat hasn't\par
been here, I hope?" asked Mrs. March rather sharply.\par
\par
"No, I should have shut the door in his face if he had," said Jo,\par
settling herself on the floor at her mother's feet. "Last summer Meg\par
left a pair of gloves over at the Laurences', and only one was returned.\par
We forgot all about it, till Teddy told me that Mr. Brooke had it. He\par
kept it in his waistcoat pocket, and once it fell out, and Teddy joked\par
him about it, and Mr. Brooke owned that he liked Meg, but didn't dare\par
say so, she was so young and he so poor. Now, isn't it a _dread_ful\par
state of things?"\par
\par
"Do you think Meg cares for him?" asked Mrs. March, with an anxious\par
look.\par
\par
"Mercy me! I don't know anything about love and such nonsense!" cried\par
Jo, with a funny mixture of interest and contempt. "In novels, the girls\par
show it by starting and blushing, fainting away, growing thin, and\par
acting like fools. Now Meg does not do anything of the sort: she eats\par
and drinks and sleeps, like a sensible creature: she looks straight in\par
my face when I talk about that man, and only blushes a little bit when\par
Teddy jokes about lovers. I forbid him to do it, but he doesn't mind me\par
as he ought."\par
\par
"Then you fancy that Meg is _not_ interested in John?"\par
\par
"Who?" cried Jo, staring.\par
\par
"Mr. Brooke. I call him 'John' now; we fell into the way of doing so at\par
the hospital, and he likes it."\par
\par
"Oh, dear! I know you'll take his part: he's been good to father, and\par
you won't send him away, but let Meg marry him, if she wants to. Mean\par
thing! to go petting papa and helping you, just to wheedle you into\par
liking him;" and Jo pulled her hair again with a wrathful tweak.\par
\par
"My dear, don't get angry about it, and I will tell you how it happened.\par
John went with me at Mr. Laurence's request, and was so devoted to poor\par
father that we couldn't help getting fond of him. He was perfectly open\par
and honorable about Meg, for he told us he loved her, but would earn a\par
comfortable home before he asked her to marry him. He only wanted our\par
leave to love her and work for her, and the right to make her love him\par
if he could. He is a truly excellent young man, and we could not refuse\par
to listen to him; but I will not consent to Meg's engaging herself so\par
young."\par
\par
"Of course not; it would be idiotic! I knew there was mischief brewing;\par
I felt it; and now it's worse than I imagined. I just wish I could marry\par
Meg myself, and keep her safe in the family."\par
\par
This odd arrangement made Mrs. March smile; but she said gravely, "Jo, I\par
confide in you, and don't wish you to say anything to Meg yet. When John\par
comes back, and I see them together, I can judge better of her feelings\par
toward him."\par
\par
"She'll see his in those handsome eyes that she talks about, and then\par
it will be all up with her. She's got such a soft heart, it will melt\par
like butter in the sun if any one looks sentimentally at her. She read\par
the short reports he sent more than she did your letters, and pinched me\par
when I spoke of it, and likes brown eyes, and doesn't think John an ugly\par
name, and she'll go and fall in love, and there's an end of peace and\par
fun, and cosy times together. I see it all! they'll go lovering around\par
the house, and we shall have to dodge; Meg will be absorbed, and no good\par
to me any more; Brooke will scratch up a fortune somehow, carry her off,\par
and make a hole in the family; and I shall break my heart, and\par
everything will be abominably uncomfortable. Oh, dear me! why weren't we\par
all boys, then there wouldn't be any bother."\par
\par
Jo leaned her chin on her knees, in a disconsolate attitude, and shook\par
her fist at the reprehensible John. Mrs. March sighed, and Jo looked up\par
with an air of relief.\par
\par
"You don't like it, mother? I'm glad of it. Let's send him about his\par
business, and not tell Meg a word of it, but all be happy together as we\par
always have been."\par
\par
"I did wrong to sigh, Jo. It is natural and right you should all go to\par
homes of your own, in time; but I do want to keep my girls as long as I\par
can; and I am sorry that this happened so soon, for Meg is only\par
seventeen, and it will be some years before John can make a home for\par
her. Your father and I have agreed that she shall not bind herself in\par
any way, nor be married, before twenty. If she and John love one\par
another, they can wait, and test the love by doing so. She is\par
conscientious, and I have no fear of her treating him unkindly. My\par
pretty, tender-hearted girl! I hope things will go happily with her."\par
\par
"Hadn't you rather have her marry a rich man?" asked Jo, as her mother's\par
voice faltered a little over the last words.\par
\par
"Money is a good and useful thing, Jo; and I hope my girls will never\par
feel the need of it too bitterly, nor be tempted by too much. I should\par
like to know that John was firmly established in some good business,\par
which gave him an income large enough to keep free from debt and make\par
Meg comfortable. I'm not ambitious for a splendid fortune, a fashionable\par
position, or a great name for my girls. If rank and money come with\par
love and virtue, also, I should accept them gratefully, and enjoy your\par
good fortune; but I know, by experience, how much genuine happiness can\par
be had in a plain little house, where the daily bread is earned, and\par
some privations give sweetness to the few pleasures. I am content to see\par
Meg begin humbly, for, if I am not mistaken, she will be rich in the\par
possession of a good man's heart, and that is better than a fortune."\par
\par
"I understand, mother, and quite agree; but I'm disappointed about Meg,\par
for I'd planned to have her marry Teddy by and by, and sit in the lap of\par
luxury all her days. Wouldn't it be nice?" asked Jo, looking up, with a\par
brighter face.\par
\par
"He is younger than she, you know," began Mrs. March; but Jo broke in,--\par
\par
"Only a little; he's old for his age, and tall; and can be quite\par
grown-up in his manners if he likes. Then he's rich and generous and\par
good, and loves us all; and _I_ say it's a pity my plan is spoilt."\par
\par
"I'm afraid Laurie is hardly grown up enough for Meg, and altogether too\par
much of a weathercock, just now, for any one to depend on. Don't make\par
plans, Jo; but let time and their own hearts mate your friends. We can't\par
meddle safely in such matters, and had better not get 'romantic\par
rubbish,' as you call it, into our heads, lest it spoil our friendship."\par
\par
"Well, I won't; but I hate to see things going all criss-cross and\par
getting snarled up, when a pull here and a snip there would straighten\par
it out. I wish wearing flat-irons on our heads would keep us from\par
growing up. But buds will be roses, and kittens, cats,--more's the\par
pity!"\par
\par
"What's that about flat-irons and cats?" asked Meg, as she crept into\par
the room, with the finished letter in her hand.\par
\par
"Only one of my stupid speeches. I'm going to bed; come, Peggy," said\par
Jo, unfolding herself, like an animated puzzle.\par
\par
"Quite right, and beautifully written. Please add that I send my love to\par
John," said Mrs. March, as she glanced over the letter, and gave it\par
back.\par
\par
"Do you call him 'John'?" asked Meg, smiling, with her innocent eyes\par
looking down into her mother's.\par
\par
"Yes; he has been like a son to us, and we are very fond of him,"\par
replied Mrs. March, returning the look with a keen one.\par
\par
"I'm glad of that, he is so lonely. Good-night, mother, dear. It is so\par
inexpressibly comfortable to have you here," was Meg's quiet answer.\par
\par
The kiss her mother gave her was a very tender one; and, as she went\par
away, Mrs. March said, with a mixture of satisfaction and regret, "She\par
does not love John yet, but will soon learn to."\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                        [Illustration: Letters]\par
\par
                                  XXI.\par
\par
               LAURIE MAKES MISCHIEF, AND JO MAKES PEACE.\par
\par
\par
Jo's face was a study next day, for the secret rather weighed upon her,\par
and she found it hard not to look mysterious and important. Meg observed\par
it, but did not trouble herself to make inquiries, for she had learned\par
that the best way to manage Jo was by the law of contraries, so she felt\par
sure of being told everything if she did not ask. She was rather\par
surprised, therefore, when the silence remained unbroken, and Jo assumed\par
a patronizing air, which decidedly aggravated Meg, who in her turn\par
assumed an air of dignified reserve, and devoted herself to her mother.\par
This left Jo to her own devices; for Mrs. March had taken her place as\par
nurse, and bade her rest, exercise, and amuse herself after her long\par
confinement. Amy being gone, Laurie was her only refuge; and, much as\par
she enjoyed his society, she rather dreaded him just then, for he was an\par
incorrigible tease, and she feared he would coax her secret from her.\par
\par
She was quite right, for the mischief-loving lad no sooner suspected a\par
mystery than he set himself to find it out, and led Jo a trying life of\par
it. He wheedled, bribed, ridiculed, threatened, and scolded; affected\par
indifference, that he might surprise the truth from her; declared he\par
knew, then that he didn't care; and, at last, by dint of perseverance,\par
he satisfied himself that it concerned Meg and Mr. Brooke. Feeling\par
indignant that he was not taken into his tutor's confidence, he set his\par
wits to work to devise some proper retaliation for the slight.\par
\par
Meg meanwhile had apparently forgotten the matter, and was absorbed in\par
preparations for her father's return; but all of a sudden a change\par
seemed to come over her, and, for a day or two, she was quite unlike\par
herself. She started when spoken to, blushed when looked at, was very\par
quiet, and sat over her sewing, with a timid, troubled look on her face.\par
To her mother's inquiries she answered that she was quite well, and Jo's\par
she silenced by begging to be let alone.\par
\par
"She feels it in the air--love, I mean--and she's going very fast. She's\par
got most of the symptoms,--is twittery and cross, doesn't eat, lies\par
awake, and mopes in corners. I caught her singing that song he gave her,\par
and once she said 'John,' as you do, and then turned as red as a poppy.\par
Whatever shall we do?" said Jo, looking ready for any measures, however\par
violent.\par
\par
"Nothing but wait. Let her alone, be kind and patient, and father's\par
coming will settle everything," replied her mother.\par
\par
"Here's a note to you, Meg, all sealed up. How odd! Teddy never seals\par
mine," said Jo, next day, as she distributed the contents of the little\par
post-office.\par
\par
Mrs. March and Jo were deep in their own affairs, when a sound from Meg\par
made them look up to see her staring at her note, with a frightened\par
face.\par
\par
"My child, what is it?" cried her mother, running to her, while Jo tried\par
to take the paper which had done the mischief.\par
\par
"It's all a mistake--he didn't send it. O Jo, how could you do it?" and\par
Meg hid her face in her hands, crying as if her heart was quite broken.\par
\par
"Me! I've done nothing! What's she talking about?" cried Jo, bewildered.\par
\par
Meg's mild eyes kindled with anger as she pulled a crumpled note from\par
her pocket, and threw it at Jo, saying reproachfully,--\par
\par
"You wrote it, and that bad boy helped you. How could you be so rude, so\par
mean, and cruel to us both?"\par
\par
Jo hardly heard her, for she and her mother were reading the note, which\par
was written in a peculiar hand.\par
\par
        [Illustration: Jo and her mother were reading the note]\par
\par
    "MY DEAREST MARGARET,--\par
\par
    "I can no longer restrain my passion, and must know my fate\par
    before I return. I dare not tell your parents yet, but I think\par
    they would consent if they knew that we adored one another. Mr.\par
    Laurence will help me to some good place, and then, my sweet\par
    girl, you will make me happy. I implore you to say nothing to\par
    your family yet, but to send one word of hope through Laurie to\par
                                            "Your devoted JOHN."\par
\par
"Oh, the little villain! that's the way he meant to pay me for keeping\par
my word to mother. I'll give him a hearty scolding, and bring him over\par
to beg pardon," cried Jo, burning to execute immediate justice. But her\par
mother held her back, saying, with a look she seldom wore,--\par
\par
"Stop, Jo, you must clear yourself first. You have played so many\par
pranks, that I am afraid you have had a hand in this."\par
\par
"On my word, mother, I haven't! I never saw that note before, and don't\par
know anything about it, as true as I live!" said Jo, so earnestly that\par
they believed her. "If I _had_ taken a part in it I'd have done it\par
better than this, and have written a sensible note. I should think you'd\par
have known Mr. Brooke wouldn't write such stuff as that," she added,\par
scornfully tossing down the paper.\par
\par
"It's like his writing," faltered Meg, comparing it with the note in her\par
hand.\par
\par
"O Meg, you didn't answer it?" cried Mrs. March quickly.\par
\par
"Yes, I did!" and Meg hid her face again, overcome with shame.\par
\par
"Here's a scrape! _Do_ let me bring that wicked boy over to explain, and\par
be lectured. I can't rest till I get hold of him;" and Jo made for the\par
door again.\par
\par
"Hush! let me manage this, for it is worse than I thought. Margaret,\par
tell me the whole story," commanded Mrs. March, sitting down by Meg, yet\par
keeping hold of Jo, lest she should fly off.\par
\par
"I received the first letter from Laurie, who didn't look as if he knew\par
anything about it," began Meg, without looking up. "I was worried at\par
first, and meant to tell you; then I remembered how you liked Mr.\par
Brooke, so I thought you wouldn't mind if I kept my little secret for a\par
few days. I'm so silly that I liked to think no one knew; and, while I\par
was deciding what to say, I felt like the girls in books, who have such\par
things to do. Forgive me, mother, I'm paid for my silliness now; I never\par
can look him in the face again."\par
\par
"What did you say to him?" asked Mrs. March.\par
\par
"I only said I was too young to do anything about it yet; that I didn't\par
wish to have secrets from you, and he must speak to father. I was very\par
grateful for his kindness, and would be his friend, but nothing more,\par
for a long while."\par
\par
Mrs. March smiled, as if well pleased, and Jo clapped her hands,\par
exclaiming, with a laugh,--\par
\par
"You are almost equal to Caroline Percy, who was a pattern of prudence!\par
Tell on, Meg. What did he say to that?"\par
\par
"He writes in a different way entirely, telling me that he never sent\par
any love-letter at all, and is very sorry that my roguish sister, Jo,\par
should take such liberties with our names. It's very kind and\par
respectful, but think how dreadful for me!"\par
\par
Meg leaned against her mother, looking the image of despair, and Jo\par
tramped about the room, calling Laurie names. All of a sudden she\par
stopped, caught up the two notes, and, after looking at them closely,\par
said decidedly, "I don't believe Brooke ever saw either of these\par
letters. Teddy wrote both, and keeps yours to crow over me with, because\par
I wouldn't tell him my secret."\par
\par
"Don't have any secrets, Jo; tell it to mother, and keep out of trouble,\par
as I should have done," said Meg warningly.\par
\par
"Bless you, child! Mother told me."\par
\par
"That will do, Jo. I'll comfort Meg while you go and get Laurie. I shall\par
sift the matter to the bottom, and put a stop to such pranks at once."\par
\par
Away ran Jo, and Mrs. March gently told Meg Mr. Brooke's real feelings.\par
"Now, dear, what are your own? Do you love him enough to wait till he\par
can make a home for you, or will you keep yourself quite free for the\par
present?"\par
\par
"I've been so scared and worried, I don't want to have anything to do\par
with lovers for a long while,--perhaps never," answered Meg petulantly.\par
"If John _doesn't_ know anything about this nonsense, don't tell him,\par
and make Jo and Laurie hold their tongues. I won't be deceived and\par
plagued and made a fool of,--it's a shame!"\par
\par
Seeing that Meg's usually gentle temper was roused and her pride hurt by\par
this mischievous joke, Mrs. March soothed her by promises of entire\par
silence, and great discretion for the future. The instant Laurie's step\par
was heard in the hall, Meg fled into the study, and Mrs. March received\par
the culprit alone. Jo had not told him why he was wanted, fearing he\par
wouldn't come; but he knew the minute he saw Mrs. March's face, and\par
stood twirling his hat, with a guilty air which convicted him at once.\par
Jo was dismissed, but chose to march up and down the hall like a\par
sentinel, having some fear that the prisoner might bolt. The sound of\par
voices in the parlor rose and fell for half an hour; but what happened\par
during that interview the girls never knew.\par
\par
When they were called in, Laurie was standing by their mother, with such\par
a penitent face that Jo forgave him on the spot, but did not think it\par
wise to betray the fact. Meg received his humble apology, and was much\par
comforted by the assurance that Brooke knew nothing of the joke.\par
\par
"I'll never tell him to my dying day,--wild horses sha'n't drag it out\par
of me; so you'll forgive me, Meg, and I'll do anything to show how\par
out-and-out sorry I am," he added, looking very much ashamed of himself.\par
\par
"I'll try; but it was a very ungentlemanly thing to do. I didn't think\par
you could be so sly and malicious, Laurie," replied Meg, trying to hide\par
her maidenly confusion under a gravely reproachful air.\par
\par
"It was altogether abominable, and I don't deserve to be spoken to for a\par
month; but you will, though, won't you?" and Laurie folded his hands\par
together with such an imploring gesture, as he spoke in his irresistibly\par
persuasive tone, that it was impossible to frown upon him, in spite of\par
his scandalous behavior. Meg pardoned him, and Mrs. March's grave face\par
relaxed, in spite of her efforts to keep sober, when she heard him\par
declare that he would atone for his sins by all sorts of penances, and\par
abase himself like a worm before the injured damsel.\par
\par
Jo stood aloof, meanwhile, trying to harden her heart against him, and\par
succeeding only in primming up her face into an expression of entire\par
disapprobation. Laurie looked at her once or twice, but, as she showed\par
no sign of relenting, he felt injured, and turned his back on her till\par
the others were done with him, when he made her a low bow, and walked\par
off without a word.\par
\par
As soon as he had gone, she wished she had been more forgiving; and when\par
Meg and her mother went upstairs, she felt lonely, and longed for Teddy.\par
After resisting for some time, she yielded to the impulse, and, armed\par
with a book to return, went over to the big house.\par
\par
"Is Mr. Laurence in?" asked Jo, of a housemaid, who was coming down\par
stairs.\par
\par
"Yes, miss; but I don't believe he's seeable just yet."\par
\par
"Why not? is he ill?"\par
\par
"La, no, miss, but he's had a scene with Mr. Laurie, who is in one of\par
his tantrums about something, which vexes the old gentleman, so I\par
dursn't go nigh him."\par
\par
"Where is Laurie?"\par
\par
"Shut up in his room, and he won't answer, though I've been a-tapping. I\par
don't know what's to become of the dinner, for it's ready, and there's\par
no one to eat it."\par
\par
"I'll go and see what the matter is. I'm not afraid of either of them."\par
\par
Up went Jo, and knocked smartly on the door of Laurie's little study.\par
\par
"Stop that, or I'll open the door and make you!" called out the young\par
gentleman, in a threatening tone.\par
\par
Jo immediately knocked again; the door flew open, and in she bounced,\par
before Laurie could recover from his surprise. Seeing that he really\par
_was_ out of temper, Jo, who knew how to manage him, assumed a contrite\par
expression, and going artistically down upon her knees, said meekly,\par
"Please forgive me for being so cross. I came to make it up, and can't\par
go away till I have."\par
\par
"It's all right. Get up, and don't be a goose, Jo," was the cavalier\par
reply to her petition.\par
\par
              [Illustration: Get up and don't be a goose]\par
\par
"Thank you; I will. Could I ask what's the matter? You don't look\par
exactly easy in your mind."\par
\par
"I've been shaken, and I won't bear it!" growled Laurie indignantly.\par
\par
"Who did it?" demanded Jo.\par
\par
"Grandfather; if it had been any one else I'd have--" and the injured\par
youth finished his sentence by an energetic gesture of the right arm.\par
\par
"That's nothing; I often shake you, and you don't mind," said Jo\par
soothingly.\par
\par
"Pooh! you're a girl, and it's fun; but I'll allow no man to shake\par
_me_."\par
\par
"I don't think any one would care to try it, if you looked as much like\par
a thunder-cloud as you do now. Why were you treated so?"\par
\par
"Just because I wouldn't say what your mother wanted me for. I'd\par
promised not to tell, and of course I wasn't going to break my word."\par
\par
"Couldn't you satisfy your grandpa in any other way?"\par
\par
"No; he _would_ have the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the\par
truth. I'd have told my part of the scrape, if I could without bringing\par
Meg in. As I couldn't, I held my tongue, and bore the scolding till the\par
old gentleman collared me. Then I got angry, and bolted, for fear I\par
should forget myself."\par
\par
"It wasn't nice, but he's sorry, I know; so go down and make up. I'll\par
help you."\par
\par
"Hanged if I do! I'm not going to be lectured and pummelled by every\par
one, just for a bit of a frolic. I _was_ sorry about Meg, and begged\par
pardon like a man; but I won't do it again, when I wasn't in the wrong."\par
\par
"He didn't know that."\par
\par
"He ought to trust me, and not act as if I was a baby. It's no use, Jo;\par
he's got to learn that I'm able to take care of myself, and don't need\par
any one's apron-string to hold on by."\par
\par
"What pepper-pots you are!" sighed Jo. "How do you mean to settle this\par
affair?"\par
\par
"Well, he ought to beg pardon, and believe me when I say I can't tell\par
him what the fuss's about."\par
\par
"Bless you! he won't do that."\par
\par
"I won't go down till he does."\par
\par
"Now, Teddy, be sensible; let it pass, and I'll explain what I can. You\par
can't stay here, so what's the use of being melodramatic?"\par
\par
"I don't intend to stay here long, any way. I'll slip off and take a\par
journey somewhere, and when grandpa misses me he'll come round fast\par
enough."\par
\par
"I dare say; but you ought not to go and worry him."\par
\par
"Don't preach. I'll go to Washington and see Brooke; it's gay there, and\par
I'll enjoy myself after the troubles."\par
\par
"What fun you'd have! I wish I could run off too," said Jo, forgetting\par
her part of Mentor in lively visions of martial life at the capital.\par
\par
"Come on, then! Why not? You go and surprise your father, and I'll stir\par
up old Brooke. It would be a glorious joke; let's do it, Jo. We'll leave\par
a letter saying we are all right, and trot off at once. I've got money\par
enough; it will do you good, and be no harm, as you go to your father."\par
\par
For a moment Jo looked as if she would agree; for, wild as the plan was,\par
it just suited her. She was tired of care and confinement, longed for\par
change, and thoughts of her father blended temptingly with the novel\par
charms of camps and hospitals, liberty and fun. Her eyes kindled as they\par
turned wistfully toward the window, but they fell on the old house\par
opposite, and she shook her head with sorrowful decision.\par
\par
"If I was a boy, we'd run away together, and have a capital time; but as\par
I'm a miserable girl, I must be proper, and stop at home. Don't tempt\par
me, Teddy, it's a crazy plan."\par
\par
"That's the fun of it," began Laurie, who had got a wilful fit on him,\par
and was possessed to break out of bounds in some way.\par
\par
"Hold your tongue!" cried Jo, covering her ears. "'Prunes and prisms'\par
are my doom, and I may as well make up my mind to it. I came here to\par
moralize, not to hear about things that make me skip to think of."\par
\par
    [Illustration: "Hold your tongue!" cried Jo, covering her ears]\par
\par
"I know Meg would wet-blanket such a proposal, but I thought you had\par
more spirit," began Laurie insinuatingly.\par
\par
"Bad boy, be quiet! Sit down and think of your own sins, don't go making\par
me add to mine. If I get your grandpa to apologize for the shaking, will\par
you give up running away?" asked Jo seriously.\par
\par
"Yes, but you won't do it," answered Laurie, who wished "to make up,"\par
but felt that his outraged dignity must be appeased first.\par
\par
"If I can manage the young one I can the old one," muttered Jo, as she\par
walked away, leaving Laurie bent over a railroad map, with his head\par
propped up on both hands.\par
\par
"Come in!" and Mr. Laurence's gruff voice sounded gruffer than ever, as\par
Jo tapped at his door.\par
\par
"It's only me, sir, come to return a book," she said blandly, as she\par
entered.\par
\par
"Want any more?" asked the old gentleman, looking grim and vexed, but\par
trying not to show it.\par
\par
"Yes, please. I like old Sam so well, I think I'll try the second\par
volume," returned Jo, hoping to propitiate him by accepting a second\par
dose of Boswell's "Johnson," as he had recommended that lively work.\par
\par
The shaggy eyebrows unbent a little, as he rolled the steps toward the\par
shelf where the Johnsonian literature was placed. Jo skipped up, and,\par
sitting on the top step, affected to be searching for her book, but was\par
really wondering how best to introduce the dangerous object of her\par
visit. Mr. Laurence seemed to suspect that something was brewing in her\par
mind; for, after taking several brisk turns about the room, he faced\par
round on her, speaking so abruptly that "Rasselas" tumbled face downward\par
on the floor.\par
\par
"What has that boy been about? Don't try to shield him. I know he has\par
been in mischief by the way he acted when he came home. I can't get a\par
word from him; and when I threatened to shake the truth out of him he\par
bolted upstairs, and locked himself into his room."\par
\par
"He did do wrong, but we forgave him, and all promised not to say a word\par
to any one," began Jo reluctantly.\par
\par
"That won't do; he shall not shelter himself behind a promise from you\par
soft-hearted girls. If he's done anything amiss, he shall confess, beg\par
pardon, and be punished. Out with it, Jo, I won't be kept in the dark."\par
\par
Mr. Laurence looked so alarming and spoke so sharply that Jo would have\par
gladly run away, if she could, but she was perched aloft on the steps,\par
and he stood at the foot, a lion in the path, so she had to stay and\par
brave it out.\par
\par
     [Illustration: He stood at the foot, like a lion in the path]\par
\par
"Indeed, sir, I cannot tell; mother forbade it. Laurie has confessed,\par
asked pardon, and been punished quite enough. We don't keep silence to\par
shield him, but some one else, and it will make more trouble if you\par
interfere. Please don't; it was partly my fault, but it's all right now;\par
so let's forget it, and talk about the 'Rambler,' or something\par
pleasant."\par
\par
"Hang the 'Rambler!' come down and give me your word that this\par
harum-scarum boy of mine hasn't done anything ungrateful or impertinent.\par
If he has, after all your kindness to him, I'll thrash him with my own\par
hands."\par
\par
The threat sounded awful, but did not alarm Jo, for she knew the\par
irascible old gentleman would never lift a finger against his grandson,\par
whatever he might say to the contrary. She obediently descended, and\par
made as light of the prank as she could without betraying Meg or\par
forgetting the truth.\par
\par
"Hum--ha--well, if the boy held his tongue because he promised, and not\par
from obstinacy, I'll forgive him. He's a stubborn fellow, and hard to\par
manage," said Mr. Laurence, rubbing up his hair till it looked as if he\par
had been out in a gale, and smoothing the frown from his brow with an\par
air of relief.\par
\par
"So am I; but a kind word will govern me when all the king's horses and\par
all the king's men couldn't," said Jo, trying to say a kind word for her\par
friend, who seemed to get out of one scrape only to fall into another.\par
\par
"You think I'm not kind to him, hey?" was the sharp answer.\par
\par
"Oh, dear, no, sir; you are rather too kind sometimes, and then just a\par
trifle hasty when he tries your patience. Don't you think you are?"\par
\par
Jo was determined to have it out now, and tried to look quite placid,\par
though she quaked a little after her bold speech. To her great relief\par
and surprise, the old gentleman only threw his spectacles on to the\par
table with a rattle, and exclaimed frankly,--\par
\par
"You're right, girl, I am! I love the boy, but he tries my patience past\par
bearing, and I don't know how it will end, if we go on so."\par
\par
"I'll tell you, he'll run away." Jo was sorry for that speech the\par
minute it was made; she meant to warn him that Laurie would not bear\par
much restraint, and hoped he would be more forbearing with the lad.\par
\par
Mr. Laurence's ruddy face changed suddenly, and he sat down, with a\par
troubled glance at the picture of a handsome man, which hung over his\par
table. It was Laurie's father, who _had_ run away in his youth, and\par
married against the imperious old man's will. Jo fancied he remembered\par
and regretted the past, and she wished she had held her tongue.\par
\par
"He won't do it unless he is very much worried, and only threatens it\par
sometimes, when he gets tired of studying. I often think I should like\par
to, especially since my hair was cut; so, if you ever miss us, you may\par
advertise for two boys, and look among the ships bound for India."\par
\par
She laughed as she spoke, and Mr. Laurence looked relieved, evidently\par
taking the whole as a joke.\par
\par
"You hussy, how dare you talk in that way? Where's your respect for me,\par
and your proper bringing up? Bless the boys and girls! What torments\par
they are; yet we can't do without them," he said, pinching her cheeks\par
good-humoredly. "Go and bring that boy down to his dinner, tell him it's\par
all right, and advise him not to put on tragedy airs with his\par
grandfather. I won't bear it."\par
\par
"He won't come, sir; he feels badly because you didn't believe him when\par
he said he couldn't tell. I think the shaking hurt his feelings very\par
much."\par
\par
Jo tried to look pathetic, but must have failed, for Mr. Laurence began\par
to laugh, and she knew the day was won.\par
\par
"I'm sorry for that, and ought to thank him for not shaking _me_, I\par
suppose. What the dickens does the fellow expect?" and the old gentleman\par
looked a trifle ashamed of his own testiness.\par
\par
"If I were you, I'd write him an apology, sir. He says he won't come\par
down till he has one, and talks about Washington, and goes on in an\par
absurd way. A formal apology will make him see how foolish he is, and\par
bring him down quite amiable. Try it; he likes fun, and this way is\par
better than talking. I'll carry it up, and teach him his duty."\par
\par
Mr. Laurence gave her a sharp look, and put on his spectacles, saying\par
slowly, "You're a sly puss, but I don't mind being managed by you and\par
Beth. Here, give me a bit of paper, and let us have done with this\par
nonsense."\par
\par
The note was written in the terms which one gentleman would use to\par
another after offering some deep insult. Jo dropped a kiss on the top of\par
Mr. Laurence's bald head, and ran up to slip the apology under Laurie's\par
door, advising him, through the key-hole, to be submissive, decorous,\par
and a few other agreeable impossibilities. Finding the door locked\par
again, she left the note to do its work, and was going quietly away,\par
when the young gentleman slid down the banisters, and waited for her at\par
the bottom, saying, with his most virtuous expression of countenance,\par
"What a good fellow you are, Jo! Did you get blown up?" he added,\par
laughing.\par
\par
"No; he was pretty mild, on the whole."\par
\par
"Ah! I got it all round; even you cast me off over there, and I felt\par
just ready to go to the deuce," he began apologetically.\par
\par
"Don't talk in that way; turn over a new leaf and begin again, Teddy, my\par
son."\par
\par
"I keep turning over new leaves, and spoiling them, as I used to spoil\par
my copy-books; and I make so many beginnings there never will be an\par
end," he said dolefully.\par
\par
"Go and eat your dinner; you'll feel better after it. Men always croak\par
when they are hungry," and Jo whisked out at the front door after that.\par
\par
"That's a 'label' on my 'sect,'" answered Laurie, quoting Amy, as he\par
went to partake of humble-pie dutifully with his grandfather, who was\par
quite saintly in temper and overwhelmingly respectful in manner all the\par
rest of the day.\par
\par
Every one thought the matter ended and the little cloud blown over; but\par
the mischief was done, for, though others forgot it, Meg remembered. She\par
never alluded to a certain person, but she thought of him a good deal,\par
dreamed dreams more than ever; and once Jo, rummaging her sister's desk\par
for stamps, found a bit of paper scribbled over with the words, "Mrs.\par
John Brooke;" whereat she groaned tragically, and cast it into the fire,\par
feeling that Laurie's prank had hastened the evil day for her.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
  [Illustration: Beth was soon able to lie on the study sofa all day]\par
\par
                                 XXII.\par
\par
                           PLEASANT MEADOWS.\par
\par
\par
Like sunshine after storm were the peaceful weeks which followed. The\par
invalids improved rapidly, and Mr. March began to talk of returning\par
early in the new year. Beth was soon able to lie on the study sofa all\par
day, amusing herself with the well-beloved cats, at first, and, in time,\par
with doll's sewing, which had fallen sadly behindhand. Her once active\par
limbs were so stiff and feeble that Jo took her a daily airing about the\par
house in her strong arms. Meg cheerfully blackened and burnt her white\par
hands cooking delicate messes for "the dear;" while Amy, a loyal slave\par
of the ring, celebrated her return by giving away as many of her\par
treasures as she could prevail on her sisters to accept.\par
\par
As Christmas approached, the usual mysteries began to haunt the house,\par
and Jo frequently convulsed the family by proposing utterly impossible\par
or magnificently absurd ceremonies, in honor of this unusually merry\par
Christmas. Laurie was equally impracticable, and would have had\par
bonfires, sky-rockets, and triumphal arches, if he had had his own way.\par
After many skirmishes and snubbings, the ambitious pair were considered\par
effectually quenched, and went about with forlorn faces, which were\par
rather belied by explosions of laughter when the two got together.\par
\par
Several days of unusually mild weather fitly ushered in a splendid\par
Christmas Day. Hannah "felt in her bones" that it was going to be an\par
unusually fine day, and she proved herself a true prophetess, for\par
everybody and everything seemed bound to produce a grand success. To\par
begin with, Mr. March wrote that he should soon be with them; then Beth\par
felt uncommonly well that morning, and, being dressed in her mother's\par
gift,--a soft crimson merino wrapper,--was borne in triumph to the\par
window to behold the offering of Jo and Laurie. The Unquenchables had\par
done their best to be worthy of the name, for, like elves, they had\par
worked by night, and conjured up a comical surprise. Out in the garden\par
stood a stately snow-maiden, crowned with holly, bearing a basket of\par
fruit and flowers in one hand, a great roll of new music in the other, a\par
perfect rainbow of an Afghan round her chilly shoulders, and a Christmas\par
carol issuing from her lips, on a pink paper streamer:--\par
\par
                         "THE JUNGFRAU TO BETH.\par
\par
        "God bless you, dear Queen Bess!\par
          May nothing you dismay,\par
         But health and peace and happiness\par
          Be yours, this Christmas Day.\par
\par
        "Here's fruit to feed our busy bee,\par
          And flowers for her nose;\par
         Here's music for her pianee,\par
          An Afghan for her toes.\par
\par
        "A portrait of Joanna, see,\par
          By Raphael No. 2,\par
         Who labored with great industry\par
          To make it fair and true.\par
\par
        "Accept a ribbon red, I beg,\par
          For Madam Purrer's tail;\par
         And ice-cream made by lovely Peg,--\par
          A Mont Blanc in a pail.\par
\par
        "Their dearest love my makers laid\par
          Within my breast of snow:\par
         Accept it, and the Alpine maid,\par
          From Laurie and from Jo."\par
\par
                      [Illustration: The Jungfrau]\par
\par
How Beth laughed when she saw it, how Laurie ran up and down to bring in\par
the gifts, and what ridiculous speeches Jo made as she presented them!\par
\par
"I'm so full of happiness, that, if father was only here, I couldn't\par
hold one drop more," said Beth, quite sighing with contentment as Jo\par
carried her off to the study to rest after the excitement, and to\par
refresh herself with some of the delicious grapes the "Jungfrau" had\par
sent her.\par
\par
"So am I," added Jo, slapping the pocket wherein reposed the\par
long-desired Undine and Sintram.\par
\par
"I'm sure I am," echoed Amy, poring over the engraved copy of the\par
Madonna and Child, which her mother had given her, in a pretty frame.\par
\par
"Of course I am!" cried Meg, smoothing the silvery folds of her first\par
silk dress; for Mr. Laurence had insisted on giving it.\par
\par
"How can _I_ be otherwise?" said Mrs. March gratefully, as her eyes went\par
from her husband's letter to Beth's smiling face, and her hand caressed\par
the brooch made of gray and golden, chestnut and dark brown hair, which\par
the girls had just fastened on her breast.\par
\par
Now and then, in this work-a-day world, things do happen in the\par
delightful story-book fashion, and what a comfort that is. Half an hour\par
after every one had said they were so happy they could only hold one\par
drop more, the drop came. Laurie opened the parlor door, and popped his\par
head in very quietly. He might just as well have turned a somersault and\par
uttered an Indian war-whoop; for his face was so full of suppressed\par
excitement and his voice so treacherously joyful, that every one jumped\par
up, though he only said, in a queer, breathless voice, "Here's another\par
Christmas present for the March family."\par
\par
Before the words were well out of his mouth, he was whisked away\par
somehow, and in his place appeared a tall man, muffled up to the eyes,\par
leaning on the arm of another tall man, who tried to say something and\par
couldn't. Of course there was a general stampede; and for several\par
minutes everybody seemed to lose their wits, for the strangest things\par
were done, and no one said a word. Mr. March became invisible in the\par
embrace of four pairs of loving arms; Jo disgraced herself by nearly\par
fainting away, and had to be doctored by Laurie in the china-closet; Mr.\par
Brooke kissed Meg entirely by mistake, as he somewhat incoherently\par
explained; and Amy, the dignified, tumbled over a stool, and, never\par
stopping to get up, hugged and cried over her father's boots in the most\par
touching manner. Mrs. March was the first to recover herself, and held\par
up her hand with a warning, "Hush! remember Beth!"\par
\par
But it was too late; the study door flew open, the little red wrapper\par
appeared on the threshold,--joy put strength into the feeble limbs,--and\par
Beth ran straight into her father's arms. Never mind what happened just\par
after that; for the full hearts overflowed, washing away the bitterness\par
of the past, and leaving only the sweetness of the present.\par
\par
It was not at all romantic, but a hearty laugh set everybody straight\par
again, for Hannah was discovered behind the door, sobbing over the fat\par
turkey, which she had forgotten to put down when she rushed up from the\par
kitchen. As the laugh subsided, Mrs. March began to thank Mr. Brooke for\par
his faithful care of her husband, at which Mr. Brooke suddenly\par
remembered that Mr. March needed rest, and, seizing Laurie, he\par
precipitately retired. Then the two invalids were ordered to repose,\par
which they did, by both sitting in one big chair, and talking hard.\par
\par
Mr. March told how he had longed to surprise them, and how, when the\par
fine weather came, he had been allowed by his doctor to take advantage\par
of it; how devoted Brooke had been, and how he was altogether a most\par
estimable and upright young man. Why Mr. March paused a minute just\par
there, and, after a glance at Meg, who was violently poking the fire,\par
looked at his wife with an inquiring lift of the eyebrows, I leave you\par
to imagine; also why Mrs. March gently nodded her head, and asked,\par
rather abruptly, if he wouldn't have something to eat. Jo saw and\par
understood the look; and she stalked grimly away to get wine and\par
beef-tea, muttering to herself, as she slammed the door, "I hate\par
estimable young men with brown eyes!"\par
\par
There never _was_ such a Christmas dinner as they had that day. The fat\par
turkey was a sight to behold, when Hannah sent him up, stuffed, browned,\par
and decorated; so was the plum-pudding, which quite melted in one's\par
mouth; likewise the jellies, in which Amy revelled like a fly in a\par
honey-pot. Everything turned out well, which was a mercy, Hannah said,\par
"For my mind was that flustered, mum, that it's a merrycle I didn't\par
roast the pudding, and stuff the turkey with raisins, let alone bilin'\par
of it in a cloth."\par
\par
Mr. Laurence and his grandson dined with them, also Mr. Brooke,--at whom\par
Jo glowered darkly, to Laurie's infinite amusement. Two easy-chairs\par
stood side by side at the head of the table, in which sat Beth and her\par
father, feasting modestly on chicken and a little fruit. They drank\par
healths, told stories, sung songs, "reminisced," as the old folks say,\par
and had a thoroughly good time. A sleigh-ride had been planned, but the\par
girls would not leave their father; so the guests departed early, and,\par
as twilight gathered, the happy family sat together round the fire.\par
\par
"Just a year ago we were groaning over the dismal Christmas we expected\par
to have. Do you remember?" asked Jo, breaking a short pause which had\par
followed a long conversation about many things.\par
\par
"Rather a pleasant year on the whole!" said Meg, smiling at the fire,\par
and congratulating herself on having treated Mr. Brooke with dignity.\par
\par
"I think it's been a pretty hard one," observed Amy, watching the light\par
shine on her ring, with thoughtful eyes.\par
\par
"I'm glad it's over, because we've got you back," whispered Beth, who\par
sat on her father's knee.\par
\par
"Rather a rough road for you to travel, my little pilgrims, especially\par
the latter part of it. But you have got on bravely; and I think the\par
burdens are in a fair way to tumble off very soon," said Mr. March,\par
looking with fatherly satisfaction at the four young faces gathered\par
round him.\par
\par
"How do you know? Did mother tell you?" asked Jo.\par
\par
"Not much; straws show which way the wind blows, and I've made several\par
discoveries to-day."\par
\par
"Oh, tell us what they are!" cried Meg, who sat beside him.\par
\par
"Here is one;" and taking up the hand which lay on the arm of his chair,\par
he pointed to the roughened forefinger, a burn on the back, and two or\par
three little hard spots on the palm. "I remember a time when this hand\par
was white and smooth, and your first care was to keep it so. It was very\par
pretty then, but to me it is much prettier now,--for in these seeming\par
blemishes I read a little history. A burnt-offering has been made of\par
vanity; this hardened palm has earned something better than blisters;\par
and I'm sure the sewing done by these pricked fingers will last a long\par
time, so much good-will went into the stitches. Meg, my dear, I value\par
the womanly skill which keeps home happy more than white hands or\par
fashionable accomplishments. I'm proud to shake this good, industrious\par
little hand, and hope I shall not soon be asked to give it away."\par
\par
If Meg had wanted a reward for hours of patient labor, she received it\par
in the hearty pressure of her father's hand and the approving smile he\par
gave her.\par
\par
"What about Jo? Please say something nice; for she has tried so hard,\par
and been so very, very good to me," said Beth, in her father's ear.\par
\par
He laughed, and looked across at the tall girl who sat opposite, with an\par
unusually mild expression in her brown face.\par
\par
"In spite of the curly crop, I don't see the 'son Jo' whom I left a year\par
ago," said Mr. March. "I see a young lady who pins her collar straight,\par
laces her boots neatly, and neither whistles, talks slang, nor lies on\par
the rug as she used to do. Her face is rather thin and pale, just now,\par
with watching and anxiety; but I like to look at it, for it has grown\par
gentler, and her voice is lower; she doesn't bounce, but moves quietly,\par
and takes care of a certain little person in a motherly way which\par
delights me. I rather miss my wild girl; but if I get a strong, helpful,\par
tender-hearted woman in her place, I shall feel quite satisfied. I don't\par
know whether the shearing sobered our black sheep, but I do know that in\par
all Washington I couldn't find anything beautiful enough to be bought\par
with the five-and-twenty dollars which my good girl sent me."\par
\par
Jo's keen eyes were rather dim for a minute, and her thin face grew rosy\par
in the firelight, as she received her father's praise, feeling that she\par
did deserve a portion of it.\par
\par
"Now Beth," said Amy, longing for her turn, but ready to wait.\par
\par
"There's so little of her, I'm afraid to say much, for fear she will\par
slip away altogether, though she is not so shy as she used to be," began\par
their father cheerfully; but recollecting how nearly he _had_ lost her,\par
he held her close, saying tenderly, with her cheek against his own,\par
"I've got you safe, my Beth, and I'll keep you so, please God."\par
\par
After a minute's silence, he looked down at Amy, who sat on the cricket\par
at his feet, and said, with a caress of the shining hair,--\par
\par
"I observed that Amy took drumsticks at dinner, ran errands for her\par
mother all the afternoon, gave Meg her place to-night, and has waited\par
on every one with patience and good-humor. I also observe that she does\par
not fret much nor look in the glass, and has not even mentioned a very\par
pretty ring which she wears; so I conclude that she has learned to think\par
of other people more and of herself less, and has decided to try and\par
mould her character as carefully as she moulds her little clay figures.\par
I am glad of this; for though I should be very proud of a graceful\par
statue made by her, I shall be infinitely prouder of a lovable daughter,\par
with a talent for making life beautiful to herself and others."\par
\par
"What are you thinking of, Beth?" asked Jo, when Amy had thanked her\par
father and told about her ring.\par
\par
"I read in 'Pilgrim's Progress' to-day, how, after many troubles,\par
Christian and Hopeful came to a pleasant green meadow, where lilies\par
bloomed all the year round, and there they rested happily, as we do now,\par
before they went on to their journey's end," answered Beth; adding, as\par
she slipped out of her father's arms, and went slowly to the instrument,\par
"It's singing time now, and I want to be in my old place. I'll try to\par
sing the song of the shepherd-boy which the Pilgrims heard. I made the\par
music for father, because he likes the verses."\par
\par
So, sitting at the dear little piano, Beth softly touched the keys, and,\par
in the sweet voice they had never thought to hear again, sung to her own\par
accompaniment the quaint hymn, which was a singularly fitting song for\par
her:--\par
\par
        "He that is down need fear no fall,\par
          He that is low no pride;\par
         He that is humble ever shall\par
          Have God to be his guide.\par
\par
        "I am content with what I have,\par
          Little be it or much;\par
         And, Lord! contentment still I crave,\par
          Because Thou savest such.\par
\par
        "Fulness to them a burden is,\par
          That go on pilgrimage;\par
         Here little, and hereafter bliss,\par
          Is best from age to age!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                 XXIII.\par
\par
                    AUNT MARCH SETTLES THE QUESTION.\par
\par
\par
            [Illustration: Popping in her head now and then]\par
\par
Like bees swarming after their queen, mother and daughters hovered about\par
Mr. March the next day, neglecting everything to look at, wait upon, and\par
listen to the new invalid, who was in a fair way to be killed by\par
kindness. As he sat propped up in a big chair by Beth's sofa, with the\par
other three close by, and Hannah popping in her head now and then, "to\par
peek at the dear man," nothing seemed needed to complete their\par
happiness. But something _was_ needed, and the elder ones felt it,\par
though none confessed the fact. Mr. and Mrs. March looked at one another\par
with an anxious expression, as their eyes followed Meg. Jo had sudden\par
fits of sobriety, and was seen to shake her fist at Mr. Brooke's\par
umbrella, which had been left in the hall; Meg was absent-minded, shy,\par
and silent, started when the bell rang, and colored when John's name was\par
mentioned; Amy said "Every one seemed waiting for something, and\par
couldn't settle down, which was queer, since father was safe at home,"\par
and Beth innocently wondered why their neighbors didn't run over as\par
usual.\par
\par
 [Illustration: "He sat in the big chair by Beth's sofa with the other\par
                      three close by."--Page 277.]\par
\par
Laurie went by in the afternoon, and, seeing Meg at the window, seemed\par
suddenly possessed with a melodramatic fit, for he fell down upon one\par
knee in the snow, beat his breast, tore his hair, and clasped his hands\par
imploringly, as if begging some boon; and when Meg told him to behave\par
himself and go away, he wrung imaginary tears out of his handkerchief,\par
and staggered round the corner as if in utter despair.\par
\par
"What does the goose mean?" said Meg, laughing, and trying to look\par
unconscious.\par
\par
"He's showing you how your John will go on by and by. Touching, isn't\par
it?" answered Jo scornfully.\par
\par
"Don't say _my John_, it isn't proper or true;" but Meg's voice lingered\par
over the words as if they sounded pleasant to her. "Please don't plague\par
me, Jo; I've told you I don't care _much_ about him, and there isn't to\par
be anything said, but we are all to be friendly, and go on as before."\par
\par
"We can't, for something _has_ been said, and Laurie's mischief has\par
spoilt you for me. I see it, and so does mother; you are not like your\par
old self a bit, and seem ever so far away from me. I don't mean to\par
plague you, and will bear it like a man, but I do wish it was all\par
settled. I hate to wait; so if you mean ever to do it, make haste and\par
have it over quickly," said Jo pettishly.\par
\par
"_I_ can't say or do anything till he speaks, and he won't, because\par
father said I was too young," began Meg, bending over her work, with a\par
queer little smile, which suggested that she did not quite agree with\par
her father on that point.\par
\par
"If he did speak, you wouldn't know what to say, but would cry or blush,\par
or let him have his own way, instead of giving a good, decided, No."\par
\par
"I'm not so silly and weak as you think. I know just what I should say,\par
for I've planned it all, so I needn't be taken unawares; there's no\par
knowing what may happen, and I wished to be prepared."\par
\par
Jo couldn't help smiling at the important air which Meg had\par
unconsciously assumed, and which was as becoming as the pretty color\par
varying in her cheeks.\par
\par
"Would you mind telling me what you'd say?" asked Jo more respectfully.\par
\par
"Not at all; you are sixteen now, quite old enough to be my confidant,\par
and my experience will be useful to you by and by, perhaps, in your own\par
affairs of this sort."\par
\par
"Don't mean to have any; it's fun to watch other people philander, but I\par
should feel like a fool doing it myself," said Jo, looking alarmed at\par
the thought.\par
\par
"I think not, if you liked any one very much, and he liked you." Meg\par
spoke as if to herself, and glanced out at the lane, where she had often\par
seen lovers walking together in the summer twilight.\par
\par
"I thought you were going to tell your speech to that man," said Jo,\par
rudely shortening her sister's little reverie.\par
\par
"Oh, I should merely say, quite calmly and decidedly, 'Thank you, Mr.\par
Brooke, you are very kind, but I agree with father that I am too young\par
to enter into any engagement at present; so please say no more, but let\par
us be friends as we were.'"\par
\par
"Hum! that's stiff and cool enough. I don't believe you'll ever say it,\par
and I know he won't be satisfied if you do. If he goes on like the\par
rejected lovers in books, you'll give in, rather than hurt his\par
feelings."\par
\par
"No, I won't. I shall tell him I've made up my mind, and shall walk out\par
of the room with dignity."\par
\par
Meg rose as she spoke, and was just going to rehearse the dignified\par
exit, when a step in the hall made her fly into her seat, and begin to\par
sew as if her life depended on finishing that particular seam in a given\par
time. Jo smothered a laugh at the sudden change, and, when some one gave\par
a modest tap, opened the door with a grim aspect, which was anything but\par
hospitable.\par
\par
"Good afternoon. I came to get my umbrella,--that is, to see how your\par
father finds himself to-day," said Mr. Brooke, getting a trifle confused\par
as his eye went from one tell-tale face to the other.\par
\par
"It's very well, he's in the rack, I'll get him, and tell it you are\par
here," and having jumbled her father and the umbrella well together in\par
her reply, Jo slipped out of the room to give Meg a chance to make her\par
speech and air her dignity. But the instant she vanished, Meg began to\par
sidle towards the door, murmuring,--\par
\par
"Mother will like to see you. Pray sit down, I'll call her."\par
\par
"Don't go; are you afraid of me, Margaret?" and Mr. Brooke looked so\par
hurt that Meg thought she must have done something very rude. She\par
blushed up to the little curls on her forehead, for he had never called\par
her Margaret before, and she was surprised to find how natural and sweet\par
it seemed to hear him say it. Anxious to appear friendly and at her\par
ease, she put out her hand with a confiding gesture, and said\par
gratefully,--\par
\par
"How can I be afraid when you have been so kind to father? I only wish I\par
could thank you for it."\par
\par
                 [Illustration: Shall I tell you how?]\par
\par
"Shall I tell you how?" asked Mr. Brooke, holding the small hand fast in\par
both his own, and looking down at Meg with so much love in the brown\par
eyes, that her heart began to flutter, and she both longed to run away\par
and to stop and listen.\par
\par
"Oh no, please don't--I'd rather not," she said, trying to withdraw her\par
hand, and looking frightened in spite of her denial.\par
\par
"I won't trouble you, I only want to know if you care for me a little,\par
Meg. I love you so much, dear," added Mr. Brooke tenderly.\par
\par
This was the moment for the calm, proper speech, but Meg didn't make it;\par
she forgot every word of it, hung her head, and answered, "I don't\par
know," so softly, that John had to stoop down to catch the foolish\par
little reply.\par
\par
He seemed to think it was worth the trouble, for he smiled to himself as\par
if quite satisfied, pressed the plump hand gratefully, and said, in his\par
most persuasive tone, "Will you try and find out? I want to know _so_\par
much; for I can't go to work with any heart until I learn whether I am\par
to have my reward in the end or not."\par
\par
"I'm too young," faltered Meg, wondering why she was so fluttered, yet\par
rather enjoying it.\par
\par
"I'll wait; and in the meantime, you could be learning to like me. Would\par
it be a very hard lesson, dear?"\par
\par
"Not if I chose to learn it, but--"\par
\par
"Please choose to learn, Meg. I love to teach, and this is easier than\par
German," broke in John, getting possession of the other hand, so that\par
she had no way of hiding her face, as he bent to look into it.\par
\par
His tone was properly beseeching; but, stealing a shy look at him, Meg\par
saw that his eyes were merry as well as tender, and that he wore the\par
satisfied smile of one who had no doubt of his success. This nettled\par
her; Annie Moffat's foolish lessons in coquetry came into her mind, and\par
the love of power, which sleeps in the bosoms of the best of little\par
women, woke up all of a sudden and took possession of her. She felt\par
excited and strange, and, not knowing what else to do, followed a\par
capricious impulse, and, withdrawing her hands, said petulantly, "I\par
_don't_ choose. Please go away and let me be!"\par
\par
Poor Mr. Brooke looked as if his lovely castle in the air was tumbling\par
about his ears, for he had never seen Meg in such a mood before, and it\par
rather bewildered him.\par
\par
"Do you really mean that?" he asked anxiously, following her as she\par
walked away.\par
\par
"Yes, I do; I don't want to be worried about such things. Father says I\par
needn't; it's too soon and I'd rather not."\par
\par
"Mayn't I hope you'll change your mind by and by? I'll wait, and say\par
nothing till you have had more time. Don't play with me, Meg. I didn't\par
think that of you."\par
\par
"Don't think of me at all. I'd rather you wouldn't," said Meg, taking a\par
naughty satisfaction in trying her lover's patience and her own power.\par
\par
He was grave and pale now, and looked decidedly more like the novel\par
heroes whom she admired; but he neither slapped his forehead nor tramped\par
about the room, as they did; he just stood looking at her so wistfully,\par
so tenderly, that she found her heart relenting in spite of her. What\par
would have happened next I cannot say, if Aunt March had not come\par
hobbling in at this interesting minute.\par
\par
The old lady couldn't resist her longing to see her nephew; for she had\par
met Laurie as she took her airing, and, hearing of Mr. March's arrival,\par
drove straight out to see him. The family were all busy in the back part\par
of the house, and she had made her way quietly in, hoping to surprise\par
them. She did surprise two of them so much that Meg started as if she\par
had seen a ghost, and Mr. Brooke vanished into the study.\par
\par
"Bless me, what's all this?" cried the old lady, with a rap of her cane,\par
as she glanced from the pale young gentleman to the scarlet young lady.\par
\par
               [Illustration: Bless me, what's all this?]\par
\par
"It's father's friend. I'm _so_ surprised to see you!" stammered Meg,\par
feeling that she was in for a lecture now.\par
\par
"That's evident," returned Aunt March, sitting down. "But what is\par
father's friend saying to make you look like a peony? There's mischief\par
going on, and I insist upon knowing what it is," with another rap.\par
\par
"We were merely talking. Mr. Brooke came for his umbrella," began Meg,\par
wishing that Mr. Brooke and the umbrella were safely out of the house.\par
\par
"Brooke? That boy's tutor? Ah! I understand now. I know all about it. Jo\par
blundered into a wrong message in one of your father's letters, and I\par
made her tell me. You haven't gone and accepted him, child?" cried Aunt\par
March, looking scandalized.\par
\par
"Hush! he'll hear. Sha'n't I call mother?" said Meg, much troubled.\par
\par
"Not yet. I've something to say to you, and I must free my mind at once.\par
Tell me, do you mean to marry this Cook? If you do, not one penny of my\par
money ever goes to you. Remember that, and be a sensible girl," said the\par
old lady impressively.\par
\par
Now Aunt March possessed in perfection the art of rousing the spirit of\par
opposition in the gentlest people, and enjoyed doing it. The best of us\par
have a spice of perversity in us, especially when we are young and in\par
love. If Aunt March had begged Meg to accept John Brooke, she would\par
probably have declared she couldn't think of it; but as she was\par
peremptorily ordered _not_ to like him, she immediately made up her mind\par
that she would. Inclination as well as perversity made the decision\par
easy, and, being already much excited, Meg opposed the old lady with\par
unusual spirit.\par
\par
"I shall marry whom I please, Aunt March, and you can leave your money\par
to any one you like," she said, nodding her head with a resolute air.\par
\par
"Highty tighty! Is that the way you take my advice, miss? You'll be\par
sorry for it, by and by, when you've tried love in a cottage, and found\par
it a failure."\par
\par
"It can't be a worse one than some people find in big houses," retorted\par
Meg.\par
\par
Aunt March put on her glasses and took a look at the girl, for she did\par
not know her in this new mood. Meg hardly knew herself, she felt so\par
brave and independent,--so glad to defend John, and assert her right to\par
love him, if she liked. Aunt March saw that she had begun wrong, and,\par
after a little pause, made a fresh start, saying, as mildly as she\par
could, "Now, Meg, my dear, be reasonable, and take my advice. I mean it\par
kindly, and don't want you to spoil your whole life by making a mistake\par
at the beginning. You ought to marry well, and help your family; it's\par
your duty to make a rich match, and it ought to be impressed upon you."\par
\par
"Father and mother don't think so; they like John, though he _is_ poor."\par
\par
"Your parents, my dear, have no more worldly wisdom than two babies."\par
\par
"I'm glad of it," cried Meg stoutly.\par
\par
Aunt March took no notice, but went on with her lecture. "This Rook is\par
poor, and hasn't got any rich relations, has he?"\par
\par
"No; but he has many warm friends."\par
\par
"You can't live on friends; try it, and see how cool they'll grow. He\par
hasn't any business, has he?"\par
\par
"Not yet; Mr. Laurence is going to help him."\par
\par
"That won't last long. James Laurence is a crotchety old fellow, and not\par
to be depended on. So you intend to marry a man without money, position,\par
or business, and go on working harder than you do now, when you might be\par
comfortable all your days by minding me and doing better? I thought you\par
had more sense, Meg."\par
\par
"I couldn't do better if I waited half my life! John is good and wise;\par
he's got heaps of talent; he's willing to work, and sure to get on, he's\par
so energetic and brave. Every one likes and respects him, and I'm proud\par
to think he cares for me, though I'm so poor and young and silly," said\par
Meg, looking prettier than ever in her earnestness.\par
\par
"He knows _you_ have got rich relations, child; that's the secret of his\par
liking, I suspect."\par
\par
"Aunt March, how dare you say such a thing? John is above such meanness,\par
and I won't listen to you a minute if you talk so," cried Meg\par
indignantly, forgetting everything but the injustice of the old lady's\par
suspicions. "My John wouldn't marry for money, anymore than I would. We\par
are willing to work, and we mean to wait. I'm not afraid of being poor,\par
for I've been happy so far, and I know I shall be with him, because he\par
loves me, and I--"\par
\par
Meg stopped there, remembering all of a sudden that she hadn't made up\par
her mind; that she had told "her John" to go away, and that he might be\par
overhearing her inconsistent remarks.\par
\par
Aunt March was very angry, for she had set her heart on having her\par
pretty niece make a fine match, and something in the girl's happy young\par
face made the lonely old woman feel both sad and sour.\par
\par
"Well, I wash my hands of the whole affair! You are a wilful child, and\par
you've lost more than you know by this piece of folly. No, I won't stop;\par
I'm disappointed in you, and haven't spirits to see your father now.\par
Don't expect anything from me when you are married; your Mr. Book's\par
friends must take care of you. I'm done with you forever."\par
\par
And, slamming the door in Meg's face, Aunt March drove off in high\par
dudgeon. She seemed to take all the girl's courage with her; for, when\par
left alone, Meg stood a moment, undecided whether to laugh or cry.\par
Before she could make up her mind, she was taken possession of by Mr.\par
Brooke, who said, all in one breath, "I couldn't help hearing, Meg.\par
Thank you for defending me, and Aunt March for proving that you _do_\par
care for me a little bit."\par
\par
"I didn't know how much, till she abused you," began Meg.\par
\par
"And I needn't go away, but may stay and be happy, may I, dear?"\par
\par
Here was another fine chance to make the crushing speech and the stately\par
exit, but Meg never thought of doing either, and disgraced herself\par
forever in Jo's eyes by meekly whispering, "Yes, John," and hiding her\par
face on Mr. Brooke's waistcoat.\par
\par
Fifteen minutes after Aunt March's departure, Jo came softly down\par
stairs, paused an instant at the parlor door, and, hearing no sound\par
within, nodded and smiled, with a satisfied expression, saying to\par
herself, "She has sent him away as we planned, and that affair is\par
settled. I'll go and hear the fun, and have a good laugh over it."\par
\par
But poor Jo never got her laugh, for she was transfixed upon the\par
threshold by a spectacle which held her there, staring with her mouth\par
nearly as wide open as her eyes. Going in to exult over a fallen enemy,\par
and to praise a strong-minded sister for the banishment of an\par
objectionable lover, it certainly _was_ a shock to behold the aforesaid\par
enemy serenely sitting on the sofa, with the strong-minded sister\par
enthroned upon his knee, and wearing an expression of the most abject\par
submission. Jo gave a sort of gasp, as if a cold shower-bath had\par
suddenly fallen upon her,--for such an unexpected turning of the tables\par
actually took her breath away. At the odd sound, the lovers turned and\par
saw her. Meg jumped up, looking both proud and shy; but "that man," as\par
Jo called him, actually laughed, and said coolly, as he kissed the\par
astonished new-comer, "Sister Jo, congratulate us!"\par
\par
That was adding insult to injury,--it was altogether too much,--and,\par
making some wild demonstration with her hands, Jo vanished without a\par
word. Rushing upstairs, she startled the invalids by exclaiming\par
tragically, as she burst into the room, "Oh, _do_ somebody go down\par
quick; John Brooke is acting dreadfully, and Meg likes it!"\par
\par
Mr. and Mrs. March left the room with speed; and, casting herself upon\par
the bed, Jo cried and scolded tempestuously as she told the awful news\par
to Beth and Amy. The little girls, however, considered it a most\par
agreeable and interesting event, and Jo got little comfort from them; so\par
she went up to her refuge in the garret, and confided her troubles to\par
the rats.\par
\par
Nobody ever knew what went on in the parlor that afternoon; but a great\par
deal of talking was done, and quiet Mr. Brooke astonished his friends by\par
the eloquence and spirit with which he pleaded his suit, told his plans,\par
and persuaded them to arrange everything just as he wanted it.\par
\par
The tea-bell rang before he had finished describing the paradise which\par
he meant to earn for Meg, and he proudly took her in to supper, both\par
looking so happy that Jo hadn't the heart to be jealous or dismal. Amy\par
was very much impressed by John's devotion and Meg's dignity. Beth\par
beamed at them from a distance, while Mr. and Mrs. March surveyed the\par
young couple with such tender satisfaction that it was perfectly evident\par
Aunt March was right in calling them as "unworldly as a pair of babies."\par
No one ate much, but every one looked very happy, and the old room\par
seemed to brighten up amazingly when the first romance of the family\par
began there.\par
\par
"You can't say nothing pleasant ever happens now, can you, Meg?" said\par
Amy, trying to decide how she would group the lovers in the sketch she\par
was planning to take.\par
\par
"No, I'm sure I can't. How much has happened since I said that! It seems\par
a year ago," answered Meg, who was in a blissful dream, lifted far above\par
such common things as bread and butter.\par
\par
"The joys come close upon the sorrows this time, and I rather think the\par
changes have begun," said Mrs. March. "In most families there comes, now\par
and then, a year full of events; this has been such an one, but it ends\par
well, after all."\par
\par
"Hope the next will end better," muttered Jo, who found it very hard to\par
see Meg absorbed in a stranger before her face; for Jo loved a few\par
persons very dearly, and dreaded to have their affection lost or\par
lessened in any way.\par
\par
"I hope the third year from this _will_ end better; I mean it shall, if\par
I live to work out my plans," said Mr. Brooke, smiling at Meg, as if\par
everything had become possible to him now.\par
\par
"Doesn't it seem very long to wait?" asked Amy, who was in a hurry for\par
the wedding.\par
\par
"I've got so much to learn before I shall be ready, it seems a short\par
time to me," answered Meg, with a sweet gravity in her face, never seen\par
there before.\par
\par
"You have only to wait; _I_ am to do the work," said John, beginning his\par
labors by picking up Meg's napkin, with an expression which caused Jo to\par
shake her head, and then say to herself, with an air of relief, as the\par
front door banged, "Here comes Laurie. Now we shall have a little\par
sensible conversation."\par
\par
But Jo was mistaken; for Laurie came prancing in, overflowing with\par
spirits, bearing a great bridal-looking bouquet for "Mrs. John Brooke,"\par
and evidently laboring under the delusion that the whole affair had been\par
brought about by his excellent management.\par
\par
                  [Illustration: For Mrs. John Brooke]\par
\par
"I knew Brooke would have it all his own way, he always does; for when\par
he makes up his mind to accomplish anything, it's done, though the sky\par
falls," said Laurie, when he had presented his offering and his\par
congratulations.\par
\par
"Much obliged for that recommendation. I take it as a good omen for the\par
future, and invite you to my wedding on the spot," answered Mr. Brooke,\par
who felt at peace with all mankind, even his mischievous pupil.\par
\par
"I'll come if I'm at the ends of the earth; for the sight of Jo's face\par
alone, on that occasion, would be worth a long journey. You don't look\par
festive, ma'am; what's the matter?" asked Laurie, following her into a\par
corner of the parlor, whither all had adjourned to greet Mr. Laurence.\par
\par
"I don't approve of the match, but I've made up my mind to bear it, and\par
shall not say a word against it," said Jo solemnly. "You can't know how\par
hard it is for me to give up Meg," she continued, with a little quiver\par
in her voice.\par
\par
"You don't give her up. You only go halves," said Laurie consolingly.\par
\par
"It never can be the same again. I've lost my dearest friend," sighed\par
Jo.\par
\par
"You've got me, anyhow. I'm not good for much, I know; but I'll stand\par
by you, Jo, all the days of my life; upon my word I will!" and Laurie\par
meant what he said.\par
\par
"I know you will, and I'm ever so much obliged; you are always a great\par
comfort to me, Teddy," returned Jo, gratefully shaking hands.\par
\par
"Well, now, don't be dismal, there's a good fellow. It's all right, you\par
see. Meg is happy; Brooke will fly round and get settled immediately;\par
grandpa will attend to him, and it will be very jolly to see Meg in her\par
own little house. We'll have capital times after she is gone, for I\par
shall be through college before long, and then we'll go abroad, or some\par
nice trip or other. Wouldn't that console you?"\par
\par
"I rather think it would; but there's no knowing what may happen in\par
three years," said Jo thoughtfully.\par
\par
"That's true. Don't you wish you could take a look forward, and see\par
where we shall all be then? I do," returned Laurie.\par
\par
"I think not, for I might see something sad; and every one looks so\par
happy now, I don't believe they could be much improved," and Jo's eyes\par
went slowly round the room, brightening as they looked, for the prospect\par
was a pleasant one.\par
\par
Father and mother sat together, quietly re-living the first chapter of\par
the romance which for them began some twenty years ago. Amy was drawing\par
the lovers, who sat apart in a beautiful world of their own, the light\par
of which touched their faces with a grace the little artist could not\par
copy. Beth lay on her sofa, talking cheerily with her old friend, who\par
held her little hand as if he felt that it possessed the power to lead\par
him along the peaceful way she walked. Jo lounged in her favorite low\par
seat, with the grave, quiet look which best became her; and Laurie,\par
leaning on the back of her chair, his chin on a level with her curly\par
head, smiled with his friendliest aspect, and nodded at her in the long\par
glass which reflected them both.\par
\par
                   *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
So grouped, the curtain falls upon Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy. Whether it\par
ever rises again, depends upon the reception given to the first act of\par
the domestic drama called "LITTLE WOMEN."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                [Illustration: Home of the Little Women]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            The Second Part\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                     [Illustration: The Dove Cote]\par
\par
                                 XXIV.\par
\par
                                GOSSIP.\par
\par
\par
In order that we may start afresh, and go to Meg's wedding with free\par
minds, it will be well to begin with a little gossip about the Marches.\par
And here let me premise, that if any of the elders think there is too\par
much "lovering" in the story, as I fear they may (I'm not afraid the\par
young folks will make that objection), I can only say with Mrs. March,\par
"What _can_ you expect when I have four gay girls in the house, and a\par
dashing young neighbor over the way?"\par
\par
The three years that have passed have brought but few changes to the\par
quiet family. The war is over, and Mr. March safely at home, busy with\par
his books and the small parish which found in him a minister by nature\par
as by grace,--a quiet, studious man, rich in the wisdom that is better\par
than learning, the charity which calls all mankind "brother," the piety\par
that blossoms into character, making it august and lovely.\par
\par
These attributes, in spite of poverty and the strict integrity which\par
shut him out from the more worldly successes, attracted to him many\par
admirable persons, as naturally as sweet herbs draw bees, and as\par
naturally he gave them the honey into which fifty years of hard\par
experience had distilled no bitter drop. Earnest young men found the\par
gray-headed scholar as young at heart as they; thoughtful or troubled\par
women instinctively brought their doubts and sorrows to him, sure of\par
finding the gentlest sympathy, the wisest counsel; sinners told their\par
sins to the pure-hearted old man, and were both rebuked and saved;\par
gifted men found a companion in him; ambitious men caught glimpses of\par
nobler ambitions than their own; and even worldlings confessed that his\par
beliefs were beautiful and true, although "they wouldn't pay."\par
\par
To outsiders, the five energetic women seemed to rule the house, and so\par
they did in many things; but the quiet scholar, sitting among his books,\par
was still the head of the family, the household conscience, anchor, and\par
comforter; for to him the busy, anxious women always turned in troublous\par
times, finding him, in the truest sense of those sacred words, husband\par
and father.\par
\par
The girls gave their hearts into their mother's keeping, their souls\par
into their father's; and to both parents, who lived and labored so\par
faithfully for them, they gave a love that grew with their growth, and\par
bound them tenderly together by the sweetest tie which blesses life and\par
outlives death.\par
\par
Mrs. March is as brisk and cheery, though rather grayer, than when we\par
saw her last, and just now so absorbed in Meg's affairs that the\par
hospitals and homes, still full of wounded "boys" and soldiers' widows,\par
decidedly miss the motherly missionary's visits.\par
\par
John Brooke did his duty manfully for a year, got wounded, was sent\par
home, and not allowed to return. He received no stars or bars, but he\par
deserved them, for he cheerfully risked all he had; and life and love\par
are very precious when both are in full bloom. Perfectly resigned to his\par
discharge, he devoted himself to getting well, preparing for business,\par
and earning a home for Meg. With the good sense and sturdy independence\par
that characterized him, he refused Mr. Laurence's more generous offers,\par
and accepted the place of book-keeper feeling better satisfied to begin\par
with an honestly-earned salary, than by running any risks with borrowed\par
money.\par
\par
Meg had spent the time in working as well as waiting, growing womanly in\par
character, wise in housewifely arts, and prettier than ever; for love is\par
a great beautifier. She had her girlish ambitions and hopes, and felt\par
some disappointment at the humble way in which the new life must begin.\par
Ned Moffat had just married Sallie Gardiner, and Meg couldn't help\par
contrasting their fine house and carriage, many gifts, and splendid\par
outfit, with her own, and secretly wishing she could have the same. But\par
somehow envy and discontent soon vanished when she thought of all the\par
patient love and labor John had put into the little home awaiting her;\par
and when they sat together in the twilight, talking over their small\par
plans, the future always grew so beautiful and bright that she forgot\par
Sallie's splendor, and felt herself the richest, happiest girl in\par
Christendom.\par
\par
Jo never went back to Aunt March, for the old lady took such a fancy to\par
Amy that she bribed her with the offer of drawing lessons from one of\par
the best teachers going; and for the sake of this advantage, Amy would\par
have served a far harder mistress. So she gave her mornings to duty, her\par
afternoons to pleasure, and prospered finely. Jo, meantime, devoted\par
herself to literature and Beth, who remained delicate long after the\par
fever was a thing of the past. Not an invalid exactly, but never again\par
the rosy, healthy creature she had been; yet always hopeful, happy, and\par
serene, busy with the quiet duties she loved, every one's friend, and an\par
angel in the house, long before those who loved her most had learned to\par
know it.\par
\par
As long as "The Spread Eagle" paid her a dollar a column for her\par
"rubbish," as she called it, Jo felt herself a woman of means, and spun\par
her little romances diligently. But great plans fermented in her busy\par
brain and ambitious mind, and the old tin kitchen in the garret held a\par
slowly increasing pile of blotted manuscript, which was one day to place\par
the name of March upon the roll of fame.\par
\par
Laurie, having dutifully gone to college to please his grandfather, was\par
now getting through it in the easiest possible manner to please\par
himself. A universal favorite, thanks to money, manners, much talent,\par
and the kindest heart that ever got its owner into scrapes by trying to\par
get other people out of them, he stood in great danger of being spoilt,\par
and probably would have been, like many another promising boy, if he had\par
not possessed a talisman against evil in the memory of the kind old man\par
who was bound up in his success, the motherly friend who watched over\par
him as if he were her son, and last, but not least by any means, the\par
knowledge that four innocent girls loved, admired, and believed in him\par
with all their hearts.\par
\par
Being only "a glorious human boy," of course he frolicked and flirted,\par
grew dandified, aquatic, sentimental, or gymnastic, as college fashions\par
ordained; hazed and was hazed, talked slang, and more than once came\par
perilously near suspension and expulsion. But as high spirits and the\par
love of fun were the causes of these pranks, he always managed to save\par
himself by frank confession, honorable atonement, or the irresistible\par
power of persuasion which he possessed in perfection. In fact, he rather\par
prided himself on his narrow escapes, and liked to thrill the girls with\par
graphic accounts of his triumphs over wrathful tutors, dignified\par
professors, and vanquished enemies. The "men of my class" were heroes in\par
the eyes of the girls, who never wearied of the exploits of "our\par
fellows," and were frequently allowed to bask in the smiles of these\par
great creatures, when Laurie brought them home with him.\par
\par
Amy especially enjoyed this high honor, and became quite a belle among\par
them; for her ladyship early felt and learned to use the gift of\par
fascination with which she was endowed. Meg was too much absorbed in her\par
private and particular John to care for any other lords of creation, and\par
Beth too shy to do more than peep at them, and wonder how Amy dared to\par
order them about so; but Jo felt quite in her element, and found it very\par
difficult to refrain from imitating the gentlemanly attitudes, phrases,\par
and feats, which seemed more natural to her than the decorums prescribed\par
for young ladies. They all liked Jo immensely, but never fell in love\par
with her, though very few escaped without paying the tribute of a\par
sentimental sigh or two at Amy's shrine. And speaking of sentiment\par
brings us very naturally to the "Dove-cote."\par
\par
That was the name of the little brown house which Mr. Brooke had\par
prepared for Meg's first home. Laurie had christened it, saying it was\par
highly appropriate to the gentle lovers, who "went on together like a\par
pair of turtle-doves, with first a bill and then a coo." It was a tiny\par
house, with a little garden behind, and a lawn about as big as a\par
pocket-handkerchief in front. Here Meg meant to have a fountain,\par
shrubbery, and a profusion of lovely flowers; though just at present,\par
the fountain was represented by a weather-beaten urn, very like a\par
dilapidated slop-bowl; the shrubbery consisted of several young larches,\par
undecided whether to live or die; and the profusion of flowers was\par
merely hinted by regiments of sticks, to show where seeds were planted.\par
But inside, it was altogether charming, and the happy bride saw no fault\par
from garret to cellar. To be sure, the hall was so narrow, it was\par
fortunate that they had no piano, for one never could have been got in\par
whole; the dining-room was so small that six people were a tight fit;\par
and the kitchen stairs seemed built for the express purpose of\par
precipitating both servants and china pell-mell into the coal-bin. But\par
once get used to these slight blemishes, and nothing could be more\par
complete, for good sense and good taste had presided over the\par
furnishing, and the result was highly satisfactory. There were no\par
marble-topped tables, long mirrors, or lace curtains in the little\par
parlor, but simple furniture, plenty of books, a fine picture or two, a\par
stand of flowers in the bay-window, and, scattered all about, the pretty\par
gifts which came from friendly hands, and were the fairer for the loving\par
messages they brought.\par
\par
I don't think the Parian Psyche Laurie gave lost any of its beauty\par
because John put up the bracket it stood upon; that any upholsterer\par
could have draped the plain muslin curtains more gracefully than Amy's\par
artistic hand; or that any store-room was ever better provided with good\par
wishes, merry words, and happy hopes, than that in which Jo and her\par
mother put away Meg's few boxes, barrels, and bundles; and I am morally\par
certain that the spandy-new kitchen never _could_ have looked so cosey\par
and neat if Hannah had not arranged every pot and pan a dozen times\par
over, and laid the fire all ready for lighting, the minute "Mis. Brooke\par
came home." I also doubt if any young matron ever began life with so\par
rich a supply of dusters, holders, and piece-bags; for Beth made enough\par
to last till the silver wedding came round, and invented three\par
different kinds of dishcloths for the express service of the bridal\par
china.\par
\par
People who hire all these things done for them never know what they\par
lose; for the homeliest tasks get beautified if loving hands do them,\par
and Meg found so many proofs of this, that everything in her small nest,\par
from the kitchen roller to the silver vase on her parlor table, was\par
eloquent of home love and tender forethought.\par
\par
What happy times they had planning together, what solemn shopping\par
excursions; what funny mistakes they made, and what shouts of laughter\par
arose over Laurie's ridiculous bargains. In his love of jokes, this\par
young gentleman, though nearly through college, was as much of a boy as\par
ever. His last whim had been to bring with him, on his weekly visits,\par
some new, useful, and ingenious article for the young housekeeper. Now a\par
bag of remarkable clothes-pins; next, a wonderful nutmeg-grater, which\par
fell to pieces at the first trial; a knife-cleaner that spoilt all the\par
knives; or a sweeper that picked the nap neatly off the carpet, and left\par
the dirt; labor-saving soap that took the skin off one's hands;\par
infallible cements which stuck firmly to nothing but the fingers of the\par
deluded buyer; and every kind of tin-ware, from a toy savings-bank for\par
odd pennies, to a wonderful boiler which would wash articles in its own\par
steam, with every prospect of exploding in the process.\par
\par
In vain Meg begged him to stop. John laughed at him, and Jo called him\par
"Mr. Toodles." He was possessed with a mania for patronizing Yankee\par
ingenuity, and seeing his friends fitly furnished forth. So each week\par
beheld some fresh absurdity.\par
\par
Everything was done at last, even to Amy's arranging different colored\par
soaps to match the different colored rooms, and Beth's setting the table\par
for the first meal.\par
\par
"Are you satisfied? Does it seem like home, and do you feel as if you\par
should be happy here?" asked Mrs. March, as she and her daughter went\par
through the new kingdom, arm-in-arm; for just then they seemed to cling\par
together more tenderly than ever.\par
\par
"Yes, mother, perfectly satisfied, thanks to you all, and _so_ happy\par
that I can't talk about it," answered Meg, with a look that was better\par
than words.\par
\par
"If she only had a servant or two it would be all right," said Amy,\par
coming out of the parlor, where she had been trying to decide whether\par
the bronze Mercury looked best on the whatnot or the mantle-piece.\par
\par
"Mother and I have talked that over, and I have made up my mind to try\par
her way first. There will be so little to do, that, with Lotty to run my\par
errands and help me here and there, I shall only have enough work to\par
keep me from getting lazy or homesick," answered Meg tranquilly.\par
\par
"Sallie Moffat has four," began Amy.\par
\par
"If Meg had four the house wouldn't hold them, and master and missis\par
would have to camp in the garden," broke in Jo, who, enveloped in a big\par
blue pinafore, was giving the last polish to the door-handles.\par
\par
"Sallie isn't a poor man's wife, and many maids are in keeping with her\par
fine establishment. Meg and John begin humbly, but I have a feeling that\par
there will be quite as much happiness in the little house as in the big\par
one. It's a great mistake for young girls like Meg to leave themselves\par
nothing to do but dress, give orders, and gossip. When I was first\par
married, I used to long for my new clothes to wear out or get torn, so\par
that I might have the pleasure of mending them; for I got heartily sick\par
of doing fancy work and tending my pocket handkerchief."\par
\par
"Why didn't you go into the kitchen and make messes, as Sallie says she\par
does, to amuse herself, though they never turn out well, and the\par
servants laugh at her," said Meg.\par
\par
"I did, after a while; not to 'mess,' but to learn of Hannah how things\par
should be done, that my servants need _not_ laugh at me. It was play\par
then; but there came a time when I was truly grateful that I not only\par
possessed the will but the power to cook wholesome food for my little\par
girls, and help myself when I could no longer afford to hire help. You\par
begin at the other end, Meg, dear; but the lessons you learn now will be\par
of use to you by and by, when John is a richer man, for the mistress of\par
a house, however splendid, should know how work ought to be done, if she\par
wishes to be well and honestly served."\par
\par
"Yes, mother, I'm sure of that," said Meg, listening respectfully to the\par
little lecture; for the best of women will hold forth upon the\par
all-absorbing subject of housekeeping. "Do you know I like this room\par
most of all in my baby-house," added Meg, a minute after, as they went\par
upstairs, and she looked into her well-stored linen-closet.\par
\par
Beth was there, laying the snowy piles smoothly on the shelves, and\par
exulting over the goodly array. All three laughed as Meg spoke; for that\par
linen-closet was a joke. You see, having said that if Meg married "that\par
Brooke" she shouldn't have a cent of her money, Aunt March was rather in\par
a quandary, when time had appeased her wrath and made her repent her\par
vow. She never broke her word, and was much exercised in her mind how to\par
get round it, and at last devised a plan whereby she could satisfy\par
herself. Mrs. Carrol, Florence's mamma, was ordered to buy, have made,\par
and marked, a generous supply of house and table linen, and send it as\par
_her_ present, all of which was faithfully done; but the secret leaked\par
out, and was greatly enjoyed by the family; for Aunt March tried to look\par
utterly unconscious, and insisted that she could give nothing but the\par
old-fashioned pearls, long promised to the first bride.\par
\par
"That's a housewifely taste which I am glad to see. I had a young friend\par
who set up housekeeping with six sheets, but she had finger bowls for\par
company, and that satisfied her," said Mrs. March, patting the damask\par
table-cloths, with a truly feminine appreciation of their fineness.\par
\par
"I haven't a single finger-bowl, but this is a 'set out' that will last\par
me all my days, Hannah says;" and Meg looked quite contented, as well\par
she might.\par
\par
"Toodles is coming," cried Jo from below; and they all went down to meet\par
Laurie, whose weekly visit was an important event in their quiet lives.\par
\par
A tall, broad-shouldered young fellow, with a cropped head, a felt-basin\par
of a hat, and a fly-away coat, came tramping down the road at a great\par
pace, walked over the low fence without stopping to open the gate,\par
straight up to Mrs. March, with both hands out, and a hearty--\par
\par
"Here I am, mother! Yes, it's all right."\par
\par
The last words were in answer to the look the elder lady gave him; a\par
kindly questioning look, which the handsome eyes met so frankly that the\par
little ceremony closed, as usual, with a motherly kiss.\par
\par
"For Mrs. John Brooke, with the maker's congratulations and compliments.\par
Bless you, Beth! What a refreshing spectacle you are, Jo. Amy, you are\par
getting altogether too handsome for a single lady."\par
\par
As Laurie spoke, he delivered a brown paper parcel to Meg, pulled Beth's\par
hair-ribbon, stared at Jo's big pinafore, and fell into an attitude of\par
mock rapture before Amy, then shook hands all round, and every one began\par
to talk.\par
\par
"Where is John?" asked Meg anxiously.\par
\par
"Stopped to get the license for to-morrow, ma'am."\par
\par
"Which side won the last match, Teddy?" inquired Jo, who persisted in\par
feeling an interest in manly sports, despite her nineteen years.\par
\par
"Ours, of course. Wish you'd been there to see."\par
\par
"How is the lovely Miss Randal?" asked Amy, with a significant smile.\par
\par
"More cruel than ever; don't you see how I'm pining away?" and Laurie\par
gave his broad chest a sounding slap and heaved a melodramatic sigh.\par
\par
"What's the last joke? Undo the bundle and see, Meg," said Beth, eying\par
the knobby parcel with curiosity.\par
\par
"It's a useful thing to have in the house in case of fire or thieves,"\par
observed Laurie, as a watchman's rattle appeared, amid the laughter of\par
the girls.\par
\par
               [Illustration: A small watchman's rattle]\par
\par
"Any time when John is away, and you get frightened, Mrs. Meg, just\par
swing that out of the front window, and it will rouse the neighborhood\par
in a jiffy. Nice thing, isn't it?" and Laurie gave them a sample of its\par
powers that made them cover up their ears.\par
\par
"There's gratitude for you! and speaking of gratitude reminds me to\par
mention that you may thank Hannah for saving your wedding-cake from\par
destruction. I saw it going into your house as I came by, and if she\par
hadn't defended it manfully I'd have had a pick at it, for it looked\par
like a remarkably plummy one."\par
\par
"I wonder if you will ever grow up, Laurie," said Meg, in a matronly\par
tone.\par
\par
"I'm doing my best, ma'am, but can't get much higher, I'm afraid, as six\par
feet is about all men can do in these degenerate days," responded the\par
young gentleman, whose head was about level with the little chandelier.\par
"I suppose it would be profanation to eat anything in this spick and\par
span new bower, so, as I'm tremendously hungry, I propose an\par
adjournment," he added presently.\par
\par
"Mother and I are going to wait for John. There are some last things to\par
settle," said Meg, bustling away.\par
\par
"Beth and I are going over to Kitty Bryant's to get more flowers for\par
to-morrow," added Amy, tying a picturesque hat over her picturesque\par
curls, and enjoying the effect as much as anybody.\par
\par
"Come, Jo, don't desert a fellow. I'm in such a state of exhaustion I\par
can't get home without help. Don't take off your apron, whatever you do;\par
it's peculiarly becoming," said Laurie, as Jo bestowed his especial\par
aversion in her capacious pocket, and offered him her arm to support his\par
feeble steps.\par
\par
"Now, Teddy, I want to talk seriously to you about to-morrow," began Jo,\par
as they strolled away together. "You _must_ promise to behave well, and\par
not cut up any pranks, and spoil our plans."\par
\par
"Not a prank."\par
\par
"And don't say funny things when we ought to be sober."\par
\par
"I never do; you are the one for that."\par
\par
"And I implore you not to look at me during the ceremony; I shall\par
certainly laugh if you do."\par
\par
"You won't see me; you'll be crying so hard that the thick fog round you\par
will obscure the prospect."\par
\par
"I never cry unless for some great affliction."\par
\par
"Such as fellows going to college, hey?" cut in Laurie, with a\par
suggestive laugh.\par
\par
"Don't be a peacock. I only moaned a trifle to keep the girls company."\par
\par
"Exactly. I say, Jo, how is grandpa this week; pretty amiable?"\par
\par
"Very; why, have you got into a scrape, and want to know how he'll take\par
it?" asked Jo rather sharply.\par
\par
"Now, Jo, do you think I'd look your mother in the face, and say 'All\par
right,' if it wasn't?" and Laurie stopped short, with an injured air.\par
\par
"No, I don't."\par
\par
"Then don't go and be suspicious; I only want some money," said Laurie,\par
walking on again, appeased by her hearty tone.\par
\par
"You spend a great deal, Teddy."\par
\par
"Bless you, _I_ don't spend it; it spends itself, somehow, and is gone\par
before I know it."\par
\par
"You are so generous and kind-hearted that you let people borrow, and\par
can't say 'No' to any one. We heard about Henshaw, and all you did for\par
him. If you always spent money in that way, no one would blame you,"\par
said Jo warmly.\par
\par
"Oh, he made a mountain out of a mole-hill. You wouldn't have me let\par
that fine fellow work himself to death, just for the want of a little\par
help, when he is worth a dozen of us lazy chaps, would you?"\par
\par
"Of course not; but I don't see the use of your having seventeen\par
waistcoats, endless neckties, and a new hat every time you come home. I\par
thought you'd got over the dandy period; but every now and then it\par
breaks out in a new spot. Just now it's the fashion to be hideous,--to\par
make your head look like a scrubbing-brush, wear a strait-jacket,\par
orange gloves, and clumping, square-toed boots. If it was cheap\par
ugliness, I'd say nothing; but it costs as much as the other, and I\par
don't get any satisfaction out of it."\par
\par
Laurie threw back his head, and laughed so heartily at this attack, that\par
the felt-basin fell off, and Jo walked on it, which insult only afforded\par
him an opportunity for expatiating on the advantages of a\par
rough-and-ready costume, as he folded up the maltreated hat, and stuffed\par
it into his pocket.\par
\par
"Don't lecture any more, there's a good soul! I have enough all through\par
the week, and like to enjoy myself when I come home. I'll get myself up\par
regardless of expense, to-morrow, and be a satisfaction to my friends."\par
\par
"I'll leave you in peace if you'll _only_ let your hair grow. I'm not\par
aristocratic, but I do object to being seen with a person who looks like\par
a young prize-fighter," observed Jo severely.\par
\par
"This unassuming style promotes study; that's why we adopt it," returned\par
Laurie, who certainly could not be accused of vanity, having voluntarily\par
sacrificed a handsome curly crop to the demand for\par
quarter-of-an-inch-long stubble.\par
\par
"By the way, Jo, I think that little Parker is really getting desperate\par
about Amy. He talks of her constantly, writes poetry, and moons about in\par
a most suspicious manner. He'd better nip his little passion in the bud,\par
hadn't he?" added Laurie, in a confidential, elder-brotherly tone, after\par
a minute's silence.\par
\par
"Of course he had; we don't want any more marrying in this family for\par
years to come. Mercy on us, what _are_ the children thinking of?" and Jo\par
looked as much scandalized as if Amy and little Parker were not yet in\par
their teens.\par
\par
"It's a fast age, and I don't know what we are coming to, ma'am. You are\par
a mere infant, but you'll go next, Jo, and we'll be left lamenting,"\par
said Laurie, shaking his head over the degeneracy of the times.\par
\par
"Don't be alarmed; I'm not one of the agreeable sort. Nobody will want\par
me, and it's a mercy, for there should always be one old maid in a\par
family."\par
\par
"You won't give any one a chance," said Laurie, with a sidelong glance,\par
and a little more color than before in his sunburnt face. "You won't\par
show the soft side of your character; and if a fellow gets a peep at it\par
by accident, and can't help showing that he likes it, you treat him as\par
Mrs. Gummidge did her sweetheart,--throw cold water over him,--and get\par
so thorny no one dares touch or look at you."\par
\par
"I don't like that sort of thing; I'm too busy to be worried with\par
nonsense, and I think it's dreadful to break up families so. Now don't\par
say any more about it; Meg's wedding has turned all our heads, and we\par
talk of nothing but lovers and such absurdities. I don't wish to get\par
cross, so let's change the subject;" and Jo looked quite ready to fling\par
cold water on the slightest provocation.\par
\par
Whatever his feelings might have been, Laurie found a vent for them in a\par
long low whistle, and the fearful prediction, as they parted at the\par
gate, "Mark my words, Jo, you'll go next."\par
\par
