\par
                   [Illustration: The First Wedding]\par
\par
                                  XXV.\par
\par
                           THE FIRST WEDDING.\par
\par
\par
The June roses over the porch were awake bright and early on that\par
morning, rejoicing with all their hearts in the cloudless sunshine, like\par
friendly little neighbors, as they were. Quite flushed with excitement\par
were their ruddy faces, as they swung in the wind, whispering to one\par
another what they had seen; for some peeped in at the dining-room\par
windows, where the feast was spread, some climbed up to nod and smile at\par
the sisters as they dressed the bride, others waved a welcome to those\par
who came and went on various errands in garden, porch, and hall, and\par
all, from the rosiest full-blown flower to the palest baby-bud, offered\par
their tribute of beauty and fragrance to the gentle mistress who had\par
loved and tended them so long.\par
\par
Meg looked very like a rose herself; for all that was best and sweetest\par
in heart and soul seemed to bloom into her face that day, making it fair\par
and tender, with a charm more beautiful than beauty. Neither silk, lace,\par
nor orange-flowers would she have. "I don't want to look strange or\par
fixed up to-day," she said. "I don't want a fashionable wedding, but\par
only those about me whom I love, and to them I wish to look and be my\par
familiar self."\par
\par
So she made her wedding gown herself, sewing into it the tender hopes\par
and innocent romances of a girlish heart. Her sisters braided up her\par
pretty hair, and the only ornaments she wore were the lilies of the\par
valley, which "her John" liked best of all the flowers that grew.\par
\par
"You _do_ look just like our own dear Meg, only so very sweet and lovely\par
that I should hug you if it wouldn't crumple your dress," cried Amy,\par
surveying her with delight, when all was done.\par
\par
"Then I am satisfied. But please hug and kiss me, every one, and don't\par
mind my dress; I want a great many crumples of this sort put into it\par
to-day;" and Meg opened her arms to her sisters, who clung about her\par
with April faces for a minute, feeling that the new love had not changed\par
the old.\par
\par
"Now I'm going to tie John's cravat for him, and then to stay a few\par
minutes with father quietly in the study;" and Meg ran down to perform\par
these little ceremonies, and then to follow her mother wherever she\par
went, conscious that, in spite of the smiles on the motherly face, there\par
was a secret sorrow hid in the motherly heart at the flight of the first\par
bird from the nest.\par
\par
As the younger girls stand together, giving the last touches to their\par
simple toilet, it may be a good time to tell of a few changes which\par
three years have wrought in their appearance; for all are looking their\par
best just now.\par
\par
Jo's angles are much softened; she has learned to carry herself with\par
ease, if not grace. The curly crop has lengthened into a thick coil,\par
more becoming to the small head atop of the tall figure. There is a\par
fresh color in her brown cheeks, a soft shine in her eyes, and only\par
gentle words fall from her sharp tongue to-day.\par
\par
Beth has grown slender, pale, and more quiet than ever; the beautiful,\par
kind eyes are larger, and in them lies an expression that saddens one,\par
although it is not sad itself. It is the shadow of pain which touches\par
the young face with such pathetic patience; but Beth seldom complains,\par
and always speaks hopefully of "being better soon."\par
\par
Amy is with truth considered "the flower of the family;" for at sixteen\par
she has the air and bearing of a full-grown woman--not beautiful, but\par
possessed of that indescribable charm called grace. One saw it in the\par
lines of her figure, the make and motion of her hands, the flow of her\par
dress, the droop of her hair,--unconscious, yet harmonious, and as\par
attractive to many as beauty itself. Amy's nose still afflicted her, for\par
it never _would_ grow Grecian; so did her mouth, being too wide, and\par
having a decided chin. These offending features gave character to her\par
whole face, but she never could see it, and consoled herself with her\par
wonderfully fair complexion, keen blue eyes, and curls, more golden and\par
abundant than ever.\par
\par
All three wore suits of thin silver gray (their best gowns for the\par
summer), with blush-roses in hair and bosom; and all three looked just\par
what they were,--fresh-faced, happy-hearted girls, pausing a moment in\par
their busy lives to read with wistful eyes the sweetest chapter in the\par
romance of womanhood.\par
\par
There were to be no ceremonious performances, everything was to be as\par
natural and homelike as possible; so when Aunt March arrived, she was\par
scandalized to see the bride come running to welcome and lead her in, to\par
find the bridegroom fastening up a garland that had fallen down, and to\par
catch a glimpse of the paternal minister marching upstairs with a grave\par
countenance, and a wine-bottle under each arm.\par
\par
"Upon my word, here's a state of things!" cried the old lady, taking the\par
seat of honor prepared for her, and settling the folds of her lavender\par
_moire_ with a great rustle. "You oughtn't to be seen till the last\par
minute, child."\par
\par
"I'm not a show, aunty, and no one is coming to stare at me, to\par
criticise my dress, or count the cost of my luncheon. I'm too happy to\par
care what any one says or thinks, and I'm going to have my little\par
wedding just as I like it. John, dear, here's your hammer;" and away\par
went Meg to help "that man" in his highly improper employment.\par
\par
Mr. Brooke didn't even say "Thank you," but as he stooped for the\par
unromantic tool, he kissed his little bride behind the folding-door,\par
with a look that made Aunt March whisk out her pocket-handkerchief, with\par
a sudden dew in her sharp old eyes.\par
\par
A crash, a cry, and a laugh from Laurie, accompanied by the indecorous\par
exclamation, "Jupiter Ammon! Jo's upset the cake again!" caused a\par
momentary flurry, which was hardly over when a flock of cousins arrived,\par
and "the party came in," as Beth used to say when a child.\par
\par
"Don't let that young giant come near me; he worries me worse than\par
mosquitoes," whispered the old lady to Amy, as the rooms filled, and\par
Laurie's black head towered above the rest.\par
\par
"He has promised to be very good to-day, and he _can_ be perfectly\par
elegant if he likes," returned Amy, gliding away to warn Hercules to\par
beware of the dragon, which warning caused him to haunt the old lady\par
with a devotion that nearly distracted her.\par
\par
There was no bridal procession, but a sudden silence fell upon the room\par
as Mr. March and the young pair took their places under the green arch.\par
Mother and sisters gathered close, as if loath to give Meg up; the\par
fatherly voice broke more than once, which only seemed to make the\par
service more beautiful and solemn; the bridegroom's hand trembled\par
visibly, and no one heard his replies; but Meg looked straight up in her\par
husband's eyes, and said, "I will!" with such tender trust in her own\par
face and voice that her mother's heart rejoiced, and Aunt March sniffed\par
audibly.\par
\par
Jo did _not_ cry, though she was very near it once, and was only saved\par
from a demonstration by the consciousness that Laurie was staring\par
fixedly at her, with a comical mixture of merriment and emotion in his\par
wicked black eyes. Beth kept her face hidden on her mother's shoulder,\par
but Amy stood like a graceful statue, with a most becoming ray of\par
sunshine touching her white forehead and the flower in her hair.\par
\par
It wasn't at all the thing, I'm afraid, but the minute she was fairly\par
married, Meg cried, "The first kiss for Marmee!" and, turning, gave it\par
with her heart on her lips. During the next fifteen minutes she looked\par
more like a rose than ever, for every one availed themselves of their\par
privileges to the fullest extent, from Mr. Laurence to old Hannah, who,\par
adorned with a head-dress fearfully and wonderfully made, fell upon her\par
in the hall, crying, with a sob and a chuckle, "Bless you, deary, a\par
hundred times! The cake ain't hurt a mite, and everything looks lovely."\par
\par
Everybody cleared up after that, and said something brilliant, or tried\par
to, which did just as well, for laughter is ready when hearts are light.\par
There was no display of gifts, for they were already in the little\par
house, nor was there an elaborate breakfast, but a plentiful lunch of\par
cake and fruit, dressed with flowers. Mr. Laurence and Aunt March\par
shrugged and smiled at one another when water, lemonade, and coffee were\par
found to be the only sorts of nectar which the three Hebes carried\par
round. No one said anything, however, till Laurie, who insisted on\par
serving the bride, appeared before her, with a loaded salver in his hand\par
and a puzzled expression on his face.\par
\par
"Has Jo smashed all the bottles by accident?" he whispered, "or am I\par
merely laboring under a delusion that I saw some lying about loose this\par
morning?"\par
\par
"No; your grandfather kindly offered us his best, and Aunt March\par
actually sent some, but father put away a little for Beth, and\par
despatched the rest to the Soldiers' Home. You know he thinks that wine\par
should be used only in illness, and mother says that neither she nor her\par
daughters will ever offer it to any young man under her roof."\par
\par
Meg spoke seriously, and expected to see Laurie frown or laugh; but he\par
did neither, for after a quick look at her, he said, in his impetuous\par
way, "I like that! for I've seen enough harm done to wish other women\par
would think as you do."\par
\par
"You are not made wise by experience, I hope?" and there was an anxious\par
accent in Meg's voice.\par
\par
"No; I give you my word for it. Don't think too well of me, either; this\par
is not one of my temptations. Being brought up where wine is as common\par
as water, and almost as harmless, I don't care for it; but when a pretty\par
girl offers it, one doesn't like to refuse, you see."\par
\par
"But you will, for the sake of others, if not for your own. Come,\par
Laurie, promise, and give me one more reason to call this the happiest\par
day of my life."\par
\par
A demand so sudden and so serious made the young man hesitate a moment,\par
for ridicule is often harder to bear than self-denial. Meg knew that if\par
he gave the promise he would keep it at all costs; and, feeling her\par
power, used it as a woman may for her friend's good. She did not speak,\par
but she looked up at him with a face made very eloquent by happiness,\par
and a smile which said, "No one can refuse me anything to-day." Laurie\par
certainly could not; and, with an answering smile, he gave her his hand,\par
saying heartily, "I promise, Mrs. Brooke!"\par
\par
"I thank you, very, very much."\par
\par
"And I drink 'long life to your resolution,' Teddy," cried Jo, baptizing\par
him with a splash of lemonade, as she waved her glass, and beamed\par
approvingly upon him.\par
\par
So the toast was drunk, the pledge made, and loyally kept, in spite of\par
many temptations; for, with instinctive wisdom, the girls had seized a\par
happy moment to do their friend a service, for which he thanked them all\par
his life.\par
\par
After lunch, people strolled about, by twos and threes, through house\par
and garden, enjoying the sunshine without and within. Meg and John\par
happened to be standing together in the middle of the grass-plot, when\par
Laurie was seized with an inspiration which put the finishing touch to\par
this unfashionable wedding.\par
\par
"All the married people take hands and dance round the new-made husband\par
and wife, as the Germans do, while we bachelors and spinsters prance in\par
couples outside!" cried Laurie, promenading down the path with Amy, with\par
such infectious spirit and skill that every one else followed their\par
example without a murmur. Mr. and Mrs. March, Aunt and Uncle Carrol,\par
began it; others rapidly joined in; even Sallie Moffat, after a moment's\par
hesitation, threw her train over her arm, and whisked Ned into the ring.\par
But the crowning joke was Mr. Laurence and Aunt March; for when the\par
stately old gentleman _chasséed_ solemnly up to the old lady, she just\par
tucked her cane under her arm, and hopped briskly away to join hands\par
with the rest, and dance about the bridal pair, while the young folks\par
pervaded the garden, like butterflies on a midsummer day.\par
\par
Want of breath brought the impromptu ball to a close, and then people\par
began to go.\par
\par
"I wish you well, my dear, I heartily wish you well; but I think you'll\par
be sorry for it," said Aunt March to Meg, adding to the bridegroom, as\par
he led her to the carriage, "You've got a treasure, young man, see that\par
you deserve it."\par
\par
"That is the prettiest wedding I've been to for an age, Ned, and I don't\par
see why, for there wasn't a bit of style about it," observed Mrs. Moffat\par
to her husband, as they drove away.\par
\par
"Laurie, my lad, if you ever want to indulge in this sort of thing, get\par
one of those little girls to help you, and I shall be perfectly\par
satisfied," said Mr. Laurence, settling himself in his easy-chair to\par
rest, after the excitement of the morning.\par
\par
"I'll do my best to gratify you, sir," was Laurie's unusually dutiful\par
reply, as he carefully unpinned the posy Jo had put in his button-hole.\par
\par
The little house was not far away, and the only bridal journey Meg had\par
was the quiet walk with John, from the old home to the new. When she\par
came down, looking like a pretty Quakeress in her dove-colored suit and\par
straw bonnet tied with white, they all gathered about her to say\par
"good-by," as tenderly as if she had been going to make the grand tour.\par
\par
"Don't feel that I am separated from you, Marmee dear, or that I love\par
you any the less for loving John so much," she said, clinging to her\par
mother, with full eyes, for a moment. "I shall come every day, father,\par
and expect to keep my old place in all your hearts, though I _am_\par
married. Beth is going to be with me a great deal, and the other girls\par
will drop in now and then to laugh at my housekeeping struggles. Thank\par
you all for my happy wedding-day. Good-by, good-by!"\par
\par
They stood watching her, with faces full of love and hope and tender\par
pride, as she walked away, leaning on her husband's arm, with her hands\par
full of flowers, and the June sunshine brightening her happy face,--and\par
so Meg's married life began.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                   [Illustration: Artistic Attempts]\par
\par
                                 XXVI.\par
\par
                           ARTISTIC ATTEMPTS.\par
\par
\par
It takes people a long time to learn the difference between talent and\par
genius, especially ambitious young men and women. Amy was learning this\par
distinction through much tribulation; for, mistaking enthusiasm for\par
inspiration, she attempted every branch of art with youthful audacity.\par
For a long time there was a lull in the "mud-pie" business, and she\par
devoted herself to the finest pen-and-ink drawing, in which she showed\par
such taste and skill that her graceful handiwork proved both pleasant\par
and profitable. But overstrained eyes soon caused pen and ink to be laid\par
aside for a bold attempt at poker-sketching. While this attack lasted,\par
the family lived in constant fear of a conflagration; for the odor of\par
burning wood pervaded the house at all hours; smoke issued from attic\par
and shed with alarming frequency, red-hot pokers lay about\par
promiscuously, and Hannah never went to bed without a pail of water and\par
the dinner-bell at her door, in case of fire. Raphael's face was found\par
boldly executed on the under side of the moulding-board, and Bacchus on\par
the head of a beer-barrel; a chanting cherub adorned the cover of the\par
sugar-bucket, and attempts to portray Romeo and Juliet supplied\par
kindlings for some time.\par
\par
From fire to oil was a natural transition for burnt fingers, and Amy\par
fell to painting with undiminished ardor. An artist friend fitted her\par
out with his cast-off palettes, brushes, and colors; and she daubed\par
away, producing pastoral and marine views such as were never seen on\par
land or sea. Her monstrosities in the way of cattle would have taken\par
prizes at an agricultural fair; and the perilous pitching of her vessels\par
would have produced sea-sickness in the most nautical observer, if the\par
utter disregard to all known rules of shipbuilding and rigging had not\par
convulsed him with laughter at the first glance. Swarthy boys and\par
dark-eyed Madonnas, staring at you from one corner of the studio,\par
suggested Murillo; oily-brown shadows of faces, with a lurid streak in\par
the wrong place, meant Rembrandt; buxom ladies and dropsical infants,\par
Rubens; and Turner appeared in tempests of blue thunder, orange\par
lightning, brown rain, and purple clouds, with a tomato-colored splash\par
in the middle, which might be the sun or a buoy, a sailor's shirt or a\par
king's robe, as the spectator pleased.\par
\par
Charcoal portraits came next; and the entire family hung in a row,\par
looking as wild and crocky as if just evoked from a coal-bin. Softened\par
into crayon sketches, they did better; for the likenesses were good, and\par
Amy's hair, Jo's nose, Meg's mouth, and Laurie's eyes were pronounced\par
"wonderfully fine." A return to clay and plaster followed, and ghostly\par
casts of her acquaintances haunted corners of the house, or tumbled off\par
closet-shelves on to people's heads. Children were enticed in as models,\par
till their incoherent accounts of her mysterious doings caused Miss Amy\par
to be regarded in the light of a young ogress. Her efforts in this line,\par
however, were brought to an abrupt close by an untoward accident, which\par
quenched her ardor. Other models failing her for a time, she undertook\par
to cast her own pretty foot, and the family were one day alarmed by an\par
unearthly bumping and screaming, and running to the rescue, found the\par
young enthusiast hopping wildly about the shed, with her foot held fast\par
in a pan-full of plaster, which had hardened with unexpected rapidity.\par
With much difficulty and some danger she was dug out; for Jo was so\par
overcome with laughter while she excavated, that her knife went too far,\par
cut the poor foot, and left a lasting memorial of one artistic attempt,\par
at least.\par
\par
       [Illustration: Her foot held fast in a panful of plaster]\par
\par
After this Amy subsided, till a mania for sketching from nature set her\par
to haunting river, field, and wood, for picturesque studies, and sighing\par
for ruins to copy. She caught endless colds sitting on damp grass to\par
book "a delicious bit," composed of a stone, a stump, one mushroom, and\par
a broken mullein-stalk, or "a heavenly mass of clouds," that looked like\par
a choice display of feather-beds when done. She sacrificed her\par
complexion floating on the river in the midsummer sun, to study light\par
and shade, and got a wrinkle over her nose, trying after "points of\par
sight," or whatever the squint-and-string performance is called.\par
\par
If "genius is eternal patience," as Michael Angelo affirms, Amy\par
certainly had some claim to the divine attribute, for she persevered in\par
spite of all obstacles, failures, and discouragements, firmly believing\par
that in time she should do something worthy to be called "high art."\par
\par
She was learning, doing, and enjoying other things, meanwhile, for she\par
had resolved to be an attractive and accomplished woman, even if she\par
never became a great artist. Here she succeeded better; for she was one\par
of those happily created beings who please without effort, make friends\par
everywhere, and take life so gracefully and easily that less fortunate\par
souls are tempted to believe that such are born under a lucky star.\par
Everybody liked her, for among her good gifts was tact. She had an\par
instinctive sense of what was pleasing and proper, always said the right\par
thing to the right person, did just what suited the time and place, and\par
was so self-possessed that her sisters used to say, "If Amy went to\par
court without any rehearsal beforehand, she'd know exactly what to do."\par
\par
One of her weaknesses was a desire to move in "our best society,"\par
without being quite sure what the _best_ really was. Money, position,\par
fashionable accomplishments, and elegant manners were most desirable\par
things in her eyes, and she liked to associate with those who possessed\par
them, often mistaking the false for the true, and admiring what was not\par
admirable. Never forgetting that by birth she was a gentlewoman, she\par
cultivated her aristocratic tastes and feelings, so that when the\par
opportunity came she might be ready to take the place from which poverty\par
now excluded her.\par
\par
"My lady," as her friends called her, sincerely desired to be a genuine\par
lady, and was so at heart, but had yet to learn that money cannot buy\par
refinement of nature, that rank does not always confer nobility, and\par
that true breeding makes itself felt in spite of external drawbacks.\par
\par
"I want to ask a favor of you, mamma," Amy said, coming in, with an\par
important air, one day.\par
\par
"Well, little girl, what is it?" replied her mother, in whose eyes the\par
stately young lady still remained "the baby."\par
\par
"Our drawing class breaks up next week, and before the girls separate\par
for the summer, I want to ask them out here for a day. They are wild to\par
see the river, sketch the broken bridge, and copy some of the things\par
they admire in my book. They have been very kind to me in many ways, and\par
I am grateful, for they are all rich, and know I am poor, yet they never\par
made any difference."\par
\par
"Why should they?" and Mrs. March put the question with what the girls\par
called her "Maria Theresa air."\par
\par
"You know as well as I that it _does_ make a difference with nearly\par
every one, so don't ruffle up, like a dear, motherly hen, when your\par
chickens get pecked by smarter birds; the ugly duckling turned out a\par
swan, you know;" and Amy smiled without bitterness, for she possessed a\par
happy temper and hopeful spirit.\par
\par
Mrs. March laughed, and smoothed down her maternal pride as she asked,--\par
\par
"Well, my swan, what is your plan?"\par
\par
"I should like to ask the girls out to lunch next week, to take them a\par
drive to the places they want to see, a row on the river, perhaps, and\par
make a little artistic _fête_ for them."\par
\par
"That looks feasible. What do you want for lunch? Cake, sandwiches,\par
fruit, and coffee will be all that is necessary, I suppose?"\par
\par
"Oh dear, no! we must have cold tongue and chicken, French chocolate and\par
ice-cream, besides. The girls are used to such things, and I want my\par
lunch to be proper and elegant, though I _do_ work for my living."\par
\par
"How many young ladies are there?" asked her mother, beginning to look\par
sober.\par
\par
"Twelve or fourteen in the class, but I dare say they won't all come."\par
\par
"Bless me, child, you will have to charter an omnibus to carry them\par
about."\par
\par
"Why, mother, how _can_ you think of such a thing? Not more than six or\par
eight will probably come, so I shall hire a beach-wagon, and borrow Mr.\par
Laurence's cherry-bounce." (Hannah's pronunciation of _char-à-banc_.)\par
\par
"All this will be expensive, Amy."\par
\par
"Not very; I've calculated the cost, and I'll pay for it myself."\par
\par
"Don't you think, dear, that as these girls are used to such things, and\par
the best we can do will be nothing new, that some simpler plan would be\par
pleasanter to them, as a change, if nothing more, and much better for us\par
than buying or borrowing what we don't need, and attempting a style not\par
in keeping with our circumstances?"\par
\par
"If I can't have it as I like, I don't care to have it at all. I know\par
that I can carry it out perfectly well, if you and the girls will help\par
a little; and I don't see why I can't if I'm willing to pay for it,"\par
said Amy, with the decision which opposition was apt to change into\par
obstinacy.\par
\par
Mrs. March knew that experience was an excellent teacher, and when it\par
was possible she left her children to learn alone the lessons which she\par
would gladly have made easier, if they had not objected to taking advice\par
as much as they did salts and senna.\par
\par
"Very well, Amy; if your heart is set upon it, and you see your way\par
through without too great an outlay of money, time, and temper, I'll say\par
no more. Talk it over with the girls, and whichever way you decide, I'll\par
do my best to help you."\par
\par
"Thanks, mother; you are always _so_ kind;" and away went Amy to lay her\par
plan before her sisters.\par
\par
Meg agreed at once, and promised her aid, gladly offering anything she\par
possessed, from her little house itself to her very best salt-spoons.\par
But Jo frowned upon the whole project, and would have nothing to do with\par
it at first.\par
\par
"Why in the world should you spend your money, worry your family, and\par
turn the house upside down for a parcel of girls who don't care a\par
sixpence for you? I thought you had too much pride and sense to truckle\par
to any mortal woman just because she wears French boots and rides in a\par
_coupé_," said Jo, who, being called from the tragical climax of her\par
novel, was not in the best mood for social enterprises.\par
\par
"I _don't_ truckle, and I hate being patronized as much as you do!"\par
returned Amy indignantly, for the two still jangled when such questions\par
arose. "The girls do care for me, and I for them, and there's a great\par
deal of kindness and sense and talent among them, in spite of what you\par
call fashionable nonsense. You don't care to make people like you, to go\par
into good society, and cultivate your manners and tastes. I do, and I\par
mean to make the most of every chance that comes. _You_ can go through\par
the world with your elbows out and your nose in the air, and call it\par
independence, if you like. That's not my way."\par
\par
When Amy whetted her tongue and freed her mind she usually got the best\par
of it, for she seldom failed to have common sense on her side, while Jo\par
carried her love of liberty and hate of conventionalities to such an\par
unlimited extent that she naturally found herself worsted in an\par
argument. Amy's definition of Jo's idea of independence was such a good\par
hit that both burst out laughing, and the discussion took a more amiable\par
turn. Much against her will, Jo at length consented to sacrifice a day\par
to Mrs. Grundy, and help her sister through what she regarded as "a\par
nonsensical business."\par
\par
The invitations were sent, nearly all accepted, and the following Monday\par
was set apart for the grand event. Hannah was out of humor because her\par
week's work was deranged, and prophesied that "ef the washin' and\par
ironin' warn't done reg'lar nothin' would go well anywheres." This hitch\par
in the mainspring of the domestic machinery had a bad effect upon the\par
whole concern; but Amy's motto was "Nil desperandum," and having made up\par
her mind what to do, she proceeded to do it in spite of all obstacles.\par
To begin with, Hannah's cooking didn't turn out well: the chicken was\par
tough, the tongue too salt, and the chocolate wouldn't froth properly.\par
Then the cake and ice cost more than Amy expected, so did the wagon; and\par
various other expenses, which seemed trifling at the outset, counted up\par
rather alarmingly afterward. Beth got cold and took to her bed, Meg had\par
an unusual number of callers to keep her at home, and Jo was in such a\par
divided state of mind that her breakages, accidents, and mistakes were\par
uncommonly numerous, serious, and trying.\par
\par
"If it hadn't been for mother I never should have got through," as Amy\par
declared afterward, and gratefully remembered when "the best joke of the\par
season" was entirely forgotten by everybody else.\par
\par
If it was not fair on Monday, the young ladies were to come on\par
Tuesday,--an arrangement which aggravated Jo and Hannah to the last\par
degree. On Monday morning the weather was in that undecided state which\par
is more exasperating than a steady pour. It drizzled a little, shone a\par
little, blew a little, and didn't make up its mind till it was too late\par
for any one else to make up theirs. Amy was up at dawn, hustling people\par
out of their beds and through their breakfasts, that the house might be\par
got in order. The parlor struck her as looking uncommonly shabby; but\par
without stopping to sigh for what she had not, she skilfully made the\par
best of what she had, arranging chairs over the worn places in the\par
carpet, covering stains on the walls with pictures framed in ivy, and\par
filling up empty corners with home-made statuary, which gave an artistic\par
air to the room, as did the lovely vases of flowers Jo scattered about.\par
\par
The lunch looked charmingly; and as she surveyed it, she sincerely hoped\par
it would taste well, and that the borrowed glass, china, and silver\par
would get safely home again. The carriages were promised, Meg and mother\par
were all ready to do the honors, Beth was able to help Hannah behind the\par
scenes, Jo had engaged to be as lively and amiable as an absent mind, an\par
aching head, and a very decided disapproval of everybody and everything\par
would allow, and, as she wearily dressed, Amy cheered herself with\par
anticipations of the happy moment, when, lunch safely over, she should\par
drive away with her friends for an afternoon of artistic delights; for\par
the "cherry-bounce" and the broken bridge were her strong points.\par
\par
Then came two hours of suspense, during which she vibrated from parlor\par
to porch, while public opinion varied like the weathercock. A smart\par
shower at eleven had evidently quenched the enthusiasm of the young\par
ladies who were to arrive at twelve, for nobody came; and at two the\par
exhausted family sat down in a blaze of sunshine to consume the\par
perishable portions of the feast, that nothing might be lost.\par
\par
"No doubt about the weather to-day; they will certainly come, so we must\par
fly round and be ready for them," said Amy, as the sun woke her next\par
morning. She spoke briskly, but in her secret soul she wished she had\par
said nothing about Tuesday, for her interest, like her cake, was getting\par
a little stale.\par
\par
"I can't get any lobsters, so you will have to do without salad to-day,"\par
said Mr. March, coming in half an hour later, with an expression of\par
placid despair.\par
\par
"Use the chicken, then; the toughness won't matter in a salad," advised\par
his wife.\par
\par
"Hannah left it on the kitchen-table a minute, and the kittens got at\par
it. I'm very sorry, Amy," added Beth, who was still a patroness of cats.\par
\par
"Then I _must_ have a lobster, for tongue alone won't do," said Amy\par
decidedly.\par
\par
"Shall I rush into town and demand one?" asked Jo, with the magnanimity\par
of a martyr.\par
\par
"You'd come bringing it home under your arm, without any paper, just to\par
try me. I'll go myself," answered Amy, whose temper was beginning to\par
fail.\par
\par
Shrouded in a thick veil and armed with a genteel travelling-basket, she\par
departed, feeling that a cool drive would soothe her ruffled spirit, and\par
fit her for the labors of the day. After some delay, the object of her\par
desire was procured, likewise a bottle of dressing, to prevent further\par
loss of time at home, and off she drove again, well pleased with her own\par
forethought.\par
\par
As the omnibus contained only one other passenger, a sleepy old lady,\par
Amy pocketed her veil, and beguiled the tedium of the way by trying to\par
find out where all her money had gone to. So busy was she with her card\par
full of refractory figures that she did not observe a new-comer, who\par
entered without stopping the vehicle, till a masculine voice said,\par
"Good-morning, Miss March," and, looking up, she beheld one of Laurie's\par
most elegant college friends. Fervently hoping that he would get out\par
before she did, Amy utterly ignored the basket at her feet, and,\par
congratulating herself that she had on her new travelling dress,\par
returned the young man's greeting with her usual suavity and spirit.\par
\par
They got on excellently; for Amy's chief care was soon set at rest by\par
learning that the gentleman would leave first, and she was chatting away\par
in a peculiarly lofty strain, when the old lady got out. In stumbling to\par
the door, she upset the basket, and--oh, horror!--the lobster, in all\par
its vulgar size and brilliancy, was revealed to the highborn eyes of a\par
Tudor.\par
\par
"By Jove, she's forgotten her dinner!" cried the unconscious youth,\par
poking the scarlet monster into its place with his cane, and preparing\par
to hand out the basket after the old lady.\par
\par
"Please don't--it's--it's mine," murmured Amy, with a face nearly as red\par
as her fish.\par
\par
                [Illustration: Please don't, it's mine]\par
\par
"Oh, really, I beg pardon; it's an uncommonly fine one, isn't it?" said\par
Tudor, with great presence of mind, and an air of sober interest that\par
did credit to his breeding.\par
\par
Amy recovered herself in a breath, set her basket boldly on the seat,\par
and said, laughing,--\par
\par
"Don't you wish you were to have some of the salad he's to make, and to\par
see the charming young ladies who are to eat it?"\par
\par
Now that was tact, for two of the ruling foibles of the masculine mind\par
were touched: the lobster was instantly surrounded by a halo of pleasing\par
reminiscences, and curiosity about "the charming young ladies" diverted\par
his mind from the comical mishap.\par
\par
"I suppose he'll laugh and joke over it with Laurie, but I sha'n't see\par
them; that's a comfort," thought Amy, as Tudor bowed and departed.\par
\par
She did not mention this meeting at home (though she discovered that,\par
thanks to the upset, her new dress was much damaged by the rivulets of\par
dressing that meandered down the skirt), but went through with the\par
preparations which now seemed more irksome than before; and at twelve\par
o'clock all was ready again. Feeling that the neighbors were interested\par
in her movements, she wished to efface the memory of yesterday's\par
failure by a grand success to-day; so she ordered the "cherry-bounce,"\par
and drove away in state to meet and escort her guests to the banquet.\par
\par
"There's the rumble, they're coming! I'll go into the porch to meet\par
them; it looks hospitable, and I want the poor child to have a good time\par
after all her trouble," said Mrs. March, suiting the action to the word.\par
But after one glance, she retired, with an indescribable expression,\par
for, looking quite lost in the big carriage, sat Amy and one young lady.\par
\par
"Run, Beth, and help Hannah clear half the things off the table; it will\par
be too absurd to put a luncheon for twelve before a single girl," cried\par
Jo, hurrying away to the lower regions, too excited to stop even for a\par
laugh.\par
\par
In came Amy, quite calm, and delightfully cordial to the one guest who\par
had kept her promise; the rest of the family, being of a dramatic turn,\par
played their parts equally well, and Miss Eliott found them a most\par
hilarious set; for it was impossible to entirely control the merriment\par
which possessed them. The remodelled lunch being gayly partaken of, the\par
studio and garden visited, and art discussed with enthusiasm, Amy\par
ordered a buggy (alas for the elegant cherry-bounce!) and drove her\par
friend quietly about the neighborhood till sunset, when "the party went\par
out."\par
\par
As she came walking in, looking very tired, but as composed as ever, she\par
observed that every vestige of the unfortunate _fête_ had disappeared,\par
except a suspicious pucker about the corners of Jo's mouth.\par
\par
"You've had a lovely afternoon for your drive, dear," said her mother,\par
as respectfully as if the whole twelve had come.\par
\par
"Miss Eliott is a very sweet girl, and seemed to enjoy herself, I\par
thought," observed Beth, with unusual warmth.\par
\par
"Could you spare me some of your cake? I really need some, I have so\par
much company, and I can't make such delicious stuff as yours," asked Meg\par
soberly.\par
\par
"Take it all; I'm the only one here who likes sweet things, and it will\par
mould before I can dispose of it," answered Amy, thinking with a sigh of\par
the generous store she had laid in for such an end as this.\par
\par
"It's a pity Laurie isn't here to help us," began Jo, as they sat down\par
to ice-cream and salad for the second time in two days.\par
\par
A warning look from her mother checked any further remarks, and the\par
whole family ate in heroic silence, till Mr. March mildly observed,\par
"Salad was one of the favorite dishes of the ancients, and Evelyn"--here\par
a general explosion of laughter cut short the "history of sallets," to\par
the great surprise of the learned gentleman.\par
\par
"Bundle everything into a basket and send it to the Hummels: Germans\par
like messes. I'm sick of the sight of this; and there's no reason you\par
should all die of a surfeit because I've been a fool," cried Amy, wiping\par
her eyes.\par
\par
"I thought I _should_ have died when I saw you two girls rattling about\par
in the what-you-call-it, like two little kernels in a very big nutshell,\par
and mother waiting in state to receive the throng," sighed Jo, quite\par
spent with laughter.\par
\par
"I'm very sorry you were disappointed, dear, but we all did our best to\par
satisfy you," said Mrs. March, in a tone full of motherly regret.\par
\par
"I _am_ satisfied; I've done what I undertook, and it's not my fault\par
that it failed; I comfort myself with that," said Amy, with a little\par
quiver in her voice. "I thank you all very much for helping me, and I'll\par
thank you still more if you won't allude to it for a month, at least."\par
\par
No one did for several months; but the word "_fête_" always produced a\par
general smile, and Laurie's birthday gift to Amy was a tiny coral\par
lobster in the shape of a charm for her watch-guard.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                    [Illustration: Literary Lessons]\par
\par
                                 XXVII.\par
\par
                           LITERARY LESSONS.\par
\par
\par
Fortune suddenly smiled upon Jo, and dropped a good-luck penny in her\par
path. Not a golden penny, exactly, but I doubt if half a million would\par
have given more real happiness than did the little sum that came to her\par
in this wise.\par
\par
Every few weeks she would shut herself up in her room, put on her\par
scribbling suit, and "fall into a vortex," as she expressed it, writing\par
away at her novel with all her heart and soul, for till that was\par
finished she could find no peace. Her "scribbling suit" consisted of a\par
black woollen pinafore on which she could wipe her pen at will, and a\par
cap of the same material, adorned with a cheerful red bow, into which\par
she bundled her hair when the decks were cleared for action. This cap\par
was a beacon to the inquiring eyes of her family, who during these\par
periods kept their distance, merely popping in their heads\par
semi-occasionally, to ask, with interest, "Does genius burn, Jo?" They\par
did not always venture even to ask this question, but took an\par
observation of the cap, and judged accordingly. If this expressive\par
article of dress was drawn low upon the forehead, it was a sign that\par
hard work was going on; in exciting moments it was pushed rakishly\par
askew; and when despair seized the author it was plucked wholly off, and\par
cast upon the floor. At such times the intruder silently withdrew; and\par
not until the red bow was seen gayly erect upon the gifted brow, did any\par
one dare address Jo.\par
\par
She did not think herself a genius by any means; but when the writing\par
fit came on, she gave herself up to it with entire abandon, and led a\par
blissful life, unconscious of want, care, or bad weather, while she sat\par
safe and happy in an imaginary world, full of friends almost as real and\par
dear to her as any in the flesh. Sleep forsook her eyes, meals stood\par
untasted, day and night were all too short to enjoy the happiness which\par
blessed her only at such times, and made these hours worth living, even\par
if they bore no other fruit. The divine afflatus usually lasted a week\par
or two, and then she emerged from her "vortex," hungry, sleepy, cross,\par
or despondent.\par
\par
She was just recovering from one of these attacks when she was prevailed\par
upon to escort Miss Crocker to a lecture, and in return for her virtue\par
was rewarded with a new idea. It was a People's Course, the lecture on\par
the Pyramids, and Jo rather wondered at the choice of such a subject for\par
such an audience, but took it for granted that some great social evil\par
would be remedied or some great want supplied by unfolding the glories\par
of the Pharaohs to an audience whose thoughts were busy with the price\par
of coal and flour, and whose lives were spent in trying to solve harder\par
riddles than that of the Sphinx.\par
\par
They were early; and while Miss Crocker set the heel of her stocking, Jo\par
amused herself by examining the faces of the people who occupied the\par
seat with them. On her left were two matrons, with massive foreheads,\par
and bonnets to match, discussing Woman's Rights and making tatting.\par
Beyond sat a pair of humble lovers, artlessly holding each other by the\par
hand, a sombre spinster eating peppermints out of a paper bag, and an\par
old gentleman taking his preparatory nap behind a yellow bandanna. On\par
her right, her only neighbor was a studious-looking lad absorbed in a\par
newspaper.\par
\par
It was a pictorial sheet, and Jo examined the work of art nearest her,\par
idly wondering what unfortuitous concatenation of circumstances needed\par
the melodramatic illustration of an Indian in full war costume, tumbling\par
over a precipice with a wolf at his throat, while two infuriated young\par
gentlemen, with unnaturally small feet and big eyes, were stabbing each\par
other close by, and a dishevelled female was flying away in the\par
background with her mouth wide open. Pausing to turn a page, the lad saw\par
her looking, and, with boyish good-nature, offered half his paper,\par
saying bluntly, "Want to read it? That's a first-rate story."\par
\par
Jo accepted it with a smile, for she had never outgrown her liking for\par
lads, and soon found herself involved in the usual labyrinth of love,\par
mystery, and murder, for the story belonged to that class of light\par
literature in which the passions have a holiday, and when the author's\par
invention fails, a grand catastrophe clears the stage of one half the\par
_dramatis personæ_, leaving the other half to exult over their downfall.\par
\par
"Prime, isn't it?" asked the boy, as her eye went down the last\par
paragraph of her portion.\par
\par
"I think you and I could do as well as that if we tried," returned Jo,\par
amused at his admiration of the trash.\par
\par
"I should think I was a pretty lucky chap if I could. She makes a good\par
living out of such stories, they say;" and he pointed to the name of\par
Mrs. S. L. A. N. G. Northbury, under the title of the tale.\par
\par
"Do you know her?" asked Jo, with sudden interest.\par
\par
"No; but I read all her pieces, and I know a fellow who works in the\par
office where this paper is printed."\par
\par
"Do you say she makes a good living out of stories like this?" and Jo\par
looked more respectfully at the agitated group and thickly-sprinkled\par
exclamation-points that adorned the page.\par
\par
"Guess she does! She knows just what folks like, and gets paid well for\par
writing it."\par
\par
Here the lecture began, but Jo heard very little of it, for while Prof.\par
Sands was prosing away about Belzoni, Cheops, scarabei, and\par
hieroglyphics, she was covertly taking down the address of the paper,\par
and boldly resolving to try for the hundred-dollar prize offered in its\par
columns for a sensational story. By the time the lecture ended and the\par
audience awoke, she had built up a splendid fortune for herself (not the\par
first founded upon paper), and was already deep in the concoction of her\par
story, being unable to decide whether the duel should come before the\par
elopement or after the murder.\par
\par
She said nothing of her plan at home, but fell to work next day, much to\par
the disquiet of her mother, who always looked a little anxious when\par
"genius took to burning." Jo had never tried this style before,\par
contenting herself with very mild romances for the "Spread Eagle." Her\par
theatrical experience and miscellaneous reading were of service now, for\par
they gave her some idea of dramatic effect, and supplied plot, language,\par
and costumes. Her story was as full of desperation and despair as her\par
limited acquaintance with those uncomfortable emotions enabled her to\par
make it, and, having located it in Lisbon, she wound up with an\par
earthquake, as a striking and appropriate _dénouement_. The manuscript\par
was privately despatched, accompanied by a note, modestly saying that if\par
the tale didn't get the prize, which the writer hardly dared expect, she\par
would be very glad to receive any sum it might be considered worth.\par
\par
Six weeks is a long time to wait, and a still longer time for a girl to\par
keep a secret; but Jo did both, and was just beginning to give up all\par
hope of ever seeing her manuscript again, when a letter arrived which\par
almost took her breath away; for on opening it, a check for a hundred\par
dollars fell into her lap. For a minute she stared at it as if it had\par
been a snake, then she read her letter and began to cry. If the amiable\par
gentleman who wrote that kindly note could have known what intense\par
happiness he was giving a fellow-creature, I think he would devote his\par
leisure hours, if he has any, to that amusement; for Jo valued the\par
letter more than the money, because it was encouraging; and after years\par
of effort it was _so_ pleasant to find that she had learned to do\par
something, though it was only to write a sensation story.\par
\par
            [Illustration: A check for one hundred dollars]\par
\par
A prouder young woman was seldom seen than she, when, having composed\par
herself, she electrified the family by appearing before them with the\par
letter in one hand, the check in the other, announcing that she had won\par
the prize. Of course there was a great jubilee, and when the story came\par
every one read and praised it; though after her father had told her that\par
the language was good, the romance fresh and hearty, and the tragedy\par
quite thrilling, he shook his head, and said in his unworldly way,--\par
\par
"You can do better than this, Jo. Aim at the highest, and never mind the\par
money."\par
\par
"_I_ think the money is the best part of it. What _will_ you do with\par
such a fortune?" asked Amy, regarding the magic slip of paper with a\par
reverential eye.\par
\par
"Send Beth and mother to the seaside for a month or two," answered Jo\par
promptly.\par
\par
"Oh, how splendid! No, I can't do it, dear, it would be so selfish,"\par
cried Beth, who had clapped her thin hands, and taken a long breath, as\par
if pining for fresh ocean-breezes; then stopped herself, and motioned\par
away the check which her sister waved before her.\par
\par
"Ah, but you shall go, I've set my heart on it; that's what I tried for,\par
and that's why I succeeded. I never get on when I think of myself alone,\par
so it will help me to work for you, don't you see? Besides, Marmee needs\par
the change, and she won't leave you, so you _must_ go. Won't it be fun\par
to see you come home plump and rosy again? Hurrah for Dr. Jo, who always\par
cures her patients!"\par
\par
To the sea side they went, after much discussion; and though Beth didn't\par
come home as plump and rosy as could be desired, she was much better,\par
while Mrs. March declared she felt ten years younger; so Jo was\par
satisfied with the investment of her prize money, and fell to work with\par
a cheery spirit, bent on earning more of those delightful checks. She\par
did earn several that year, and began to feel herself a power in the\par
house; for by the magic of a pen, her "rubbish" turned into comforts for\par
them all. "The Duke's Daughter" paid the butcher's bill, "A Phantom\par
Hand" put down a new carpet, and the "Curse of the Coventrys" proved the\par
blessing of the Marches in the way of groceries and gowns.\par
\par
Wealth is certainly a most desirable thing, but poverty has its sunny\par
side, and one of the sweet uses of adversity is the genuine satisfaction\par
which comes from hearty work of head or hand; and to the inspiration of\par
necessity, we owe half the wise, beautiful, and useful blessings of the\par
world. Jo enjoyed a taste of this satisfaction, and ceased to envy\par
richer girls, taking great comfort in the knowledge that she could\par
supply her own wants, and need ask no one for a penny.\par
\par
Little notice was taken of her stories, but they found a market; and,\par
encouraged by this fact, she resolved to make a bold stroke for fame and\par
fortune. Having copied her novel for the fourth time, read it to all her\par
confidential friends, and submitted it with fear and trembling to three\par
publishers, she at last disposed of it, on condition that she would cut\par
it down one third, and omit all the parts which she particularly\par
admired.\par
\par
"Now I must either bundle it back into my tin-kitchen to mould, pay for\par
printing it myself, or chop it up to suit purchasers, and get what I can\par
for it. Fame is a very good thing to have in the house, but cash is more\par
convenient; so I wish to take the sense of the meeting on this important\par
subject," said Jo, calling a family council.\par
\par
"Don't spoil your book, my girl, for there is more in it than you know,\par
and the idea is well worked out. Let it wait and ripen," was her\par
father's advice; and he practised as he preached, having waited\par
patiently thirty years for fruit of his own to ripen, and being in no\par
haste to gather it, even now, when it was sweet and mellow.\par
\par
"It seems to me that Jo will profit more by making the trial than by\par
waiting," said Mrs. March. "Criticism is the best test of such work, for\par
it will show her both unsuspected merits and faults, and help her to do\par
better next time. We are too partial; but the praise and blame of\par
outsiders will prove useful, even if she gets but little money."\par
\par
"Yes," said Jo, knitting her brows, "that's just it; I've been fussing\par
over the thing so long, I really don't know whether it's good, bad, or\par
indifferent. It will be a great help to have cool, impartial persons\par
take a look at it, and tell me what they think of it."\par
\par
"I wouldn't leave out a word of it; you'll spoil it if you do, for the\par
interest of the story is more in the minds than in the actions of the\par
people, and it will be all a muddle if you don't explain as you go on,"\par
said Meg, who firmly believed that this book was the most remarkable\par
novel ever written.\par
\par
"But Mr. Allen says, 'Leave out the explanations, make it brief and\par
dramatic, and let the characters tell the story,'" interrupted Jo,\par
turning to the publisher's note.\par
\par
"Do as he tells you; he knows what will sell, and we don't. Make a good,\par
popular book, and get as much money as you can. By and by, when, you've\par
got a name, you can afford to digress, and have philosophical and\par
metaphysical people in your novels," said Amy, who took a strictly\par
practical view of the subject.\par
\par
"Well," said Jo, laughing, "if my people _are_ 'philosophical and\par
metaphysical,' it isn't my fault, for I know nothing about such things,\par
except what I hear father say, sometimes. If I've got some of his wise\par
ideas jumbled up with my romance, so much the better for me. Now, Beth,\par
what do you say?"\par
\par
"I should so like to see it printed _soon_," was all Beth said, and\par
smiled in saying it; but there was an unconscious emphasis on the last\par
word, and a wistful look in the eyes that never lost their childlike\par
candor, which chilled Jo's heart, for a minute, with a foreboding fear,\par
and decided her to make her little venture "soon."\par
\par
So, with Spartan firmness, the young authoress laid her first-born on\par
her table, and chopped it up as ruthlessly as any ogre. In the hope of\par
pleasing every one, she took every one's advice; and, like the old man\par
and his donkey in the fable, suited nobody.\par
\par
Her father liked the metaphysical streak which had unconsciously got\par
into it; so that was allowed to remain, though she had her doubts about\par
it. Her mother thought that there _was_ a trifle too much description;\par
out, therefore, it nearly all came, and with it many necessary links in\par
the story. Meg admired the tragedy; so Jo piled up the agony to suit\par
her, while Amy objected to the fun, and, with the best intentions in\par
life, Jo quenched the sprightly scenes which relieved the sombre\par
character of the story. Then, to complete the ruin, she cut it down one\par
third, and confidingly sent the poor little romance, like a picked\par
robin, out into the big, busy world, to try its fate.\par
\par
Well, it was printed, and she got three hundred dollars for it; likewise\par
plenty of praise and blame, both so much greater than she expected that\par
she was thrown into a state of bewilderment, from which it took her some\par
time to recover.\par
\par
"You said, mother, that criticism would help me; but how can it, when\par
it's so contradictory that I don't know whether I've written a promising\par
book or broken all the ten commandments?" cried poor Jo, turning over a\par
heap of notices, the perusal of which filled her with pride and joy one\par
minute, wrath and dire dismay the next. "This man says 'An exquisite\par
book, full of truth, beauty, and earnestness; all is sweet, pure, and\par
healthy,'" continued the perplexed authoress. "The next, 'The theory of\par
the book is bad, full of morbid fancies, spiritualistic ideas, and\par
unnatural characters.' Now, as I had no theory of any kind, don't\par
believe in Spiritualism, and copied my characters from life, I don't see\par
how this critic _can_ be right. Another says, 'It's one of the best\par
American novels which has appeared for years' (I know better than that);\par
and the next asserts that 'though it is original, and written with great\par
force and feeling, it is a dangerous book.' 'Tisn't! Some make fun of\par
it, some over-praise, and nearly all insist that I had a deep theory to\par
expound, when I only wrote it for the pleasure and the money. I wish I'd\par
printed it whole or not at all, for I do hate to be so misjudged."\par
\par
Her family and friends administered comfort and commendation liberally;\par
yet it was a hard time for sensitive, high-spirited Jo, who meant so\par
well, and had apparently done so ill. But it did her good, for those\par
whose opinion had real value gave her the criticism which is an author's\par
best education; and when the first soreness was over, she could laugh at\par
her poor little book, yet believe in it still, and feel herself the\par
wiser and stronger for the buffeting she had received.\par
\par
"Not being a genius, like Keats, it won't kill me," she said stoutly;\par
"and I've got the joke on my side, after all; for the parts that were\par
taken straight out of real life are denounced as impossible and absurd,\par
and the scenes that I made up out of my own silly head are pronounced\par
'charmingly natural, tender, and true.' So I'll comfort myself with\par
that; and when I'm ready, I'll up again and take another."\par
\par
