\par
\par
                  [Illustration: Domestic Experiences]\par
\par
                                XXVIII.\par
\par
                         DOMESTIC EXPERIENCES.\par
\par
\par
Like most other young matrons, Meg began her married life with the\par
determination to be a model housekeeper. John should find home a\par
paradise; he should always see a smiling face, should fare sumptuously\par
every day, and never know the loss of a button. She brought so much\par
love, energy, and cheerfulness to the work that she could not but\par
succeed, in spite of some obstacles. Her paradise was not a tranquil\par
one; for the little woman fussed, was over-anxious to please, and\par
bustled about like a true Martha, cumbered with many cares. She was too\par
tired, sometimes, even to smile; John grew dyspeptic after a course of\par
dainty dishes, and ungratefully demanded plain fare. As for buttons, she\par
soon learned to wonder where they went, to shake her head over the\par
carelessness of men, and to threaten to make him sew them on himself,\par
and then see if _his_ work would stand impatient tugs and clumsy fingers\par
any better than hers.\par
\par
They were very happy, even after they discovered that they couldn't live\par
on love alone. John did not find Meg's beauty diminished, though she\par
beamed at him from behind the familiar coffee-pot; nor did Meg miss any\par
of the romance from the daily parting, when her husband followed up his\par
kiss with the tender inquiry, "Shall I send home veal or mutton for\par
dinner, darling?" The little house ceased to be a glorified bower, but\par
it became a home, and the young couple soon felt that it was a change\par
for the better. At first they played keep-house, and frolicked over it\par
like children; then John took steadily to business, feeling the cares of\par
the head of a family upon his shoulders; and Meg laid by her cambric\par
wrappers, put on a big apron, and fell to work, as before said, with\par
more energy than discretion.\par
\par
While the cooking mania lasted she went through Mrs. Cornelius's Receipt\par
Book as if it were a mathematical exercise, working out the problems\par
with patience and care. Sometimes her family were invited in to help eat\par
up a too bounteous feast of successes, or Lotty would be privately\par
despatched with a batch of failures, which were to be concealed from all\par
eyes in the convenient stomachs of the little Hummels. An evening with\par
John over the account-books usually produced a temporary lull in the\par
culinary enthusiasm, and a frugal fit would ensue, during which the poor\par
man was put through a course of bread-pudding, hash, and warmed-over\par
coffee, which tried his soul, although he bore it with praiseworthy\par
fortitude. Before the golden mean was found, however, Meg added to her\par
domestic possessions what young couples seldom get on long without,--a\par
family jar.\par
\par
Fired with a housewifely wish to see her store-room stocked with\par
home-made preserves, she undertook to put up her own currant jelly. John\par
was requested to order home a dozen or so of little pots, and an extra\par
quantity of sugar, for their own currants were ripe, and were to be\par
attended to at once. As John firmly believed that "my wife" was equal to\par
anything, and took a natural pride in her skill, he resolved that she\par
should be gratified, and their only crop of fruit laid by in a most\par
pleasing form for winter use. Home came four dozen delightful little\par
pots, half a barrel of sugar, and a small boy to pick the currants for\par
her. With her pretty hair tucked into a little cap, arms bared to the\par
elbow, and a checked apron which had a coquettish look in spite of the\par
bib, the young housewife fell to work, feeling no doubts about her\par
success; for hadn't she seen Hannah do it hundreds of times? The array\par
of pots rather amazed her at first, but John was so fond of jelly, and\par
the nice little jars would look so well on the top shelf, that Meg\par
resolved to fill them all, and spent a long day picking, boiling,\par
straining, and fussing over her jelly. She did her best; she asked\par
advice of Mrs. Cornelius; she racked her brain to remember what Hannah\par
did that she had left undone; she reboiled, resugared, and restrained,\par
but that dreadful stuff wouldn't "_jell_."\par
\par
She longed to run home, bib and all, and ask mother to lend a hand, but\par
John and she had agreed that they would never annoy any one with their\par
private worries, experiments, or quarrels. They had laughed over that\par
last word as if the idea it suggested was a most preposterous one; but\par
they had held to their resolve, and whenever they could get on without\par
help they did so, and no one interfered, for Mrs. March had advised the\par
plan. So Meg wrestled alone with the refractory sweetmeats all that hot\par
summer day, and at five o'clock sat down in her topsy-turvy kitchen,\par
wrung her bedaubed hands, lifted up her voice and wept.\par
\par
Now, in the first flush of the new life, she had often said,--\par
\par
"My husband shall always feel free to bring a friend home whenever he\par
likes. I shall always be prepared; there shall be no flurry, no\par
scolding, no discomfort, but a neat house, a cheerful wife, and a good\par
dinner. John, dear, never stop to ask my leave, invite whom you please,\par
and be sure of a welcome from me."\par
\par
How charming that was, to be sure! John quite glowed with pride to hear\par
her say it, and felt what a blessed thing it was to have a superior\par
wife. But, although they had had company from time to time, it never\par
happened to be unexpected, and Meg had never had an opportunity to\par
distinguish herself till now. It always happens so in this vale of\par
tears; there is an inevitability about such things which we can only\par
wonder at, deplore, and bear as we best can.\par
\par
If John had not forgotten all about the jelly, it really would have been\par
unpardonable in him to choose that day, of all the days in the year, to\par
bring a friend home to dinner unexpectedly. Congratulating himself that\par
a handsome repast had been ordered that morning, feeling sure that it\par
would be ready to the minute, and indulging in pleasant anticipations of\par
the charming effect it would produce, when his pretty wife came running\par
out to meet him, he escorted his friend to his mansion, with the\par
irrepressible satisfaction of a young host and husband.\par
\par
It is a world of disappointments, as John discovered when he reached the\par
Dove-cote. The front door usually stood hospitably open; now it was not\par
only shut, but locked, and yesterday's mud still adorned the steps. The\par
parlor-windows were closed and curtained, no picture of the pretty wife\par
sewing on the piazza, in white, with a distracting little bow in her\par
hair, or a bright-eyed hostess, smiling a shy welcome as she greeted her\par
guest. Nothing of the sort, for not a soul appeared, but a\par
sanguinary-looking boy asleep under the currant-bushes.\par
\par
"I'm afraid something has happened. Step into the garden, Scott, while I\par
look up Mrs. Brooke," said John, alarmed at the silence and solitude.\par
\par
Round the house he hurried, led by a pungent smell of burnt sugar, and\par
Mr. Scott strolled after him, with a queer look on his face. He paused\par
discreetly at a distance when Brooke disappeared; but he could both see\par
and hear, and, being a bachelor, enjoyed the prospect mightily.\par
\par
In the kitchen reigned confusion and despair; one edition of jelly was\par
trickled from pot to pot, another lay upon the floor, and a third was\par
burning gayly on the stove. Lotty, with Teutonic phlegm, was calmly\par
eating bread and currant wine, for the jelly was still in a hopelessly\par
liquid state, while Mrs. Brooke, with her apron over her head, sat\par
sobbing dismally.\par
\par
"My dearest girl, what is the matter?" cried John, rushing in, with\par
awful visions of scalded hands, sudden news of affliction, and secret\par
consternation at the thought of the guest in the garden.\par
\par
"O John, I _am_ so tired and hot and cross and worried! I've been at it\par
till I'm all worn out. Do come and help me or I _shall_ die!" and the\par
exhausted housewife cast herself upon his breast, giving him a sweet\par
welcome in every sense of the word, for her pinafore had been baptized\par
at the same time as the floor.\par
\par
"What worries you, dear? Has anything dreadful happened?" asked the\par
anxious John, tenderly kissing the crown of the little cap, which was\par
all askew.\par
\par
"Yes," sobbed Meg despairingly.\par
\par
"Tell me quick, then. Don't cry, I can bear anything better than that.\par
Out with it, love."\par
\par
"The--the jelly won't jell and I don't know what to do!"\par
\par
John Brooke laughed then as he never dared to laugh afterward; and the\par
derisive Scott smiled involuntarily as he heard the hearty peal, which\par
put the finishing stroke to poor Meg's woe.\par
\par
"Is that all? Fling it out of window, and don't bother any more about\par
it. I'll buy you quarts if you want it; but for heaven's sake don't have\par
hysterics, for I've brought Jack Scott home to dinner, and--"\par
\par
John got no further, for Meg cast him off, and clasped her hands with a\par
tragic gesture as she fell into a chair, exclaiming in a tone of mingled\par
indignation, reproach, and dismay,--\par
\par
"A man to dinner, and everything in a mess! John Brooke, how _could_ you\par
do such a thing?"\par
\par
"Hush, he's in the garden! I forgot the confounded jelly, but it can't\par
be helped now," said John, surveying the prospect with an anxious eye.\par
\par
"You ought to have sent word, or told me this morning, and you ought to\par
have remembered how busy I was," continued Meg petulantly; for even\par
turtle-doves will peck when ruffled.\par
\par
"I didn't know it this morning, and there was no time to send word, for\par
I met him on the way out. I never thought of asking leave, when you have\par
always told me to do as I liked. I never tried it before, and hang me if\par
I ever do again!" added John, with an aggrieved air.\par
\par
"I should hope not! Take him away at once; I can't see him, and there\par
isn't any dinner."\par
\par
"Well, I like that! Where's the beef and vegetables I sent home, and the\par
pudding you promised?" cried John, rushing to the larder.\par
\par
"I hadn't time to cook anything; I meant to dine at mother's. I'm sorry,\par
but I was _so_ busy;" and Meg's tears began again.\par
\par
John was a mild man, but he was human; and after a long day's work, to\par
come home tired, hungry, and hopeful, to find a chaotic house, an empty\par
table, and a cross wife was not exactly conducive to repose of mind or\par
manner. He restrained himself, however, and the little squall would have\par
blown over, but for one unlucky word.\par
\par
"It's a scrape, I acknowledge; but if you will lend a hand, we'll pull\par
through, and have a good time yet. Don't cry, dear, but just exert\par
yourself a bit, and knock us up something to eat. We're both as hungry\par
as hunters, so we sha'n't mind what it is. Give us the cold meat, and\par
bread and cheese; we won't ask for jelly."\par
\par
He meant it for a good-natured joke; but that one word sealed his fate.\par
Meg thought it was _too_ cruel to hint about her sad failure, and the\par
last atom of patience vanished as he spoke.\par
\par
"You must get yourself out of the scrape as you can; I'm too used up to\par
'exert' myself for any one. It's like a man to propose a bone and vulgar\par
bread and cheese for company. I won't have anything of the sort in my\par
house. Take that Scott up to mother's, and tell him I'm away, sick,\par
dead,--anything. I won't see him, and you two can laugh at me and my\par
jelly as much as you like: you won't have anything else here;" and\par
having delivered her defiance all in one breath, Meg cast away her\par
pinafore, and precipitately left the field to bemoan herself in her own\par
room.\par
\par
What those two creatures did in her absence, she never knew; but Mr.\par
Scott was not taken "up to mother's," and when Meg descended, after they\par
had strolled away together, she found traces of a promiscuous lunch\par
which filled her with horror. Lotty reported that they had eaten "a\par
much, and greatly laughed, and the master bid her throw away all the\par
sweet stuff, and hide the pots."\par
\par
Meg longed to go and tell mother; but a sense of shame at her own\par
short-comings, of loyalty to John, "who might be cruel, but nobody\par
should know it," restrained her; and after a summary clearing up, she\par
dressed herself prettily, and sat down to wait for John to come and be\par
forgiven.\par
\par
Unfortunately, John didn't come, not seeing the matter in that light. He\par
had carried it off as a good joke with Scott, excused his little wife as\par
well as he could, and played the host so hospitably that his friend\par
enjoyed the impromptu dinner, and promised to come again. But John was\par
angry, though he did not show it; he felt that Meg had got him into a\par
scrape, and then deserted him in his hour of need. "It wasn't fair to\par
tell a man to bring folks home any time, with perfect freedom, and when\par
he took you at your word, to flame up and blame him, and leave him in\par
the lurch, to be laughed at or pitied. No, by George, it wasn't! and Meg\par
must know it." He had fumed inwardly during the feast, but when the\par
flurry was over, and he strolled home, after seeing Scott off, a milder\par
mood came over him. "Poor little thing! it was hard upon her when she\par
tried so heartily to please me. She was wrong, of course, but then she\par
was young. I must be patient and teach her." He hoped she had not gone\par
home--he hated gossip and interference. For a minute he was ruffled\par
again at the mere thought of it; and then the fear that Meg would cry\par
herself sick softened his heart, and sent him on at a quicker pace,\par
resolving to be calm and kind, but firm, quite firm, and show her where\par
she had failed in her duty to her spouse.\par
\par
Meg likewise resolved to be "calm and kind, but firm," and show _him_\par
his duty. She longed to run to meet him, and beg pardon, and be kissed\par
and comforted, as she was sure of being; but, of course, she did nothing\par
of the sort, and when she saw John coming, began to hum quite naturally,\par
as she rocked and sewed, like a lady of leisure in her best parlor.\par
\par
John was a little disappointed not to find a tender Niobe; but, feeling\par
that his dignity demanded the first apology, he made none, only came\par
leisurely in, and laid himself upon the sofa, with the singularly\par
relevant remark,--\par
\par
"We are going to have a new moon, my dear."\par
\par
"I've no objection," was Meg's equally soothing remark.\par
\par
A few other topics of general interest were introduced by Mr. Brooke,\par
and wet-blanketed by Mrs. Brooke, and conversation languished. John went\par
to one window, unfolded his paper, and wrapped himself in it,\par
figuratively speaking. Meg went to the other window, and sewed as if new\par
rosettes for her slippers were among the necessaries of life. Neither\par
spoke; both looked quite "calm and firm," and both felt desperately\par
uncomfortable.\par
\par
          [Illustration: Both felt desperately uncomfortable]\par
\par
"Oh dear," thought Meg, "married life is very trying, and does need\par
infinite patience, as well as love, as mother says." The word "mother"\par
suggested other maternal counsels, given long ago, and received with\par
unbelieving protests.\par
\par
"John is a good man, but he has his faults, and you must learn to see\par
and bear with them, remembering your own. He is very decided, but never\par
will be obstinate, if you reason kindly, not oppose impatiently. He is\par
very accurate, and particular about the truth--a good trait, though you\par
call him 'fussy.' Never deceive him by look or word, Meg, and he will\par
give you the confidence you deserve, the support you need. He has a\par
temper, not like ours,--one flash, and then all over,--but the white,\par
still anger, that is seldom stirred, but once kindled, is hard to\par
quench. Be careful, very careful, not to wake this anger against\par
yourself, for peace and happiness depend on keeping his respect. Watch\par
yourself, be the first to ask pardon if you both err, and guard against\par
the little piques, misunderstandings, and hasty words that often pave\par
the way for bitter sorrow and regret."\par
\par
These words came back to Meg, as she sat sewing in the sunset,\par
especially the last. This was the first serious disagreement; her own\par
hasty speeches sounded both silly and unkind, as she recalled them, her\par
own anger looked childish now, and thoughts of poor John coming home to\par
such a scene quite melted her heart. She glanced at him with tears in\par
her eyes, but he did not see them; she put down her work and got up,\par
thinking, "I _will_ be the first to say, 'Forgive me,'" but he did not\par
seem to hear her; she went very slowly across the room, for pride was\par
hard to swallow, and stood by him, but he did not turn his head. For a\par
minute she felt as if she really couldn't do it; then came the thought,\par
"This is the beginning, I'll do my part, and have nothing to reproach\par
myself with," and stooping down, she softly kissed her husband on the\par
forehead. Of course that settled it; the penitent kiss was better than a\par
world of words, and John had her on his knee in a minute, saying\par
tenderly,--\par
\par
"It was too bad to laugh at the poor little jelly-pots. Forgive me,\par
dear, I never will again!"\par
\par
But he did, oh bless you, yes, hundreds of times, and so did Meg, both\par
declaring that it was the sweetest jelly they ever made; for family\par
peace was preserved in that little family jar.\par
\par
After this, Meg had Mr. Scott to dinner by special invitation, and\par
served him up a pleasant feast without a cooked wife for the first\par
course; on which occasion she was so gay and gracious, and made\par
everything go off so charmingly, that Mr. Scott told John he was a happy\par
fellow, and shook his head over the hardships of bachelorhood all the\par
way home.\par
\par
In the autumn, new trials and experiences came to Meg. Sallie Moffat\par
renewed her friendship, was always running out for a dish of gossip at\par
the little house, or inviting "that poor dear" to come in and spend the\par
day at the big house. It was pleasant, for in dull weather Meg often\par
felt lonely; all were busy at home, John absent till night, and nothing\par
to do but sew, or read, or potter about. So it naturally fell out that\par
Meg got into the way of gadding and gossiping with her friend. Seeing\par
Sallie's pretty things made her long for such, and pity herself because\par
she had not got them. Sallie was very kind, and often offered her the\par
coveted trifles; but Meg declined them, knowing that John wouldn't like\par
it; and then this foolish little woman went and did what John disliked\par
infinitely worse.\par
\par
She knew her husband's income, and she loved to feel that he trusted\par
her, not only with his happiness, but what some men seem to value\par
more,--his money. She knew where it was, was free to take what she\par
liked, and all he asked was that she should keep account of every penny,\par
pay bills once a month, and remember that she was a poor man's wife.\par
Till now, she had done well, been prudent and exact, kept her little\par
account-books neatly, and showed them to him monthly without fear. But\par
that autumn the serpent got into Meg's paradise, and tempted her, like\par
many a modern Eve, not with apples, but with dress. Meg didn't like to\par
be pitied and made to feel poor; it irritated her, but she was ashamed\par
to confess it, and now and then she tried to console herself by buying\par
something pretty, so that Sallie needn't think she had to economize. She\par
always felt wicked after it, for the pretty things were seldom\par
necessaries; but then they cost so little, it wasn't worth worrying\par
about; so the trifles increased unconsciously, and in the shopping\par
excursions she was no longer a passive looker-on.\par
\par
But the trifles cost more than one would imagine; and when she cast up\par
her accounts at the end of the month, the sum total rather scared her.\par
John was busy that month, and left the bills to her; the next month he\par
was absent; but the third he had a grand quarterly settling up, and Meg\par
never forgot it. A few days before she had done a dreadful thing, and it\par
weighed upon her conscience. Sallie had been buying silks, and Meg\par
longed for a new one,--just a handsome light one for parties, her black\par
silk was so common, and thin things for evening wear were only proper\par
for girls. Aunt March usually gave the sisters a present of twenty-five\par
dollars apiece at New Year; that was only a month to wait, and here was\par
a lovely violet silk going at a bargain, and she had the money, if she\par
only dared to take it. John always said what was his was hers; but would\par
he think it right to spend not only the prospective five-and-twenty, but\par
another five-and-twenty out of the household fund? That was the\par
question. Sallie had urged her to do it, had offered to loan the money,\par
and with the best intentions in life, had tempted Meg beyond her\par
strength. In an evil moment the shopman held up the lovely, shimmering\par
folds, and said, "A bargain, I assure you, ma'am." She answered, "I'll\par
take it;" and it was cut off and paid for, and Sallie had exulted, and\par
she had laughed as if it were a thing of no consequence, and driven\par
away, feeling as if she had stolen something, and the police were after\par
her.\par
\par
             [Illustration: A bargain, I assure you, ma'am]\par
\par
When she got home, she tried to assuage the pangs of remorse by\par
spreading forth the lovely silk; but it looked less silvery now, didn't\par
become her, after all, and the words "fifty dollars" seemed stamped like\par
a pattern down each breadth. She put it away; but it haunted her, not\par
delightfully, as a new dress should, but dreadfully, like the ghost of a\par
folly that was not easily laid. When John got out his books that night,\par
Meg's heart sank, and for the first time in her married life, she was\par
afraid of her husband. The kind, brown eyes looked as if they could be\par
stern; and though he was unusually merry, she fancied he had found her\par
out, but didn't mean to let her know it. The house-bills were all paid,\par
the books all in order. John had praised her, and was undoing the old\par
pocket-book which they called the "bank," when Meg, knowing that it was\par
quite empty, stopped his hand, saying nervously,--\par
\par
"You haven't seen my private expense book yet."\par
\par
John never asked to see it; but she always insisted on his doing so, and\par
used to enjoy his masculine amazement at the queer things women wanted,\par
and made him guess what "piping" was, demand fiercely the meaning of a\par
"hug-me-tight," or wonder how a little thing composed of three rosebuds,\par
a bit of velvet, and a pair of strings, could possibly be a bonnet, and\par
cost five or six dollars. That night he looked as if he would like the\par
fun of quizzing her figures and pretending to be horrified at her\par
extravagance, as he often did, being particularly proud of his prudent\par
wife.\par
\par
The little book was brought slowly out, and laid down before him. Meg\par
got behind his chair under pretence of smoothing the wrinkles out of his\par
tired forehead, and standing there, she said, with her panic increasing\par
with every word,--\par
\par
"John, dear, I'm ashamed to show you my book, for I've really been\par
dreadfully extravagant lately. I go about so much I must have things,\par
you know, and Sallie advised my getting it, so I did; and my New-Year's\par
money will partly pay for it: but I was sorry after I'd done it, for I\par
knew you'd think it wrong in me."\par
\par
John laughed, and drew her round beside him, saying good-humoredly,\par
"Don't go and hide. I won't beat you if you _have_ got a pair of killing\par
boots; I'm rather proud of my wife's feet, and don't mind if she does\par
pay eight or nine dollars for her boots, if they are good ones."\par
\par
That had been one of her last "trifles," and John's eye had fallen on it\par
as he spoke. "Oh, what _will_ he say when he comes to that awful fifty\par
dollars!" thought Meg, with a shiver.\par
\par
"It's worse than boots, it's a silk dress," she said, with the calmness\par
of desperation, for she wanted the worst over.\par
\par
"Well, dear, what is the 'dem'd total,' as Mr. Mantalini says?"\par
\par
That didn't sound like John, and she knew he was looking up at her with\par
the straightforward look that she had always been ready to meet and\par
answer with one as frank till now. She turned the page and her head at\par
the same time, pointing to the sum which would have been bad enough\par
without the fifty, but which was appalling to her with that added. For a\par
minute the room was very still; then John said slowly,--but she could\par
feel it cost him an effort to express no displeasure,--\par
\par
"Well, I don't know that fifty is much for a dress, with all the\par
furbelows and notions you have to have to finish it off these days."\par
\par
"It isn't made or trimmed," sighed Meg faintly, for a sudden\par
recollection of the cost still to be incurred quite overwhelmed her.\par
\par
"Twenty-five yards of silk seems a good deal to cover one small woman,\par
but I've no doubt my wife will look as fine as Ned Moffat's when she\par
gets it on," said John dryly.\par
\par
"I know you are angry, John, but I can't help it. I don't mean to waste\par
your money, and I didn't think those little things would count up so. I\par
can't resist them when I see Sallie buying all she wants, and pitying me\par
because I don't. I try to be contented, but it is hard, and I'm tired of\par
being poor."\par
\par
The last words were spoken so low she thought he did not hear them, but\par
he did, and they wounded him deeply, for he had denied himself many\par
pleasures for Meg's sake. She could have bitten her tongue out the\par
minute she had said it, for John pushed the books away, and got up,\par
saying, with a little quiver in his voice, "I was afraid of this; I do\par
my best, Meg." If he had scolded her, or even shaken her, it would not\par
have broken her heart like those few words. She ran to him and held him\par
close, crying, with repentant tears, "O John, my dear, kind,\par
hard-working boy, I didn't mean it! It was so wicked, so untrue and\par
ungrateful, how could I say it! Oh, how could I say it!"\par
\par
He was very kind, forgave her readily, and did not utter one reproach;\par
but Meg knew that she had done and said a thing which would not be\par
forgotten soon, although he might never allude to it again. She had\par
promised to love him for better for worse; and then she, his wife, had\par
reproached him with his poverty, after spending his earnings recklessly.\par
It was dreadful; and the worst of it was John went on so quietly\par
afterward, just as if nothing had happened, except that he stayed in\par
town later, and worked at night when she had gone to cry herself to\par
sleep. A week of remorse nearly made Meg sick; and the discovery that\par
John had countermanded the order for his new great-coat reduced her to a\par
state of despair which was pathetic to behold. He had simply said, in\par
answer to her surprised inquiries as to the change, "I can't afford it,\par
my dear."\par
\par
Meg said no more, but a few minutes after he found her in the hall, with\par
her face buried in the old great-coat, crying as if her heart would\par
break.\par
\par
They had a long talk that night, and Meg learned to love her husband\par
better for his poverty, because it seemed to have made a man of him,\par
given him the strength and courage to fight his own way, and taught him\par
a tender patience with which to bear and comfort the natural longings\par
and failures of those he loved.\par
\par
Next day she put her pride in her pocket, went to Sallie, told the\par
truth, and asked her to buy the silk as a favor. The good-natured Mrs.\par
Moffat willingly did so, and had the delicacy not to make her a present\par
of it immediately afterward. Then Meg ordered home the great-coat, and,\par
when John arrived, she put it on, and asked him how he liked her new\par
silk gown. One can imagine what answer he made, how he received his\par
present, and what a blissful state of things ensued. John came home\par
early, Meg gadded no more; and that great-coat was put on in the morning\par
by a very happy husband, and taken off at night by a most devoted little\par
wife. So the year rolled round, and at midsummer there came to Meg a new\par
experience,--the deepest and tenderest of a woman's life.\par
\par
Laurie came sneaking into the kitchen of the Dove-cote, one Saturday,\par
with an excited face, and was received with the clash of cymbals; for\par
Hannah clapped her hands with a saucepan in one and the cover in the\par
other.\par
\par
"How's the little mamma? Where is everybody? Why didn't you tell me\par
before I came home?" began Laurie, in a loud whisper.\par
\par
"Happy as a queen, the dear! Every soul of 'em is upstairs a worshipin';\par
we didn't want no hurrycanes round. Now you go into the parlor, and I'll\par
send 'em down to you," with which somewhat involved reply Hannah\par
vanished, chuckling ecstatically.\par
\par
Presently Jo appeared, proudly bearing a flannel bundle laid forth upon\par
a large pillow. Jo's face was very sober, but her eyes twinkled, and\par
there was an odd sound in her voice of repressed emotion of some sort.\par
\par
"Shut your eyes and hold out your arms," she said invitingly.\par
\par
Laurie backed precipitately into a corner, and put his hands behind him\par
with an imploring gesture: "No, thank you, I'd rather not. I shall drop\par
it or smash it, as sure as fate."\par
\par
"Then you sha'n't see your nevvy," said Jo decidedly, turning as if to\par
go.\par
\par
"I will, I will! only you must be responsible for damages;" and, obeying\par
orders, Laurie heroically shut his eyes while something was put into his\par
arms. A peal of laughter from Jo, Amy, Mrs. March, Hannah, and John\par
caused him to open them the next minute, to find himself invested with\par
two babies instead of one.\par
\par
[Illustration: Laurie heroically shut his eyes while something was put\par
                             into his arms]\par
\par
No wonder they laughed, for the expression of his face was droll enough\par
to convulse a Quaker, as he stood and stared wildly from the unconscious\par
innocents to the hilarious spectators, with such dismay that Jo sat down\par
on the floor and screamed.\par
\par
"Twins, by Jupiter!" was all he said for a minute; then, turning to the\par
women with an appealing look that was comically piteous, he added, "Take\par
'em quick, somebody! I'm going to laugh, and I shall drop 'em."\par
\par
John rescued his babies, and marched up and down, with one on each arm,\par
as if already initiated into the mysteries of baby-tending, while Laurie\par
laughed till the tears ran down his cheeks.\par
\par
"It's the best joke of the season, isn't it? I wouldn't have you told,\par
for I set my heart on surprising you, and I flatter myself I've done\par
it," said Jo, when she got her breath.\par
\par
"I never was more staggered in my life. Isn't it fun? Are they boys?\par
What are you going to name them? Let's have another look. Hold me up,\par
Jo; for upon my life it's one too many for me," returned Laurie,\par
regarding the infants with the air of a big, benevolent Newfoundland\par
looking at a pair of infantile kittens.\par
\par
"Boy and girl. Aren't they beauties?" said the proud papa, beaming upon\par
the little, red squirmers as if they were unfledged angels.\par
\par
"Most remarkable children I ever saw. Which is which?" and Laurie bent\par
like a well-sweep to examine the prodigies.\par
\par
"Amy put a blue ribbon on the boy and a pink on the girl, French\par
fashion, so you can always tell. Besides, one has blue eyes and one\par
brown. Kiss them, Uncle Teddy," said wicked Jo.\par
\par
"I'm afraid they mightn't like it," began Laurie, with unusual timidity\par
in such matters.\par
\par
"Of course they will; they are used to it now. Do it this minute, sir!"\par
commanded Jo, fearing he might propose a proxy.\par
\par
Laurie screwed up his face, and obeyed with a gingerly peck at each\par
little cheek that produced another laugh, and made the babies squeal.\par
\par
"There, I knew they didn't like it! That's the boy; see him kick; he\par
hits out with his fists like a good one. Now then, young Brooke, pitch\par
into a man of your own size, will you?" cried Laurie, delighted with a\par
poke in the face from a tiny fist, flapping aimlessly about.\par
\par
"He's to be named John Laurence, and the girl Margaret, after mother and\par
grandmother. We shall call her Daisy, so as not to have two Megs, and I\par
suppose the mannie will be Jack, unless we find a better name," said\par
Amy, with aunt-like interest.\par
\par
"Name him Demijohn, and call him 'Demi' for short," said Laurie.\par
\par
"Daisy and Demi,--just the thing! I _knew_ Teddy would do it," cried Jo,\par
clapping her hands.\par
\par
Teddy certainly had done it that time, for the babies were "Daisy" and\par
"Demi" to the end of the chapter.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                 XXIX.\par
\par
                                 CALLS.\par
\par
\par
                         [Illustration: Calls]\par
\par
"Come, Jo, it's time."\par
\par
"For what?"\par
\par
"You don't mean to say you have forgotten that you promised to make half\par
a dozen calls with me to-day?"\par
\par
"I've done a good many rash and foolish things in my life, but I don't\par
think I ever was mad enough to say I'd make six calls in one day, when a\par
single one upsets me for a week."\par
\par
"Yes, you did; it was a bargain between us. I was to finish the crayon\par
of Beth for you, and you were to go properly with me, and return our\par
neighbors' visits."\par
\par
"If it was fair--that was in the bond; and I stand to the letter of my\par
bond, Shylock. There is a pile of clouds in the east; it's _not_ fair,\par
and I don't go."\par
\par
"Now, that's shirking. It's a lovely day, no prospect of rain, and you\par
pride yourself on keeping promises; so be honorable; come and do your\par
duty, and then be at peace for another six months."\par
\par
At that minute Jo was particularly absorbed in dressmaking; for she was\par
mantua-maker general to the family, and took especial credit to herself\par
because she could use a needle as well as a pen. It was very provoking\par
to be arrested in the act of a first trying-on, and ordered out to make\par
calls in her best array, on a warm July day. She hated calls of the\par
formal sort, and never made any till Amy compelled her with a bargain,\par
bribe, or promise. In the present instance, there was no escape; and\par
having clashed her scissors rebelliously, while protesting that she\par
smelt thunder, she gave in, put away her work, and taking up her hat and\par
gloves with an air of resignation, told Amy the victim was ready.\par
\par
"Jo March, you are perverse enough to provoke a saint! You don't intend\par
to make calls in that state, I hope," cried Amy, surveying her with\par
amazement.\par
\par
"Why not? I'm neat and cool and comfortable; quite proper for a dusty\par
walk on a warm day. If people care more for my clothes than they do for\par
me, I don't wish to see them. You can dress for both, and be as elegant\par
as you please: it pays for you to be fine; it doesn't for me, and\par
furbelows only worry me."\par
\par
"Oh dear!" sighed Amy; "now she's in a contrary fit, and will drive me\par
distracted before I can get her properly ready. I'm sure it's no\par
pleasure to me to go to-day, but it's a debt we owe society, and there's\par
no one to pay it but you and me. I'll do anything for you, Jo, if you'll\par
only dress yourself nicely, and come and help me do the civil. You can\par
talk so well, look so aristocratic in your best things, and behave so\par
beautifully, if you try, that I'm proud of you. I'm afraid to go alone;\par
do come and take care of me."\par
\par
"You're an artful little puss to flatter and wheedle your cross old\par
sister in that way. The idea of my being aristocratic and well-bred, and\par
your being afraid to go anywhere alone! I don't know which is the most\par
absurd. Well, I'll go if I must, and do my best. You shall be commander\par
of the expedition, and I'll obey blindly; will that satisfy you?" said\par
Jo, with a sudden change from perversity to lamb-like submission.\par
\par
"You're a perfect cherub! Now put on all your best things, and I'll tell\par
you how to behave at each place, so that you will make a good\par
impression. I want people to like you, and they would if you'd only try\par
to be a little more agreeable. Do your hair the pretty way, and put the\par
pink rose in your bonnet; it's becoming, and you look too sober in your\par
plain suit. Take your light gloves and the embroidered handkerchief.\par
We'll stop at Meg's, and borrow her white sunshade, and then you can\par
have my dove-colored one."\par
\par
While Amy dressed, she issued her orders, and Jo obeyed them; not\par
without entering her protest, however, for she sighed as she rustled\par
into her new organdie, frowned darkly at herself as she tied her bonnet\par
strings in an irreproachable bow, wrestled viciously with pins as she\par
put on her collar, wrinkled up her features generally as she shook out\par
the handkerchief, whose embroidery was as irritating to her nose as the\par
present mission was to her feelings; and when she had squeezed her hands\par
into tight gloves with three buttons and a tassel, as the last touch of\par
elegance, she turned to Amy with an imbecile expression of countenance,\par
saying meekly,--\par
\par
"I'm perfectly miserable; but if you consider me presentable, I die\par
happy."\par
\par
"You are highly satisfactory; turn slowly round, and let me get a\par
careful view." Jo revolved, and Amy gave a touch here and there, then\par
fell back, with her head on one side, observing graciously, "Yes, you'll\par
do; your head is all I could ask, for that white bonnet _with_ the rose\par
is quite ravishing. Hold back your shoulders, and carry your hands\par
easily, no matter if your gloves do pinch. There's one thing you can do\par
well, Jo, that is, wear a shawl--I can't; but it's very nice to see you,\par
and I'm so glad Aunt March gave you that lovely one; it's simple, but\par
handsome, and those folds over the arm are really artistic. Is the point\par
of my mantle in the middle, and have I looped my dress evenly? I like to\par
show my boots, for my feet _are_ pretty, though my nose isn't."\par
\par
"You are a thing of beauty and a joy forever," said Jo, looking through\par
her hand with the air of a connoisseur at the blue feather against the\par
gold hair. "Am I to drag my best dress through the dust, or loop it up,\par
please, ma'am?"\par
\par
"Hold it up when you walk, but drop it in the house; the sweeping style\par
suits you best, and you must learn to trail your skirts gracefully. You\par
haven't half buttoned one cuff; do it at once. You'll never look\par
finished if you are not careful about the little details, for they make\par
up the pleasing whole."\par
\par
Jo sighed, and proceeded to burst the buttons off her glove, in doing up\par
her cuff; but at last both were ready, and sailed away, looking as\par
"pretty as picters," Hannah said, as she hung out of the upper window to\par
watch them.\par
\par
"Now, Jo dear, the Chesters consider themselves very elegant people, so\par
I want you to put on your best deportment. Don't make any of your abrupt\par
remarks, or do anything odd, will you? Just be calm, cool, and\par
quiet,--that's safe and ladylike; and you can easily do it for fifteen\par
minutes," said Amy, as they approached the first place, having borrowed\par
the white parasol and been inspected by Meg, with a baby on each arm.\par
\par
"Let me see. 'Calm, cool, and quiet,'--yes, I think I can promise that.\par
I've played the part of a prim young lady on the stage, and I'll try it\par
off. My powers are great, as you shall see; so be easy in your mind, my\par
child."\par
\par
Amy looked relieved, but naughty Jo took her at her word; for, during\par
the first call, she sat with every limb gracefully composed, every fold\par
correctly draped, calm as a summer sea, cool as a snow-bank, and as\par
silent as a sphinx. In vain Mrs. Chester alluded to her "charming\par
novel," and the Misses Chester introduced parties, picnics, the opera,\par
and the fashions; each and all were answered by a smile, a bow, and a\par
demure "Yes" or "No," with the chill on. In vain Amy telegraphed the\par
word "Talk," tried to draw her out, and administered covert pokes with\par
her foot. Jo sat as if blandly unconscious of it all, with deportment\par
like Maud's face, "icily regular, splendidly null."\par
\par
"What a haughty, uninteresting creature that oldest Miss March is!" was\par
the unfortunately audible remark of one of the ladies, as the door\par
closed upon their guests. Jo laughed noiselessly all through the hall,\par
but Amy looked disgusted at the failure of her instructions, and very\par
naturally laid the blame upon Jo.\par
\par
"How could you mistake me so? I merely meant you to be properly\par
dignified and composed, and you made yourself a perfect stock and\par
stone. Try to be sociable at the Lambs', gossip as other girls do, and\par
be interested in dress and flirtations and whatever nonsense comes up.\par
They move in the best society, are valuable persons for us to know, and\par
I wouldn't fail to make a good impression there for anything."\par
\par
"I'll be agreeable; I'll gossip and giggle, and have horrors and\par
raptures over any trifle you like. I rather enjoy this, and now I'll\par
imitate what is called 'a charming girl;' I can do it, for I have May\par
Chester as a model, and I'll improve upon her. See if the Lambs don't\par
say, 'What a lively, nice creature that Jo March is!'"\par
\par
Amy felt anxious, as well she might, for when Jo turned freakish there\par
was no knowing where she would stop. Amy's face was a study when she saw\par
her sister skim into the next drawing-room, kiss all the young ladies\par
with effusion, beam graciously upon the young gentlemen, and join in the\par
chat with a spirit which amazed the beholder. Amy was taken possession\par
of by Mrs. Lamb, with whom she was a favorite, and forced to hear a long\par
account of Lucretia's last attack, while three delightful young\par
gentlemen hovered near, waiting for a pause when they might rush in and\par
rescue her. So situated, she was powerless to check Jo, who seemed\par
possessed by a spirit of mischief, and talked away as volubly as the old\par
lady. A knot of heads gathered about her, and Amy strained her ears to\par
hear what was going on; for broken sentences filled her with alarm,\par
round eyes and uplifted hands tormented her with curiosity, and frequent\par
peals of laughter made her wild to share the fun. One may imagine her\par
suffering on overhearing fragments of this sort of conversation:--\par
\par
"She rides splendidly,--who taught her?"\par
\par
"No one; she used to practise mounting, holding the reins, and sitting\par
straight on an old saddle in a tree. Now she rides anything, for she\par
doesn't know what fear is, and the stable-man lets her have horses\par
cheap, because she trains them to carry ladies so well. She has such a\par
passion for it, I often tell her if everything else fails she can be a\par
horse-breaker, and get her living so."\par
\par
At this awful speech Amy contained herself with difficulty, for the\par
impression was being given that she was rather a fast young lady, which\par
was her especial aversion. But what could she do? for the old lady was\par
in the middle of her story, and long before it was done Jo was off\par
again, making more droll revelations, and committing still more fearful\par
blunders.\par
\par
"Yes, Amy was in despair that day, for all the good beasts were gone,\par
and of three left, one was lame, one blind, and the other so balky that\par
you had to put dirt in his mouth before he would start. Nice animal for\par
a pleasure party, wasn't it?"\par
\par
"Which did she choose?" asked one of the laughing gentlemen, who enjoyed\par
the subject.\par
\par
"None of them; she heard of a young horse at the farmhouse over the\par
river, and, though a lady had never ridden him, she resolved to try,\par
because he was handsome and spirited. Her struggles were really\par
pathetic; there was no one to bring the horse to the saddle, so she took\par
the saddle to the horse. My dear creature, she actually rowed it over\par
the river, put it on her head, and marched up to the barn to the utter\par
amazement of the old man!"\par
\par
            [Illustration: She took the saddle to the horse]\par
\par
"Did she ride the horse?"\par
\par
"Of course she did, and had a capital time. I expected to see her\par
brought home in fragments, but she managed him perfectly, and was the\par
life of the party."\par
\par
"Well, I call that plucky!" and young Mr. Lamb turned an approving\par
glance upon Amy, wondering what his mother could be saying to make the\par
girl look so red and uncomfortable.\par
\par
She was still redder and more uncomfortable a moment after, when a\par
sudden turn in the conversation introduced the subject of dress. One of\par
the young ladies asked Jo where she got the pretty drab hat she wore to\par
the picnic; and stupid Jo, instead of mentioning the place where it was\par
bought two years ago, must needs answer, with unnecessary frankness,\par
"Oh, Amy painted it; you can't buy those soft shades, so we paint ours\par
any color we like. It's a great comfort to have an artistic sister."\par
\par
"Isn't that an original idea?" cried Miss Lamb, who found Jo great fun.\par
\par
"That's nothing compared to some of her brilliant performances. There's\par
nothing the child can't do. Why, she wanted a pair of blue boots for\par
Sallie's party, so she just painted her soiled white ones the loveliest\par
shade of sky-blue you ever saw, and they looked exactly like satin,"\par
added Jo, with an air of pride in her sister's accomplishments that\par
exasperated Amy till she felt that it would be a relief to throw her\par
card-case at her.\par
\par
"We read a story of yours the other day, and enjoyed it very much,"\par
observed the elder Miss Lamb, wishing to compliment the literary lady,\par
who did not look the character just then, it must be confessed.\par
\par
Any mention of her "works" always had a bad effect upon Jo, who either\par
grew rigid and looked offended, or changed the subject with a _brusque_\par
remark, as now. "Sorry you could find nothing better to read. I write\par
that rubbish because it sells, and ordinary people like it. Are you\par
going to New York this winter?"\par
\par
As Miss Lamb had "enjoyed" the story, this speech was not exactly\par
grateful or complimentary. The minute it was made Jo saw her mistake;\par
but, fearing to make the matter worse, suddenly remembered that it was\par
for her to make the first move toward departure, and did so with an\par
abruptness that left three people with half-finished sentences in their\par
mouths.\par
\par
"Amy, we _must_ go. _Good_-by, dear; _do_ come and see us; we are\par
_pining_ for a visit. I don't dare to ask _you_, Mr. Lamb; but if you\par
_should_ come, I don't think I shall have the heart to send you away."\par
\par
Jo said this with such a droll imitation of May Chester's gushing style\par
that Amy got out of the room as rapidly as possible, feeling a strong\par
desire to laugh and cry at the same time.\par
\par
"Didn't I do that well?" asked Jo, with a satisfied air, as they walked\par
away.\par
\par
"Nothing could have been worse," was Amy's crushing reply. "What\par
possessed you to tell those stories about my saddle, and the hats and\par
boots, and all the rest of it?"\par
\par
"Why, it's funny, and amuses people. They know we are poor, so it's no\par
use pretending that we have grooms, buy three or four hats a season, and\par
have things as easy and fine as they do."\par
\par
"You needn't go and tell them all our little shifts, and expose our\par
poverty in that perfectly unnecessary way. You haven't a bit of proper\par
pride, and never will learn when to hold your tongue and when to speak,"\par
said Amy despairingly.\par
\par
Poor Jo looked abashed, and silently chafed the end of her nose with the\par
stiff handkerchief, as if performing a penance for her misdemeanors.\par
\par
"How shall I behave here?" she asked, as they approached the third\par
mansion.\par
\par
"Just as you please; I wash my hands of you," was Amy's short answer.\par
\par
"Then I'll enjoy myself. The boys are at home, and we'll have a\par
comfortable time. Goodness knows I need a little change, for elegance\par
has a bad effect upon my constitution," returned Jo gruffly, being\par
disturbed by her failures to suit.\par
\par
An enthusiastic welcome from three big boys and several pretty children\par
speedily soothed her ruffled feelings; and, leaving Amy to entertain the\par
hostess and Mr. Tudor, who happened to be calling likewise, Jo devoted\par
herself to the young folks, and found the change refreshing. She\par
listened to college stories with deep interest, caressed pointers and\par
poodles without a murmur, agreed heartily that "Tom Brown was a brick,"\par
regardless of the improper form of praise; and when one lad proposed a\par
visit to his turtle-tank, she went with an alacrity which caused mamma\par
to smile upon her, as that motherly lady settled the cap which was left\par
in a ruinous condition by filial hugs, bear-like but affectionate, and\par
dearer to her than the most faultless _coiffure_ from the hands of an\par
inspired Frenchwoman.\par
\par
Leaving her sister to her own devices, Amy proceeded to enjoy herself to\par
her heart's content. Mr. Tudor's uncle had married an English lady who\par
was third cousin to a living lord, and Amy regarded the whole family\par
with great respect; for, in spite of her American birth and breeding,\par
she possessed that reverence for titles which haunts the best of\par
us,--that unacknowledged loyalty to the early faith in kings which set\par
the most democratic nation under the sun in a ferment at the coming of a\par
royal yellow-haired laddie, some years ago, and which still has\par
something to do with the love the young country bears the old, like that\par
of a big son for an imperious little mother, who held him while she\par
could, and let him go with a farewell scolding when he rebelled. But\par
even the satisfaction of talking with a distant connection of the\par
British nobility did not render Amy forgetful of time; and when the\par
proper number of minutes had passed, she reluctantly tore herself from\par
this aristocratic society, and looked about for Jo, fervently hoping\par
that her incorrigible sister would not be found in any position which\par
should bring disgrace upon the name of March.\par
\par
                [Illustration: It might have been worse]\par
\par
It might have been worse, but Amy considered it bad; for Jo sat on the\par
grass, with an encampment of boys about her, and a dirty-footed dog\par
reposing on the skirt of her state and festival dress, as she related\par
one of Laurie's pranks to her admiring audience. One small child was\par
poking turtles with Amy's cherished parasol, a second was eating\par
gingerbread over Jo's best bonnet, and a third playing ball with her\par
gloves. But all were enjoying themselves; and when Jo collected her\par
damaged property to go, her escort accompanied her, begging her to come\par
again, "it was such fun to hear about Laurie's larks."\par
\par
"Capital boys, aren't they? I feel quite young and brisk again after\par
that," said Jo, strolling along with her hands behind her, partly from\par
habit, partly to conceal the bespattered parasol.\par
\par
"Why do you always avoid Mr. Tudor?" asked Amy, wisely refraining from\par
any comment upon Jo's dilapidated appearance.\par
\par
"Don't like him; he puts on airs, snubs his sisters, worries his father,\par
and doesn't speak respectfully of his mother. Laurie says he is fast,\par
and _I_ don't consider him a desirable acquaintance; so I let him\par
alone."\par
\par
"You might treat him civilly, at least. You gave him a cool nod; and\par
just now you bowed and smiled in the politest way to Tommy Chamberlain,\par
whose father keeps a grocery store. If you had just reversed the nod and\par
the bow, it would have been right," said Amy reprovingly.\par
\par
"No, it wouldn't," returned perverse Jo; "I neither like, respect, nor\par
admire Tudor, though his grandfather's uncle's nephew's niece _was_\par
third cousin to a lord. Tommy is poor and bashful and good and very\par
clever; I think well of him, and like to show that I do, for he _is_ a\par
gentleman in spite of the brown-paper parcels."\par
\par
"It's no use trying to argue with you," began Amy.\par
\par
"Not the least, my dear," interrupted Jo; "so let us look amiable, and\par
drop a card here, as the Kings are evidently out, for which I'm deeply\par
grateful."\par
\par
The family card-case having done its duty, the girls walked on, and Jo\par
uttered another thanksgiving on reaching the fifth house, and being told\par
that the young ladies were engaged.\par
\par
"Now let us go home, and never mind Aunt March to-day. We can run down\par
there any time, and it's really a pity to trail through the dust in our\par
best bibs and tuckers, when we are tired and cross."\par
\par
"Speak for yourself, if you please. Aunt likes to have us pay her the\par
compliment of coming in style, and making a formal call; it's a little\par
thing to do, but it gives her pleasure, and I don't believe it will hurt\par
your things half so much as letting dirty dogs and clumping boys spoil\par
them. Stoop down, and let me take the crumbs off of your bonnet."\par
\par
"What a good girl you are, Amy!" said Jo, with a repentant glance from\par
her own damaged costume to that of her sister, which was fresh and\par
spotless still. "I wish it was as easy for me to do little things to\par
please people as it is for you. I think of them, but it takes too much\par
time to do them; so I wait for a chance to confer a great favor, and let\par
the small ones slip; but they tell best in the end, I fancy."\par
\par
Amy smiled, and was mollified at once, saying with a maternal air,--\par
\par
"Women should learn to be agreeable, particularly poor ones; for they\par
have no other way of repaying the kindnesses they receive. If you'd\par
remember that, and practise it, you'd be better liked than I am, because\par
there is more of you."\par
\par
"I'm a crotchety old thing, and always shall be, but I'm willing to own\par
that you are right; only it's easier for me to risk my life for a person\par
than to be pleasant to him when I don't feel like it. It's a great\par
misfortune to have such strong likes and dislikes, isn't it?"\par
\par
"It's a greater not to be able to hide them. I don't mind saying that I\par
don't approve of Tudor any more than you do; but I'm not called upon to\par
tell him so; neither are you, and there is no use in making yourself\par
disagreeable because he is."\par
\par
"But I think girls ought to show when they disapprove of young men; and\par
how can they do it except by their manners? Preaching does not do any\par
good, as I know to my sorrow, since I've had Teddy to manage; but there\par
are many little ways in which I can influence him without a word, and I\par
say we _ought_ to do it to others if we can."\par
\par
"Teddy is a remarkable boy, and can't be taken as a sample of other\par
boys," said Amy, in a tone of solemn conviction, which would have\par
convulsed the "remarkable boy," if he had heard it. "If we were belles,\par
or women of wealth and position, we might do something, perhaps; but for\par
us to frown at one set of young gentlemen because we don't approve of\par
them, and smile upon another set because we do, wouldn't have a particle\par
of effect, and we should only be considered odd and puritanical."\par
\par
"So we are to countenance things and people which we detest, merely\par
because we are not belles and millionaires, are we? That's a nice sort\par
of morality."\par
\par
"I can't argue about it, I only know that it's the way of the world; and\par
people who set themselves against it only get laughed at for their\par
pains. I don't like reformers, and I hope you will never try to be one."\par
\par
"I do like them, and I shall be one if I can; for in spite of the\par
laughing, the world would never get on without them. We can't agree\par
about that, for you belong to the old set, and I to the new: you will\par
get on the best, but I shall have the liveliest time of it. I should\par
rather enjoy the brickbats and hooting, I think."\par
\par
"Well, compose yourself now, and don't worry aunt with your new ideas."\par
\par
"I'll try not to, but I'm always possessed to burst out with some\par
particularly blunt speech or revolutionary sentiment before her; it's my\par
doom, and I can't help it."\par
\par
They found Aunt Carrol with the old lady, both absorbed in some very\par
interesting subject; but they dropped it as the girls came in, with a\par
conscious look which betrayed that they had been talking about their\par
nieces. Jo was not in a good humor, and the perverse fit returned; but\par
Amy, who had virtuously done her duty, kept her temper, and pleased\par
everybody, was in a most angelic frame of mind. This amiable spirit was\par
felt at once, and both the aunts "my deared" her affectionately, looking\par
what they afterwards said emphatically,--"That child improves every\par
day."\par
\par
"Are you going to help about the fair, dear?" asked Mrs. Carrol, as Amy\par
sat down beside her with the confiding air elderly people like so well\par
in the young.\par
\par
"Yes, aunt. Mrs. Chester asked me if I would, and I offered to tend a\par
table, as I have nothing but my time to give."\par
\par
"I'm not," put in Jo decidedly. "I hate to be patronized, and the\par
Chesters think it's a great favor to allow us to help with their highly\par
connected fair. I wonder you consented, Amy: they only want you to\par
work."\par
\par
"I am willing to work: it's for the freedmen as well as the Chesters,\par
and I think it very kind of them to let me share the labor and the fun.\par
Patronage does not trouble me when it is well meant."\par
\par
"Quite right and proper. I like your grateful spirit, my dear; it's a\par
pleasure to help people who appreciate our efforts: some do not, and\par
that is trying," observed Aunt March, looking over her spectacles at Jo,\par
who sat apart, rocking herself, with a somewhat morose expression.\par
\par
                [Illustration: The call at Aunt March's]\par
\par
If Jo had only known what a great happiness was wavering in the balance\par
for one of them, she would have turned dovelike in a minute; but,\par
unfortunately, we don't have windows in our breasts, and cannot see what\par
goes on in the minds of our friends; better for us that we cannot as a\par
general thing, but now and then it would be such a comfort, such a\par
saving of time and temper. By her next speech, Jo deprived herself of\par
several years of pleasure, and received a timely lesson in the art of\par
holding her tongue.\par
\par
"I don't like favors; they oppress and make me feel like a slave. I'd\par
rather do everything for myself, and be perfectly independent."\par
\par
"Ahem!" coughed Aunt Carrol softly, with a look at Aunt March.\par
\par
"I told you so," said Aunt March, with a decided nod to Aunt Carrol.\par
\par
Mercifully unconscious of what she had done, Jo sat with her nose in the\par
air, and a revolutionary aspect which was anything but inviting.\par
\par
"Do you speak French, dear?" asked Mrs. Carrol, laying her hand on\par
Amy's.\par
\par
"Pretty well, thanks to Aunt March, who lets Esther talk to me as often\par
as I like," replied Amy, with a grateful look, which caused the old lady\par
to smile affably.\par
\par
"How are you about languages?" asked Mrs. Carrol of Jo.\par
\par
"Don't know a word; I'm very stupid about studying anything; can't bear\par
French, it's such a slippery, silly sort of language," was the _brusque_\par
reply.\par
\par
Another look passed between the ladies, and Aunt March said to Amy, "You\par
are quite strong and well, now, dear, I believe? Eyes don't trouble you\par
any more, do they?"\par
\par
"Not at all, thank you, ma'am. I'm very well, and mean to do great\par
things next winter, so that I may be ready for Rome, whenever that\par
joyful time arrives."\par
\par
"Good girl! You deserve to go, and I'm sure you will some day," said\par
Aunt March, with an approving pat on the head, as Amy picked up her ball\par
for her.\par
\par
        "Cross-patch, draw the latch,\par
         Sit by the fire and spin,"\par
\par
squalled Polly, bending down from his perch on the back of her chair to\par
peep into Jo's face, with such a comical air of impertinent inquiry that\par
it was impossible to help laughing.\par
\par
"Most observing bird," said the old lady.\par
\par
"Come and take a walk, my dear?" cried Polly, hopping toward the\par
china-closet, with a look suggestive of lump-sugar.\par
\par
"Thank you, I will. Come, Amy;" and Jo brought the visit to an end,\par
feeling more strongly than ever that calls did have a bad effect upon\par
her constitution. She shook hands in a gentlemanly manner, but Amy\par
kissed both the aunts, and the girls departed, leaving behind them the\par
impression of shadow and sunshine; which impression caused Aunt March to\par
say, as they vanished,--\par
\par
"You'd better do it, Mary; I'll supply the money," and Aunt Carrol to\par
reply decidedly, "I certainly will, if her father and mother consent."\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
              [Illustration: You shall have another table]\par
\par
                                  XXX.\par
\par
                             CONSEQUENCES.\par
\par
\par
Mrs. Chester's fair was so very elegant and select that it was\par
considered a great honor by the young ladies of the neighborhood to be\par
invited to take a table, and every one was much interested in the\par
matter. Amy was asked, but Jo was not, which was fortunate for all\par
parties, as her elbows were decidedly akimbo at this period of her life,\par
and it took a good many hard knocks to teach her how to get on easily.\par
The "haughty, uninteresting creature" was let severely alone; but Amy's\par
talent and taste were duly complimented by the offer of the art-table,\par
and she exerted herself to prepare and secure appropriate and valuable\par
contributions to it.\par
\par
Everything went on smoothly till the day before the fair opened; then\par
there occurred one of the little skirmishes which it is almost\par
impossible to avoid, when some five and twenty women, old and young,\par
with all their private piques and prejudices, try to work together.\par
\par
May Chester was rather jealous of Amy because the latter was a greater\par
favorite than herself, and, just at this time, several trifling\par
circumstances occurred to increase the feeling. Amy's dainty pen-and-ink\par
work entirely eclipsed May's painted vases,--that was one thorn; then\par
the all-conquering Tudor had danced four times with Amy, at a late\par
party, and only once with May,--that was thorn number two; but the chief\par
grievance that rankled in her soul, and gave her an excuse for her\par
unfriendly conduct, was a rumor which some obliging gossip had whispered\par
to her, that the March girls had made fun of her at the Lambs'. All the\par
blame of this should have fallen upon Jo, for her naughty imitation had\par
been too lifelike to escape detection, and the frolicsome Lambs had\par
permitted the joke to escape. No hint of this had reached the culprits,\par
however, and Amy's dismay can be imagined, when, the very evening before\par
the fair, as she was putting the last touches to her pretty table, Mrs.\par
Chester, who, of course, resented the supposed ridicule of her daughter,\par
said, in a bland tone, but with a cold look,--\par
\par
"I find, dear, that there is some feeling among the young ladies about\par
my giving this table to any one but my girls. As this is the most\par
prominent, and some say the most attractive table of all, and they are\par
the chief getters-up of the fair, it is thought best for them to take\par
this place. I'm sorry, but I know you are too sincerely interested in\par
the cause to mind a little personal disappointment, and you shall have\par
another table if you like."\par
\par
Mrs. Chester had fancied beforehand that it would be easy to deliver\par
this little speech; but when the time came, she found it rather\par
difficult to utter it naturally, with Amy's unsuspicious eyes looking\par
straight at her, full of surprise and trouble.\par
\par
Amy felt that there was something behind this, but could not guess what,\par
and said quietly, feeling hurt, and showing that she did,--\par
\par
"Perhaps you had rather I took no table at all?"\par
\par
"Now, my dear, don't have any ill feeling, I beg; it's merely a matter\par
of expediency, you see; my girls will naturally take the lead, and this\par
table is considered their proper place. _I_ think it very appropriate\par
to you, and feel very grateful for your efforts to make it so pretty;\par
but we must give up our private wishes, of course, and I will see that\par
you have a good place elsewhere. Wouldn't you like the flower-table? The\par
little girls undertook it, but they are discouraged. You could make a\par
charming thing of it, and the flower-table is always attractive, you\par
know."\par
\par
"Especially to gentlemen," added May, with a look which enlightened Amy\par
as to one cause of her sudden fall from favor. She colored angrily, but\par
took no other notice of that girlish sarcasm, and answered, with\par
unexpected amiability,--\par
\par
"It shall be as you please, Mrs. Chester. I'll give up my place here at\par
once, and attend to the flowers, if you like."\par
\par
"You can put your own things on your own table, if you prefer," began\par
May, feeling a little conscience-stricken, as she looked at the pretty\par
racks, the painted shells, and quaint illuminations Amy had so carefully\par
made and so gracefully arranged. She meant it kindly, but Amy mistook\par
her meaning, and said quickly,--\par
\par
"Oh, certainly, if they are in your way;" and sweeping her contributions\par
into her apron, pell-mell, she walked off, feeling that herself and her\par
works of art had been insulted past forgiveness.\par
\par
"Now she's mad. Oh, dear, I wish I hadn't asked you to speak, mamma,"\par
said May, looking disconsolately at the empty spaces on her table.\par
\par
"Girls' quarrels are soon over," returned her mother, feeling a trifle\par
ashamed of her own part in this one, as well she might.\par
\par
The little girls hailed Amy and her treasures with delight, which\par
cordial reception somewhat soothed her perturbed spirit, and she fell to\par
work, determined to succeed florally, if she could not artistically. But\par
everything seemed against her: it was late, and she was tired; every one\par
was too busy with their own affairs to help her; and the little girls\par
were only hindrances, for the dears fussed and chattered like so many\par
magpies, making a great deal of confusion in their artless efforts to\par
preserve the most perfect order. The evergreen arch wouldn't stay firm\par
after she got it up, but wiggled and threatened to tumble down on her\par
head when the hanging baskets were filled; her best tile got a splash of\par
water, which left a sepia tear on the Cupid's cheek; she bruised her\par
hands with hammering, and got cold working in a draught, which last\par
affliction filled her with apprehensions for the morrow. Any girl-reader\par
who has suffered like afflictions will sympathize with poor Amy, and\par
wish her well through with her task.\par
\par
There was great indignation at home when she told her story that\par
evening. Her mother said it was a shame, but told her she had done\par
right; Beth declared she wouldn't go to the fair at all; and Jo demanded\par
why she didn't take all her pretty things and leave those mean people to\par
get on without her.\par
\par
"Because they are mean is no reason why I should be. I hate such things,\par
and though I think I've a right to be hurt, I don't intend to show it.\par
They will feel that more than angry speeches or huffy actions, won't\par
they, Marmee?"\par
\par
"That's the right spirit, my dear; a kiss for a blow is always best,\par
though it's not very easy to give it sometimes," said her mother, with\par
the air of one who had learned the difference between preaching and\par
practising.\par
\par
In spite of various very natural temptations to resent and retaliate,\par
Amy adhered to her resolution all the next day, bent on conquering her\par
enemy by kindness. She began well, thanks to a silent reminder that came\par
to her unexpectedly, but most opportunely. As she arranged her table\par
that morning, while the little girls were in an ante-room filling the\par
baskets, she took up her pet production,--a little book, the antique\par
cover of which her father had found among his treasures, and in which,\par
on leaves of vellum, she had beautifully illuminated different texts. As\par
she turned the pages, rich in dainty devices, with very pardonable\par
pride, her eye fell upon one verse that made her stop and think. Framed\par
in a brilliant scroll-work of scarlet, blue, and gold, with little\par
spirits of good-will helping one another up and down among the thorns\par
and flowers, were the words, "Thou shalt love thy neighbor as thyself."\par
\par
"I ought, but I don't," thought Amy, as her eye went from the bright\par
page to May's discontented face behind the big vases, that could not\par
hide the vacancies her pretty work had once filled. Amy stood a minute,\par
turning the leaves in her hand, reading on each some sweet rebuke for\par
all heart-burnings and uncharitableness of spirit. Many wise and true\par
sermons are preached us every day by unconscious ministers in street,\par
school, office, or home; even a fair-table may become a pulpit, if it\par
can offer the good and helpful words which are never out of season.\par
Amy's conscience preached her a little sermon from that text, then and\par
there; and she did what many of us do not always do,--took the sermon to\par
heart, and straightway put it in practice.\par
\par
A group of girls were standing about May's table, admiring the pretty\par
things, and talking over the change of saleswomen. They dropped their\par
voices, but Amy knew they were speaking of her, hearing one side of the\par
story, and judging accordingly. It was not pleasant, but a better spirit\par
had come over her, and presently a chance offered for proving it. She\par
heard May say sorrowfully,--\par
\par
"It's too bad, for there is no time to make other things, and I don't\par
want to fill up with odds and ends. The table was just complete then:\par
now it's spoilt."\par
\par
"I dare say she'd put them back if you asked her," suggested some one.\par
\par
"How could I after all the fuss?" began May, but she did not finish, for\par
Amy's voice came across the hall, saying pleasantly,--\par
\par
"You may have them, and welcome, without asking, if you want them. I was\par
just thinking I'd offer to put them back, for they belong to your table\par
rather than mine. Here they are; please take them, and forgive me if I\par
was hasty in carrying them away last night."\par
\par
As she spoke, Amy returned her contribution, with a nod and a smile, and\par
hurried away again, feeling that it was easier to do a friendly thing\par
than it was to stay and be thanked for it.\par
\par
"Now, I call that lovely of her, don't you?" cried one girl.\par
\par
May's answer was inaudible; but another young lady, whose temper was\par
evidently a little soured by making lemonade, added, with a disagreeable\par
laugh, "Very lovely; for she knew she wouldn't sell them at her own\par
table."\par
\par
Now, that was hard; when we make little sacrifices we like to have them\par
appreciated, at least; and for a minute Amy was sorry she had done it,\par
feeling that virtue was not always its own reward. But it is,--as she\par
presently discovered; for her spirits began to rise, and her table to\par
blossom under her skilful hands; the girls were very kind, and that one\par
little act seemed to have cleared the atmosphere amazingly.\par
\par
It was a very long day, and a hard one to Amy, as she sat behind her\par
table, often quite alone, for the little girls deserted very soon: few\par
cared to buy flowers in summer, and her bouquets began to droop long\par
before night.\par
\par
The art-table _was_ the most attractive in the room; there was a crowd\par
about it all day long, and the tenders were constantly flying to and fro\par
with important faces and rattling money-boxes. Amy often looked\par
wistfully across, longing to be there, where she felt at home and happy,\par
instead of in a corner with nothing to do. It might seem no hardship to\par
some of us; but to a pretty, blithe young girl, it was not only tedious,\par
but very trying; and the thought of being found there in the evening by\par
her family, and Laurie and his friends, made it a real martyrdom.\par
\par
She did not go home till night, and then she looked so pale and quiet\par
that they knew the day had been a hard one, though she made no\par
complaint, and did not even tell what she had done. Her mother gave her\par
an extra cordial cup of tea, Beth helped her dress, and made a charming\par
little wreath for her hair, while Jo astonished her family by getting\par
herself up with unusual care, and hinting darkly that the tables were\par
about to be turned.\par
\par
"Don't do anything rude, pray, Jo. I won't have any fuss made, so let it\par
all pass, and behave yourself," begged Amy, as she departed early,\par
hoping to find a reinforcement of flowers to refresh her poor little\par
table.\par
\par
"I merely intend to make myself entrancingly agreeable to every one I\par
know, and to keep them in your corner as long as possible. Teddy and his\par
boys will lend a hand, and we'll have a good time yet," returned Jo,\par
leaning over the gate to watch for Laurie. Presently the familiar tramp\par
was heard in the dusk, and she ran out to meet him.\par
\par
"Is that my boy?"\par
\par
"As sure as this is my girl!" and Laurie tucked her hand under his arm,\par
with the air of a man whose every wish was gratified.\par
\par
"O Teddy, such doings!" and Jo told Amy's wrongs with sisterly zeal.\par
\par
"A flock of our fellows are going to drive over by and by, and I'll be\par
hanged if I don't make them buy every flower she's got, and camp down\par
before her table afterward," said Laurie, espousing her cause with\par
warmth.\par
\par
"The flowers are not at all nice, Amy says, and the fresh ones may not\par
arrive in time. I don't wish to be unjust or suspicious, but I shouldn't\par
wonder if they never came at all. When people do one mean thing they are\par
very likely to do another," observed Jo, in a disgusted tone.\par
\par
"Didn't Hayes give you the best out of our gardens? I told him to."\par
\par
"I didn't know that; he forgot, I suppose; and, as your grandpa was\par
poorly, I didn't like to worry him by asking, though I did want some."\par
\par
"Now, Jo, how could you think there was any need of asking! They are\par
just as much yours as mine. Don't we always go halves in everything?"\par
began Laurie, in the tone that always made Jo turn thorny.\par
\par
"Gracious, I hope not! half of some of your things wouldn't suit me at\par
all. But we mustn't stand philandering here; I've got to help Amy, so\par
you go and make yourself splendid; and if you'll be so very kind as to\par
let Hayes take a few nice flowers up to the Hall, I'll bless you\par
forever."\par
\par
"Couldn't you do it now?" asked Laurie, so suggestively that Jo shut the\par
gate in his face with inhospitable haste, and called through the bars,\par
"Go away, Teddy; I'm busy."\par
\par
Thanks to the conspirators, the tables _were_ turned that night; for\par
Hayes sent up a wilderness of flowers, with a lovely basket, arranged in\par
his best manner, for a centre-piece; then the March family turned out\par
_en masse_, and Jo exerted herself to some purpose, for people not only\par
came, but stayed, laughing at her nonsense, admiring Amy's taste, and\par
apparently enjoying themselves very much. Laurie and his friends\par
gallantly threw themselves into the breach, bought up the bouquets,\par
encamped before the table, and made that corner the liveliest spot in\par
the room. Amy was in her element now, and, out of gratitude, if nothing\par
more, was as sprightly and gracious as possible,--coming to the\par
conclusion, about that time, that virtue _was_ its own reward, after\par
all.\par
\par
                 [Illustration: Bought up the bouquets]\par
\par
Jo behaved herself with exemplary propriety; and when Amy was happily\par
surrounded by her guard of honor, Jo circulated about the hall, picking\par
up various bits of gossip, which enlightened her upon the subject of the\par
Chester change of base. She reproached herself for her share of the\par
ill-feeling, and resolved to exonerate Amy as soon as possible; she also\par
discovered what Amy had done about the things in the morning, and\par
considered her a model of magnanimity. As she passed the art-table, she\par
glanced over it for her sister's things, but saw no signs of them.\par
"Tucked away out of sight, I dare say," thought Jo, who could forgive\par
her own wrongs, but hotly resented any insult offered to her family.\par
\par
"Good evening, Miss Jo. How does Amy get on?" asked May, with a\par
conciliatory air, for she wanted to show that she also could be\par
generous.\par
\par
"She has sold everything she had that was worth selling, and now she is\par
enjoying herself. The flower-table is always attractive, you know,\par
'especially to gentlemen.'"\par
\par
Jo _couldn't_ resist giving that little slap, but May took it so meekly\par
she regretted it a minute after, and fell to praising the great vases,\par
which still remained unsold.\par
\par
"Is Amy's illumination anywhere about? I took a fancy to buy that for\par
father," said Jo, very anxious to learn the fate of her sister's work.\par
\par
"Everything of Amy's sold long ago; I took care that the right people\par
saw them, and they made a nice little sum of money for us," returned\par
May, who had overcome sundry small temptations, as well as Amy, that\par
day.\par
\par
Much gratified, Jo rushed back to tell the good news; and Amy looked\par
both touched and surprised by the report of May's words and manner.\par
\par
"Now, gentlemen, I want you to go and do your duty by the other tables\par
as generously as you have by mine--especially the art-table," she said,\par
ordering out "Teddy's Own," as the girls called the college friends.\par
\par
"'Charge, Chester, charge!' is the motto for that table; but do your\par
duty like men, and you'll get your money's worth of _art_ in every sense\par
of the word," said the irrepressible Jo, as the devoted phalanx prepared\par
to take the field.\par
\par
"To hear is to obey, but March is fairer far than May," said little\par
Parker, making a frantic effort to be both witty and tender, and getting\par
promptly quenched by Laurie, who said, "Very well, my son, for a small\par
boy!" and walked him off, with a paternal pat on the head.\par
\par
"Buy the vases," whispered Amy to Laurie, as a final heaping of coals of\par
fire on her enemy's head.\par
\par
To May's great delight, Mr. Laurence not only bought the vases, but\par
pervaded the hall with one under each arm. The other gentlemen\par
speculated with equal rashness in all sorts of frail trifles, and\par
wandered helplessly about afterward, burdened with wax flowers, painted\par
fans, filigree portfolios, and other useful and appropriate purchases.\par
\par
Aunt Carrol was there, heard the story, looked pleased, and said\par
something to Mrs. March in a corner, which made the latter lady beam\par
with satisfaction, and watch Amy with a face full of mingled pride and\par
anxiety, though she did not betray the cause of her pleasure till\par
several days later.\par
\par
The fair was pronounced a success; and when May bade Amy good night, she\par
did not "gush" as usual, but gave her an affectionate kiss, and a look\par
which said, "Forgive and forget." That satisfied Amy; and when she got\par
home she found the vases paraded on the parlor chimney-piece, with a\par
great bouquet in each. "The reward of merit for a magnanimous March," as\par
Laurie announced with a flourish.\par
\par
"You've a deal more principle and generosity and nobleness of character\par
than I ever gave you credit for, Amy. You've behaved sweetly, and I\par
respect you with all my heart," said Jo warmly, as they brushed their\par
hair together late that night.\par
\par
"Yes, we all do, and love her for being so ready to forgive. It must\par
have been dreadfully hard, after working so long, and setting your heart\par
on selling your own pretty things. I don't believe I could have done it\par
as kindly as you did," added Beth from her pillow.\par
\par
"Why, girls, you needn't praise me so; I only did as I'd be done by. You\par
laugh at me when I say I want to be a lady, but I mean a true\par
gentlewoman in mind and manners, and I try to do it as far as I know\par
how. I can't explain exactly, but I want to be above the little\par
meannesses and follies and faults that spoil so many women. I'm far from\par
it now, but I do my best, and hope in time to be what mother is."\par
\par
Amy spoke earnestly, and Jo said, with a cordial hug,--\par
\par
"I understand now what you mean, and I'll never laugh at you again. You\par
are getting on faster than you think, and I'll take lessons of you in\par
true politeness, for you've learned the secret, I believe. Try away,\par
deary; you'll get your reward some day, and no one will be more\par
delighted than I shall."\par
\par
A week later Amy did get her reward, and poor Jo found it hard to be\par
delighted. A letter came from Aunt Carrol, and Mrs. March's face was\par
illuminated to such a degree, when she read it, that Jo and Beth, who\par
were with her, demanded what the glad tidings were.\par
\par
"Aunt Carrol is going abroad next month, and wants--"\par
\par
"Me to go with her!" burst in Jo, flying out of her chair in an\par
uncontrollable rapture.\par
\par
"No, dear, not you; it's Amy."\par
\par
"O mother! she's too young; it's my turn first. I've wanted it so\par
long--it would do me so much good, and be so altogether splendid--I\par
_must_ go."\par
\par
"I'm afraid it's impossible, Jo. Aunt says Amy, decidedly, and it is not\par
for us to dictate when she offers such a favor."\par
\par
"It's always so. Amy has all the fun and I have all the work. It isn't\par
fair, oh, it isn't fair!" cried Jo passionately.\par
\par
"I'm afraid it is partly your own fault, dear. When Aunt spoke to me the\par
other day, she regretted your blunt manners and too independent spirit;\par
and here she writes, as if quoting something you had said,--'I planned\par
at first to ask Jo; but as "favors burden her," and she "hates French,"\par
I think I won't venture to invite her. Amy is more docile, will make a\par
good companion for Flo, and receive gratefully any help the trip may\par
give her.'"\par
\par
"Oh, my tongue, my abominable tongue! why can't I learn to keep it\par
quiet?" groaned Jo, remembering words which had been her undoing. When\par
she had heard the explanation of the quoted phrases, Mrs. March said\par
sorrowfully,--\par
\par
"I wish you could have gone, but there is no hope of it this time; so\par
try to bear it cheerfully, and don't sadden Amy's pleasure by reproaches\par
or regrets."\par
\par
"I'll try," said Jo, winking hard, as she knelt down to pick up the\par
basket she had joyfully upset. "I'll take a leaf out of her book, and\par
try not only to seem glad, but to be so, and not grudge her one minute\par
of happiness; but it won't be easy, for it is a dreadful\par
disappointment;" and poor Jo bedewed the little fat pincushion she held\par
with several very bitter tears.\par
\par
"Jo, dear, I'm very selfish, but I couldn't spare you, and I'm glad you\par
are not going quite yet," whispered Beth, embracing her, basket and all,\par
with such a clinging touch and loving face, that Jo felt comforted in\par
spite of the sharp regret that made her want to box her own ears, and\par
humbly beg Aunt Carrol to burden her with this favor, and see how\par
gratefully she would bear it.\par
\par
By the time Amy came in, Jo was able to take her part in the family\par
jubilation; not quite as heartily as usual, perhaps, but without\par
repinings at Amy's good fortune. The young lady herself received the\par
news as tidings of great joy, went about in a solemn sort of rapture,\par
and began to sort her colors and pack her pencils that evening, leaving\par
such trifles as clothes, money, and passports to those less absorbed in\par
visions of art than herself.\par
\par
"It isn't a mere pleasure trip to me, girls," she said impressively, as\par
she scraped her best palette. "It will decide my career; for if I have\par
any genius, I shall find it out in Rome, and will do something to prove\par
it."\par
\par
"Suppose you haven't?" said Jo, sewing away, with red eyes, at the new\par
collars which were to be handed over to Amy.\par
\par
"Then I shall come home and teach drawing for my living," replied the\par
aspirant for fame, with philosophic composure; but she made a wry face\par
at the prospect, and scratched away at her palette as if bent on\par
vigorous measures before she gave up her hopes.\par
\par
"No, you won't; you hate hard work, and you'll marry some rich man, and\par
come home to sit in the lap of luxury all your days," said Jo.\par
\par
"Your predictions sometimes come to pass, but I don't believe that one\par
will. I'm sure I wish it would, for if I can't be an artist myself, I\par
should like to be able to help those who are," said Amy, smiling, as if\par
the part of Lady Bountiful would suit her better than that of a poor\par
drawing-teacher.\par
\par
"Hum!" said Jo, with a sigh; "if you wish it you'll have it, for your\par
wishes are always granted--mine never."\par
\par
"Would you like to go?" asked Amy, thoughtfully patting her nose with\par
her knife.\par
\par
"Rather!"\par
\par
"Well, in a year or two I'll send for you, and we'll dig in the Forum\par
for relics, and carry out all the plans we've made so many times."\par
\par
"Thank you; I'll remind you of your promise when that joyful day comes,\par
if it ever does," returned Jo, accepting the vague but magnificent offer\par
as gratefully as she could.\par
\par
There was not much time for preparation, and the house was in a ferment\par
till Amy was off. Jo bore up very well till the last flutter of blue\par
ribbon vanished, when she retired to her refuge, the garret, and cried\par
till she couldn't cry any more. Amy likewise bore up stoutly till the\par
steamer sailed; then, just as the gangway was about to be withdrawn, it\par
suddenly came over her that a whole ocean was soon to roll between her\par
and those who loved her best, and she clung to Laurie, the last\par
lingerer, saying with a sob,--\par
\par
"Oh, take care of them for me; and if anything should happen--"\par
\par
"I will, dear, I will; and if anything happens, I'll come and comfort\par
you," whispered Laurie, little dreaming that he would be called upon to\par
keep his word.\par
\par
So Amy sailed away to find the old world, which is always new and\par
beautiful to young eyes, while her father and friend watched her from\par
the shore, fervently hoping that none but gentle fortunes would befall\par
the happy-hearted girl, who waved her hand to them till they could see\par
nothing but the summer sunshine dazzling on the sea.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
             [Illustration: Flo and I ordered a hansom-cab]\par
\par
                                 XXXI.\par
\par
                       OUR FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT.\par
\par
\par
                                                    "LONDON.\par
    "DEAREST PEOPLE,--\par
\par
    "Here I really sit at a front window of the Bath Hotel,\par
    Piccadilly. It's not a fashionable place, but uncle stopped here\par
    years ago, and won't go anywhere else; however, we don't mean to\par
    stay long, so it's no great matter. Oh, I can't begin to tell\par
    you how I enjoy it all! I never can, so I'll only give you bits\par
    out of my note-book, for I've done nothing but sketch and\par
    scribble since I started.\par
\par
    "I sent a line from Halifax, when I felt pretty miserable, but\par
    after that I got on delightfully, seldom ill, on deck all day,\par
    with plenty of pleasant people to amuse me. Every one was very\par
    kind to me, especially the officers. Don't laugh, Jo; gentlemen\par
    really are very necessary aboard ship, to hold on to, or to wait\par
    upon one; and as they have nothing to do, it's a mercy to make\par
    them useful, otherwise they would smoke themselves to death, I'm\par
    afraid.\par
\par
        [Illustration: "Every one was very kind, especially the\par
                         officers."--Page 378.]\par
\par
    "Aunt and Flo were poorly all the way, and liked to be let\par
    alone, so when I had done what I could for them, I went and\par
    enjoyed myself. Such walks on deck, such sunsets, such splendid\par
    air and waves! It was almost as exciting as riding a fast\par
    horse, when we went rushing on so grandly. I wish Beth could\par
    have come, it would have done her so much good; as for Jo, she\par
    would have gone up and sat on the main-top jib, or whatever the\par
    high thing is called, made friends with the engineers, and\par
    tooted on the captain's speaking-trumpet, she'd have been in\par
    such a state of rapture.\par
\par
    "It was all heavenly, but I was glad to see the Irish coast, and\par
    found it very lovely, so green and sunny, with brown cabins here\par
    and there, ruins on some of the hills, and gentlemen's\par
    country-seats in the valleys, with deer feeding in the parks. It\par
    was early in the morning, but I didn't regret getting up to see\par
    it, for the bay was full of little boats, the shore _so_\par
    picturesque, and a rosy sky overhead. I never shall forget it.\par
\par
    "At Queenstown one of my new acquaintances left us,--Mr.\par
    Lennox,--and when I said something about the Lakes of Killarney,\par
    he sighed and sung, with a look at me,--\par
\par
        'Oh, have you e'er heard of Kate Kearney?\par
         She lives on the banks of Killarney;\par
         From the glance of her eye,\par
         Shun danger and fly,\par
         For fatal's the glance of Kate Kearney.'\par
\par
    Wasn't that nonsensical?\par
\par
    "We only stopped at Liverpool a few hours. It's a dirty, noisy\par
    place, and I was glad to leave it. Uncle rushed out and bought a\par
    pair of dog-skin gloves, some ugly, thick shoes, and an\par
    umbrella, and got shaved _à la_ mutton-chop, the first thing.\par
    Then he flattered himself that he looked like a true Briton; but\par
    the first time he had the mud cleaned off his shoes, the little\par
    bootblack knew that an American stood in them, and said, with a\par
    grin, 'There yer har, sir. I've give 'em the latest Yankee\par
    shine.' It amused uncle immensely. Oh, I _must_ tell you what\par
    that absurd Lennox did! He got his friend Ward, who came on with\par
    us, to order a bouquet for me, and the first thing I saw in my\par
    room was a lovely one, with 'Robert Lennox's compliments,' on\par
    the card. Wasn't that fun, girls? I like travelling.\par
\par
    "I never _shall_ get to London if I don't hurry. The trip was\par
    like riding through a long picture-gallery, full of lovely\par
    landscapes. The farmhouses were my delight; with thatched roofs,\par
    ivy up to the eaves, latticed windows, and stout women with rosy\par
    children at the doors. The very cattle looked more tranquil than\par
    ours, as they stood knee-deep in clover, and the hens had a\par
    contented cluck, as if they never got nervous, like Yankee\par
    biddies. Such perfect color I never saw,--the grass so green,\par
    sky so blue, grain so yellow, woods so dark,--I was in a rapture\par
    all the way. So was Flo; and we kept bouncing from one side to\par
    the other, trying to see everything while we were whisking along\par
    at the rate of sixty miles an hour. Aunt was tired and went to\par
    sleep, but uncle read his guide-book, and wouldn't be astonished\par
    at anything. This is the way we went on: Amy, flying up,--'Oh,\par
    that must be Kenilworth, that gray place among the trees!' Flo,\par
    darting to my window,--'How sweet! We must go there some time,\par
    won't we, papa?' Uncle, calmly admiring his boots,--'No, my\par
    dear, not unless you want beer; that's a brewery.'\par
\par
    "A pause,--then Flo cried out, 'Bless me, there's a gallows and\par
    a man going up.' 'Where, where?' shrieks Amy, staring out at two\par
    tall posts with a cross-beam and some dangling chains. 'A\par
    colliery,' remarks uncle, with a twinkle of the eye. 'Here's a\par
    lovely flock of lambs all lying down,' says Amy. 'See, papa,\par
    aren't they pretty!' added Flo sentimentally. 'Geese, young\par
    ladies,' returns uncle, in a tone that keeps us quiet till Flo\par
    settles down to enjoy 'The Flirtations of Capt. Cavendish,' and\par
    I have the scenery all to myself.\par
\par
    "Of course it rained when we got to London, and there was\par
    nothing to be seen but fog and umbrellas. We rested, unpacked,\par
    and shopped a little between the showers. Aunt Mary got me some\par
    new things, for I came off in such a hurry I wasn't half ready.\par
    A white hat and blue feather, a muslin dress to match, and the\par
    loveliest mantle you ever saw. Shopping in Regent Street is\par
    perfectly splendid; things seem so cheap--nice ribbons only\par
    sixpence a yard. I laid in a stock, but shall get my gloves in\par
    Paris. Doesn't that sound sort of elegant and rich?\par
\par
    "Flo and I, for the fun of it, ordered a hansom cab, while aunt\par
    and uncle were out, and went for a drive, though we learned\par
    afterward that it wasn't the thing for young ladies to ride in\par
    them alone. It was so droll! for when we were shut in by the\par
    wooden apron, the man drove so fast that Flo was frightened, and\par
    told me to stop him. But he was up outside behind somewhere, and\par
    I couldn't get at him. He didn't hear me call, nor see me flap\par
    my parasol in front, and there we were, quite helpless, rattling\par
    away, and whirling around corners at a break-neck pace. At last,\par
    in my despair, I saw a little door in the roof, and on poking it\par
    open, a red eye appeared, and a beery voice said,--\par
\par
    "'Now then, mum?'\par
\par
    "I gave my order as soberly as I could, and slamming down the\par
    door, with an 'Aye, aye, mum,' the man made his horse walk, as\par
    if going to a funeral. I poked again, and said, 'A little\par
    faster;' then off he went, helter-skelter, as before, and we\par
    resigned ourselves to our fate.\par
\par
    "To-day was fair and we went to Hyde Park, close by, for we are\par
    more aristocratic than we look. The Duke of Devonshire lives\par
    near. I often see his footmen lounging at the back gate; and the\par
    Duke of Wellington's house is not far off. Such sights as I saw,\par
    my dear! It was as good as Punch, for there were fat dowagers\par
    rolling about in their red and yellow coaches, with gorgeous\par
    Jeameses in silk stockings and velvet coats, up behind, and\par
    powdered coachmen in front. Smart maids, with the rosiest\par
    children I ever saw; handsome girls, looking half asleep;\par
    dandies, in queer English hats and lavender kids, lounging\par
    about, and tall soldiers, in short red jackets and muffin caps\par
    stuck on one side, looking so funny I longed to sketch them.\par
\par
    "Rotten Row means '_Route de Roi_,' or the king's way; but now\par
    it's more like a riding-school than anything else. The horses\par
    are splendid, and the men, especially the grooms, ride well; but\par
    the women are stiff, and bounce, which isn't according to our\par
    rules. I longed to show them a tearing American gallop, for they\par
    trotted solemnly up and down, in their scant habits and high\par
    hats, looking like the women in a toy Noah's Ark. Every one\par
    rides,--old men, stout ladies, little children,--and the young\par
    folks do a deal of flirting here; I saw a pair exchange\par
    rosebuds, for it's the thing to wear one in the button-hole, and\par
    I thought it rather a nice little idea.\par
\par
    "In the P.M. to Westminster Abbey; but don't expect me to\par
    describe it, that's impossible--so I'll only say it was sublime!\par
    This evening we are going to see Fechter, which will be an\par
    appropriate end to the happiest day of my life.\par
\par
                                                          "MIDNIGHT.\par
\par
    "It's very late, but I can't let my letter go in the morning\par
    without telling you what happened last evening. Who do you think\par
    came in, as we were at tea? Laurie's English friends, Fred and\par
    Frank Vaughn! I was _so_ surprised, for I shouldn't have known\par
    them but for the cards. Both are tall fellows, with whiskers;\par
    Fred handsome in the English style, and Frank much better, for\par
    he only limps slightly, and uses no crutches. They had heard\par
    from Laurie where we were to be, and came to ask us to their\par
    house; but uncle won't go, so we shall return the call, and see\par
    them as we can. They went to the theatre with us, and we did\par
    have _such_ a good time, for Frank devoted himself to Flo, and\par
    Fred and I talked over past, present, and future fun as if we\par
    had known each other all our days. Tell Beth Frank asked for\par
    her, and was sorry to hear of her ill health. Fred laughed when\par
    I spoke of Jo, and sent his 'respectful compliments to the big\par
    hat.' Neither of them had forgotten Camp Laurence, or the fun we\par
    had there. What ages ago it seems, doesn't it?\par
\par
    "Aunt is tapping on the wall for the third time, so I _must_\par
    stop. I really feel like a dissipated London fine lady, writing\par
    here so late, with my room full of pretty things, and my head a\par
    jumble of parks, theatres, new gowns, and gallant creatures who\par
    say 'Ah!' and twirl their blond mustaches with the true English\par
    lordliness. I long to see you all, and in spite of my nonsense\par
    am, as ever, your loving\par
                                                               AMY."\par
\par
\par
    "DEAR GIRLS,--                                           "PARIS.\par
\par
    "In my last I told you about our London visit,--how kind the\par
    Vaughns were, and what pleasant parties they made for us. I\par
    enjoyed the trips to Hampton Court and the Kensington Museum\par
    more than anything else,--for at Hampton I saw Raphael's\par
    cartoons, and, at the Museum, rooms full of pictures by Turner,\par
    Lawrence, Reynolds, Hogarth, and the other great creatures. The\par
    day in Richmond Park was charming, for we had a regular English\par
    picnic, and I had more splendid oaks and groups of deer than I\par
    could copy; also heard a nightingale, and saw larks go up. We\par
    'did' London to our hearts' content, thanks to Fred and Frank,\par
    and were sorry to go away; for, though English people are slow\par
    to take you in, when they once make up their minds to do it they\par
    cannot be outdone in hospitality, _I_ think. The Vaughns hope to\par
    meet us in Rome next winter, and I shall be dreadfully\par
    disappointed if they don't, for Grace and I are great friends,\par
    and the boys very nice fellows,--especially Fred.\par
\par
    "Well, we were hardly settled here, when he turned up again,\par
    saying he had come for a holiday, and was going to Switzerland.\par
    Aunt looked sober at first, but he was so cool about it she\par
    couldn't say a word; and now we get on nicely, and are very glad\par
    he came, for he speaks French like a native, and I don't know\par
    what we should do without him. Uncle doesn't know ten words, and\par
    insists on talking English very loud, as if that would make\par
    people understand him. Aunt's pronunciation is old-fashioned,\par
    and Flo and I, though we flattered ourselves that we knew a good\par
    deal, find we don't, and are very grateful to have Fred do the\par
    '_parley vooing_,' as uncle calls it.\par
\par
    "Such delightful times as we are having! sight-seeing from\par
    morning till night, stopping for nice lunches in the gay\par
    _cafés_, and meeting with all sorts of droll adventures. Rainy\par
    days I spend in the Louvre, revelling in pictures. Jo would turn\par
    up her naughty nose at some of the finest, because she has no\par
    soul for art; but _I_ have, and I'm cultivating eye and taste as\par
    fast as I can. She would like the relics of great people better,\par
    for I've seen her Napoleon's cocked hat and gray coat, his\par
    baby's cradle and his old toothbrush; also Marie Antoinette's\par
    little shoe, the ring of Saint Denis, Charlemagne's sword, and\par
    many other interesting things. I'll talk for hours about them\par
    when I come, but haven't time to write.\par
\par
    "The Palais Royale is a heavenly place,--so full of _bijouterie_\par
    and lovely things that I'm nearly distracted because I can't buy\par
    them. Fred wanted to get me some, but of course I didn't allow\par
    it. Then the Bois and the Champs Elysées are _très magnifique_.\par
    I've seen the imperial family several times,--the emperor an\par
    ugly, hard-looking man, the empress pale and pretty, but dressed\par
    in bad taste, _I_ thought,--purple dress, green hat, and yellow\par
    gloves. Little Nap. is a handsome boy, who sits chatting to his\par
    tutor, and kisses his hand to the people as he passes in his\par
    four-horse barouche, with postilions in red satin jackets, and a\par
    mounted guard before and behind.\par
\par
      [Illustration: I've seen the imperial family several times]\par
\par
    "We often walk in the Tuileries Gardens, for they are lovely,\par
    though the antique Luxembourg Gardens suit me better. Père la\par
    Chaise is very curious, for many of the tombs are like small\par
    rooms, and, looking in, one sees a table, with images or\par
    pictures of the dead, and chairs for the mourners to sit in\par
    when they come to lament. That is so Frenchy.\par
\par
    "Our rooms are on the Rue de Rivoli, and, sitting in the\par
    balcony, we look up and down the long, brilliant street. It is\par
    so pleasant that we spend our evenings talking there, when too\par
    tired with our day's work to go out. Fred is very entertaining,\par
    and is altogether the most agreeable young man I ever\par
    knew,--except Laurie, whose manners are more charming. I wish\par
    Fred was dark, for I don't fancy light men; however, the Vaughns\par
    are very rich, and come of an excellent family, so I won't find\par
    fault with their yellow hair, as my own is yellower.\par
\par
    "Next week we are off to Germany and Switzerland; and, as we\par
    shall travel fast, I shall only be able to give you hasty\par
    letters. I keep my diary, and try to 'remember correctly and\par
    describe clearly all that I see and admire,' as father advised.\par
    It is good practice for me, and, with my sketch-book, will give\par
    you a better idea of my tour than these scribbles.\par
\par
    "Adieu; I embrace you tenderly.                     VOTRE AMIE."\par
\par
\par
    "MY DEAR MAMMA,--                                   "HEIDELBERG.\par
\par
    "Having a quiet hour before we leave for Berne, I'll try to tell\par
    you what has happened, for some of it is very important, as you\par
    will see.\par
\par
    "The sail up the Rhine was perfect, and I just sat and enjoyed\par
    it with all my might. Get father's old guide-books, and read\par
    about it; I haven't words beautiful enough to describe it. At\par
    Coblentz we had a lovely time, for some students from Bonn, with\par
    whom Fred got acquainted on the boat, gave us a serenade. It was\par
    a moonlight night, and, about one o'clock, Flo and I were waked\par
    by the most delicious music under our windows. We flew up, and\par
    hid behind the curtains; but sly peeps showed us Fred and the\par
    students singing away down below. It was the most romantic thing\par
    I ever saw,--the river, the bridge of boats, the great fortress\par
    opposite, moonlight everywhere, and music fit to melt a heart of\par
    stone.\par
\par
    "When they were done we threw down some flowers, and saw them\par
    scramble for them, kiss their hands to the invisible ladies, and\par
    go laughing away,--to smoke and drink beer, I suppose. Next\par
    morning Fred showed me one of the crumpled flowers in his\par
    vest-pocket, and looked very sentimental. I laughed at him, and\par
    said I didn't throw it, but Flo, which seemed to disgust him,\par
    for he tossed it out of the window, and turned sensible again.\par
    I'm afraid I'm going to have trouble with that boy, it begins to\par
    look like it.\par
\par
    "The baths at Nassau were very gay, so was Baden-Baden, where\par
    Fred lost some money, and I scolded him. He needs some one to\par
    look after him when Frank is not with him. Kate said once she\par
    hoped he'd marry soon, and I quite agree with her that it would\par
    be well for him. Frankfort was delightful; I saw Goethe's house,\par
    Schiller's statue, and Dannecker's famous 'Ariadne.' It was very\par
    lovely, but I should have enjoyed it more if I had known the\par
    story better. I didn't like to ask, as every one knew it, or\par
    pretended they did. I wish Jo would tell me all about it; I\par
    ought to have read more, for I find I don't know anything, and\par
    it mortifies me.\par
\par
    "Now comes the serious part,--for it happened here, and Fred is\par
    just gone. He has been so kind and jolly that we all got quite\par
    fond of him; I never thought of anything but a travelling\par
    friendship, till the serenade night. Since then I've begun to\par
    feel that the moonlight walks, balcony talks, and daily\par
    adventures were something more to him than fun. I haven't\par
    flirted, mother, truly, but remembered what you said to me, and\par
    have done my very best. I can't help it if people like me; I\par
    don't try to make them, and it worries me if I don't care for\par
    them, though Jo says I haven't got any heart. Now I know mother\par
    will shake her head, and the girls say, 'Oh, the mercenary\par
    little wretch!' but I've made up my mind, and, if Fred asks me,\par
    I shall accept him, though I'm not madly in love. I like him,\par
    and we get on comfortably together. He is handsome, young,\par
    clever enough, and very rich,--ever so much richer than the\par
    Laurences. I don't think his family would object, and I should\par
    be very happy, for they are all kind, well-bred, generous\par
    people, and they like me. Fred, as the eldest twin, will have\par
    the estate, I suppose, and such a splendid one as it is! A city\par
    house in a fashionable street, not so showy as our big houses,\par
    but twice as comfortable, and full of solid luxury, such as\par
    English people believe in. I like it, for it's genuine. I've\par
    seen the plate, the family jewels, the old servants, and\par
    pictures of the country place, with its park, great house,\par
    lovely grounds, and fine horses. Oh, it would be all I should\par
    ask! and I'd rather have it than any title such as girls snap up\par
    so readily, and find nothing behind. I may be mercenary, but I\par
    hate poverty, and don't mean to bear it a minute longer than I\par
    can help. One of us _must_ marry well; Meg didn't, Jo won't,\par
    Beth can't yet, so I shall, and make everything cosey all round.\par
    I wouldn't marry a man I hated or despised. You may be sure of\par
    that; and, though Fred is not my model hero, he does very well,\par
    and, in time, I should get fond enough of him if he was very\par
    fond of me, and let me do just as I liked. So I've been turning\par
    the matter over in my mind the last week, for it was impossible\par
    to help seeing that Fred liked me. He said nothing, but little\par
    things showed it; he never goes with Flo, always gets on my side\par
    of the carriage, table, or promenade, looks sentimental when we\par
    are alone, and frowns at any one else who ventures to speak to\par
    me. Yesterday, at dinner, when an Austrian officer stared at us,\par
    and then said something to his friend,--a rakish-looking\par
    baron,--about '_ein wonderschönes Blöndchen_,' Fred looked as\par
    fierce as a lion, and cut his meat so savagely, it nearly flew\par
    off his plate. He isn't one of the cool, stiff Englishmen, but\par
    is rather peppery, for he has Scotch blood in him, as one might\par
    guess from his bonnie blue eyes.\par
\par
    "Well, last evening we went up to the castle about sunset,--at\par
    least all of us but Fred, who was to meet us there, after going\par
    to the Post Restante for letters. We had a charming time poking\par
    about the ruins, the vaults where the monster tun is, and the\par
    beautiful gardens made by the elector, long ago, for his English\par
    wife. I liked the great terrace best, for the view was divine;\par
    so, while the rest went to see the rooms inside, I sat there\par
    trying to sketch the gray stone lion's head on the wall, with\par
    scarlet woodbine sprays hanging round it. I felt as if I'd got\par
    into a romance, sitting there, watching the Neckar rolling\par
    through the valley, listening to the music of the Austrian band\par
    below, and waiting for my lover, like a real story-book girl. I\par
    had a feeling that something was going to happen, and I was\par
    ready for it. I didn't feel blushy or quakey, but quite cool,\par
    and only a little excited.\par
\par
[Illustration: Trying to sketch the gray-stone lion's head on the wall]\par
\par
    "By and by I heard Fred's voice, and then he came hurrying\par
    through the great arch to find me. He looked so troubled that I\par
    forgot all about myself, and asked what the matter was. He said\par
    he'd just got a letter begging him to come home, for Frank was\par
    very ill; so he was going at once, in the night train, and only\par
    had time to say good-by. I was very sorry for him, and\par
    disappointed for myself, but only for a minute, because he\par
    said, as he shook hands,--and said it in a way that I could not\par
    mistake,--'I shall soon come back; you won't forget me, Amy?'\par
\par
    "I didn't promise, but I looked at him, and he seemed satisfied,\par
    and there was no time for anything but messages and good-byes,\par
    for he was off in an hour, and we all miss him very much. I know\par
    he wanted to speak, but I think, from something he once hinted,\par
    that he had promised his father not to do anything of the sort\par
    yet awhile, for he is a rash boy, and the old gentleman dreads a\par
    foreign daughter-in-law. We shall soon meet in Rome; and then,\par
    if I don't change my mind, I'll say 'Yes, thank you,' when he\par
    says 'Will you, please?'\par
\par
    "Of course this is all _very private_, but I wished you to know\par
    what was going on. Don't be anxious about me; remember I am your\par
    'prudent Amy,' and be sure I will do nothing rashly. Send me as\par
    much advice as you like; I'll use it if I can. I wish I could\par
    see you for a good talk, Marmee. Love and trust me.\par
\par
                                    "Ever your                 AMY."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                 XXXII.\par
\par
                            TENDER TROUBLES.\par
\par
\par
"Jo, I'm anxious about Beth."\par
\par
"Why, mother, she has seemed unusually well since the babies came."\par
\par
"It's not her health that troubles me now; it's her spirits. I'm sure\par
there is something on her mind, and I want you to discover what it is."\par
\par
"What makes you think so, mother?"\par
\par
"She sits alone a good deal, and doesn't talk to her father as much as\par
she used. I found her crying over the babies the other day. When she\par
sings, the songs are always sad ones, and now and then I see a look in\par
her face that I don't understand. This isn't like Beth, and it worries\par
me."\par
\par
"Have you asked her about it?"\par
\par
"I have tried once or twice; but she either evaded my questions, or\par
looked so distressed that I stopped. I never force my children's\par
confidence, and I seldom have to wait for it long."\par
\par
Mrs. March glanced at Jo as she spoke, but the face opposite seemed\par
quite unconscious of any secret disquietude but Beth's; and, after\par
sewing thoughtfully for a minute, Jo said,--\par
\par
"I think she is growing up, and so begins to dream dreams, and have\par
hopes and fears and fidgets, without knowing why, or being able to\par
explain them. Why, mother, Beth's eighteen, but we don't realize it, and\par
treat her like a child, forgetting she's a woman."\par
\par
"So she is. Dear heart, how fast you do grow up," returned her mother,\par
with a sigh and a smile.\par
\par
"Can't be helped, Marmee, so you must resign yourself to all sorts of\par
worries, and let your birds hop out of the nest, one by one. I promise\par
never to hop very far, if that is any comfort to you."\par
\par
"It is a great comfort, Jo; I always feel strong when you are at home,\par
now Meg is gone. Beth is too feeble and Amy too young to depend upon;\par
but when the tug comes, you are always ready."\par
\par
"Why, you know I don't mind hard jobs much, and there must always be one\par
scrub in a family. Amy is splendid in fine works, and I'm not; but I\par
feel in my element when all the carpets are to be taken up, or half the\par
family fall sick at once. Amy is distinguishing herself abroad; but if\par
anything is amiss at home, I'm your man."\par
\par
"I leave Beth to your hands, then, for she will open her tender little\par
heart to her Jo sooner than to any one else. Be very kind, and don't let\par
her think any one watches or talks about her. If she only would get\par
quite strong and cheerful again, I shouldn't have a wish in the world."\par
\par
"Happy woman! I've got heaps."\par
\par
"My dear, what are they?"\par
\par
"I'll settle Bethy's troubles, and then I'll tell you mine. They are not\par
very wearing, so they'll keep;" and Jo stitched away, with a wise nod\par
which set her mother's heart at rest about her, for the present at\par
least.\par
\par
While apparently absorbed in her own affairs, Jo watched Beth; and,\par
after many conflicting conjectures, finally settled upon one which\par
seemed to explain the change in her. A slight incident gave Jo the clue\par
to the mystery, she thought, and lively fancy, loving heart did the\par
rest. She was affecting to write busily one Saturday afternoon, when she\par
and Beth were alone together; yet as she scribbled, she kept her eye on\par
her sister, who seemed unusually quiet. Sitting at the window, Beth's\par
work often dropped into her lap, and she leaned her head upon her hand,\par
in a dejected attitude, while her eyes rested on the dull, autumnal\par
landscape. Suddenly some one passed below, whistling like an operatic\par
blackbird, and a voice called out,--\par
\par
           [Illustration: She leaned her head upon her hands]\par
\par
"All serene! Coming in to-night."\par
\par
Beth started, leaned forward, smiled and nodded, watched the passer-by\par
till his quick tramp died away, then said softly, as if to herself,--\par
\par
"How strong and well and happy that dear boy looks."\par
\par
"Hum!" said Jo, still intent upon her sister's face; for the bright\par
color faded as quickly as it came, the smile vanished, and presently a\par
tear lay shining on the window-ledge. Beth whisked it off, and glanced\par
apprehensively at Jo; but she was scratching away at a tremendous rate,\par
apparently engrossed in "Olympia's Oath." The instant Beth turned, Jo\par
began her watch again, saw Beth's hand go quietly to her eyes more than\par
once, and, in her half-averted face, read a tender sorrow that made her\par
own eyes fill. Fearing to betray herself, she slipped away, murmuring\par
something about needing more paper.\par
\par
"Mercy on me, Beth loves Laurie!" she said, sitting down in her own\par
room, pale with the shock of the discovery which she believed she had\par
just made. "I never dreamt of such a thing. What _will_ mother say? I\par
wonder if he--" there Jo stopped, and turned scarlet with a sudden\par
thought. "If he shouldn't love back again, how dreadful it would be. He\par
must; I'll make him!" and she shook her head threateningly at the\par
picture of the mischievous-looking boy laughing at her from the wall.\par
"Oh dear, we _are_ growing up with a vengeance. Here's Meg married and a\par
mamma, Amy flourishing away at Paris, and Beth in love. I'm the only one\par
that has sense enough to keep out of mischief." Jo thought intently for\par
a minute, with her eyes fixed on the picture; then she smoothed out her\par
wrinkled forehead, and said, with a decided nod at the face opposite,\par
"No, thank you, sir; you're very charming, but you've no more stability\par
than a weathercock; so you needn't write touching notes, and smile in\par
that insinuating way, for it won't do a bit of good, and I won't have\par
it."\par
\par
Then she sighed, and fell into a reverie, from which she did not wake\par
till the early twilight sent her down to take new observations, which\par
only confirmed her suspicion. Though Laurie flirted with Amy and joked\par
with Jo, his manner to Beth had always been peculiarly kind and gentle,\par
but so was everybody's; therefore, no one thought of imagining that he\par
cared more for her than for the others. Indeed, a general impression had\par
prevailed in the family, of late, that "our boy" was getting fonder than\par
ever of Jo, who, however, wouldn't hear a word upon the subject, and\par
scolded violently if any one dared to suggest it. If they had known the\par
various tender passages of the past year, or rather attempts at tender\par
passages which had been nipped in the bud, they would have had the\par
immense satisfaction of saying, "I told you so." But Jo hated\par
"philandering," and wouldn't allow it, always having a joke or a smile\par
ready at the least sign of impending danger.\par
\par
When Laurie first went to college, he fell in love about once a month;\par
but these small flames were as brief as ardent, did no damage, and much\par
amused Jo, who took great interest in the alternations of hope, despair,\par
and resignation, which were confided to her in their weekly\par
conferences. But there came a time when Laurie ceased to worship at many\par
shrines, hinted darkly at one all-absorbing passion, and indulged\par
occasionally in Byronic fits of gloom. Then he avoided the tender\par
subject altogether, wrote philosophical notes to Jo, turned studious,\par
and gave out that he was going to "dig," intending to graduate in a\par
blaze of glory. This suited the young lady better than twilight\par
confidences, tender pressures of the hand, and eloquent glances of the\par
eye; for with Jo, brain developed earlier than heart, and she preferred\par
imaginary heroes to real ones, because, when tired of them, the former\par
could be shut up in the tin-kitchen till called for, and the latter were\par
less manageable.\par
\par
Things were in this state when the grand discovery was made, and Jo\par
watched Laurie that night as she had never done before. If she had not\par
got the new idea into her head, she would have seen nothing unusual in\par
the fact that Beth was very quiet, and Laurie very kind to her. But\par
having given the rein to her lively fancy, it galloped away with her at\par
a great pace; and common sense, being rather weakened by a long course\par
of romance writing, did not come to the rescue. As usual, Beth lay on\par
the sofa, and Laurie sat in a low chair close by, amusing her with all\par
sorts of gossip; for she depended on her weekly "spin," and he never\par
disappointed her. But that evening, Jo fancied that Beth's eyes rested\par
on the lively, dark face beside her with peculiar pleasure, and that she\par
listened with intense interest to an account of some exciting\par
cricket-match, though the phrases, "caught off a tice," "stumped off his\par
ground," and "the leg hit for three," were as intelligible to her as\par
Sanscrit. She also fancied, having set her heart upon seeing it, that\par
she saw a certain increase of gentleness in Laurie's manner, that he\par
dropped his voice now and then, laughed less than usual, was a little\par
absent-minded, and settled the afghan over Beth's feet with an assiduity\par
that was really almost tender.\par
\par
"Who knows? stranger things have happened," thought Jo, as she fussed\par
about the room. "She will make quite an angel of him, and he will make\par
life delightfully easy and pleasant for the dear, if they only love each\par
other. I don't see how he can help it; and I do believe he would if the\par
rest of us were out of the way."\par
\par
As every one _was_ out of the way but herself, Jo began to feel that\par
she ought to dispose of herself with all speed. But where should she go?\par
and burning to lay herself upon the shrine of sisterly devotion, she sat\par
down to settle that point.\par
\par
Now, the old sofa was a regular patriarch of a sofa,--long, broad,\par
well-cushioned, and low; a trifle shabby, as well it might be, for the\par
girls had slept and sprawled on it as babies, fished over the back, rode\par
on the arms, and had menageries under it as children, and rested tired\par
heads, dreamed dreams, and listened to tender talk on it as young women.\par
They all loved it, for it was a family refuge, and one corner had always\par
been Jo's favorite lounging-place. Among the many pillows that adorned\par
the venerable couch was one, hard, round, covered with prickly\par
horsehair, and furnished with a knobby button at each end; this\par
repulsive pillow was her especial property, being used as a weapon of\par
defence, a barricade, or a stern preventive of too much slumber.\par
\par
Laurie knew this pillow well, and had cause to regard it with deep\par
aversion, having been unmercifully pummelled with it in former days,\par
when romping was allowed, and now frequently debarred by it from taking\par
the seat he most coveted, next to Jo in the sofa corner. If "the\par
sausage" as they called it, stood on end, it was a sign that he might\par
approach and repose; but if it lay flat across the sofa, woe to the man,\par
woman, or child who dared disturb it! That evening Jo forgot to\par
barricade her corner, and had not been in her seat five minutes, before\par
a massive form appeared beside her, and, with both arms spread over the\par
sofa-back, both long legs stretched out before him, Laurie exclaimed,\par
with a sigh of satisfaction,--\par
\par
"Now, _this_ is filling at the price."\par
\par
           [Illustration: Now, this is filling at the price]\par
\par
"No slang," snapped Jo, slamming down the pillow. But it was too late,\par
there was no room for it; and, coasting on to the floor, it disappeared\par
in a most mysterious manner.\par
\par
"Come, Jo, don't be thorny. After studying himself to a skeleton all the\par
week, a fellow deserves petting, and ought to get it."\par
\par
"Beth will pet you; I'm busy."\par
\par
"No, she's not to be bothered with me; but you like that sort of thing,\par
unless you've suddenly lost your taste for it. Have you? Do you hate\par
your boy, and want to fire pillows at him?"\par
\par
Anything more wheedlesome than that touching appeal was seldom heard,\par
but Jo quenched "her boy" by turning on him with the stern query,--\par
\par
"How many bouquets have you sent Miss Randal this week?"\par
\par
"Not one, upon my word. She's engaged. Now then."\par
\par
"I'm glad of it; that's one of your foolish extravagances,--sending\par
flowers and things to girls for whom you don't care two pins," continued\par
Jo reprovingly.\par
\par
"Sensible girls, for whom I do care whole papers of pins, won't let me\par
send them 'flowers and things,' so what can I do? My feelings must have\par
a _went_."\par
\par
"Mother doesn't approve of flirting, even in fun; and you do flirt\par
desperately, Teddy."\par
\par
"I'd give anything if I could answer, 'So do you.' As I can't, I'll\par
merely say that I don't see any harm in that pleasant little game, if\par
all parties understand that it's only play."\par
\par
"Well, it does look pleasant, but I can't learn how it's done. I've\par
tried, because one feels awkward in company, not to do as everybody else\par
is doing; but I don't seem to get on," said Jo, forgetting to play\par
Mentor.\par
\par
"Take lessons of Amy; she has a regular talent for it."\par
\par
"Yes, she does it very prettily, and never seems to go too far. I\par
suppose it's natural to some people to please without trying, and others\par
to always say and do the wrong thing in the wrong place."\par
\par
"I'm glad you can't flirt; it's really refreshing to see a sensible,\par
straightforward girl, who can be jolly and kind without making a fool of\par
herself. Between ourselves, Jo, some of the girls I know really do go on\par
at such a rate I'm ashamed of them. They don't mean any harm, I'm sure;\par
but if they knew how we fellows talked about them afterward, they'd mend\par
their ways, I fancy."\par
\par
"They do the same; and, as their tongues are the sharpest, you fellows\par
get the worst of it, for you are as silly as they, every bit. If you\par
behaved properly, they would; but, knowing you like their nonsense, they\par
keep it up, and then you blame them."\par
\par
"Much you know about it, ma'am," said Laurie, in a superior tone. "We\par
don't like romps and flirts, though we may act as if we did sometimes.\par
The pretty, modest girls are never talked about, except respectfully,\par
among gentlemen. Bless your innocent soul! If you could be in my place\par
for a month you'd see things that would astonish you a trifle. Upon my\par
word, when I see one of those harum-scarum girls, I always want to say\par
with our friend Cock Robin,--\par
\par
        "'Out upon you, fie upon you,\par
           Bold-faced jig!'"\par
\par
It was impossible to help laughing at the funny conflict between\par
Laurie's chivalrous reluctance to speak ill of womankind, and his very\par
natural dislike of the unfeminine folly of which fashionable society\par
showed him many samples. Jo knew that "young Laurence" was regarded as a\par
most eligible _parti_ by worldly mammas, was much smiled upon by their\par
daughters, and flattered enough by ladies of all ages to make a coxcomb\par
of him; so she watched him rather jealously, fearing he would be spoilt,\par
and rejoiced more than she confessed to find that he still believed in\par
modest girls. Returning suddenly to her admonitory tone, she said,\par
dropping her voice, "If you _must_ have a 'went,' Teddy, go and devote\par
yourself to one of the 'pretty, modest girls' whom you do respect, and\par
not waste your time with the silly ones."\par
\par
"You really advise it?" and Laurie looked at her with an odd mixture of\par
anxiety and merriment in his face.\par
\par
"Yes, I do; but you'd better wait till you are through college, on the\par
whole, and be fitting yourself for the place meantime. You're not half\par
good enough for--well, whoever the modest girl maybe," and Jo looked a\par
little queer likewise, for a name had almost escaped her.\par
\par
"That I'm not!" acquiesced Laurie, with an expression of humility quite\par
new to him, as he dropped his eyes, and absently wound Jo's apron-tassel\par
round his finger.\par
\par
"Mercy on us, this will never do," thought Jo; adding aloud, "Go and\par
sing to me. I'm dying for some music, and always like yours."\par
\par
"I'd rather stay here, thank you."\par
\par
"Well, you can't; there isn't room. Go and make yourself useful, since\par
you are too big to be ornamental. I thought you hated to be tied to a\par
woman's apron-string?" retorted Jo, quoting certain rebellious words of\par
his own.\par
\par
"Ah, that depends on who wears the apron!" and Laurie gave an audacious\par
tweak at the tassel.\par
\par
"Are you going?" demanded Jo, diving for the pillow.\par
\par
He fled at once, and the minute it was well "Up with the bonnets of\par
bonnie Dundee," she slipped away, to return no more till the young\par
gentleman had departed in high dudgeon.\par
\par
          [Illustration: Up with the Bonnets of Bonnie Dundee]\par
\par
Jo lay long awake that night, and was just dropping off when the sound\par
of a stifled sob made her fly to Beth's bedside, with the anxious\par
inquiry, "What is it, dear?"\par
\par
"I thought you were asleep," sobbed Beth.\par
\par
"Is it the old pain, my precious?"\par
\par
"No; it's a new one; but I can bear it," and Beth tried to check her\par
tears.\par
\par
"Tell me all about it, and let me cure it as I often did the other."\par
\par
"You can't; there is no cure." There Beth's voice gave way, and,\par
clinging to her sister, she cried so despairingly that Jo was\par
frightened.\par
\par
"Where is it? Shall I call mother?"\par
\par
Beth did not answer the first question; but in the dark one hand went\par
involuntarily to her heart, as if the pain were there; with the other\par
she held Jo fast, whispering eagerly, "No, no, don't call her, don't\par
tell her. I shall be better soon. Lie down here and 'poor' my head. I'll\par
be quiet, and go to sleep; indeed I will."\par
\par
Jo obeyed; but as her hand went softly to and fro across Beth's hot\par
forehead and wet eyelids, her heart was very full, and she longed to\par
speak. But young as she was, Jo had learned that hearts, like flowers,\par
cannot be rudely handled, but must open naturally; so, though she\par
believed she knew the cause of Beth's new pain, she only said, in her\par
tenderest tone, "Does anything trouble you, deary?"\par
\par
"Yes, Jo," after a long pause.\par
\par
"Wouldn't it comfort you to tell me what it is?"\par
\par
"Not now, not yet."\par
\par
"Then I won't ask; but remember, Bethy, that mother and Jo are always\par
glad to hear and help you, if they can."\par
\par
"I know it. I'll tell you by and by."\par
\par
"Is the pain better now?"\par
\par
"Oh, yes, much better; you are so comfortable, Jo!"\par
\par
"Go to sleep, dear; I'll stay with you."\par
\par
So cheek to cheek they fell asleep, and on the morrow Beth seemed quite\par
herself again; for at eighteen, neither heads nor hearts ache long, and\par
a loving word can medicine most ills.\par
\par
But Jo had made up her mind, and, after pondering over a project for\par
some days, she confided it to her mother.\par
\par
"You asked me the other day what my wishes were. I'll tell you one of\par
them, Marmee," she began, as they sat alone together. "I want to go away\par
somewhere this winter for a change."\par
\par
"Why, Jo?" and her mother looked up quickly, as if the words suggested a\par
double meaning.\par
\par
With her eyes on her work, Jo answered soberly, "I want something new; I\par
feel restless, and anxious to be seeing, doing, and learning more than I\par
am. I brood too much over my own small affairs, and need stirring up,\par
so, as I can be spared this winter, I'd like to hop a little way, and\par
try my wings."\par
\par
"Where will you hop?"\par
\par
"To New York. I had a bright idea yesterday, and this is it. You know\par
Mrs. Kirke wrote to you for some respectable young person to teach her\par
children and sew. It's rather hard to find just the thing, but I think I\par
should suit if I tried."\par
\par
"My dear, go out to service in that great boarding-house!" and Mrs.\par
March looked surprised, but not displeased.\par
\par
"It's not exactly going out to service; for Mrs. Kirke is your\par
friend,--the kindest soul that ever lived,--and would make things\par
pleasant for me, I know. Her family is separate from the rest, and no\par
one knows me there. Don't care if they do; it's honest work, and I'm not\par
ashamed of it."\par
\par
"Nor I; but your writing?"\par
\par
"All the better for the change. I shall see and hear new things, get new\par
ideas, and, even if I haven't much time there, I shall bring home\par
quantities of material for my rubbish."\par
\par
"I have no doubt of it; but are these your only reasons for this sudden\par
fancy?"\par
\par
"No, mother."\par
\par
"May I know the others?"\par
\par
Jo looked up and Jo looked down, then said slowly, with sudden color in\par
her cheeks, "It may be vain and wrong to say it, but--I'm afraid--Laurie\par
is getting too fond of me."\par
\par
"Then you don't care for him in the way it is evident he begins to care\par
for you?" and Mrs. March looked anxious as she put the question.\par
\par
"Mercy, no! I love the dear boy, as I always have, and am immensely\par
proud of him; but as for anything more, it's out of the question."\par
\par
"I'm glad of that, Jo."\par
\par
"Why, please?"\par
\par
"Because, dear, I don't think you suited to one another. As friends you\par
are very happy, and your frequent quarrels soon blow over; but I fear\par
you would both rebel if you were mated for life. You are too much alike\par
and too fond of freedom, not to mention hot tempers and strong wills, to\par
get on happily together, in a relation which needs infinite patience and\par
forbearance, as well as love."\par
\par
"That's just the feeling I had, though I couldn't express it. I'm glad\par
you think he is only beginning to care for me. It would trouble me sadly\par
to make him unhappy; for I couldn't fall in love with the dear old\par
fellow merely out of gratitude, could I?"\par
\par
"You are sure of his feeling for you?"\par
\par
The color deepened in Jo's cheeks, as she answered, with the look of\par
mingled pleasure, pride, and pain which young girls wear when speaking\par
of first lovers,--\par
\par
"I'm afraid it is so, mother; he hasn't said anything, but he looks a\par
great deal. I think I had better go away before it comes to anything."\par
\par
"I agree with you, and if it can be managed you shall go."\par
\par
Jo looked relieved, and, after a pause, said, smiling, "How Mrs. Moffat\par
would wonder at your want of management, if she knew; and how she will\par
rejoice that Annie still may hope."\par
\par
"Ah, Jo, mothers may differ in their management, but the hope is the\par
same in all,--the desire to see their children happy. Meg is so, and I\par
am content with her success. You I leave to enjoy your liberty till you\par
tire of it; for only then will you find that there is something sweeter.\par
Amy is my chief care now, but her good sense will help her. For Beth, I\par
indulge no hopes except that she may be well. By the way, she seems\par
brighter this last day or two. Have you spoken to her?"\par
\par
"Yes; she owned she had a trouble, and promised to tell me by and by. I\par
said no more, for I think I know it;" and Jo told her little story.\par
\par
Mrs. March shook her head, and did not take so romantic a view of the\par
case, but looked grave, and repeated her opinion that, for Laurie's\par
sake, Jo should go away for a time.\par
\par
"Let us say nothing about it to him till the plan is settled; then I'll\par
run away before he can collect his wits and be tragical. Beth must think\par
I'm going to please myself, as I am, for I can't talk about Laurie to\par
her; but she can pet and comfort him after I'm gone, and so cure him of\par
this romantic notion. He's been through so many little trials of the\par
sort, he's used to it, and will soon get over his love-lornity."\par
\par
Jo spoke hopefully, but could not rid herself of the foreboding fear\par
that this "little trial" would be harder than the others, and that\par
Laurie would not get over his "love-lornity" as easily as heretofore.\par
\par
The plan was talked over in a family council, and agreed upon; for Mrs.\par
Kirke gladly accepted Jo, and promised to make a pleasant home for her.\par
The teaching would render her independent; and such leisure as she got\par
might be made profitable by writing, while the new scenes and society\par
would be both useful and agreeable. Jo liked the prospect and was eager\par
to be gone, for the home-nest was growing too narrow for her restless\par
nature and adventurous spirit. When all was settled, with fear and\par
trembling she told Laurie; but to her surprise he took it very quietly.\par
He had been graver than usual of late, but very pleasant; and, when\par
jokingly accused of turning over a new leaf, he answered soberly, "So I\par
am; and I mean this one shall stay turned."\par
\par
Jo was very much relieved that one of his virtuous fits should come on\par
just then, and made her preparations with a lightened heart,--for Beth\par
seemed more cheerful,--and hoped she was doing the best for all.\par
\par
"One thing I leave to your especial care," she said, the night before\par
she left.\par
\par
"You mean your papers?" asked Beth.\par
\par
"No, my boy. Be very good to him, won't you?"\par
\par
"Of course I will; but I can't fill your place, and he'll miss you\par
sadly."\par
\par
"It won't hurt him; so remember, I leave him in your charge, to plague,\par
pet, and keep in order."\par
\par
"I'll do my best, for your sake," promised Beth, wondering why Jo looked\par
at her so queerly.\par
\par
When Laurie said "Good-by," he whispered significantly, "It won't do a\par
bit of good, Jo. My eye is on you; so mind what you do, or I'll come and\par
bring you home."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
  [Illustration: I amused myself by dropping gingerbread nuts over the\par
                                 seat]\par
\par
                                XXXIII.\par
\par
                             JO'S JOURNAL.\par
\par
\par
                                                "NEW YORK, November.\par
\par
    "DEAR MARMEE AND BETH,--\par
\par
    "I'm going to write you a regular volume, for I've got heaps to\par
    tell, though I'm not a fine young lady travelling on the\par
    continent. When I lost sight of father's dear old face, I felt a\par
    trifle blue, and might have shed a briny drop or two, if an\par
    Irish lady with four small children, all crying more or less,\par
    hadn't diverted my mind; for I amused myself by dropping\par
    gingerbread nuts over the seat every time they opened their\par
    mouths to roar.\par
\par
    "Soon the sun came out, and taking it as a good omen, I cleared\par
    up likewise, and enjoyed my journey with all my heart.\par
\par
    "Mrs. Kirke welcomed me so kindly I felt at home at once, even\par
    in that big house full of strangers. She gave me a funny little\par
    sky-parlor--all she had; but there is a stove in it, and a nice\par
    table in a sunny window, so I can sit here and write whenever I\par
    like. A fine view and a church-tower opposite atone for the many\par
    stairs, and I took a fancy to my den on the spot. The nursery,\par
    where I am to teach and sew, is a pleasant room next Mrs.\par
    Kirke's private parlor, and the two little girls are pretty\par
    children,--rather spoilt, I fancy, but they took to me after\par
    telling them 'The Seven Bad Pigs;' and I've no doubt I shall\par
    make a model governess.\par
\par
    "I am to have my meals with the children, if I prefer it to the\par
    great table, and for the present I do, for I _am_ bashful,\par
    though no one will believe it.\par
\par
    "'Now, my dear, make yourself at home,' said Mrs. K. in her\par
    motherly way; 'I'm on the drive from morning to night, as you\par
    may suppose with such a family; but a great anxiety will be off\par
    my mind if I know the children are safe with you. My rooms are\par
    always open to you, and your own shall be as comfortable as I\par
    can make it. There are some pleasant people in the house if you\par
    feel sociable, and your evenings are always free. Come to me if\par
    anything goes wrong, and be as happy as you can. There's the\par
    tea-bell; I must run and change my cap;' and off she bustled,\par
    leaving me to settle myself in my new nest.\par
\par
    "As I went downstairs, soon after, I saw something I liked. The\par
    flights are very long in this tall house, and as I stood waiting\par
    at the head of the third one for a little servant girl to lumber\par
    up, I saw a gentleman come along behind her, take the heavy hod\par
    of coal out of her hand, carry it all the way up, put it down at\par
    a door near by, and walk away, saying, with a kind nod and a\par
    foreign accent,--\par
\par
    "'It goes better so. The little back is too young to haf such\par
    heaviness.'\par
\par
    "Wasn't it good of him? I like such things, for, as father says,\par
    trifles show character. When I mentioned it to Mrs. K., that\par
    evening, she laughed, and said,--\par
\par
    "'That must have been Professor Bhaer; he's always doing things\par
    of that sort.'\par
\par
    "Mrs. K. told me he was from Berlin; very learned and good, but\par
    poor as a church-mouse, and gives lessons to support himself and\par
    two little orphan nephews whom he is educating here, according\par
    to the wishes of his sister, who married an American. Not a very\par
    romantic story, but it interested me; and I was glad to hear\par
    that Mrs. K. lends him her parlor for some of his scholars.\par
    There is a glass door between it and the nursery, and I mean to\par
    peep at him, and then I'll tell you how he looks. He's almost\par
    forty, so it's no harm, Marmee.\par
\par
    "After tea and a go-to-bed romp with the little girls, I\par
    attacked the big work-basket, and had a quiet evening chatting\par
    with my new friend. I shall keep a journal-letter, and send it\par
    once a week; so good-night, and more to-morrow."\par
\par
                                                     "_Tuesday Eve._\par
\par
    "Had a lively time in my seminary, this morning, for the\par
    children acted like Sancho; and at one time I really thought I\par
    should shake them all round. Some good angel inspired me to try\par
    gymnastics, and I kept it up till they were glad to sit down and\par
    keep still. After luncheon, the girl took them out for a walk,\par
    and I went to my needle-work, like little Mabel, 'with a willing\par
    mind.' I was thanking my stars that I'd learned to make nice\par
    button-holes, when the parlor-door opened and shut, and some one\par
    began to hum,--\par
\par
                         'Kennst du das land,'\par
\par
    like a big bumble-bee. It was dreadfully improper, I know, but I\par
    couldn't resist the temptation; and lifting one end of the\par
    curtain before the glass door, I peeped in. Professor Bhaer was\par
    there; and while he arranged his books, I took a good look at\par
    him. A regular German,--rather stout, with brown hair tumbled\par
    all over his head, a bushy beard, good nose, the kindest eyes I\par
    ever saw, and a splendid big voice that does one's ears good,\par
    after our sharp or slipshod American gabble. His clothes were\par
    rusty, his hands were large, and he hadn't a really handsome\par
    feature in his face, except his beautiful teeth; yet I liked\par
    him, for he had a fine head; his linen was very nice, and he\par
    looked like a gentleman, though two buttons were off his coat,\par
    and there was a patch on one shoe. He looked sober in spite of\par
    his humming, till he went to the window to turn the hyacinth\par
    bulbs toward the sun, and stroke the cat, who received him like\par
    an old friend. Then he smiled; and when a tap came at the door,\par
    called out in a loud, brisk tone,--\par
\par
    "'Herein!'\par
\par
    "I was just going to run, when I caught sight of a morsel of a\par
    child carrying a big book, and stopped to see what was going on.\par
\par
    "'Me wants my Bhaer,' said the mite, slamming down her book, and\par
    running to meet him.\par
\par
    "'Thou shalt haf thy Bhaer; come, then, and take a goot hug from\par
    him, my Tina,' said the Professor, catching her up, with a\par
    laugh, and holding her so high over his head that she had to\par
    stoop her little face to kiss him.\par
\par
                [Illustration: Thou shalt haf thy Bhaer]\par
\par
    "'Now me mus tuddy my lessin,' went on the funny little thing;\par
    so he put her up at the table, opened the great dictionary she\par
    had brought, and gave her a paper and pencil, and she scribbled\par
    away, turning a leaf now and then, and passing her little fat\par
    finger down the page, as if finding a word, so soberly that I\par
    nearly betrayed myself by a laugh, while Mr. Bhaer stood\par
    stroking her pretty hair, with a fatherly look, that made me\par
    think she must be his own, though she looked more French than\par
    German.\par
\par
    "Another knock and the appearance of two young ladies sent me\par
    back to my work, and there I virtuously remained through all the\par
    noise and gabbling that went on next door. One of the girls kept\par
    laughing affectedly, and saying 'Now Professor,' in a\par
    coquettish tone, and the other pronounced her German with an\par
    accent that must have made it hard for him to keep sober.\par
\par
    "Both seemed to try his patience sorely; for more than once I\par
    heard him say emphatically, 'No, no, it is _not_ so; you haf not\par
    attend to what I say;' and once there was a loud rap, as if he\par
    struck the table with his book, followed by the despairing\par
    exclamation, 'Prut! it all goes bad this day.'\par
\par
    "Poor man, I pitied him; and when the girls were gone, took just\par
    one more peep, to see if he survived it. He seemed to have\par
    thrown himself back in his chair, tired out, and sat there with\par
    his eyes shut till the clock struck two, when he jumped up, put\par
    his books in his pocket, as if ready for another lesson, and,\par
    taking little Tina, who had fallen asleep on the sofa, in his\par
    arms, he carried her quietly away. I fancy he has a hard life of\par
    it.\par
\par
    "Mrs. Kirke asked me if I wouldn't go down to the five o'clock\par
    dinner; and, feeling a little bit homesick, I thought I would,\par
    just to see what sort of people are under the same roof with me.\par
    So I made myself respectable, and tried to slip in behind Mrs.\par
    Kirke; but as she is short, and I'm tall, my efforts at\par
    concealment were rather a failure. She gave me a seat by her,\par
    and after my face cooled off, I plucked up courage, and looked\par
    about me. The long table was full, and every one intent on\par
    getting their dinner,--the gentlemen especially, who seemed to\par
    be eating on time, for they _bolted_ in every sense of the word,\par
    vanishing as soon as they were done. There was the usual\par
    assortment of young men absorbed in themselves; young couples\par
    absorbed in each other; married ladies in their babies, and old\par
    gentlemen in politics. I don't think I shall care to have much\par
    to do with any of them, except one sweet-faced maiden lady, who\par
    looks as if she had something in her.\par
\par
    "Cast away at the very bottom of the table was the Professor,\par
    shouting answers to the questions of a very inquisitive, deaf\par
    old gentleman on one side, and talking philosophy with a\par
    Frenchman on the other. If Amy had been here, she'd have turned\par
    her back on him forever, because, sad to relate, he had a great\par
    appetite, and shovelled in his dinner in a manner which would\par
    have horrified 'her ladyship.' I didn't mind, for I like 'to see\par
    folks eat with a relish,' as Hannah says, and the poor man must\par
    have needed a deal of food after teaching idiots all day.\par
\par
    "As I went upstairs after dinner, two of the young men were\par
    settling their hats before the hall-mirror, and I heard one say\par
    low to the other, 'Who's the new party?'\par
\par
    "'Governess, or something of that sort.'\par
\par
    "'What the deuce is she at our table for?'\par
\par
    "'Friend of the old lady's.'\par
\par
    "'Handsome head, but no style.'\par
\par
    "'Not a bit of it. Give us a light and come on.'\par
\par
    "I felt angry at first, and then I didn't care, for a governess\par
    is as good as a clerk, and I've got sense, if I haven't style,\par
    which is more than some people have, judging from the remarks of\par
    the elegant beings who clattered away, smoking like bad\par
    chimneys. I hate ordinary people!"\par
\par
                                                        "_Thursday._\par
\par
    "Yesterday was a quiet day, spent in teaching, sewing, and\par
    writing in my little room, which is very cosey, with a light and\par
    fire. I picked up a few bits of news, and was introduced to the\par
    Professor. It seems that Tina is the child of the Frenchwoman\par
    who does the fine ironing in the laundry here. The little thing\par
    has lost her heart to Mr. Bhaer, and follows him about the house\par
    like a dog whenever he is at home, which delights him, as he is\par
    very fond of children, though a 'bacheldore.' Kitty and Minnie\par
    Kirke likewise regard him with affection, and tell all sorts of\par
    stories about the plays he invents, the presents he brings, and\par
    the splendid tales he tells. The young men quiz him, it seems,\par
    call him Old Fritz, Lager Beer, Ursa Major, and make all manner\par
    of jokes on his name. But he enjoys it like a boy, Mrs. K. says,\par
    and takes it so good-naturedly that they all like him, in spite\par
    of his foreign ways.\par
\par
    "The maiden lady is a Miss Norton,--rich, cultivated, and kind.\par
    She spoke to me at dinner to-day (for I went to table again,\par
    it's such fun to watch people), and asked me to come and see her\par
    at her room. She has fine books and pictures, knows interesting\par
    persons, and seems friendly; so I shall make myself agreeable,\par
    for I _do_ want to get into good society, only it isn't the same\par
    sort that Amy likes.\par
\par
    "I was in our parlor last evening, when Mr. Bhaer came in with\par
    some newspapers for Mrs. Kirke. She wasn't there, but Minnie,\par
    who is a little old woman, introduced me very prettily: 'This is\par
    mamma's friend, Miss March.'\par
\par
    "'Yes; and she's jolly and we like her lots,' added Kitty, who\par
    is an _enfant terrible_.\par
\par
    "We both bowed, and then we laughed, for the prim introduction\par
    and the blunt addition were rather a comical contrast.\par
\par
    "'Ah, yes, I hear these naughty ones go to vex you, Mees Marsch.\par
    If so again, call at me and I come,' he said, with a threatening\par
    frown that delighted the little wretches.\par
\par
    "I promised I would, and he departed; but it seems as if I was\par
    doomed to see a good deal of him, for to-day, as I passed his\par
    door on my way out, by accident I knocked against it with my\par
    umbrella. It flew open, and there he stood in his dressing gown,\par
    with a big blue sock on one hand, and a darning-needle in the\par
    other; he didn't seem at all ashamed of it, for when I explained\par
    and hurried on, he waved his hand, sock and all, saying in his\par
    loud, cheerful way,--\par
\par
            [Illustration: He waved his hand, sock and all]\par
\par
    "'You haf a fine day to make your walk. _Bon voyage,\par
    mademoiselle._'\par
\par
    "I laughed all the way downstairs; but it was a little pathetic,\par
    also, to think of the poor man having to mend his own clothes.\par
    The German gentlemen embroider, I know; but darning hose is\par
    another thing, and not so pretty."\par
\par
                                                        "_Saturday._\par
\par
    "Nothing has happened to write about, except a call on Miss\par
    Norton, who has a room full of lovely things, and who was very\par
    charming, for she showed me all her treasures, and asked me if I\par
    would sometimes go with her to lectures and concerts, as her\par
    escort,--if I enjoyed them. She put it as a favor, but I'm sure\par
    Mrs. Kirke has told her about us, and she does it out of\par
    kindness to me. I'm as proud as Lucifer, but such favors from\par
    such people don't burden me, and I accepted gratefully.\par
\par
    "When I got back to the nursery there was such an uproar in the\par
    parlor that I looked in; and there was Mr. Bhaer down on his\par
    hands and knees, with Tina on his back, Kitty leading him with a\par
    jump-rope, and Minnie feeding two small boys with seed-cakes, as\par
    they roared and ramped in cages built of chairs.\par
\par
    "'We are playing _nargerie_,' explained Kitty.\par
\par
    "'Dis is mine effalunt!' added Tina, holding on by the\par
    Professor's hair.\par
\par
                  [Illustration: Dis is mine effalunt]\par
\par
    "'Mamma always allows us to do what we like Saturday afternoon,\par
    when Franz and Emil come, doesn't she, Mr. Bhaer?' said\par
    Minnie.\par
\par
    "The 'effalunt' sat up, looking as much in earnest as any of\par
    them, and said soberly to me,--\par
\par
    "'I gif you my wort it is so. If we make too large a noise you\par
    shall say "Hush!" to us, and we go more softly.'\par
\par
    "I promised to do so, but left the door open, and enjoyed the\par
    fun as much as they did,--for a more glorious frolic I never\par
    witnessed. They played tag and soldiers, danced and sung, and\par
    when it began to grow dark they all piled on to the sofa about\par
    the Professor, while he told charming fairy stories of the\par
    storks on the chimney-tops, and the little 'kobolds,' who ride\par
    the snow-flakes as they fall. I wish Americans were as simple\par
    and natural as Germans, don't you?\par
\par
    "I'm so fond of writing, I should go spinning on forever if\par
    motives of economy didn't stop me, for though I've used thin\par
    paper and written fine, I tremble to think of the stamps this\par
    long letter will need. Pray forward Amy's as soon as you can\par
    spare them. My small news will sound very flat after her\par
    splendors, but you will like them, I know. Is Teddy studying so\par
    hard that he can't find time to write to his friends? Take good\par
    care of him for me, Beth, and tell me all about the babies, and\par
    give heaps of love to every one.\par
\par
                                "From your faithful              JO.\par
\par
    "P. S. On reading over my letter it strikes me as rather Bhaery;\par
    but I am always interested in odd people, and I really had\par
    nothing else to write about. Bless you!"\par
\par
                                                         "DECEMBER.\par
\par
    "MY PRECIOUS BETSEY,--\par
\par
    "As this is to be a scribble-scrabble letter, I direct it to\par
    you, for it may amuse you, and give you some idea of my goings\par
    on; for, though quiet, they are rather amusing, for which, oh,\par
    be joyful! After what Amy would call Herculaneum efforts, in the\par
    way of mental and moral agriculture, my young ideas begin to\par
    shoot and my little twigs to bend as I could wish. They are not\par
    so interesting to me as Tina and the boys, but I do my duty by\par
    them, and they are fond of me. Franz and Emil are jolly little\par
    lads, quite after my own heart; for the mixture of German and\par
    American spirit in them produces a constant state of\par
    effervescence. Saturday afternoons are riotous times, whether\par
    spent in the house or out; for on pleasant days they all go to\par
    walk, like a seminary, with the Professor and myself to keep\par
    order; and then such fun!\par
\par
    "We are very good friends now, and I've begun to take lessons. I\par
    really couldn't help it, and it all came about in such a droll\par
    way that I must tell you. To begin at the beginning, Mrs. Kirke\par
    called to me, one day, as I passed Mr. Bhaer's room, where she\par
    was rummaging.\par
\par
    "'Did you ever see such a den, my dear? Just come and help me\par
    put these books to rights, for I've turned everything upside\par
    down, trying to discover what he has done with the six new\par
    handkerchiefs I gave him not long ago.'\par
\par
    "I went in, and while we worked I looked about me, for it was 'a\par
    den,' to be sure. Books and papers everywhere; a broken\par
    meerschaum, and an old flute over the mantel-piece as if done\par
    with; a ragged bird, without any tail, chirped on one\par
    window-seat, and a box of white mice adorned the other;\par
    half-finished boats and bits of string lay among the\par
    manuscripts; dirty little boots stood drying before the fire;\par
    and traces of the dearly beloved boys, for whom he makes a slave\par
    of himself, were to be seen all over the room. After a grand\par
    rummage three of the missing articles were found,--one over the\par
    bird-cage, one covered with ink, and a third burnt brown, having\par
    been used as a holder.\par
\par
    "'Such a man!' laughed good-natured Mrs. K., as she put the\par
    relics in the rag-bag. 'I suppose the others are torn up to rig\par
    ships, bandage cut fingers, or make kite-tails. It's dreadful,\par
    but I can't scold him: he's so absent-minded and good-natured,\par
    he lets those boys ride over him rough-shod. I agreed to do his\par
    washing and mending, but he forgets to give out his things and I\par
    forget to look them over, so he comes to a sad pass sometimes.'\par
\par
    "'Let me mend them,' said I. 'I don't mind it, and he needn't\par
    know. I'd like to,--he's so kind to me about bringing my letters\par
    and lending books.'\par
\par
    "So I have got his things in order, and knit heels into two\par
    pairs of the socks,--for they were boggled out of shape with his\par
    queer darns. Nothing was said, and I hoped he wouldn't find it\par
    out, but one day last week he caught me at it. Hearing the\par
    lessons he gives to others has interested and amused me so much\par
    that I took a fancy to learn; for Tina runs in and out, leaving\par
    the door open, and I can hear. I had been sitting near this\par
    door, finishing off the last sock, and trying to understand what\par
    he said to a new scholar, who is as stupid as I am. The girl had\par
    gone, and I thought he had also, it was so still, and I was\par
    busily gabbling over a verb, and rocking to and fro in a most\par
    absurd way, when a little crow made me look up, and there was\par
    Mr. Bhaer looking and laughing quietly, while he made signs to\par
    Tina not to betray him.\par
\par
    "'So!' he said, as I stopped and stared like a goose, 'you peep\par
    at me, I peep at you, and that is not bad; but see, I am not\par
    pleasanting when I say, haf you a wish for German?'\par
\par
    "'Yes; but you are too busy. I am too stupid to learn,' I\par
    blundered out, as red as a peony.\par
\par
    "'Prut! we will make the time, and we fail not to find the\par
    sense. At efening I shall gif a little lesson with much\par
    gladness; for, look you, Mees Marsch, I haf this debt to pay,'\par
    and he pointed to my work. 'Yes, they say to one another, these\par
    so kind ladies, "he is a stupid old fellow; he will see not what\par
    we do; he will never opserve that his sock-heels go not in holes\par
    any more, he will think his buttons grow out new when they fall,\par
    and believe that strings make theirselves." Ah! but I haf an\par
    eye, and I see much. I haf a heart, and I feel the thanks for\par
    this. Come, a little lesson then and now, or no more good fairy\par
    works for me and mine.'\par
\par
    "Of course I couldn't say anything after that, and as it really\par
    is a splendid opportunity, I made the bargain, and we began. I\par
    took four lessons, and then I stuck fast in a grammatical bog.\par
    The Professor was very patient with me, but it must have been\par
    torment to him, and now and then he'd look at me with such an\par
    expression of mild despair that it was a toss-up with me whether\par
    to laugh or cry. I tried both ways; and when it came to a sniff\par
    of utter mortification and woe, he just threw the grammar on to\par
    the floor, and marched out of the room. I felt myself disgraced\par
    and deserted forever, but didn't blame him a particle, and was\par
    scrambling my papers together, meaning to rush upstairs and\par
    shake myself hard, when in he came, as brisk and beaming as if\par
    I'd covered myself with glory.\par
\par
    "'Now we shall try a new way. You and I will read these pleasant\par
    little Märchen together, and dig no more in that dry book, that\par
    goes in the corner for making us trouble.'\par
\par
    "He spoke so kindly, and opened Hans Andersen's fairy tales so\par
    invitingly before me, that I was more ashamed than ever, and\par
    went at my lesson in a neck-or-nothing style that seemed to\par
    amuse him immensely. I forgot my bashfulness, and pegged away\par
    (no other word will express it) with all my might, tumbling over\par
    long words, pronouncing according to the inspiration of the\par
    minute, and doing my very best. When I finished reading my first\par
    page, and stopped for breath, he clapped his hands and cried\par
    out, in his hearty way, 'Das ist gute! Now we go well! My turn.\par
    I do him in German; gif me your ear.' And away he went, rumbling\par
    out the words with his strong voice, and a relish which was good\par
    to see as well as hear. Fortunately the story was the 'Constant\par
    Tin Soldier,' which is droll, you know, so I could laugh,--and I\par
    did,--though I didn't understand half he read, for I couldn't\par
    help it, he was so earnest, I so excited, and the whole thing so\par
    comical.\par
\par
    "After that we got on better, and now I read my lessons pretty\par
    well; for this way of studying suits me, and I can see that the\par
    grammar gets tucked into the tales and poetry as one gives pills\par
    in jelly. I like it very much, and he doesn't seem tired of it\par
    yet,--which is very good of him, isn't it? I mean to give him\par
    something on Christmas, for I dare not offer money. Tell me\par
    something nice, Marmee.\par
\par
    "I'm glad Laurie seems so happy and busy, that he has given up\par
    smoking, and lets his hair grow. You see Beth manages him better\par
    than I did. I'm not jealous, dear; do your best, only don't make\par
    a saint of him. I'm afraid I couldn't like him without a spice\par
    of human naughtiness. Read him bits of my letters. I haven't\par
    time to write much, and that will do just as well. Thank Heaven\par
    Beth continues so comfortable."\par
\par
\par
                                                           "JANUARY.\par
\par
    "A Happy New Year to you all, my dearest family, which of course\par
    includes Mr. L. and a young man by the name of Teddy. I can't\par
    tell you how much I enjoyed your Christmas bundle, for I didn't\par
    get it till night, and had given up hoping. Your letter came in\par
    the morning, but you said nothing about a parcel, meaning it\par
    for a surprise; so I was disappointed, for I'd had a 'kind of a\par
    feeling' that you wouldn't forget me. I felt a little low in my\par
    mind, as I sat up in my room, after tea; and when the big,\par
    muddy, battered-looking bundle was brought to me, I just hugged\par
    it, and pranced. It was so _homey_ and refreshing, that I sat\par
    down on the floor and read and looked and ate and laughed and\par
    cried, in my usual absurd way. The things were just what I\par
    wanted, and all the better for being made instead of bought.\par
    Beth's new 'ink-bib' was capital; and Hannah's box of hard\par
    gingerbread will be a treasure. I'll be sure and wear the nice\par
    flannels you sent, Marmee, and read carefully the books father\par
    has marked. Thank you all, heaps and heaps!\par
\par
 [Illustration: I sat down upon the floor and read and looked and ate]\par
\par
    "Speaking of books reminds me that I'm getting rich in that\par
    line for, on New Year's Day, Mr. Bhaer gave me a fine\par
    Shakespeare. It is one he values much, and I've often admired\par
    it, set up in the place of honor, with his German Bible, Plato,\par
    Homer, and Milton; so you may imagine how I felt when he\par
    brought it down, without its cover, and showed me my name in\par
    it, 'from my friend Friedrich Bhaer.'\par
\par
    "'You say often you wish a library: here I gif you one; for\par
    between these lids (he meant covers) is many books in one. Read\par
    him well, and he will help you much; for the study of character\par
    in this book will help you to read it in the world and paint it\par
    with your pen.'\par
\par
    "I thanked him as well as I could, and talk now about 'my\par
    library,' as if I had a hundred books. I never knew how much\par
    there was in Shakespeare before; but then I never had a Bhaer to\par
    explain it to me. Now _don't_ laugh at his horrid name; it isn't\par
    pronounced either Bear or Beer, as people _will_ say it, but\par
    something between the two, as only Germans can give it. I'm glad\par
    you both like what I tell you about him, and hope you will know\par
    him some day. Mother would admire his warm heart, father his\par
    wise head. I admire both, and feel rich in my new 'friend\par
    Friedrich Bhaer.'\par
\par
    "Not having much money, or knowing what he'd like, I got several\par
    little things, and put them about the room, where he would find\par
    them unexpectedly. They were useful, pretty, or funny,--a new\par
    standish on his table, a little vase for his flower,--he always\par
    has one, or a bit of green in a glass, to keep him fresh, he\par
    says,--and a holder for his blower, so that he needn't burn up\par
    what Amy calls 'mouchoirs.' I made it like those Beth\par
    invented,--a big butterfly with a fat body, and black and yellow\par
    wings, worsted feelers, and bead eyes. It took his fancy\par
    immensely, and he put it on his mantel-piece as an article of\par
    _vertu_; so it was rather a failure after all. Poor as he is, he\par
    didn't forget a servant or a child in the house; and not a soul\par
    here, from the French laundry-woman to Miss Norton, forgot him.\par
    I was so glad of that.\par
\par
    "They got up a masquerade, and had a gay time New Year's Eve. I\par
    didn't mean to go down, having no dress; but at the last minute,\par
    Mrs. Kirke remembered some old brocades, and Miss Norton lent me\par
    lace and feathers; so I dressed up as Mrs. Malaprop, and sailed\par
    in with a mask on. No one knew me, for I disguised my voice, and\par
    no one dreamed of the silent, haughty Miss March (for they think\par
    I am very stiff and cool, most of them; and so I am to\par
    whipper-snappers) could dance and dress, and burst out into a\par
    'nice derangement of epitaphs, like an allegory on the banks of\par
    the Nile.' I enjoyed it very much; and when we unmasked, it was\par
    fun to see them stare at me. I heard one of the young men tell\par
    another that he knew I'd been an actress; in fact, he thought he\par
    remembered seeing me at one of the minor theatres. Meg will\par
    relish that joke. Mr. Bhaer was Nick Bottom, and Tina was\par
    Titania,--a perfect little fairy in his arms. To see them dance\par
    was 'quite a landscape,' to use a Teddyism.\par
\par
    "I had a very happy New Year, after all; and when I thought it\par
    over in my room, I felt as if I was getting on a little in spite\par
    of my many failures; for I'm cheerful all the time now, work\par
    with a will, and take more interest in other people than I used\par
    to, which is satisfactory. Bless you all! Ever your loving\par
\par
                                                                JO."\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
           [Illustration: In the presence of three gentlemen]\par
\par
                                 XXXIV.\par
\par
                               A FRIEND.\par
\par
\par
Though very happy in the social atmosphere about her, and very busy with\par
the daily work that earned her bread, and made it sweeter for the\par
effort, Jo still found time for literary labors. The purpose which now\par
took possession of her was a natural one to a poor and ambitious girl;\par
but the means she took to gain her end were not the best. She saw that\par
money conferred power: money and power, therefore, she resolved to have;\par
not to be used for herself alone, but for those whom she loved more than\par
self.\par
\par
The dream of filling home with comforts, giving Beth everything she\par
wanted, from strawberries in winter to an organ in her bedroom; going\par
abroad herself, and always having _more_ than enough, so that she might\par
indulge in the luxury of charity, had been for years Jo's most cherished\par
castle in the air.\par
\par
The prize-story experience had seemed to open a way which might, after\par
long travelling and much up-hill work lead to this delightful _château\par
en Espagne_. But the novel disaster quenched her courage for a time, for\par
public opinion is a giant which has frightened stouter-hearted Jacks on\par
bigger bean-stalks than hers. Like that immortal hero, she reposed\par
awhile after the first attempt, which resulted in a tumble, and the\par
least lovely of the giant's treasures, if I remember rightly. But the\par
"up again and take another" spirit was as strong in Jo as in Jack; so\par
she scrambled up, on the shady side this time, and got more booty, but\par
nearly left behind her what was far more precious than the money-bags.\par
\par
She took to writing sensation stories; for in those dark ages, even\par
all-perfect America read rubbish. She told no one, but concocted a\par
"thrilling tale," and boldly carried it herself to Mr. Dashwood, editor\par
of the "Weekly Volcano." She had never read "Sartor Resartus," but she\par
had a womanly instinct that clothes possess an influence more powerful\par
over many than the worth of character or the magic of manners. So she\par
dressed herself in her best, and, trying to persuade herself that she\par
was neither excited nor nervous, bravely climbed two pairs of dark and\par
dirty stairs to find herself in a disorderly room, a cloud of\par
cigar-smoke, and the presence of three gentlemen, sitting with their\par
heels rather higher than their hats, which articles of dress none of\par
them took the trouble to remove on her appearance. Somewhat daunted by\par
this reception, Jo hesitated on the threshold, murmuring in much\par
embarrassment,--\par
\par
"Excuse me, I was looking for the 'Weekly Volcano' office; I wished to\par
see Mr. Dashwood."\par
\par
Down went the highest pair of heels, up rose the smokiest gentleman,\par
and, carefully cherishing his cigar between his fingers, he advanced,\par
with a nod, and a countenance expressive of nothing but sleep. Feeling\par
that she must get through the matter somehow, Jo produced her\par
manuscript, and, blushing redder and redder with each sentence,\par
blundered out fragments of the little speech carefully prepared for the\par
occasion.\par
\par
"A friend of mine desired me to offer--a story--just as an\par
experiment--would like your opinion--be glad to write more if this\par
suits."\par
\par
While she blushed and blundered, Mr. Dashwood had taken the manuscript,\par
and was turning over the leaves with a pair of rather dirty fingers, and\par
casting critical glances up and down the neat pages.\par
\par
"Not a first attempt, I take it?" observing that the pages were\par
numbered, covered only on one side, and not tied up with a ribbon,--sure\par
sign of a novice.\par
\par
"No, sir; she has had some experience, and got a prize for a tale in the\par
'Blarneystone Banner.'"\par
\par
"Oh, did she?" and Mr. Dashwood gave Jo a quick look, which seemed to\par
take note of everything she had on, from the bow in her bonnet to the\par
buttons on her boots. "Well, you can leave it, if you like. We've more\par
of this sort of thing on hand than we know what to do with at present;\par
but I'll run my eye over it, and give you an answer next week."\par
\par
Now, Jo did _not_ like to leave it, for Mr. Dashwood didn't suit her at\par
all; but, under the circumstances, there was nothing for her to do but\par
bow and walk away, looking particularly tall and dignified, as she was\par
apt to do when nettled or abashed. Just then she was both; for it was\par
perfectly evident, from the knowing glances exchanged among the\par
gentlemen, that her little fiction of "my friend" was considered a good\par
joke; and a laugh, produced by some inaudible remark of the editor, as\par
he closed the door, completed her discomfiture. Half resolving never to\par
return, she went home, and worked off her irritation by stitching\par
pinafores vigorously; and in an hour or two was cool enough to laugh\par
over the scene, and long for next week.\par
\par
When she went again, Mr. Dashwood was alone, whereat she rejoiced; Mr.\par
Dashwood was much wider awake than before, which was agreeable; and Mr.\par
Dashwood was not too deeply absorbed in a cigar to remember his manners:\par
so the second interview was much more comfortable than the first.\par
\par
"We'll take this" (editors never say I), "if you don't object to a few\par
alterations. It's too long, but omitting the passages I've marked will\par
make it just the right length," he said, in a business-like tone.\par
\par
Jo hardly knew her own MS. again, so crumpled and underscored were its\par
pages and paragraphs; but, feeling as a tender parent might on being\par
asked to cut off her baby's legs in order that it might fit into a new\par
cradle, she looked at the marked passages, and was surprised to find\par
that all the moral reflections--which she had carefully put in as\par
ballast for much romance--had been stricken out.\par
\par
"But, sir, I thought every story should have some sort of a moral, so I\par
took care to have a few of my sinners repent."\par
\par
Mr. Dashwood's editorial gravity relaxed into a smile, for Jo had\par
forgotten her "friend," and spoken as only an author could.\par
\par
"People want to be amused, not preached at, you know. Morals don't sell\par
nowadays;" which was not quite a correct statement, by the way.\par
\par
"You think it would do with these alterations, then?"\par
\par
"Yes; it's a new plot, and pretty well worked up--language good, and so\par
on," was Mr. Dashwood's affable reply.\par
\par
"What do you--that is, what compensation--" began Jo, not exactly\par
knowing how to express herself.\par
\par
"Oh, yes, well, we give from twenty-five to thirty for things of this\par
sort. Pay when it comes out," returned Mr. Dashwood, as if that point\par
had escaped him; such trifles often do escape the editorial mind, it is\par
said.\par
\par
"Very well; you can have it," said Jo, handing back the story, with a\par
satisfied air; for, after the dollar-a-column work, even twenty-five\par
seemed good pay.\par
\par
"Shall I tell my friend you will take another if she has one better than\par
this?" asked Jo, unconscious of her little slip of the tongue, and\par
emboldened by her success.\par
\par
"Well, we'll look at it; can't promise to take it. Tell her to make it\par
short and spicy, and never mind the moral. What name would your friend\par
like to put to it?" in a careless tone.\par
\par
"None at all, if you please; she doesn't wish her name to appear, and\par
has no _nom de plume_," said Jo, blushing in spite of herself.\par
\par
"Just as she likes, of course. The tale will be out next week; will you\par
call for the money, or shall I send it?" asked Mr. Dashwood, who felt a\par
natural desire to know who his new contributor might be.\par
\par
"I'll call. Good morning, sir."\par
\par
As she departed, Mr. Dashwood put up his feet, with the graceful remark,\par
"Poor and proud, as usual, but she'll do."\par
\par
Following Mr. Dashwood's directions, and making Mrs. Northbury her\par
model, Jo rashly took a plunge into the frothy sea of sensational\par
literature; but, thanks to the life-preserver thrown her by a friend,\par
she came up again, not much the worse for her ducking.\par
\par
Like most young scribblers, she went abroad for her characters and\par
scenery; and banditti, counts, gypsies, nuns, and duchesses appeared\par
upon her stage, and played their parts with as much accuracy and spirit\par
as could be expected. Her readers were not particular about such trifles\par
as grammar, punctuation, and probability, and Mr. Dashwood graciously\par
permitted her to fill his columns at the lowest prices, not thinking it\par
necessary to tell her that the real cause of his hospitality was the\par
fact that one of his hacks, on being offered higher wages, had basely\par
left him in the lurch.\par
\par
She soon became interested in her work, for her emaciated purse grew\par
stout, and the little hoard she was making to take Beth to the mountains\par
next summer grew slowly but surely as the weeks passed. One thing\par
disturbed her satisfaction, and that was that she did not tell them at\par
home. She had a feeling that father and mother would not approve, and\par
preferred to have her own way first, and beg pardon afterward. It was\par
easy to keep her secret, for no name appeared with her stories; Mr.\par
Dashwood had, of course, found it out very soon, but promised to be\par
dumb; and, for a wonder, kept his word.\par
\par
She thought it would do her no harm, for she sincerely meant to write\par
nothing of which she should be ashamed, and quieted all pricks of\par
conscience by anticipations of the happy minute when she should show her\par
earnings and laugh over her well-kept secret.\par
\par
But Mr. Dashwood rejected any but thrilling tales; and, as thrills could\par
not be produced except by harrowing up the souls of the readers, history\par
and romance, land and sea, science and art, police records and lunatic\par
asylums, had to be ransacked for the purpose. Jo soon found that her\par
innocent experience had given her but few glimpses of the tragic world\par
which underlies society; so, regarding it in a business light, she set\par
about supplying her deficiencies with characteristic energy. Eager to\par
find material for stories, and bent on making them original in plot, if\par
not masterly in execution, she searched newspapers for accidents,\par
incidents, and crimes; she excited the suspicions of public librarians\par
by asking for works on poisons; she studied faces in the street, and\par
characters, good, bad, and indifferent, all about her; she delved in the\par
dust of ancient times for facts or fictions so old that they were as\par
good as new, and introduced herself to folly, sin, and misery, as well\par
as her limited opportunities allowed. She thought she was prospering\par
finely; but, unconsciously, she was beginning to desecrate some of the\par
womanliest attributes of a woman's character. She was living in bad\par
society; and, imaginary though it was, its influence affected her, for\par
she was feeding heart and fancy on dangerous and unsubstantial food, and\par
was fast brushing the innocent bloom from her nature by a premature\par
acquaintance with the darker side of life, which comes soon enough to\par
all of us.\par
\par
She was beginning to feel rather than see this, for much describing of\par
other people's passions and feelings set her to studying and speculating\par
about her own,--a morbid amusement, in which healthy young minds do not\par
voluntarily indulge. Wrong-doing always brings its own punishment; and,\par
when Jo most needed hers, she got it.\par
\par
I don't know whether the study of Shakespeare helped her to read\par
character, or the natural instinct of a woman for what was honest,\par
brave, and strong; but while endowing her imaginary heroes with every\par
perfection under the sun, Jo was discovering a live hero, who interested\par
her in spite of many human imperfections. Mr. Bhaer, in one of their\par
conversations, had advised her to study simple, true, and lovely\par
characters, wherever she found them, as good training for a writer. Jo\par
took him at his word, for she coolly turned round and studied him,--a\par
proceeding which would have much surprised him, had he known it, for the\par
worthy Professor was very humble in his own conceit.\par
\par
Why everybody liked him was what puzzled Jo, at first. He was neither\par
rich nor great, young nor handsome; in no respect what is called\par
fascinating, imposing, or brilliant; and yet he was as attractive as a\par
genial fire, and people seemed to gather about him as naturally as about\par
a warm hearth. He was poor, yet always appeared to be giving something\par
away; a stranger, yet every one was his friend; no longer young, but as\par
happy-hearted as a boy; plain and peculiar, yet his face looked\par
beautiful to many, and his oddities were freely forgiven for his sake.\par
Jo often watched him, trying to discover the charm, and, at last,\par
decided that it was benevolence which worked the miracle. If he had any\par
sorrow, "it sat with its head under its wing," and he turned only his\par
sunny side to the world. There were lines upon his forehead, but Time\par
seemed to have touched him gently, remembering how kind he was to\par
others. The pleasant curves about his mouth were the memorials of many\par
friendly words and cheery laughs; his eyes were never cold or hard, and\par
his big hand had a warm, strong grasp that was more expressive than\par
words.\par
\par
His very clothes seemed to partake of the hospitable nature of the\par
wearer. They looked as if they were at ease, and liked to make him\par
comfortable; his capacious waistcoat was suggestive of a large heart\par
underneath; his rusty coat had a social air, and the baggy pockets\par
plainly proved that little hands often went in empty and came out full;\par
his very boots were benevolent, and his collars never stiff and raspy\par
like other people's.\par
\par
"That's it!" said Jo to herself, when she at length discovered that\par
genuine good-will towards one's fellow-men could beautify and dignify\par
even a stout German teacher, who shovelled in his dinner, darned his own\par
socks, and was burdened with the name of Bhaer.\par
\par
Jo valued goodness highly, but she also possessed a most feminine\par
respect for intellect, and a little discovery which she made about the\par
Professor added much to her regard for him. He never spoke of himself,\par
and no one ever knew that in his native city he had been a man much\par
honored and esteemed for learning and integrity, till a countryman came\par
to see him, and, in a conversation with Miss Norton, divulged the\par
pleasing fact. From her Jo learned it, and liked it all the better\par
because Mr. Bhaer had never told it. She felt proud to know that he was\par
an honored Professor in Berlin, though only a poor language-master in\par
America; and his homely, hard-working life was much beautified by the\par
spice of romance which this discovery gave it.\par
\par
Another and a better gift than intellect was shown her in a most\par
unexpected manner. Miss Norton had the _entrée_ into literary society,\par
which Jo would have had no chance of seeing but for her. The solitary\par
woman felt an interest in the ambitious girl, and kindly conferred many\par
favors of this sort both on Jo and the Professor. She took them with\par
her, one night, to a select symposium, held in honor of several\par
celebrities.\par
\par
                   [Illustration: A select symposium]\par
\par
Jo went prepared to bow down and adore the mighty ones whom she had\par
worshipped with youthful enthusiasm afar off. But her reverence for\par
genius received a severe shock that night, and it took her some time to\par
recover from the discovery that the great creatures were only men and\par
women after all. Imagine her dismay, on stealing a glance of timid\par
admiration at the poet whose lines suggested an ethereal being fed on\par
"spirit, fire, and dew," to behold him devouring his supper with an\par
ardor which flushed his intellectual countenance. Turning as from a\par
fallen idol, she made other discoveries which rapidly dispelled her\par
romantic illusions. The great novelist vibrated between two decanters\par
with the regularity of a pendulum; the famous divine flirted openly with\par
one of the Madame de Staëls of the age, who looked daggers at another\par
Corinne, who was amiably satirizing her, after out-manœuvring her in\par
efforts to absorb the profound philosopher, who imbibed tea Johnsonianly\par
and appeared to slumber, the loquacity of the lady rendering speech\par
impossible. The scientific celebrities, forgetting their mollusks and\par
glacial periods, gossiped about art, while devoting themselves to\par
oysters and ices with characteristic energy; the young musician, who was\par
charming the city like a second Orpheus, talked horses; and the specimen\par
of the British nobility present happened to be the most ordinary man of\par
the party.\par
\par
Before the evening was half over, Jo felt so completely _désillusionée_,\par
that she sat down in a corner to recover herself. Mr. Bhaer soon joined\par
her, looking rather out of his element, and presently several of the\par
philosophers, each mounted on his hobby, came ambling up to hold an\par
intellectual tournament in the recess. The conversation was miles beyond\par
Jo's comprehension, but she enjoyed it, though Kant and Hegel were\par
unknown gods, the Subjective and Objective unintelligible terms; and the\par
only thing "evolved from her inner consciousness," was a bad headache\par
after it was all over. It dawned upon her gradually that the world was\par
being picked to pieces, and put together on new, and, according to the\par
talkers, on infinitely better principles than before; that religion was\par
in a fair way to be reasoned into nothingness, and intellect was to be\par
the only God. Jo knew nothing about philosophy or metaphysics of any\par
sort, but a curious excitement, half pleasurable, half painful, came\par
over her, as she listened with a sense of being turned adrift into time\par
and space, like a young balloon out on a holiday.\par
\par
She looked round to see how the Professor liked it, and found him\par
looking at her with the grimmest expression she had ever seen him wear.\par
He shook his head, and beckoned her to come away; but she was\par
fascinated, just then, by the freedom of Speculative Philosophy, and\par
kept her seat, trying to find out what the wise gentlemen intended to\par
rely upon after they had annihilated all the old beliefs.\par
\par
Now, Mr. Bhaer was a diffident man, and slow to offer his own opinions,\par
not because they were unsettled, but too sincere and earnest to be\par
lightly spoken. As he glanced from Jo to several other young people,\par
attracted by the brilliancy of the philosophic pyrotechnics, he knit his\par
brows, and longed to speak, fearing that some inflammable young soul\par
would be led astray by the rockets, to find, when the display was over,\par
that they had only an empty stick or a scorched hand.\par
\par
He bore it as long as he could; but when he was appealed to for an\par
opinion, he blazed up with honest indignation, and defended religion\par
with all the eloquence of truth,--an eloquence which made his broken\par
English musical, and his plain face beautiful. He had a hard fight, for\par
the wise men argued well; but he didn't know when he was beaten, and\par
stood to his colors like a man. Somehow, as he talked, the world got\par
right again to Jo; the old beliefs, that had lasted so long, seemed\par
better than the new; God was not a blind force, and immortality was not\par
a pretty fable, but a blessed fact. She felt as if she had solid ground\par
under her feet again; and when Mr. Bhaer paused, out-talked, but not one\par
whit convinced, Jo wanted to clap her hands and thank him.\par
\par
She did neither; but she remembered this scene, and gave the Professor\par
her heartiest respect, for she knew it cost him an effort to speak out\par
then and there, because his conscience would not let him be silent. She\par
began to see that character is a better possession than money, rank,\par
intellect, or beauty; and to feel that if greatness is what a wise man\par
has defined it to be, "truth, reverence, and good-will," then her friend\par
Friedrich Bhaer was not only good, but great.\par
\par
This belief strengthened daily. She valued his esteem, she coveted his\par
respect, she wanted to be worthy of his friendship; and, just when the\par
wish was sincerest, she came near losing everything. It all grew out of\par
a cocked hat; for one evening the Professor came in to give Jo her\par
lesson, with a paper soldier-cap on his head, which Tina had put there,\par
and he had forgotten to take off.\par
\par
"It's evident he doesn't look in his glass before coming down," thought\par
Jo, with a smile, as he said "Goot efening," and sat soberly down, quite\par
unconscious of the ludicrous contrast between his subject and his\par
head-gear, for he was going to read her the "Death of Wallenstein."\par
\par
      [Illustration: He doesn't prink at his glass before coming]\par
\par
She said nothing at first, for she liked to hear him laugh out his big,\par
hearty laugh, when anything funny happened, so she left him to discover\par
it for himself, and presently forgot all about it; for to hear a German\par
read Schiller is rather an absorbing occupation. After the reading came\par
the lesson, which was a lively one, for Jo was in a gay mood that night,\par
and the cocked-hat kept her eyes dancing with merriment. The Professor\par
didn't know what to make of her, and stopped at last, to ask, with an\par
air of mild surprise that was irresistible,--\par
\par
"Mees Marsch, for what do you laugh in your master's face? Haf you no\par
respect for me, that you go on so bad?"\par
\par
"How can I be respectful, sir, when you forget to take your hat off?"\par
said Jo.\par
\par
Lifting his hand to his head, the absent-minded Professor gravely felt\par
and removed the little cocked-hat, looked at it a minute, and then threw\par
back his head, and laughed like a merry bass-viol.\par
\par
"Ah! I see him now; it is that imp Tina who makes me a fool with my cap.\par
Well, it is nothing; but see you, if this lesson goes not well, you too\par
shall wear him."\par
\par
But the lesson did not go at all for a few minutes, because Mr. Bhaer\par
caught sight of a picture on the hat, and, unfolding it, said, with an\par
air of great disgust,--\par
\par
"I wish these papers did not come in the house; they are not for\par
children to see, nor young people to read. It is not well, and I haf no\par
patience with those who make this harm."\par
\par
Jo glanced at the sheet, and saw a pleasing illustration composed of a\par
lunatic, a corpse, a villain, and a viper. She did not like it; but the\par
impulse that made her turn it over was not one of displeasure, but fear,\par
because, for a minute, she fancied the paper was the "Volcano." It was\par
not, however, and her panic subsided as she remembered that, even if it\par
had been, and one of her own tales in it, there would have been no name\par
to betray her. She had betrayed herself, however, by a look and a blush;\par
for, though an absent man, the Professor saw a good deal more than\par
people fancied. He knew that Jo wrote, and had met her down among the\par
newspaper offices more than once; but as she never spoke of it, he asked\par
no questions, in spite of a strong desire to see her work. Now it\par
occurred to him that she was doing what she was ashamed to own, and it\par
troubled him. He did not say to himself, "It is none of my business;\par
I've no right to say anything," as many people would have done; he only\par
remembered that she was young and poor, a girl far away from mother's\par
love and father's care; and he was moved to help her with an impulse as\par
quick and natural as that which would prompt him to put out his hand to\par
save a baby from a puddle. All this flashed through his mind in a\par
minute, but not a trace of it appeared in his face; and by the time the\par
paper was turned, and Jo's needle threaded, he was ready to say quite\par
naturally, but very gravely,--\par
\par
"Yes, you are right to put it from you. I do not like to think that good\par
young girls should see such things. They are made pleasant to some, but\par
I would more rather give my boys gunpowder to play with than this bad\par
trash."\par
\par
"All may not be bad, only silly, you know; and if there is a demand for\par
it, I don't see any harm in supplying it. Many very respectable people\par
make an honest living out of what are called sensation stories," said\par
Jo, scratching gathers so energetically that a row of little slits\par
followed her pin.\par
\par
"There is a demand for whiskey, but I think you and I do not care to\par
sell it. If the respectable people knew what harm they did, they would\par
not feel that the living _was_ honest. They haf no right to put poison\par
in the sugar-plum, and let the small ones eat it. No; they should think\par
a little, and sweep mud in the street before they do this thing."\par
\par
Mr. Bhaer spoke warmly, and walked to the fire, crumpling the paper in\par
his hands. Jo sat still, looking as if the fire had come to her; for her\par
cheeks burned long after the cocked hat had turned to smoke, and gone\par
harmlessly up the chimney.\par
\par
"I should like much to send all the rest after him," muttered the\par
Professor, coming back with a relieved air.\par
\par
Jo thought what a blaze her pile of papers upstairs would make, and her\par
hard-earned money lay rather heavily on her conscience at that minute.\par
Then she thought consolingly to herself, "Mine are not like that; they\par
are only silly, never bad, so I won't be worried;" and taking up her\par
book, she said, with a studious face,--\par
\par
"Shall we go on, sir? I'll be very good and proper now."\par
\par
"I shall hope so," was all he said, but he meant more than she imagined;\par
and the grave, kind look he gave her made her feel as if the words\par
"Weekly Volcano" were printed in large type on her forehead.\par
\par
As soon as she went to her room, she got out her papers, and carefully\par
re-read every one of her stories. Being a little short-sighted, Mr.\par
Bhaer sometimes used eye-glasses, and Jo had tried them once, smiling to\par
see how they magnified the fine print of her book; now she seemed to\par
have got on the Professor's mental or moral spectacles also; for the\par
faults of these poor stories glared at her dreadfully, and filled her\par
with dismay.\par
\par
"They _are_ trash, and will soon be worse than trash if I go on; for\par
each is more sensational than the last. I've gone blindly on, hurting\par
myself and other people, for the sake of money; I know it's so, for I\par
can't read this stuff in sober earnest without being horribly ashamed of\par
it; and what _should_ I do if they were seen at home, or Mr. Bhaer got\par
hold of them?"\par
\par
Jo turned hot at the bare idea, and stuffed the whole bundle into her\par
stove, nearly setting the chimney afire with the blaze.\par
\par
         [Illustration: Jo stuffed the whole bundle into the stove]\par
\par
"Yes, that's the best place for such inflammable nonsense; I'd better\par
burn the house down, I suppose, than let other people blow themselves up\par
with my gunpowder," she thought, as she watched the "Demon of the Jura"\par
whisk away, a little black cinder with fiery eyes.\par
\par
But when nothing remained of all her three months' work except a heap of\par
ashes, and the money in her lap, Jo looked sober, as she sat on the\par
floor, wondering what she ought to do about her wages.\par
\par
"I think I haven't done much harm _yet_, and may keep this to pay for my\par
time," she said, after a long meditation, adding impatiently, "I almost\par
wish I hadn't any conscience, it's so inconvenient. If I didn't care\par
about doing right, and didn't feel uncomfortable when doing wrong, I\par
should get on capitally. I can't help wishing sometimes, that father and\par
mother hadn't been so particular about such things."\par
\par
Ah, Jo, instead of wishing that, thank God that "father and mother\par
_were_ particular," and pity from your heart those who have no such\par
guardians to hedge them round with principles which may seem like\par
prison-walls to impatient youth, but which will prove sure foundations\par
to build character upon in womanhood.\par
\par
Jo wrote no more sensational stories, deciding that the money did not\par
pay for her share of the sensation; but, going to the other extreme, as\par
is the way with people of her stamp, she took a course of Mrs. Sherwood,\par
Miss Edgeworth, and Hannah More; and then produced a tale which might\par
have been more properly called an essay or a sermon, so intensely moral\par
was it. She had her doubts about it from the beginning; for her lively\par
fancy and girlish romance felt as ill at ease in the new style as she\par
would have done masquerading in the stiff and cumbrous costume of the\par
last century. She sent this didactic gem to several markets, but it\par
found no purchaser; and she was inclined to agree with Mr. Dashwood,\par
that morals didn't sell.\par
\par
Then she tried a child's story, which she could easily have disposed of\par
if she had not been mercenary enough to demand filthy lucre for it. The\par
only person who offered enough to make it worth her while to try\par
juvenile literature was a worthy gentleman who felt it his mission to\par
convert all the world to his particular belief. But much as she liked to\par
write for children, Jo could not consent to depict all her naughty boys\par
as being eaten by bears or tossed by mad bulls, because they did not go\par
to a particular Sabbath-school, nor all the good infants, who did go, as\par
rewarded by every kind of bliss, from gilded gingerbread to escorts of\par
angels, when they departed this life with psalms or sermons on their\par
lisping tongues. So nothing came of these trials; and Jo corked up her\par
inkstand, and said, in a fit of very wholesome humility,--\par
\par
"I don't know anything; I'll wait till I do before I try again, and,\par
meantime, 'sweep mud in the street,' if I can't do better; that's\par
honest, at least;" which decision proved that her second tumble down the\par
bean-stalk had done her some good.\par
\par
While these internal revolutions were going on, her external life had\par
been as busy and uneventful as usual; and if she sometimes looked\par
serious or a little sad no one observed it but Professor Bhaer. He did\par
it so quietly that Jo never knew he was watching to see if she would\par
accept and profit by his reproof; but she stood the test, and he was\par
satisfied; for, though no words passed between them, he knew that she\par
had given up writing. Not only did he guess it by the fact that the\par
second finger of her right hand was no longer inky, but she spent her\par
evenings downstairs now, was met no more among newspaper offices, and\par
studied with a dogged patience, which assured him that she was bent on\par
occupying her mind with something useful, if not pleasant.\par
\par
He helped her in many ways, proving himself a true friend, and Jo was\par
happy; for, while her pen lay idle, she was learning other lessons\par
beside German, and laying a foundation for the sensation story of her\par
own life.\par
\par
It was a pleasant winter and a long one, for she did not leave Mrs.\par
Kirke till June. Every one seemed sorry when the time came; the children\par
were inconsolable, and Mr. Bhaer's hair stuck straight up all over his\par
head, for he always rumpled it wildly when disturbed in mind.\par
\par
"Going home? Ah, you are happy that you haf a home to go in," he said,\par
when she told him, and sat silently pulling his beard, in the corner,\par
while she held a little levee on that last evening.\par
\par
She was going early, so she bade them all good-by over night; and when\par
his turn came, she said warmly,--\par
\par
"Now, sir, you won't forget to come and see us, if you ever travel our\par
way, will you? I'll never forgive you if you do, for I want them all to\par
know my friend."\par
\par
"Do you? Shall I come?" he asked, looking down at her with an eager\par
expression which she did not see.\par
\par
"Yes, come next month; Laurie graduates then, and you'd enjoy\par
Commencement as something new."\par
\par
"That is your best friend, of whom you speak?" he said, in an altered\par
tone.\par
\par
"Yes, my boy Teddy; I'm very proud of him, and should like you to see\par
him."\par
\par
Jo looked up then, quite unconscious of anything but her own pleasure in\par
the prospect of showing them to one another. Something in Mr. Bhaer's\par
face suddenly recalled the fact that she might find Laurie more than a\par
"best friend," and, simply because she particularly wished not to look\par
as if anything was the matter, she involuntarily began to blush; and the\par
more she tried not to, the redder she grew. If it had not been for Tina\par
on her knee, she didn't know what would have become of her. Fortunately,\par
the child was moved to hug her; so she managed to hide her face an\par
instant, hoping the Professor did not see it. But he did, and his own\par
changed again from that momentary anxiety to its usual expression, as he\par
said cordially,--\par
\par
"I fear I shall not make the time for that, but I wish the friend much\par
success, and you all happiness. Gott bless you!" and with that, he shook\par
hands warmly, shouldered Tina, and went away.\par
\par
But after the boys were abed, he sat long before his fire, with the\par
tired look on his face, and the "_heimweh_," or homesickness, lying\par
heavy at his heart. Once, when he remembered Jo, as she sat with the\par
little child in her lap and that new softness in her face, he leaned his\par
head on his hands a minute, and then roamed about the room, as if in\par
search of something that he could not find.\par
\par
"It is not for me; I must not hope it now," he said to himself, with a\par
sigh that was almost a groan; then, as if reproaching himself for the\par
longing that he could not repress, he went and kissed the two towzled\par
heads upon the pillow, took down his seldom-used meerschaum, and opened\par
his Plato.\par
\par
He did his best, and did it manfully; but I don't think he found that a\par
pair of rampant boys, a pipe, or even the divine Plato, were very\par
satisfactory substitutes for wife and child and home.\par
\par
Early as it was, he was at the station, next morning, to see Jo off;\par
and, thanks to him, she began her solitary journey with the pleasant\par
memory of a familiar face smiling its farewell, a bunch of violets to\par
keep her company, and, best of all, the happy thought,--\par
\par
"Well, the winter's gone, and I've written no books, earned no fortune;\par
but I've made a friend worth having, and I'll try to keep him all my\par
life."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
          [Illustration: He put the sisters into the carriage]\par
\par
                                 XXXV.\par
\par
                               HEARTACHE.\par
\par
\par
Whatever his motive might have been, Laurie studied to some purpose that\par
year, for he graduated with honor, and gave the Latin oration with the\par
grace of a Phillips and the eloquence of a Demosthenes, so his friends\par
said. They were all there, his grandfather,--oh, so proud!--Mr. and Mrs.\par
March, John and Meg, Jo and Beth, and all exulted over him with the\par
sincere admiration which boys make light of at the time, but fail to win\par
from the world by any after-triumphs.\par
\par
"I've got to stay for this confounded supper, but I shall be home early\par
to-morrow; you'll come and meet me as usual, girls?" Laurie said, as he\par
put the sisters into the carriage after the joys of the day were over.\par
He said "girls," but he meant Jo, for she was the only one who kept up\par
the old custom; she had not the heart to refuse her splendid, successful\par
boy anything, and answered warmly,--\par
\par
"I'll come, Teddy, rain or shine, and march before you, playing '_Hail\par
the conquering hero comes_,' on a jews-harp."\par
\par
Laurie thanked her with a look that made her think, in a sudden panic,\par
"Oh, deary me! I know he'll say something, and then what shall I do?"\par
\par
Evening meditation and morning work somewhat allayed her fears, and\par
having decided that she wouldn't be vain enough to think people were\par
going to propose when she had given them every reason to know what her\par
answer would be, she set forth at the appointed time, hoping Teddy\par
wouldn't do anything to make her hurt his poor little feelings. A call\par
at Meg's, and a refreshing sniff and sip at the Daisy and Demijohn,\par
still further fortified her for the _tête-à-tête_, but when she saw a\par
stalwart figure looming in the distance, she had a strong desire to turn\par
about and run away.\par
\par
"Where's the jews-harp, Jo?" cried Laurie, as soon as he was within\par
speaking distance.\par
\par
"I forgot it;" and Jo took heart again, for that salutation could not be\par
called lover-like.\par
\par
She always used to take his arm on these occasions; now she did not, and\par
he made no complaint, which was a bad sign, but talked on rapidly about\par
all sorts of far-away subjects, till they turned from the road into the\par
little path that led homeward through the grove. Then he walked more\par
slowly, suddenly lost his fine flow of language, and, now and then, a\par
dreadful pause occurred. To rescue the conversation from one of the\par
wells of silence into which it kept falling, Jo said hastily,--\par
\par
"Now you must have a good long holiday!"\par
\par
"I intend to."\par
\par
Something in his resolute tone made Jo look up quickly to find him\par
looking down at her with an expression that assured her the dreaded\par
moment had come, and made her put out her hand with an imploring,--\par
\par
"No, Teddy, please don't!"\par
\par
"I will, and you _must_ hear me. It's no use, Jo; we've got to have it\par
out, and the sooner the better for both of us," he answered, getting\par
flushed and excited all at once.\par
\par
"Say what you like, then; I'll listen," said Jo, with a desperate sort\par
of patience.\par
\par
Laurie was a young lover, but he was in earnest, and meant to "have it\par
out," if he died in the attempt; so he plunged into the subject with\par
characteristic impetuosity, saying in a voice that _would_ get choky now\par
and then, in spite of manful efforts to keep it steady,--\par
\par
"I've loved you ever since I've known you, Jo; couldn't help it, you've\par
been so good to me. I've tried to show it, but you wouldn't let me; now\par
I'm going to make you hear, and give me an answer, for I _can't_ go on\par
so any longer."\par
\par
"I wanted to save you this; I thought you'd understand--" began Jo,\par
finding it a great deal harder than she expected.\par
\par
"I know you did; but girls are so queer you never know what they mean.\par
They say No when they mean Yes, and drive a man out of his wits just for\par
the fun of it," returned Laurie, entrenching himself behind an\par
undeniable fact.\par
\par
"_I_ don't. I never wanted to make you care for me so, and I went away\par
to keep you from it if I could."\par
\par
"I thought so; it was like you, but it was no use. I only loved you all\par
the more, and I worked hard to please you, and I gave up billiards and\par
everything you didn't like, and waited and never complained, for I hoped\par
you'd love me, though I'm not half good enough--" here there was a choke\par
that couldn't be controlled, so he decapitated buttercups while he\par
cleared his "confounded throat."\par
\par
"Yes, you are; you're a great deal too good for me, and I'm so grateful\par
to you, and so proud and fond of you, I don't see why I can't love you\par
as you want me to. I've tried, but I can't change the feeling, and it\par
would be a lie to say I do when I don't."\par
\par
"Really, truly, Jo?"\par
\par
He stopped short, and caught both her hands as he put his question with\par
a look that she did not soon forget.\par
\par
"Really, truly, dear."\par
\par
They were in the grove now, close by the stile; and when the last words\par
fell reluctantly from Jo's lips, Laurie dropped her hands and turned as\par
if to go on, but for once in his life that fence was too much for him;\par
so he just laid his head down on the mossy post, and stood so still that\par
Jo was frightened.\par
\par
        [Illustration: He laid his head down on the mossy post]\par
\par
"O Teddy, I'm so sorry, so desperately sorry, I could kill myself if it\par
would do any good! I wish you wouldn't take it so hard. I can't help it;\par
you know it's impossible for people to make themselves love other people\par
if they don't," cried Jo inelegantly but remorsefully, as she softly\par
patted his shoulder, remembering the time when he had comforted her so\par
long ago.\par
\par
"They do sometimes," said a muffled voice from the post.\par
\par
"I don't believe it's the right sort of love, and I'd rather not try\par
it," was the decided answer.\par
\par
There was a long pause, while a blackbird sung blithely on the willow by\par
the river, and the tall grass rustled in the wind. Presently Jo said\par
very soberly, as she sat down on the step of the stile,--\par
\par
"Laurie, I want to tell you something."\par
\par
He started as if he had been shot, threw up his head, and cried out, in\par
a fierce tone--\par
\par
"_Don't_ tell me that, Jo; I can't bear it now!"\par
\par
"Tell what?" she asked, wondering at his violence.\par
\par
"That you love that old man."\par
\par
"What old man?" demanded Jo, thinking he must mean his grandfather.\par
\par
"That devilish Professor you were always writing about. If you say you\par
love him, I know I shall do something desperate;" and he looked as if he\par
would keep his word, as he clenched his hands, with a wrathful spark in\par
his eyes.\par
\par
Jo wanted to laugh, but restrained herself, and said warmly, for she,\par
too, was getting excited with all this,--\par
\par
"Don't swear, Teddy! He isn't old, nor anything bad, but good and kind,\par
and the best friend I've got, next to you. Pray, don't fly into a\par
passion; I want to be kind, but I know I shall get angry if you abuse my\par
Professor. I haven't the least idea of loving him or anybody else."\par
\par
"But you will after a while, and then what will become of me?"\par
\par
"You'll love some one else too, like a sensible boy, and forget all this\par
trouble."\par
\par
"I _can't_ love any one else; and I'll never forget you, Jo, never!\par
never!" with a stamp to emphasize his passionate words.\par
\par
"What _shall_ I do with him?" sighed Jo, finding that emotions were more\par
unmanageable than she expected. "You haven't heard what I wanted to tell\par
you. Sit down and listen; for indeed I want to do right and make you\par
happy," she said, hoping to soothe him with a little reason, which\par
proved that she knew nothing about love.\par
\par
Seeing a ray of hope in that last speech, Laurie threw himself down on\par
the grass at her feet, leaned his arm on the lower step of the stile,\par
and looked up at her with an expectant face. Now that arrangement was\par
not conducive to calm speech or clear thought on Jo's part; for how\par
_could_ she say hard things to her boy while he watched her with eyes\par
full of love and longing, and lashes still wet with the bitter drop or\par
two her hardness of heart had wrung from him? She gently turned his head\par
away, saying, as she stroked the wavy hair which had been allowed to\par
grow for her sake,--how touching that was, to be sure!--\par
\par
"I agree with mother that you and I are not suited to each other,\par
because our quick tempers and strong wills would probably make us very\par
miserable, if we were so foolish as to--" Jo paused a little over the\par
last word, but Laurie uttered it with a rapturous expression,--\par
\par
"Marry,--no, we shouldn't! If you loved me, Jo, I should be a perfect\par
saint, for you could make me anything you like."\par
\par
"No, I can't. I've tried it and failed, and I won't risk our happiness\par
by such a serious experiment. We don't agree and we never shall; so\par
we'll be good friends all our lives, but we won't go and do anything\par
rash."\par
\par
"Yes, we will if we get the chance," muttered Laurie rebelliously.\par
\par
"Now do be reasonable, and take a sensible view of the case," implored\par
Jo, almost at her wit's end.\par
\par
"I won't be reasonable; I don't want to take what you call 'a sensible\par
view;' it won't help me, and it only makes you harder. I don't believe\par
you've got any heart."\par
\par
"I wish I hadn't!"\par
\par
There was a little quiver in Jo's voice, and, thinking it a good omen,\par
Laurie turned round, bringing all his persuasive powers to bear as he\par
said, in the wheedlesome tone that had never been so dangerously\par
wheedlesome before,--\par
\par
"Don't disappoint us, dear! Every one expects it. Grandpa has set his\par
heart upon it, your people like it, and I can't get on without you. Say\par
you will, and let's be happy. Do, do!"\par
\par
Not until months afterward did Jo understand how she had the strength of\par
mind to hold fast to the resolution she had made when she decided that\par
she did not love her boy, and never could. It was very hard to do, but\par
she did it, knowing that delay was both useless and cruel.\par
\par
"I can't say 'Yes' truly, so I won't say it at all. You'll see that I'm\par
right, by and by, and thank me for it"--she began solemnly.\par
\par
"I'll be hanged if I do!" and Laurie bounced up off the grass, burning\par
with indignation at the bare idea.\par
\par
"Yes, you will!" persisted Jo; "you'll get over this after a while, and\par
find some lovely, accomplished girl, who will adore you, and make a fine\par
mistress for your fine house. I shouldn't. I'm homely and awkward and\par
odd and old, and you'd be ashamed of me, and we should quarrel,--we\par
can't help it even now, you see,--and I shouldn't like elegant society\par
and you would, and you'd hate my scribbling, and I couldn't get on\par
without it, and we should be unhappy, and wish we hadn't done it, and\par
everything would be horrid!"\par
\par
"Anything more?" asked Laurie, finding it hard to listen patiently to\par
this prophetic burst.\par
\par
"Nothing more, except that I don't believe I shall ever marry. I'm happy\par
as I am, and love my liberty too well to be in any hurry to give it up\par
for any mortal man."\par
\par
"I know better!" broke in Laurie. "You think so now; but there'll come a\par
time when you _will_ care for somebody, and you'll love him\par
tremendously, and live and die for him. I know you will, it's your way,\par
and I shall have to stand by and see it;" and the despairing lover cast\par
his hat upon the ground with a gesture that would have seemed comical,\par
if his face had not been so tragical.\par
\par
"Yes, I _will_ live and die for him, if he ever comes and makes me love\par
him in spite of myself, and you must do the best you can!" cried Jo,\par
losing patience with poor Teddy. "I've done my best, but you _won't_ be\par
reasonable, and it's selfish of you to keep teasing for what I can't\par
give. I shall always be fond of you, very fond indeed, as a friend, but\par
I'll never marry you; and the sooner you believe it, the better for both\par
of us,--so now!"\par
\par
That speech was like fire to gunpowder. Laurie looked at her a minute as\par
if he did not quite know what to do with himself, then turned sharply\par
away, saying, in a desperate sort of tone,--\par
\par
"You'll be sorry some day, Jo."\par
\par
"Oh, where are you going?" she cried, for his face frightened her.\par
\par
"To the devil!" was the consoling answer.\par
\par
For a minute Jo's heart stood still, as he swung himself down the bank,\par
toward the river; but it takes much folly, sin, or misery to send a\par
young man to a violent death, and Laurie was not one of the weak sort\par
who are conquered by a single failure. He had no thought of a\par
melodramatic plunge, but some blind instinct led him to fling hat and\par
coat into his boat, and row away with all his might, making better time\par
up the river than he had done in many a race. Jo drew a long breath and\par
unclasped her hands as she watched the poor fellow trying to outstrip\par
the trouble which he carried in his heart.\par
\par
"That will do him good, and he'll come home in such a tender, penitent\par
state of mind, that I sha'n't dare to see him," she said; adding, as she\par
went slowly home, feeling as if she had murdered some innocent thing,\par
and buried it under the leaves,--\par
\par
"Now I must go and prepare Mr. Laurence to be very kind to my poor boy.\par
I wish he'd love Beth; perhaps he may, in time, but I begin to think I\par
was mistaken about her. Oh dear! how can girls like to have lovers and\par
refuse them. I think it's dreadful."\par
\par
Being sure that no one could do it so well as herself, she went straight\par
to Mr. Laurence, told the hard story bravely through, and then broke\par
down, crying so dismally over her own insensibility that the kind old\par
gentleman, though sorely disappointed, did not utter a reproach. He\par
found it difficult to understand how any girl could help loving Laurie,\par
and hoped she would change her mind, but he knew even better than Jo\par
that love cannot be forced, so he shook his head sadly, and resolved to\par
carry his boy out of harm's way; for Young Impetuosity's parting words\par
to Jo disturbed him more than he would confess.\par
\par
When Laurie came home, dead tired, but quite composed, his grandfather\par
met him as if he knew nothing, and kept up the delusion very\par
successfully for an hour or two. But when they sat together in the\par
twilight, the time they used to enjoy so much, it was hard work for the\par
old man to ramble on as usual, and harder still for the young one to\par
listen to praises of the last year's success, which to him now seemed\par
love's labor lost. He bore it as long as he could, then went to his\par
piano, and began to play. The windows were open; and Jo, walking in the\par
garden with Beth, for once understood music better than her sister, for\par
he played the "Sonata Pathétique," and played it as he never did before.\par
\par
"That's very fine, I dare say, but it's sad enough to make one cry; give\par
us something gayer, lad," said Mr. Laurence, whose kind old heart was\par
full of sympathy, which he longed to show, but knew not how.\par
\par
Laurie dashed into a livelier strain, played stormily for several\par
minutes, and would have got through bravely, if, in a momentary lull,\par
Mrs. March's voice had not been heard calling,--\par
\par
"Jo, dear, come in; I want you."\par
\par
Just what Laurie longed to say, with a different meaning! As he\par
listened, he lost his place; the music ended with a broken chord, and\par
the musician sat silent in the dark.\par
\par
"I can't stand this," muttered the old gentleman. Up he got, groped his\par
way to the piano, laid a kind hand on either of the broad shoulders, and\par
said, as gently as a woman,--\par
\par
"I know, my boy, I know."\par
\par
No answer for an instant; then Laurie asked sharply,--\par
\par
"Who told you?"\par
\par
"Jo herself."\par
\par
"Then there's an end of it!" and he shook off his grandfather's hands\par
with an impatient motion; for, though grateful for the sympathy, his\par
man's pride could not bear a man's pity.\par
\par
"Not quite; I want to say one thing, and then there shall be an end of\par
it," returned Mr. Laurence, with unusual mildness. "You won't care to\par
stay at home just now, perhaps?"\par
\par
"I don't intend to run away from a girl. Jo can't prevent my seeing her,\par
and I shall stay and do it as long as I like," interrupted Laurie, in a\par
defiant tone.\par
\par
"Not if you are the gentleman I think you. I'm disappointed, but the\par
girl can't help it; and the only thing left for you to do is to go away\par
for a time. Where will you go?"\par
\par
"Anywhere. I don't care what becomes of me;" and Laurie got up, with a\par
reckless laugh, that grated on his grandfather's ear.\par
\par
"Take it like a man, and don't do anything rash, for God's sake. Why not\par
go abroad, as you planned, and forget it?"\par
\par
"I can't."\par
\par
"But you've been wild to go, and I promised you should when you got\par
through college."\par
\par
"Ah, but I didn't mean to go alone!" and Laurie walked fast through the\par
room, with an expression which it was well his grandfather did not see.\par
\par
"I don't ask you to go alone; there's some one ready and glad to go with\par
you, anywhere in the world."\par
\par
"Who, sir?" stopping to listen.\par
\par
"Myself."\par
\par
Laurie came back as quickly as he went, and put out his hand, saying\par
huskily,--\par
\par
"I'm a selfish brute; but--you know--grandfather--"\par
\par
"Lord help me, yes, I do know, for I've been through it all before, once\par
in my own young days, and then with your father. Now, my dear boy, just\par
sit quietly down, and hear my plan. It's all settled, and can be carried\par
out at once," said Mr. Laurence, keeping hold of the young man, as if\par
fearful that he would break away, as his father had done before him.\par
\par
"Well, sir, what is it?" and Laurie sat down, without a sign of interest\par
in face or voice.\par
\par
"There is business in London that needs looking after; I meant you\par
should attend to it; but I can do it better myself, and things here will\par
get on very well with Brooke to manage them. My partners do almost\par
everything; I'm merely holding on till you take my place, and can be off\par
at any time."\par
\par
"But you hate travelling, sir; I can't ask it of you at your age," began\par
Laurie, who was grateful for the sacrifice, but much preferred to go\par
alone, if he went at all.\par
\par
The old gentleman knew that perfectly well, and particularly desired to\par
prevent it; for the mood in which he found his grandson assured him that\par
it would not be wise to leave him to his own devices. So, stifling a\par
natural regret at the thought of the home comforts he would leave behind\par
him, he said stoutly,--\par
\par
"Bless your soul, I'm not superannuated yet. I quite enjoy the idea; it\par
will do me good, and my old bones won't suffer, for travelling nowadays\par
is almost as easy as sitting in a chair."\par
\par
A restless movement from Laurie suggested that _his_ chair was not easy,\par
or that he did not like the plan, and made the old man add hastily,--\par
\par
"I don't mean to be a marplot or a burden; I go because I think you'd\par
feel happier than if I was left behind. I don't intend to gad about with\par
you, but leave you free to go where you like, while I amuse myself in my\par
own way. I've friends in London and Paris, and should like to visit\par
them; meantime you can go to Italy, Germany, Switzerland, where you\par
will, and enjoy pictures, music, scenery, and adventures to your heart's\par
content."\par
\par
Now, Laurie felt just then that his heart was entirely broken, and the\par
world a howling wilderness; but at the sound of certain words which the\par
old gentleman artfully introduced into his closing sentence, the broken\par
heart gave an unexpected leap, and a green oasis or two suddenly\par
appeared in the howling wilderness. He sighed, and then said, in a\par
spiritless tone,--\par
\par
"Just as you like, sir; it doesn't matter where I go or what I do."\par
\par
"It does to me, remember that, my lad; I give you entire liberty, but I\par
trust you to make an honest use of it. Promise me that, Laurie."\par
\par
"Anything you like, sir."\par
\par
"Good," thought the old gentleman. "You don't care now, but there'll\par
come a time when that promise will keep you out of mischief, or I'm much\par
mistaken."\par
\par
Being an energetic individual, Mr. Laurence struck while the iron was\par
hot; and before the blighted being recovered spirit enough to rebel,\par
they were off. During the time necessary for preparation, Laurie bore\par
himself as young gentlemen usually do in such cases. He was moody,\par
irritable, and pensive by turns; lost his appetite, neglected his dress,\par
and devoted much time to playing tempestuously on his piano; avoided Jo,\par
but consoled himself by staring at her from his window, with a tragical\par
face that haunted her dreams by night, and oppressed her with a heavy\par
sense of guilt by day. Unlike some sufferers, he never spoke of his\par
unrequited passion, and would allow no one, not even Mrs. March, to\par
attempt consolation or offer sympathy. On some accounts, this was a\par
relief to his friends; but the weeks before his departure were very\par
uncomfortable, and every one rejoiced that the "poor, dear fellow was\par
going away to forget his trouble, and come home happy." Of course, he\par
smiled darkly at their delusion, but passed it by, with the sad\par
superiority of one who knew that his fidelity, like his love, was\par
unalterable.\par
\par
When the parting came he affected high spirits, to conceal certain\par
inconvenient emotions which seemed inclined to assert themselves. This\par
gayety did not impose upon anybody, but they tried to look as if it did,\par
for his sake, and he got on very well till Mrs. March kissed him, with\par
a whisper full of motherly solicitude; then, feeling that he was going\par
very fast, he hastily embraced them all round, not forgetting the\par
afflicted Hannah, and ran downstairs as if for his life. Jo followed a\par
minute after to wave her hand to him if he looked round. He did look\par
round, came back, put his arms about her, as she stood on the step above\par
him, and looked up at her with a face that made his short appeal both\par
eloquent and pathetic.\par
\par
"O Jo, can't you?"\par
\par
                    [Illustration: O Jo, can't you?]\par
\par
"Teddy, dear, I wish I could!"\par
\par
That was all, except a little pause; then Laurie straightened himself\par
up, said "It's all right, never mind," and went away without another\par
word. Ah, but it wasn't all right, and Jo _did_ mind; for while the\par
curly head lay on her arm a minute after her hard answer, she felt as if\par
she had stabbed her dearest friend; and when he left her without a look\par
behind him, she knew that the boy Laurie never would come again.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                 XXXVI.\par
\par
                             BETH'S SECRET.\par
\par
\par
When Jo came home that spring, she had been struck with the change in\par
Beth. No one spoke of it or seemed aware of it, for it had come too\par
gradually to startle those who saw her daily; but to eyes sharpened by\par
absence, it was very plain; and a heavy weight fell on Jo's heart as she\par
saw her sister's face. It was no paler and but little thinner than in\par
the autumn; yet there was a strange, transparent look about it, as if\par
the mortal was being slowly refined away, and the immortal shining\par
through the frail flesh with an indescribably pathetic beauty. Jo saw\par
and felt it, but said nothing at the time, and soon the first impression\par
lost much of its power; for Beth seemed happy, no one appeared to doubt\par
that she was better; and, presently, in other cares, Jo for a time\par
forgot her fear.\par
\par
But when Laurie was gone, and peace prevailed again, the vague anxiety\par
returned and haunted her. She had confessed her sins and been forgiven;\par
but when she showed her savings and proposed the mountain trip, Beth had\par
thanked her heartily, but begged not to go so far away from home.\par
Another little visit to the seashore would suit her better, and, as\par
grandma could not be prevailed upon to leave the babies, Jo took Beth\par
down to the quiet place, where she could live much in the open air, and\par
let the fresh sea-breezes blow a little color into her pale cheeks.\par
\par
It was not a fashionable place, but, even among the pleasant people\par
there, the girls made few friends, preferring to live for one another.\par
Beth was too shy to enjoy society, and Jo too wrapped up in her to care\par
for any one else; so they were all in all to each other, and came and\par
went, quite unconscious of the interest they excited in those about\par
them, who watched with sympathetic eyes the strong sister and the feeble\par
one, always together, as if they felt instinctively that a long\par
separation was not far away.\par
\par
They did feel it, yet neither spoke of it; for often between ourselves\par
and those nearest and dearest to us there exists a reserve which it is\par
very hard to overcome. Jo felt as if a veil had fallen between her heart\par
and Beth's; but when she put out her hand to lift it up, there seemed\par
something sacred in the silence, and she waited for Beth to speak. She\par
wondered, and was thankful also, that her parents did not seem to see\par
what she saw; and, during the quiet weeks, when the shadow grew so plain\par
to her, she said nothing of it to those at home, believing that it would\par
tell itself when Beth came back no better. She wondered still more if\par
her sister really guessed the hard truth, and what thoughts were passing\par
through her mind during the long hours when she lay on the warm rocks,\par
with her head in Jo's lap, while the winds blew healthfully over her,\par
and the sea made music at her feet.\par
\par
     [Illustration: With her head in Jo's lap, while the wind blew\par
                        healthfully over her]\par
\par
One day Beth told her. Jo thought she was asleep, she lay so still; and,\par
putting down her book, sat looking at her with wistful eyes, trying to\par
see signs of hope in the faint color on Beth's cheeks. But she could not\par
find enough to satisfy her, for the cheeks were very thin, and the hands\par
seemed too feeble to hold even the rosy little shells they had been\par
gathering. It came to her then more bitterly than ever that Beth was\par
slowly drifting away from her, and her arms instinctively tightened\par
their hold upon the dearest treasure she possessed. For a minute her\par
eyes were too dim for seeing, and, when they cleared, Beth was looking\par
up at her so tenderly that there was hardly any need for her to say,--\par
\par
"Jo, dear, I'm glad you know it. I've tried to tell you, but I\par
couldn't."\par
\par
There was no answer except her sister's cheek against her own, not even\par
tears; for when most deeply moved, Jo did not cry. She was the weaker,\par
then, and Beth tried to comfort and sustain her, with her arms about\par
her, and the soothing words she whispered in her ear.\par
\par
"I've known it for a good while, dear, and, now I'm used to it, it isn't\par
hard to think of or to bear. Try to see it so, and don't be troubled\par
about me, because it's best; indeed it is."\par
\par
"Is this what made you so unhappy in the autumn, Beth? You did not feel\par
it then, and keep it to yourself so long, did you?" asked Jo, refusing\par
to see or say that it _was_ best, but glad to know that Laurie had no\par
part in Beth's trouble.\par
\par
"Yes, I gave up hoping then, but I didn't like to own it. I tried to\par
think it was a sick fancy, and would not let it trouble any one. But\par
when I saw you all so well and strong, and full of happy plans, it was\par
hard to feel that I could never be like you, and then I was miserable,\par
Jo."\par
\par
"O Beth, and you didn't tell me, didn't let me comfort and help you! How\par
could you shut me out, and bear it all alone?"\par
\par
Jo's voice was full of tender reproach, and her heart ached to think of\par
the solitary struggle that must have gone on while Beth learned to say\par
good-by to health, love, and life, and take up her cross so cheerfully.\par
\par
"Perhaps it was wrong, but I tried to do right; I wasn't sure, no one\par
said anything, and I hoped I was mistaken. It would have been selfish to\par
frighten you all when Marmee was so anxious about Meg, and Amy away, and\par
you so happy with Laurie,--at least, I thought so then."\par
\par
"And I thought that you loved him, Beth, and I went away because I\par
couldn't," cried Jo, glad to say all the truth.\par
\par
Beth looked so amazed at the idea that Jo smiled in spite of her pain,\par
and added softly,--\par
\par
"Then you didn't, deary? I was afraid it was so, and imagined your poor\par
little heart full of love-lornity all that while."\par
\par
"Why, Jo, how could I, when he was so fond of you?" asked Beth, as\par
innocently as a child. "I do love him dearly; he is so good to me, how\par
can I help it? But he never could be anything to me but my brother. I\par
hope he truly will be, sometime."\par
\par
"Not through me," said Jo decidedly. "Amy is left for him, and they\par
would suit excellently; but I have no heart for such things, now. I\par
don't care what becomes of anybody but you, Beth. You _must_ get well."\par
\par
"I want to, oh, so much! I try, but every day I lose a little, and feel\par
more sure that I shall never gain it back. It's like the tide, Jo, when\par
it turns, it goes slowly, but it can't be stopped."\par
\par
"It _shall_ be stopped, your tide must not turn so soon, nineteen is too\par
young. Beth, I can't let you go. I'll work and pray and fight against\par
it. I'll keep you in spite of everything; there must be ways, it can't\par
be too late. God won't be so cruel as to take you from me," cried poor\par
Jo rebelliously, for her spirit was far less piously submissive than\par
Beth's.\par
\par
Simple, sincere people seldom speak much of their piety; it shows itself\par
in acts, rather than in words, and has more influence than homilies or\par
protestations. Beth could not reason upon or explain the faith that gave\par
her courage and patience to give up life, and cheerfully wait for death.\par
Like a confiding child, she asked no questions, but left everything to\par
God and nature, Father and mother of us all, feeling sure that they, and\par
they only, could teach and strengthen heart and spirit for this life and\par
the life to come. She did not rebuke Jo with saintly speeches, only\par
loved her better for her passionate affection, and clung more closely to\par
the dear human love, from which our Father never means us to be weaned,\par
but through which He draws us closer to Himself. She could not say, "I'm\par
glad to go," for life was very sweet to her; she could only sob out, "I\par
try to be willing," while she held fast to Jo, as the first bitter wave\par
of this great sorrow broke over them together.\par
\par
By and by Beth said, with recovered serenity,--\par
\par
"You'll tell them this when we go home?"\par
\par
"I think they will see it without words," sighed Jo; for now it seemed\par
to her that Beth changed every day.\par
\par
"Perhaps not; I've heard that the people who love best are often\par
blindest to such things. If they don't see it, you will tell them for\par
me. I don't want any secrets, and it's kinder to prepare them. Meg has\par
John and the babies to comfort her, but you must stand by father and\par
mother, won't you, Jo?"\par
\par
"If I can; but, Beth, I don't give up yet; I'm going to believe that it\par
_is_ a sick fancy, and not let you think it's true," said Jo, trying to\par
speak cheerfully.\par
\par
Beth lay a minute thinking, and then said in her quiet way,--\par
\par
"I don't know how to express myself, and shouldn't try, to any one but\par
you, because I can't speak out, except to my Jo. I only mean to say\par
that I have a feeling that it never was intended I should live long. I'm\par
not like the rest of you; I never made any plans about what I'd do when\par
I grew up; I never thought of being married, as you all did. I couldn't\par
seem to imagine myself anything but stupid little Beth, trotting about\par
at home, of no use anywhere but there. I never wanted to go away, and\par
the hard part now is the leaving you all. I'm not afraid, but it seems\par
as if I should be homesick for you even in heaven."\par
\par
Jo could not speak; and for several minutes there was no sound but the\par
sigh of the wind and the lapping of the tide. A white-winged gull flew\par
by, with the flash of sunshine on its silvery breast; Beth watched it\par
till it vanished, and her eyes were full of sadness. A little\par
gray-coated sand-bird came tripping over the beach, "peeping" softly to\par
itself, as if enjoying the sun and sea; it came quite close to Beth,\par
looked at her with a friendly eye, and sat upon a warm stone, dressing\par
its wet feathers, quite at home. Beth smiled, and felt comforted, for\par
the tiny thing seemed to offer its small friendship, and remind her that\par
a pleasant world was still to be enjoyed.\par
\par
"Dear little bird! See, Jo, how tame it is. I like peeps better than the\par
gulls: they are not so wild and handsome, but they seem happy, confiding\par
little things. I used to call them my birds, last summer; and mother\par
said they reminded her of me,--busy, quaker-colored creatures, always\par
near the shore, and always chirping that contented little song of\par
theirs. You are the gull, Jo, strong and wild, fond of the storm and the\par
wind, flying far out to sea, and happy all alone. Meg is the\par
turtle-dove, and Amy is like the lark she writes about, trying to get up\par
among the clouds, but always dropping down into its nest again. Dear\par
little girl! she's so ambitious, but her heart is good and tender; and\par
no matter how high she flies, she never will forget home. I hope I shall\par
see her again, but she seems _so_ far away."\par
\par
"She is coming in the spring, and I mean that you shall be all ready to\par
see and enjoy her. I'm going to have you well and rosy by that time,"\par
began Jo, feeling that of all the changes in Beth, the talking change\par
was the greatest, for it seemed to cost no effort now, and she thought\par
aloud in a way quite unlike bashful Beth.\par
\par
"Jo, dear, don't hope any more; it won't do any good, I'm sure of that.\par
We won't be miserable, but enjoy being together while we wait. We'll\par
have happy times, for I don't suffer much, and I think the tide will go\par
out easily, if you help me."\par
\par
Jo leaned down to kiss the tranquil face; and with that silent kiss, she\par
dedicated herself soul and body to Beth.\par
\par
She was right: there was no need of any words when they got home, for\par
father and mother saw plainly, now, what they had prayed to be saved\par
from seeing. Tired with her short journey, Beth went at once to bed,\par
saying how glad she was to be at home; and when Jo went down, she found\par
that she would be spared the hard task of telling Beth's secret. Her\par
father stood leaning his head on the mantel-piece, and did not turn as\par
she came in; but her mother stretched out her arms as if for help, and\par
Jo went to comfort her without a word.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
             [Illustration: He hurried forward to meet her]\par
\par
                                XXXVII.\par
\par
                            NEW IMPRESSIONS.\par
\par
\par
At three o'clock in the afternoon, all the fashionable world at Nice may\par
be seen on the Promenade des Anglais,--a charming place; for the wide\par
walk, bordered with palms, flowers, and tropical shrubs, is bounded on\par
one side by the sea, on the other by the grand drive, lined with hotels\par
and villas, while beyond lie orange-orchards and the hills. Many nations\par
are represented, many languages spoken, many costumes worn; and, on a\par
sunny day, the spectacle is as gay and brilliant as a carnival. Haughty\par
English, lively French, sober Germans, handsome Spaniards, ugly\par
Russians, meek Jews, free-and-easy Americans, all drive, sit, or saunter\par
here, chatting over the news, and criticising the latest celebrity who\par
has arrived,--Ristori or Dickens, Victor Emmanuel or the Queen of the\par
Sandwich Islands. The equipages are as varied as the company, and\par
attract as much attention, especially the low basket-barouches in which\par
ladies drive themselves, with a pair of dashing ponies, gay nets to keep\par
their voluminous flounces from overflowing the diminutive vehicles, and\par
little grooms on the perch behind.\par
\par
Along this walk, on Christmas Day, a tall young man walked slowly, with\par
his hands behind him, and a somewhat absent expression of countenance.\par
He looked like an Italian, was dressed like an Englishman, and had the\par
independent air of an American,--a combination which caused sundry pairs\par
of feminine eyes to look approvingly after him, and sundry dandies in\par
black velvet suits, with rose-colored neckties, buff gloves, and\par
orange-flowers in their button-holes, to shrug their shoulders, and then\par
envy him his inches. There were plenty of pretty faces to admire, but\par
the young man took little notice of them, except to glance, now and\par
then, at some blonde girl, or lady in blue. Presently he strolled out of\par
the promenade, and stood a moment at the crossing, as if undecided\par
whether to go and listen to the band in the Jardin Publique, or to\par
wander along the beach toward Castle Hill. The quick trot of ponies'\par
feet made him look up, as one of the little carriages, containing a\par
single lady, came rapidly down the street. The lady was young, blonde,\par
and dressed in blue. He stared a minute, then his whole face woke up,\par
and, waving his hat like a boy, he hurried forward to meet her.\par
\par
"O Laurie, is it really you? I thought you'd never come!" cried Amy,\par
dropping the reins, and holding out both hands, to the great\par
scandalization of a French mamma, who hastened her daughter's steps,\par
lest she should be demoralized by beholding the free manners of these\par
"mad English."\par
\par
"I was detained by the way, but I promised to spend Christmas with you,\par
and here I am."\par
\par
"How is your grandfather? When did you come? Where are you staying?"\par
\par
"Very well--last night--at the Chauvain. I called at your hotel, but you\par
were all out."\par
\par
"I have so much to say, I don't know where to begin! Get in, and we can\par
talk at our ease; I was going for a drive, and longing for company.\par
Flo's saving up for to-night."\par
\par
"What happens then, a ball?"\par
\par
"A Christmas party at our hotel. There are many Americans there, and\par
they give it in honor of the day. You'll go with us, of course? Aunt\par
will be charmed."\par
\par
"Thank you. Where now?" asked Laurie, leaning back and folding his arms,\par
a proceeding which suited Amy, who preferred to drive; for her\par
parasol-whip and blue reins over the white ponies' backs, afforded her\par
infinite satisfaction.\par
\par
"I'm going to the banker's first, for letters, and then to Castle Hill;\par
the view is so lovely, and I like to feed the peacocks. Have you ever\par
been there?"\par
\par
"Often, years ago; but I don't mind having a look at it."\par
\par
"Now tell me all about yourself. The last I heard of you, your\par
grandfather wrote that he expected you from Berlin."\par
\par
"Yes, I spent a month there, and then joined him in Paris, where he has\par
settled for the winter. He has friends there, and finds plenty to amuse\par
him; so I go and come, and we get on capitally."\par
\par
"That's a sociable arrangement," said Amy, missing something in Laurie's\par
manner, though she couldn't tell what.\par
\par
"Why, you see he hates to travel, and I hate to keep still; so we each\par
suit ourselves, and there is no trouble. I am often with him, and he\par
enjoys my adventures, while I like to feel that some one is glad to see\par
me when I get back from my wanderings. Dirty old hole, isn't it?" he\par
added, with a look of disgust, as they drove along the boulevard to the\par
Place Napoleon, in the old city.\par
\par
"The dirt is picturesque, so I don't mind. The river and the hills are\par
delicious, and these glimpses of the narrow cross-streets are my\par
delight. Now we shall have to wait for that procession to pass; it's\par
going to the Church of St. John."\par
\par
While Laurie listlessly watched the procession of priests under their\par
canopies, white-veiled nuns bearing lighted tapers, and some brotherhood\par
in blue, chanting as they walked, Amy watched him, and felt a new sort\par
of shyness steal over her; for he was changed, and she could not find\par
the merry-faced boy she left in the moody-looking man beside her. He was\par
handsomer than ever, and greatly improved, she thought; but now that the\par
flush of pleasure at meeting her was over, he looked tired and\par
spiritless,--not sick, nor exactly unhappy, but older and graver than a\par
year or two of prosperous life should have made him. She couldn't\par
understand it, and did not venture to ask questions; so she shook her\par
head, and touched up her ponies, as the procession wound away across the\par
arches of the Paglioni bridge, and vanished in the church.\par
\par
"_Que pensez vous_?" she said, airing her French, which had improved in\par
quantity, if not in quality, since she came abroad.\par
\par
"That mademoiselle has made good use of her time, and the result is\par
charming," replied Laurie, bowing, with his hand on his heart, and an\par
admiring look.\par
\par
She blushed with pleasure, but somehow the compliment did not satisfy\par
her like the blunt praises he used to give her at home, when he\par
promenaded round her on festival occasions, and told her she was\par
"altogether jolly," with a hearty smile and an approving pat on the\par
head. She didn't like the new tone; for, though not _blasé_, it sounded\par
indifferent in spite of the look.\par
\par
"If that's the way he's going to grow up, I wish he'd stay a boy," she\par
thought, with a curious sense of disappointment and discomfort, trying\par
meantime to seem quite easy and gay.\par
\par
At Avigdor's she found the precious home-letters, and, giving the reins\par
to Laurie, read them luxuriously as they wound up the shady road between\par
green hedges, where tea-roses bloomed as freshly as in June.\par
\par
"Beth is very poorly, mother says. I often think I ought to go home, but\par
they all say 'stay;' so I do, for I shall never have another chance like\par
this," said Amy, looking sober over one page.\par
\par
"I think you are right, there; you could do nothing at home, and it is a\par
great comfort to them to know that you are well and happy, and enjoying\par
so much, my dear."\par
\par
He drew a little nearer, and looked more like his old self, as he said\par
that; and the fear that sometimes weighed on Amy's heart was lightened,\par
for the look, the act, the brotherly "my dear," seemed to assure her\par
that if any trouble did come, she would not be alone in a strange land.\par
Presently she laughed, and showed him a small sketch of Jo in her\par
scribbling-suit, with the bow rampantly erect upon her cap, and issuing\par
from her mouth the words, "Genius burns!"\par
\par
Laurie smiled, took it, put it in his vest-pocket, "to keep it from\par
blowing away," and listened with interest to the lively letter Amy read\par
him.\par
\par
"This will be a regularly merry Christmas to me, with presents in the\par
morning, you and letters in the afternoon, and a party at night," said\par
Amy, as they alighted among the ruins of the old fort, and a flock of\par
splendid peacocks came trooping about them, tamely waiting to be fed.\par
While Amy stood laughing on the bank above him as she scattered crumbs\par
to the brilliant birds, Laurie looked at her as she had looked at him,\par
with a natural curiosity to see what changes time and absence had\par
wrought. He found nothing to perplex or disappoint, much to admire and\par
approve; for, overlooking a few little affectations of speech and\par
manner, she was as sprightly and graceful as ever, with the addition of\par
that indescribable something in dress and bearing which we call\par
elegance. Always mature for her age, she had gained a certain _aplomb_\par
in both carriage and conversation, which made her seem more of a woman\par
of the world than she was; but her old petulance now and then showed\par
itself, her strong will still held its own, and her native frankness was\par
unspoiled by foreign polish.\par
\par
Laurie did not read all this while he watched her feed the peacocks, but\par
he saw enough to satisfy and interest him, and carried away a pretty\par
little picture of a bright-faced girl standing in the sunshine, which\par
brought out the soft hue of her dress, the fresh color of her cheeks,\par
the golden gloss of her hair, and made her a prominent figure in the\par
pleasant scene.\par
\par
As they came up on to the stone plateau that crowns the hill, Amy waved\par
her hand as if welcoming him to her favorite haunt, and said, pointing\par
here and there,--\par
\par
"Do you remember the Cathedral and the Corso, the fishermen dragging\par
their nets in the bay, and the lovely road to Villa Franca, Schubert's\par
Tower, just below, and, best of all, that speck far out to sea which\par
they say is Corsica?"\par
\par
"I remember; it's not much changed," he answered, without enthusiasm.\par
\par
"What Jo would give for a sight of that famous speck!" said Amy, feeling\par
in good spirits, and anxious to see him so also.\par
\par
"Yes," was all he said, but he turned and strained his eyes to see the\par
island which a greater usurper than even Napoleon now made interesting\par
in his sight.\par
\par
"Take a good look at it for her sake, and then come and tell me what you\par
have been doing with yourself all this while," said Amy, seating\par
herself, ready for a good talk.\par
\par
But she did not get it; for, though he joined her, and answered all her\par
questions freely, she could only learn that he had roved about the\par
continent and been to Greece. So, after idling away an hour, they drove\par
home again; and, having paid his respects to Mrs. Carrol, Laurie left\par
them, promising to return in the evening.\par
\par
It must be recorded of Amy that she deliberately "prinked" that night.\par
Time and absence had done its work on both the young people; she had\par
seen her old friend in a new light, not as "our boy," but as a handsome\par
and agreeable man, and she was conscious of a very natural desire to\par
find favor in his sight. Amy knew her good points, and made the most of\par
them, with the taste and skill which is a fortune to a poor and pretty\par
woman.\par
\par
Tarlatan and tulle were cheap at Nice, so she enveloped herself in them\par
on such occasions, and, following the sensible English fashion of simple\par
dress for young girls, got up charming little toilettes with fresh\par
flowers, a few trinkets, and all manner of dainty devices, which were\par
both inexpensive and effective. It must be confessed that the artist\par
sometimes got possession of the woman, and indulged in antique\par
_coiffures_, statuesque attitudes, and classic draperies. But, dear\par
heart, we all have our little weaknesses, and find it easy to pardon\par
such in the young, who satisfy our eyes with their comeliness, and keep\par
our hearts merry with their artless vanities.\par
\par
"I do want him to think I look well, and tell them so at home," said Amy\par
to herself, as she put on Flo's old white silk ball-dress, and covered\par
it with a cloud of fresh illusion, out of which her white shoulders and\par
golden head emerged with a most artistic effect. Her hair she had the\par
sense to let alone, after gathering up the thick waves and curls into a\par
Hebe-like knot at the back of her head.\par
\par
"It's not the fashion, but it's becoming, and I can't afford to make a\par
fright of myself," she used to say, when advised to frizzle, puff, or\par
braid, as the latest style commanded.\par
\par
Having no ornaments fine enough for this important occasion, Amy looped\par
her fleecy skirts with rosy clusters of azalea, and framed the white\par
shoulders in delicate green vines. Remembering the painted boots, she\par
surveyed her white satin slippers with girlish satisfaction, and\par
_chasséed_ down the room, admiring her aristocratic feet all by herself.\par
\par
"My new fan just matches my flowers, my gloves fit to a charm, and the\par
real lace on aunt's _mouchoir_ gives an air to my whole dress. If I only\par
had a classical nose and mouth I should be perfectly happy," she said,\par
surveying herself with a critical eye, and a candle in each hand.\par
\par
In spite of this affliction, she looked unusually gay and graceful as\par
she glided away; she seldom ran,--it did not suit her style, she\par
thought, for, being tall, the stately and Junoesque was more appropriate\par
than the sportive or piquante. She walked up and down the long saloon\par
while waiting for Laurie, and once arranged herself under the\par
chandelier, which had a good effect upon her hair; then she thought\par
better of it, and went away to the other end of the room, as if ashamed\par
of the girlish desire to have the first view a propitious one. It so\par
happened that she could not have done a better thing, for Laurie came in\par
so quietly she did not hear him; and, as she stood at the distant\par
window, with her head half turned, and one hand gathering up her dress,\par
the slender, white figure against the red curtains was as effective as a\par
well-placed statue.\par
\par
"Good evening, Diana!" said Laurie, with the look of satisfaction she\par
liked to see in his eyes when they rested on her.\par
\par
"Good evening, Apollo!" she answered, smiling back at him, for he, too,\par
looked unusually _debonnaire_, and the thought of entering the ball-room\par
on the arm of such a personable man caused Amy to pity the four plain\par
Misses Davis from the bottom of her heart.\par
\par
"Here are your flowers; I arranged them myself, remembering that you\par
didn't like what Hannah calls a 'sot-bookay,'" said Laurie, handing her\par
a delicate nosegay, in a holder that she had long coveted as she daily\par
passed it in Cardiglia's window.\par
\par
                  [Illustration: Here are your flowers]\par
\par
"How kind you are!" she exclaimed gratefully. "If I'd known you were\par
coming I'd have had something ready for you to-day, though not as pretty\par
as this, I'm afraid."\par
\par
"Thank you; it isn't what it should be, but you have improved it," he\par
added, as she snapped the silver bracelet on her wrist.\par
\par
"Please don't."\par
\par
"I thought you liked that sort of thing?"\par
\par
"Not from you; it doesn't sound natural, and I like your old bluntness\par
better."\par
\par
"I'm glad of it," he answered, with a look of relief; then buttoned her\par
gloves for her, and asked if his tie was straight, just as he used to do\par
when they went to parties together, at home.\par
\par
The company assembled in the long _salle à manger_, that evening, was\par
such as one sees nowhere but on the Continent. The hospitable Americans\par
had invited every acquaintance they had in Nice, and, having no\par
prejudice against titles, secured a few to add lustre to their Christmas\par
ball.\par
\par
A Russian prince condescended to sit in a corner for an hour, and talk\par
with a massive lady, dressed like Hamlet's mother, in black velvet, with\par
a pearl bridle under her chin. A Polish count, aged eighteen, devoted\par
himself to the ladies, who pronounced him "a fascinating dear," and a\par
German Serene Something, having come for the supper alone, roamed\par
vaguely about, seeking what he might devour. Baron Rothschild's private\par
secretary, a large-nosed Jew, in tight boots, affably beamed upon the\par
world, as if his master's name crowned him with a golden halo; a stout\par
Frenchman, who knew the Emperor, came to indulge his mania for dancing,\par
and Lady de Jones, a British matron, adorned the scene with her little\par
family of eight. Of course, there were many light-footed, shrill-voiced\par
American girls, handsome, lifeless-looking English ditto, and a few\par
plain but piquante French demoiselles; likewise the usual set of\par
travelling young gentlemen, who disported themselves gayly, while mammas\par
of all nations lined the walls, and smiled upon them benignly when they\par
danced with their daughters.\par
\par
Any young girl can imagine Amy's state of mind when she "took the stage"\par
that night, leaning on Laurie's arm. She knew she looked well, she loved\par
to dance, she felt that her foot was on her native heath in a ball-room,\par
and enjoyed the delightful sense of power which comes when young girls\par
first discover the new and lovely kingdom they are born to rule by\par
virtue of beauty, youth, and womanhood. She did pity the Davis girls,\par
who were awkward, plain, and destitute of escort, except a grim papa and\par
three grimmer maiden aunts, and she bowed to them in her friendliest\par
manner as she passed; which was good of her, as it permitted them to see\par
her dress, and burn with curiosity to know who her distinguished-looking\par
friend might be. With the first burst of the band, Amy's color rose, her\par
eyes began to sparkle, and her feet to tap the floor impatiently; for\par
she danced well, and wanted Laurie to know it: therefore the shock she\par
received can better be imagined than described, when he said, in a\par
perfectly tranquil tone,--\par
\par
"Do you care to dance?"\par
\par
"One usually does at a ball."\par
\par
Her amazed look and quick answer caused Laurie to repair his error as\par
fast as possible.\par
\par
"I meant the first dance. May I have the honor?"\par
\par
"I can give you one if I put off the Count. He dances divinely; but he\par
will excuse me, as you are an old friend," said Amy, hoping that the\par
name would have a good effect, and show Laurie that she was not to be\par
trifled with.\par
\par
"Nice little boy, but rather a short Pole to support\par
\par
        "'A daughter of the gods,\par
          Divinely tall, and most divinely fair,'"\par
\par
was all the satisfaction she got, however.\par
\par
The set in which they found themselves was composed of English, and Amy\par
was compelled to walk decorously through a cotillon, feeling all the\par
while as if she could dance the Tarantula with a relish. Laurie resigned\par
her to the "nice little boy," and went to do his duty to Flo, without\par
securing Amy for the joys to come, which reprehensible want of\par
forethought was properly punished, for she immediately engaged herself\par
till supper, meaning to relent if he then gave any signs of penitence.\par
She showed him her ball-book with demure satisfaction when he strolled,\par
instead of rushing, up to claim her for the next, a glorious\par
polka-redowa; but his polite regrets didn't impose upon her, and when\par
she gallopaded away with the Count, she saw Laurie sit down by her aunt\par
with an actual expression of relief.\par
\par
That was unpardonable; and Amy took no more notice of him for a long\par
while, except a word now and then, when she came to her chaperon,\par
between the dances, for a necessary pin or a moment's rest. Her anger\par
had a good effect, however, for she hid it under a smiling face, and\par
seemed unusually blithe and brilliant. Laurie's eyes followed her with\par
pleasure, for she neither romped nor sauntered, but danced with spirit\par
and grace, making the delightsome pastime what it should be. He very\par
naturally fell to studying her from this new point of view; and, before\par
the evening was half over, had decided that "little Amy was going to\par
make a very charming woman."\par
\par
It was a lively scene, for soon the spirit of the social season took\par
possession of every one, and Christmas merriment made all faces shine,\par
hearts happy, and heels light. The musicians fiddled, tooted, and banged\par
as if they enjoyed it; everybody danced who could, and those who\par
couldn't admired their neighbors with uncommon warmth. The air was dark\par
with Davises, and many Joneses gambolled like a flock of young giraffes.\par
The golden secretary darted through the room like a meteor, with a\par
dashing Frenchwoman, who carpeted the floor with her pink satin train.\par
The Serene Teuton found the supper-table, and was happy, eating steadily\par
through the bill of fare, and dismayed the _garçons_ by the ravages he\par
committed. But the Emperor's friend covered himself with glory, for he\par
danced everything, whether he knew it or not, and introduced impromptu\par
pirouettes when the figures bewildered him. The boyish abandon of that\par
stout man was charming to behold; for, though he "carried weight," he\par
danced like an india-rubber ball. He ran, he flew, he pranced; his face\par
glowed, his bald head shone; his coat-tails waved wildly, his pumps\par
actually twinkled in the air, and when the music stopped, he wiped the\par
drops from his brow, and beamed upon his fellow-men like a French\par
Pickwick without glasses.\par
\par
Amy and her Pole distinguished themselves by equal enthusiasm, but more\par
graceful agility; and Laurie found himself involuntarily keeping time to\par
the rhythmic rise and fall of the white slippers as they flew by as\par
indefatigably as if winged. When little Vladimir finally relinquished\par
her, with assurances that he was "desolated to leave so early," she was\par
ready to rest, and see how her recreant knight had borne his punishment.\par
\par
It had been successful; for, at three-and-twenty, blighted affections\par
find a balm in friendly society, and young nerves will thrill, young\par
blood dance, and healthy young spirits rise, when subjected to the\par
enchantment of beauty, light, music, and motion. Laurie had a waked-up\par
look as he rose to give her his seat; and when he hurried away to bring\par
her some supper, she said to herself, with a satisfied smile,--\par
\par
"Ah, I thought that would do him good!"\par
\par
"You look like Balzac's 'Femme peinte par elle-même,'" he said, as he\par
fanned her with one hand, and held her coffee-cup in the other.\par
\par
"My rouge won't come off;" and Amy rubbed her brilliant cheek, and\par
showed him her white glove with a sober simplicity that made him laugh\par
outright.\par
\par
"What do you call this stuff?" he asked, touching a fold of her dress\par
that had blown over his knee.\par
\par
"Illusion."\par
\par
"Good name for it; it's very pretty--new thing, isn't it?"\par
\par
"It's as old as the hills; you have seen it on dozens of girls, and you\par
never found out that it was pretty till now--_stupide_!"\par
\par
"I never saw it on you before, which accounts for the mistake, you see."\par
\par
"None of that, it is forbidden; I'd rather take coffee than compliments\par
just now. No, don't lounge, it makes me nervous."\par
\par
Laurie sat bolt upright, and meekly took her empty plate, feeling an odd\par
sort of pleasure in having "little Amy" order him about; for she had\par
lost her shyness now, and felt an irresistible desire to trample on him,\par
as girls have a delightful way of doing when lords of creation show any\par
signs of subjection.\par
\par
"Where did you learn all this sort of thing?" he asked, with a quizzical\par
look.\par
\par
"As 'this sort of thing' is rather a vague expression, would you kindly\par
explain?" returned Amy, knowing perfectly well what he meant, but\par
wickedly leaving him to describe what is indescribable.\par
\par
"Well--the general air, the style, the self-possession,\par
the--the--illusion--you know," laughed Laurie, breaking down, and\par
helping himself out of his quandary with the new word.\par
\par
Amy was gratified, but, of course, didn't show it, and demurely\par
answered, "Foreign life polishes one in spite of one's self; I study as\par
well as play; and as for this"--with a little gesture toward her\par
dress--"why, tulle is cheap, posies to be had for nothing, and I am used\par
to making the most of my poor little things."\par
\par
Amy rather regretted that last sentence, fearing it wasn't in good\par
taste; but Laurie liked her the better for it, and found himself both\par
admiring and respecting the brave patience that made the most of\par
opportunity, and the cheerful spirit that covered poverty with flowers.\par
Amy did not know why he looked at her so kindly, nor why he filled up\par
her book with his own name, and devoted himself to her for the rest of\par
the evening, in the most delightful manner; but the impulse that wrought\par
this agreeable change was the result of one of the new impressions which\par
both of them were unconsciously giving and receiving.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                     [Illustration: Demi and Daisy]\par
\par
                                XXXVIII.\par
\par
                             ON THE SHELF.\par
\par
\par
In France the young girls have a dull time of it till they are married,\par
when "_Vive la liberté_" becomes their motto. In America, as every one\par
knows, girls early sign the declaration of independence, and enjoy their\par
freedom with republican zest; but the young matrons usually abdicate\par
with the first heir to the throne, and go into a seclusion almost as\par
close as a French nunnery, though by no means as quiet. Whether they\par
like it or not, they are virtually put upon the shelf as soon as the\par
wedding excitement is over, and most of them might exclaim, as did a\par
very pretty woman the other day, "I'm as handsome as ever, but no one\par
takes any notice of me because I'm married."\par
\par
Not being a belle or even a fashionable lady, Meg did not experience\par
this affliction till her babies were a year old, for in her little world\par
primitive customs prevailed, and she found herself more admired and\par
beloved than ever.\par
\par
As she was a womanly little woman, the maternal instinct was very\par
strong, and she was entirely absorbed in her children, to the utter\par
exclusion of everything and everybody else. Day and night she brooded\par
over them with tireless devotion and anxiety, leaving John to the tender\par
mercies of the help, for an Irish lady now presided over the kitchen\par
department. Being a domestic man, John decidedly missed the wifely\par
attentions he had been accustomed to receive; but, as he adored his\par
babies, he cheerfully relinquished his comfort for a time, supposing,\par
with masculine ignorance, that peace would soon be restored. But three\par
months passed, and there was no return of repose; Meg looked worn and\par
nervous, the babies absorbed every minute of her time, the house was\par
neglected, and Kitty, the cook, who took life "aisy," kept him on short\par
commons. When he went out in the morning he was bewildered by small\par
commissions for the captive mamma; if he came gayly in at night, eager\par
to embrace his family, he was quenched by a "Hush! they are just asleep\par
after worrying all day." If he proposed a little amusement at home, "No,\par
it would disturb the babies." If he hinted at a lecture or concert, he\par
was answered with a reproachful look, and a decided "Leave my children\par
for pleasure, never!" His sleep was broken by infant wails and visions\par
of a phantom figure pacing noiselessly to and fro in the watches of the\par
night; his meals were interrupted by the frequent flight of the\par
presiding genius, who deserted him, half-helped, if a muffled chirp\par
sounded from the nest above; and when he read his paper of an evening,\par
Demi's colic got into the shipping-list, and Daisy's fall affected the\par
price of stocks, for Mrs. Brooke was only interested in domestic news.\par
\par
The poor man was very uncomfortable, for the children had bereft him of\par
his wife; home was merely a nursery, and the perpetual "hushing" made\par
him feel like a brutal intruder whenever he entered the sacred precincts\par
of Babyland. He bore it very patiently for six months, and, when no\par
signs of amendment appeared, he did what other paternal exiles\par
do,--tried to get a little comfort elsewhere. Scott had married and gone\par
to housekeeping not far off, and John fell into the way of running over\par
for an hour or two of an evening, when his own parlor was empty, and his\par
own wife singing lullabies that seemed to have no end. Mrs. Scott was a\par
lively, pretty girl, with nothing to do but be agreeable, and she\par
performed her mission most successfully. The parlor was always bright\par
and attractive, the chess-board ready, the piano in tune, plenty of gay\par
gossip, and a nice little supper set forth in tempting style.\par
\par
John would have preferred his own fireside if it had not been so lonely;\par
but as it was, he gratefully took the next best thing, and enjoyed his\par
neighbor's society.\par
\par
Meg rather approved of the new arrangement at first, and found it a\par
relief to know that John was having a good time instead of dozing in the\par
parlor, or tramping about the house and waking the children. But by and\par
by, when the teething worry was over, and the idols went to sleep at\par
proper hours, leaving mamma time to rest, she began to miss John, and\par
find her work-basket dull company, when he was not sitting opposite in\par
his old dressing-gown, comfortably scorching his slippers on the fender.\par
She would not ask him to stay at home, but felt injured because he did\par
not know that she wanted him without being told, entirely forgetting the\par
many evenings he had waited for her in vain. She was nervous and worn\par
out with watching and worry, and in that unreasonable frame of mind\par
which the best of mothers occasionally experience when domestic cares\par
oppress them. Want of exercise robs them of cheerfulness, and too much\par
devotion to that idol of American women, the teapot, makes them feel as\par
if they were all nerve and no muscle.\par
\par
"Yes," she would say, looking in the glass, "I'm getting old and ugly;\par
John doesn't find me interesting any longer, so he leaves his faded wife\par
and goes to see his pretty neighbor, who has no incumbrances. Well, the\par
babies love me; they don't care if I am thin and pale, and haven't time\par
to crimp my hair; they are my comfort, and some day John will see what\par
I've gladly sacrificed for them, won't he, my precious?"\par
\par
To which pathetic appeal Daisy would answer with a coo, or Demi with a\par
crow, and Meg would put by her lamentations for a maternal revel, which\par
soothed her solitude for the time being. But the pain increased as\par
politics absorbed John, who was always running over to discuss\par
interesting points with Scott, quite unconscious that Meg missed him.\par
Not a word did she say, however, till her mother found her in tears one\par
day, and insisted on knowing what the matter was, for Meg's drooping\par
spirits had not escaped her observation.\par
\par
"I wouldn't tell any one except you, mother; but I really do need\par
advice, for, if John goes on so much longer I might as well be widowed,"\par
replied Mrs. Brooke, drying her tears on Daisy's bib, with an injured\par
air.\par
\par
"Goes on how, my dear?" asked her mother anxiously.\par
\par
"He's away all day, and at night, when I want to see him, he is\par
continually going over to the Scotts'. It isn't fair that I should have\par
the hardest work, and never any amusement. Men are very selfish, even\par
the best of them."\par
\par
"So are women; don't blame John till you see where you are wrong\par
yourself."\par
\par
"But it can't be right for him to neglect me."\par
\par
"Don't you neglect him?"\par
\par
"Why, mother, I thought you'd take my part!"\par
\par
"So I do, as far as sympathizing goes; but I think the fault is yours,\par
Meg."\par
\par
"I don't see how."\par
\par
"Let me show you. Did John ever neglect you, as you call it, while you\par
made it a point to give him your society of an evening, his only leisure\par
time?"\par
\par
"No; but I can't do it now, with two babies to tend."\par
\par
"I think you could, dear; and I think you ought. May I speak quite\par
freely, and will you remember that it's mother who blames as well as\par
mother who sympathizes?"\par
\par
"Indeed I will! Speak to me as if I were little Meg again. I often feel\par
as if I needed teaching more than ever since these babies look to me for\par
everything."\par
\par
Meg drew her low chair beside her mother's, and, with a little\par
interruption in either lap, the two women rocked and talked lovingly\par
together, feeling that the tie of motherhood made them more one than\par
ever.\par
\par
"You have only made the mistake that most young wives make,--forgotten\par
your duty to your husband in your love for your children. A very natural\par
and forgivable mistake, Meg, but one that had better be remedied before\par
you take to different ways; for children should draw you nearer than\par
ever, not separate you, as if they were all yours, and John had nothing\par
to do but support them. I've seen it for some weeks, but have not\par
spoken, feeling sure it would come right in time."\par
\par
"I'm afraid it won't. If I ask him to stay, he'll think I'm jealous; and\par
I wouldn't insult him by such an idea. He doesn't see that I want him,\par
and I don't know how to tell him without words."\par
\par
"Make it so pleasant he won't want to go away. My dear, he's longing for\par
his little home; but it isn't home without you, and you are always in\par
the nursery."\par
\par
"Oughtn't I to be there?"\par
\par
"Not all the time; too much confinement makes you nervous, and then you\par
are unfitted for everything. Besides, you owe something to John as well\par
as to the babies; don't neglect husband for children, don't shut him out\par
of the nursery, but teach him how to help in it. His place is there as\par
well as yours, and the children need him; let him feel that he has his\par
part to do, and he will do it gladly and faithfully, and it will be\par
better for you all."\par
\par
"You really think so, mother?"\par
\par
"I know it, Meg, for I've tried it; and I seldom give advice unless I've\par
proved its practicability. When you and Jo were little, I went on just\par
as you are, feeling as if I didn't do my duty unless I devoted myself\par
wholly to you. Poor father took to his books, after I had refused all\par
offers of help, and left me to try my experiment alone. I struggled\par
along as well as I could, but Jo was too much for me. I nearly spoilt\par
her by indulgence. You were poorly, and I worried about you till I fell\par
sick myself. Then father came to the rescue, quietly managed everything,\par
and made himself so helpful that I saw my mistake, and never have been\par
able to get on without him since. That is the secret of our home\par
happiness: he does not let business wean him from the little cares and\par
duties that affect us all, and I try not to let domestic worries destroy\par
my interest in his pursuits. Each do our part alone in many things, but\par
at home we work together, always."\par
\par
"It is so, mother; and my great wish is to be to my husband and children\par
what you have been to yours. Show me how; I'll do anything you say."\par
\par
"You always were my docile daughter. Well, dear, if I were you, I'd let\par
John have more to do with the management of Demi, for the boy needs\par
training, and it's none too soon to begin. Then I'd do what I have often\par
proposed, let Hannah come and help you; she is a capital nurse, and you\par
may trust the precious babies to her while you do more housework. You\par
need the exercise, Hannah would enjoy the rest, and John would find his\par
wife again. Go out more; keep cheerful as well as busy, for you are the\par
sunshine-maker of the family, and if you get dismal there is no fair\par
weather. Then I'd try to take an interest in whatever John likes,--talk\par
with him, let him read to you, exchange ideas, and help each other in\par
that way. Don't shut yourself up in a bandbox because you are a woman,\par
but understand what is going on, and educate yourself to take your part\par
in the world's work, for it all affects you and yours."\par
\par
"John is so sensible, I'm afraid he will think I'm stupid if I ask\par
questions about politics and things."\par
\par
"I don't believe he would; love covers a multitude of sins, and of whom\par
could you ask more freely than of him? Try it, and see if he doesn't\par
find your society far more agreeable than Mrs. Scott's suppers."\par
\par
"I will. Poor John! I'm afraid I _have_ neglected him sadly, but I\par
thought I was right, and he never said anything."\par
\par
"He tried not to be selfish, but he _has_ felt rather forlorn, I fancy.\par
This is just the time, Meg, when young married people are apt to grow\par
apart, and the very time when they ought to be most together; for the\par
first tenderness soon wears off, unless care is taken to preserve it;\par
and no time is so beautiful and precious to parents as the first years\par
of the little lives given them to train. Don't let John be a stranger to\par
the babies, for they will do more to keep him safe and happy in this\par
world of trial and temptation than anything else, and through them you\par
will learn to know and love one another as you should. Now, dear,\par
good-by; think over mother's preachment, act upon it if it seems good,\par
and God bless you all!"\par
\par
Meg did think it over, found it good, and acted upon it, though the\par
first attempt was not made exactly as she planned to have it. Of course\par
the children tyrannized over her, and ruled the house as soon as they\par
found out that kicking and squalling brought them whatever they wanted.\par
Mamma was an abject slave to their caprices, but papa was not so easily\par
subjugated, and occasionally afflicted his tender spouse by an attempt\par
at paternal discipline with his obstreperous son. For Demi inherited a\par
trifle of his sire's firmness of character,--we won't call it\par
obstinacy,--and when he made up his little mind to have or to do\par
anything, all the king's horses and all the king's men could not change\par
that pertinacious little mind. Mamma thought the dear too young to be\par
taught to conquer his prejudices, but papa believed that it never was\par
too soon to learn obedience; so Master Demi early discovered that when\par
he undertook to "wrastle" with "parpar," he always got the worst of it;\par
yet, like the Englishman, Baby respected the man who conquered him, and\par
loved the father whose grave "No, no," was more impressive than all\par
mamma's love-pats.\par
\par
A few days after the talk with her mother, Meg resolved to try a social\par
evening with John; so she ordered a nice supper, set the parlor in\par
order, dressed herself prettily, and put the children to bed early, that\par
nothing should interfere with her experiment. But, unfortunately, Demi's\par
most unconquerable prejudice was against going to bed, and that night he\par
decided to go on a rampage; so poor Meg sung and rocked, told stories\par
and tried every sleep-provoking wile she could devise, but all in vain,\par
the big eyes wouldn't shut; and long after Daisy had gone to byelow,\par
like the chubby little bunch of good-nature she was, naughty Demi lay\par
staring at the light, with the most discouragingly wide-awake expression\par
of countenance.\par
\par
"Will Demi lie still like a good boy, while mamma runs down and gives\par
poor papa his tea?" asked Meg, as the hall-door softly closed, and the\par
well-known step went tiptoeing into the dining-room.\par
\par
"Me has tea!" said Demi, preparing to join in the revel.\par
\par
"No; but I'll save you some little cakies for breakfast, if you'll go\par
bye-by like Daisy. Will you, lovey?"\par
\par
"Iss!" and Demi shut his eyes tight, as if to catch sleep and hurry the\par
desired day.\par
\par
Taking advantage of the propitious moment, Meg slipped away, and ran\par
down to greet her husband with a smiling face, and the little blue bow\par
in her hair which was his especial admiration. He saw it at once, and\par
said, with pleased surprise,--\par
\par
"Why, little mother, how gay we are to-night. Do you expect company?"\par
\par
"Only you, dear."\par
\par
"Is it a birthday, anniversary, or anything?"\par
\par
"No; I'm tired of being a dowdy, so I dressed up as a change. You always\par
make yourself nice for table, no matter how tired you are; so why\par
shouldn't I when I have the time?"\par
\par
"I do it out of respect to you, my dear," said old-fashioned John.\par
\par
"Ditto, ditto, Mr. Brooke," laughed Meg, looking young and pretty again,\par
as she nodded to him over the teapot.\par
\par
"Well, it's altogether delightful, and like old times. This tastes\par
right. I drink your health, dear." And John sipped his tea with an air\par
of reposeful rapture, which was of very short duration, however; for, as\par
he put down his cup, the door-handle rattled mysteriously, and a little\par
voice was heard, saying impatiently,--\par
\par
"Opy doy; me's tummin!"\par
\par
"It's that naughty boy. I told him to go to sleep alone, and here he is,\par
downstairs, getting his death a-cold pattering over that canvas," said\par
Meg, answering the call.\par
\par
                      [Illustration: Mornin' now]\par
\par
"Mornin' now," announced Demi, in a joyful tone, as he entered, with his\par
long night-gown gracefully festooned over his arm, and every curl\par
bobbing gayly as he pranced about the table, eying the "cakies" with\par
loving glances.\par
\par
"No, it isn't morning yet. You must go to bed, and not trouble poor\par
mamma; then you can have the little cake with sugar on it."\par
\par
"Me loves parpar," said the artful one, preparing to climb the paternal\par
knee, and revel in forbidden joys. But John shook his head, and said to\par
Meg,--\par
\par
"If you told him to stay up there, and go to sleep alone, make him do\par
it, or he will never learn to mind you."\par
\par
"Yes, of course. Come, Demi;" and Meg led her son away, feeling a\par
strong desire to spank the little marplot who hopped beside her,\par
laboring under the delusion that the bribe was to be administered as\par
soon as they reached the nursery.\par
\par
Nor was he disappointed; for that short-sighted woman actually gave him\par
a lump of sugar, tucked him into his bed, and forbade any more\par
promenades till morning.\par
\par
"Iss!" said Demi the perjured, blissfully sucking his sugar, and\par
regarding his first attempt as eminently successful.\par
\par
Meg returned to her place, and supper was progressing pleasantly, when\par
the little ghost walked again, and exposed the maternal delinquencies by\par
boldly demanding,--\par
\par
"More sudar, marmar."\par
\par
"Now this won't do," said John, hardening his heart against the engaging\par
little sinner. "We shall never know any peace till that child learns to\par
go to bed properly. You have made a slave of yourself long enough; give\par
him one lesson, and then there will be an end of it. Put him in his bed\par
and leave him, Meg."\par
\par
"He won't stay there; he never does, unless I sit by him."\par
\par
"I'll manage him. Demi, go upstairs, and get into your bed, as mamma\par
bids you."\par
\par
"S'ant!" replied the young rebel, helping himself to the coveted\par
"cakie," and beginning to eat the same with calm audacity.\par
\par
"You must never say that to papa; I shall carry you if you don't go\par
yourself."\par
\par
"Go 'way; me don't love parpar;" and Demi retired to his mother's skirts\par
for protection.\par
\par
But even that refuge proved unavailing, for he was delivered over to the\par
enemy, with a "Be gentle with him, John," which struck the culprit with\par
dismay; for when mamma deserted him, then the judgment-day was at hand.\par
Bereft of his cake, defrauded of his frolic, and borne away by a strong\par
hand to that detested bed, poor Demi could not restrain his wrath, but\par
openly defied papa, and kicked and screamed lustily all the way\par
upstairs. The minute he was put into bed on one side, he rolled out on\par
the other, and made for the door, only to be ignominiously caught up by\par
the tail of his little toga, and put back again, which lively\par
performance was kept up till the young man's strength gave out, when he\par
devoted himself to roaring at the top of his voice. This vocal exercise\par
usually conquered Meg; but John sat as unmoved as the post which is\par
popularly believed to be deaf. No coaxing, no sugar, no lullaby, no\par
story; even the light was put out, and only the red glow of the fire\par
enlivened the "big dark" which Demi regarded with curiosity rather than\par
fear. This new order of things disgusted him, and he howled dismally for\par
"marmar," as his angry passions subsided, and recollections of his\par
tender bondwoman returned to the captive autocrat. The plaintive wail\par
which succeeded the passionate roar went to Meg's heart, and she ran up\par
to say beseechingly,--\par
\par
"Let me stay with him; he'll be good, now, John."\par
\par
"No, my dear, I've told him he must go to sleep, as you bid him; and he\par
must, if I stay here all night."\par
\par
"But he'll cry himself sick," pleaded Meg, reproaching herself for\par
deserting her boy.\par
\par
"No, he won't, he's so tired he will soon drop off, and then the matter\par
is settled; for he will understand that he has got to mind. Don't\par
interfere; I'll manage him."\par
\par
"He's my child, and I can't have his spirit broken by harshness."\par
\par
"He's my child, and I won't have his temper spoilt by indulgence. Go\par
down, my dear, and leave the boy to me."\par
\par
When John spoke in that masterful tone, Meg always obeyed, and never\par
regretted her docility.\par
\par
"Please let me kiss him once, John?"\par
\par
"Certainly. Demi, say 'good-night' to mamma, and let her go and rest,\par
for she is very tired with taking care of you all day."\par
\par
Meg always insisted upon it that the kiss won the victory; for after it\par
was given, Demi sobbed more quietly, and lay quite still at the bottom\par
of the bed, whither he had wriggled in his anguish of mind.\par
\par
"Poor little man, he's worn out with sleep and crying. I'll cover him\par
up, and then go and set Meg's heart at rest," thought John, creeping to\par
the bedside, hoping to find his rebellious heir asleep.\par
\par
But he wasn't; for the moment his father peeped at him, Demi's eyes\par
opened, his little chin began to quiver, and he put up his arms, saying,\par
with a penitent hiccough, "Me's dood, now."\par
\par
Sitting on the stairs, outside, Meg wondered at the long silence which\par
followed the uproar; and, after imagining all sorts of impossible\par
accidents, she slipped into the room, to set her fears at rest. Demi lay\par
fast asleep; not in his usual spread-eagle attitude, but in a subdued\par
bunch, cuddled close in the circle of his father's arm and holding his\par
father's finger, as if he felt that justice was tempered with mercy, and\par
had gone to sleep a sadder and a wiser baby. So held, John had waited\par
with womanly patience till the little hand relaxed its hold; and, while\par
waiting, had fallen asleep, more tired by that tussle with his son than\par
with his whole day's work.\par
\par
As Meg stood watching the two faces on the pillow, she smiled to\par
herself, and then slipped away again, saying, in a satisfied tone,--\par
\par
"I never need fear that John will be too harsh with my babies: he _does_\par
know how to manage them, and will be a great help, for Demi _is_ getting\par
too much for me."\par
\par
When John came down at last, expecting to find a pensive or reproachful\par
wife, he was agreeably surprised to find Meg placidly trimming a bonnet,\par
and to be greeted with the request to read something about the election,\par
if he was not too tired. John saw in a minute that a revolution of some\par
kind was going on, but wisely asked no questions, knowing that Meg was\par
such a transparent little person, she couldn't keep a secret to save her\par
life, and therefore the clew would soon appear. He read a long debate\par
with the most amiable readiness, and then explained it in his most lucid\par
manner, while Meg tried to look deeply interested, to ask intelligent\par
questions, and keep her thoughts from wandering from the state of the\par
nation to the state of her bonnet. In her secret soul, however, she\par
decided that politics were as bad as mathematics, and that the mission\par
of politicians seemed to be calling each other names; but she kept these\par
feminine ideas to herself, and when John paused, shook her head, and\par
said with what she thought diplomatic ambiguity,--\par
\par
"Well, I really don't see what we are coming to."\par
\par
John laughed, and watched her for a minute, as she poised a pretty\par
little preparation of lace and flowers on her hand, and regarded it with\par
the genuine interest which his harangue had failed to waken.\par
\par
"She is trying to like politics for my sake, so I'll try and like\par
millinery for hers, that's only fair," thought John the Just, adding\par
aloud,--\par
\par
"That's very pretty; is it what you call a breakfast-cap?"\par
\par
               [Illustration: My dear man, it's a bonnet]\par
\par
"My dear man, it's a bonnet! My very best go-to-concert-and-theatre\par
bonnet."\par
\par
"I beg your pardon; it was so small, I naturally mistook it for one of\par
the fly-away things you sometimes wear. How do you keep it on?"\par
\par
"These bits of lace are fastened under the chin with a rosebud, so;" and\par
Meg illustrated by putting on the bonnet, and regarding him with an air\par
of calm satisfaction that was irresistible.\par
\par
"It's a love of a bonnet, but I prefer the face inside, for it looks\par
young and happy again," and John kissed the smiling face, to the great\par
detriment of the rosebud under the chin.\par
\par
"I'm glad you like it, for I want you to take me to one of the new\par
concerts some night; I really need some music to put me in tune. Will\par
you, please?"\par
\par
"Of course I will, with all my heart, or anywhere else you like. You\par
have been shut up so long, it will do you no end of good, and I shall\par
enjoy it, of all things. What put it into your head, little mother?"\par
\par
"Well, I had a talk with Marmee the other day, and told her how nervous\par
and cross and out of sorts I felt, and she said I needed change and less\par
care; so Hannah is to help me with the children, and I'm to see to\par
things about the house more, and now and then have a little fun, just to\par
keep me from getting to be a fidgety, broken-down old woman before my\par
time. It's only an experiment, John, and I want to try it for your sake\par
as much as for mine, because I've neglected you shamefully lately, and\par
I'm going to make home what it used to be, if I can. You don't object, I\par
hope?"\par
\par
Never mind what John said, or what a very narrow escape the little\par
bonnet had from utter ruin; all that we have any business to know, is\par
that John did _not_ appear to object, judging from the changes which\par
gradually took place in the house and its inmates. It was not all\par
Paradise by any means, but every one was better for the division of\par
labor system; the children throve under the paternal rule, for accurate,\par
steadfast John brought order and obedience into Babydom, while Meg\par
recovered her spirits and composed her nerves by plenty of wholesome\par
exercise, a little pleasure, and much confidential conversation with her\par
sensible husband. Home grew home-like again, and John had no wish to\par
leave it, unless he took Meg with him. The Scotts came to the Brookes'\par
now, and every one found the little house a cheerful place, full of\par
happiness, content, and family love. Even gay Sallie Moffatt liked to go\par
there. "It is always so quiet and pleasant here; it does me good, Meg,"\par
she used to say, looking about her with wistful eyes, as if trying to\par
discover the charm, that she might use it in her great house, full of\par
splendid loneliness; for there were no riotous, sunny-faced babies\par
there, and Ned lived in a world of his own, where there was no place for\par
her.\par
\par
This household happiness did not come all at once, but John and Meg had\par
found the key to it, and each year of married life taught them how to\par
use it, unlocking the treasuries of real home-love and mutual\par
helpfulness, which the poorest may possess, and the richest cannot buy.\par
This is the sort of shelf on which young wives and mothers may consent\par
to be laid, safe from the restless fret and fever of the world, finding\par
loyal lovers in the little sons and daughters who cling to them,\par
undaunted by sorrow, poverty, or age; walking side by side, through fair\par
and stormy weather, with a faithful friend, who is, in the true sense of\par
the good old Saxon word, the "house-band," and learning, as Meg learned,\par
that a woman's happiest kingdom is home, her highest honor the art of\par
ruling it, not as a queen, but a wise wife and mother.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
     [Illustration: Sat piping on a stone while his goats skipped]\par
\par
                                 XXXIX.\par
\par
                             LAZY LAURENCE.\par
\par
\par
Laurie went to Nice intending to stay a week, and remained a month. He\par
was tired of wandering about alone, and Amy's familiar presence seemed\par
to give a home-like charm to the foreign scenes in which she bore a\par
part. He rather missed the "petting" he used to receive, and enjoyed a\par
taste of it again; for no attentions, however flattering, from\par
strangers, were half so pleasant as the sisterly adoration of the girls\par
at home. Amy never would pet him like the others, but she was very glad\par
to see him now, and quite clung to him, feeling that he was the\par
representative of the dear family for whom she longed more than she\par
would confess. They naturally took comfort in each other's society, and\par
were much together, riding, walking, dancing, or dawdling, for, at Nice,\par
no one can be very industrious during the gay season. But, while\par
apparently amusing themselves in the most careless fashion, they were\par
half-consciously making discoveries and forming opinions about each\par
other. Amy rose daily in the estimation of her friend, but he sunk in\par
hers, and each felt the truth before a word was spoken. Amy tried to\par
please, and succeeded, for she was grateful for the many pleasures he\par
gave her, and repaid him with the little services to which womanly women\par
know how to lend an indescribable charm. Laurie made no effort of any\par
kind, but just let himself drift along as comfortably as possible,\par
trying to forget, and feeling that all women owed him a kind word\par
because one had been cold to him. It cost him no effort to be generous,\par
and he would have given Amy all the trinkets in Nice if she would have\par
taken them; but, at the same time, he felt that he could not change the\par
opinion she was forming of him, and he rather dreaded the keen blue eyes\par
that seemed to watch him with such half-sorrowful, half-scornful\par
surprise.\par
\par
"All the rest have gone to Monaco for the day; I preferred to stay at\par
home and write letters. They are done now, and I am going to Valrosa to\par
sketch; will you come?" said Amy, as she joined Laurie one lovely day\par
when he lounged in as usual, about noon.\par
\par
"Well, yes; but isn't it rather warm for such a long walk?" he answered\par
slowly, for the shaded _salon_ looked inviting, after the glare without.\par
\par
"I'm going to have the little carriage, and Baptiste can drive, so\par
you'll have nothing to do but hold your umbrella and keep your gloves\par
nice," returned Amy, with a sarcastic glance at the immaculate kids,\par
which were a weak point with Laurie.\par
\par
"Then I'll go with pleasure;" and he put out his hand for her\par
sketch-book. But she tucked it under her arm with a sharp--\par
\par
"Don't trouble yourself; it's no exertion to me, but _you_ don't look\par
equal to it."\par
\par
Laurie lifted his eyebrows, and followed at a leisurely pace as she ran\par
downstairs; but when they got into the carriage he took the reins\par
himself, and left little Baptiste nothing to do but fold his arms and\par
fall asleep on his perch.\par
\par
The two never quarrelled,--Amy was too well-bred, and just now Laurie\par
was too lazy; so, in a minute he peeped under her hat-brim with an\par
inquiring air; she answered with a smile, and they went on together in\par
the most amicable manner.\par
\par
It was a lovely drive, along winding roads rich in the picturesque\par
scenes that delight beauty-loving eyes. Here an ancient monastery,\par
whence the solemn chanting of the monks came down to them. There a\par
bare-legged shepherd, in wooden shoes, pointed hat, and rough jacket\par
over one shoulder, sat piping on a stone, while his goats skipped among\par
the rocks or lay at his feet. Meek, mouse-colored donkeys, laden with\par
panniers of freshly-cut grass, passed by, with a pretty girl in a\par
_capaline_ sitting between the green piles, or an old woman spinning\par
with a distaff as she went. Brown, soft-eyed children ran out from the\par
quaint stone hovels to offer nosegays, or bunches of oranges still on\par
the bough. Gnarled olive-trees covered the hills with their dusky\par
foliage, fruit hung golden in the orchard, and great scarlet anemones\par
fringed the roadside; while beyond green slopes and craggy heights, the\par
Maritime Alps rose sharp and white against the blue Italian sky.\par
\par
Valrosa well deserved its name, for, in that climate of perpetual\par
summer, roses blossomed everywhere. They overhung the archway, thrust\par
themselves between the bars of the great gate with a sweet welcome to\par
passers-by, and lined the avenue, winding through lemon-trees and\par
feathery palms up to the villa on the hill. Every shadowy nook, where\par
seats invited one to stop and rest, was a mass of bloom; every cool\par
grotto had its marble nymph smiling from a veil of flowers, and every\par
fountain reflected crimson, white, or pale pink roses, leaning down to\par
smile at their own beauty. Roses covered the walls of the house, draped\par
the cornices, climbed the pillars, and ran riot over the balustrade of\par
the wide terrace, whence one looked down on the sunny Mediterranean, and\par
the white-walled city on its shore.\par
\par
"This is a regular honeymoon Paradise, isn't it? Did you ever see such\par
roses?" asked Amy, pausing on the terrace to enjoy the view, and a\par
luxurious whiff of perfume that came wandering by.\par
\par
"No, nor felt such thorns," returned Laurie, with his thumb in his\par
mouth, after a vain attempt to capture a solitary scarlet flower that\par
grew just beyond his reach.\par
\par
"Try lower down, and pick those that have no thorns," said Amy,\par
gathering three of the tiny cream-colored ones that starred the wall\par
behind her. She put them in his button-hole, as a peace-offering, and he\par
stood a minute looking down at them with a curious expression, for in\par
the Italian part of his nature there was a touch of superstition, and\par
he was just then in that state of half-sweet, half-bitter melancholy,\par
when imaginative young men find significance in trifles, and food for\par
romance everywhere. He had thought of Jo in reaching after the thorny\par
red rose, for vivid flowers became her, and she had often worn ones like\par
that from the greenhouse at home. The pale roses Amy gave him were the\par
sort that the Italians lay in dead hands, never in bridal wreaths, and,\par
for a moment, he wondered if the omen was for Jo or for himself; but the\par
next instant his American common-sense got the better of sentimentality,\par
and he laughed a heartier laugh than Amy had heard since he came.\par
\par
"It's good advice; you'd better take it and save your fingers," she\par
said, thinking her speech amused him.\par
\par
"Thank you, I will," he answered in jest, and a few months later he did\par
it in earnest.\par
\par
"Laurie, when are you going to your grandfather?" she asked presently,\par
as she settled herself on a rustic seat.\par
\par
"Very soon."\par
\par
"You have said that a dozen times within the last three weeks."\par
\par
"I dare say; short answers save trouble."\par
\par
"He expects you, and you really ought to go."\par
\par
"Hospitable creature! I know it."\par
\par
"Then why don't you do it?"\par
\par
"Natural depravity, I suppose."\par
\par
"Natural indolence, you mean. It's really dreadful!" and Amy looked\par
severe.\par
\par
"Not so bad as it seems, for I should only plague him if I went, so I\par
might as well stay, and plague you a little longer, you can bear it\par
better; in fact, I think it agrees with you excellently;" and Laurie\par
composed himself for a lounge on the broad ledge of the balustrade.\par
\par
Amy shook her head, and opened her sketch-book with an air of\par
resignation; but she had made up her mind to lecture "that boy," and in\par
a minute she began again.\par
\par
"What are you doing just now?"\par
\par
"Watching lizards."\par
\par
"No, no; I mean what do you intend and wish to do?"\par
\par
"Smoke a cigarette, if you'll allow me."\par
\par
"How provoking you are! I don't approve of cigars, and I will only allow\par
it on condition that you let me put you into my sketch; I need a\par
figure."\par
\par
"With all the pleasure in life. How will you have me,--full-length or\par
three-quarters, on my head or my heels? I should respectfully suggest a\par
recumbent posture, then put yourself in also, and call it '_Dolce far\par
niente._'"\par
\par
"Stay as you are, and go to sleep if you like. _I_ intend to work hard,"\par
said Amy, in her most energetic tone.\par
\par
"What delightful enthusiasm!" and he leaned against a tall urn with an\par
air of entire satisfaction.\par
\par
"What would Jo say if she saw you now?" asked Amy impatiently, hoping to\par
stir him up by the mention of her still more energetic sister's name.\par
\par
"As usual, 'Go away, Teddy, I'm busy!'" He laughed as he spoke, but the\par
laugh was not natural, and a shade passed over his face, for the\par
utterance of the familiar name touched the wound that was not healed\par
yet. Both tone and shadow struck Amy, for she had seen and heard them\par
before, and now she looked up in time to catch a new expression on\par
Laurie's face,--a hard, bitter look, full of pain, dissatisfaction, and\par
regret. It was gone before she could study it, and the listless\par
expression back again. She watched him for a moment with artistic\par
pleasure, thinking how like an Italian he looked, as he lay basking in\par
the sun with uncovered head, and eyes full of southern dreaminess; for\par
he seemed to have forgotten her, and fallen into a reverie.\par
\par
"You look like the effigy of a young knight asleep on his tomb," she\par
said, carefully tracing the well-cut profile defined against the dark\par
stone.\par
\par
"Wish I was!"\par
\par
"That's a foolish wish, unless you have spoilt your life. You are so\par
changed, I sometimes think--" there Amy stopped, with a half-timid,\par
half-wistful look, more significant than her unfinished speech.\par
\par
Laurie saw and understood the affectionate anxiety which she hesitated\par
to express, and looking straight into her eyes, said, just as he used to\par
say it to her mother,--\par
\par
"It's all right, ma'am."\par
\par
That satisfied her and set at rest the doubts that had begun to worry\par
her lately. It also touched her, and she showed that it did, by the\par
cordial tone in which she said,--\par
\par
"I'm glad of that! I didn't think you'd been a very bad boy, but I\par
fancied you might have wasted money at that wicked Baden-Baden, lost\par
your heart to some charming Frenchwoman with a husband, or got into some\par
of the scrapes that young men seem to consider a necessary part of a\par
foreign tour. Don't stay out there in the sun; come and lie on the grass\par
here, and 'let us be friendly,' as Jo used to say when we got in the\par
sofa-corner and told secrets."\par
\par
         [Illustration: Laurie threw himself down on the turf]\par
\par
Laurie obediently threw himself down on the turf, and began to amuse\par
himself by sticking daisies into the ribbons of Amy's hat, that lay\par
there.\par
\par
"I'm all ready for the secrets;" and he glanced up with a decided\par
expression of interest in his eyes.\par
\par
"I've none to tell; you may begin."\par
\par
"Haven't one to bless myself with. I thought perhaps you'd had some news\par
from home."\par
\par
"You have heard all that has come lately. Don't you hear often? I\par
fancied Jo would send you volumes."\par
\par
"She's very busy; I'm roving about so, it's impossible to be regular,\par
you know. When do you begin your great work of art, Raphaella?" he\par
asked, changing the subject abruptly after another pause, in which he\par
had been wondering if Amy knew his secret, and wanted to talk about it.\par
\par
"Never," she answered, with a despondent but decided air. "Rome took all\par
the vanity out of me; for after seeing the wonders there, I felt too\par
insignificant to live, and gave up all my foolish hopes in despair."\par
\par
"Why should you, with so much energy and talent?"\par
\par
"That's just why,--because talent isn't genius, and no amount of energy\par
can make it so. I want to be great, or nothing. I won't be a\par
common-place dauber, so I don't intend to try any more."\par
\par
"And what are you going to do with yourself now, if I may ask?"\par
\par
"Polish up my other talents, and be an ornament to society, if I get the\par
chance."\par
\par
It was a characteristic speech, and sounded daring; but audacity becomes\par
young people, and Amy's ambition had a good foundation. Laurie smiled,\par
but he liked the spirit with which she took up a new purpose when a\par
long-cherished one died, and spent no time lamenting.\par
\par
"Good! and here is where Fred Vaughn comes in, I fancy."\par
\par
Amy preserved a discreet silence, but there was a conscious look in her\par
downcast face, that made Laurie sit up and say gravely,--\par
\par
"Now I'm going to play brother, and ask questions. May I?"\par
\par
"I don't promise to answer."\par
\par
"Your face will, if your tongue won't. You aren't woman of the world\par
enough yet to hide your feelings, my dear. I heard rumors about Fred and\par
you last year, and it's my private opinion that, if he had not been\par
called home so suddenly and detained so long, something would have come\par
of it--hey?"\par
\par
"That's not for me to say," was Amy's prim reply; but her lips would\par
smile, and there was a traitorous sparkle of the eye, which betrayed\par
that she knew her power and enjoyed the knowledge.\par
\par
"You are not engaged, I hope?" and Laurie looked very elder-brotherly\par
and grave all of a sudden.\par
\par
"No."\par
\par
"But you will be, if he comes back and goes properly down upon his\par
knees, won't you?"\par
\par
"Very likely."\par
\par
"Then you are fond of old Fred?"\par
\par
"I could be, if I tried."\par
\par
"But you don't intend to try till the proper moment? Bless my soul, what\par
unearthly prudence! He's a good fellow, Amy, but not the man I fancied\par
you'd like."\par
\par
"He is rich, a gentleman, and has delightful manners," began Amy, trying\par
to be quite cool and dignified, but feeling a little ashamed of herself,\par
in spite of the sincerity of her intentions.\par
\par
"I understand; queens of society can't get on without money, so you mean\par
to make a good match, and start in that way? Quite right and proper, as\par
the world goes, but it sounds odd from the lips of one of your mother's\par
girls."\par
\par
"True, nevertheless."\par
\par
A short speech, but the quiet decision with which it was uttered\par
contrasted curiously with the young speaker. Laurie felt this\par
instinctively, and laid himself down again, with a sense of\par
disappointment which he could not explain. His look and silence, as well\par
as a certain inward self-disapproval, ruffled Amy, and made her resolve\par
to deliver her lecture without delay.\par
\par
"I wish you'd do me the favor to rouse yourself a little," she said\par
sharply.\par
\par
"Do it for me, there's a dear girl."\par
\par
"I could, if I tried;" and she looked as if she would like doing it in\par
the most summary style.\par
\par
"Try, then; I give you leave," returned Laurie, who enjoyed having some\par
one to tease, after his long abstinence from his favorite pastime.\par
\par
"You'd be angry in five minutes."\par
\par
"I'm never angry with you. It takes two flints to make a fire: you are\par
as cool and soft as snow."\par
\par
"You don't know what I can do; snow produces a glow and a tingle, if\par
applied rightly. Your indifference is half affectation, and a good\par
stirring up would prove it."\par
\par
"Stir away; it won't hurt me and it may amuse you, as the big man said\par
when his little wife beat him. Regard me in the light of a husband or a\par
carpet, and beat till you are tired, if that sort of exercise agrees\par
with you."\par
\par
Being decidedly nettled herself, and longing to see him shake off the\par
apathy that so altered him, Amy sharpened both tongue and pencil, and\par
began:--\par
\par
"Flo and I have got a new name for you; it's 'Lazy Laurence.' How do you\par
like it?"\par
\par
She thought it would annoy him; but he only folded his arms under his\par
head, with an imperturbable "That's not bad. Thank you, ladies."\par
\par
"Do you want to know what I honestly think of you?"\par
\par
"Pining to be told."\par
\par
"Well, I despise you."\par
\par
If she had even said "I hate you," in a petulant or coquettish tone, he\par
would have laughed, and rather liked it; but the grave, almost sad,\par
accent of her voice made him open his eyes, and ask quickly,--\par
\par
"Why, if you please?"\par
\par
"Because, with every chance for being good, useful, and happy, you are\par
faulty, lazy, and miserable."\par
\par
"Strong language, mademoiselle."\par
\par
"If you like it, I'll go on."\par
\par
"Pray, do; it's quite interesting."\par
\par
"I thought you'd find it so; selfish people always like to talk about\par
themselves."\par
\par
"Am _I_ selfish?" The question slipped out involuntarily and in a tone\par
of surprise, for the one virtue on which he prided himself was\par
generosity.\par
\par
"Yes, very selfish," continued Amy, in a calm, cool voice, twice as\par
effective, just then, as an angry one. "I'll show you how, for I've\par
studied you while we have been frolicking, and I'm not at all satisfied\par
with you. Here you have been abroad nearly six months, and done nothing\par
but waste time and money and disappoint your friends."\par
\par
"Isn't a fellow to have any pleasure after a four-years grind?"\par
\par
"You don't look as if you'd had much; at any rate, you are none the\par
better for it, as far as I can see. I said, when we first met, that you\par
had improved. Now I take it all back, for I don't think you half so nice\par
as when I left you at home. You have grown abominably lazy; you like\par
gossip, and waste time on frivolous things; you are contented to be\par
petted and admired by silly people, instead of being loved and respected\par
by wise ones. With money, talent, position, health, and beauty,--ah, you\par
like that, Old Vanity! but it's the truth, so I can't help saying\par
it,--with all these splendid things to use and enjoy, you can find\par
nothing to do but dawdle; and, instead of being the man you might and\par
ought to be, you are only--" There she stopped, with a look that had\par
both pain and pity in it.\par
\par
"Saint Laurence on a gridiron," added Laurie, blandly finishing the\par
sentence. But the lecture began to take effect, for there was a\par
wide-awake sparkle in his eyes now, and a half-angry, half-injured\par
expression replaced the former indifference.\par
\par
"I supposed you'd take it so. You men tell us we are angels, and say we\par
can make you what we will; but the instant we honestly try to do you\par
good, you laugh at us, and won't listen, which proves how much your\par
flattery is worth." Amy spoke bitterly, and turned her back on the\par
exasperating martyr at her feet.\par
\par
In a minute a hand came down over the page, so that she could not draw,\par
and Laurie's voice said, with a droll imitation of a penitent child,--\par
\par
"I will be good, oh, I will be good!"\par
\par
But Amy did not laugh, for she was in earnest; and, tapping on the\par
outspread hand with her pencil, said soberly,--\par
\par
"Aren't you ashamed of a hand like that? It's as soft and white as a\par
woman's, and looks as if it never did anything but wear Jouvin's best\par
gloves, and pick flowers for ladies. You are not a dandy, thank Heaven!\par
so I'm glad to see there are no diamonds or big seal-rings on it, only\par
the little old one Jo gave you so long ago. Dear soul, I wish she was\par
here to help me!"\par
\par
"So do I!"\par
\par
The hand vanished as suddenly as it came, and there was energy enough in\par
the echo of her wish to suit even Amy. She glanced down at him with a\par
new thought in her mind; but he was lying with his hat half over his\par
face, as if for shade, and his mustache hid his mouth. She only saw his\par
chest rise and fall, with a long breath that might have been a sigh, and\par
the hand that wore the ring nestled down into the grass, as if to hide\par
something too precious or too tender to be spoken of. All in a minute\par
various hints and trifles assumed shape and significance in Amy's mind,\par
and told her what her sister never had confided to her. She remembered\par
that Laurie never spoke voluntarily of Jo; she recalled the shadow on\par
his face just now, the change in his character, and the wearing of the\par
little old ring, which was no ornament to a handsome hand. Girls are\par
quick to read such signs and feel their eloquence. Amy had fancied that\par
perhaps a love trouble was at the bottom of the alteration, and now she\par
was sure of it. Her keen eyes filled, and, when she spoke again, it was\par
in a voice that could be beautifully soft and kind when she chose to\par
make it so.\par
\par
"I know I have no right to talk so to you, Laurie; and if you weren't\par
the sweetest-tempered fellow in the world, you'd be very angry with me.\par
But we are all so fond and proud of you, I couldn't bear to think they\par
should be disappointed in you at home as I have been, though, perhaps,\par
they would understand the change better than I do."\par
\par
"I think they would," came from under the hat, in a grim tone, quite as\par
touching as a broken one.\par
\par
"They ought to have told me, and not let me go blundering and scolding,\par
when I should have been more kind and patient than ever. I never did\par
like that Miss Randal, and now I hate her!" said artful Amy, wishing to\par
be sure of her facts this time.\par
\par
"Hang Miss Randal!" and Laurie knocked the hat off his face with a look\par
that left no doubt of his sentiments toward that young lady.\par
\par
"I beg pardon; I thought--" and there she paused diplomatically.\par
\par
"No, you didn't; you knew perfectly well I never cared for any one but\par
Jo." Laurie said that in his old, impetuous tone, and turned his face\par
away as he spoke.\par
\par
"I did think so; but as they never said anything about it, and you came\par
away, I supposed I was mistaken. And Jo wouldn't be kind to you? Why, I\par
was sure she loved you dearly."\par
\par
"She _was_ kind, but not in the right way; and it's lucky for her she\par
didn't love me, if I'm the good-for-nothing fellow you think me. It's\par
her fault, though, and you may tell her so."\par
\par
The hard, bitter look came back again as he said that, and it troubled\par
Amy, for she did not know what balm to apply.\par
\par
"I was wrong, I didn't know. I'm very sorry I was so cross, but I can't\par
help wishing you'd bear it better, Teddy, dear."\par
\par
"Don't, that's her name for me!" and Laurie put up his hand with a quick\par
gesture to stop the words spoken in Jo's half-kind, half-reproachful\par
tone. "Wait till you've tried it yourself," he added, in a low voice, as\par
he pulled up the grass by the handful.\par
\par
"I'd take it manfully, and be respected if I couldn't be loved," said\par
Amy, with the decision of one who knew nothing about it.\par
\par
Now, Laurie flattered himself that he _had_ borne it remarkably well,\par
making no moan, asking no sympathy, and taking his trouble away to live\par
it down alone. Amy's lecture put the matter in a new light, and for the\par
first time it did look weak and selfish to lose heart at the first\par
failure, and shut himself up in moody indifference. He felt as if\par
suddenly shaken out of a pensive dream, and found it impossible to go to\par
sleep again. Presently he sat up, and asked slowly,--\par
\par
"Do you think Jo would despise me as you do?"\par
\par
"Yes, if she saw you now. She hates lazy people. Why don't you do\par
something splendid, and _make_ her love you?"\par
\par
"I did my best, but it was no use."\par
\par
"Graduating well, you mean? That was no more than you ought to have\par
done, for your grandfather's sake. It would have been shameful to fail\par
after spending so much time and money, when every one knew you _could_\par
do well."\par
\par
"I did fail, say what you will, for Jo wouldn't love me," began Laurie,\par
leaning his head on his hand in a despondent attitude.\par
\par
"No, you didn't, and you'll say so in the end, for it did you good, and\par
proved that you could do something if you tried. If you'd only set about\par
another task of some sort, you'd soon be your hearty, happy self again,\par
and forget your trouble."\par
\par
"That's impossible."\par
\par
"Try it and see. You needn't shrug your shoulders, and think, 'Much she\par
knows about such things.' I don't pretend to be wise, but I _am_\par
observing, and I see a great deal more than you'd imagine. I'm\par
interested in other people's experiences and inconsistencies; and,\par
though I can't explain, I remember and use them for my own benefit. Love\par
Jo all your days, if you choose, but don't let it spoil you, for it's\par
wicked to throw away so many good gifts because you can't have the one\par
you want. There, I won't lecture any more, for I know you'll wake up and\par
be a man in spite of that hardhearted girl."\par
\par
Neither spoke for several minutes. Laurie sat turning the little ring on\par
his finger, and Amy put the last touches to the hasty sketch she had\par
been working at while she talked. Presently she put it on his knee,\par
merely saying,--\par
\par
"How do you like that?"\par
\par
He looked and then he smiled, as he could not well help doing, for it\par
was capitally done,--the long, lazy figure on the grass, with listless\par
face, half-shut eyes, and one hand holding a cigar, from which came the\par
little wreath of smoke that encircled the dreamer's head.\par
\par
"How well you draw!" he said, with genuine surprise and pleasure at her\par
skill, adding, with a half-laugh,--\par
\par
"Yes, that's me."\par
\par
"As you are: this is as you were;" and Amy laid another sketch beside\par
the one he held.\par
\par
It was not nearly so well done, but there was a life and spirit in it\par
which atoned for many faults, and it recalled the past so vividly that a\par
sudden change swept over the young man's face as he looked. Only a rough\par
sketch of Laurie taming a horse; hat and coat were off, and every line\par
of the active figure, resolute face, and commanding attitude, was full\par
of energy and meaning. The handsome brute, just subdued, stood arching\par
his neck under the tightly drawn rein, with one foot impatiently pawing\par
the ground, and ears pricked up as if listening for the voice that had\par
mastered him. In the ruffled mane, the rider's breezy hair and erect\par
attitude, there was a suggestion of suddenly arrested motion, of\par
strength, courage, and youthful buoyancy, that contrasted sharply with\par
the supine grace of the "_Dolce far niente_" sketch. Laurie said\par
nothing; but, as his eye went from one to the other, Amy saw him flush\par
up and fold his lips together as if he read and accepted the little\par
lesson she had given him. That satisfied her; and, without waiting for\par
him to speak, she said, in her sprightly way,--\par
\par
        [Illustration: A rough sketch of Laurie taming a horse]\par
\par
"Don't you remember the day you played Rarey with Puck, and we all\par
looked on? Meg and Beth were frightened, but Jo clapped and pranced, and\par
I sat on the fence and drew you. I found that sketch in my portfolio the\par
other day, touched it up, and kept it to show you."\par
\par
"Much obliged. You've improved immensely since then, and I congratulate\par
you. May I venture to suggest in 'a honeymoon Paradise' that five\par
o'clock is the dinner-hour at your hotel?"\par
\par
Laurie rose as he spoke, returned the pictures with a smile and a bow,\par
and looked at his watch, as if to remind her that even moral lectures\par
should have an end. He tried to resume his former easy, indifferent air,\par
but it _was_ an affectation now, for the rousing had been more\par
efficacious than he would confess. Amy felt the shade of coldness in his\par
manner, and said to herself,--\par
\par
"Now I've offended him. Well, if it does him good, I'm glad; if it makes\par
him hate me, I'm sorry; but it's true, and I can't take back a word of\par
it."\par
\par
They laughed and chatted all the way home; and little Baptiste, up\par
behind, thought that monsieur and mademoiselle were in charming spirits.\par
But both felt ill at ease; the friendly frankness was disturbed, the\par
sunshine had a shadow over it, and despite their apparent gayety, there\par
was a secret discontent in the heart of each.\par
\par
"Shall we see you this evening, _mon frère_?" asked Amy as they parted\par
at her aunt's door.\par
\par
"Unfortunately I have an engagement. _Au revoir, mademoiselle_," and\par
Laurie bent as if to kiss her hand, in the foreign fashion, which became\par
him better than many men. Something in his face made Amy say quickly and\par
warmly,--\par
\par
"No; be yourself with me, Laurie, and part in the good old way. I'd\par
rather have a hearty English hand-shake than all the sentimental\par
salutations in France."\par
\par
"Good-by, dear," and with these words, uttered in the tone she liked,\par
Laurie left her, after a hand-shake almost painful in its heartiness.\par
\par
Next morning, instead of the usual call, Amy received a note which made\par
her smile at the beginning and sigh at the end:--\par
\par
    "MY DEAR MENTOR,--\par
\par
    "Please make my adieux to your aunt, and exult within yourself,\par
    for 'Lazy Laurence' has gone to his grandpa, like the best of\par
    boys. A pleasant winter to you, and may the gods grant you a\par
    blissful honeymoon at Valrosa! I think Fred would be benefited\par
    by a rouser. Tell him so, with my congratulations.\par
\par
                            "Yours gratefully,          TELEMACHUS."\par
\par
"Good boy! I'm glad he's gone," said Amy, with an approving smile; the\par
next minute her face fell as she glanced about the empty room, adding,\par
with an involuntary sigh,--\par
\par
"Yes, I _am_ glad, but how I shall miss him!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                [Illustration: The Valley of the Shadow]\par
\par
                                  XL.\par
\par
                       THE VALLEY OF THE SHADOW.\par
\par
\par
When the first bitterness was over, the family accepted the inevitable,\par
and tried to bear it cheerfully, helping one another by the increased\par
affection which comes to bind households tenderly together in times of\par
trouble. They put away their grief, and each did his or her part toward\par
making that last year a happy one.\par
\par
The pleasantest room in the house was set apart for Beth, and in it was\par
gathered everything that she most loved,--flowers, pictures, her piano,\par
the little work-table, and the beloved pussies. Father's best books\par
found their way there, mother's easy-chair, Jo's desk, Amy's finest\par
sketches; and every day Meg brought her babies on a loving pilgrimage,\par
to make sunshine for Aunty Beth. John quietly set apart a little sum,\par
that he might enjoy the pleasure of keeping the invalid supplied with\par
the fruit she loved and longed for; old Hannah never wearied of\par
concocting dainty dishes to tempt a capricious appetite, dropping tears\par
as she worked; and from across the sea came little gifts and cheerful\par
letters, seeming to bring breaths of warmth and fragrance from lands\par
that know no winter.\par
\par
Here, cherished like a household saint in its shrine, sat Beth, tranquil\par
and busy as ever; for nothing could change the sweet, unselfish nature,\par
and even while preparing to leave life, she tried to make it happier for\par
those who should remain behind. The feeble fingers were never idle, and\par
one of her pleasures was to make little things for the school-children\par
daily passing to and fro,--to drop a pair of mittens from her window for\par
a pair of purple hands, a needle-book for some small mother of many\par
dolls, pen-wipers for young penmen toiling through forests of pot-hooks,\par
scrap-books for picture-loving eyes, and all manner of pleasant devices,\par
till the reluctant climbers up the ladder of learning found their way\par
strewn with flowers, as it were, and came to regard the gentle giver as\par
a sort of fairy godmother, who sat above there, and showered down gifts\par
miraculously suited to their tastes and needs. If Beth had wanted any\par
reward, she found it in the bright little faces always turned up to her\par
window, with nods and smiles, and the droll little letters which came to\par
her, full of blots and gratitude.\par
\par
The first few months were very happy ones, and Beth often used to look\par
round, and say "How beautiful this is!" as they all sat together in her\par
sunny room, the babies kicking and crowing on the floor, mother and\par
sisters working near, and father reading, in his pleasant voice, from\par
the wise old books which seemed rich in good and comfortable words, as\par
applicable now as when written centuries ago; a little chapel, where a\par
paternal priest taught his flock the hard lessons all must learn, trying\par
to show them that hope can comfort love, and faith make resignation\par
possible. Simple sermons, that went straight to the souls of those who\par
listened; for the father's heart was in the minister's religion, and the\par
frequent falter in the voice gave a double eloquence to the words he\par
spoke or read.\par
\par
It was well for all that this peaceful time was given them as\par
preparation for the sad hours to come; for, by and by, Beth said the\par
needle was "so heavy," and put it down forever; talking wearied her,\par
faces troubled her, pain claimed her for its own, and her tranquil\par
spirit was sorrowfully perturbed by the ills that vexed her feeble\par
flesh. Ah me! such heavy days, such long, long nights, such aching\par
hearts and imploring prayers, when those who loved her best were forced\par
to see the thin hands stretched out to them beseechingly, to hear the\par
bitter cry, "Help me, help me!" and to feel that there was no help. A\par
sad eclipse of the serene soul, a sharp struggle of the young life with\par
death; but both were mercifully brief, and then, the natural rebellion\par
over, the old peace returned more beautiful than ever. With the wreck of\par
her frail body, Beth's soul grew strong; and, though she said little,\par
those about her felt that she was ready, saw that the first pilgrim\par
called was likewise the fittest, and waited with her on the shore,\par
trying to see the Shining Ones coming to receive her when she crossed\par
the river.\par
\par
Jo never left her for an hour since Beth had said, "I feel stronger when\par
you are here." She slept on a couch in the room, waking often to renew\par
the fire, to feed, lift, or wait upon the patient creature who seldom\par
asked for anything, and "tried not to be a trouble." All day she haunted\par
the room, jealous of any other nurse, and prouder of being chosen then\par
than of any honor her life ever brought her. Precious and helpful hours\par
to Jo, for now her heart received the teaching that it needed; lessons\par
in patience were so sweetly taught her that she could not fail to learn\par
them; charity for all, the lovely spirit that can forgive and truly\par
forget unkindness, the loyalty to duty that makes the hardest easy, and\par
the sincere faith that fears nothing, but trusts undoubtingly.\par
\par
Often, when she woke, Jo found Beth reading in her well-worn little\par
book, heard her singing softly, to beguile the sleepless night, or saw\par
her lean her face upon her hands, while slow tears dropped through the\par
transparent fingers; and Jo would lie watching her, with thoughts too\par
deep for tears, feeling that Beth, in her simple, unselfish way, was\par
trying to wean herself from the dear old life, and fit herself for the\par
life to come, by sacred words of comfort, quiet prayers, and the music\par
she loved so well.\par
\par
Seeing this did more for Jo than the wisest sermons, the saintliest\par
hymns, the most fervent prayers that any voice could utter; for, with\par
eyes made clear by many tears, and a heart softened by the tenderest\par
sorrow, she recognized the beauty of her sister's life,--uneventful,\par
unambitious, yet full of the genuine virtues which "smell sweet, and\par
blossom in the dust," the self-forgetfulness that makes the humblest on\par
earth remembered soonest in heaven, the true success which is possible\par
to all.\par
\par
One night, when Beth looked among the books upon her table, to find\par
something to make her forget the mortal weariness that was almost as\par
hard to bear as pain, as she turned the leaves of her old favorite\par
Pilgrim's Progress, she found a little paper, scribbled over in Jo's\par
hand. The name caught her eye, and the blurred look of the lines made\par
her sure that tears had fallen on it.\par
\par
"Poor Jo! she's fast asleep, so I won't wake her to ask leave; she shows\par
me all her things, and I don't think she'll mind if I look at this,"\par
thought Beth, with a glance at her sister, who lay on the rug, with the\par
tongs beside her, ready to wake up the minute the log fell apart.\par
\par
\par
                               "MY BETH.\par
\par
        "Sitting patient in the shadow\par
           Till the blessed light shall come,\par
         A serene and saintly presence\par
           Sanctifies our troubled home.\par
         Earthly joys and hopes and sorrows\par
           Break like ripples on the strand\par
         Of the deep and solemn river\par
           Where her willing feet now stand.\par
\par
        "O my sister, passing from me,\par
           Out of human care and strife,\par
         Leave me, as a gift, those virtues\par
           Which have beautified your life.\par
         Dear, bequeath me that great patience\par
           Which has power to sustain\par
         A cheerful, uncomplaining spirit\par
           In its prison-house of pain.\par
\par
        "Give me, for I need it sorely,\par
           Of that courage, wise and sweet,\par
         Which has made the path of duty\par
           Green beneath your willing feet.\par
         Give me that unselfish nature,\par
           That with charity divine\par
         Can pardon wrong for love's dear sake--\par
           Meek heart, forgive me mine!\par
\par
        "Thus our parting daily loseth\par
          Something of its bitter pain,\par
         And while learning this hard lesson,\par
           My great loss becomes my gain.\par
         For the touch of grief will render\par
           My wild nature more serene,\par
         Give to life new aspirations,\par
           A new trust in the unseen.\par
\par
        "Henceforth, safe across the river,\par
           I shall see forevermore\par
         A beloved, household spirit\par
           Waiting for me on the shore.\par
         Hope and faith, born of my sorrow,\par
           Guardian angels shall become,\par
         And the sister gone before me\par
           By their hands shall lead me home."\par
\par
Blurred and blotted, faulty and feeble, as the lines were, they brought\par
a look of inexpressible comfort to Beth's face, for her one regret had\par
been that she had done so little; and this seemed to assure her that her\par
life had not been useless, that her death would not bring the despair\par
she feared. As she sat with the paper folded between her hands, the\par
charred log fell asunder. Jo started up, revived the blaze, and crept to\par
the bedside, hoping Beth slept.\par
\par
"Not asleep, but so happy, dear. See, I found this and read it; I knew\par
you wouldn't care. Have I been all that to you, Jo?" she asked, with\par
wistful, humble earnestness.\par
\par
"O Beth, so much, so much!" and Jo's head went down upon the pillow,\par
beside her sister's.\par
\par
"Then I don't feel as if I'd wasted my life. I'm not so good as you make\par
me, but I _have_ tried to do right; and now, when it's too late to\par
begin even to do better, it's such a comfort to know that some one loves\par
me so much, and feels as if I'd helped them."\par
\par
"More than any one in the world, Beth. I used to think I couldn't let\par
you go; but I'm learning to feel that I don't lose you; that you'll be\par
more to me than ever, and death can't part us, though it seems to."\par
\par
"I know it cannot, and I don't fear it any longer, for I'm sure I shall\par
be your Beth still, to love and help you more than ever. You must take\par
my place, Jo, and be everything to father and mother when I'm gone. They\par
will turn to you, don't fail them; and if it's hard to work alone,\par
remember that I don't forget you, and that you'll be happier in doing\par
that than writing splendid books or seeing all the world; for love is\par
the only thing that we can carry with us when we go, and it makes the\par
end so easy."\par
\par
"I'll try, Beth;" and then and there Jo renounced her old ambition,\par
pledged herself to a new and better one, acknowledging the poverty of\par
other desires, and feeling the blessed solace of a belief in the\par
immortality of love.\par
\par
So the spring days came and went, the sky grew clearer, the earth\par
greener, the flowers were up fair and early, and the birds came back in\par
time to say good-by to Beth, who, like a tired but trustful child, clung\par
to the hands that had led her all her life, as father and mother guided\par
her tenderly through the Valley of the Shadow, and gave her up to God.\par
\par
Seldom, except in books, do the dying utter memorable words, see\par
visions, or depart with beatified countenances; and those who have sped\par
many parting souls know that to most the end comes as naturally and\par
simply as sleep. As Beth had hoped, the "tide went out easily;" and in\par
the dark hour before the dawn, on the bosom where she had drawn her\par
first breath, she quietly drew her last, with no farewell but one loving\par
look, one little sigh.\par
\par
With tears and prayers and tender hands, mother and sisters made her\par
ready for the long sleep that pain would never mar again, seeing with\par
grateful eyes the beautiful serenity that soon replaced the pathetic\par
patience that had wrung their hearts so long, and feeling, with reverent\par
joy, that to their darling death was a benignant angel, not a phantom\par
full of dread.\par
\par
When morning came, for the first time in many months the fire was out,\par
Jo's place was empty, and the room was very still. But a bird sang\par
blithely on a budding bough, close by, the snow-drops blossomed freshly\par
at the window, and the spring sunshine streamed in like a benediction\par
over the placid face upon the pillow,--a face so full of painless peace\par
that those who loved it best smiled through their tears, and thanked God\par
that Beth was well at last.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                [Illustration: Sat staring up at the busts]\par
\par
                                  XLI.\par
\par
                          LEARNING TO FORGET.\par
\par
\par
Amy's lecture did Laurie good, though, of course, he did not own it till\par
long afterward; men seldom do, for when women are the advisers, the\par
lords of creation don't take the advice till they have persuaded\par
themselves that it is just what they intended to do; then they act upon\par
it, and, if it succeeds, they give the weaker vessel half the credit of\par
it; if it fails, they generously give her the whole. Laurie went back to\par
his grandfather, and was so dutifully devoted for several weeks that the\par
old gentleman declared the climate of Nice had improved him wonderfully,\par
and he had better try it again. There was nothing the young gentleman\par
would have liked better, but elephants could not have dragged him back\par
after the scolding he had received; pride forbid, and whenever the\par
longing grew very strong, he fortified his resolution by repeating the\par
words that had made the deepest impression, "I despise you;" "Go and do\par
something splendid that will _make_ her love you."\par
\par
Laurie turned the matter over in his mind so often that he soon brought\par
himself to confess that he _had_ been selfish and lazy; but then when a\par
man has a great sorrow, he should be indulged in all sorts of vagaries\par
till he has lived it down. He felt that his blighted affections were\par
quite dead now; and, though he should never cease to be a faithful\par
mourner, there was no occasion to wear his weeds ostentatiously. Jo\par
_wouldn't_ love him, but he might _make_ her respect and admire him by\par
doing something which should prove that a girl's "No" had not spoilt his\par
life. He had always meant to do something, and Amy's advice was quite\par
unnecessary. He had only been waiting till the aforesaid blighted\par
affections were decently interred; that being done, he felt that he was\par
ready to "hide his stricken heart, and still toil on."\par
\par
As Goethe, when he had a joy or a grief, put it into a song, so Laurie\par
resolved to embalm his love-sorrow in music, and compose a Requiem which\par
should harrow up Jo's soul and melt the heart of every hearer. Therefore\par
the next time the old gentleman found him getting restless and moody,\par
and ordered him off, he went to Vienna, where he had musical friends,\par
and fell to work with the firm determination to distinguish himself.\par
But, whether the sorrow was too vast to be embodied in music, or music\par
too ethereal to uplift a mortal woe, he soon discovered that the Requiem\par
was beyond him, just at present. It was evident that his mind was not in\par
working order yet, and his ideas needed clarifying; for often in the\par
middle of a plaintive strain, he would find himself humming a dancing\par
tune that vividly recalled the Christmas ball at Nice, especially the\par
stout Frenchman, and put an effectual stop to tragic composition for the\par
time being.\par
\par
Then he tried an Opera, for nothing seemed impossible in the beginning;\par
but here, again, unforeseen difficulties beset him. He wanted Jo for his\par
heroine, and called upon his memory to supply him with tender\par
recollections and romantic visions of his love. But memory turned\par
traitor; and, as if possessed by the perverse spirit of the girl, would\par
only recall Jo's oddities, faults, and freaks, would only show her in\par
the most unsentimental aspects,--beating mats with her head tied up in\par
a bandanna, barricading herself with the sofa-pillow, or throwing cold\par
water over his passion _à la_ Gummidge,--and an irresistible laugh\par
spoilt the pensive picture he was endeavoring to paint. Jo wouldn't be\par
put into the Opera at any price, and he had to give her up with a "Bless\par
that girl, what a torment she is!" and a clutch at his hair, as became a\par
distracted composer.\par
\par
When he looked about him for another and a less intractable damsel to\par
immortalize in melody, memory produced one with the most obliging\par
readiness. This phantom wore many faces, but it always had golden hair,\par
was enveloped in a diaphanous cloud, and floated airily before his\par
mind's eye in a pleasing chaos of roses, peacocks, white ponies, and\par
blue ribbons. He did not give the complacent wraith any name, but he\par
took her for his heroine, and grew quite fond of her, as well he might;\par
for he gifted her with every gift and grace under the sun, and escorted\par
her, unscathed, through trials which would have annihilated any mortal\par
woman.\par
\par
Thanks to this inspiration, he got on swimmingly for a time, but\par
gradually the work lost its charm, and he forgot to compose, while he\par
sat musing, pen in hand, or roamed about the gay city to get new ideas\par
and refresh his mind, which seemed to be in a somewhat unsettled state\par
that winter. He did not do much, but he thought a great deal and was\par
conscious of a change of some sort going on in spite of himself. "It's\par
genius simmering, perhaps. I'll let it simmer, and see what comes of\par
it," he said, with a secret suspicion, all the while, that it wasn't\par
genius, but something far more common. Whatever it was, it simmered to\par
some purpose, for he grew more and more discontented with his desultory\par
life, began to long for some real and earnest work to go at, soul and\par
body, and finally came to the wise conclusion that every one who loved\par
music was not a composer. Returning from one of Mozart's grand operas,\par
splendidly performed at the Royal Theatre, he looked over his own,\par
played a few of the best parts, sat staring up at the busts of\par
Mendelssohn, Beethoven, and Bach, who stared benignly back again; then\par
suddenly he tore up his music-sheets, one by one, and, as the last\par
fluttered out of his hand, he said soberly to himself,--\par
\par
"She is right! Talent isn't genius, and you can't make it so. That\par
music has taken the vanity out of me as Rome took it out of her, and I\par
won't be a humbug any longer. Now what shall I do?"\par
\par
That seemed a hard question to answer, and Laurie began to wish he had\par
to work for his daily bread. Now, if ever, occurred an eligible\par
opportunity for "going to the devil," as he once forcibly expressed it,\par
for he had plenty of money and nothing to do, and Satan is proverbially\par
fond of providing employment for full and idle hands. The poor fellow\par
had temptations enough from without and from within, but he withstood\par
them pretty well; for, much as he valued liberty, he valued good faith\par
and confidence more, so his promise to his grandfather, and his desire\par
to be able to look honestly into the eyes of the women who loved him,\par
and say "All's well," kept him safe and steady.\par
\par
Very likely some Mrs. Grundy will observe, "I don't believe it; boys\par
will be boys, young men must sow their wild oats, and women must not\par
expect miracles." I dare say _you_ don't, Mrs. Grundy, but it's true\par
nevertheless. Women work a good many miracles, and I have a persuasion\par
that they may perform even that of raising the standard of manhood by\par
refusing to echo such sayings. Let the boys be boys, the longer the\par
better, and let the young men sow their wild oats if they must; but\par
mothers, sisters, and friends may help to make the crop a small one, and\par
keep many tares from spoiling the harvest, by believing, and showing\par
that they believe, in the possibility of loyalty to the virtues which\par
make men manliest in good women's eyes. If it _is_ a feminine delusion,\par
leave us to enjoy it while we may, for without it half the beauty and\par
the romance of life is lost, and sorrowful forebodings would embitter\par
all our hopes of the brave, tender-hearted little lads, who still love\par
their mothers better than themselves, and are not ashamed to own it.\par
\par
Laurie thought that the task of forgetting his love for Jo would absorb\par
all his powers for years; but, to his great surprise, he discovered it\par
grew easier every day. He refused to believe it at first, got angry with\par
himself, and couldn't understand it; but these hearts of ours are\par
curious and contrary things, and time and nature work their will in\par
spite of us. Laurie's heart _wouldn't_ ache; the wound persisted in\par
healing with a rapidity that astonished him, and, instead of trying to\par
forget, he found himself trying to remember. He had not foreseen this\par
turn of affairs, and was not prepared for it. He was disgusted with\par
himself, surprised at his own fickleness, and full of a queer mixture of\par
disappointment and relief that he could recover from such a tremendous\par
blow so soon. He carefully stirred up the embers of his lost love, but\par
they refused to burst into a blaze: there was only a comfortable glow\par
that warmed and did him good without putting him into a fever, and he\par
was reluctantly obliged to confess that the boyish passion was slowly\par
subsiding into a more tranquil sentiment, very tender, a little sad and\par
resentful still, but that was sure to pass away in time, leaving a\par
brotherly affection which would last unbroken to the end.\par
\par
As the word "brotherly" passed through his mind in one of these\par
reveries, he smiled, and glanced up at the picture of Mozart that was\par
before him:--\par
\par
"Well, he was a great man; and when he couldn't have one sister he took\par
the other, and was happy."\par
\par
Laurie did not utter the words, but he thought them; and the next\par
instant kissed the little old ring, saying to himself,--\par
\par
"No, I won't! I haven't forgotten, I never can. I'll try again, and if\par
that fails, why, then--"\par
\par
Leaving his sentence unfinished, he seized pen and paper and wrote to\par
Jo, telling her that he could not settle to anything while there was the\par
least hope of her changing her mind. Couldn't she, wouldn't she, and let\par
him come home and be happy? While waiting for an answer he did nothing,\par
but he did it energetically, for he was in a fever of impatience. It\par
came at last, and settled his mind effectually on one point, for Jo\par
decidedly couldn't and wouldn't. She was wrapped up in Beth, and never\par
wished to hear the word "love" again. Then she begged him to be happy\par
with somebody else, but always to keep a little corner of his heart for\par
his loving sister Jo. In a postscript she desired him not to tell Amy\par
that Beth was worse; she was coming home in the spring, and there was no\par
need of saddening the remainder of her stay. That would be time enough,\par
please God, but Laurie must write to her often, and not let her feel\par
lonely, homesick, or anxious.\par
\par
"So I will, at once. Poor little girl; it will be a sad going home for\par
her, I'm afraid;" and Laurie opened his desk, as if writing to Amy had\par
been the proper conclusion of the sentence left unfinished some weeks\par
before.\par
\par
But he did not write the letter that day; for, as he rummaged out his\par
best paper, he came across something which changed his purpose. Tumbling\par
about in one part of the desk, among bills, passports, and business\par
documents of various kinds, were several of Jo's letters, and in another\par
compartment were three notes from Amy, carefully tied up with one of her\par
blue ribbons, and sweetly suggestive of the little dead roses put away\par
inside. With a half-repentant, half-amused expression, Laurie gathered\par
up all Jo's letters, smoothed, folded, and put them neatly into a small\par
drawer of the desk, stood a minute turning the ring thoughtfully on his\par
finger, then slowly drew it off, laid it with the letters, locked the\par
drawer, and went out to hear High Mass at Saint Stefan's, feeling as if\par
there had been a funeral; and, though not overwhelmed with affliction,\par
this seemed a more proper way to spend the rest of the day than in\par
writing letters to charming young ladies.\par
\par
     [Illustration: Turning the ring thoughtfully upon his finger]\par
\par
The letter went very soon, however, and was promptly answered, for Amy\par
_was_ homesick, and confessed it in the most delightfully confiding\par
manner. The correspondence flourished famously, and letters flew to and\par
fro, with unfailing regularity, all through the early spring. Laurie\par
sold his busts, made allumettes of his opera, and went back to Paris,\par
hoping somebody would arrive before long. He wanted desperately to go to\par
Nice, but would not till he was asked; and Amy would not ask him, for\par
just then she was having little experiences of her own, which made her\par
rather wish to avoid the quizzical eyes of "our boy."\par
\par
Fred Vaughn had returned, and put the question to which she had once\par
decided to answer "Yes, thank you;" but now she said, "No, thank you,"\par
kindly but steadily; for, when the time came, her courage failed her,\par
and she found that something more than money and position was needed to\par
satisfy the new longing that filled her heart so full of tender hopes\par
and fears. The words, "Fred is a good fellow, but not at all the man I\par
fancied you would ever like," and Laurie's face when he uttered them,\par
kept returning to her as pertinaciously as her own did when she said in\par
look, if not in words, "I shall marry for money." It troubled her to\par
remember that now, she wished she could take it back, it sounded so\par
unwomanly. She didn't want Laurie to think her a heartless, worldly\par
creature; she didn't care to be a queen of society now half so much as\par
she did to be a lovable woman; she was so glad he didn't hate her for\par
the dreadful things she said, but took them so beautifully, and was\par
kinder than ever. His letters were such a comfort, for the home letters\par
were very irregular, and were not half so satisfactory as his when they\par
did come. It was not only a pleasure, but a duty to answer them, for the\par
poor fellow was forlorn, and needed petting, since Jo persisted in being\par
stony-hearted. She ought to have made an effort, and tried to love him;\par
it couldn't be very hard, many people would be proud and glad to have\par
such a dear boy care for them; but Jo never would act like other girls,\par
so there was nothing to do but be very kind, and treat him like a\par
brother.\par
\par
If all brothers were treated as well as Laurie was at this period, they\par
would be a much happier race of beings than they are. Amy never lectured\par
now; she asked his opinion on all subjects; she was interested in\par
everything he did, made charming little presents for him, and sent him\par
two letters a week, full of lively gossip, sisterly confidences, and\par
captivating sketches of the lovely scenes about her. As few brothers are\par
complimented by having their letters carried about in their sisters'\par
pockets, read and reread diligently, cried over when short, kissed when\par
long, and treasured carefully, we will not hint that Amy did any of\par
these fond and foolish things. But she certainly did grow a little pale\par
and pensive that spring, lost much of her relish for society, and went\par
out sketching alone a good deal. She never had much to show when she\par
came home, but was studying nature, I dare say, while she sat for hours,\par
with her hands folded, on the terrace at Valrosa, or absently sketched\par
any fancy that occurred to her,--a stalwart knight carved on a tomb, a\par
young man asleep in the grass, with his hat over his eyes, or a\par
curly-haired girl in gorgeous array, promenading down a ball-room on the\par
arm of a tall gentleman, both faces being left a blur according to the\par
last fashion in art, which was safe, but not altogether satisfactory.\par
\par
Her aunt thought that she regretted her answer to Fred; and, finding\par
denials useless and explanations impossible, Amy left her to think what\par
she liked, taking care that Laurie should know that Fred had gone to\par
Egypt. That was all, but he understood it, and looked relieved, as he\par
said to himself, with a venerable air,--\par
\par
"I was sure she would think better of it. Poor old fellow! I've been\par
through it all, and I can sympathize."\par
\par
With that he heaved a great sigh, and then, as if he had discharged his\par
duty to the past, put his feet up on the sofa, and enjoyed Amy's letter\par
luxuriously.\par
\par
While these changes were going on abroad, trouble had come at home; but\par
the letter telling that Beth was failing never reached Amy, and when the\par
next found her, the grass was green above her sister. The sad news met\par
her at Vevay, for the heat had driven them from Nice in May, and they\par
had travelled slowly to Switzerland, by way of Genoa and the Italian\par
lakes. She bore it very well, and quietly submitted to the family decree\par
that she should not shorten her visit, for, since it was too late to say\par
good-by to Beth, she had better stay, and let absence soften her sorrow.\par
But her heart was very heavy; she longed to be at home, and every day\par
looked wistfully across the lake, waiting for Laurie to come and comfort\par
her.\par
\par
He did come very soon; for the same mail brought letters to them both,\par
but he was in Germany, and it took some days to reach him. The moment he\par
read it, he packed his knapsack, bade adieu to his fellow-pedestrians,\par
and was off to keep his promise, with a heart full of joy and sorrow,\par
hope and suspense.\par
\par
He knew Vevay well; and as soon as the boat touched the little quay, he\par
hurried along the shore to La Tour, where the Carrols were living _en\par
pension_. The _garçon_ was in despair that the whole family had gone to\par
take a promenade on the lake; but no, the blond mademoiselle might be in\par
the chateau garden. If monsieur would give himself the pain of sitting\par
down, a flash of time should present her. But monsieur could not wait\par
even "a flash of time," and, in the middle of the speech, departed to\par
find mademoiselle himself.\par
\par
A pleasant old garden on the borders of the lovely lake, with chestnuts\par
rustling overhead, ivy climbing everywhere, and the black shadow of the\par
tower falling far across the sunny water. At one corner of the wide, low\par
wall was a seat, and here Amy often came to read or work, or console\par
herself with the beauty all about her. She was sitting here that day,\par
leaning her head on her hand, with a homesick heart and heavy eyes,\par
thinking of Beth, and wondering why Laurie did not come. She did not\par
hear him cross the court-yard beyond, nor see him pause in the archway\par
that led from the subterranean path into the garden. He stood a minute,\par
looking at her with new eyes, seeing what no one had ever seen\par
before,--the tender side of Amy's character. Everything about her mutely\par
suggested love and sorrow,--the blotted letters in her lap, the black\par
ribbon that tied up her hair, the womanly pain and patience in her face;\par
even the little ebony cross at her throat seemed pathetic to Laurie, for\par
he had given it to her, and she wore it as her only ornament. If he had\par
any doubts about the reception she would give him, they were set at\par
rest the minute she looked up and saw him; for, dropping everything, she\par
ran to him, exclaiming, in a tone of unmistakable love and longing,--\par
\par
"O Laurie, Laurie, I knew you'd come to me!"\par
\par
          [Illustration: O Laurie, Laurie, I knew you'd come]\par
\par
I think everything was said and settled then; for, as they stood\par
together quite silent for a moment, with the dark head bent down\par
protectingly over the light one, Amy felt that no one could comfort and\par
sustain her so well as Laurie, and Laurie decided that Amy was the only\par
woman in the world who could fill Jo's place, and make him happy. He did\par
not tell her so; but she was not disappointed, for both felt the truth,\par
were satisfied, and gladly left the rest to silence.\par
\par
In a minute Amy went back to her place; and, while she dried her tears,\par
Laurie gathered up the scattered papers, finding in the sight of sundry\par
well-worn letters and suggestive sketches good omens for the future. As\par
he sat down beside her, Amy felt shy again, and turned rosy red at the\par
recollection of her impulsive greeting.\par
\par
"I couldn't help it; I felt so lonely and sad, and was so very glad to\par
see you. It was such a surprise to look up and find you, just as I was\par
beginning to fear you wouldn't come," she said, trying in vain to speak\par
quite naturally.\par
\par
"I came the minute I heard. I wish I could say something to comfort you\par
for the loss of dear little Beth; but I can only feel, and--" He could\par
not get any further, for he, too, turned bashful all of a sudden, and\par
did not quite know what to say. He longed to lay Amy's head down on his\par
shoulder, and tell her to have a good cry, but he did not dare; so took\par
her hand instead, and gave it a sympathetic squeeze that was better than\par
words.\par
\par
"You needn't say anything; this comforts me," she said softly. "Beth is\par
well and happy, and I mustn't wish her back; but I dread the going home,\par
much as I long to see them all. We won't talk about it now, for it makes\par
me cry, and I want to enjoy you while you stay. You needn't go right\par
back, need you?"\par
\par
"Not if you want me, dear."\par
\par
"I do, so much. Aunt and Flo are very kind; but you seem like one of the\par
family, and it would be so comfortable to have you for a little while."\par
\par
Amy spoke and looked so like a homesick child, whose heart was full,\par
that Laurie forgot his bashfulness all at once, and gave her just what\par
she wanted,--the petting she was used to and the cheerful conversation\par
she needed.\par
\par
"Poor little soul, you look as if you'd grieved yourself half-sick! I'm\par
going to take care of you, so don't cry any more, but come and walk\par
about with me; the wind is too chilly for you to sit still," he said, in\par
the half-caressing, half-commanding way that Amy liked, as he tied on\par
her hat, drew her arm through his, and began to pace up and down the\par
sunny walk, under the new-leaved chestnuts. He felt more at ease upon\par
his legs; and Amy found it very pleasant to have a strong arm to lean\par
upon, a familiar face to smile at her, and a kind voice to talk\par
delightfully for her alone.\par
\par
The quaint old garden had sheltered many pairs of lovers, and seemed\par
expressly made for them, so sunny and secluded was it, with nothing but\par
the tower to overlook them, and the wide lake to carry away the echo of\par
their words, as it rippled by below. For an hour this new pair walked\par
and talked, or rested on the wall, enjoying the sweet influences which\par
gave such a charm to time and place; and when an unromantic dinner-bell\par
warned them away, Amy felt as if she left her burden of loneliness and\par
sorrow behind her in the chateau garden.\par
\par
The moment Mrs. Carrol saw the girl's altered face, she was illuminated\par
with a new idea, and exclaimed to herself, "Now I understand it\par
all,--the child has been pining for young Laurence. Bless my heart, I\par
never thought of such a thing!"\par
\par
With praiseworthy discretion, the good lady said nothing, and betrayed\par
no sign of enlightenment; but cordially urged Laurie to stay, and begged\par
Amy to enjoy his society, for it would do her more good than so much\par
solitude. Amy was a model of docility; and, as her aunt was a good deal\par
occupied with Flo, she was left to entertain her friend, and did it with\par
more than her usual success.\par
\par
At Nice, Laurie had lounged and Amy had scolded; at Vevay, Laurie was\par
never idle, but always walking, riding, boating, or studying, in the\par
most energetic manner, while Amy admired everything he did, and followed\par
his example as far and as fast as she could. He said the change was\par
owing to the climate, and she did not contradict him, being glad of a\par
like excuse for her own recovered health and spirits.\par
\par
The invigorating air did them both good, and much exercise worked\par
wholesome changes in minds as well as bodies. They seemed to get clearer\par
views of life and duty up there among the everlasting hills; the fresh\par
winds blew away desponding doubts, delusive fancies, and moody mists;\par
the warm spring sunshine brought out all sorts of aspiring ideas, tender\par
hopes, and happy thoughts; the lake seemed to wash away the troubles of\par
the past, and the grand old mountains to look benignly down upon them,\par
saying, "Little children, love one another."\par
\par
In spite of the new sorrow, it was a very happy time, so happy that\par
Laurie could not bear to disturb it by a word. It took him a little\par
while to recover from his surprise at the rapid cure of his first, and,\par
as he had firmly believed, his last and only love. He consoled himself\par
for the seeming disloyalty by the thought that Jo's sister was almost\par
the same as Jo's self, and the conviction that it would have been\par
impossible to love any other woman but Amy so soon and so well. His\par
first wooing had been of the tempestuous order, and he looked back upon\par
it as if through a long vista of years, with a feeling of compassion\par
blended with regret. He was not ashamed of it, but put it away as one of\par
the bitter-sweet experiences of his life, for which he could be grateful\par
when the pain was over. His second wooing he resolved should be as calm\par
and simple as possible; there was no need of having a scene, hardly any\par
need of telling Amy that he loved her; she knew it without words, and\par
had given him his answer long ago. It all came about so naturally that\par
no one could complain, and he knew that everybody would be pleased, even\par
Jo. But when our first little passion has been crushed, we are apt to be\par
wary and slow in making a second trial; so Laurie let the days pass,\par
enjoying every hour, and leaving to chance the utterance of the word\par
that would put an end to the first and sweetest part of his new romance.\par
\par
He had rather imagined that the _dénouement_ would take place in the\par
chateau garden by moonlight, and in the most graceful and decorous\par
manner; but it turned out exactly the reverse, for the matter was\par
settled on the lake, at noonday, in a few blunt words. They had been\par
floating about all the morning, from gloomy St. Gingolf to sunny\par
Montreux, with the Alps of Savoy on one side, Mont St. Bernard and the\par
Dent du Midi on the other, pretty Vevay in the valley, and Lausanne upon\par
the hill beyond, a cloudless blue sky overhead, and the bluer lake\par
below, dotted with the picturesque boats that look like white-winged\par
gulls.\par
\par
They had been talking of Bonnivard, as they glided past Chillon, and of\par
Rousseau, as they looked up at Clarens, where he wrote his "Héloise."\par
Neither had read it, but they knew it was a love-story, and each\par
privately wondered if it was half as interesting as their own. Amy had\par
been dabbling her hand in the water during the little pause that fell\par
between them, and, when she looked up, Laurie was leaning on his oars,\par
with an expression in his eyes that made her say hastily, merely for the\par
sake of saying something,--\par
\par
"You must be tired; rest a little, and let me row; it will do me good;\par
for, since you came, I have been altogether lazy and luxurious."\par
\par
"I'm not tired; but you may take an oar, if you like. There's room\par
enough, though I have to sit nearly in the middle, else the boat won't\par
trim," returned Laurie, as if he rather liked the arrangement.\par
\par
Feeling that she had not mended matters much, Amy took the offered third\par
of a seat, shook her hair over her face, and accepted an oar. She rowed\par
as well as she did many other things; and, though she used both hands,\par
and Laurie but one, the oars kept time, and the boat went smoothly\par
through the water.\par
\par
               [Illustration: How well we pull together]\par
\par
"How well we pull together, don't we?" said Amy, who objected to silence\par
just then.\par
\par
"So well that I wish we might always pull in the same boat. Will you,\par
Amy?" very tenderly.\par
\par
"Yes, Laurie," very low.\par
\par
Then they both stopped rowing, and unconsciously added a pretty little\par
_tableau_ of human love and happiness to the dissolving views reflected\par
in the lake.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                 XLII.\par
\par
                               ALL ALONE.\par
\par
\par
It was easy to promise self-abnegation when self was wrapped up in\par
another, and heart and soul were purified by a sweet example; but when\par
the helpful voice was silent, the daily lesson over, the beloved\par
presence gone, and nothing remained but loneliness and grief, then Jo\par
found her promise very hard to keep. How could she "comfort father and\par
mother," when her own heart ached with a ceaseless longing for her\par
sister; how could she "make the house cheerful," when all its light and\par
warmth and beauty seemed to have deserted it when Beth left the old home\par
for the new; and where in all the world could she "find some useful,\par
happy work to do," that would take the place of the loving service which\par
had been its own reward? She tried in a blind, hopeless way to do her\par
duty, secretly rebelling against it all the while, for it seemed unjust\par
that her few joys should be lessened, her burdens made heavier, and life\par
get harder and harder as she toiled along. Some people seemed to get all\par
sunshine, and some all shadow; it was not fair, for she tried more than\par
Amy to be good, but never got any reward, only disappointment, trouble,\par
and hard work.\par
\par
Poor Jo, these were dark days to her, for something like despair came\par
over her when she thought of spending all her life in that quiet house,\par
devoted to humdrum cares, a few small pleasures, and the duty that never\par
seemed to grow any easier. "I can't do it. I wasn't meant for a life\par
like this, and I know I shall break away and do something desperate if\par
somebody don't come and help me," she said to herself, when her first\par
efforts failed, and she fell into the moody, miserable state of mind\par
which often comes when strong wills have to yield to the inevitable.\par
\par
But some one did come and help her, though Jo did not recognize her good\par
angels at once, because they wore familiar shapes, and used the simple\par
spells best fitted to poor humanity. Often she started up at night,\par
thinking Beth called her; and when the sight of the little empty bed\par
made her cry with the bitter cry of an unsubmissive sorrow, "O Beth,\par
come back! come back!" she did not stretch out her yearning arms in\par
vain; for, as quick to hear her sobbing as she had been to hear her\par
sister's faintest whisper, her mother came to comfort her, not with\par
words only, but the patient tenderness that soothes by a touch, tears\par
that were mute reminders of a greater grief than Jo's, and broken\par
whispers, more eloquent than prayers, because hopeful resignation went\par
hand-in-hand with natural sorrow. Sacred moments, when heart talked to\par
heart in the silence of the night, turning affliction to a blessing,\par
which chastened grief and strengthened love. Feeling this, Jo's burden\par
seemed easier to bear, duty grew sweeter, and life looked more\par
endurable, seen from the safe shelter of her mother's arms.\par
\par
When aching heart was a little comforted, troubled mind likewise found\par
help; for one day she went to the study, and, leaning over the good gray\par
head lifted to welcome her with a tranquil smile, she said, very\par
humbly,--\par
\par
"Father, talk to me as you did to Beth. I need it more than she did, for\par
I'm all wrong."\par
\par
"My dear, nothing can comfort me like this," he answered, with a falter\par
in his voice, and both arms round her, as if he, too, needed help, and\par
did not fear to ask it.\par
\par
                   [Illustration: Jo and her father]\par
\par
Then, sitting in Beth's little chair close beside him, Jo told her\par
troubles,--the resentful sorrow for her loss, the fruitless efforts that\par
discouraged her, the want of faith that made life look so dark, and all\par
the sad bewilderment which we call despair. She gave him entire\par
confidence, he gave her the help she needed, and both found consolation\par
in the act; for the time had come when they could talk together not only\par
as father and daughter, but as man and woman, able and glad to serve\par
each other with mutual sympathy as well as mutual love. Happy,\par
thoughtful times there in the old study which Jo called "the church of\par
one member," and from which she came with fresh courage, recovered\par
cheerfulness, and a more submissive spirit; for the parents who had\par
taught one child to meet death without fear, were trying now to teach\par
another to accept life without despondency or distrust, and to use its\par
beautiful opportunities with gratitude and power.\par
\par
Other helps had Jo,--humble, wholesome duties and delights that would\par
not be denied their part in serving her, and which she slowly learned to\par
see and value. Brooms and dishcloths never could be as distasteful as\par
they once had been, for Beth had presided over both; and something of\par
her housewifely spirit seemed to linger round the little mop and the old\par
brush, that was never thrown away. As she used them, Jo found herself\par
humming the songs Beth used to hum, imitating Beth's orderly ways, and\par
giving the little touches here and there that kept everything fresh and\par
cosey, which was the first step toward making home happy, though she\par
didn't know it, till Hannah said with an approving squeeze of the\par
hand,--\par
\par
"You thoughtful creter, you're determined we sha'n't miss that dear lamb\par
ef you can help it. We don't say much, but we see it, and the Lord will\par
bless you for't, see ef He don't."\par
\par
As they sat sewing together, Jo discovered how much improved her sister\par
Meg was; how well she could talk, how much she knew about good, womanly\par
impulses, thoughts, and feelings, how happy she was in husband and\par
children, and how much they were all doing for each other.\par
\par
"Marriage is an excellent thing, after all. I wonder if I should blossom\par
out half as well as you have, if I tried it?" said Jo, as she\par
constructed a kite for Demi, in the topsy-turvy nursery.\par
\par
"It's just what you need to bring out the tender, womanly half of your\par
nature, Jo. You are like a chestnut-burr, prickly outside, but\par
silky-soft within, and a sweet kernel, if one can only get at it. Love\par
will make you show your heart some day, and then the rough burr will\par
fall off."\par
\par
"Frost opens chestnut-burrs, ma'am, and it takes a good shake to bring\par
them down. Boys go nutting, and I don't care to be bagged by them,"\par
returned Jo, pasting away at the kite which no wind that blows would\par
ever carry up, for Daisy had tied herself on as a bob.\par
\par
Meg laughed, for she was glad to see a glimmer of Jo's old spirit, but\par
she felt it her duty to enforce her opinion by every argument in her\par
power; and the sisterly chats were not wasted, especially as two of\par
Meg's most effective arguments were the babies, whom Jo loved tenderly.\par
Grief is the best opener for some hearts, and Jo's was nearly ready for\par
the bag: a little more sunshine to ripen the nut, then, not a boy's\par
impatient shake, but a man's hand reached up to pick it gently from the\par
burr, and find the kernel sound and sweet. If she had suspected this,\par
she would have shut up tight, and been more prickly than ever;\par
fortunately she wasn't thinking about herself, so, when the time came,\par
down she dropped.\par
\par
Now, if she had been the heroine of a moral story-book, she ought at\par
this period of her life to have become quite saintly, renounced the\par
world, and gone about doing good in a mortified bonnet, with tracts in\par
her pocket. But, you see, Jo wasn't a heroine; she was only a struggling\par
human girl, like hundreds of others, and she just acted out her nature,\par
being sad, cross, listless, or energetic, as the mood suggested. It's\par
highly virtuous to say we'll be good, but we can't do it all at once,\par
and it takes a long pull, a strong pull, and a pull all together,\par
before some of us even get our feet set in the right way. Jo had got so\par
far, she was learning to do her duty, and to feel unhappy if she did\par
not; but to do it cheerfully--ah, that was another thing! She had often\par
said she wanted to do something splendid, no matter how hard; and now\par
she had her wish, for what could be more beautiful than to devote her\par
life to father and mother, trying to make home as happy to them as they\par
had to her? And, if difficulties were necessary to increase the splendor\par
of the effort, what could be harder for a restless, ambitious girl than\par
to give up her own hopes, plans, and desires, and cheerfully live for\par
others?\par
\par
Providence had taken her at her word; here was the task, not what she\par
had expected, but better, because self had no part in it: now, could she\par
do it? She decided that she would try; and, in her first attempt, she\par
found the helps I have suggested. Still another was given her, and she\par
took it, not as a reward, but as a comfort, as Christian took the\par
refreshment afforded by the little arbor where he rested, as he climbed\par
the hill called Difficulty.\par
\par
"Why don't you write? That always used to make you happy," said her\par
mother, once, when the desponding fit overshadowed Jo.\par
\par
"I've no heart to write, and if I had, nobody cares for my things."\par
\par
"We do; write something for us, and never mind the rest of the world.\par
Try it, dear; I'm sure it would do you good, and please us very much."\par
\par
"Don't believe I can;" but Jo got out her desk, and began to overhaul\par
her half-finished manuscripts.\par
\par
An hour afterward her mother peeped in, and there she was, scratching\par
away, with her black pinafore on, and an absorbed expression, which\par
caused Mrs. March to smile, and slip away, well pleased with the success\par
of her suggestion. Jo never knew how it happened, but something got into\par
that story that went straight to the hearts of those who read it; for,\par
when her family had laughed and cried over it, her father sent it, much\par
against her will, to one of the popular magazines, and, to her utter\par
surprise, it was not only paid for, but others requested. Letters from\par
several persons, whose praise was honor, followed the appearance of the\par
little story, newspapers copied it, and strangers as well as friends\par
admired it. For a small thing it was a great success; and Jo was more\par
astonished than when her novel was commended and condemned all at once.\par
\par
"I don't understand it. What _can_ there be in a simple little story\par
like that, to make people praise it so?" she said, quite bewildered.\par
\par
"There is truth in it, Jo, that's the secret; humor and pathos make it\par
alive, and you have found your style at last. You wrote with no thought\par
of fame or money, and put your heart into it, my daughter; you have had\par
the bitter, now comes the sweet. Do your best, and grow as happy as we\par
are in your success."\par
\par
"If there _is_ anything good or true in what I write, it isn't mine; I\par
owe it all to you and mother and to Beth," said Jo, more touched by her\par
father's words than by any amount of praise from the world.\par
\par
So, taught by love and sorrow, Jo wrote her little stories, and sent\par
them away to make friends for themselves and her, finding it a very\par
charitable world to such humble wanderers; for they were kindly\par
welcomed, and sent home comfortable tokens to their mother, like dutiful\par
children whom good fortune overtakes.\par
\par
When Amy and Laurie wrote of their engagement, Mrs. March feared that Jo\par
would find it difficult to rejoice over it, but her fears were soon set\par
at rest; for, though Jo looked grave at first, she took it very quietly,\par
and was full of hopes and plans for "the children" before she read the\par
letter twice. It was a sort of written duet, wherein each glorified the\par
other in lover-like fashion, very pleasant to read and satisfactory to\par
think of, for no one had any objection to make.\par
\par
"You like it, mother?" said Jo, as they laid down the closely written\par
sheets, and looked at one another.\par
\par
"Yes, I hoped it would be so, ever since Amy wrote that she had refused\par
Fred. I felt sure then that something better than what you call the\par
'mercenary spirit' had come over her, and a hint here and there in her\par
letters made me suspect that love and Laurie would win the day."\par
\par
"How sharp you are, Marmee, and how silent! You never said a word to\par
me."\par
\par
"Mothers have need of sharp eyes and discreet tongues when they have\par
girls to manage. I was half afraid to put the idea into your head, lest\par
you should write and congratulate them before the thing was settled."\par
\par
"I'm not the scatter-brain I was; you may trust me, I'm sober and\par
sensible enough for any one's _confidante_ now."\par
\par
"So you are, dear, and I should have made you mine, only I fancied it\par
might pain you to learn that your Teddy loved any one else."\par
\par
"Now, mother, did you really think I could be so silly and selfish,\par
after I'd refused his love, when it was freshest, if not best?"\par
\par
"I knew you were sincere then, Jo, but lately I have thought that if he\par
came back, and asked again, you might, perhaps, feel like giving another\par
answer. Forgive me, dear, I can't help seeing that you are very lonely,\par
and sometimes there is a hungry look in your eyes that goes to my heart;\par
so I fancied that your boy might fill the empty place if he tried now."\par
\par
"No, mother, it is better as it is, and I'm glad Amy has learned to love\par
him. But you are right in one thing: I _am_ lonely, and perhaps if Teddy\par
had tried again, I might have said 'Yes,' not because I love him any\par
more, but because I care more to be loved than when he went away."\par
\par
"I'm glad of that, Jo, for it shows that you are getting on. There are\par
plenty to love you, so try to be satisfied with father and mother,\par
sisters and brothers, friends and babies, till the best lover of all\par
comes to give you your reward."\par
\par
"Mothers are the _best_ lovers in the world; but I don't mind whispering\par
to Marmee that I'd like to try all kinds. It's very curious, but the\par
more I try to satisfy myself with all sorts of natural affections, the\par
more I seem to want. I'd no idea hearts could take in so many; mine is\par
so elastic, it never seems full now, and I used to be quite contented\par
with my family. I don't understand it."\par
\par
"I do;" and Mrs. March smiled her wise smile, as Jo turned back the\par
leaves to read what Amy said of Laurie.\par
\par
"It is so beautiful to be loved as Laurie loves me; he isn't\par
sentimental, doesn't say much about it, but I see and feel it in all he\par
says and does, and it makes me so happy and so humble that I don't seem\par
to be the same girl I was. I never knew how good and generous and tender\par
he was till now, for he lets me read his heart, and I find it full of\par
noble impulses and hopes and purposes, and am so proud to know it's\par
mine. He says he feels as if he 'could make a prosperous voyage now\par
with me aboard as mate, and lots of love for ballast.' I pray he may,\par
and try to be all he believes me, for I love my gallant captain with all\par
my heart and soul and might, and never will desert him, while God lets\par
us be together. O mother, I never knew how much like heaven this world\par
could be, when two people love and live for one another!"\par
\par
"And that's our cool, reserved, and worldly Amy! Truly, love does work\par
miracles. How very, very happy they must be!" And Jo laid the rustling\par
sheets together with a careful hand, as one might shut the covers of a\par
lovely romance, which holds the reader fast till the end comes, and he\par
finds himself alone in the work-a-day world again.\par
\par
By and by Jo roamed away upstairs, for it was rainy, and she could not\par
walk. A restless spirit possessed her, and the old feeling came again,\par
not bitter as it once was, but a sorrowfully patient wonder why one\par
sister should have all she asked, the other nothing. It was not true;\par
she knew that, and tried to put it away, but the natural craving for\par
affection was strong, and Amy's happiness woke the hungry longing for\par
some one to "love with heart and soul, and cling to while God let them\par
be together."\par
\par
Up in the garret, where Jo's unquiet wanderings ended, stood four little\par
wooden chests in a row, each marked with its owner's name, and each\par
filled with relics of the childhood and girlhood ended now for all. Jo\par
glanced into them, and when she came to her own, leaned her chin on the\par
edge, and stared absently at the chaotic collection, till a bundle of\par
old exercise-books caught her eye. She drew them out, turned them over,\par
and re-lived that pleasant winter at kind Mrs. Kirke's. She had smiled\par
at first, then she looked thoughtful, next sad, and when she came to a\par
little message written in the Professor's hand, her lips began to\par
tremble, the books slid out of her lap, and she sat looking at the\par
friendly words, as if they took a new meaning, and touched a tender spot\par
in her heart.\par
\par
"Wait for me, my friend. I may be a little late, but I shall surely\par
come."\par
\par
"Oh, if he only would! So kind, so good, so patient with me always; my\par
dear old Fritz, I didn't value him half enough when I had him, but now\par
how I should love to see him, for every one seems going away from me,\par
and I'm all alone."\par
\par
And holding the little paper fast, as if it were a promise yet to be\par
fulfilled, Jo laid her head down on a comfortable rag-bag, and cried, as\par
if in opposition to the rain pattering on the roof.\par
\par
  [Illustration: Jo laid her head on a comfortable rag-bag and cried]\par
\par
Was it all self-pity, loneliness, or low spirits? or was it the waking\par
up of a sentiment which had bided its time as patiently as its inspirer?\par
Who shall say?\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
     [Illustration: A substantial lifelike ghost leaning over her]\par
\par
                                 XLIII.\par
\par
                               SURPRISES.\par
\par
\par
Jo was alone in the twilight, lying on the old sofa, looking at the\par
fire, and thinking. It was her favorite way of spending the hour of\par
dusk; no one disturbed her, and she used to lie there on Beth's little\par
red pillow, planning stories, dreaming dreams, or thinking tender\par
thoughts of the sister who never seemed far away. Her face looked tired,\par
grave, and rather sad; for to-morrow was her birthday, and she was\par
thinking how fast the years went by, how old she was getting, and how\par
little she seemed to have accomplished. Almost twenty-five, and nothing\par
to show for it. Jo was mistaken in that; there was a good deal to show,\par
and by and by she saw, and was grateful for it.\par
\par
"An old maid, that's what I'm to be. A literary spinster, with a pen for\par
a spouse, a family of stories for children, and twenty years hence a\par
morsel of fame, perhaps; when, like poor Johnson, I'm old, and can't\par
enjoy it, solitary, and can't share it, independent, and don't need it.\par
Well, I needn't be a sour saint nor a selfish sinner; and, I dare say,\par
old maids are very comfortable when they get used to it; but--" and\par
there Jo sighed, as if the prospect was not inviting.\par
\par
It seldom is, at first, and thirty seems the end of all things to\par
five-and-twenty; but it's not so bad as it looks, and one can get on\par
quite happily if one has something in one's self to fall back upon. At\par
twenty-five, girls begin to talk about being old maids, but secretly\par
resolve that they never will be; at thirty they say nothing about it,\par
but quietly accept the fact, and, if sensible, console themselves by\par
remembering that they have twenty more useful, happy years, in which\par
they may be learning to grow old gracefully. Don't laugh at the\par
spinsters, dear girls, for often very tender, tragical romances are\par
hidden away in the hearts that beat so quietly under the sober gowns,\par
and many silent sacrifices of youth, health, ambition, love itself, make\par
the faded faces beautiful in God's sight. Even the sad, sour sisters\par
should be kindly dealt with, because they have missed the sweetest part\par
of life, if for no other reason; and, looking at them with compassion,\par
not contempt, girls in their bloom should remember that they too may\par
miss the blossom time; that rosy cheeks don't last forever, that silver\par
threads will come in the bonnie brown hair, and that, by and by,\par
kindness and respect will be as sweet as love and admiration now.\par
\par
Gentlemen, which means boys, be courteous to the old maids, no matter\par
how poor and plain and prim, for the only chivalry worth having is that\par
which is the readiest to pay deference to the old, protect the feeble,\par
and serve womankind, regardless of rank, age, or color. Just recollect\par
the good aunts who have not only lectured and fussed, but nursed and\par
petted, too often without thanks; the scrapes they have helped you out\par
of, the "tips" they have given you from their small store, the stitches\par
the patient old fingers have set for you, the steps the willing old feet\par
have taken, and gratefully pay the dear old ladies the little attentions\par
that women love to receive as long as they live. The bright-eyed girls\par
are quick to see such traits, and will like you all the better for them;\par
and if death, almost the only power that can part mother and son,\par
should rob you of yours, you will be sure to find a tender welcome and\par
maternal cherishing from some Aunt Priscilla, who has kept the warmest\par
corner of her lonely old heart for the "the best nevvy in the world."\par
\par
Jo must have fallen asleep (as I dare say my reader has during this\par
little homily), for suddenly Laurie's ghost seemed to stand before\par
her,--a substantial, lifelike ghost,--leaning over her, with the very\par
look he used to wear when he felt a good deal and didn't like to show\par
it. But, like Jenny in the ballad,--\par
\par
        "She could not think it he,"\par
\par
and lay staring up at him in startled silence, till he stooped and\par
kissed her. Then she knew him, and flew up, crying joyfully,--\par
\par
"O my Teddy! O my Teddy!"\par
\par
"Dear Jo, you are glad to see me, then?"\par
\par
"Glad! My blessed boy, words can't express my gladness. Where's Amy?"\par
\par
"Your mother has got her down at Meg's. We stopped there by the way, and\par
there was no getting my wife out of their clutches."\par
\par
"Your what?" cried Jo, for Laurie uttered those two words with an\par
unconscious pride and satisfaction which betrayed him.\par
\par
"Oh, the dickens! now I've done it;" and he looked so guilty that Jo was\par
down upon him like a flash.\par
\par
"You've gone and got married!"\par
\par
"Yes, please, but I never will again;" and he went down upon his knees,\par
with a penitent clasping of hands, and a face full of mischief, mirth,\par
and triumph.\par
\par
"Actually married?"\par
\par
"Very much so, thank you."\par
\par
"Mercy on us! What dreadful thing will you do next?" and Jo fell into\par
her seat, with a gasp.\par
\par
"A characteristic, but not exactly complimentary, congratulation,"\par
returned Laurie, still in an abject attitude, but beaming with\par
satisfaction.\par
\par
"What can you expect, when you take one's breath away, creeping in like\par
a burglar, and letting cats out of bags like that? Get up, you\par
ridiculous boy, and tell me all about it."\par
\par
"Not a word, unless you let me come in my old place, and promise not to\par
barricade."\par
\par
Jo laughed at that as she had not done for many a long day, and patted\par
the sofa invitingly, as she said, in a cordial tone,--\par
\par
"The old pillow is up garret, and we don't need it now; so, come and\par
'fess, Teddy."\par
\par
"How good it sounds to hear you say 'Teddy'! No one ever calls me that\par
but you;" and Laurie sat down, with an air of great content.\par
\par
"What does Amy call you?"\par
\par
"My lord."\par
\par
"That's like her. Well, you look it;" and Jo's eyes plainly betrayed\par
that she found her boy comelier than ever.\par
\par
The pillow was gone, but there _was_ a barricade, nevertheless,--a\par
natural one, raised by time, absence, and change of heart. Both felt it,\par
and for a minute looked at one another as if that invisible barrier cast\par
a little shadow over them. It was gone directly, however, for Laurie\par
said, with a vain attempt at dignity,--\par
\par
"Don't I look like a married man and the head of a family?"\par
\par
"Not a bit, and you never will. You've grown bigger and bonnier, but you\par
are the same scapegrace as ever."\par
\par
"Now, really, Jo, you ought to treat me with more respect," began\par
Laurie, who enjoyed it all immensely.\par
\par
"How can I, when the mere idea of you, married and settled, is so\par
irresistibly funny that I can't keep sober!" answered Jo, smiling all\par
over her face, so infectiously that they had another laugh, and then\par
settled down for a good talk, quite in the pleasant old fashion.\par
\par
"It's no use your going out in the cold to get Amy, for they are all\par
coming up presently. I couldn't wait; I wanted to be the one to tell you\par
the grand surprise, and have 'first skim,' as we used to say when we\par
squabbled about the cream."\par
\par
"Of course you did, and spoilt your story by beginning at the wrong end.\par
Now, start right, and tell me how it all happened; I'm pining to know."\par
\par
"Well, I did it to please Amy," began Laurie, with a twinkle that made\par
Jo exclaim,--\par
\par
"Fib number one; Amy did it to please you. Go on, and tell the truth, if\par
you can, sir."\par
\par
"Now she's beginning to marm it; isn't it jolly to hear her?" said\par
Laurie to the fire, and the fire glowed and sparkled as if it quite\par
agreed. "It's all the same, you know, she and I being one. We planned to\par
come home with the Carrols, a month or more ago, but they suddenly\par
changed their minds, and decided to pass another winter in Paris. But\par
grandpa wanted to come home; he went to please me, and I couldn't let\par
him go alone, neither could I leave Amy; and Mrs. Carrol had got English\par
notions about chaperons and such nonsense, and wouldn't let Amy come\par
with us. So I just settled the difficulty by saying, 'Let's be married,\par
and then we can do as we like.'"\par
\par
"Of course you did; you always have things to suit you."\par
\par
"Not always;" and something in Laurie's voice made Jo say hastily,--\par
\par
"How did you ever get aunt to agree?"\par
\par
"It was hard work; but, between us, we talked her over, for we had heaps\par
of good reasons on our side. There wasn't time to write and ask leave,\par
but you all liked it, had consented to it by and by, and it was only\par
'taking Time by the fetlock,' as my wife says."\par
\par
"Aren't we proud of those two words, and don't we like to say them?"\par
interrupted Jo, addressing the fire in her turn, and watching with\par
delight the happy light it seemed to kindle in the eyes that had been so\par
tragically gloomy when she saw them last.\par
\par
"A trifle, perhaps; she's such a captivating little woman I can't help\par
being proud of her. Well, then, uncle and aunt were there to play\par
propriety; we were so absorbed in one another we were of no mortal use\par
apart, and that charming arrangement would make everything easy all\par
round; so we did it."\par
\par
"When, where, how?" asked Jo, in a fever of feminine interest and\par
curiosity, for she could not realize it a particle.\par
\par
"Six weeks ago, at the American consul's, in Paris; a very quiet\par
wedding, of course, for even in our happiness we didn't forget dear\par
little Beth."\par
\par
Jo put her hand in his as he said that, and Laurie gently smoothed the\par
little red pillow, which he remembered well.\par
\par
"Why didn't you let us know afterward?" asked Jo, in a quieter tone,\par
when they had sat quite still a minute.\par
\par
"We wanted to surprise you; we thought we were coming directly home, at\par
first; but the dear old gentleman, as soon as we were married, found he\par
couldn't be ready under a month, at least, and sent us off to spend our\par
honeymoon wherever we liked. Amy had once called Valrosa a regular\par
honeymoon home, so we went there, and were as happy as people are but\par
once in their lives. My faith! wasn't it love among the roses!"\par
\par
Laurie seemed to forget Jo for a minute, and Jo was glad of it; for the\par
fact that he told her these things so freely and naturally assured her\par
that he had quite forgiven and forgotten. She tried to draw away her\par
hand; but, as if he guessed the thought that prompted the\par
half-involuntary impulse, Laurie held it fast, and said, with a manly\par
gravity she had never seen in him before,--\par
\par
"Jo, dear, I want to say one thing, and then we'll put it by forever. As\par
I told you in my letter, when I wrote that Amy had been so kind to me, I\par
never shall stop loving you; but the love is altered, and I have learned\par
to see that it is better as it is. Amy and you change places in my\par
heart, that's all. I think it was meant to be so, and would have come\par
about naturally, if I had waited, as you tried to make me; but I never\par
could be patient, and so I got a heartache. I was a boy then, headstrong\par
and violent; and it took a hard lesson to show me my mistake. For it\par
_was_ one, Jo, as you said, and I found it out, after making a fool of\par
myself. Upon my word, I was so tumbled up in my mind, at one time, that\par
I didn't know which I loved best, you or Amy, and tried to love both\par
alike; but I couldn't, and when I saw her in Switzerland, everything\par
seemed to clear up all at once. You both got into your right places, and\par
I felt sure that it was well off with the old love before it was on with\par
the new; that I could honestly share my heart between sister Jo and wife\par
Amy, and love them both dearly. Will you believe it, and go back to the\par
happy old times when we first knew one another?"\par
\par
"I'll believe it, with all my heart; but, Teddy, we never can be boy and\par
girl again: the happy old times can't come back, and we mustn't expect\par
it. We are man and woman now, with sober work to do, for playtime is\par
over, and we must give up frolicking. I'm sure you feel this; I see the\par
change in you, and you'll find it in me. I shall miss my boy, but I\par
shall love the man as much, and admire him more, because he means to be\par
what I hoped he would. We can't be little playmates any longer, but we\par
will be brother and sister, to love and help one another all our lives,\par
won't we, Laurie?"\par
\par
He did not say a word, but took the hand she offered him, and laid his\par
face down on it for a minute, feeling that out of the grave of a boyish\par
passion, there had risen a beautiful, strong friendship to bless them\par
both. Presently Jo said cheerfully, for she didn't want the coming home\par
to be a sad one,--\par
\par
"I can't make it true that you children are really married, and going to\par
set up housekeeping. Why, it seems only yesterday that I was buttoning\par
Amy's pinafore, and pulling your hair when you teased. Mercy me, how\par
time does fly!"\par
\par
"As one of the children is older than yourself, you needn't talk so like\par
a grandma. I flatter myself I'm a 'gentleman growed,' as Peggotty said\par
of David; and when you see Amy, you'll find her rather a precocious\par
infant," said Laurie, looking amused at her maternal air.\par
\par
"You may be a little older in years, but I'm ever so much older in\par
feeling, Teddy. Women always are; and this last year has been such a\par
hard one that I feel forty."\par
\par
"Poor Jo! we left you to bear it alone, while we went pleasuring. You\par
_are_ older; here's a line, and there's another; unless you smile, your\par
eyes look sad, and when I touched the cushion, just now, I found a tear\par
on it. You've had a great deal to bear, and had to bear it all alone.\par
What a selfish beast I've been!" and Laurie pulled his own hair, with a\par
remorseful look.\par
\par
But Jo only turned over the traitorous pillow, and answered, in a tone\par
which she tried to make quite cheerful,--\par
\par
"No, I had father and mother to help me, the dear babies to comfort me,\par
and the thought that you and Amy were safe and happy, to make the\par
troubles here easier to bear. I _am_ lonely, sometimes, but I dare say\par
it's good for me, and--"\par
\par
"You never shall be again," broke in Laurie, putting his arm about her,\par
as if to fence out every human ill. "Amy and I can't get on without\par
you, so you must come and teach 'the children' to keep house, and go\par
halves in everything, just as we used to do, and let us pet you, and all\par
be blissfully happy and friendly together."\par
\par
"If I shouldn't be in the way, it would be very pleasant. I begin to\par
feel quite young already; for, somehow, all my troubles seemed to fly\par
away when you came. You always were a comfort, Teddy;" and Jo leaned her\par
head on his shoulder, just as she did years ago, when Beth lay ill, and\par
Laurie told her to hold on to him.\par
\par
He looked down at her, wondering if she remembered the time, but Jo was\par
smiling to herself, as if, in truth, her troubles _had_ all vanished at\par
his coming.\par
\par
"You are the same Jo still, dropping tears about one minute, and\par
laughing the next. You look a little wicked now; what is it, grandma?"\par
\par
"I was wondering how you and Amy get on together."\par
\par
"Like angels!"\par
\par
"Yes, of course, at first; but which rules?"\par
\par
"I don't mind telling you that she does, now; at least I let her think\par
so,--it pleases her, you know. By and by we shall take turns, for\par
marriage, they say, halves one's rights and doubles one's duties."\par
\par
"You'll go on as you begin, and Amy will rule you all the days of your\par
life."\par
\par
"Well, she does it so imperceptibly that I don't think I shall mind\par
much. She is the sort of woman who knows how to rule well; in fact, I\par
rather like it, for she winds one round her finger as softly and\par
prettily as a skein of silk, and makes you feel as if she was doing you\par
a favor all the while."\par
\par
"That ever I should live to see you a henpecked husband and enjoying\par
it!" cried Jo, with uplifted hands.\par
\par
It was good to see Laurie square his shoulders, and smile with masculine\par
scorn at that insinuation, as he replied, with his "high and mighty"\par
air,--\par
\par
"Amy is too well-bred for that, and I am not the sort of man to submit\par
to it. My wife and I respect ourselves and one another too much ever to\par
tyrannize or quarrel."\par
\par
Jo liked that, and thought the new dignity very becoming, but the boy\par
seemed changing very fast into the man, and regret mingled with her\par
pleasure.\par
\par
"I am sure of that; Amy and you never did quarrel as we used to. She is\par
the sun and I the wind, in the fable, and the sun managed the man best,\par
you remember."\par
\par
"She can blow him up as well as shine on him," laughed Laurie. "Such a\par
lecture as I got at Nice! I give you my word it was a deal worse than\par
any of your scoldings,--a regular rouser. I'll tell you all about it\par
sometime,--_she_ never will, because, after telling me that she despised\par
and was ashamed of me, she lost her heart to the despicable party and\par
married the good-for-nothing."\par
\par
"What baseness! Well, if she abuses you, come to me, and I'll defend\par
you."\par
\par
"I look as if I needed it, don't I?" said Laurie, getting up and\par
striking an attitude which suddenly changed from the imposing to the\par
rapturous, as Amy's voice was heard calling,--\par
\par
"Where is she? Where's my dear old Jo?"\par
\par
In trooped the whole family, and every one was hugged and kissed all\par
over again, and, after several vain attempts, the three wanderers were\par
set down to be looked at and exulted over. Mr. Laurence, hale and hearty\par
as ever, was quite as much improved as the others by his foreign tour,\par
for the crustiness seemed to be nearly gone, and the old-fashioned\par
courtliness had received a polish which made it kindlier than ever. It\par
was good to see him beam at "my children," as he called the young pair;\par
it was better still to see Amy pay him the daughterly duty and affection\par
which completely won his old heart; and best of all, to watch Laurie\par
revolve about the two, as if never tired of enjoying the pretty picture\par
they made.\par
\par
The minute she put her eyes upon Amy, Meg became conscious that her own\par
dress hadn't a Parisian air, that young Mrs. Moffat would be entirely\par
eclipsed by young Mrs. Laurence, and that "her ladyship" was altogether\par
a most elegant and graceful woman. Jo thought, as she watched the pair,\par
"How well they look together! I was right, and Laurie has found the\par
beautiful, accomplished girl who will become his home better than clumsy\par
old Jo, and be a pride, not a torment to him." Mrs. March and her\par
husband smiled and nodded at each other with happy faces, for they saw\par
that their youngest had done well, not only in worldly things, but the\par
better wealth of love, confidence, and happiness.\par
\par
For Amy's face was full of the soft brightness which betokens a peaceful\par
heart, her voice had a new tenderness in it, and the cool, prim carriage\par
was changed to a gentle dignity, both womanly and winning. No little\par
affectations marred it, and the cordial sweetness of her manner was more\par
charming than the new beauty or the old grace, for it stamped her at\par
once with the unmistakable sign of the true gentlewoman she had hoped to\par
become.\par
\par
"Love has done much for our little girl," said her mother softly.\par
\par
"She has had a good example before her all her life, my dear," Mr. March\par
whispered back, with a loving look at the worn face and gray head beside\par
him.\par
\par
Daisy found it impossible to keep her eyes off her "pitty aunty," but\par
attached herself like a lap-dog to the wonderful châtelaine full of\par
delightful charms. Demi paused to consider the new relationship before\par
he compromised himself by the rash acceptance of a bribe, which took the\par
tempting form of a family of wooden bears from Berne. A flank movement\par
produced an unconditional surrender, however, for Laurie knew where to\par
have him.\par
\par
"Young man, when I first had the honor of making your acquaintance you\par
hit me in the face: now I demand the satisfaction of a gentleman;" and\par
with that the tall uncle proceeded to toss and tousle the small nephew\par
in a way that damaged his philosophical dignity as much as it delighted\par
his boyish soul.\par
\par
  [Illustration: The tall uncle proceeded to toss and tousle the small\par
                                nephew]\par
\par
"Blest if she ain't in silk from head to foot? Ain't it a relishin'\par
sight to see her settin' there as fine as a fiddle, and hear folks\par
calling little Amy, Mis. Laurence?" muttered old Hannah, who could not\par
resist frequent "peeks" through the slide as she set the table in a most\par
decidedly promiscuous manner.\par
\par
Mercy on us, how they did talk! first one, then the other, then all\par
burst out together, trying to tell the history of three years in half an\par
hour. It was fortunate that tea was at hand, to produce a lull and\par
provide refreshment, for they would have been hoarse and faint if they\par
had gone on much longer. Such a happy procession as filed away into the\par
little dining-room! Mr. March proudly escorted "Mrs. Laurence;" Mrs.\par
March as proudly leaned on the arm of "my son;" the old gentleman took\par
Jo, with a whispered "You must be my girl now," and a glance at the\par
empty corner by the fire, that made Jo whisper back, with trembling\par
lips, "I'll try to fill her place, sir."\par
\par
The twins pranced behind, feeling that the millennium was at hand, for\par
every one was so busy with the new-comers that they were left to revel\par
at their own sweet will, and you may be sure they made the most of the\par
opportunity. Didn't they steal sips of tea, stuff gingerbread _ab\par
libitum_, get a hot biscuit apiece, and, as a crowning trespass, didn't\par
they each whisk a captivating little tart into their tiny pockets, there\par
to stick and crumble treacherously, teaching them that both human nature\par
and pastry are frail? Burdened with the guilty consciousness of the\par
sequestered tarts, and fearing that Dodo's sharp eyes would pierce the\par
thin disguise of cambric and merino which hid their booty, the little\par
sinners attached themselves to "Dranpa," who hadn't his spectacles on.\par
Amy, who was handed about like refreshments, returned to the parlor on\par
Father Laurence's arm; the others paired off as before, and this\par
arrangement left Jo companionless. She did not mind it at the minute,\par
for she lingered to answer Hannah's eager inquiry,--\par
\par
"Will Miss Amy ride in her coop (_coupé_), and use all them lovely\par
silver dishes that's stored away over yander?"\par
\par
"Shouldn't wonder if she drove six white horses, ate off gold plate, and\par
wore diamonds and point-lace every day. Teddy thinks nothing too good\par
for her," returned Jo with infinite satisfaction.\par
\par
"No more there is! Will you have hash or fish-balls for breakfast?"\par
asked Hannah, who wisely mingled poetry and prose.\par
\par
"I don't care;" and Jo shut the door, feeling that food was an\par
uncongenial topic just then. She stood a minute looking at the party\par
vanishing above, and, as Demi's short plaid legs toiled up the last\par
stair, a sudden sense of loneliness came over her so strongly that she\par
looked about her with dim eyes, as if to find something to lean upon,\par
for even Teddy had deserted her. If she had known what birthday gift was\par
coming every minute nearer and nearer, she would not have said to\par
herself, "I'll weep a little weep when I go to bed; it won't do to be\par
dismal now." Then she drew her hand over her eyes,--for one of her\par
boyish habits was never to know where her handkerchief was,--and had\par
just managed to call up a smile when there came a knock at the\par
porch-door.\par
\par
She opened it with hospitable haste, and started as if another ghost had\par
come to surprise her; for there stood a tall, bearded gentleman, beaming\par
on her from the darkness like a midnight sun.\par
\par
"O Mr. Bhaer, I _am_ so glad to see you!" cried Jo, with a clutch, as if\par
she feared the night would swallow him up before she could get him in.\par
\par
          [Illustration: O Mr. Bhaer, I am so glad to see you]\par
\par
"And I to see Miss Marsch,--but no, you haf a party--" and the Professor\par
paused as the sound of voices and the tap of dancing feet came down to\par
them.\par
\par
"No, we haven't, only the family. My sister and friends have just come\par
home, and we are all very happy. Come in, and make one of us."\par
\par
Though a very social man, I think Mr. Bhaer would have gone decorously\par
away, and come again another day; but how could he, when Jo shut the\par
door behind him, and bereft him of his hat? Perhaps her face had\par
something to do with it, for she forgot to hide her joy at seeing him,\par
and showed it with a frankness that proved irresistible to the solitary\par
man, whose welcome far exceeded his boldest hopes.\par
\par
"If I shall not be Monsieur de Trop, I will so gladly see them all. You\par
haf been ill, my friend?"\par
\par
He put the question abruptly, for, as Jo hung up his coat, the light\par
fell on her face, and he saw a change in it.\par
\par
"Not ill, but tired and sorrowful. We have had trouble since I saw you\par
last."\par
\par
"Ah, yes, I know. My heart was sore for you when I heard that;" and he\par
shook hands again, with such a sympathetic face that Jo felt as if no\par
comfort could equal the look of the kind eyes, the grasp of the big,\par
warm hand.\par
\par
"Father, mother, this is my friend, Professor Bhaer," she said, with a\par
face and tone of such irrepressible pride and pleasure that she might as\par
well have blown a trumpet and opened the door with a flourish.\par
\par
If the stranger had had any doubts about his reception, they were set at\par
rest in a minute by the cordial welcome he received. Every one greeted\par
him kindly, for Jo's sake at first, but very soon they liked him for his\par
own. They could not help it, for he carried the talisman that opens all\par
hearts, and these simple people warmed to him at once, feeling even the\par
more friendly because he was poor; for poverty enriches those who live\par
above it, and is a sure passport to truly hospitable spirits. Mr. Bhaer\par
sat looking about him with the air of a traveller who knocks at a\par
strange door, and, when it opens, finds himself at home. The children\par
went to him like bees to a honey-pot; and, establishing themselves on\par
each knee, proceeded to captivate him by rifling his pockets, pulling\par
his beard, and investigating his watch, with juvenile audacity. The\par
women telegraphed their approval to one another, and Mr. March, feeling\par
that he had got a kindred spirit, opened his choicest stores for his\par
guest's benefit, while silent John listened and enjoyed the talk, but\par
said not a word, and Mr. Laurence found it impossible to go to sleep.\par
\par
If Jo had not been otherwise engaged, Laurie's behavior would have\par
amused her; for a faint twinge, not of jealousy, but something like\par
suspicion, caused that gentleman to stand aloof at first, and observe\par
the new-comer with brotherly circumspection. But it did not last long.\par
He got interested in spite of himself, and, before he knew it, was drawn\par
into the circle; for Mr. Bhaer talked well in this genial atmosphere,\par
and did himself justice. He seldom spoke to Laurie, but he looked at him\par
often, and a shadow would pass across his face, as if regretting his own\par
lost youth, as he watched the young man in his prime. Then his eye would\par
turn to Jo so wistfully that she would have surely answered the mute\par
inquiry if she had seen it; but Jo had her own eyes to take care of,\par
and, feeling that they could not be trusted, she prudently kept them on\par
the little sock she was knitting, like a model maiden aunt.\par
\par
A stealthy glance now and then refreshed her like sips of fresh water\par
after a dusty walk, for the sidelong peeps showed her several propitious\par
omens. Mr. Bhaer's face had lost the absent-minded expression, and\par
looked all alive with interest in the present moment, actually young and\par
handsome, she thought, forgetting to compare him with Laurie, as she\par
usually did strange men, to their great detriment. Then he seemed quite\par
inspired, though the burial customs of the ancients, to which the\par
conversation had strayed, might not be considered an exhilarating topic.\par
Jo quite glowed with triumph when Teddy got quenched in an argument, and\par
thought to herself, as she watched her father's absorbed face, "How he\par
would enjoy having such a man as my Professor to talk with every day!"\par
Lastly, Mr. Bhaer was dressed in a new suit of black, which made him\par
look more like a gentleman than ever. His bushy hair had been cut and\par
smoothly brushed, but didn't stay in order long, for, in exciting\par
moments, he rumpled it up in the droll way he used to do; and Jo liked\par
it rampantly erect better than flat, because she thought it gave his\par
fine forehead a Jove-like aspect. Poor Jo, how she did glorify that\par
plain man, as she sat knitting away so quietly, yet letting nothing\par
escape her, not even the fact that Mr. Bhaer actually had gold\par
sleeve-buttons in his immaculate wristbands!\par
\par
"Dear old fellow! He couldn't have got himself up with more care if he'd\par
been going a-wooing," said Jo to herself; and then a sudden thought,\par
born of the words, made her blush so dreadfully that she had to drop her\par
ball, and go down after it to hide her face.\par
\par
The manœuvre did not succeed as well as she expected, however; for,\par
though just in the act of setting fire to a funeral-pile, the Professor\par
dropped his torch, metaphorically speaking, and made a dive after the\par
little blue ball. Of course they bumped their heads smartly together,\par
saw stars, and both came up flushed and laughing, without the ball, to\par
resume their seats, wishing they had not left them.\par
\par
Nobody knew where the evening went to; for Hannah skilfully abstracted\par
the babies at an early hour, nodding like two rosy poppies, and Mr.\par
Laurence went home to rest. The others sat round the fire, talking\par
away, utterly regardless of the lapse of time, till Meg, whose maternal\par
mind was impressed with a firm conviction that Daisy had tumbled out of\par
bed, and Demi set his night-gown afire studying the structure of\par
matches, made a move to go.\par
\par
"We must have our sing, in the good old way, for we are all together\par
again once more," said Jo, feeling that a good shout would be a safe and\par
pleasant vent for the jubilant emotions of her soul.\par
\par
They were not _all_ there. But no one found the words thoughtless or\par
untrue; for Beth still seemed among them, a peaceful presence,\par
invisible, but dearer than ever, since death could not break the\par
household league that love made indissoluble. The little chair stood in\par
its old place; the tidy basket, with the bit of work she left unfinished\par
when the needle grew "so heavy," was still on its accustomed shelf; the\par
beloved instrument, seldom touched now, had not been moved; and above it\par
Beth's face, serene and smiling, as in the early days, looked down upon\par
them, seeming to say, "Be happy. I am here."\par
\par
"Play something, Amy. Let them hear how much you have improved," said\par
Laurie, with pardonable pride in his promising pupil.\par
\par
But Amy whispered, with full eyes, as she twirled the faded stool,--\par
\par
"Not to-night, dear. I can't show off to-night."\par
\par
But she did show something better than brilliancy or skill; for she sung\par
Beth's songs with a tender music in her voice which the best master\par
could not have taught, and touched the listeners' hearts with a sweeter\par
power than any other inspiration could have given her. The room was very\par
still, when the clear voice failed suddenly at the last line of Beth's\par
favorite hymn. It was hard to say,--\par
\par
        "Earth hath no sorrow that heaven cannot heal;"\par
\par
and Amy leaned against her husband, who stood behind her, feeling that\par
her welcome home was not quite perfect without Beth's kiss.\par
\par
"Now, we must finish with Mignon's song; for Mr. Bhaer sings that," said\par
Jo, before the pause grew painful. And Mr. Bhaer cleared his throat with\par
a gratified "Hem!" as he stepped into the corner where Jo stood,\par
saying,--\par
\par
"You will sing with me? We go excellently well together."\par
\par
A pleasing fiction, by the way; for Jo had no more idea of music than a\par
grasshopper. But she would have consented if he had proposed to sing a\par
whole opera, and warbled away, blissfully regardless of time and tune.\par
It didn't much matter; for Mr. Bhaer sang like a true German, heartily\par
and well; and Jo soon subsided into a subdued hum, that she might listen\par
to the mellow voice that seemed to sing for her alone.\par
\par
                [Illustration: Mr. Bhaer sang heartily]\par
\par
        "Know'st thou the land where the citron blooms,"\par
\par
used to be the Professor's favorite line, for "das land" meant Germany\par
to him; but now he seemed to dwell, with peculiar warmth and melody,\par
upon the words,--\par
\par
        "There, oh there, might I with thee,\par
         O my beloved, go!"\par
\par
and one listener was so thrilled by the tender invitation that she\par
longed to say she did know the land, and would joyfully depart thither\par
whenever he liked.\par
\par
The song was considered a great success, and the singer retired covered\par
with laurels. But a few minutes afterward, he forgot his manners\par
entirely, and stared at Amy putting on her bonnet; for she had been\par
introduced simply as "my sister," and no one had called her by her new\par
name since he came. He forgot himself still further when Laurie said, in\par
his most gracious manner, at parting,--\par
\par
"My wife and I are very glad to meet you, sir. Please remember that\par
there is always a welcome waiting for you over the way."\par
\par
Then the Professor thanked him so heartily, and looked so suddenly\par
illuminated with satisfaction, that Laurie thought him the most\par
delightfully demonstrative old fellow he ever met.\par
\par
"I too shall go; but I shall gladly come again, if you will gif me\par
leave, dear madame, for a little business in the city will keep me here\par
some days."\par
\par
He spoke to Mrs. March, but he looked at Jo; and the mother's voice gave\par
as cordial an assent as did the daughter's eyes; for Mrs. March was not\par
so blind to her children's interest as Mrs. Moffat supposed.\par
\par
"I suspect that is a wise man," remarked Mr. March, with placid\par
satisfaction, from the hearth-rug, after the last guest had gone.\par
\par
"I know he is a good one," added Mrs. March, with decided approval, as\par
she wound up the clock.\par
\par
"I thought you'd like him," was all Jo said, as she slipped away to her\par
bed.\par
\par
She wondered what the business was that brought Mr. Bhaer to the city,\par
and finally decided that he had been appointed to some great honor,\par
somewhere, but had been too modest to mention the fact. If she had seen\par
his face when, safe in his own room, he looked at the picture of a\par
severe and rigid young lady, with a good deal of hair, who appeared to\par
be gazing darkly into futurity, it might have thrown some light upon the\par
subject, especially when he turned off the gas, and kissed the picture\par
in the dark.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
       [Illustration: Mrs. Laurence sitting in her mother's lap]\par
\par
                                 XLIV.\par
\par
                           MY LORD AND LADY.\par
\par
\par
"Please, Madam Mother, could you lend me my wife for half an hour? The\par
luggage has come, and I've been making hay of Amy's Paris finery, trying\par
to find some things I want," said Laurie, coming in the next day to find\par
Mrs. Laurence sitting in her mother's lap, as if being made "the baby"\par
again.\par
\par
"Certainly. Go, dear; I forget that you have any home but this," and\par
Mrs. March pressed the white hand that wore the wedding-ring, as if\par
asking pardon for her maternal covetousness.\par
\par
"I shouldn't have come over if I could have helped it; but I can't get\par
on without my little woman any more than a--"\par
\par
"Weathercock can without wind," suggested Jo, as he paused for a simile;\par
Jo had grown quite her own saucy self again since Teddy came home.\par
\par
"Exactly; for Amy keeps me pointing due west most of the time, with only\par
an occasional whiffle round to the south, and I haven't had an easterly\par
spell since I was married; don't know anything about the north, but am\par
altogether salubrious and balmy, hey, my lady?"\par
\par
"Lovely weather so far; I don't know how long it will last, but I'm not\par
afraid of storms, for I'm learning how to sail my ship. Come home, dear,\par
and I'll find your bootjack; I suppose that's what you are rummaging\par
after among my things. Men are _so_ helpless, mother," said Amy, with a\par
matronly air, which delighted her husband.\par
\par
"What are you going to do with yourselves after you get settled?" asked\par
Jo, buttoning Amy's cloak as she used to button her pinafores.\par
\par
"We have our plans; we don't mean to say much about them yet, because we\par
are such very new brooms, but we don't intend to be idle. I'm going into\par
business with a devotion that shall delight grandfather, and prove to\par
him that I'm not spoilt. I need something of the sort to keep me steady.\par
I'm tired of dawdling, and mean to work like a man."\par
\par
"And Amy, what is she going to do?" asked Mrs. March, well pleased at\par
Laurie's decision, and the energy with which he spoke.\par
\par
"After doing the civil all round, and airing our best bonnet, we shall\par
astonish you by the elegant hospitalities of our mansion, the brilliant\par
society we shall draw about us, and the beneficial influence we shall\par
exert over the world at large. That's about it, isn't it, Madame\par
Récamier?" asked Laurie, with a quizzical look at Amy.\par
\par
"Time will show. Come away, Impertinence, and don't shock my family by\par
calling me names before their faces," answered Amy, resolving that there\par
should be a home with a good wife in it before she set up a _salon_ as a\par
queen of society.\par
\par
"How happy those children seem together!" observed Mr. March, finding it\par
difficult to become absorbed in his Aristotle after the young couple had\par
gone.\par
\par
"Yes, and I think it will last," added Mrs. March, with the restful\par
expression of a pilot who has brought a ship safely into port.\par
\par
"I know it will. Happy Amy!" and Jo sighed, then smiled brightly as\par
Professor Bhaer opened the gate with an impatient push.\par
\par
Later in the evening, when his mind had been set at rest about the\par
bootjack, Laurie said suddenly to his wife, who was flitting about,\par
arranging her new art treasures,--\par
\par
"Mrs. Laurence."\par
\par
"My lord!"\par
\par
"That man intends to marry our Jo!"\par
\par
"I hope so; don't you, dear?"\par
\par
"Well, my love, I consider him a trump, in the fullest sense of that\par
expressive word, but I do wish he was a little younger and a good deal\par
richer."\par
\par
"Now, Laurie, don't be too fastidious and worldly-minded. If they love\par
one another it doesn't matter a particle how old they are nor how poor.\par
Women _never_ should marry for money--" Amy caught herself up short as\par
the words escaped her, and looked at her husband, who replied, with\par
malicious gravity,--\par
\par
"Certainly not, though you do hear charming girls say that they intend\par
to do it sometimes. If my memory serves me, you once thought it your\par
duty to make a rich match; that accounts, perhaps, for your marrying a\par
good-for-nothing like me."\par
\par
"O my dearest boy, don't, don't say that! I forgot you were rich when I\par
said 'Yes.' I'd have married you if you hadn't a penny, and I sometimes\par
wish you _were_ poor that I might show how much I love you;" and Amy,\par
who was very dignified in public and very fond in private, gave\par
convincing proofs of the truth of her words.\par
\par
"You don't really think I am such a mercenary creature as I tried to be\par
once, do you? It would break my heart if you didn't believe that I'd\par
gladly pull in the same boat with you, even if you had to get your\par
living by rowing on the lake."\par
\par
"Am I an idiot and a brute? How could I think so, when you refused a\par
richer man for me, and won't let me give you half I want to now, when I\par
have the right? Girls do it every day, poor things, and are taught to\par
think it is their only salvation; but you had better lessons, and,\par
though I trembled for you at one time, I was not disappointed, for the\par
daughter was true to the mother's teaching. I told mamma so yesterday,\par
and she looked as glad and grateful as if I'd given her a check for a\par
million, to be spent in charity. You are not listening to my moral\par
remarks, Mrs. Laurence;" and Laurie paused, for Amy's eyes had an absent\par
look, though fixed upon his face.\par
\par
"Yes, I am, and admiring the dimple in your chin at the same time. I\par
don't wish to make you vain, but I must confess that I'm prouder of my\par
handsome husband than of all his money. Don't laugh, but your nose is\par
_such_ a comfort to me;" and Amy softly caressed the well-cut feature\par
with artistic satisfaction.\par
\par
Laurie had received many compliments in his life, but never one that\par
suited him better, as he plainly showed, though he did laugh at his\par
wife's peculiar taste, while she said slowly,--\par
\par
"May I ask you a question, dear?"\par
\par
"Of course you may."\par
\par
"Shall you care if Jo does marry Mr. Bhaer?"\par
\par
"Oh, that's the trouble, is it? I thought there was something in the\par
dimple that didn't suit you. Not being a dog in the manger, but the\par
happiest fellow alive, I assure you I can dance at Jo's wedding with a\par
heart as light as my heels. Do you doubt it, my darling?"\par
\par
Amy looked up at him, and was satisfied; her last little jealous fear\par
vanished forever, and she thanked him, with a face full of love and\par
confidence.\par
\par
"I wish we could do something for that capital old Professor. Couldn't\par
we invent a rich relation, who shall obligingly die out there in\par
Germany, and leave him a tidy little fortune?" said Laurie, when they\par
began to pace up and down the long drawing-room, arm-in-arm, as they\par
were fond of doing, in memory of the chateau garden.\par
\par
             [Illustration: They began to pace up and down]\par
\par
"Jo would find us out, and spoil it all; she is very proud of him, just\par
as he is, and said yesterday that she thought poverty was a beautiful\par
thing."\par
\par
"Bless her dear heart! she won't think so when she has a literary\par
husband, and a dozen little professors and professorins to support. We\par
won't interfere now, but watch our chance, and do them a good turn in\par
spite of themselves. I owe Jo for a part of my education, and she\par
believes in people's paying their honest debts, so I'll get round her in\par
that way."\par
\par
"How delightful it is to be able to help others, isn't it? That was\par
always one of my dreams, to have the power of giving freely; and, thanks\par
to you, the dream has come true."\par
\par
"Ah! we'll do quantities of good, won't we? There's one sort of poverty\par
that I particularly like to help. Out-and-out beggars get taken care of,\par
but poor gentlefolks fare badly, because they won't ask, and people\par
don't dare to offer charity; yet there are a thousand ways of helping\par
them, if one only knows how to do it so delicately that it does not\par
offend. I must say, I like to serve a decayed gentleman better than a\par
blarneying beggar; I suppose it's wrong, but I do, though it is\par
harder."\par
\par
"Because it takes a gentleman to do it," added the other member of the\par
domestic admiration society.\par
\par
"Thank you, I'm afraid I don't deserve that pretty compliment. But I was\par
going to say that while I was dawdling about abroad, I saw a good many\par
talented young fellows making all sorts of sacrifices, and enduring real\par
hardships, that they might realize their dreams. Splendid fellows, some\par
of them, working like heroes, poor and friendless, but so full of\par
courage, patience, and ambition, that I was ashamed of myself, and\par
longed to give them a right good lift. Those are people whom it's a\par
satisfaction to help, for if they've got genius, it's an honor to be\par
allowed to serve them, and not let it be lost or delayed for want of\par
fuel to keep the pot boiling; if they haven't, it's a pleasure to\par
comfort the poor souls, and keep them from despair when they find it\par
out."\par
\par
"Yes, indeed; and there's another class who can't ask, and who suffer in\par
silence. I know something of it, for I belonged to it before you made a\par
princess of me, as the king does the beggar-maid in the old story.\par
Ambitious girls have a hard time, Laurie, and often have to see youth,\par
health, and precious opportunities go by, just for want of a little help\par
at the right minute. People have been very kind to me; and whenever I\par
see girls struggling along, as we used to do, I want to put out my hand\par
and help them, as I was helped."\par
\par
"And so you shall, like an angel as you are!" cried Laurie, resolving,\par
with a glow of philanthropic zeal, to found and endow an institution for\par
the express benefit of young women with artistic tendencies. "Rich\par
people have no right to sit down and enjoy themselves, or let their\par
money accumulate for others to waste. It's not half so sensible to leave\par
legacies when one dies as it is to use the money wisely while alive, and\par
enjoy making one's fellow-creatures happy with it. We'll have a good\par
time ourselves, and add an extra relish to our own pleasure by giving\par
other people a generous taste. Will you be a little Dorcas, going about\par
emptying a big basket of comforts, and filling it up with good deeds?"\par
\par
"With all my heart, if you will be a brave St. Martin, stopping, as you\par
ride gallantly through the world, to share your cloak with the beggar."\par
\par
"It's a bargain, and we shall get the best of it!"\par
\par
So the young pair shook hands upon it, and then paced happily on again,\par
feeling that their pleasant home was more home-like because they hoped\par
to brighten other homes, believing that their own feet would walk more\par
uprightly along the flowery path before them, if they smoothed rough\par
ways for other feet, and feeling that their hearts were more closely\par
knit together by a love which could tenderly remember those less blest\par
than they.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                                  XLV.\par
\par
                            DAISY AND DEMI.\par
\par
\par
I cannot feel that I have done my duty as humble historian of the March\par
family, without devoting at least one chapter to the two most precious\par
and important members of it. Daisy and Demi had now arrived at years of\par
discretion; for in this fast age babies of three or four assert their\par
rights, and get them, too, which is more than many of their elders do.\par
If there ever were a pair of twins in danger of being utterly spoilt by\par
adoration, it was these prattling Brookes. Of course they were the most\par
remarkable children ever born, as will be shown when I mention that they\par
walked at eight months, talked fluently at twelve months, and at two\par
years they took their places at table, and behaved with a propriety\par
which charmed all beholders. At three, Daisy demanded a "needler," and\par
actually made a bag with four stitches in it; she likewise set up\par
housekeeping in the sideboard, and managed a microscopic cooking-stove\par
with a skill that brought tears of pride to Hannah's eyes, while Demi\par
learned his letters with his grandfather, who invented a new mode of\par
teaching the alphabet by forming the letters with his arms and legs,\par
thus uniting gymnastics for head and heels. The boy early developed a\par
mechanical genius which delighted his father and distracted his mother,\par
for he tried to imitate every machine he saw, and kept the nursery in a\par
chaotic condition, with his "sewin-sheen,"--a mysterious structure of\par
string, chairs, clothes-pins, and spools, for wheels to go "wound and\par
wound;" also a basket hung over the back of a big chair, in which he\par
vainly tried to hoist his too confiding sister, who, with feminine\par
devotion, allowed her little head to be bumped till rescued, when the\par
young inventor indignantly remarked, "Why, marmar, dat's my lellywaiter,\par
and me's trying to pull her up."\par
\par
Though utterly unlike in character, the twins got on remarkably well\par
together, and seldom quarrelled more than thrice a day. Of course, Demi\par
tyrannized over Daisy, and gallantly defended her from every other\par
aggressor; while Daisy made a galley-slave of herself, and adored her\par
brother as the one perfect being in the world. A rosy, chubby, sunshiny\par
little soul was Daisy, who found her way to everybody's heart, and\par
nestled there. One of the captivating children, who seem made to be\par
kissed and cuddled, adorned and adored like little goddesses, and\par
produced for general approval on all festive occasions. Her small\par
virtues were so sweet that she would have been quite angelic if a few\par
small naughtinesses had not kept her delightfully human. It was all fair\par
weather in her world, and every morning she scrambled up to the window\par
in her little night-gown to look out, and say, no matter whether it\par
rained or shone, "Oh, pitty day, oh, pitty day!" Every one was a friend,\par
and she offered kisses to a stranger so confidingly that the most\par
inveterate bachelor relented, and baby-lovers became faithful\par
worshippers.\par
\par
                   [Illustration: Me loves evvybody]\par
\par
"Me loves evvybody," she once said, opening her arms, with her spoon in\par
one hand, and her mug in the other, as if eager to embrace and nourish\par
the whole world.\par
\par
As she grew, her mother began to feel that the Dove-cote would be blest\par
by the presence of an inmate as serene and loving as that which had\par
helped to make the old house home, and to pray that she might be spared\par
a loss like that which had lately taught them how long they had\par
entertained an angel unawares. Her grandfather often called her "Beth,"\par
and her grandmother watched over her with untiring devotion, as if\par
trying to atone for some past mistake, which no eye but her own could\par
see.\par
\par
Demi, like a true Yankee, was of an inquiring turn, wanting to know\par
everything, and often getting much disturbed because he could not get\par
satisfactory answers to his perpetual "What for?"\par
\par
He also possessed a philosophic bent, to the great delight of his\par
grandfather, who used to hold Socratic conversations with him, in which\par
the precocious pupil occasionally posed his teacher, to the undisguised\par
satisfaction of the womenfolk.\par
\par
             [Illustration: What makes my legs go, dranpa?]\par
\par
"What makes my legs go, dranpa?" asked the young philosopher, surveying\par
those active portions of his frame with a meditative air, while resting\par
after a go-to-bed frolic one night.\par
\par
"It's your little mind, Demi," replied the sage, stroking the yellow\par
head respectfully.\par
\par
"What is a little mine?"\par
\par
"It is something which makes your body move, as the spring made the\par
wheels go in my watch when I showed it to you."\par
\par
"Open me; I want to see it go wound."\par
\par
"I can't do that any more than you could open the watch. God winds you\par
up, and you go till He stops you."\par
\par
"Does I?" and Demi's brown eyes grew big and bright as he took in the\par
new thought. "Is I wounded up like the watch?"\par
\par
"Yes; but I can't show you how; for it is done when we don't see."\par
\par
Demi felt of his back, as if expecting to find it like that of the\par
watch, and then gravely remarked,--\par
\par
"I dess Dod does it when I's asleep."\par
\par
A careful explanation followed, to which he listened so attentively that\par
his anxious grandmother said,--\par
\par
"My dear, do you think it wise to talk about such things to that baby?\par
He's getting great bumps over his eyes, and learning to ask the most\par
unanswerable questions."\par
\par
"If he is old enough to ask the questions he is old enough to receive\par
true answers. I am not putting the thoughts into his head, but helping\par
him unfold those already there. These children are wiser than we are,\par
and I have no doubt the boy understands every word I have said to him.\par
Now, Demi, tell me where you keep your mind?"\par
\par
If the boy had replied like Alcibiades, "By the gods, Socrates, I cannot\par
tell," his grandfather would not have been surprised; but when, after\par
standing a moment on one leg, like a meditative young stork, he\par
answered, in a tone of calm conviction, "In my little belly," the old\par
gentleman could only join in grandma's laugh, and dismiss the class in\par
metaphysics.\par
\par
There might have been cause for maternal anxiety, if Demi had not given\par
convincing proofs that he was a true boy, as well as a budding\par
philosopher; for, often, after a discussion which caused Hannah to\par
prophesy, with ominous nods, "That child ain't long for this world," he\par
would turn about and set her fears at rest by some of the pranks with\par
which dear, dirty, naughty little rascals distract and delight their\par
parents' souls.\par
\par
Meg made many moral rules, and tried to keep them; but what mother was\par
ever proof against the winning wiles, the ingenious evasions, or the\par
tranquil audacity of the miniature men and women who so early show\par
themselves accomplished Artful Dodgers?\par
\par
"No more raisins, Demi, they'll make you sick," says mamma to the young\par
person who offers his services in the kitchen with unfailing regularity\par
on plum-pudding day.\par
\par
"Me likes to be sick."\par
\par
"I don't want to have you, so run away and help Daisy make patty-cakes."\par
\par
He reluctantly departs, but his wrongs weigh upon his spirit; and, by\par
and by, when an opportunity comes to redress them, he outwits mamma by a\par
shrewd bargain.\par
\par
"Now you have been good children, and I'll play anything you like," says\par
Meg, as she leads her assistant cooks upstairs, when the pudding is\par
safely bouncing in the pot.\par
\par
"Truly, marmar?" asks Demi, with a brilliant idea in his well-powdered\par
head.\par
\par
"Yes, truly; anything you say," replies the short-sighted parent,\par
preparing herself to sing "The Three Little Kittens" half a dozen times\par
over, or to take her family to "Buy a penny bun," regardless of wind or\par
limb. But Demi corners her by the cool reply,--\par
\par
"Then we'll go and eat up all the raisins."\par
\par
Aunt Dodo was chief playmate and _confidante_ of both children, and the\par
trio turned the little house topsy-turvy. Aunt Amy was as yet only a\par
name to them, Aunt Beth soon faded into a pleasantly vague memory, but\par
Aunt Dodo was a living reality, and they made the most of her, for which\par
compliment she was deeply grateful. But when Mr. Bhaer came, Jo\par
neglected her playfellows, and dismay and desolation fell upon their\par
little souls. Daisy, who was fond of going about peddling kisses, lost\par
her best customer and became bankrupt; Demi, with infantile penetration,\par
soon discovered that Dodo liked to play with "the bear-man" better than\par
she did with him; but, though hurt, he concealed his anguish, for he\par
hadn't the heart to insult a rival who kept a mine of chocolate-drops in\par
his waistcoat-pocket, and a watch that could be taken out of its case\par
and freely shaken by ardent admirers.\par
\par
Some persons might have considered these pleasing liberties as bribes;\par
but Demi didn't see it in that light, and continued to patronize the\par
"bear-man" with pensive affability, while Daisy bestowed her small\par
affections upon him at the third call, and considered his shoulder her\par
throne, his arm her refuge, his gifts treasures of surpassing worth.\par
\par
Gentlemen are sometimes seized with sudden fits of admiration for the\par
young relatives of ladies whom they honor with their regard; but this\par
counterfeit philoprogenitiveness sits uneasily upon them, and does not\par
deceive anybody a particle. Mr. Bhaer's devotion was sincere, however\par
likewise effective,--for honesty is the best policy in love as in law;\par
he was one of the men who are at home with children, and looked\par
particularly well when little faces made a pleasant contrast with his\par
manly one. His business, whatever it was, detained him from day to day,\par
but evening seldom failed to bring him out to see--well, he always asked\par
for Mr. March, so I suppose _he_ was the attraction. The excellent papa\par
labored under the delusion that he was, and revelled in long discussions\par
with the kindred spirit, till a chance remark of his more observing\par
grandson suddenly enlightened him.\par
\par
Mr. Bhaer came in one evening to pause on the threshold of the study,\par
astonished by the spectacle that met his eye. Prone upon the floor lay\par
Mr. March, with his respectable legs in the air, and beside him,\par
likewise prone, was Demi, trying to imitate the attitude with his own\par
short, scarlet-stockinged legs, both grovellers so seriously absorbed\par
that they were unconscious of spectators, till Mr. Bhaer laughed his\par
sonorous laugh, and Jo cried out, with a scandalized face,--\par
\par
"Father, father, here's the Professor!"\par
\par
Down went the black legs and up came the gray head, as the preceptor\par
said, with undisturbed dignity,--\par
\par
"Good evening, Mr. Bhaer. Excuse me for a moment; we are just finishing\par
our lesson. Now, Demi, make the letter and tell its name."\par
\par
"I knows him!" and, after a few convulsive efforts, the red legs took\par
the shape of a pair of compasses, and the intelligent pupil triumphantly\par
shouted, "It's a We, dranpa, it's a We!"\par
\par
                   [Illustration: Dranpa, it's a We]\par
\par
"He's a born Weller," laughed Jo, as her parent gathered himself up, and\par
her nephew tried to stand on his head, as the only mode of expressing\par
his satisfaction that school was over.\par
\par
"What have you been at to-day, bübchen?" asked Mr. Bhaer, picking up the\par
gymnast.\par
\par
"Me went to see little Mary."\par
\par
"And what did you there?"\par
\par
"I kissed her," began Demi, with artless frankness.\par
\par
"Prut! thou beginnest early. What did the little Mary say to that?"\par
asked Mr. Bhaer, continuing to confess the young sinner, who stood upon\par
his knee, exploring the waistcoat-pocket.\par
\par
"Oh, she liked it, and she kissed me, and I liked it. _Don't_ little\par
boys like little girls?" added Demi, with his mouth full, and an air of\par
bland satisfaction.\par
\par
"You precocious chick! Who put that into your head?" said Jo, enjoying\par
the innocent revelations as much as the Professor.\par
\par
"'Tisn't in mine head; it's in mine mouf," answered literal Demi,\par
putting out his tongue, with a chocolate-drop on it, thinking she\par
alluded to confectionery, not ideas.\par
\par
"Thou shouldst save some for the little friend: sweets to the sweet,\par
mannling;" and Mr. Bhaer offered Jo some, with a look that made her\par
wonder if chocolate was not the nectar drunk by the gods. Demi also saw\par
the smile, was impressed by it, and artlessly inquired,--\par
\par
"Do great boys like great girls, too, 'Fessor?"\par
\par
Like young Washington, Mr. Bhaer "couldn't tell a lie;" so he gave the\par
somewhat vague reply that he believed they did sometimes, in a tone that\par
made Mr. March put down his clothes-brush, glance at Jo's retiring face,\par
and then sink into his chair, looking as if the "precocious chick" had\par
put an idea into _his_ head that was both sweet and sour.\par
\par
Why Dodo, when she caught him in the china-closet half an hour\par
afterward, nearly squeezed the breath out of his little body with a\par
tender embrace, instead of shaking him for being there, and why she\par
followed up this novel performance by the unexpected gift of a big slice\par
of bread and jelly, remained one of the problems over which Demi puzzled\par
his small wits, and was forced to leave unsolved forever.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
       [Illustration: Mr. Bhaer and Jo were enjoying promenades]\par
\par
                                 XLVI.\par
\par
                          UNDER THE UMBRELLA.\par
\par
\par
While Laurie and Amy were taking conjugal strolls over velvet carpets,\par
as they set their house in order, and planned a blissful future, Mr.\par
Bhaer and Jo were enjoying promenades of a different sort, along muddy\par
roads and sodden fields.\par
\par
"I always do take a walk toward evening, and I don't know why I should\par
give it up, just because I often happen to meet the Professor on his way\par
out," said Jo to herself, after two or three encounters; for, though\par
there were two paths to Meg's, whichever one she took she was sure to\par
meet him, either going or returning. He was always walking rapidly, and\par
never seemed to see her till quite close, when he would look as if his\par
short-sighted eyes had failed to recognize the approaching lady till\par
that moment. Then, if she was going to Meg's, he always had something\par
for the babies; if her face was turned homeward, he had merely strolled\par
down to see the river, and was just about returning, unless they were\par
tired of his frequent calls.\par
\par
Under the circumstances, what could Jo do but greet him civilly, and\par
invite him in? If she _was_ tired of his visits, she concealed her\par
weariness with perfect skill, and took care that there should be coffee\par
for supper, "as Friedrich--I mean Mr. Bhaer--doesn't like tea."\par
\par
By the second week, every one knew perfectly well what was going on, yet\par
every one tried to look as if they were stone-blind to the changes in\par
Jo's face. They never asked why she sang about her work, did up her hair\par
three times a day, and got so blooming with her evening exercise; and no\par
one seemed to have the slightest suspicion that Professor Bhaer, while\par
talking philosophy with the father, was giving the daughter lessons in\par
love.\par
\par
Jo couldn't even lose her heart in a decorous manner, but sternly tried\par
to quench her feelings; and, failing to do so, led a somewhat agitated\par
life. She was mortally afraid of being laughed at for surrendering,\par
after her many and vehement declarations of independence. Laurie was her\par
especial dread; but, thanks to the new manager, he behaved with\par
praiseworthy propriety, never called Mr. Bhaer "a capital old fellow" in\par
public, never alluded, in the remotest manner, to Jo's improved\par
appearance, or expressed the least surprise at seeing the Professor's\par
hat on the Marches' hall-table nearly every evening. But he exulted in\par
private and longed for the time to come when he could give Jo a piece of\par
plate, with a bear and a ragged staff on it as an appropriate\par
coat-of-arms.\par
\par
For a fortnight, the Professor came and went with lover-like regularity;\par
then he stayed away for three whole days, and made no sign,--a\par
proceeding which caused everybody to look sober, and Jo to become\par
pensive, at first, and then--alas for romance!--very cross.\par
\par
"Disgusted, I dare say, and gone home as suddenly as he came. It's\par
nothing to me, of course; but I _should_ think he would have come and\par
bid us good-by, like a gentleman," she said to herself, with a\par
despairing look at the gate, as she put on her things for the customary\par
walk, one dull afternoon.\par
\par
"You'd better take the little umbrella, dear; it looks like rain," said\par
her mother, observing that she had on her new bonnet, but not alluding\par
to the fact.\par
\par
"Yes, Marmee; do you want anything in town? I've got to run in and get\par
some paper," returned Jo, pulling out the bow under her chin before the\par
glass as an excuse for not looking at her mother.\par
\par
"Yes; I want some twilled silesia, a paper of number nine needles, and\par
two yards of narrow lavender ribbon. Have you got your thick boots on,\par
and something warm under your cloak?"\par
\par
"I believe so," answered Jo absently.\par
\par
"If you happen to meet Mr. Bhaer, bring him home to tea. I quite long to\par
see the dear man," added Mrs. March.\par
\par
Jo heard _that_, but made no answer, except to kiss her mother, and walk\par
rapidly away, thinking with a glow of gratitude, in spite of her\par
heartache,--\par
\par
"How good she is to me! What _do_ girls do who haven't any mothers to\par
help them through their troubles?"\par
\par
The dry-goods stores were not down among the counting-houses, banks, and\par
wholesale warerooms, where gentlemen most do congregate; but Jo found\par
herself in that part of the city before she did a single errand,\par
loitering along as if waiting for some one, examining engineering\par
instruments in one window and samples of wool in another with most\par
unfeminine interest; tumbling over barrels, being half-smothered by\par
descending bales, and hustled unceremoniously by busy men who looked as\par
if they wondered "how the deuce she got there." A drop of rain on her\par
cheek recalled her thoughts from baffled hopes to ruined ribbons; for\par
the drops continued to fall, and, being a woman as well as a lover, she\par
felt that, though it was too late to save her heart, she might her\par
bonnet. Now she remembered the little umbrella, which she had forgotten\par
to take in her hurry to be off; but regret was unavailing, and nothing\par
could be done but borrow one or submit to a drenching. She looked up at\par
the lowering sky, down at the crimson bow already flecked with black,\par
forward along the muddy street, then one long, lingering look behind, at\par
a certain grimy warehouse, with "Hoffmann, Swartz, & Co." over the door,\par
and said to herself, with a sternly reproachful air,--\par
\par
"It serves me right! What business had I to put on all my best things\par
and come philandering down here, hoping to see the Professor? Jo, I'm\par
ashamed of you! No, you shall _not_ go there to borrow an umbrella, or\par
find out where he is, from his friends. You shall trudge away, and do\par
your errands in the rain; and if you catch your death and ruin your\par
bonnet, it's no more than you deserve. Now then!"\par
\par
With that she rushed across the street so impetuously that she narrowly\par
escaped annihilation from a passing truck, and precipitated herself into\par
the arms of a stately old gentleman, who said, "I beg pardon, ma'am,"\par
and looked mortally offended. Somewhat daunted, Jo righted herself,\par
spread her handkerchief over the devoted ribbons, and, putting\par
temptation behind her, hurried on, with increasing dampness about the\par
ankles, and much clashing of umbrellas overhead. The fact that a\par
somewhat dilapidated blue one remained stationary above the unprotected\par
bonnet, attracted her attention; and, looking up, she saw Mr. Bhaer\par
looking down.\par
\par
              [Illustration: Looking up she saw Mr. Bhaer]\par
\par
"I feel to know the strong-minded lady who goes so bravely under many\par
horse-noses, and so fast through much mud. What do you down here, my\par
friend?"\par
\par
"I'm shopping."\par
\par
Mr. Bhaer smiled, as he glanced from the pickle-factory on one side, to\par
the wholesale hide and leather concern on the other; but he only said\par
politely,--\par
\par
"You haf no umbrella. May I go also, and take for you the bundles?"\par
\par
"Yes, thank you."\par
\par
Jo's cheeks were as red as her ribbon, and she wondered what he thought\par
of her; but she didn't care, for in a minute she found herself walking\par
away arm-in-arm with her Professor, feeling as if the sun had suddenly\par
burst out with uncommon brilliancy, that the world was all right again,\par
and that one thoroughly happy woman was paddling through the wet that\par
day.\par
\par
"We thought you had gone," said Jo hastily, for she knew he was looking\par
at her. Her bonnet wasn't big enough to hide her face, and she feared he\par
might think the joy it betrayed unmaidenly.\par
\par
"Did you believe that I should go with no farewell to those who haf been\par
so heavenly kind to me?" he asked so reproachfully that she felt as if\par
she had insulted him by the suggestion, and answered heartily,--\par
\par
"No, _I_ didn't; I knew you were busy about your own affairs, but we\par
rather missed you,--father and mother especially."\par
\par
"And you?"\par
\par
"I'm always glad to see you, sir."\par
\par
In her anxiety to keep her voice quite calm, Jo made it rather cool, and\par
the frosty little monosyllable at the end seemed to chill the Professor,\par
for his smile vanished, as he said gravely,--\par
\par
"I thank you, and come one time more before I go."\par
\par
"You _are_ going, then?"\par
\par
"I haf no longer any business here; it is done."\par
\par
"Successfully, I hope?" said Jo, for the bitterness of disappointment\par
was in that short reply of his.\par
\par
"I ought to think so, for I haf a way opened to me by which I can make\par
my bread and gif my Jünglings much help."\par
\par
"Tell me, please! I like to know all about the--the boys," said Jo\par
eagerly.\par
\par
"That is so kind, I gladly tell you. My friends find for me a place in\par
a college, where I teach as at home, and earn enough to make the way\par
smooth for Franz and Emil. For this I should be grateful, should I not?"\par
\par
"Indeed you should. How splendid it will be to have you doing what you\par
like, and be able to see you often, and the boys!" cried Jo, clinging to\par
the lads as an excuse for the satisfaction she could not help betraying.\par
\par
"Ah! but we shall not meet often, I fear; this place is at the West."\par
\par
"So far away!" and Jo left her skirts to their fate, as if it didn't\par
matter now what became of her clothes or herself.\par
\par
Mr. Bhaer could read several languages, but he had not learned to read\par
women yet. He flattered himself that he knew Jo pretty well, and was,\par
therefore, much amazed by the contradictions of voice, face, and manner,\par
which she showed him in rapid succession that day, for she was in half a\par
dozen different moods in the course of half an hour. When she met him\par
she looked surprised, though it was impossible to help suspecting that\par
she had come for that express purpose. When he offered her his arm, she\par
took it with a look that filled him with delight; but when he asked if\par
she missed him, she gave such a chilly, formal reply that despair fell\par
upon him. On learning his good fortune she almost clapped her hands: was\par
the joy all for the boys? Then, on hearing his destination, she said,\par
"So far away!" in a tone of despair that lifted him on to a pinnacle of\par
hope; but the next minute she tumbled him down again by observing, like\par
one entirely absorbed in the matter,--\par
\par
"Here's the place for my errands; will you come in? It won't take long."\par
\par
Jo rather prided herself upon her shopping capabilities, and\par
particularly wished to impress her escort with the neatness and despatch\par
with which she would accomplish the business. But, owing to the flutter\par
she was in, everything went amiss; she upset the tray of needles, forgot\par
the silesia was to be "twilled" till it was cut off, gave the wrong\par
change, and covered herself with confusion by asking for lavender ribbon\par
at the calico counter. Mr. Bhaer stood by, watching her blush and\par
blunder; and, as he watched, his own bewilderment seemed to subside,\par
for he was beginning to see that on some occasions women, like dreams,\par
go by contraries.\par
\par
When they came out, he put the parcel under his arm with a more cheerful\par
aspect, and splashed through the puddles as if he rather enjoyed it, on\par
the whole.\par
\par
"Should we not do a little what you call shopping for the babies, and\par
haf a farewell feast to-night if I go for my last call at your so\par
pleasant home?" he asked, stopping before a window full of fruit and\par
flowers.\par
\par
"What will we buy?" said Jo, ignoring the latter part of his speech, and\par
sniffing the mingled odors with an affectation of delight as they went\par
in.\par
\par
"May they haf oranges and figs?" asked Mr. Bhaer, with a paternal air.\par
\par
"They eat them when they can get them."\par
\par
"Do you care for nuts?"\par
\par
"Like a squirrel."\par
\par
"Hamburg grapes; yes, we shall surely drink to the Fatherland in those?"\par
\par
Jo frowned upon that piece of extravagance, and asked why he didn't buy\par
a frail of dates, a cask of raisins, and a bag of almonds, and done with\par
it? Whereat Mr. Bhaer confiscated her purse, produced his own, and\par
finished the marketing by buying several pounds of grapes, a pot of rosy\par
daisies, and a pretty jar of honey, to be regarded in the light of a\par
demijohn. Then, distorting his pockets with the knobby bundles, and\par
giving her the flowers to hold, he put up the old umbrella, and they\par
travelled on again.\par
\par
"Miss Marsch, I haf a great favor to ask of you," began the Professor,\par
after a moist promenade of half a block.\par
\par
"Yes, sir;" and Jo's heart began to beat so hard she was afraid he would\par
hear it.\par
\par
"I am bold to say it in spite of the rain, because so short a time\par
remains to me."\par
\par
"Yes, sir;" and Jo nearly crushed the small flower-pot with the sudden\par
squeeze she gave it.\par
\par
"I wish to get a little dress for my Tina, and I am too stupid to go\par
alone. Will you kindly gif me a word of taste and help?"\par
\par
"Yes, sir;" and Jo felt as calm and cool, all of a sudden, as if she had\par
stepped into a refrigerator.\par
\par
"Perhaps also a shawl for Tina's mother, she is so poor and sick, and\par
the husband is such a care. Yes, yes, a thick, warm shawl would be a\par
friendly thing to take the little mother."\par
\par
"I'll do it with pleasure, Mr. Bhaer. I'm going very fast and he's\par
getting dearer every minute," added Jo to herself; then, with a mental\par
shake, she entered into the business with an energy which was pleasant\par
to behold.\par
\par
Mr. Bhaer left it all to her, so she chose a pretty gown for Tina, and\par
then ordered out the shawls. The clerk, being a married man,\par
condescended to take an interest in the couple, who appeared to be\par
shopping for their family.\par
\par
"Your lady may prefer this; it's a superior article, a most desirable\par
color, quite chaste and genteel," he said, shaking out a comfortable\par
gray shawl, and throwing it over Jo's shoulders.\par
\par
             [Illustration: Does this suit you, Mr. Bhaer?]\par
\par
"Does this suit you, Mr. Bhaer?" she asked, turning her back to him,\par
and feeling deeply grateful for the chance of hiding her face.\par
\par
"Excellently well; we will haf it," answered the Professor, smiling to\par
himself as he paid for it, while Jo continued to rummage the counters\par
like a confirmed bargain-hunter.\par
\par
"Now shall we go home?" he asked, as if the words were very pleasant to\par
him.\par
\par
"Yes; it's late, and I'm _so_ tired." Jo's voice was more pathetic than\par
she knew; for now the sun seemed to have gone in as suddenly as it came\par
out, the world grew muddy and miserable again, and for the first time\par
she discovered that her feet were cold, her head ached, and that her\par
heart was colder than the former, fuller of pain than the latter. Mr.\par
Bhaer was going away; he only cared for her as a friend; it was all a\par
mistake, and the sooner it was over the better. With this idea in her\par
head, she hailed an approaching omnibus with such a hasty gesture that\par
the daisies flew out of the pot and were badly damaged.\par
\par
"This is not our omniboos," said the Professor, waving the loaded\par
vehicle away, and stopping to pick up the poor little flowers.\par
\par
"I beg your pardon, I didn't see the name distinctly. Never mind, I can\par
walk. I'm used to plodding in the mud," returned Jo, winking hard,\par
because she would have died rather than openly wipe her eyes.\par
\par
Mr. Bhaer saw the drops on her cheeks, though she turned her head away;\par
the sight seemed to touch him very much, for, suddenly stooping down, he\par
asked in a tone that meant a great deal,--\par
\par
"Heart's dearest, why do you cry?"\par
\par
Now, if Jo had not been new to this sort of thing she would have said\par
she wasn't crying, had a cold in her head, or told any other feminine\par
fib proper to the occasion; instead of which that undignified creature\par
answered, with an irrepressible sob,--\par
\par
"Because you are going away."\par
\par
"Ach, mein Gott, that is _so_ good!" cried Mr. Bhaer, managing to clasp\par
his hands in spite of the umbrella and the bundles. "Jo, I haf nothing\par
but much love to gif you; I came to see if you could care for it, and I\par
waited to be sure that I was something more than a friend. Am I? Can\par
you make a little place in your heart for old Fritz?" he added, all in\par
one breath.\par
\par
"Oh, yes!" said Jo; and he was quite satisfied, for she folded both\par
hands over his arm, and looked up at him with an expression that plainly\par
showed how happy she would be to walk through life beside him, even\par
though she had no better shelter than the old umbrella, if he carried\par
it.\par
\par
It was certainly proposing under difficulties, for, even if he had\par
desired to do so, Mr. Bhaer could not go down upon his knees, on account\par
of the mud; neither could he offer Jo his hand, except figuratively, for\par
both were full; much less could he indulge in tender demonstrations in\par
the open street, though he was near it: so the only way in which he\par
could express his rapture was to look at her, with an expression which\par
glorified his face to such a degree that there actually seemed to be\par
little rainbows in the drops that sparkled on his beard. If he had not\par
loved Jo very much, I don't think he could have done it _then_, for she\par
looked far from lovely, with her skirts in a deplorable state, her\par
rubber boots splashed to the ankle, and her bonnet a ruin. Fortunately,\par
Mr. Bhaer considered her the most beautiful woman living, and she found\par
him more "Jove-like" than ever, though his hat-brim was quite limp with\par
the little rills trickling thence upon his shoulders (for he held the\par
umbrella all over Jo), and every finger of his gloves needed mending.\par
\par
Passers-by probably thought them a pair of harmless lunatics, for they\par
entirely forgot to hail a 'bus, and strolled leisurely along, oblivious\par
of deepening dusk and fog. Little they cared what anybody thought, for\par
they were enjoying the happy hour that seldom comes but once in any\par
life, the magical moment which bestows youth on the old, beauty on the\par
plain, wealth on the poor, and gives human hearts a foretaste of heaven.\par
The Professor looked as if he had conquered a kingdom, and the world had\par
nothing more to offer him in the way of bliss; while Jo trudged beside\par
him, feeling as if her place had always been there, and wondering how\par
she ever could have chosen any other lot. Of course, she was the first\par
to speak--intelligibly, I mean, for the emotional remarks which followed\par
her impetuous "Oh, yes!" were not of a coherent or reportable\par
character.\par
\par
"Friedrich, why didn't you--"\par
\par
"Ah, heaven, she gifs me the name that no one speaks since Minna died!"\par
cried the Professor, pausing in a puddle to regard her with grateful\par
delight.\par
\par
"I always call you so to myself--I forgot; but I won't, unless you like\par
it."\par
\par
"Like it? it is more sweet to me than I can tell. Say 'thou,' also, and\par
I shall say your language is almost as beautiful as mine."\par
\par
"Isn't 'thou' a little sentimental?" asked Jo, privately thinking it a\par
lovely monosyllable.\par
\par
"Sentimental? Yes. Thank Gott, we Germans believe in sentiment, and keep\par
ourselves young mit it. Your English 'you' is so cold, say 'thou,'\par
heart's dearest, it means so much to me," pleaded Mr. Bhaer, more like a\par
romantic student than a grave professor.\par
\par
"Well, then, why didn't thou tell me all this sooner?" asked Jo\par
bashfully.\par
\par
"Now I shall haf to show thee all my heart, and I so gladly will,\par
because thou must take care of it hereafter. See, then, my Jo,--ah, the\par
dear, funny little name!--I had a wish to tell something the day I said\par
good-by, in New York; but I thought the handsome friend was betrothed to\par
thee, and so I spoke not. Wouldst thou have said 'Yes,' then, if I _had_\par
spoken?"\par
\par
"I don't know; I'm afraid not, for I didn't have any heart just then."\par
\par
"Prut! that I do not believe. It was asleep till the fairy prince came\par
through the wood, and waked it up. Ah, well, 'Die erste Liebe ist die\par
beste;' but that I should not expect."\par
\par
"Yes, the first love _is_ the best; so be contented, for I never had\par
another. Teddy was only a boy, and soon got over his little fancy," said\par
Jo, anxious to correct the Professor's mistake.\par
\par
"Good! then I shall rest happy, and be sure that thou givest me all. I\par
haf waited so long, I am grown selfish, as thou wilt find, Professorin."\par
\par
"I like that," cried Jo, delighted with her new name. "Now tell me what\par
brought you, at last, just when I most wanted you?"\par
\par
"This;" and Mr. Bhaer took a little worn paper out of his\par
waistcoat-pocket.\par
\par
Jo unfolded it, and looked much abashed, for it was one of her own\par
contributions to a paper that paid for poetry, which accounted for her\par
sending it an occasional attempt.\par
\par
"How could that bring you?" she asked, wondering what he meant.\par
\par
"I found it by chance; I knew it by the names and the initials, and in\par
it there was one little verse that seemed to call me. Read and find him;\par
I will see that you go not in the wet."\par
\par
Jo obeyed, and hastily skimmed through the lines which she had\par
christened--\par
\par
                        "IN THE GARRET.\par
\par
        "Four little chests all in a row,\par
           Dim with dust, and worn by time,\par
         All fashioned and filled, long ago,\par
           By children now in their prime.\par
         Four little keys hung side by side,\par
           With faded ribbons, brave and gay\par
         When fastened there, with childish pride,\par
           Long ago, on a rainy day.\par
         Four little names, one on each lid,\par
           Carved out by a boyish hand,\par
         And underneath there lieth hid\par
           Histories of the happy band\par
         Once playing here, and pausing oft\par
           To hear the sweet refrain,\par
         That came and went on the roof aloft,\par
           In the falling summer rain.\par
\par
        "'Meg' on the first lid, smooth and fair.\par
           I look in with loving eyes,\par
         For folded here, with well-known care,\par
           A goodly gathering lies,\par
         The record of a peaceful life,--\par
           Gifts to gentle child and girl,\par
         A bridal gown, lines to a wife,\par
           A tiny shoe, a baby curl.\par
         No toys in this first chest remain,\par
           For all are carried away,\par
         In their old age, to join again\par
           In another small Meg's play.\par
         Ah, happy mother! well I know\par
           You hear, like a sweet refrain,\par
         Lullabies ever soft and low\par
           In the falling summer rain.\par
\par
        "'Jo' on the next lid, scratched and worn,\par
           And within a motley store\par
         Of headless dolls, of school-books torn,\par
           Birds and beasts that speak no more;\par
         Spoils brought home from the fairy ground\par
           Only trod by youthful feet,\par
         Dreams of a future never found,\par
           Memories of a past still sweet;\par
         Half-writ poems, stories wild,\par
           April letters, warm and cold,\par
         Diaries of a wilful child,\par
           Hints of a woman early old;\par
         A woman in a lonely home,\par
           Hearing, like a sad refrain,--\par
         'Be worthy love, and love will come,'\par
           In the falling summer rain.\par
\par
        "My Beth! the dust is always swept\par
           From the lid that bears your name,\par
         As if by loving eyes that wept,\par
           By careful hands that often came.\par
         Death canonized for us one saint,\par
           Ever less human than divine,\par
         And still we lay, with tender plaint,\par
           Relics in this household shrine.--\par
         The silver bell, so seldom rung,\par
           The little cap which last she wore,\par
         The fair, dead Catherine that hung\par
           By angels borne above her door;\par
         The songs she sang, without lament,\par
           In her prison-house of pain,\par
         Forever are they sweetly blent\par
           With the falling summer rain.\par
\par
        "Upon the last lid's polished field--\par
           Legend now both fair and true--\par
         A gallant knight bears on his shield,\par
           'Amy,' in letters gold and blue.\par
         Within lie snoods that bound her hair,\par
           Slippers that have danced their last,\par
         Faded flowers laid by with care,\par
           Fans whose airy toils are past;\par
         Gay valentines, all ardent flames,\par
           Trifles that have borne their part\par
         In girlish hopes and fears and shames,--\par
           The record of a maiden heart\par
         Now learning fairer, truer spells,\par
           Hearing, like a blithe refrain,\par
         The silver sound of bridal bells\par
           In the falling summer rain.\par
\par
        "Four little chests all in a row,\par
           Dim with dust, and worn by time,\par
         Four women, taught by weal and woe\par
           To love and labor in their prime.\par
         Four sisters, parted for an hour,\par
           None lost, one only gone before,\par
         Made by love's immortal power,\par
           Nearest and dearest evermore.\par
         Oh, when these hidden stores of ours\par
           Lie open to the Father's sight,\par
         May they be rich in golden hours,\par
           Deeds that show fairer for the light,\par
         Lives whose brave music long shall ring,\par
           Like a spirit-stirring strain,\par
         Souls that shall gladly soar and sing\par
           In the long sunshine after rain.\par
\par
                                                             "J. M."\par
\par
"It's very bad poetry, but I felt it when I wrote it, one day when I was\par
very lonely, and had a good cry on a rag-bag. I never thought it would\par
go where it could tell tales," said Jo, tearing up the verses the\par
Professor had treasured so long.\par
\par
"Let it go, it has done its duty, and I will haf a fresh one when I read\par
all the brown book in which she keeps her little secrets," said Mr.\par
Bhaer, with a smile, as he watched the fragments fly away on the wind.\par
"Yes," he added earnestly, "I read that, and I think to myself, 'She has\par
a sorrow, she is lonely, she would find comfort in true love. I haf a\par
heart full, full for her; shall I not go and say, 'If this is not too\par
poor a thing to gif for what I shall hope to receive, take it in Gott's\par
name?'"\par
\par
"And so you came to find that it was not too poor, but the one precious\par
thing I needed," whispered Jo.\par
\par
"I had no courage to think that at first, heavenly kind as was your\par
welcome to me. But soon I began to hope, and then I said, 'I will haf\par
her if I die for it,' and so I will!" cried Mr. Bhaer, with a defiant\par
nod, as if the walls of mist closing round them were barriers which he\par
was to surmount or valiantly knock down.\par
\par
Jo thought that was splendid, and resolved to be worthy of her knight,\par
though he did not come prancing on a charger in gorgeous array.\par
\par
"What made you stay away so long?" she asked presently, finding it so\par
pleasant to ask confidential questions and get delightful answers that\par
she could not keep silent.\par
\par
"It was not easy, but I could not find the heart to take you from that\par
so happy home until I could haf a prospect of one to give you, after\par
much time, perhaps, and hard work. How could I ask you to gif up so much\par
for a poor old fellow, who has no fortune but a little learning?"\par
\par
"I'm glad you _are_ poor; I couldn't bear a rich husband," said Jo\par
decidedly, adding, in a softer tone, "Don't fear poverty; I've known it\par
long enough to lose my dread, and be happy working for those I love; and\par
don't call yourself old,--forty is the prime of life. I couldn't help\par
loving you if you were seventy!"\par
\par
The Professor found that so touching that he would have been glad of his\par
handkerchief, if he could have got at it; as he couldn't, Jo wiped his\par
eyes for him, and said, laughing, as she took away a bundle or two,--\par
\par
"I may be strong-minded, but no one can say I'm out of my sphere now,\par
for woman's special mission is supposed to be drying tears and bearing\par
burdens. I'm to carry my share, Friedrich, and help to earn the home.\par
Make up your mind to that, or I'll never go," she added resolutely, as\par
he tried to reclaim his load.\par
\par
"We shall see. Haf you patience to wait a long time, Jo? I must go away\par
and do my work alone. I must help my boys first, because, even for you,\par
I may not break my word to Minna. Can you forgif that, and be happy\par
while we hope and wait?"\par
\par
"Yes, I know I can; for we love one another, and that makes all the rest\par
easy to bear. I have my duty, also, and my work. I couldn't enjoy myself\par
if I neglected them even for you, so there's no need of hurry or\par
impatience. You can do your part out West, I can do mine here, and both\par
be happy hoping for the best, and leaving the future to be as God\par
wills."\par
\par
"Ah! thou gifest me such hope and courage, and I haf nothing to gif back\par
but a full heart and these empty hands," cried the Professor, quite\par
overcome.\par
\par
                   [Illustration: Under the umbrella]\par
\par
Jo never, never would learn to be proper; for when he said that as they\par
stood upon the steps, she just put both hands into his, whispering\par
tenderly, "Not empty now;" and, stooping down, kissed her Friedrich\par
under the umbrella. It was dreadful, but she would have done it if the\par
flock of draggle-tailed sparrows on the hedge had been human beings, for\par
she was very far gone indeed, and quite regardless of everything but her\par
own happiness. Though it came in such a very simple guise, that was the\par
crowning moment of both their lives, when, turning from the night and\par
storm and loneliness to the household light and warmth and peace waiting\par
to receive them, with a glad "Welcome home!" Jo led her lover in, and\par
shut the door.\par
\par
                       [Illustration: Tail-piece]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                      [Illustration: Harvest time]\par
\par
                                 XLVII.\par
\par
                             HARVEST TIME.\par
\par
\par
For a year Jo and her Professor worked and waited, hoped and loved, met\par
occasionally, and wrote such voluminous letters that the rise in the\par
price of paper was accounted for, Laurie said. The second year began\par
rather soberly, for their prospects did not brighten, and Aunt March\par
died suddenly. But when their first sorrow was over,--for they loved the\par
old lady in spite of her sharp tongue,--they found they had cause for\par
rejoicing, for she had left Plumfield to Jo, which made all sorts of\par
joyful things possible.\par
\par
"It's a fine old place, and will bring a handsome sum; for of course you\par
intend to sell it," said Laurie, as they were all talking the matter\par
over, some weeks later.\par
\par
"No, I don't," was Jo's decided answer, as she petted the fat poodle,\par
whom she had adopted, out of respect to his former mistress.\par
\par
"You don't mean to live there?"\par
\par
"Yes, I do."\par
\par
"But, my dear girl, it's an immense house, and will take a power of\par
money to keep it in order. The garden and orchard alone need two or\par
three men, and farming isn't in Bhaer's line, I take it."\par
\par
"He'll try his hand at it there, if I propose it."\par
\par
"And you expect to live on the produce of the place? Well, that sounds\par
paradisiacal, but you'll find it desperate hard work."\par
\par
"The crop we are going to raise is a profitable one;" and Jo laughed.\par
\par
"Of what is this fine crop to consist, ma'am?"\par
\par
"Boys. I want to open a school for little lads,--a good, happy, homelike\par
school, with me to take care of them, and Fritz to teach them."\par
\par
"There's a truly Joian plan for you! Isn't that just like her?" cried\par
Laurie, appealing to the family, who looked as much surprised as he.\par
\par
"I like it," said Mrs. March decidedly.\par
\par
"So do I," added her husband, who welcomed the thought of a chance for\par
trying the Socratic method of education on modern youth.\par
\par
"It will be an immense care for Jo," said Meg, stroking the head of her\par
one all-absorbing son.\par
\par
"Jo can do it, and be happy in it. It's a splendid idea. Tell us all\par
about it," cried Mr. Laurence, who had been longing to lend the lovers a\par
hand, but knew that they would refuse his help.\par
\par
"I knew you'd stand by me, sir. Amy does too--I see it in her eyes,\par
though she prudently waits to turn it over in her mind before she\par
speaks. Now, my dear people," continued Jo earnestly, "just understand\par
that this isn't a new idea of mine, but a long-cherished plan. Before my\par
Fritz came, I used to think how, when I'd made my fortune, and no one\par
needed me at home, I'd hire a big house, and pick up some poor, forlorn\par
little lads, who hadn't any mothers, and take care of them, and make\par
life jolly for them before it was too late. I see so many going to ruin,\par
for want of help at the right minute; I love so to do anything for them;\par
I seem to feel their wants, and sympathize with their troubles, and, oh,\par
I should _so_ like to be a mother to them!"\par
\par
Mrs. March held out her hand to Jo, who took it, smiling, with tears in\par
her eyes, and went on in the old enthusiastic way, which they had not\par
seen for a long while.\par
\par
"I told my plan to Fritz once, and he said it was just what he would\par
like, and agreed to try it when we got rich. Bless his dear heart, he's\par
been doing it all his life,--helping poor boys, I mean, not getting\par
rich; that he'll never be; money doesn't stay in his pocket long enough\par
to lay up any. But now, thanks to my good old aunt, who loved me better\par
than I ever deserved, _I'm_ rich, at least I feel so, and we can live at\par
Plumfield perfectly well, if we have a flourishing school. It's just the\par
place for boys, the house is big, and the furniture strong and plain.\par
There's plenty of room for dozens inside, and splendid grounds outside.\par
They could help in the garden and orchard: such work is healthy, isn't\par
it, sir? Then Fritz can train and teach in his own way, and father will\par
help him. I can feed and nurse and pet and scold them; and mother will\par
be my stand-by. I've always longed for lots of boys, and never had\par
enough; now I can fill the house full, and revel in the little dears to\par
my heart's content. Think what luxury,--Plumfield my own, and a\par
wilderness of boys to enjoy it with me!"\par
\par
As Jo waved her hands, and gave a sigh of rapture, the family went off\par
into a gale of merriment, and Mr. Laurence laughed till they thought\par
he'd have an apoplectic fit.\par
\par
"I don't see anything funny," she said gravely, when she could be heard.\par
"Nothing could be more natural or proper than for my Professor to open a\par
school, and for me to prefer to reside on my own estate."\par
\par
"She is putting on airs already," said Laurie, who regarded the idea in\par
the light of a capital joke. "But may I inquire how you intend to\par
support the establishment? If all the pupils are little ragamuffins, I'm\par
afraid your crop won't be profitable in a worldly sense, Mrs. Bhaer."\par
\par
"Now don't be a wet-blanket, Teddy. Of course I shall have rich pupils,\par
also,--perhaps begin with such altogether; then, when I've got a start,\par
I can take a ragamuffin or two, just for a relish. Rich people's\par
children often need care and comfort, as well as poor. I've seen\par
unfortunate little creatures left to servants, or backward ones pushed\par
forward, when it's real cruelty. Some are naughty through mismanagement\par
or neglect, and some lose their mothers. Besides, the best have to get\par
through the hobbledehoy age, and that's the very time they need most\par
patience and kindness. People laugh at them, and hustle them about, try\par
to keep them out of sight, and expect them to turn, all at once, from\par
pretty children into fine young men. They don't complain much,--plucky\par
little souls,--but they feel it. I've been through something of it, and\par
I know all about it. I've a special interest in such young bears, and\par
like to show them that I see the warm, honest, well-meaning boys'\par
hearts, in spite of the clumsy arms and legs and the topsy-turvy heads.\par
I've had experience, too, for haven't I brought up one boy to be a pride\par
and honor to his family?"\par
\par
"I'll testify that you tried to do it," said Laurie, with a grateful\par
look.\par
\par
"And I've succeeded beyond my hopes; for here you are, a steady,\par
sensible business man, doing heaps of good with your money, and laying\par
up the blessings of the poor, instead of dollars. But you are not merely\par
a business man: you love good and beautiful things, enjoy them yourself,\par
and let others go halves, as you always did in the old times. I _am_\par
proud of you, Teddy, for you get better every year, and every one feels\par
it, though you won't let them say so. Yes, and when I have my flock,\par
I'll just point to you, and say, 'There's your model, my lads.'"\par
\par
Poor Laurie didn't know where to look; for, man though he was, something\par
of the old bashfulness came over him as this burst of praise made all\par
faces turn approvingly upon him.\par
\par
"I say, Jo, that's rather too much," he began, just in his old boyish\par
way. "You have all done more for me than I can ever thank you for,\par
except by doing my best not to disappoint you. You have rather cast me\par
off lately, Jo, but I've had the best of help, nevertheless; so, if I've\par
got on at all, you may thank these two for it;" and he laid one hand\par
gently on his grandfather's white head, the other on Amy's golden one,\par
for the three were never far apart.\par
\par
"I do think that families are the most beautiful things in all the\par
world!" burst out Jo, who was in an unusually uplifted frame of mind\par
just then. "When I have one of my own, I hope it will be as happy as the\par
three I know and love the best. If John and my Fritz were only here, it\par
would be quite a little heaven on earth," she added more quietly. And\par
that night, when she went to her room, after a blissful evening of\par
family counsels, hopes, and plans, her heart was so full of happiness\par
that she could only calm it by kneeling beside the empty bed always near\par
her own, and thinking tender thoughts of Beth.\par
\par
It was a very astonishing year altogether, for things seemed to happen\par
in an unusually rapid and delightful manner. Almost before she knew\par
where she was, Jo found herself married and settled at Plumfield. Then a\par
family of six or seven boys sprung up like mushrooms, and flourished\par
surprisingly, poor boys as well as rich; for Mr. Laurence was\par
continually finding some touching case of destitution, and begging the\par
Bhaers to take pity on the child, and he would gladly pay a trifle for\par
its support. In this way the sly old gentleman got round proud Jo, and\par
furnished her with the style of boy in which she most delighted.\par
\par
Of course it was up-hill work at first, and Jo made queer mistakes; but\par
the wise Professor steered her safely into calmer waters, and the most\par
rampant ragamuffin was conquered in the end. How Jo did enjoy her\par
"wilderness of boys," and how poor, dear Aunt March would have lamented\par
had she been there to see the sacred precincts of prim, well-ordered\par
Plumfield overrun with Toms, Dicks, and Harrys! There was a sort of\par
poetic justice about it, after all, for the old lady had been the terror\par
of the boys for miles round; and now the exiles feasted freely on\par
forbidden plums, kicked up the gravel with profane boots unreproved, and\par
played cricket in the big field where the irritable "cow with a crumpled\par
horn" used to invite rash youths to come and be tossed. It became a sort\par
of boys' paradise, and Laurie suggested that it should be called the\par
"Bhaer-garten," as a compliment to its master and appropriate to its\par
inhabitants.\par
\par
It never was a fashionable school, and the Professor did not lay up a\par
fortune; but it _was_ just what Jo intended it to be,--"a happy,\par
homelike place for boys, who needed teaching, care, and kindness."\par
Every room in the big house was soon full; every little plot in the\par
garden soon had its owner; a regular menagerie appeared in barn and\par
shed, for pet animals were allowed; and, three times a day, Jo smiled at\par
her Fritz from the head of a long table lined on either side with rows\par
of happy young faces, which all turned to her with affectionate eyes,\par
confiding words, and grateful hearts, full of love for "Mother Bhaer."\par
She had boys enough now, and did not tire of them, though they were not\par
angels, by any means, and some of them caused both Professor and\par
Professorin much trouble and anxiety. But her faith in the good spot\par
which exists in the heart of the naughtiest, sauciest, most tantalizing\par
little ragamuffin gave her patience, skill, and, in time, success; for\par
no mortal boy could hold out long with Father Bhaer shining on him as\par
benevolently as the sun, and Mother Bhaer forgiving him seventy times\par
seven. Very precious to Jo was the friendship of the lads; their\par
penitent sniffs and whispers after wrong-doing; their droll or touching\par
little confidences; their pleasant enthusiasms, hopes, and plans; even\par
their misfortunes, for they only endeared them to her all the more.\par
There were slow boys and bashful boys; feeble boys and riotous boys;\par
boys that lisped and boys that stuttered; one or two lame ones; and a\par
merry little quadroon, who could not be taken in elsewhere, but who was\par
welcome to the "Bhaer-garten," though some people predicted that his\par
admission would ruin the school.\par
\par
Yes; Jo was a very happy woman there, in spite of hard work, much\par
anxiety, and a perpetual racket. She enjoyed it heartily, and found the\par
applause of her boys more satisfying than any praise of the world; for\par
now she told no stories except to her flock of enthusiastic believers\par
and admirers. As the years went on, two little lads of her own came to\par
increase her happiness,--Rob, named for grandpa, and Teddy, a\par
happy-go-lucky baby, who seemed to have inherited his papa's sunshiny\par
temper as well as his mother's lively spirit. How they ever grew up\par
alive in that whirlpool of boys was a mystery to their grandma and\par
aunts; but they flourished like dandelions in spring, and their rough\par
nurses loved and served them well.\par
\par
There were a great many holidays at Plumfield, and one of the most\par
delightful was the yearly apple-picking; for then the Marches,\par
Laurences, Brookes, and Bhaers turned out in full force, and made a day\par
of it. Five years after Jo's wedding, one of these fruitful festivals\par
occurred,--a mellow October day, when the air was full of an\par
exhilarating freshness which made the spirits rise, and the blood dance\par
healthily in the veins. The old orchard wore its holiday attire;\par
golden-rod and asters fringed the mossy walls; grasshoppers skipped\par
briskly in the sere grass, and crickets chirped like fairy pipers at a\par
feast; squirrels were busy with their small harvesting; birds twittered\par
their adieux from the alders in the lane; and every tree stood ready to\par
send down its shower of red or yellow apples at the first shake.\par
Everybody was there; everybody laughed and sang, climbed up and tumbled\par
down; everybody declared that there never had been such a perfect day or\par
such a jolly set to enjoy it; and every one gave themselves up to the\par
simple pleasures of the hour as freely as if there were no such things\par
as care or sorrow in the world.\par
\par
Mr. March strolled placidly about, quoting Tusser, Cowley, and Columella\par
to Mr. Laurence, while enjoying--\par
\par
        "The gentle apple's winey juice."\par
\par
The Professor charged up and down the green aisles like a stout Teutonic\par
knight, with a pole for a lance, leading on the boys, who made a hook\par
and ladder company of themselves, and performed wonders in the way of\par
ground and lofty tumbling. Laurie devoted himself to the little ones,\par
rode his small daughter in a bushel-basket, took Daisy up among the\par
birds' nests, and kept adventurous Rob from breaking his neck. Mrs.\par
March and Meg sat among the apple piles like a pair of Pomonas, sorting\par
the contributions that kept pouring in; while Amy, with a beautiful\par
motherly expression in her face, sketched the various groups, and\par
watched over one pale lad, who sat adoring her with his little crutch\par
beside him.\par
\par
Jo was in her element that day, and rushed about, with her gown pinned\par
up, her hat anywhere but on her head, and her baby tucked under her arm,\par
ready for any lively adventure which might turn up. Little Teddy bore a\par
charmed life, for nothing ever happened to him, and Jo never felt any\par
anxiety when he was whisked up into a tree by one lad, galloped off on\par
the back of another, or supplied with sour russets by his indulgent\par
papa, who labored under the Germanic delusion that babies could digest\par
anything, from pickled cabbage to buttons, nails, and their own small\par
shoes. She knew that little Ted would turn up again in time, safe and\par
rosy, dirty and serene, and she always received him back with a hearty\par
welcome, for Jo loved her babies tenderly.\par
\par
               [Illustration: Teddy bore a charmed life]\par
\par
At four o'clock a lull took place, and baskets remained empty, while the\par
apple-pickers rested, and compared rents and bruises. Then Jo and Meg,\par
with a detachment of the bigger boys, set forth the supper on the grass,\par
for an out-of-door tea was always the crowning joy of the day. The land\par
literally flowed with milk and honey on such occasions, for the lads\par
were not required to sit at table, but allowed to partake of refreshment\par
as they liked,--freedom being the sauce best beloved by the boyish soul.\par
They availed themselves of the rare privilege to the fullest extent, for\par
some tried the pleasing experiment of drinking milk while standing on\par
their heads, others lent a charm to leap-frog by eating pie in the\par
pauses of the game, cookies were sown broadcast over the field, and\par
apple-turnovers roosted in the trees like a new style of bird. The\par
little girls had a private tea-party, and Ted roved among the edibles at\par
his own sweet will.\par
\par
When no one could eat any more, the Professor proposed the first regular\par
toast, which was always drunk at such times,--"Aunt March, God bless\par
her!" A toast heartily given by the good man, who never forgot how much\par
he owed her, and quietly drunk by the boys, who had been taught to\par
keep her memory green.\par
\par
"Now, grandma's sixtieth birthday! Long life to her, with three times\par
three!"\par
\par
That was given with a will, as you may well believe; and the cheering\par
once begun, it was hard to stop it. Everybody's health was proposed,\par
from Mr. Laurence, who was considered their special patron, to the\par
astonished guinea-pig, who had strayed from its proper sphere in search\par
of its young master. Demi, as the oldest grandchild, then presented the\par
queen of the day with various gifts, so numerous that they were\par
transported to the festive scene in a wheelbarrow. Funny presents, some\par
of them, but what would have been defects to other eyes were ornaments\par
to grandma's,--for the children's gifts were all their own. Every stitch\par
Daisy's patient little fingers had put into the handkerchiefs she hemmed\par
was better than embroidery to Mrs. March; Demi's shoe-box was a miracle\par
of mechanical skill, though the cover wouldn't shut; Rob's footstool had\par
a wiggle in its uneven legs, that she declared was very soothing; and no\par
page of the costly book Amy's child gave her was so fair as that on\par
which appeared, in tipsy capitals, the words,--"To dear Grandma, from\par
her little Beth."\par
\par
During this ceremony the boys had mysteriously disappeared; and, when\par
Mrs. March had tried to thank her children, and broken down, while Teddy\par
wiped her eyes on his pinafore, the Professor suddenly began to sing.\par
Then, from above him, voice after voice took up the words, and from tree\par
to tree echoed the music of the unseen choir, as the boys sung, with all\par
their hearts, the little song Jo had written, Laurie set to music, and\par
the Professor trained his lads to give with the best effect. This was\par
something altogether new, and it proved a grand success; for Mrs. March\par
couldn't get over her surprise, and insisted on shaking hands with every\par
one of the featherless birds, from tall Franz and Emil to the little\par
quadroon, who had the sweetest voice of all.\par
\par
After this, the boys dispersed for a final lark, leaving Mrs. March and\par
her daughters under the festival tree.\par
\par
[Illustration: "Leaving Mrs. March and her daughters under the festival\par
                           tree."--Page 583]\par
\par
"I don't think I ever ought to call myself 'Unlucky Jo' again, when my\par
greatest wish has been so beautifully gratified," said Mrs. Bhaer,\par
taking Teddy's little fist out of the milk-pitcher, in which he was\par
rapturously churning.\par
\par
"And yet your life is very different from the one you pictured so long\par
ago. Do you remember our castles in the air?" asked Amy, smiling as she\par
watched Laurie and John playing cricket with the boys.\par
\par
"Dear fellows! It does my heart good to see them forget business, and\par
frolic for a day," answered Jo, who now spoke in a maternal way of all\par
mankind. "Yes, I remember; but the life I wanted then seems selfish,\par
lonely, and cold to me now. I haven't given up the hope that I may write\par
a good book yet, but I can wait, and I'm sure it will be all the better\par
for such experiences and illustrations as these;" and Jo pointed from\par
the lively lads in the distance to her father, leaning on the\par
Professor's arm, as they walked to and fro in the sunshine, deep in one\par
of the conversations which both enjoyed so much, and then to her mother,\par
sitting enthroned among her daughters, with their children in her lap\par
and at her feet, as if all found help and happiness in the face which\par
never could grow old to them.\par
\par
"My castle was the most nearly realized of all. I asked for splendid\par
things, to be sure, but in my heart I knew I should be satisfied, if I\par
had a little home, and John, and some dear children like these. I've got\par
them all, thank God, and am the happiest woman in the world;" and Meg\par
laid her hand on her tall boy's head, with a face full of tender and\par
devout content.\par
\par
"My castle is very different from what I planned, but I would not alter\par
it, though, like Jo, I don't relinquish all my artistic hopes, or\par
confine myself to helping others fulfil their dreams of beauty. I've\par
begun to model a figure of baby, and Laurie says it is the best thing\par
I've ever done. I think so myself, and mean to do it in marble, so that,\par
whatever happens, I may at least keep the image of my little angel."\par
\par
As Amy spoke, a great tear dropped on the golden hair of the sleeping\par
child in her arms; for her one well-beloved daughter was a frail little\par
creature and the dread of losing her was the shadow over Amy's sunshine.\par
This cross was doing much for both father and mother, for one love and\par
sorrow bound them closely together. Amy's nature was growing sweeter,\par
deeper, and more tender; Laurie was growing more serious, strong, and\par
firm; and both were learning that beauty, youth, good fortune, even love\par
itself, cannot keep care and pain, loss and sorrow, from the most blest;\par
for--\par
\par
        "Into each life some rain must fall,\par
         Some days must be dark and sad and dreary."\par
\par
"She is growing better, I am sure of it, my dear. Don't despond, but\par
hope and keep happy," said Mrs. March, as tender-hearted Daisy stooped\par
from her knee, to lay her rosy cheek against her little cousin's pale\par
one.\par
\par
"I never ought to, while I have you to cheer me up, Marmee, and Laurie\par
to take more than half of every burden," replied Amy warmly. "He never\par
lets me see his anxiety, but is so sweet and patient with me, so devoted\par
to Beth, and such a stay and comfort to me always, that I can't love him\par
enough. So, in spite of my one cross, I can say with Meg, 'Thank God,\par
I'm a happy woman.'"\par
\par
"There's no need for me to say it, for every one can see that I'm far\par
happier than I deserve," added Jo, glancing from her good husband to her\par
chubby children, tumbling on the grass beside her. "Fritz is getting\par
gray and stout; I'm growing as thin as a shadow, and am thirty; we never\par
shall be rich, and Plumfield may burn up any night, for that\par
incorrigible Tommy Bangs _will_ smoke sweet-fern cigars under the\par
bed-clothes, though he's set himself afire three times already. But in\par
spite of these unromantic facts, I have nothing to complain of, and\par
never was so jolly in my life. Excuse the remark, but living among boys,\par
I can't help using their expressions now and then."\par
\par
"Yes, Jo, I think your harvest will be a good one," began Mrs. March,\par
frightening away a big black cricket that was staring Teddy out of\par
countenance.\par
\par
"Not half so good as yours, mother. Here it is, and we never can thank\par
you enough for the patient sowing and reaping you have done," cried Jo,\par
with the loving impetuosity which she never could outgrow.\par
\par
"I hope there will be more wheat and fewer tares every year," said Amy\par
softly.\par
\par
"A large sheaf, but I know there's room in your heart for it, Marmee\par
dear," added Meg's tender voice.\par
\par
Touched to the heart, Mrs. March could only stretch out her arms, as if\par
to gather children and grandchildren to herself, and say, with face and\par
voice full of motherly love, gratitude, and humility,--\par
\par
"O, my girls, however long you may live, I never can wish you a greater\par
happiness than this!"\par
