                          Transcriber's Notes:\par
\par
\par
Project Gutenberg has two versions of Little Women by Louisa May Alcott.\par
   Original Edition\par
   Illustrated Edition\par
\par
Welcome to the Project Gutenberg Illustrated Edition of Little Women by\par
Louisa May Alcott, published by Little, Brown, and Company. Some\par
versions of the book, such as this one, use the full title of the book\par
from the title page, _Little Women; Or Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy_.\par
\par
We used the version of the book from Little, Brown, and Company:\par
copyright 1896, for this transcription. A scanned copy of this book is\par
available through the internet archive, courtesy of the New York Public\par
Library.\par
\par
A copy of the first version of the novel, published in 1869, was\par
consulted for emendations, the proper rendering of words hyphenated and\par
split between two lines for spacing, and other issues in transcribing\par
the novel. We are not trying to change this version of the novel back to\par
the 1869 novel, but correct the errors that were made in re-transcribing\par
and updating the text.\par
\par
Passages in italics were indicated by _underscores_.\par
\par
Passages in bold were indicated by =equal signs=.\par
\par
Small caps were replaced with ALL CAPS.\par
\par
Throughout the dialogues, there were words used to mimic accents of\par
the speakers. Those words were retained as-is.\par
\par
Errors in punctuations and hyphenation were not corrected unless\par
otherwise noted below.\par
\par
A note about spacing of illustrations: If there are four lines above the\par
illustration, then the illustration was at the top of a new chapter. If\par
there are four lines below the illustration, then the illustration was\par
at the end of a chapter. If there is two lines between a chapter heading\par
and the illustration, then the illustration was an inline illustration\par
(usually a drop-cap illustration).\par
\par
On page vii, in the Table of Contents, change page 7 to 1 for the\par
beginning of Chapter One.\par
\par
In the List of Illustrations, for the illustration on page 147,\par
"postmistress" was replaced with "post-mistress".\par
\par
In the List of Illustrations, for the illustration on page 235, \par
"tea-pot" was replaced with "teapot".\par
\par
On page 30, the punctuation after 'stained they are' may be a colon, but\par
on page 41 of the 1869 book, it is a semicolon. We used the semi-colon. \par
\par
On page 34, transcribe red-headed with the hyphen. See page 44 of the\par
1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 40, a period was added after "room where old Mr". See page 50 of\par
the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 41, the semicolon after "Laurie went on the box" was replaced\par
with a comma. See page 52 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 62, mantel-piece was transcribed with the hyphen. See Page 75 of\par
the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 63, checked the clause "and I've been trying to do it this ever\par
so long." It is written the same way on page 77 of the 1869 book. No\par
change was made.\par
\par
On page 64, add period after "red and shining with merriment." See page\par
79 of the 1869 book.\par
\par
On page 68, changed weet to sweet in 'the damp weet air.' See page 84 of\par
the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 79, add comma after I remain in the letter. See page 95 of the\par
1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 84, ferrule was an adjustment from the 1869 book, which only\par
used one r in spelling the word (see page 101). Webster's dictionary\par
spells ferrule with two rs.\par
\par
On page 109, a period was added after "and the old man quite dotes on\par
them". See page 130 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 109, a period was added after "asked another voice". See page\par
131 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 112, change colon to semicolon after "if you don't;"--see page\par
134 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 113, transcribe ear-rings with the hyphen. See page 135 of the\par
1869 novel.\par
\par
On Page 123, "One thing remember, my girls:" is written as it appears in\par
the 1896 novel. A comma instead of a colon was used after my girls in\par
the 1869 novel (see page 146). "One thing =to= remember," may work\par
better today, or even "Remember one thing," but we left this as Ms.\par
Alcott wrote it.\par
\par
On Page 124, the P. C. is the Pickwick Club from a novel by Charles\par
Dickens. Samuel Pickwick, Tracy Tupman, Augustus Snodgrass, and\par
Nathaniel Winkle were introdued by Charles Dickens in the first chapter\par
of The Pickwick Papers. Samuel Weller makes his first appearance in\par
Chapter Ten of that novel.\par
\par
On page 128, in the Pickwick Portfolio, there is no period after "it is\par
nearly school time" in N. Winkle's letter. This period was also missing\par
on page 151 of the 1869 novel. The missing period was intentional.\par
\par
On page 135, the ambiguous punctuation after "Oh, dear, no!" is an\par
exclamation point. See page 160 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 146, change buttonholes to button-holes. On page 173 of the 1869\par
novel, this word was hyphenated and split between two lines for spacing.\par
There were seven other occurrences of button-hole or button-holes in the\par
novel.  We therefore used the hyphen.\par
\par
On page 150, "Betty" was replaced with "Bethy". This error was also made\par
in the 1869 novel--see page 177. The character addressed is Beth. \par
\par
On page 158, a period was added after "still kneeling". See page 187 of\par
the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 160, "hard a lee" is spelled the same way in the 1869 novel (see\par
page 189) and this novel. We left this as is.\par
\par
On page 166, a comma was added after "Meg" in "Meg obediently\par
following". See page 195 of the 1869 book.\par
\par
On page 185, "receptable" was replaced with "receptacle". See page 217\par
of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 185, transcribe door-way with the hyphen. See page 217 of the\par
1869 novel. Also, change doorway to door-way a few lines down the same\par
page. See page 218 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 189, the comma after "published every day" was replaced with a\par
period. See page 225 of 1869 book.\par
\par
On page 198, the clause: "Beth, go and ask Mr. Laurence for a couple of\par
bottles of old wine:" was written as it appeared in the 1896 novel. The\par
clause ended in with a semi-colon in the 1869 book (see page 234). \par
\par
On page 200, change needlework to needle-work. See page 236 of the 1869\par
novel.\par
\par
On page 209, "turnovers" was replaced with "turn-overs". See page 246 of\par
the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 214, the single quotation mark before "Head Nurse of Ward" was\par
replaced with a double quotation mark. See page 252 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 218, "Year's ago" was replaced with "Years ago". See page 257 of\par
the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 219, "ask him so help" was replaced with "ask him to help". See\par
page 257 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 219, add period after "give it to her." See page 258 of the 1869\par
novel. \par
\par
On page 230, "two, A.M." is spelled the same way, with the comma, in\par
this book and in the 1869 novel (on page 272). The comma was retained.\par
\par
On page 244, "postscrips" was replaced with "postscripts". See page 287\par
of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 279, place exclamation point after won't in 'No, I won't!' See\par
page 329 in the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 286, "actingly" was replaced with "acting". See page 337 of the\par
1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 288, add comma after mankind in the clause "who felt at peace\par
with all mankind even his mischievous pupil." See page 339 of the 1869\par
novel.\par
\par
On page 294, transcribe gray-headed with the hyphen. See page 5 of the\par
1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 295, add a comma after salary in the phrase "with an\par
honestly-earned salary." See page 7 of the 1869 book.\par
\par
Checked the clause "But once get used to these slight blemishes" on page\par
297. The sentence appears the same way on page 10 of the 1869 novel. \par
\par
Checked the clause "People who hire all these things done for them never\par
know what they lose" on page 298. The sentence has a comma after them,\par
but is otherwise written the same way on page 11 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 299, transcribe door-handles with the hyphen. See page 13 of the\par
1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 339, "shortcomings" was replaced with "short-comings". See page\par
62 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 345, "furbelows and notions" was written "furbelows and\par
quinny-dingles" in the 1869 novel. See page 59 of the 1869 novel. We\par
made no change, and only point this out because quinny-dingles is such a\par
memorable word that those intimate with the novel may notice the change.\par
\par
On page 353, change snowbank to snow-bank. See page 79 of the 1869\par
novel.\par
\par
On page 363, a double quotation mark was added before "Cross-patch, draw\par
the latch". See page 91 of the 1869 book.\par
\par
On page 379, change period after Jo to a comma in the clause "as for Jo.\par
she would have gone up". See page 109 of the 1869 book.\par
\par
On page 380, a comma was added after "all lying down". See page 111 of\par
the 1869 book.\par
\par
On page 393, the punctuation after 'but so was everybody's' is difficult\par
to read. It could be a colon or semicolon. In the 1869 novel, the mark\par
is a semi-colon (see page 126). We used the semi-colon.\par
\par
On page 396, the second line of the verse beginning with "'Out upon\par
you," is indented. In the 1869 version, the capital B of "Bold-faced\par
jig!'" is lined-up under Out. We aligned the verse as the 1869 version\par
of the novel--see page 131.\par
\par
On page 404, add period after heaviness. See page 140 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 405, transcribe needle-work with the hyphen. See page 141 of the\par
1869 book.\par
\par
On Page 411, a letter is curiously addressed to Betsey, both here and on\par
page 148 of the 1869 book. \par
\par
On Page 413, removed double quotes around Yes in "Yes," they say to one\par
another, these so kind ladies. Instead, place a single quote in front of\par
Yes, because Bhaer is resuming his quote. The resumed quote concludes\par
with a single quote after me and mine. See page 151 of the 1869 novel.\par
The double quote before 'he is a stupid old fellow' is actually a\par
triple-nested quote, ending in make themselves.\par
\par
On page 417, transcribe Teddy-ism as Teddyism. See page 155 of the 1869\par
novel.\par
\par
On page 451, a period was added after "I can't let you go". See page 196\par
of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 463, "Tarantula" was used as the name of a dance, but the author\par
might have meant "Tarantella," which is the name of an Italian dance\par
about tarantulas.\par
\par
On page 468, transcribe chess-board with the hyphen. See page 218 of the\par
1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 512, a period was added after "she said softly". If you see page\par
272 of the 1869 novel, you will also notice a comma in 'she said,\par
softly.' We added the period but not the comma.\par
\par
On page 514, the 1869 novel did not have a comma after oar in the\par
sentence: "I'm not tired; but you may take an oar, if you like. See page\par
525 of the 1869 novel. We did not remove the comma.\par
\par
On page 527, "the 'the best nevvy" was replaced with "the best nevvy".\par
\par
On page 527, change he to the in 'like Jenny and he ballad'. See page\par
291 of the 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 531, David and Peggotty refer to two characters from the novel\par
"David Copperfield" by Charles Dickens.\par
\par
On page 534, change of to off in the clause: Daisy found it impossible\par
to keep her eyes of her "pitty aunty," ... see page 300 of the 1869\par
novel.\par
\par
On page 541, "know'st thou the land where the citron blooms," was broken\par
into two stanzas in the book for spacing. We transcribed this as one\par
line. See page 308 in 1869 novel.\par
\par
On page 551, transcribe Dove-cote with the hyphen. See page 319 of the\par
1869 novel.\par
\par
On the first page of ads, a period was added after "THE LITTLE WOMEN\par
SERIES. New Illustrated Edition".\par
\par
On the second page of ads, in the blurb for the book Comic Tragedies, a\par
period as placed after "Portraits, etc".\par
\par
After the novel is a list of The Works of Louisa May Alcott. The list is\par
not complete: for example, there are no listings for her work as Flora\par
Fairfield or A. M. Barnard. Nevertheless, the pages are a fine\par
structured outline of Ms. Alcott's best work.\par
\par
Most of the novels and stories in these four pages are published by\par
Project Gutenberg. We included links to these titles for the reader's\par
convenience. A change had to be made for one item: We had to list each\par
of the three volumes of Lulu's Library to provide the links to that\par
book. These links will only work in the HTML document.\par
\par
The final page is a listing of eleven stories originally published in\par
other volumes, such as Jo's Scrap-Bag, Lulu's Library, and A Garland for\par
Girls. These works were subsequently published separately in small\par
volumes, generally less than 100 pages, in The Children's Friend Series. \par
